(1199, 288, 765, 'At least three crucial events will take place when jesus return the general Resurrection, the last judgement, and the renewal of creation, \r\n General Resurrection , in crist return everyone that has ever died will be resurrected,both the wikked and righteous will be given new body that will endure forever,regarding the resurected body of the belivers will ge supperated from the correption and the presents of the sin,in our final state our bodies will be glorious ,\r\nThe second major things will take place in Christ coming is a final judgement, immidiate after the ressurection christ will show his power of the athority to judjment ,every human being will be included inthe last judjment, \r\n renewal of creation, just as jesus will judje humanity,and purge the unbelivers from it and renew the creation itself ', 7, '2019-06-21 17:26:15', '2019-07-28 04:59:57', NULL),
(1200, 293, 675, 'Biblical Hermeneutics is the study of interpreting the meaning and significance of scripture. Hermeneutical process are the main procedures we follow as we interpret Bible. \r\n1. Preparation: The process of preparation take place before we begin our interpretation because we always approach scripture influenced by various concepts, behaviors and emotions. We need to prepare by listening to the Spirit of God, having good tools, good materials written by others, praying and allowing God to speak to us. \r\n2. Investigation: Investigation is the process of concentrating on the original meaning of the passage. We focus on the original meaning intended by God and human authors, and on scripture\\\'s first audience. Also we look in to the linguistic background. The original meaning of the passage is crucial to biblical interpretation. \r\n3. Application: It is the process of appropriately connecting scriptures\\\' original meaning to contemporary audience. \r\nAs Bible is the infallible word of God the true interpretation of it is necessary to understand the message of God to mankind. Biblical hermeneutics is to know the meaning of the scripture and to apply in practical life. ', 9, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1201, 293, 676, 'Scientific hermeneutics refers to, how biblical scholars have approached the interpretation of the scripture like a scientific exercise. \r\nBiblical Roots: The biblical root of scientific hermeneutics make it a legitimate exercise. Biblical authors interpreted other scriptures with an orientation toward factual and logical analysis. The New Testament authors had a scientific approach in writing the scripture, quoting from the Old testament and authentically proving as the fulfilment. \r\nIn Romans 4:3-5, Paul quoted from Genesis 15:6, where God credited righteousness to Abraham when he believed God\\\'s promise. Logically Paul claimed that Abraham was granted righteousness as a free gift through faith. He was proving his argument of Righteousness by faith. \r\nRepeatedly the biblical authors presented this kind of interpretation of the scripture. And their approach to scripture indicates that scientific biblical hermeneutics is firmly rooted in scripture itself. ', 8, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1202, 293, 677, 'Devotional Hermeneutics focuses primarily on the divine authorship of the scripture. Though the biblical interpreters adopt principles from general and scientific hermeneutics, much importance is given for devotional hermeneutics too. Christians believe the inspiration of the Bible and accept it as the word of God. 2 Timothy 3:16\r\nIn Hebrews 4:12, the author quotes from Psalms 95, and again in Hebrews 4:7, quoted the same Psalms, saying that God himself\\\" spoke through David\\\". And in Hebrews 3:7, the author introduces Psalms95 with the word \\\' as the Holy Spirit says\\\". The author of Hebrews uses widely the principle of scientific hermeneutics, but also tells us that it should go along with devotional hermeneutics. \r\nHistorically speaking, Origen of Alexandria, though he was a scientific interpreter of the scripture, he gave much importance to devotional hermeneutics. His advise to Gregory was \\\' Lectio Divina\\\' meaning Divine Reading, is well known. He emphasized the fact that when you read Bible, you should gain the spiritual meaning of it. Interpreters like Augustin and Aquinas also gave emphasis to devotional hermeneutics. \r\nOnly the academic and intellectual approach to the Scripture will not help us much in knowing the heart of God and drawing close to Him. Devotional approach to interpretation is very much essential for our daily walk with Christ and a personal transformative experience. ', 9, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1203, 293, 678, 'There are three important views regarding inspiration.\r\n1. Romantic view: It says scripture is a reflection of human authors. Just as the secular authors moved to write poems or dramas, biblical authors also wrote the scriptures.\r\n2. Mechanical view: It holds the opinion that just like Muslims and Mormons believe, the Bible also was literally dictated. \r\n3. Organic view: According to the theory of organic inspiration, the Holy Spirit moved human beings to write God\\\'s revelation as scripture and superintended their work in a way that made their writing in fallible. As a result the words of the scripture are the words of God. At the same time the Holy Spirit used the personalities, experiences, outlooks and intentions of human authors as He supervised their writing. So the words of scripture are the also very much the words of its human authors. God has inspired the authors to write his words but let them express in their own way. 2 Peter 3:15\r\nTwo important aspects of Organic inspiration are ; \r\n1. Divine source. The Holy Spirit was the divine source of the Scripture.  So the Holy Spirit has the intimate knowledge of the meaning of the scripture. So we need the guidance of the Holy Spirit to know and to interpret the scripture. Biblical portions like Acts 4:25 and Hebrews 3:7-8 identified the Holy Spirit as the inspirer. \r\n2. Human means: We do not believe the literal dictation of the Bible. But it was composed by means of human authors; which was  revealed through the gifts and abilities of human authors As the scripture is organically inspired, we always have to acknowledge the divine and human authorship.\r\n', 9, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1204, 293, 679, 'Illumination can be defined as the work of the Holy Spirit in conveying a proper understanding of the scripture to a human being. The Holy Spirit indwells in Christian believers and opens our minds to understand and to receive what he has inspired in the Bible. In 1 Corinthians 2:11-13 Paul speaks of illumination. The illumination of the Holy Spirit impacts our imagination, emotions, desire will and moral conscience. God is shedding light through the Holy Spirit, giving us spiritual discernment and ability to understand the scripture.\r\n Human effort and divine illumination are necessary for the proper interpretation and understanding of the scripture. 2 Timothy 2:15. The Holy Spirit designed Scriptures to communicate through human means, including human effort from the part of the reader. ', 8, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1205, 293, 680, 'Exegesis is drawing meaning out of biblical texts, especially by looking at things like the historical context, literary forms, grammar, vocabulary, theological setting and so on. The following are the benefits I received out of my exegetical experience.\r\n Every effort that I  put in to the exegesis of the scripture helped me to prepare  for further study. The knowledge, attitude and skills I developed from one encounter with the Bible influenced me the next time I go to the scripture. Exegesis gave me  the in depth meaning of the scripture in its context. It enabled me to handle the scriptures more effectively especially in communicating the message as a preacher of the word. The most important benefit as an exegete I gained is drawing true and deep meanings of the Scripture in its context and communicating it effectively from the pulpit so that the audience are deeply inspired and moved with the truths of the word of God. ', 8, '2019-07-02 11:00:24', '2019-07-02 11:00:24', NULL),
(1206, 300, 593, 'This origin is  more  or  less  confirmed by  the  fact  that  cognate terms are found  not  only in the  western or Germanic dialects and languages but also in the eastern  or Slovakian  nations that were  converted  under  the Greek influence   For  a  close examination of the biblical  ideas behind the  English term “church  it be necessary  to examine  not the English word,  but  the  word  or words  in  the  source  languages  from which the  English Bible  has been\r\n\r\n\r\nThrough most  of the  Old Testament the  Hebrew  term “qahal” is translated as  “assembl  or “congregation”  especially  as  the  people of  God  in  the Old  Testament are called  either  \r\n\r\nThe same Hebrew  word,  “qahal,” can sometimes refer  to  the entirety of  the  “visible  church” as  well. In Nehemiah 13:1, the people read  Deuteronomy chapter twenty-3 There they learned that Ammonites and Moabites should not come into  the congregation of God forever (in Deuteronomy,  however, the  phrase is “congregation of the LORD”.Wdo not understand  the ensuing  separation in  Nehemiah  13:3 to have reference  to the national  and physical  presence  of the  Moabites  and Ammonite. They were not cast  from their  homes  and sent packing to other nations and  localities. Rather  this exercise in Nehemiah  has  typical and  symbolic  import for  the  whole  covenant  community of  God Just as  the Ammonite  and Moabite were  cast  out of  the visible  church  for  their wicked  acts  so too are  the openly and visibly wicked to be cast out of the visible  church  in  this our own da', 8, '2019-07-22 17:44:17', '2019-07-28 04:58:45', NULL),
(1207, 300, 594, 'We  do not claim that  the  form of  church governmentmust be believed  unto salvation  though we  do claim  that  saving  faith doesnot reject  ny  clear teaching  of Scripture and that  it  is  a  sinful  avoiding of Scripture teaching that leads to false  views of church  government We do understand that  there  is a  difference  between  those  essentials  of the faith that are  necessary to be believe  to the saving of the soul and\r\n\r\n    ose  less  fundamental and less foundational  building bocks of doctrine that are not directly related to our salvation.We  agree  with  Thomas Witherows  statement  in his booklet  The  Apostolic Church Which Is  It there  is such a  thing as  being a  Presbyterian without being a Christian, as it is possible to be  a Christian without being  a Presbyterian. Depend upon it, it  is best to be both. \r\n\r\nThree ideas surface repeatedly through  discussions  of church  polity because  they  belong to  the very fundamentals   of biblical  or  constitutional  Presbyterian  church government.\r\n\r\n1)The sparity  or  equality of  all  the  ministers  of the Word and sacraments. Biblical  Presbyterianism  rejects  as  destructive of church  polity the unbiblical idea  of one  minister having a  greater authority of office  than any other.  Whether  we find it  in Romanism, Prelacy,  or Methodism, the  principle  of  one  minister being  “a  pastor of pastors” is foreign and even  anathema to constitutional Presbyterian\r\n\r\n2) dimportan and fundamental  jus divinum  feature  of constitutional  Presbyterianism  is  the fact that  the government  of the church  is vestedi in iuling elders. This biblical form of  church  government helps to  insure  the church  against the encroachments of  ministerial ambitions.In   a perfect world  with  perfect people  ministers would constantly remember  their role  as servants of Christ and his church. But, alas, we  live  in  a world  much  affected  by the fall of man. God has therefore,  in his wisdom, not deposited church\r\n\r\n3)  final principle of  biblical  Presbyterianism is  that  of the confederacy of like-minded churches As much as possible  Presbyterian churches  attempt to  demonstrate the unity  of the church by connection with other  churches. However,  in order to be true  to the first principle  the church  is  finally to be  governed  by the Word of God \r\n\r\n4). By the law of the house Christ the King and legislator whose glory fills his house   governs everything in  his house  its structure, the entrances and exits and where they  shall be, all  the house’s designs,  its  statutes  and  all its laws.  The  law of God’s  house is  written in  the  Bible  as  the  infallible  and all-sufficient revelation of  the  will and character of God .  The  entire church throughout all ages, therefore, may observe  its whole  design and all its statutes, and do them. It is the law of the  house.', 9, '2019-07-22 17:44:17', '2019-07-28 04:58:45', NULL),
(1208, 300, 595, 'When  God’s  people repent and know the  shamefulness of  their sins,  then  God shows them the  form of his house,  as Ezekiel 43:11 states. It  is the duty of His people, then, to  become acquainted  with the rules and duties of His house.  God shows His people  the ordinances  of His  house so  that  they may  observe and do  them (Deuteronomy  29:29 cf. Matthew 28:19). Matthew Henry  has  well  expressed the privilege and duty  of believers who  live  in the  day of  Ezekiel’s  temple  and  the relationship the privileges and duties bear  to one another\r\n\r\nThe  outcome of  this consideration should be  an  awareness of  the importance  of the  visible ministry and  ordinances  of God  as established  in the  visible  church  as  they  are  his instruments for  perpetuating not only the  visible  church  considered as  visible,  but for perpetuating the  visible church as it  is  the means  of  gathering  and  perfecting the elect  of  God — the bride of  the  Lamb.\r\n\r\nThe ideal Church Thu exists eternally in the mind of God The historicoal  outworking of the ideal  is found in the effectual calling justificatio., conversion, adoption, sanctification, and final glorification of the  elec t In the  context of  that  historical outworking, God  has decreed  to use church  ordinances to bring  about  the calling faith  repentance perseverance, and glorification of  those who are  parts  or members  of his ideal church as   those  ordinances are  visible  and evident in history', 8, '2019-07-22 17:44:17', '2019-07-28 04:58:45', NULL),
(1209, 300, 596, 'Many things we found in church negative and positive \r\n', 0, '2019-07-22 17:44:17', '2019-07-22 17:44:17', NULL),
(1210, 301, 681, 'The concepts, behaviors and emotions of the writers influenced the writing of the scripture. According to organic inspiration, the document reflects the thoughts, intentions, feelings, literary skills of the author. The Holy Spirit chose to communicate His word through human writers. Focusing on the human writer helps us to understand the true meaning. The Holy Spirit communicated through the personalities, experiences, emotions and patterns of thought of human biblical writers. The significance of human writers explicitly mentioned in different portions of the Bible. \r\nFor example, Jesus mentioned about David  in Matthew 222:41-45 Here Jesus referred the authorship of Psalms 110 to David. The scripture is more clearly understood when we read and study in the context of its author. The Bible reinforces its meaning by the life background of the authors. The original context of the author and audience give us much deeper understanding of the meaning of the scripture. What the original author meant and what the original audience heard is very important in identifying the original meaning of the word of God. \r\nThe Bible is people oriented. Life and experiences of the people especially the authors highlighted in the scripture to be a model for the readers. So the true meaning is understood more clearly through understanding the author in his life context.', 9, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1211, 301, 682, 'God gave his word to his people in ways that suited their historical circumstances so that the original audience can understand in their context. That means God always accommodated his revelation to his original audience. For example, the Ten commandments were written on stone and the Apostles wrote the NT in Greek language which was understood by the people of the day. \r\nGod adapted the words and ideas of the scripture to the culture, technology, social structure and even religious experiences of its first audience, so that they would understand what he was saying. \r\nThere are General and specific accommodations. In General, the scripture was written to suit universal human conditions. God revealed himself in away that suit every human being through out history. We see this universal accommodation through out the scripture. Great God peaking to mere human  beings just like a nurse speaking to an infant. Divine revelations were designed to meet our general human limitations For example Jesus redefined the aspect of divorce in Matthew 19, quoting from Deuteronomy 24. \r\nGod also accommodated his revelation to specifically, speaking to special people. Many of the New Testament writings are addressed to individual Churches and persons. \r\nIn order to understand the revelation properly, we have to learn as much as we can about those original audience. God has been speaking to a particular people, in particular setting and in particular time. Without considering the context , the efforts to know the true meaning of the scripture will not be successful. \r\n\r\n', 8, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1212, 301, 683, 'In the process of understanding the true meaning of the scripture, the study of the original language and text are very significant. Doctrines of organic inspiration and divine accommodation play vital role in the process of investigation. \r\nOrganic inspiration: It is true that the Holy Spirit inspired human writers. But it does not mean that every copy or translation are perfect. Jeremiah 8:8 gives the clue that copies of biblical documents can contain mistakes. Also we see that different translations and versions of the biblical documents vary in many ways. \r\nDivine inspiration is limited only to the original documents of the author, not to its copies or translations. So we need to understand the original document to know exactly what God communicated. It is true that most of the original documents are missing and the available manuscripts are only copies. But we need to understand that though the original manuscripts of the OT were unavailable during His time, Jesus and the Apostles approved the authenticity of the Old Testament.  In His providence God has preserved the scripture in remarkable way. Though there are scribal errors and alterations in the manuscripts, a comparative study of different manuscripts will help in understanding the original words and meanings. \r\nDivine Accommodation: As God has accommodated his revelation generally and specifically, knowing the original audience and context is important in interpreting the scripture. \r\nSo the ancient manuscripts can shed much light to the scriptures in providing true meaning to its readers of different periods and backgrounds. ', 8, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1213, 301, 684, 'Following are the three approaches towards the objects and subjects of Knowledge. \r\n1. Objectivism: According to this approach, under the right circumstances it is possible to arrive at impartial or objective knowledge. Rational scientific objectivism has influenced biblical interpretation too.  It has led us to critical biblical studies. It has also influenced evangelical biblical studies. \r\nCritical scholars stand for rational investigation of the scripture. But solely they end up in rejecting many claims and teachings of the scripture. \r\nObjective approach is very much helpful in interpreting Bible carefully and responsibly with reason and sound methods. Ans we must be aware that human beings can never be completely objective, unbiased investigators of facts. \r\n2. Subjectivism: Subjectivists believe that our knowledge is always influenced by our personal biases, making impartial objectivity impossible. This approach strongly relies upon personal faculties like intuition and emotions. Philosophers like Immanuel Kant promoted this view. Subjective approach also influenced both critical and evangelical study of the scripture. Critically it has gone to an extend of finding their own meaning than the original meaning of the scripture. \r\n3. Dialogism: This is a middle ground which emphasizes constant dialogue or interplay between the objective reality and our subjective perspective. This approach refers to the idea that interpretation involves a type of dialogue or discussion between the reader and the text. Th e basic idea is that the text has an objective meaning, but this objective meaning is best discovered through a subjective interaction or dialogue between the reader and the text.', 8, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1214, 301, 685, 'Modern subjectivism emerged partly in response to the objectivism of 17th and 18th centaury enlightenment. Philosophers like David Hume argued that reason and scientific study can\\\'t lead us to objective knowledge about the world. They believed that our emotions, desires and mental categories influence our thinking, making impartial objectivity impossible. German philosopher Immanuel Kant concluded that the knowledge of the world always involves both our empirical perceptions and our mental conceptualization. \r\nAfter Hume and Kant, subjective approach continued to develop in the 19th centaury through movements like romanticism. They insisted that interpreters should relay on their own personal human characteristics in interpreting texts. @0th centaury post structuralism shifted the subjective approach. They emphasized that objective claims of knowledge can\\\'t be trusted because they are far too limited and far too influenced by subjective prejudices, feelings and existing believes. \r\nNow subjectivism has wide spread. The say that since we can\\\'t discover the objective understanding of the world around us, then the meaning of the art and literature, including the Bible must be located within us. \r\nThough Christians don\\\'t allow currents of culture to influence the interpretation, in reality, no one ca entirely escape the effects of culture o our approach to biblical hermeneutics. ', 9, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1215, 301, 686, 'Comparison: Objective and subjective approaches to meaning oppose each other in some fundamental ways., but they have something very important in common. Both models ultimately make the authority of the interpreters equal to or even greater than the authority of the Bible itself. Objectivism tends to overestimate how reliable our rational and scientifically objective views are; where as subjectivism tends to overestimate how reliable our personal intuitions and opinions are. \r\n\r\nAuthority- dialogue and Objective Models: This model acknowledges that objective truth can be found in the text of scripture. The Bible is God\\\'s revelation to us and everything it says is objectively true and meaningful If the methods comply with the biblical standards, it can help us in understanding scripture.  The authority dialogue approach helps us to remember that rational and scientific judgement must always be viewed in submission to the authority of the word of God. \r\n\r\nAuthority - Dialogue and subjective Models: This approach says that we all come to the scripture with perspectives and believes that influence the way we interpret the Bible.  More over it agrees with scripture and subjectivism that the personal subjective input we bring to interpretation is vulnerable.  The subjectivity should be submitted to the authority of the Word. Investigating the Bible is a life long process in which scripture changes us and cause us to grow in our Christian faith. The dialogue become successful when the Holy Spirit moves us towards grater understanding and application of the scripture. ', 8, '2019-07-24 11:08:04', '2019-07-28 04:57:59', NULL),
(1216, 298, 766, 'jesus was anointed by holy spirit at His baptism ,this anointed officially declare jesus to be the christ,Anad alos empowerd him for ministry ,and god incarnate , jesus was omnipotent ,he vield his power and the glory inorder to be like the people came to save,\r\n   jesus reliaid the power of the holy spirit for his ministry but his appoinment was not declared untill his baptism ,when it was anounced to the world by other member of trinity ,the holy spirit confirmed that jesus is christ by decending him like a dove. ', 7, '2019-07-24 16:45:51', '2019-07-28 04:59:22', NULL),
(1217, 298, 767, 'There were three impotent for jesus temptation ,\r\n  First ,Temptation taught him Obedience Hebrew 5.8-9, Son though he was, he learned obedience from what he suffered 9 and, once made perfect, he became the source of eternal salvation for all who obey him, jesus never dispbay God,but he was aloso a fully human,as we see inthe temptation he suffered jesus obaid God both by keeping the requirmen ot his law,and sumbiting to the father\\\'s plane for his life,\r\n The second idea will mention that Jesus temptation gave his sympathy for his people, jesus didnt give into the tmptation ,but he still felt it acutaly,there is a sence in wich everithing that jesus endure become a model that how christian can deal with a temptation, \r\n The third idea of temptaion make  impeccability ,inability to sin, all christian understant that jesus never sinned, ', 7, '2019-07-24 16:45:51', '2019-07-28 04:59:22', NULL),
(1218, 298, 768, 'Crucifixion was a form of a death penalty used in the ancient roman empire , Jesus crucifixion was unique offcorse becose it also served as an atoning offering of the sin,as the christ it was his responsability to die on behalf of his people as we read in hebrw 9.11-22, jesus gave him self on the cross as a substitute of his people ,befor our sin was imuted to him,he was blameless and perfect,but as strange, and it may sound once our sin was reckoned to his account,without any sin he crusifide, so his crusifixen was unique.', 6, '2019-07-24 16:45:51', '2019-07-28 04:59:22', NULL),
(1219, 298, 769, 'Death is the greatest tragedy that human beings experience, and the worst manifestation of sin in this world. But the good news is that God’s anointed Christ conquered death for all of us. When he rose from the grave through the power of the Spirit, he proved to all creation that he really is God’s favored Son and the heir to his kingdom. And even more wonderful than this, he ensured the future resurrection and blessing of all his faithful followers.  In this sense, Jesus’ resurrection was a critical step that enabled God to fulfill his covenant promises. This is one of the reasons that the New Testament calls the resurrection an affirmation of Jesus’ role as Christ, as we see in Luke 24:45-46; John 2:17-22; Acts 17:3; and Romans 1:1-4.  ', 8, '2019-07-24 16:45:51', '2019-07-28 04:59:22', NULL),
(1220, 298, 770, 'When the Scriptures describe what Jesus is doing now, they often say that he’s seated at the right hand of God the Father. This language can be misleading to some modern readers. Jesus isn’t just sitting next to the Father waiting for the time of his return; he’s sitting on a throne. And that means he’s reigning over his kingdom. He’s the vassal king who sits at God’s right hand. And he’ll keep reigning over us and interceding for us until he returns. Jesus’ session proves that he’s victorious over sin and death, and it authorizes him to give his people continual comfort in the midst of every problem we face in life. \r\n ', 7, '2019-07-24 16:45:51', '2019-07-28 04:59:22', NULL),
(1221, 302, 589, 'At the point of the biblical account humanity was clearly distinguished from the rest of creation.\r\nHuman being were created in Gods image, and authority over his other creatures.we simply want to point out that not only was humanity part of creation; it was also pinnacle.\r\nGod created humanity to reflect his glory and his attributes in ways that other creatures couldn\'t in a later lesson, we explore the concept of the image is to be like a picture of God.', 18, '2019-08-08 16:43:37', '2019-08-09 21:57:41', NULL),
(1222, 302, 590, 'God covenant benevolence was one full display after Adam fell into sin .in ge2:17 God had warned Adam that humanity die if they law by eating the fruit of the tree of knowledge of good and evil. When thay eat that fruit they don\'t die but they loss Gods gifts \r\n\r\n But God plan for a requirement of human loyalty, \r\nWhen God created them God gifted many things but thay do sin ... there accomplish God s wish', 15, '2019-08-08 16:56:16', '2019-08-09 21:57:41', NULL),
(1223, 302, 591, 'Human fell into sin early in our existence. Gen3 1-7 the serpent temped Eve to eat the forbidden fruit, and she did . Then she gave some of it to Adam, and he eat to .\r\nImmediately, they realize they were naked \r\nThen in the 3: 8-24 God confront Adam and Eve and cursed then \r\nBecause of their disloyal.\r\nGod word say \" the fall\" reflect the idea that Adam and Eve sin cause ed humanity fall out god Faber and blessings.\r\nBut God open for one way for saving man from sin that is Jesus Christ', 23, '2019-08-08 17:11:17', '2019-08-09 21:57:41', NULL),
(1224, 302, 592, 'Trichotomis see in Gen 2:7 the first implications of the Constitution of man nature \r\nThis vers is of such deep significant that interpretation can never exhaust it it is the foundation of all true anthropology\r\n\r\n1 these 5 :23\r\n \r\nHebrews 4:12', 20, '2019-08-08 17:23:51', '2019-08-09 21:57:41', NULL),
(1225, 308, 655, 'The holy Sprite has led the church to pursue the exegesis of Scripture in three main ways , literary analysis, historical analysis and thematic analysis.\r\n\r\n  Historical analysis looks at scripture as a Window to history, explore history event lying behind \r\nthe Bible. \r\nThe Bible report many different kinds of history event, but biblical theology primary ask , \r\n\"What do the scripture say that God has done \"\r\nBecause Christian answer this question in different ways, we need to pause for a moment to reflect on what the Bible teach about act of God in history', 8, '2019-08-26 16:42:33', '2019-08-26 19:20:59', NULL),
(1226, 308, 656, 'In biblical theology Theological reflection is based on historical analysis of the acts of God in Scripture, but historical analysis can take different from. It helps to think of at least two main tendencies., Factual historical analysis and Theological historical analysis. This two tendency go hand in hand, but there main concern are quite different.\r\nMore often than not mden reader of Bible take a factual approach to biblical history. That is to say , they are with how the event report in Scripture fit within the large environment of the ancient.\r\nA factual approach to history analysis is connected with questions like the date of the exdos .the goal of factual historical analysis is rather straight ward it to establish a reliable account of the fact of history', 8, '2019-08-26 16:57:01', '2019-08-26 19:20:59', NULL),
(1227, 308, 657, 'Biblical theology is in large measure a response to culture shift that can be tracked back to the enlightenment of the 17 th century.this is not to say that the concern of biblical theology were entirely new.belon only modem period.\r\nBiblical theology is a Christian response to a prominent intellectual movement in the modern period, called morden historian. In very general terms, morden historiam is belif that history hold the key for understanding ourselves and the world around us. In this view an adequate understanding of anything can only be gained\r\nOne of most well known enlightenment figure who expressed this culture shift was the German philosophy Georg Wilhelm f who lived 1770 to 1841.', 7, '2019-08-26 17:11:00', '2019-08-26 19:20:59', NULL),
(1228, 308, 658, 'Morden historiam inspired many critical theologian to approach the scripture with new questions and priorities . \r\nNow it is important to realize that as a critical theologian Galen believe that the findings of biblical theology might be of some interest from to time to time, but modern Christian should believe only those parts of the Bible that pass the standard of modern rational and scientific analysis.in his views the scripture reflect the naive practice and belif of people who lived before the modern rational pered', 7, '2019-08-26 17:22:20', '2019-08-26 19:20:59', NULL),
(1229, 308, 659, 'One traditional and helpful way to speak of God activity in history appear in the (Westminster)\r\nGod\'s activity in the world give us a covenant summery of some important perspective listen to the way God\'s Providence is described there', 6, '2019-08-26 17:29:24', '2019-08-26 19:20:59', NULL),
(1230, 310, 660, 'The word synchronic from two Greek word.the preposition sun which means with or \'together with \'\r\nAnd non chrons which means \'time\'\r\nSynchronic applied in historical events it describes occurrence that took place\"together in time\" or \" at the time\"\r\nTo illustrate this idea same way as a many movies scens as a full  story movie\r\nOld testament as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of it entire history \r\nBiblical theologian also deal with large period of time like Abraham life in Gen 11-25 a time that spanned around 175 years.ev', 9, '2019-08-30 16:48:02', '2019-09-05 12:19:36', NULL),
(1231, 310, 661, 'In the middle of the 20 th century many studies distinguished biblical theology from systematic theology by pointing out the of logic in each discipline.\r\nIs easy to see that logic has a vital fiction in traditional systematic theology. But critical theologian argument that logic should not play such a major role in biblical theology \r\nWhile the introduction of these discussions go far beyond this study summers there pistons in a helpful way. In essence, critical theologian believed that logic was a primary features of what they called the Greek mindset but it was relatively foreign to the Hebrew mindset based on a number of linguistics and culture assessment they argued that the Greek focused on abstraction and logic order', 8, '2019-08-30 17:00:59', '2019-09-05 12:19:36', NULL),
(1232, 310, 662, 'Poets of the old testament were interested in two worlds that Tell us about  history. On the one hand they have attention to the world they wrote about what we will call that world when writing about that world they provided object fact \r\nFor example one well know poetical passage is the song that Moses and Miriam sang at the red sea in Ex 15 \r\nReaders knowing that what God has done in red sea\r\nSecond...Ps 1 explain Gods law and \r\nIn third place Isaiah 40 Jude return there land\r\nMuch like poet and author of narrative also stood between two worlds. One the one side they wrote about the world about the world that was the object of ther text \r\nOld testament author often wrote about time that preceded the day in which they lived by hundreds of years \r\nOne the other side author of narrative also', 8, '2019-08-30 17:16:01', '2019-09-05 12:19:36', NULL),
(1233, 310, 663, 'In reality neither the writing of King nor the author of chronicles interested to be utterly precious.what they wrote was history true. \r\nResponsible interpretation distill what God said with level of precision that match the biblical record. \r\nAs we explore the genre of narrative in synchronic synthetic.we face many different kinds of impression.number of people measurement geography reference and the like often do not meet morden scientific started. But this lack of morden precious Don\'s not mean the account are nutrition one of the country, we can be carful not to overtime \r\nFinally let s consider the fact that old testament narrative are not objective by morden started it s common in our day to think that reliable historical writinger remain object in there reporting.', 7, '2019-08-30 17:28:30', '2019-09-05 12:19:36', NULL),
(1234, 310, 664, 'As we look for the synthetic logical arrangements of old testament theology in particular period of history, in soon because appear that various levels \r\n\r\nBasic level - synthetic Theological structure \r\nThe most basic theologian structure appears in the logical connection and implications among specific act and words of God.', 3, '2019-08-30 17:36:12', '2019-09-05 12:19:36', NULL),
(1235, 312, 655, NULL, 0, '2019-09-01 17:31:12', '2019-09-01 17:31:12', NULL),
