(1239, 312, 659, NULL, 0, '2019-09-01 17:31:38', '2019-09-01 17:31:38', NULL),
(1240, 311, 665, 'Diachronic derives from 2 Greek word 1) dia- \"through\" or throughout, and second the Greek noun \"chronos \" - mean-\"time\"\r\nDiachrony is a concern with the passage of time.in the case of biblical theology, the team dichroic \r\nPoints to the way old testament theology transformed , changed, or developed through time \r\nIt will help to see how diachronic approved to the ot relate to synchronic synthesis , \r\nIt ask a question\" what changes in theology occurred as God acted and spoke in different way during this period of time.\r\nHow God revelation at the burning bush anticipate his work in Egypt\" thes and many other similar issue became of central importance in diachronic approach to this part of the old testament', 9, '2019-09-03 16:49:49', '2019-09-05 12:15:35', NULL),
(1241, 311, 666, 'We use the term development instead of simply speaking of diachronic change to suggest two main ideas, 1) change in old testament Theological structure always move towards fulfilled God\'s purpose for history, and 2) we speak of development because Theological change only occur through God provides involvement in history, Consider first the divine purpose behind every change in ot theology \r\nShift in OT Theological structure were not random or pointless. They were purposefully they unfailing brought about Gods short term goal as well as the fulfillment of his kingdom plan . Knowing that Theological change in the old testament moved history irrevocably toward God\'s goals we need to add that these developments occurred in connection with God\'s Providence involvement in history', 9, '2019-09-03 17:03:35', '2019-09-05 12:15:35', NULL),
(1242, 311, 667, '1) Universal Covenant --\r\nCovenant with Adam and Noah as \" universal\" because they were made between God and all humanity. So the Theological structure of these covenant tell us much about the relationship between God and all people \r\n\r\n2) National covenant --\r\nFollowing the universal covenant, God established national covenant with his special people Israel; the covenants with Abraham, Moses and David. \r\n3)New covenant --\r\nIn the later history of the old testament, the prophets of Israel faced the time when Israel would go into exile , they speak, ,\r\nHowever, of a covenant that would be established after the exile, at that time Israel would repent of sin and God would bring history to final ...', 8, '2019-09-03 17:17:04', '2019-09-05 12:15:35', NULL),
(1243, 311, 668, 'Biblical theology is Theological reflection drawn from historical analysis of acts of God reported in Scripture \r\n\r\nBiblical theology focus one Scripture account of God involvement in history and drawn inferences for Christian theology from those events', 5, '2019-09-03 17:24:39', '2019-09-05 12:15:35', NULL),
(1244, 311, 669, 'Now as important as it is to reconsider ability authorized of earlier covenant, we must also acknowledge that', 4, '2019-09-03 17:31:55', '2019-09-05 12:15:35', NULL),
(1245, 315, 670, 'This twofold concept of Revelation has characterized biblical theology of new Testament as well . On the one hand , the new Testament reports many revelatory acts of God, such as Christ earthly ministry \r\nBiblical theologian have pointed out that the New Testament contain all three kinds of word revelation as well. The Gospel report how God spoke through the words of Jesus to reflect on Jesus earlier action. They also report times when Jesus teaching explained his simultaneous action \r\nAs well as time when Jesus predicted future action. The same can be said of the authors and characters in the books of acts and revelation as well as the new Testament epistles \r\nNT theologian have drawn attention to the twofold manner in which God revelation himself.', 9, '2019-09-06 16:45:33', '2019-09-10 03:30:20', NULL),
(1246, 315, 671, 'Diachronic Theologian developments like thes appear in new Testament period, but new Testament history present at least three major obstacles to extensive diachronic analysis. In the first place by comparison with the old testament, the new Testament cover a very short period of history. Compare the length of history in the Old and new Testament for a moment. Not including the prehistory days of the first eleven chapter of Genesis, \r\nThe old testament deal with over 1600 years of history extending from the time of Abraham who bc 2000 and last prophet who ministred around 400bc \r\nNew Testament history 100 years.', 2, '2019-09-06 16:57:13', '2019-09-10 03:30:20', NULL),
(1247, 315, 672, 'Abraham, Moses , and David. Each of these stage contributed essential elements to the eschatology of the Old testament \r\nOn the other hand many rabbis also spoke of a second period of history, the time of future glory for Israel. They called this future period the age to come This time in history would be a never ending age of Israel blessing and triumph over evil . \r\nAt that time God gather all the people judge the unfaithful life. \r\nJugs the wicked among the nations', 2, '2019-09-06 17:08:52', '2019-09-10 03:30:20', NULL),
(1248, 315, 673, 'Old testament prophet, first century Jews and even John the Baptist though of the arrival of the last days more or less as a single historical step . Jesus also viewed the transition to the last days as the last step of history,but consider his analogy \r\nBut if we look at more closely, it difficult to see that it can be divided into at least three phase:\r\nLifting your foot from the ground, extending it through the air and lowering your foot to the ground.in the same way Jesus explained that the last days or the eschatology\r\nAs a morden followses of Christ we entered the Christian faith with a morden cultural background. We all came to Christ with views of life that are very different from the background of new Testament writers.and because of these differentes we often have to work very hard', 1, '2019-09-06 17:23:52', '2019-09-10 03:30:20', NULL),
(1249, 315, 674, NULL, 0, '2019-09-06 17:29:34', '2019-09-06 17:29:34', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(1250, 319, 687, 'Thomas Aquinas, the famous Theologian held the view that the literal sense of the scripture was foundational to all its other senses. He insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal sense of the passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere In scripture in spiritual sense. He insisted in principle that every sense of a passage of scripture must be tied to its literal meaning. But this view was not adopted by every one. \r\nUnfortunately this method was misused by medieval Church and asserted that they had special God given insights in to spiritual meanings to support many doctrines. \r\nBut renaissance scholars began to study classical, literary, philosophical and religious texts in their original language. They began to interpret the text apart from the authority of the Church. The protestant Scholars believed that scripture\'s literal sense is both singular and plural. They saw the literal sense as a complex meaning that included historical, doctrinal, moral, and eschatological aspects. \r\nThe most responsible approach to the richness of the scripture is to ensure that everything we say about a biblical passage is tied to its grammar set within the historical context of the ancient world. Then we will be able to discover the complex meaning that God and his inspired authors intended to communicate to the original audience of the scripture.', 8, '2019-09-18 09:43:39', '2019-10-22 13:39:04', NULL),
(1251, 319, 688, 'Biblical elaborations are places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture. \r\nThe Bible elaborates the commandment against murder in many places. It is recorded first in Exodus 20: 13. The first elaboration of this passage we see a repetition in Deuteronomy 5:17, where Moses reminded people of Israel about the law. Repetition confirmed the command and reminded the terms of covenant. \r\nThe second type of elaboration we see as a clarification of the command in Numbers 35:20-25, where Moses distinguished between murder and accidental man slaughter. \r\nThe third type of biblical elaboration was expansion, in which scripture provides additional information about the passage. We see this in Matthew 5: 21-22. There Jesus criticized the Rabbis of his day for wrongly limiting the scope of the commandment. Here the command is expanded beyond physical act of unlawfully taking a life. According to Jesus\' elaboration, unrighteous anger violates the same principle that murder does. \r\nThe biblical elaborations confirm other parts of the Bible by repeating them, by clarifying them and even by expanding on their original meaning.', 9, '2019-09-18 09:55:19', '2019-10-22 13:39:04', NULL),
(1252, 319, 689, 'The interpretation of the scripture can be interpreted relevantly without misapplying them by considering the following key aspects in interpretation.\r\n1. The original meaning. The original meaning of a passage means the concepts, behaviors and emotions that the divine and human writers jointly intended the document to communicate to iys first audience. The original meaning is always multi faceted and there is complexity with it. So the reformers looked in to both the grammatical and historical setting. The original meaning contributes to the full value of the scripture. \r\n2. The Biblical Elaboration. It is places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comment on an aspect of the meaning of another passage. These elaborations always confirm the original meaning. Scripture is repeated, clarified and expanded. \r\n3. Legitimate Applications. It is the impacts that the original meaning and biblical elaborations of a passage should have on their audience. All legitimate applications of scripture must be derived from and consistent with the Bible\'s original meaning and elaborations. \r\nIn the complexity of the meaning, the scripture can be interpreted effectively and relevantly by giving heed to the original meaning, biblical elaborations and legitimate applications.', 8, '2019-09-18 10:07:26', '2019-10-22 13:39:04', NULL),
(1253, 319, 690, 'The following are the intentional fallacies that can be made by interpreters. \r\n1. Over emphasizing the Author. We might make un warranted assumptions about an authors\' intentions by speculating about what he was thinking when he wrote. \r\n\r\n2. Over Emphasizing Document. This error is known as a graphic fallacy. This over emphasizes the document itself to the relative exclusion of contextual considerations like the writer and the audience. We might commit this fallacy by thinking that we can sufficiently grasp the meaning of passage merely by analyzing its vocabulary, grammar and structure without author or original audience. \r\n3.  A third type of interpretive mistake we can make is over emphasizing audience. This is often called an affective fallacy because it focuses too heavily on how scripture affects its audience. \r\n\r\nThese fallacies mostly happen when our information  about the writer, document and the audience are limited. When our information more limited, its generally wise for our interpretations to be more generalized. We need to properly account for the interdependence of the guidance we receive from the writer, the document and the audience, to avoid these fallacies.', 9, '2019-09-18 10:20:33', '2019-10-22 13:39:04', NULL),
(1254, 319, 691, 'Making multiple summaries of the same passage is a meaningful method to extract the true meaning of the scripture passage. Multiple legitimate summaries bring us closer to the passage\'s full meaning. \r\n\r\nAll interpreters come to biblical writings with different sets of concerns, assumptions, background and questions. Each of us read scripture differently because God has gifted us differently. \r\nPeople\'s gift and backgrounds lead them to summarize biblical passages in distinct ways. The personal strength of the interpreters lead each to bring out important but different aspects of passage\'s original meaning. \r\n At the same time, both types of summaries cam also be hindered by interpreter\'s\' weaknesses. \r\nInterdependence on other interpreters is a way beneficial for better understanding. We need to learn from what others have seen from the scripture than we study the scripture by ourselves only. \r\nGod intended the Bible and biblical interpretations to be understood in a context of fellowship with other believers. \r\nDifferent people receiving insights of the scripture according to their gifts and back grounds can help each other in knowing the truths and bringing out more relevant and legitimate applications to the context.', 8, '2019-09-18 10:35:02', '2019-10-22 13:39:04', NULL),
(1255, 321, 771, 'Critical interpreters have suggested at least two alternatives \r\nSome interpetrs have looked for a unknown jemas in the early church \r\n1) they say there is no mention of the author relationship to Jesus. \r\n2) some critical scholars assume pseudonymity because the book give evidence the author was aware of Hellenistic or Greek culture, and James was a Jew from Palestine. \r\n3) argument for pseudonymity point to inconsistent with the Theological point.. some of the idea express in the epistle of James don\'t Mach Theological outlook attributes to James in these this book', 6, '2019-09-20 16:07:46', '2019-10-22 13:39:56', NULL),
(1256, 321, 772, 'James 1:1 the original audience was made up primary of Jewish Christian who lived outside of Palestine.\r\nJames addressed him audience as Brothers but how did James living in Jerusalem, know his audience well enough to speak to them in this way??  Act 8 : 1- we read that, in the time of persecution, member of the church were scattered out Judea and Samaria. It\'s possible then that James, as the leader of the Jerusalem Church \r\n\r\nJames original reader were Jewish Christian of Jesus, \r\nFor example - 2:2 James chose the Word \"sunagoge \" this is topical way to say Jew gathering \r\n5:4 James used the phrase\" Lord Almighty \" or kurios sabaoth ... this is come from OT name of the God of Israel,', 6, '2019-09-20 16:21:38', '2019-10-22 13:39:56', NULL),
(1257, 321, 773, '1)The location of the author isn\'t difficult to discuss, Both the NT early church fathers suggest that James lived his life of ministry in Jerusalem. And his last time he also lived in Jerusalem..so there is no other reason to think that he wrote the book from in any other locations \r\n2) Date earliest and latest likely Date for this letter are fairly easy to establish first the earliest likely AD 44, when he has a leader of a early church act 12:17\r\nThe Time of Peter release... that was Agrippa died time that was ad 44\r\n3) purpose 1:2-4 indicated James audience was facing trials of many kinds of problems. But James say stay as full joy ther trials \r\n\r\nJames directed believes to humble selves so that God would lift up he taught that humanity before God is a path', 6, '2019-09-20 16:36:41', '2019-10-22 13:39:56', NULL),
(1258, 321, 774, 'This book focusing a great deal of attention on wisdom as the way to find joy in times of trial. \r\nBy the time this letter, there had been a long history of wisdom literature stemming OT .OT wisdom writing including job and Ecclesiastes, as well as the book of proverbs and a number of so called wisdom Psalm and prophets. \r\nThe book of James also reflects the content of influence wisdom books outside of Scripture like the wisdom of sirach . Also known simply as sirach Solomon \r\nThe wisdom literature of James in the the structure of the book is quiet different from we might expect. Even a brief look at this letter tel us that it organization isn\'t simple. In fact from our modern point of view, it can seem quite disorganized.', 6, '2019-09-20 16:51:46', '2019-10-22 13:39:56', NULL),
(1259, 321, 775, '1:2 this passage may seem to us especially because it address people who were facing trials of many kinds \r\nBut James appeal to consider trials pure joy is not as unusual as we might think \r\nThe phrase\" pure joy\" come from the Greek word \' pasan charan\' that may be translated  \"complete, unmitigated Joy\' this kind of encouragement fits well with other wisdom literature of James day . Many times wisdom writings encouraged those who suffered to consider themselves blessed.', 5, '2019-09-20 17:00:27', '2019-10-22 13:39:56', NULL),
(1260, 328, 655, 'Biblical theology approaches  the scripture with historical analysis ,Historical analysis looks scripture as a window to History ,exploring the historical events take place in the history ,especially in ancient church, and by Christ ,   historical view that the main context of the ,place and time, the circumstances, also the culture of the people who was lived there , , and what do scripture  says about it ,and how God was done , what was the acts of God in history', 5, '2019-10-14 16:45:19', '2019-10-22 13:40:48', NULL),
(1261, 328, 656, 'In biblical theology theological reflection based on the historical analysis of the acts of God in scripture,but historical analysis can take id different forms ,manly factual historical analysis and theological historical analysis ,\r\n   factual analysis based on how the events report in Scripture, its always concern about Date, the people, how this possible, and journey under Moses when it ha pend, and evidence of battles, what datas have in scripture about it,But theological historical evidence depend upon more clearly in the bible , how God acts in this miraculous  works, the power of almighty Gods hand , how character of god revealed through this mighty works, and how people sin, righteous ,  forgiveness having relation with this matter ,always biblical theology reflects the relation to God, by all events what scriptur teaching us about God,', 6, '2019-10-14 17:00:03', '2019-10-22 13:40:48', NULL),
(1262, 328, 657, 'Christian theologians rightly sought to fulfill the great commission by reforming christian theology , ancient and midi val church have responsibility to bring truth of Christ to  Mediterranean world  ,Christianity face many challenges of philosophy, so they have to stand to prof the faithfulness of the scripture, christian stand for theological scripture ,  in 17 th century biblical theology responds to cultural issues, christian have always explore the acts of God in scripture, but in modern period  theologian have interest to historical deviance never before, its looking the cultural issues too.', 7, '2019-10-14 17:13:20', '2019-10-22 13:40:48', NULL),
