(1294, 348, 674, 'Kingdom of God is shapes everyday by adding people to the church ,for that every individual need to share gospel,win soul, by sharing gospel,someone ind will change, life will change ,some community changes, God wants everyone to be saved, so we should change the our concepts of kingdom of God with Gods concepts ,love the people, as Christ Love, help them to grow in Christ everyday ,teach the to stay in his kingdom, developed them to be the part of build his kingdom,', 5, '2019-12-11 06:42:58', '2019-12-18 21:26:22', NULL),
(1295, 363, 776, 'The identity of the Authorship of Hebrews is a difficulty as the it is not revealed in the letter. Earlier most of the Scholars believed of Paul\'s authorship. But Scholars also suggested Barnabas, Luke, Apollos and even Clement of Rome as authors. Origen commented\' only God knows the truth of the matter\'. \r\nDue to this fact , Hebrews was avoided from Marcionite canon and Muratorian canon. But by the end of patristic period the majority of the interpreters in the eastern and western Church come to recognize Hebrews as part of Canon. They generally agreed Pauline authorship. \r\nTheme like Melchizedek, Tabernacle, Christ as High priest distinguishes Hebrews from other Pauline letters.  Tee strong reason for doubting Pauline authorship is the way of Hebrews distanced himself from the first generation of Jesus\' followers. Paul mentions in letters that he received the gospel from the Lord. Gal 1:11,12. But in Hebrews 2:3 contrasts this thought and says the salvation was announced by the Lord and confirmed by those who heard him. Also it is the opinion of the Scholars that it was a Hellenistic Jew who wrote it because of the quotes from Septuagint. The vocabulary and style shows the evidence of a mastery of the language. So Pauline authorship is questioned by most of the critical Scholars.', 8, '2020-01-08 09:43:43', '2020-01-10 13:37:57', NULL),
(1296, 363, 777, 'The Book of Hebrews does not identify the audience by name, city or region, but he wrote to an audience familiar to him. He shows his intent to visit them. 13:19-24. Following are the five important factors about the audience. \r\n1. Jewish: A good portion of the original audience were Jewish. Hebrews 1:1. Here the author referred, how God revealed himself to Israel in the Old Testament. \r\n2. Hellenistic: The content of Hebrews indicates that the audience was familiar with theological teachings that were more common among Jews living outside of Palestine. People like Clement referred the place as Rome on the basis of Hebrews 13:24. \r\n3. Immature: Hebrews 5 : 12 address them as spiritual babes; though they were supposed to be teachers. They need to be taught the elementary truths of the word. \r\nHow ever the book contains most advanced , sophisticated theological teachings in the entire NT. \r\n4. Persecuted: It become evident in the book that the original audience had already faced persecution in the past. Some were suffering in the present. 10:32-35. Also there is a warning of expected persecution in future. \r\n5. Near Apostacy: They were in danger of turning away from Christ. 10: 26-27. They were warned of Apostacy and judgement if they turn away from Christ.', 9, '2020-01-08 09:55:29', '2020-01-10 13:37:57', NULL),
(1297, 363, 778, 'The author of Hebrews had many literary devices to express his exhortations of which Rhetorical device appears common. \r\n1. Synkrisis: this is a rhetorical device used in Hebrews 7:111-28 where a detailed comparison between two or more things expressed. The author described an eight point comparison between Melchizedek and Christ: their parentage, genealogy, birth, death, office, actions, status and achievements. \r\n2. Exempla: These are list of examples or illustrations that follow onw after another to build arguments. This is seen in Hebrews 11. \r\n3. Qolwahomer: This is a Hebrew expression which is well known in Greeco - Roman and Rabbinical traditions. It can be translated \' light t heavy or \' less to great\' or \'simple to complex\'. An example of this thought is  seen in Hebrews 10:28-29. \r\nWith these literary styles the author was expressing the seriousness of the situation and convincing them to make right decisions.', 8, '2020-01-08 10:05:40', '2020-01-10 13:37:57', NULL),
(1298, 363, 779, 'The original audience of the book of Hebrews were believers of long enough for the author to say \' by this time you ought to be teachers\". But they needed to be taught elementary truths of God\'s word all over again. \r\nHowever the book contains some of the most advanced, sophisticated theological teachings in the entire NT. Balancing these two facts is a difficult task. The early Christians adopted a practice of reading and explanation of the scripture from synagogue practices of first centaury. Just as in the synagogue, elders used to teach the scripture to the congregations.\r\nSo the author of Hebrews wrote some of the most theologically sophisticated NT writings because he expected Church leaders to teach this to the congregation. \r\nAccording to Hebrews 5:11, it is vivid that the original audience were slow in understanding the truths of the scripture and obeying their leaders Hebrews 13:17 underlines this fact.', 9, '2020-01-08 10:13:41', '2020-01-10 13:37:57', NULL),
(1299, 363, 780, 'To challenge the false doctrine and to accomplish the goal of calling his audience to faithful service to Jesus, the author organized his exhortations to five major divisions. \r\n1. The supremacy of Christ over Angelic Revelations. 1: 1-2:18. Refuting all Jewish false teachings about the angels as superior beings, the author was establishing the superiority of Christ over them.\r\n2. Jesus is above Moses and his authority. 3:1- 4:13. The local Jewish community called for Christians to return to Moses\' teachings and his ways. Here the author affirmed his point that Jesus was even greater than Moses, the faithful servant, because he was God\'s royal son. \r\n3. Melchizedek\'s priesthood 4:14 -7:28. Here the author argued that Jesus was the supreme royal priest after the order of Melchizedek. Local Jewish community had the idea that Melchizedek will appear the great high priest in the last days. So the author presented Jesus as the true royal priest appeared in the last days. \r\n4. Supremacy of the New covenant in Jesus 8: 1- 11:40. The teachings of the local Jewish community raised doubt about the Christian claim of new covenant made by Jesus. The author affirmed that Jesus is the mediator of the New covenant promised by Jeremiah. \r\n5. Practical perseverance. 12:1- 13:25. In the light of so many challenges to their from the local Jewish community and else where, the author wrote to inspire and energize his audience. Exhorted them to remain faithful, reminding promises and blessings in Jesus.', 9, '2020-01-08 10:29:17', '2020-01-10 13:37:57', NULL),
(1300, 374, 589, NULL, 0, '2020-02-06 05:50:00', '2020-02-06 05:51:13', NULL),
(1301, 374, 590, NULL, 0, '2020-02-06 06:09:22', '2020-02-06 06:11:16', NULL),
(1304, 381, 589, 'Theological view of creation and man we can find in the book of genesis ,creation satori is extremely significant . by contrast ,the bible relate the true story of creation in order to explain how humanity was actually designed to function within the world .\r\n  moral requirements established by Gods work of creation ,The idea is gods work were perfect ,god created man in his own image ,and give athority over other creation , theologians are beliving man created as God\'s rpresentative in this word, so that man should aklways keep relation with God, man were created for a particular porpos, god created man little lower than angels, , \r\n man created with physicaly body and immatirial soul, we are autherised to fill the earth with God\'s image, with full athority over other creaturs, trhats what God created us in his own image, image of God in possision, image of God in atributs, image of God in natur, image of God in our relation ship, man is the shadow of the god, the defferent constituents of man are  body , spirit and ,soul, so that theologians belive that, spirit is always having arelation ship with God, soul is for our self awareness, body is to know the world.', 20, '2020-02-17 04:50:10', '2020-02-18 21:09:35', NULL),
(1302, 374, 591, NULL, 0, '2020-02-06 06:28:43', '2020-02-06 06:31:19', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(1305, 381, 590, 'The biblical view of the personality of man is so important, because bible believe that man is the most impotent creation of God, every individual are representative of God\'s image, so that every personalities are important, bible wants man should roukl over all other creatures as stand for God as a representative, so that god give all authority to men,  if you are the representative, you are having a peronality than any other creatures, \r\n while we are representative of God we must have a character , behavior like God, if we are not we reject his power and put shame on his image, god want men should to govern all other creatures and subdue the earth it self, we have power and responsibility, \r\n               according to my view, ever human being should understand the personality he or she having is so important ,being a image of God ,we know the importance of moral life, to chose right and wrong, to realize good and evil, also a rational, and a spiritul life we must keep, we ahe a responsibility to show the charecter of God to this world throgh our personality, everyone respect each other becouse of every individual are representive og god ,and image of god. so human personality is important, we have dignity, we must need justice, Love , these all are important in our personality.', 21, '2020-02-17 05:07:13', '2020-02-18 21:09:35', NULL),
(1303, 374, 592, 'The word Trichotomy means Human being consist of three part,Body soul and spirit, ( He.4:12,1 Cor .:15:14,1 Tess :5:23,Tricotomists argue that spirit and soul are not the same thing, ,Soul : Lower Immaterial function, Spirit : higher Immaterial function, after al scripture talk aboutour Immaterial souls in a wide veraty of source,we should acknowledge that many evangelical holds in other view in good conscience and we shoul emphasis both dichotomous and trichomist, agree that human being are partly phisical ,and partly Immaterial, sometimes we can focoued on spirituality that we failed to care for our physical needs ,we emphasize the importance of physical life in earth, but our body and sole being encouraged us to recognise the importance,and the interrelatedness of both,', 10, '2020-02-06 06:45:07', '2020-02-12 16:34:09', NULL),
(1306, 381, 591, 'God created man perfect , and god given all authority to man rule over everything, and also god created everything for men, but bible and theologian  believe than men disobeyed God and they did mistake against  God and his plan, man did Sin against God, by man mistake sin was wreaked havoc on our world, thier man Fall, sin brought curs over whole human race ,\r\n    the main reason of the fall of man was the rebellion against god,  to continue in God\'s covenant, wich God made in creation to be fritfull in the world, they needed to rtemain loyal to god\'s covenent,god\'s covenant obligation was fill the earth with huamn beings,cultivate the world and make it with god\'s present,work and take care of garden of eden,but EVE firstly fall in serpent temptation, and she broght his bhuband Adam to the sin,the curs of Adem and eve applaid to all othed thier desendance exept christ jesus, \r\n          God\'s plane were to save them, god was not ready to rejuct them for ever, God made again aplan to save them through his son, Christ Jesus, so he came to this world as ahuman being, he incarnated to the world without sin, he lived and died, with our curs and for our sin, thogh his death, all human curs taken away,', 20, '2020-02-17 05:26:40', '2020-02-18 21:09:35', NULL),
(1307, 381, 592, 'As christian we know that human being aren\'t merely physical creatures ,after all,scriptur talk about our immaterial souls in wide variety of ways ,the most common view among evangelical theologians and scholars is \"dichotomy\"or biparative view, this views says human being are composed with two fundamental parts ,body and soul, human beings are m,ade off physical substance, besides using the term body bible aslo speak our physical qalitys as Flesh, Colo.1:24,fesh and blood,1cor 15:15, Hebrew 2:14 fresh and born, obivasly body consist many different parts, as with body scripture use a variety of terms reffer the immatirel aspects of our human nature ,one of the most commen term is \"soul\'Gen 2:27 Adem become living soul, \r\n      Even so not all evangelical theologiance belive in triparism, some belive in Trchtonomy ,says human being consist of three parts ,Body soul and spirit, Heb 2:14, 1 cont 15:44,1 Tess 5:23,They belive humans are composed with three parts.', 18, '2020-02-17 05:46:28', '2020-02-18 21:09:35', NULL),
(1308, 388, 781, 'The book of Hebrews quotes, refer to or alludes to the OT nearly 100 times. More than quoting simple historical facts, the author focused on theological beliefs affirmed by the scriptures. \r\n1. Affirmed the declaration that every King in David\'s dynasty called God\'s son. Hebrews 1:5.\r\n2. Angels are described as Serving spirits. Hebrews 1:7\r\n3. God ordained human beings to be lower than angels only until the end when mankind will rule with Christ. Hebrews 2:6-8. \r\n4. Quoting from Isaiah 8:6, the author describes that the blessings of God\'s vindication will be shared among the members of Abraham\'s family. Hebrews 2:13.\r\n5. Promises of Abraham was permanent and extending to the NT times. Hebrews 6:13-14.\r\n6. Deuteronomy 4:24 is quoted in Hebrews 12;29 to describe God as the consuming fire to strengthen his teaching that God is still a consuming fire in Christ. \r\nBy all these references and quotations from OT, the author of Hebrews insisted that certain theological outlooks established in the OT continued to be true in the NT times.', 8, '2020-02-21 09:41:45', '2020-02-21 23:53:37', NULL),
(1309, 388, 782, 'A number of Jewish writings from Qumran as well as books like Ephesians and Colossians, indicate hat Jewish communities in the first centaury often exalted angels as powerful glorious creatures who brought divine revelations to inferior human beings. \r\nThe author of Hebrews responded to this challenge of exaggerated honor for angels. \r\nThe author stated in Hebrews 1;1-14 that his audience should follow Jesus because Jesus is God\'s supreme source of Divine revelation. Hebrews 1:5-14, the author explained that Jesus is greater than angels because he alone is God\'s messianic son. Angels are just spirits sent to serve the saints. \r\nIn Hebrews 2:1-4, the audience are exhorted to pay careful attention to the great message of salvation announced by Jesus because more judgement will come if it is rejected. \r\nHebrews 2:5-9 explains the supremacy of Christ as who now rules over angels.  in Hebrews 2:10-18, the author demonstrated that Jesus is Abraham\'s descendent, and will share his glory with Abraham\'s children, not with angels.\r\nBy all these explanations the author substantially proven the superiority of Jesus over angels.', 9, '2020-02-21 09:52:05', '2020-02-21 23:53:37', NULL),
(1310, 388, 783, 'The term New covenant is derived from Jeremiah 31:31 where the prophet predicted that God would grant a covenant of final renewal to Israel and Judah. In eight ways this covenant is described in Hebrews. \r\n1. Hebrews 8:1-13 introduces the idea that Jesus mediates the New covenant as the Royal high priest in heaven. The Levitical priesthood failed to accomplish the purpose but the New covenant is based on a better promise through Christ. 8:6\r\n2. Jesus\' heavenly priesthood is superior to the Levitical priesthood. Sacrifices of the earthly tabernacle could not fully resolve the problem of sin but had to be repeated. But Jesu did away with sin once for all because his blood is sprinkled in man made sanctuary. \r\n3. Hebrews 10:1-18 compare and contrast the Old and New covenants and declare that the animal sacrifices needed because Jesus brought final forgiveness of sin through his sacrifice. \r\n4. Jesus has opened the way in to the most holy place. Hebrews 10:19-23\r\n5. There is severity of punishment upon them that deny the salvation through Christ. Hebrews 10:24-31.\r\n6. The audience are exhorted not to throw away their confidence through Christ. !0;32-35.\r\n7. Hebrews 10:36-39 warns that God does not take pleasure in those who withdraw from living by faith. \r\n8. Hebrews 11 gives a long list of saints and explained, they would share with the audience in being made perfect when Christ returns. \r\nBy all these references and explanations the author established the superiority of the New Covenant in Christ.', 9, '2020-02-21 10:08:32', '2020-02-21 23:53:37', NULL),
(1311, 388, 784, 'The Old Testament is important and useful in ministry because of the following reasons. \r\n1. In order to understand the  historical and theological back ground of the NT , the OT is important. For example in order to interpret the person of Melchizedek, it is impossible without the help of the OT.\r\n2. Theological outlooks of the Old Testament and the New Testament are closely bound. Believes about God and other matters in close relation with God affirmed by the Hebrew scriptures matters to the New testament theology. \r\n3. God had placed certain moral requirements on his people in OT times. These obligations are to remain standards for God\'s people In NT times too. \r\n4. Too many Eschatological predictions are mentioned in the Old Testament. For the interpretation and understanding of all prophetic scriptures, the OT is essential.\r\n5. Dynastic ideals from the old Testament are very much important to prove the messianic lineage of Jesus Christ as the descendent of Davidic throne. \r\nThe proper understanding and exposition of the NT is possible only when it is explored in the light of the Old Testament.', 8, '2020-02-21 10:19:49', '2020-02-21 23:53:37', NULL),
(1312, 388, 785, 'Jesus mediates the New Covenant as the Royal high priest in heaven. Hebrews 8:1-13. The sacrifices of the earthly Tabernacle could not fully resolve the problem of sin but had to be repeated every year. Hebrews 9:1-28 explains the insufficiency of the Old testament sacrifices for the forgiveness of sin. But 9:11 declares that those who have faith in Christ been freed from sin through his perfect priestly atonement and now we have open access to the throne of grace in heaven. \r\nJesus is the great high priest who, once he has sacrificed himself for sin and sit down at God\'s right hand, where as the priests of the old remained standing on their feet because there was work to be done yet. Jesus\' work is complete , the sin is fully  dealt with, it\'s done. Hebrews 9:26 says Jesus did away with sin once for all because his blood was not sprinkled on man made sanctuary; but heavenly. \r\nWhen the Old Testament priesthood and sacrifices were insufficient to deal with the problem of sin. Jesus through his eternal and heavenly priesthood and once for all atonement through his blood, brought eternal solution to the problem of sin. \r\nThe book of Hebrews declares that Christ in his sufficiency caused restitution for the sin of mankind and effected redemption and salvation for all who believe in his great atoning work on the cross.', 8, '2020-02-21 10:33:33', '2020-02-21 23:53:37', NULL),
(1313, 389, 654, 'इन चारो सुसमाचारों को सदैव परमेसवार की विश्वासयोग्य कलीसियाओं दुवारा विशुद और अधिकारपुरक रूप से सुवकर किया गया/ इन चारो सुसमाचारों में ारभिका कलीसिया के सम्पूर्ण विस्वाश के कारण', 2, '2020-02-25 13:49:28', '2020-02-26 03:55:35', NULL),
(1314, 389, 757, 'सय्योजन सुसमाचारों का सय्योजन यह बतात है वे किस प्रकार से लिखे गए थे  समानता \r\nसमदर्शी सुसमाचार माथव मरकुस और लुका सब्द सबदरसी का ार्थं है एक साथ दिखाना सियोजन के सिद्धान निश्चित है', 2, '2020-02-25 13:56:18', '2020-02-26 03:55:35', NULL),
(1315, 389, 758, 'ऐसे कई स्थान है जहा सुसमाचार के वार्डन भिन्न भ्भिन बाटे कहते प्रतीत होते है १)घटनाक्रम वह करम है जिसमे घटनाओ  का वार्डन भ्भिन सुसमाचारों में किया गया है २)विलोप एक या अधिक सुसमाचारों मई विवरणों का लोप या अनु उपस्तिथि ३)भ्भिन घटनाये भ्भिन धटनाओ में पाए जाने वाली समानता ४)भ्भिन कथनो में कभी कभी बाथे पाए जाती है', 4, '2020-02-25 14:06:41', '2020-02-26 03:55:35', NULL),
(1316, 389, 759, 'र्प्र्तेक  सुसमाचा एक भ्भिन लेखक के दुवारा लिखा गया जीनोने अपने ही रूपों में भ्भिन लिखित को प्रगट  किया द्र्स्तिकोड विषय नए नियम की साडी सुसमाचार  कहानिया एक ही येशु का वार्डन करती है भ्भिन रूपों मई येशु के बारे में बात करती है ऐशु की सेवकाई के भ्भिन पहलुओं को विसस्थाओ से दिखती है |', 1, '2020-02-25 14:14:12', '2020-02-26 03:55:35', NULL),
(1317, 389, 760, 'बाइबिल सम्पूर्ण इतिहास के परमेश्वर के लोगो से सबधित सब प्रकार अच्चे और भूरे समाचारो का बेवरा देथी है सुसमाचार हमारे प्रभु और उधारकर्ता के वक्तित्व और कार्य के जीवान परिवर्तित कर र देनेवाले विवरण है |', 1, '2020-02-25 14:25:03', '2020-02-26 03:55:35', NULL),
(1318, 391, 675, 'Hermeneutics refers to the interpretation or explaination of some kind of message or communication and similarly  Biblical Hermeneutics refers to the study of interpreting the meaning and significance of scripture. Friedrich Schleiremacher is the father of modern Hermeneutics. The word hermeneu comes from the verb hermeneu meaning interpret or explain. Biblical hermeneutics is unique in a particular sense that when we try to relate all the data of scripture together they dont contradict one another,  while they speak of the variety of GOd\'s revelation ,they also speak in agreement with itself. it has three main processes :investigation,preparation and application', 8, '2020-03-11 00:41:44', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1319, 391, 676, 'Scientific hermeneutics refers to how biblical scholars have approached the interpretation of scripture like a scientific exercise. The bilblical roots of scientific hermeneutics make it a legitimate exercise. Biblical authors often interpreted other Scriptures with an orientation toward factual and logical analysis . some of the examples who did are ORigen of Alexandria, Augustine of Hippo, Thomas Aquinas. Renaissance , the Protestant Reformation ,The enlightment in WEstern Europe all took Scientific hermeneutics further.', 7, '2020-03-11 00:49:16', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1320, 391, 677, 'Devotional hermeneutics refers to the Christian tradition of emphasizing our need to draw near to God as we interpret the scriptures. The Biblical authors approached the Sciptutes devotionally.  we can see it in the way Origen encouraged Gregory  to devote himslef in divine reading or Lectino Divina in the letter of Origen to Gregory.  Lectino Divina involves Lectio :reading of Scripture\r\nMeditato: silent pondering of the content of what is read\r\noratio :earnest prayer to God to grant enlightment\r\ncontemplatio:waiting for the Holy Spirit to grant intuitive', 7, '2020-03-11 00:58:25', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1321, 391, 678, 'The two important aspects of Organic Inspiration that help us orient ourselves to the task of interpretation are  : first ,the fact that Holy Spirit was the divine source . It means that Holy SPirit has intimate knowledge of tge Bible\'s meaning and of the way it communicates that meaning. one of the main implication is the Bible\'s unquestionable veracity. Second aspect is the fact that Holy SPirit worked through human means to produce scripture. The Scriptures were written by different people  and they reflect the diversity of that human authorship.', 8, '2020-03-11 01:08:20', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1322, 391, 679, 'In the context of Biblical hermeneutics ,illumination can be defined as the Holy Spirit\'s work of proper understanding of Scripture to a human being. Holy Spirit through illumination grants us knowledge of His word. It impacts our imagination, intuition, motivation, desire, moral conscience and any part of us that contributes to our understanding of  Scripture. Illumination is where the Holy Spirit stands with us. He indwells Christian believers and opens our minds to understand and to receive what he has inspire in the Bible.', 7, '2020-03-11 01:16:52', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1323, 391, 680, 'Exegesis means drawing meaning out of biblical texts by looking at things like the historical context , the use of vocabulary and grammar , theological setting etc. It helps us to prepare us for the task of interpretation. Every time we re involved in it prepares us for further interpretation. the knowledge , skills , and attitude we develop from one encounter with the Bible influence us the next time we go to scripture.  When we study Biblical vocabulary and grammar , it increases our ability handle these scriptures more responsibly. Every effort we put into the exegesis of Scripture , it helps us prepare us for further study. Being an exegate of Scripture myself has definitely helped me to understand the Scriptures better, and being in ministry it has made me more confident as we share the Word Of God with more Knowledge of every details.', 8, '2020-03-11 01:30:03', '2020-03-11 21:38:33', NULL),
(1324, 395, 681, 'There is a sound theological basis for emphasizing three aspects of original meaning in our investigation of Scripture. They are 1. writer 2. audience . document. \r\nWRITER- The HOly Spirit chose to communicate through the personalities , experiences,emotions and patterns of thought of human biblical writers.  \r\n AUDIENCE- God always accommodated his revelation to his original audiences so that they could understand .Divine accommodation is the idea that God designed his revelation to be understood by its first audience.\r\nDOCUMENT-The theological basis for emphasizing the biblical documents can be found in two important doctrines : the doctrine of organic inspiration and doctrine of divine accommodation. The doctrine of divine inspiration teaches that Holy Spirit inspired human authors to write scripture. The doctrine of divine accommodation implies that everything in Scripture  like its words ,grammar and literary style rose out of the cultural and linguistic conventions of its day', 8, '2020-03-26 00:48:35', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1325, 395, 682, 'ORGANIC INSPIRATION teaches that Holy Spirit inspired human authors to write Scripture. But it doesn\'t not say that every copy made of that document will be perfect or that every translation made from that document will be perfect.Because the doctrine of inspiration extends only to the original texts of Scripture , only those documents have the full authority of God himself. The Changes that have taken place in these texts as they have been copied over the centuries are not our inspired by God and neither are the translations too. so in order to increase our confidence that we have rightly understood the original meaning of the scripture , we should study the writings that God actually inspired.\r\nDIVINE ACCOMMODATION  : the  doctrine of accommodation implies that everything in scripture like its words , grammar and literary style rose out of the cultural and linguistic conventions of its day. So if we pay careful attention to the ways o Scripture reflects , we will more correctly understand it.', 9, '2020-03-26 01:00:01', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1326, 395, 683, 'Having an access to many manuscripts that contain the the original words of Scripture  by the scholars helps us to understand the original meaning of the inspiration. Modern translations are not infallible , but they do  represent one of the most important teaching ministries of the church. In order to grasp the original meaning of Biblical passages access to original manuscripts is very important.  Modern Christians have the advantage of many decades of scholarly research devoted to studying and comparing ancient copies of scripture. These explorations are have confirmed over and over that the Hebrew and Greek texts if the Bible are far more reliable than the texts we have of most other ancient writings.', 8, '2020-03-26 01:17:45', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1327, 395, 684, 'There afre three major appraches toward tge objects and subjects of knowledge . OBJECTIVISM , SUBJECTIVISM  AND DIALOGISM\r\nOBJECTIVE APPROACH says that under the right circumstances its possible to arrive at impartial or objective knowledge. They believe that this way they can interpret scripture impartially  and they can know its meaning with relative certainity.\r\nSUBJECTIVE APPROACH SAYS that our knowledge is always influenced by our personal biases, making impartial objectivity impossible. They recognize that human beings and the world , especially matters if faith are often too complex to be discerned by the scientific rationalism.\r\nDIALOGICAL APPRAOCH empasizes the constant dailog or interplay between the objective reality and our subjective perspectives. The word dialogical refers to the idea that interpratation involves atype of dilaog or discussion between the reader and the text.', 9, '2020-03-26 01:38:59', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1328, 395, 685, 'Philosophers like David Hume argued that reason and scientific study cant lead us to objective knowledge about the world; Hume believed that our emotions and, desires and mental categories always influence our thinking , making impartial objectivity impossible. The German Philosopher KAnt argued that we only perceive the world as it appears to us ad then process our perceptions through the rational categories pr concepts that already exists in our minds. \r\nsubjectivism has widespread especially in the interpretation of the art and literature. theses interpreters argue that music, paintings , books are viewed by different cultures , different ethnics groups , different economic classes , different genders in different way.', 8, '2020-03-26 01:51:06', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1329, 395, 686, 'Authority -dailog Objective acknowledges that objective truth can be found in the text of the scripture . the Bible is GOd,s word and revelation to us , and everything its says is objectively true and meaningful. And the methods of interpretation can help us to understand this revelation as long as the methods comply with biblical standards. \r\nAuthority dialog and subjective: it acknowledges that we all come to the Scriptures with perspectives and beliefs that influence the way we interpret biblical passages\r\n. It agrees that the personal and subjective input  we bring is valuable.\r\nit indicates that investigating the Bible is a life long process ij which Scripture changes us and causes us to grow and mature in our Christian Faith', 8, '2020-03-26 02:02:12', '2020-03-27 03:55:25', NULL),
(1330, 401, 655, 'Biblical theology is more concerned with theological historical analysis. Biblical theologians are more interested in theological significance of the act of god reported in scriptures. To understand what we mean, we should turn to a basic definition of theology found in the works of Thomas Aquinas that indicates what most Christians mean when they speak of theological reflection. \r\nA theological matter is anything that refers directly to God. Second, a theological matter is anything that describes other subjects in relation to God. This twofold definition gives us insight into the ways biblical theology involves theological reflection. On the one side, biblical theologians explore what the bible says about Act of God to see what they teach us about God Himself.', 9, '2020-03-30 16:07:29', '2020-03-31 00:24:17', NULL),
(1331, 401, 656, 'Modern Historic ism inspired many critical theologians to approach the scripture with new questions and priorities. We can grasp the heart of the matter by briefly touching on two historical stages of development. First we will look at the early stages in the 18th century and the second we will describe some of the later developments in more recent history. \r\nBiblical theology must be defined as the confessional recital of the act of God in a particular history together with the emphasis drawn therefrom.\r\nBiblical theology focuses on the act of God instead of actual events that happened. \r\nConfessional history that appears in the bible by using two different terms; redemptive history . Salvation history is the expression of Religious sentiments in the form of history telling. Redemptive history is the confessional recital events that we fin in the bible.', 8, '2020-03-30 16:20:40', '2020-03-31 00:24:17', NULL),
(1332, 400, 675, 'Hermeneutics is a common word in Theological and biblical studies, but we don\'t use it often in our daily lives.\r\nHermeneutics from the family of Greek terms that includes the name \"hermes\" the mythological messager of the gods . The world from family of Greek word related verb \"hermeneuo\" which mean interpret or explain so broadly explanation some kind of communication \r\n   \r\n  The modern hermeneutics Friedrich S say about\"general hermeneutics a unified theory for understanding all literature \r\nHe acknowledged what we should approach different subject with their own special hermeneutics, \r\nBut he say \" all hermeneutics should share a common method of interpretation\"\r\n\r\n   Hermeneutics is also useful in psychology, sociology, and even fields like physics and biology. \r\nBy the end of the 20 th century leading scholar say need of general hermeneutics because the process of interpretation had become in important fact', 8, '2020-03-30 16:25:17', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1333, 401, 657, 'Biblical theology is in large measure a response to cultural shifts that can be traced back to the enlightenment of the 17th century. This is not to say that the concern of biblical theology were entirely new or belong only to the modern period, Christian have always explored the act of God reported in scripture but in the modern period significant cultural shifts took place that led theologians to emphasize these historical interests as never before. Many forms of historic-ism rose to prominence like archaeological discoveries, geology, biology in terms of Darwinian theory. Similarly also took place in theology. Therefore with the emphasis of modern historic ism , Christian Theologians also responded to theology in these ways.', 8, '2020-03-30 16:31:14', '2020-03-31 00:24:17', NULL),
(1334, 400, 676, 'People living in biblical time were t modern scientific. But this doesn\'t mean that they were unintelligible or irrational. \r\nBiblical author themselves afon interpreted other Scripture with an orientation towards factual and logical analysis. For the sake of time \r\nRoman 4:3- in the vers Paul quoted from Genesis 15:6  where God created righteousness to Abraham when he believe Gods promise..\r\nPaul analysis 4,5 Words created or reckoned as the Greek tern logizomai may be translated from his knowledge of Greek he argued that wages are not created...as a gift, but as a as n obligation \r\nWho trust in God there Faith not in work..it credited as righteousness,so he concluded on the basis of this reasoning that Genesis 15:6 Abraham was granted righteousness as gift free from through faith \r\nThat we see Paul handle logical analysis', 6, '2020-03-30 16:43:36', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1335, 401, 658, 'Modern Historic ism inspired many critical theologians to approach the scripture with new questions and priorities. We can grasp the heart of the matter by briefly touching on two historical stages of development that the early stages in the 18th century and second of the later developments in recent history.\r\nJohann Gabbler spoke of dogmatic or systematic theology. The goal of systematic theology was not to examine or explain the Bible but to determine what Christians should believe in the modern world through science and religion. Instead of treating the Bible as true history they began to look at the scriptures as expressions of ancient religious sentiments presented as historical claims and they explored how these religious feelings and experiences might be useful to modern Christians.', 7, '2020-03-30 16:44:02', '2020-03-31 00:24:17', NULL),
(1336, 401, 659, 'The divine goal of all world history is the extension of God\'s perfect heavenly reign to every corner of the earth. When God\'s will is done perfectly on earth as it is in heaven, every creature will bow before God and honor Him as the divine king, supreme creator of all.  We speak of biblical history as the process by which GOD will be ultimately glorified because the bible describes the way God began to turn this chaotic world into his kingdom with his creation  but humanity rebelled against this divine commission. Despite this failure, Go sent his eternal Son into the world and through his death the failures were rectified. \r\nThe vast variety of all divine actions, great or small found in the bible find their culmination in the work of Christ who will bring ultimate glory of God through the establishment of His kingdom in the new heavens and new earth.', 9, '2020-03-30 16:58:20', '2020-03-31 00:24:17', NULL),
(1337, 400, 677, 'Even though biblical author\'s often examine the scripture in more or less scientific way it as important to see that they also approach the scripture devotional \r\n\r\n1) Hebrew 4:12 \r\nReferring Ps 95 vers call \" the word of of God\"\r\nHebrews 4:7 he quoted the same ps95   say \" spoke through David\" \r\nHebrews 3:7 he introduced Ps 95 with word he say \" as the holy Spirit\" \r\nNow see the Divine authorship of the Psalm . Hebrews writer Describe \r\nHe say word of God living and activ\r\n\r\n Devotional interpretation of the Bible was particularly important in the patristic period of church history. \r\nMany scholars \r\nEx. Origen , Alexandria mentioned this subject \r\nMost followers of Christ begin to read the scripture with a devotional spirit. But when they become more adept at scholarly biblical interpretation, they often lose sight of the important of devotional hermeneutics', 7, '2020-03-30 17:03:18', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1338, 400, 678, '1) Divine source \r\n    \r\n          As the one who inspired all Scripture, the holy Spirit has intimate knowledge of the Bible meaning and of the way it communication that means so preparing interpret \r\n\r\n  Holy Spirit is a ultimate author of Scripture. For instance, act 4:25 Peter and John led the church in an affirmative of Ps 2 say .....\r\nNot kill passage... more passage say \r\n One of the most important implications of the Divine origin of Scripture is the biblical unquestionable veracity from time to time some say we believe in the inspiration of Scripture but they don\'t affirm that the holy Spirit protected from error. But listen to what Jesus say about holy Spirit in John 14:16-17\r\n2( human means \r\n   Instead God had the scripture composed by means of human author. He revealed himself through the gift and ability of different human being. \r\n Gospel writers 4 people story also different...but word of God same \r\nHaving see how the ins', 5, '2020-03-30 17:23:21', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1339, 400, 679, 'Through his illumination, the holy Spirit grants us knowledge of his word . And this knowledge isn\'t purely cognitive. \r\n Cor 2:11 Paul say without the work of the holy Spirit, we have no hope of grasping the thought of God as we should. This is why the spirit personal illumination is so important to our interpretation of the scripture \r\n\r\nRev Thad James say \r\n \" Inspiration is what God did when he inspired the writer. So we are no longer being inspired. But we are illuminated which mean that God is through the holy Spirit shedding light.....\"\r\nIn fact the holy Spirit ordinary Illuminaes us by means of the efforts we put into preparation. Just as our body normally receive nourishing through the process of eating food. The spirit typical work through the process our reading of our experience', 8, '2020-03-30 17:38:21', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1340, 400, 680, 'Every involvement we have with the exegesis of Scripture prepare us for further interpretation of the Bible. The knowledge, skills and attitudes we develop from one encounter with the Bible influence us the next time we go to Scripture. When we work to understand the literally type of Scripture. Such as narrative laws , poetry, prophet, proverbs, and the like we\'re better equipped to understand them at a later time. And as we learn about the ancient history of the Bible, were prepared to come back to the scripture for further understanding. Every effort we put into the exegesis of Scripture help prepare us for further study', 7, '2020-03-30 17:49:59', '2020-03-31 00:23:21', NULL),
(1341, 398, 687, 'The famous theologian Thomas Aquinas insisted that the literal sense of Scripture was foundational to all its other senses. Unlike his other contemporaries , he insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal senses of a passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere in Scripture in the literal sense. Although Aquinas efforts to anchor spiritual meanings in the literal meaning of the Bible may seem like common sense to us ,his pint of view wasn\'t adopted by everyone. The church authorities asserted that they had special God -given insights into every spiritual meanings that had no connection to the literal meaning f the Bible. Not all scholars would agree that Aquinas always followed these principles as he interpreted the scriptures.', 8, '2020-03-31 00:41:58', '2020-04-01 17:24:28', NULL),
(1342, 398, 688, 'Biblical elaborations are places where one part of Scripture directly or indirect,y comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture. These elaborations always record with and confirm original meaning as all Scripture is inspired and infallible.\r\nThree types of elaborations used are :\r\n1: as repetition of a facet of the original meaning\r\n2: as a clarification of things that weren\'t entirely apparent  or well understood\r\n3:as an expansion of the meaning of  particular passage.\r\nThe first Biblical elaboration is primarily a repetition of the words as in You shall not murder (Exo20:13) and in Deu5:17 again You shall not murder. This repetition confirmed  the commandment and reminded God; people of the terms if his covenant.\r\nThe second type of elaboration was clarification as we see in numbers 35:20-25. Here we see a clarification that provides information that was important to understand the commandment against murder.\r\nThe third type of elaboration was expansion , in which Scripture provides addition all information about the passage or topic it references.', 9, '2020-03-31 00:57:15', '2020-04-01 17:24:28', NULL),
(1343, 398, 689, 'The more we study the Scriptures , the more we see that the Bible elaborates on Itself over and over. The prophets and Psalmists regularly refer back to Moses, law. Jesus continually referred back to the old testament. And New testament writers did it the same again and again. But Biblical elaborations confirm other parts of the bible by repeating them, clarifying them and expanding them under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit.  so, as we explore the meaning of Scriptures , we must acknowledge and submit ourselves to all places where the Scripture elaborate on themselves. And the legitimate applications of Scripture must be derived from and consistent with the Bible\'s original meaning and elaborations. As our applications are not inspired by God, we can make mistake s, so our applications must be subject to improvement and modification.', 8, '2020-03-31 01:11:56', '2020-04-01 17:24:28', NULL),
(1344, 398, 690, 'The writer, the document and the audience are three  interdependent guides to meaning. If we fail to account for their interdependence , its very easy to fall into error.\r\n 1: INTENTIONAL FALLACY-If we overemphasize the author, we often fall into intentional fallacy. An intentional fallacy relies too heavily on what we think we know about a writer and his intentions , and de-emphasizes the things we learn about the document and the audience.\r\n2: GRAPHIC FALLACY: when we overemphasize the document to the reclusion of contextual considerations of document and audience. the word graphe means writing.this is a fallacy because the same document can mean very different things on who wrote it or for whom  it was written\r\n3: AFFECTIVE FALLACY: A third type of interpretive mistake we can make is over emphasizing the audience. It focuses too heavily on the how Scripture effects  the audience. This happens when an interpreter speculates too much about the mindset of the audience and fails to pay attention to the writer and audience.', 9, '2020-03-31 01:27:02', '2020-04-01 17:24:28', NULL),
(1345, 398, 691, 'All interpreters come to  Biblical writings with different sets of concerns , assumptions, background, and questions. Each of us read the Scriptures differently because God has gifted each of us differently. We all  have strengths and weaknesses ,and we process information  in a variety of ways, based on our knowledge and experience, God has designed this church so that we each can benefit from the strengths of others. Peoples gifts and backgrounds leads them to summarize Biblical passages in distinctive ways. A historian may summarize differently from an  artist. A historian describe s about the order and an artist describes about the beauty. SO the personal strengths of interpreters lead each to bring out the important but different aspects of the passages original meaning.\r\nMaking multiple summaries helps us to focus on the individual\'s aspects of meanings and it also allows us to learn more and more of the passages s full meaning. There can be many legitimate ways to summarize the meaning of the passage in the Bible. and these summaries cans help us better understand the aspects of its meaning. And taken together, multiple legitimate summaries bring us closer to the passages\'s full meaning.', 9, '2020-03-31 01:44:00', '2020-04-01 17:24:28', NULL),
(1346, 407, 776, 'Identify the author of Hebrew is not as simple as many other new testament book,because  the author never named his name in the book,Early on the Apostle paul was the candidate named most frequently, scholars also suggest t Barnabas,Luke,Appallos, and even Clemen of Rom,\r\n        Throughout  the medieval period  most scholars continued to believe that Paul wrote the book of Hebrew,but during the reformation protestant reformers questioned many ecclesiastic traditions view of Pauline authorship, \r\n   Today the majority of scholars reject the Pauline authorship  ,there are three major reason to reject Pauline authorship . 1.this book is anonymous, Paul practice to wrote his name in every epistles he wrote, 2.the book of Hebrew emphasis the subject that docent revive much in any other Pauline epistles, Malkizedek, tabernacle .3. the main strogest reson to reject pauline autorship is , writer of Hebre distance him self frome the first genaration of christ followers, , the most we can say writer of hebrew was asecond genaration follower, Heb 2:3, he reffeers to those who heard frome christ, and handed on wat they hard heard from christ, so he seems to be putting him self in the second genaration, that is wat majority of bilive paul was not the author of hHebrew', 8, '2020-04-02 10:52:05', '2020-04-10 16:25:09', NULL),
(1347, 407, 777, 'The book of Hebrew doesn\'t clearly identify the original audience by name ,city or region, still in general terms we can say that aouther rote this book to aspecific audience with whome he was previously familiar,in 13:19-24 he telling that he wish to visit them again, and he speak abot Thimothy, as our brother, \r\n   Five important part about orginal audience \r\n            1.JEWISH : Scholars believe that a majour part of audience of hebrew was jews , becouse chapter 1:1 he tells,in the past God spoke to our forefathers,to the prophet and many time in various way,   here author reffer how God reviled him self to Israel, he telling our forefathers ,ancestor of the author and his audience , \r\n            2.Hellenistic: secondly we can say tha audience of major part is Hellenistic, familiar with Theological teaching ,that common among the Jews who lived out side the Palestine ,many interpretters telling that audience was helenestic, \r\n             3:immature :the orginal audience of hebre was immature,  in  5:12 he telling that , through by the time you ought to be teachers, they should have great doctrinal progress , but they needed to be taught, \r\n            4.persicuted, there are audience being persicuteed, becose some of them face persiction, auther apriciate ther treble, and ecoraginge ,the, they expeld from synagoug, and frome the temple, some sufferd more, \r\n            5:near apostesy: some', 8, '2020-04-02 11:12:13', '2020-04-10 16:25:09', NULL),
(1348, 407, 778, 'the book of Hebrew has often been characterized as highly rhetorical, hebre is probably a best example inthe new testament to use literary device or method to express and reviel his view, and he uses lot of different structural,\r\n One of litrary device he used in Hebrew is Synkrisis in Greek,  it is a detailed comparison between two or more things to designed to convince audiene to cofirm his view of his messege, here he compair Jesus with Melchizedek, he telling that Jesus is a royal priest like Melchizedek , throgh that he convay 8 main meseege to his audien, comarition of, Parentage, geniology, birth, death, office,  action, starts and archive, \r\nEXAMPLA: another literacy method he used in his book, list of illustration and example that follows one after the other  to build a particular argument in a point of view, he listed some faithful servants from old testament, Abel,Enoch,oah, Abraham, etc\r\nQOL WAHOMER:the tghird litrary device he used is qol wahomer, its technic to start from small end with big matter, light to heavy, less to great,  begins with simple, end with big messege, here heb:10:28-29, he started with  moses law, if anyone recject moses law, he will died, without mercy, how much more if you reject son of God,God will punishe them who reject his, son, these was the litrary method to use express his view.', 8, '2020-04-02 11:31:50', '2020-04-10 16:25:09', NULL),
(1349, 407, 779, 'According to Hebrew 5:12, every believer should be mature in spiritual life, especially , Hebrews audience were still like newborn babies , they were not rady to recive message about christ in deep, some of fals teaching still cooed device them and force to them to give up thier faith, same way every belivers must be progress ther spiritual life day today by row of god, we need to learn word of God some time theological too, that will help us to know Christ more deeply, biblical doctrines must be learn by every believer, that will help them to trust God, grow in faith, if i still stay on elementary teaching about christ i cant be move ahead , readfing word of God is the best way to stay in faith, inearly church, every congrigation, and every gathering tghey were read and explain word of God, so that they could stay in faith even in the persicuted time, , to be in faith i must learn and study word of god every day and grow in it.', 8, '2020-04-02 11:43:43', '2020-04-10 16:25:09', NULL),
(1350, 407, 780, 'Audience of Hebrew has suffers a lot that persicution was tempting them to astray, apstasy, but this struggles was not what we first imagine , these all about false doctrine, the same way we all facing may kinds of false teaching, Hebrew author manly telling about ceremonial food, basic teaching, Melchizedek,angels, the same thug is happening today also, we also facing such kind of teachings,  many kind of fals teaching about non canonical books, like hebrew author telling, the rule of community, demascus covenant, , and also some people are still forsing us to stay only in the vbasic teachings like, bBaptism, Repetace, in now days also we facing the same, and some problem they faced is a fales teaching intrude Melchizedek as as god, these also , now days also same kind of teaching coming to reject jesus sonship, and the royal son ship of God, we have to focus on Word of of God than any other fals teaching ,to reamin faithful,  we must study word of God well, and firm our faith in chriat, and pray to God to help us to stay in his love and to love him, so that we can be faithful to christ .', 8, '2020-04-02 12:01:36', '2020-04-10 16:25:09', NULL),
(1351, 410, 781, 'The author of Hebrew used old testament support to express his view more than 100 times, these interaction with old testament was so crucial for author ,it was easy to challenge local jews cummunity wiyh old testament, , \r\n  factual background,  he draw attention to factyul bakground of old testament, simply put some historical details from old testament,though that he presented christian faith , he explained the name of Malkhizedek , a king of shalom, and king of righteousness,and king of pease , then he compared with christ,  \r\n Theological outlooks,he taught aobut angels, form ot, angels were serving spirit, David\'s dynasty, Gods promis throgh Abraham\'s family, , by this he proved that Jesus is supirer to the old testament \r\n moral obligation, israel should not rebel, not to be discoraged, adhter to righteousness, confidence in God,\r\nEscatolaogical predictions ,he talked about angels worship, universal sovrginity of christ, royal prist hood of chris, he used old teastament to prove these all things.', 8, '2020-04-03 14:19:48', '2020-04-10 16:24:39', NULL),
(1352, 410, 782, 'Number of jewish writings from  qumran,the author responded to challenge angelic revaluation many of jewish writtings given more holy importence to angels, they were thinking angels are like God, for hebrew auther he need to make understand his audiens that christ is superior to angels,\r\n   the author acknowledge that God spke through angels and other means throughoutbut he old testament history, but he insisted that the divinity appointed royal high priest of the last days , jesus brought revaluation that far superior to any revelation given through angels, hebrew writer telling that jesus is above all angels beuscous he is alone is Gods messianic son, he told that all should give impotent to the message by Jesus about salvation, he remain thay violation message from angels will punish by God in the past, but christ is salvation, if anyone ignore him will be punished, Hebrew-9, jesus will rule over the all angels in heaven , also in future all belivers will righ over them with him, \r\n finaly he told that jesus is the descendant iof Abraham, gods othe is not to angels, is to human beings, author prove that Jesus is above angels through these prof', 8, '2020-04-03 14:38:44', '2020-04-10 16:24:39', NULL),
(1353, 410, 783, 'Focus on new covenant author of Hebrew explain the supremacy  of Christ as Gods ordains royal high priest by discussing how new covenant superior to the old covenant ,the terminology new covenant jer 31.31,prophet predict that god will give a covenant  of final renewal of  Israel and Jude, this eschatological covenant called as a covenant of peace, he tells that Jesus is mediating a new covenant in heaven as the royal high priest, by pointed that Christ the royal high priest in heaven serving in the true tabernacle  made by God,not by man, in other words he mean that the Leviticus priesthood only in the earth,they fulfilled their role in earth itself, but thier priesthood was based on law,  because of Israels sin, but the new covenant of Jeremiah never fail, its found on\' better promos\' , heb8.6,A \'better promise \' offer the complete transformation of Gods people and final forgiveness of thier sin, sacrifice of earthly tabernacle could not full filld, but by christ death forgivnes of sin was complete, faith in Christ have been freed from sin, through his priestly atonement, that is why New covenant is super to old,', 8, '2020-04-03 14:57:50', '2020-04-10 16:24:39', NULL),
(1354, 410, 784, 'Author of the Hebrew refers old testament nearly 100 times to put his vies a to his audience,manly he used it to defend the fals teaching which was too familiar in the Jewish community,  also he used it to proves that many old testament promises and words fills with Christ Jesus, and his death , aslo he wanted to prove that Jesus was the Gosds royal prist , the messiahwhom all Jewish community waiting for, in my life and ministry old testament is impotent for to defend the false teaching wich is coming ageist Christ son ship,and lord ship,  especially , cult people  like \'Jehovah witness\'also, frome [people who reject old testament, and trinity, old testament gives lots of promis to israel, the same time its encourage me too, cose these all promis will be for us to who obay God, and who follow christ, old testament is important in my life its because christ full fild the old testament promises, old testament is telling all about christ the redemer,', 8, '2020-04-03 15:09:53', '2020-04-10 16:24:39', NULL),
(1355, 410, 785, 'Christ has overcome sin for us, its a biblical truth, by teaching this truth  to the people i can communicate this truth to the people who are in my area, i need to makre the awareness to peiople that all are effected by sin in birth itself, to come out from that sin, everyone need a redeemer , christ is the real redeemer to save us from sin, become he send by god aganest whom all mankind did sin, to save every human being christ death was more than enough, to save ourselfs from sin, we no need to do any other thing than accepting Christ as our personal savior,  he will forgive our si, cose God love everyone through Christ, thuis message is enough to communicate to the people in my area, and in my ministry .', 7, '2020-04-03 15:17:06', '2020-04-10 16:24:39', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(1356, 404, 692, 'Traditional Christian theology teaches that God is immutable, meaning that He doesn\'t change. Since GOd is immutable ,and all audiences owe their loyalty and obligations to Him, there are certain strong connections between the impact the scripture was to have  on its original audience and in its modern audience.\r\nthere are three critical aspects to God\'s immutability.They are:\r\n1. ETERNAL COUNSEL-\r\nWE all agree that what God has done and will do is apart of a unified plan. GOd knows everything, He predestined everything,predestination was juts a part of his eternal counsel. And this counsel is immutable because God causes everything to conform to it.The immutability of God\' s plan assures us that his ways in ancient times accord with his ways today.\r\n2. CHARACTER:  God reveals different aspects of his character at some times more prominent than others , Sometimes he displays his mercy, sometimes his wrath and sometimes he reveals his omniscience and sometimes he hides it. God\'s immutable character helps us see that there will always be connections between the original meaning and modern application\r\n3 .covenant promises:\r\nWhen God promises , hid word is immutable. God will fulfill everything he has ever sworn in covenant. the original audiences were supposed to understand every passage of scripture in the light  that every revelation of God in scripture assumes that God will keep his covenant promises and similarly modern audiences should do the same', 9, '2020-04-04 00:54:09', '2020-04-10 16:24:11', NULL),
(1357, 404, 693, 'There are at least three ways in which modern people are very similar to the people who received the Scriptures.\r\n1. SINFUL IMAGES: first ,a ll human beings ,no matter when or where they love , are sinful images of God. When God created humanity, he created us in his image.This means that all human beings are rational. linguistic, moral and religious vie-regents of God. and at the same time , all human beings  have also fallen into sin.Humanity now no longer uses the above said values to glorify God, both believers and unbelievers fail in their loyalty to him in all ages.\r\n2. RELIGIOUS DIVISIONS: SECOND, WE suffer religious divisions, they are : unbelievers,  false believers, and believers.\r\nUnbelievers make themselves God\'s enemies by refusing to submit to him. False believers make superficial commitments to God , by their fake appearance , and have no true faith.\r\nbelievers are those who make sincere, faithful, commitments to God and thereby redeemed from sin and saved from eternal judgement.\r\n3. CLASSES: at all times , human beings can be classified into old, young, male and female, rich and poor,. We can also be classified according to the relationships. We might be parents, children, siblings. masters, servants , friends. These classes exist in every age, so these too form a meaningful connections between the original audience and modern audiences. Ancient and Modern rich people, a leaders of both these ages can draw similar applications about wealth and leadership respectively.', 9, '2020-04-04 01:11:39', '2020-04-10 16:24:11', NULL),
(1358, 404, 694, 'The Bible\'s perspective on world history can be summarized in three stages: creation ,when God first made the world, fall: when humanity committed sin,and was cursed by God, and redemption , the period following the Fall in which God redeems us from our sin . the progressive nature of God\'s rule over creation has resulted in periodic developments , the most obvious epochal development occured between the Old and New Testaments.\r\nTheologians identify epochs according to the various covenants associated with Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses and David \'s in the Old testament and with Jesus in the New testament.\r\nFor example the laws regarding the atoning sacrifices required different things at different points in redemptive history. In Moses time , they required Sacrifices at the tabernacle, In Solomon \'s time , it was required at the temple. but in the NEw Testament time,They Required Jesus death on the cross. finally in the later NEw testament they ceased to be offered altogether. SO we see that in many ways , the biblical history is like growing tree. Every tree grows from a seed and finally becomes  a trees that contains everything that is initially contained in that seed.', 8, '2020-04-04 01:27:13', '2020-04-10 16:24:11', NULL),
(1359, 404, 695, 'The old testament reports many theological changes that took place throughout the history and these changes occured every time God altered his expectations regarding concepts b behaviors and emotions of his people.  Like the growth of a tree from a small seed to  full maturity, there were different stages of maturity which correspond to epochal divisions of the Old testament.\r\n1. VARIETY OF WAYS . Theologians have different ways to divide the history recorded  as, for one thing time doesn\'t pass in a sharply segmented periods. the  transitions took place gradually.and the epochs overlapped. For another thing, they used criteria  to divide. When a tree goes through stages of growth, its different parts doesn\'t grow at the same time pr at the same pace. sometimes disease stunt its growth. the different parts of a plant grow at different paces , the bark of the tree grows slowly than the leaves.\r\nIn a similar way, some parts of theology advanced slowly, some rapidly and some at medium pace. But as it stand, theologians have divided Old Testament history in a variety of ways', 9, '2020-04-04 01:44:58', '2020-04-10 16:24:11', NULL),
(1360, 404, 696, 'New Testament believers should never apply the scriptures as if they live ij earlier periods of history. \r\nFor example, Moses required that Israelites sacrifice at the tabernacle, at Solomon, time the sacrifices were at the temple.  Now when we think about the modern implication of the Old testament theme of sacrifice in worship, we have got be aware that we live in the epoch of the new covenant. As the New testament explains repeatedly that, Christ\'s sacrifice once for all time, perfect sacrifices has superseded every earlier form of sacrifice.  So ,a s we live in a different epoch, and the New testament requires as to reorient the practice of sacrifice.\r\nAnother example can be seen in leadership in battle. In David\'s period , there was literal battle  under a king ,unlike the ages the judges where was no king. \r\nF we live din Moses age and preferred a tribal patriarch as God \'s people did i the age od Abraham.\r\nIn every age , we need to follow the military leadership that God has established for that age. When leaders like Moses and David led God\'s people in physical , flesh and blood battles, today we modern Christians have spiritual battle.\r\nWe are led by Jesus against all invisible forces of evil.', 8, '2020-04-04 02:01:11', '2020-04-10 16:24:11', NULL),
(1361, 405, 681, 'Bible is organically inspired by God \r\nThe holy Spirit chose to communicate his word through the personality, experience, emotions, and patterns of thought of human biblical writiner\r\n  \r\n   Matthew 22:41- in this passage, Jesus referred to David\'s authorship of Ps- 110\r\nAnd Jesus specifically tired his interpretation of Psalm to the fact that it\'s human author was David \r\n \r\n  Focus on the human writer help us understand many features of Scripture.as another example, consider the different ways that \r\n2 Samuel and 1 chronicles tell the story of kingship of the king David \r\n2 Samuel mentioned Sin with Bathsheba but 1 chronicles not mentioned this \r\n Samuel showing through his book full life of David but chronicles writer just mention Israel history', 7, '2020-04-04 15:52:09', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1362, 405, 682, 'The theoretical basic for emphasis the biblical document in two main doctrine 1) organic inspiration \r\n2) divine accommodation \r\n\r\n1) organic inspiration \r\n      Mani teach - Holy spirit inspired human author to write Scripture.but it does not say that every copy made of that document will be perfect, or that every translation made from that document will be perfect. \r\n(Jeremiah 8:8 mentioned that )\r\n one of main thing remember that the original documents o\r\n\r\n2) Divine Accommodation \r\n  \r\n    The doctrine of accommodation implies that everything in Scripture  including things like it\'s words, grammar and literally style rose out of the culture and linguistic conversation of its day \r\nJohn 20:16 ex- teacher not in Greek this is Aramic', 7, '2020-04-04 16:08:06', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1363, 405, 683, 'We live in a day when the important of original means of any text, not just the Bible, hea been questioned in a variety of ways. As we already seen in the past, many interpretation spoke of many meaning for every biblical passage \r\n\r\n  Many aspects of biblical interpretation are like going...b\r\nThe ways the holy Spirit and his inspired author intended to impact the concept, behavior and emotional of their original audience . Doing our best to grasp the original means bears the authority of God himself for all of improve our understanding of the original means of every biblical passage, so that we can insure that every morden application we make accords with its authoritative original meaning', 7, '2020-04-04 16:20:31', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1364, 405, 684, '1) Objective \r\n\r\n2) Subjective \r\n\r\n3) Dialogical\r\n\r\n1) Objectives believe that under the right circumstances, it\'s possible to arrive at important or objective knowledge \r\nWhen objective say Genesis 1:1 means that\" God created everything\" they believe that they understand the vers without bias so they tend to think that anyone who reject their interpretation simply disagree with an obvious facts \r\n\r\n2) Subjective believe that our knowledge is always influence by our personal biases , making impartial objective impossible \r\nHuman being and world and especially matter \r\n   John 13:34-35 \r\n\r\n3) third is dialogism ..this approach emphasize the content\" dialog\" or interplay between objective reality and subjective peresctiv', 7, '2020-04-04 16:35:41', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1365, 405, 685, 'Modern subjective gaind promisnc partly in response to the objectiveisam of the seventeenth and eighteenth century... like philosopher David hume ...they believe our emotions, desire and mental category always influence our thinking making impartial objective impossible \r\n\r\nImmanuel Kant believe we only perceive the world as it appears to us and then process our perception through the rationale category or concept that already exists in our minds.\r\n\r\nIn our day subjectivism has become widespread, especially in the interpretation of art and literature, subject interpret argue that since we can\'t discover the objective understand of the world around us. Then the meaning of art and literature, including the Bible, must be located within is. So rather than speaking of objective means in art and literature culture, different athic group , different economic classes, different gender, and so on , and they participate interesting in how these', 7, '2020-04-04 16:52:22', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1366, 405, 686, '1) Objective model, an authority - dialog model acknowledge that objective truth can be found in the text of Scripture. 2 Timothy 2:15 \r\nPaul mentioned correct way to handle the word of truth. And significant, he compared this correct way to the labors \r\nWhile an authority - dialog model share thes wholesome outlooks with hermeneuts . It helps us avoid the danger \r\n\r\n2) just as an authority - dialog model remember objective model in some ways. It also has similar with subjective model. It acknowledge that we all come to the scripture with perspective and beliefs \r\nPsalms 119:97\r\nIn this vers indicated that his personal love with Gods law impacted study this scripture \r\nAs our authority - dialog model indicate investigation the Bible is a lifelong process in which Scripture change us and cause us to grow and mature in our Christian life', 7, '2020-04-04 17:06:14', '2020-04-10 16:23:44', NULL),
(1367, 403, 660, 'The word synchronic derives from two Greek words the preposition sun which means \'with\' or \'together with\' and the noun\' chronos\' which means \'time\'. When the word synchronic is applied to historical events, it describes occurances that took place \'together in time\' or \'at the same time.\' We will use the term synchronic to indicate how biblical theologians often explore sets of events in old testament history that happened at the same time. \r\njust as a movie director conveys his whole film by breaking it down into small series which tells a portion of old story although it being all one unit. Similarly synchronic studies takes an approach in synchronic synthesis biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular  periods of time in the old testament as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of its entire history.', 8, '2020-04-05 15:36:40', '2020-04-10 16:23:07', NULL),
(1368, 403, 661, 'Synthesis means combining different components of something into a whole. We describe the ways different components of theology revealed in a particular period of history fit together in a coherent logical structure. Critical theologians believed that logic was a primary feature of what they call the Greek mindset, but it was foreign to the Hebrew mindset. Based on a number of linguistic and cultural assessments, they argued that the Greek focused on abstraction and logical order, much like systematic theology. and by contrast they suggested that the Hebrew mindset looked at everything in terms of historical dynamics. from this point of view, the old testament did not focus on logical systems or on theological relationships between beliefs. and for the this reason, to synthesize old testament theology was to misread the Hebrew bible and to force it into a Greek framework.', 8, '2020-04-05 15:42:24', '2020-04-10 16:23:07', NULL),
(1369, 403, 662, 'Poets of the old testament were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history. Old testament poetry often conveyed information about the revelatory acts and words of God in the past, present and future. Old testament poets also focused on the world of their readers which we will call \'their world\'. They focused on their world by designing their texts to influence the lives of their original readers in particular ways. \r\nOld testament narratives were designed to have many different influences. They were doxological, leading readers to praise and worship God. They were theological, explaining truth about God. Some were political, focusing on current national events as well as polemical, opposing false teachings. They were moral, explaining how God\'s people should leave. They were motivational, encouraging every kind of faithful response. They were designed to teach early readers about their lives.', 7, '2020-04-05 15:49:45', '2020-04-10 16:23:07', NULL),
(1370, 403, 663, 'Modern evangelical often make the mistake of expecting Old testament narratives to be like modern journalistic historical writings. Old testament stories were only as comprehensive as suited the didactic purposes of their writers. They did not include every significant fact. Consider an example from the book of Chronicles when the writer of Chronicles composed his history of Solomon\'s life in 2 Chronicles 1-9, he followed the record of 1 kings 1-11 fairly closely. But he omitted every negative dimensions of Solomon\'s Reign. In  the second place, Old Testament authors were only as precise as their didactic purposes required. They were only as precise as they needed to be to reach their didactic goals like in example, Gen 1;7, where Moses wrote about earth\'s atmosphere.\r\nOld testament narratives are not objective by Modern Standards.', 8, '2020-04-05 15:58:13', '2020-04-10 16:23:07', NULL),
(1371, 403, 664, 'We must say that trustworthy historical accounts must be comprehensive, precise and objective. that is to say that it will include every significant fact about a situation to give a balanced account. They will report details with exacting precision, or will at least acknowledge that they haven\'t. and they will avoid all subjective evaluations that may prejudice readers. Yet, the authors of Old Testament narratives did not completely follow these modern ideals. Now they did not propagate religious fantasies. Nor did they present historical errors or fabrication as fact. But they did write in ways that where determined largely by their didactic purposes and not by our modern sensibilities.', 7, '2020-04-05 16:10:57', '2020-04-10 16:23:07', NULL),
(1372, 413, 665, 'English term \"diachronic\" derives from the two Greek words; first, the preposition \"dia\" which often means \'through\' or \'throughout\' and second the Greek noun \"chronos\" which means time. Diachrony is a concern with the passage of time. In the case of biblical theology the term \'diachronic\' points to the ways Old Testament theology transformed, changed, or developed through time. It will help to see how Diachronic approaches to the Old Testament relate to synchronic synthesis, how it stands in contrast with synchronic synthesis. A diachronic approach however, is much more concerned with changes that occurred in theological structures. It asks questions like, \'\'what changes in theology occurred as God acted and spoke in different ways during this period of time?\"', 8, '2020-04-07 15:37:08', '2020-04-10 16:22:39', NULL),
(1373, 413, 666, 'The term \"development\" instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two main ideas. First, changes in Old testametical structures always move toward fulfilling God\'s purposes for history. and Second, we speak of development because theological changes only occur through God\'s providential involvement in history. The scriptures affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology always accomplish unfailing purposes for his creation. \r\nDivine purposes from one end to another the scriptures affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology always accomplish God\'s unfailing purposes for his creation. \r\nDivine Providence; while are right to speak of changes in Old testament theology as developmental because they fulfill God\'s purposes, we must also keep in mind how complex this development was. God revealed himself through His actions and His words in many different ways and for this reason developments in old testament theology also took place in many different place. \r\nAt the same time, some theological developments that took place in old testament often appear to us as random or inexplicable.', 9, '2020-04-07 15:45:45', '2020-04-10 16:22:39', NULL),
(1374, 411, 687, 'Thomas Aquinas who lived 1225 to 1276\r\nHe insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpreted was rooted in the literal sense of a passage.he also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being being taught else in Scripture in the literal sense.not all scholar would agree that Aquinas always followed these principles as he interested the scripture.but never thes he insisted in principle that every sense of a passage of Scripture must be tied to its literal meaning \r\n\r\n\r\nRenaissance scholar begin to study classical literal  historical sense of these texts. And it wasn\'t long before this approach was also applied to the scripture.this strategy of interpretation equation \r\nThe literal sense with what we called the original meaning \r\n This original meaning things like more than many leading figure like a Ulrich Zwingli, Martin Luther, and John calvin', 8, '2020-04-07 15:54:26', '2020-04-10 16:22:06', NULL),
(1375, 413, 667, 'The six Old testament covenants fall into three main categories; First the universal covenants with Adam and Noah, second the national covenants with Abraham, Moses and David and third, the new covenant predicted by old testament prophets.\r\nUniversal Covenant is the covenant as \'\'universal\'\' because they were made between God and all of humanity. The covenant with Adam refers to the governance of divine- human relations established in the opening chapters of Genesis. \r\nNational covenant; God established national covenant with his special people Israel the covenants with Abraham, Moses and David. The covenant with Abraham emphasize promises for numerical promise of Israel and possession of promise Land, from which Israel was to spread God\'s blessings to the entire world. and for this reason Abraham\'s covenant maybe covenant of promise. Similarly, covenant with Moses was known as covenant of law and covenant with David was known as covenant of Kingship.\r\nNew Covenant: The new covenant was designed to govern God\'s people when God fulfilled promises to reestablish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom through them to the ends of the earth. For this reason we may speak of the new covenant as \'\'the covenant of fulfillment\'\'.', 8, '2020-04-07 15:55:30', '2020-04-10 16:22:39', NULL),
(1376, 413, 668, 'Biblical typology is the study of diachronic developments between the theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in scripture. Typology is the study of types. the word\"type\" derives from the Greek word \"tupos\". In three important passages New Testament writers spoke of particular theological structures in the Old testament as types of other theological structures in New testamental faith. \r\nIn Romans 5:14, Paul declared Adam was a pattern was the one to come. From the larger context we know that the one to come is Christ, so in this case Paul observed that Adam was a type of Christ. \r\nIn 1 Peter 3:20,21- the new testament counterpart to an Old Testament type is designated the \"anti type\'\' so in this example christian baptism is presented as a new testament counterpart to Noah\'s flood.\r\nIn Colossians 2:17-  the apostle Paul once spoke of Old Testament ceremonial law with a significant variation in terminology.\r\nJesus spoke a typological connection between |Moses\' bronze serpent and himself in John 3:14,15. Jesus compared the bronze serpent with his crucifixion without any special terminology. Serpent was the type of crucifixion and crucifixion was the Anti type of serpent.', 8, '2020-04-07 16:05:00', '2020-04-10 16:22:39', NULL),
(1377, 411, 688, '1)Exodus 20:13\r\nThe first biblical elaboration of this passage well mention is primarily a repetition of these exact words in Deuteronomy 5 where Moses reminder the nation of Israel of contact of the ten commandments in dut 5:17 \r\n Deuteronomy 5:17\r\n\r\n2) second type of elaboration we listed was clarification, and we find a clarification of the commandments against murder in number 35 in the chapter, Moses distinguished between murder and accidental manslaughter.Listan to what Moses wrote in number 35:20-', 7, '2020-04-07 16:09:59', '2020-04-10 16:22:06', NULL),
(1378, 413, 669, 'We should apply the Scriptures concerning the different aspects of Old testament worship in church services as the new covenant was designed to govern God\'s people when God fulfilled his promises to re-establish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom through them to ends of the earth. it characterizes every particular theological structure from Christ\'s first coming to his glorious return Old testament sacrifices were fulfilled by the ultimate sacrifice of Christ Jesus. In New Testament, the temple is the body of every delivered Christian living in this earth, as it is said in 1 Corinthians 6:19. Passover lamb in New testament is Christ who was slain, for the redemption of Mankind.', 8, '2020-04-07 16:20:30', '2020-04-10 16:22:39', NULL),
(1379, 411, 689, 'People gift and background lead them to summarize biblical passage in distanv way for instance an historical many summary the meaning of Genesis 1 \r\n\r\nWe will consider three main factor that illustrates the benefits of making multiple summers of Scripture means and first we look at the complete', 7, '2020-04-07 16:22:10', '2020-04-10 16:22:06', NULL),
(1380, 411, 690, '1) John 3:16 we looking can we suar that when John wrote this vers , he intended to draw his reader attention to the love of God show in the death of Christ.but we can\'t be sure of all the subtitles.psychol .\r\nMotivation John wrote this word , Scripture and reliable historical simply don\'t give us enough clues about John inner thigh to grow thes type of connection and even \r\n\r\n2) overemphasize the document this kind of error is often called a graphic fallacy.the terms\" graphic\" come from the Greek word graphe \r\nWhich mean writing correspondence, the graphics fallacy overwhelming the document \r\n3) overemphasize the audience this is often called an affectv fallacy because it focuses too heavily on how Scripture affec audience this fallacy is often committed when an interpreter speculat too much about the mindset of the original audience,', 7, '2020-04-07 16:38:40', '2020-04-10 16:22:06', NULL),
(1381, 411, 691, 'People gift and background lead them to summarize biblical passage in distinctive ways . For instance an historin may summarize the meaning of Genesis 1 different than an artist the histon might description the order in which God created light and darkness, water and dry land and plant and animal in the sky \r\nBut the person straight of the interpretation lead each to bring out important but different aspects of the passage original meaning \r\n\r\nBoth types of summary can also hindered by the interpretss weakness.. each of also include important thing Genesis 1 if we read the historical summery we see that God is an organized planner, but we may over look the delight God look in his creation when he declared in Genesis 1:31 that creation was very good however if we focus solely on the artist s summary we may see God as flawlessly creative but ignore his intentions and orderliness these potential weakness help us see that no summary should be ignored simply because it\'s not the same as ours.', 8, '2020-04-07 16:56:21', '2020-04-10 16:22:06', NULL),
(1382, 416, 771, 'Critical scholars challenge authorship of James , for that thay argue with three reasons, first, they say there is no mention of the authers relation to jesus, they say that this is unthinkable that brother of jesus wouldn\'t mention his family bond when he identifiy hism self to the church, \r\n second some critical scholars assume pseudonymitry becouse this book gives evedence that aouther was aware of Hellenistic,or Greek culture, but Jame was a Jew from palatine  ,in addition the greek we find in james is so sphsticated that other greek we find in other epistels,\r\nthird argument is Inconsistency with the theological portrait of James inthe book of Acts and Galatians,this idea sujjest some of the theological vies in the book of james  dont mach with the other theological docrince in other epistles  ,\r\nBut we do affrim tghe traditional out loks that jesus brother wrot this book, \r\nin james 1:1, inthe first place credintial beyond saying that , he was asarvent of god of lord jesus christ, he simply assume that his anme alon would be recognized and would carry sufficent athority, second , The epistel of Clement,Shepherd of Hermas , both reffers from james, Ogrin aslo told \"broth', 8, '2020-04-10 09:51:02', '2020-04-10 16:20:52', NULL),
(1383, 416, 772, 'discovering the original audience of epistles is important ,if we want interpret correctly we must understant who was the orginal audience , As we saw Erlier in James 1.1, james identify his reders as , The twelve tribes scatterd among the nations, this seems to be a refference to Jews who were living outside of Israel, \r\nBelievers in our glorious lord Jesus Christ james 2.1,these verse indicate that his audiance was jews who where living outside the palastine, its possible that thay were members of Jerusalem church scattered  through Judea,and samariah, following Stephen\'s martyrdom, james vocabulary suport the idea, that the original readers were jewish followers of jesus, \"synagoge\"typical way of using for jewish gathering, \"Kurious sanbouth\"from a commen old testament name of God of Israel,', 8, '2020-04-10 10:04:50', '2020-04-10 16:20:52', NULL),
(1384, 416, 773, 'To know the The location time and the purpose  of this letter is important, the  location of this author isn\'t difficult to disern, both the new testment and church fathers suggest that James livied his life of ministry in the Jerusalem untile he was martyrdom in AD 62,becouse of this ther is any other reson to think he wrot this letter form any other location, \r\n Date , when he wrote this letter is, the earliest  and latest likely date to this letter easy to find, the earliiest date for this letter is AD 44, we know that james wrot his epistel to the leaders of jerusalem, acts 12.17 indicate tht James become a significant leaderin Jerusalem church by the time of peter release in the prison, \r\nsecond latest possible date is AD  62, the year of james martyrdom,\r\nprupos e of this letter is james calld his audience to peruse wisdom from God, so that they would joy in the Trials , pure joy in trails, and have a good friend ship with word of God, becoume mutur and complet, ask for wisdom, humility befor god is path for wisdom, these factors have led scholers to conclude.', 9, '2020-04-10 10:23:01', '2020-04-10 16:20:52', NULL),
(1385, 416, 774, 'James wrote the letter mainly  suggested to focus on wisdom the wat to find pure joy in the time of trials, many interpreters have spoken of the book of James as the new testament book of wisdom,by the time James wrote  his letter ther had been a long history of wisdomtstemming from the old testament including Job ecclesiastic ,and proverbs, James also reflect the content of influential wisdom books outside of scripture, james tells us if anyone have lack wisdom, ask to God, and he will given to him, in addition to these tyope of wisdom his letter is maching to jesus teachings, james 1.12, comapre to mathews 5.10,\r\nbecose of james close tie to wisdom literature the stuctur of Epistle is quit different what we might expect,', 7, '2020-04-10 10:37:29', '2020-04-10 16:20:52', NULL),
(1386, 416, 775, 'According to james , he encoraged his audience to have \'pure joy \'in the midst of triels, james.1.2the audience were facing different trails that could discourage them and this reason in his first word he trying to encourage them, he telling tha \'pure joy\"is not as we think, he telling that those who sufferd a lot thety consider them as blessed, jesus also taught that be glad and enjoy in the persicution, he encouraging them with that words, this triels make you \'mature and complete\" so taht you will realized the real joy, when we revived the wisdom to understand how God will work in our trails  we can be joyful, wisdom sternthen our confidence so that we can see our salvation, its will bring\"pure joy in trials \"', 8, '2020-04-10 10:48:56', '2020-04-10 16:20:52', NULL),
(1387, 415, 670, 'God disclosed himself during the Old testament in two main ways: through act revelation and word revelations. This twofold concept of revelation has characterized biblical theology of the new testament as well. On the other hand, the new testament many revelatory acts of God such as Christ\'s earthly ministry and the ministry of the holy spirit in the first century church. I t also foretells acts of God that will take place in the future such as the glorious return of Christ. But on the other hand, the new testament also reports word revelations associated with the act of God. God the Father spoke; Christ also spoke, and at times, angels and humans by the Spirit of God revealed God\'s word as well. The new testament contains all three kinds of word revelation as well. The Gospels report how God spoke through the word of Jesus to reflect Jesus\' earlier actions.', 8, '2020-04-10 15:36:09', '2020-04-10 16:20:18', NULL),
(1388, 415, 671, 'The aspect of biblical theology by touching on three issues; first, the diachronic character theology, second the obstacles to diachronic study and third a new direction that new testament biblical theologians have emphasized in the place of diachronic analysis. At the close of New testament period, God\'s word through the prophets looked forward to the blessings of God, when Israel returned from exile. When Christ appeared these theological concerns with return from exile shifted towards understanding how God had begun to pour out these blessings in Christ. The old testament deals with 1600 years and new testament is of 100 years.The old testament had a variety of circumstances like patriarchs, judges, early monarchy, later kings and leaders, exile and finally restoration. New testament was fairly consistent as no extreme changes were there. Because diachronic changes in New testament history were not as dramatic as changes as in Old testament, the new testament theologians have shifted their attention in a new direction. Instead of focusing, on different historical periods they have tended to treat the entire period of New testament history as a whole.', 9, '2020-04-10 15:44:12', '2020-04-10 16:20:18', NULL),
(1389, 412, 692, 'The scripture are clear that is only one God to whom all audience of Scripture own their loyalty and obedience.\r\n  God is immutable meaning that he doesn\'t change.since God is immutable, and since loyalty and obedience \r\n\r\n  To say that God\'s immutable mean is unchanged... totally we say God unchanging \r\nThat is not to say that God don\'t relate to us in a dynamic, relational, personal way . So he listens to our prayers he griver over our sin he delighted in our faithfully and so it been said that God is essentially immutable but relationly mutable . there a degree to which he adapts what he doing \r\n\r\nThe biblical concept of divine immediately don\'t imply that God is inactive in biblical terms an inactive God is a worthless idol', 7, '2020-04-10 15:45:26', '2020-04-11 14:53:33', NULL),
(1390, 415, 672, 'When God disclosed himself through act and word revelations, he caused developments in theology. Eschatology, what Bible teaches about last things was not immune from such diachronic developments. Just as with other subjects, the Old testament\'s teachings about the last days also developed in significant ways over time. These diachronic developments  in the Old testament set the stage for what new testament biblical theologians have discovered about Eschatology in the new testament as well. \r\nEschatology has developed alongside the major covenant administrations of the old testament. Beginning with the covenant with Adam it is chronologically through Noah, Abraham, Moses and David. Each of these stages contributed essential elements to the Eschatology of the Old Testament.', 8, '2020-04-10 15:49:17', '2020-04-10 16:20:18', NULL),
(1391, 415, 673, 'In Matthew 13-25, Jesus explained that the kingdom of God would come in three phases. It would begin with the first coming in a small way, Jesus\' earthly ministry would inaugurate the age to come with some blessings and judgement of the last days. The second phase is the continuation. the age to come would continue alongside this age for a period of time as Christ reigned in heaven and his church grew. And then at the second coming of Christ the age to come would reach its consummation and this age of sin and death would come to an end. Jesus\' teaching about the last days represented one of the most decisive ways in which Christians had broken with first century Judaism. Jewish religious leaders and General Jewish population raged against Christianity precisely because of the Christian outlook on the Messianic last days.  Christians believed that the Messiah had already come, but in a way that was unexpected. The new testament authors deeply preoccupied with Jesus\' three phase eschatology and this preoccupation is reflected in everything they wrote.', 7, '2020-04-10 15:56:54', '2020-04-10 16:20:18', NULL),
(1392, 412, 693, '1) Epochal \r\nChristian have frequent summerize the Bible perspective on world history in three stages. \r\nFirst God made World.fall when humanity first committee sin and was cursed by God \r\nHumans fall.\r\nMany theologian have recognized that he progressive natur of God rules over creation has resulted in periodic development that creation \r\n\r\n2) Cultural \r\n In thinking about the culture development between the original and morden audience of Scripture, we need to recognize both similarities and differences.\r\n\" What culture pattern do we face that closely parallel Abraham experience\"and how is our culture like David s and with regards to different \r\nThe culture in which the Bible was written was clearly very different to our own . \r\n\r\n3) Personal \r\n   There are considerabl similar between the people of the Bible and people living in our contemporary world .but we need to recognize that there are many different between morden and ancient people.\" How do our personal lives compare with those that we see in the Bible\"\r\n\"What role do we have in society\"', 8, '2020-04-10 16:03:01', '2020-04-11 14:53:33', NULL),
(1393, 415, 674, 'The understanding of the Kingdom of God helps me to know that many conflicting motivations, thoughts and desires give shape to our life and ministry. We don\'t live by instincts but rather our lives are directed by the thoughts and motives of our hearts. With the better understanding, it gives new knowledge, vision for a dedicated ministry, and we still get to experience a measure of God\'s kingdom right now. The kingdom of God was preached by John the baptist, and followed by Jesus himself. He called out his disciples to spread the Gospel, and in this new generation the duty of the one chosen is to spread that same gospel to all people. And in this way it is a burden for my ministry also to preach about sin, redemption and the judgement, As it is God\'s will that all men be saved.', 8, '2020-04-10 16:11:28', '2020-04-10 16:20:18', NULL),
(1394, 412, 694, '1) Characters \r\n\r\n  In literally terms we can say that forces of good are led by the antagonistic or hero of the story namely God himself. And the forces of evil are led by the antagonistic \r\nGod has permanently Satan to stand against him while the Divine drama playing out \r\nGod is the supremcy creator king who rules from heavily thrown and fill his celestial palace with his brilliant glory.\r\n2) storyline \r\n\r\n Despite the countless difference between covenant epochs all these variables fit within a unified all encompassing narrative about the conflict between God and Satan \r\n3) Authors \r\nOt say \" never return to the past,but never forget the past\" \r\nWe don\'t live past', 7, '2020-04-10 16:17:53', '2020-04-11 14:53:33', NULL),
(1395, 412, 695, 'Old testament report many theologian change the look place throughout history. \r\nWe will  discuss the Epochal division of OT in three steps 1) we\'ll acknowledge the various of ways scholar have divided the history record in the Bible 2) we\'ll describe a helpful Epochal outline that many Christians traditional use . 3) we\'ll point out some implications this outline has for the modern application of Scripture.lets begin with the variety of ways old testament history has been divided', 7, '2020-04-10 16:25:56', '2020-04-11 14:53:33', NULL),
(1396, 412, 696, 'As God\'s people living in the morden world, our faith is deeply connected to what God revealed long ago. We devoted to the modern application of God testament book that deal with the past. And even when we\'re apply new Testament book we\'re still looking to the past.now some books like revelation focus on the future quite a bit . but even revelation is a record of vision that like author John apply to his original audience in the past.\r\nSo in order to apply those book in the morden world we have to concentrate on the past.', 7, '2020-04-10 16:37:14', '2020-04-11 14:53:33', NULL),
(1397, 420, 761, 'The period of creation begins with the creation week and ends with humanity\'s fall into sin and expulsion from the Garden of Eden teaches that the Son was at the father\'s side during creation, and that the father created the world by or through Him. These facts are taught in places like john 1:1-3 and Hebrews 1:12. God filled the empty world in 6 days and revealed some of the basic dimensions of his eternal purpose for the world. God ordered and filled the universe in ways that pleased Him.\r\n 1-3 days: Formed the world. Separated light from darkness, sky from sea, dry land from water and created vegetation. \r\n4-6 days: Created sun, moon, and stars, fishes and other creatures of the sea birds to inhabit the air and all the animals that live in the earth.\r\n He created the universe to be his special kingdom.', 8, '2020-04-17 16:36:00', '2020-04-21 04:57:02', NULL),
(1398, 420, 762, 'The theological term \'eternal counsel\' often called eternal decree, refers to God\'s plans for the universe which we have set before his work of creation. God\'s eternal counsel is mentioned in places like Acts 2:23, Romans 8:28-30, 1 peter 1:2.\r\nDifferent theological traditions have different beliefs about the nature and extent of God\'s plans. Some believe that God\'s eternal plan includes every detail of History. Others believe that God has fixed certain things and others. But we all agree that what Christ accomplished is central to God\'s plan- that God ordained salvation in Him and that Christ won\'t fail. Foreknowledge: God\'s knowledge prior to creation of events that would occur in the course of history. Regarding his foreknowledge, God said that from beginning that is before he created the world, he knew that was still to come. And regarding his purpose, he said,\'\'My purpose will stand, and i will do all that I please.\'\' \r\nPurpose; God created the universe in order to display and increase hi glory through Christ. \r\nCovenant to Redemption: God\'s eternal decree regarding his glorious Kingdom.', 8, '2020-04-17 16:43:28', '2020-04-21 04:57:02', NULL),
(1399, 420, 763, 'God\'s promises are immutable. They can never change, and he will never break them. Whatever God promises, he will certainly fulfill. The first was the covenant of redemption in which they promised to redeem fallen humanity. Second is \'\'covenant of grace\'\'. This solemn arrangement was made between the Father on the one hand and the Son and redeemed humanity on the other hand. And it governs the entire period of redemption, beginning immediately after humanity\'s fall into the sin and coming to its ultimate fulfillment when Jesus returns in glory.', 9, '2020-04-17 16:48:10', '2020-04-21 04:57:02', NULL),
(1400, 420, 764, 'Jesus redeeming work: his inauguration of the kingdom of God; his obedience to the Father; his resurrection; Inauguration of Kingdom; God\'s people longed for the day when God would bring his kingdom to earth. Jesus brought the kingdom of God to earth in his day. This was the day when humanity\'s original mandate would finally be fulfilled. God would completely restore his creation, and his will would be done on earth just as perfectly as it is already done in  heaven. \r\nObedience; Jesus lived a life of perfect obedience to the father, culminating his death on the cross. And by his obedience, he won the blessing that Adam had lost. \r\nResurrection; Jesus\' bodily Resurrection was critical to his work of redemption. By raising from the dead, Jesus conquered death itself and secured the everlasting physical life of everyone that has faith in Him.', 8, '2020-04-17 16:54:05', '2020-04-21 04:57:02', NULL),
(1401, 420, 765, 'General Resurrection: At Christ\'s return, everyone that has ever died, will be resurrected. Both the wicked and the righteous will be given new bodies that will endure forever. This is clearly taught in John 5;28,29, where Jesus spoke these words:\r\nA time is coming when all who are in their graves, will hear the Son\'s voice and come out- those who have done good will rise to live and those who have done evil will rise to condemned. Believer\'s bodies will be free from corruption and presence of sin. Bodies of unbelievers will continue to be plagued by the effects of sin.\r\nLast Judgement; Immediately after general resurrection, Jesus will exercise his authority and power as king by destroying all his enemies and blessing all his faithful people at the last judgement. Every human being will be included in the last judgement.\r\nRenewal of Creation: Just as Jesus will judge humanity and purge the unbelievers from it, he will also purge and renew the creation itself.', 8, '2020-04-17 17:00:46', '2020-04-21 04:57:02', NULL),
(1402, 423, 766, 'Peter\'s apostolic confession confirmed that Jesus was the Christ. His affirmation of Jesus\' anointing to the office of Christ was direct revelation from God. Peter\'s confession as the spokesman for the apostles was more than that. It was an authoritative prophetic revelation from God. As such, it was an infallible confirmation of that fact that Jesus really was the Christ.\r\nTransfiguration: Jesus took 3 disciples to a mountain to pray while Moses and Elijah appeared to Jesus. God\'s voice was heard from heaven affirming that Jesus was his son. And when Peter suggested that the disciples build shelters for them singled out Jesus as being worthy of the greatest honor and obedience.This was significant because Moses was the lawgiver and Elijah was the faithful prophet, it meant Jesus  stood in continuity with the law and prophets and that He was fulfilling the expectations set by the greatest leaders of Israel\'s past. it also meant that he was the final Davidic heir who was bringing God\'s kingdom to the earth.', 10, '2020-04-20 16:37:35', '2020-04-20 18:16:21', NULL),
(1403, 423, 767, 'Jesus fasted for 40 days in the desert before being tempted by Satan. Jesus overcame temptation to bring salvation to his people.\r\n1] Obedience: As we see in the temptation he suffered, Jesus obeyed God both by keeping the requirements of his law and by submitting the Father\'s plan for His life. And his obedience prepared him for his work as Christ. \r\n2] Sympathy: Jesus didn\'t give into temptation but still felt it acutely. He recognized that the things Satan offered him, where desirable and weakened condition from fasting must have amplified his longing for them. This experience caused him to grow in compassion and understanding for us as we suffer and struggle with temptations.\r\n3] Impeccability: means inability to sin. Jesus never sinned. He never succumbed to temptation, or has a evil thought or desire or said a sinful word. because Jesus is impeccable, his obedience and sympathy are increased.', 9, '2020-04-20 16:44:31', '2020-04-20 18:16:21', NULL),
(1404, 423, 768, 'Crucifixion was a form of death penalty used in the ancient roman empire. Jesus\' crucifixion was unique, of course, because it also served as an atoning offering for sin. \r\nWhen we speak of the imputation of our sin to Jesus on the cross, God counted Jesus as if he had personally committed every sin imputed to him. Once our sin was reckoned to his account, God viewed him as being guilty of all the sins that were assigned to him. Christ\'s role as our substitute is reflected in the fact that bible often refers to him as our sacrifice. It\'s also why he is called our ransom. \r\nHuman death is always a divine judgement against sin. Jesus\' death was also a divine judgement against sin. Before laid our guilt on Him, Jesus couldn\'t\' die. But once our sin was imputed to him on the cross, his death became not only possible but necessary.', 9, '2020-04-20 16:50:57', '2020-04-20 18:16:21', NULL),
(1405, 423, 769, 'Plan of redemption: God\'s plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depend on him, fulfilling his covenant promises to establish his kingdom on the earth under the kingship of a descendant of David, otherwise known as the Christ.\r\nJesus resurrection was a critical step that enabled God to fulfill his covenant promises.\r\nBlessings of Salvation: The new testament associated Jesus resurrection was a wide variety of blessings we receive as a part of salvation. It results in our justification, which is the forgiveness of our sins. Its the source of regeneration of our spirit, and it opens the door for our eternal inheritance. It produces good works and a true witness to Christ in pour bodies and lives. And it\'s the source of future bodily resurrection of believers, when will have glorified bodies just like the one Jesus has.', 9, '2020-04-20 16:56:50', '2020-04-20 18:16:21', NULL),
(1406, 423, 770, 'Jesus is indeed the Christ can be affirmed with the evidence of the Word of God. Like we can see in John 10;24-25, Jesus himself claims to be \" the Christ\". Our Lord affirms under direct questioning that He is the \'Christ\". There is absolutely no one in this world, dead or living that can be compared like Jesus. Whatever Jesus preached he practiced in his earthly ministry. No other death can be as unique as of our Lord\'s. There is no other person who has resurrected from the dead, and who still lives. The ultimate aim of the resurrection is to give eternal life to all flesh through Jesus Christ.  He has authority over sin,over sickness, over natural powers, over demonic powers and finally, over death also. Hence, Jesus is indeed \"The Christ\" in our past, present and the future.', 8, '2020-04-20 17:07:34', '2020-04-20 18:16:21', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(1407, 424, 619, 'When we compare book of acts with third gospel two types of evidence emerge that strongly suggest one person wrote both book of Acts and Luke,   \r\nEXPLICIT evidence-the first verse of book the witter telling about his former book, which is wrote to Theophilus,that means acts is his second books, that he wrote to the same person again,this is the solid evidence  both book of acts and gospel of Luke written by Luke to Theophilous .and also the connect between these two volume reflect an ancient litrary custom, \r\nIMPLICITE-multiple similarity between gospel of Luke and acts ,  the orderly account of bothe books are same ,compositional structure also the same , beyond this there is a slimier chronological length in each books, the theames of these books are same,in addition there are similarities between the books becouse they are each part of same story, so that we can strongly belive that bothe book of acts and gospel of luke are writen by one person, that isbyLuke .', 8, '2020-04-21 09:50:45', '2020-04-22 16:01:48', NULL),
(1408, 424, 620, 'Now we have looked ate the some of evidence for common authorship of acts, now we consider the evidence provided by the early church history, (1)Manuscript:one vary old manuscript reffers to papyrus, were discoverd in 1952, it was written on papyrus and some of our earliest new testament manuscript evidence, it was probably copied some times AD 175-200,the manuscript evidence indicate that from vary earlier the gospel of luke written by Luke as well as book of Acts too\r\nThe Muratorian fragment, AD 170-180,telling, in canonical after affriming luke authership of gospel of luke, it explicity points to him as the auther of bppk of Acts, as well,\r\nThe anti marcionite prologue :an introduction to the third gospel, written around 160,discribes luke as the auther of gospel and Acts, \r\nEarly church Leaders, Irenaeus belived that luke was the auther of third gospel and acts,\r\nClement of Alexandria, :also reffers luke as the auther of Acts, \r\nTertullian:specifically attribute the third gospel to luke as well as Acts, \r\nEusebius, mentioned luke as the auther, \r\nThere is not one indication in the literature of the early church', 8, '2020-04-21 10:10:48', '2020-04-22 16:01:48', NULL),
(1409, 424, 621, 'The old testament influenced Luke\'s writings atleast in two way, in the first place luke was deeply influenced by the old testament view of history in general, and in the second place he was deeply impacted by its treatment of the history of Israel in particular,\r\n first he reffers the glory and the beuty of creation, the wonder that primates the universe  God made everythings good,God prepaeerd world to to expension of his kingdom,placed humaity his royal image and commanded humanity to multiply and rule over evrting\r\nsecond he spoke the conflict between the creations orginal glory and the and its present misery correption, or fall of human kind,adam and eve rebelled to God and did sisn aganet him \r\n and third spoke of redemption, the hope that ther will be the solutoon for the conflict,  \r\nLukes record in acts depended on the history of israel,God called abraham to go for the promised land for main two purpos (1)Abraham would father a great nation,(2)throgh Abraham all the people on the earth will be blessed ,Luke report blessing of salvation come throgh the promis of /abraham, \r\njewish christian broght the gospel to the gentile to save them, \r\nExodus under moses, david\'s dynasty.', 9, '2020-04-21 10:30:20', '2020-04-22 16:01:48', NULL),
(1410, 424, 622, 'Jewish messianic hopes took many different directions, israels hope that God will sent missianic liberator to them and israel become one of the most dominant inthe history, Zelotus and some others believe God wanted Israel to mount instruction against roman authority, apocalyptic believes that God will supernaturally intended to destroy his enemies and established his people as victors, pharisees and Sadducee were believing that God would not invented untile Israelite obay the law, \r\n               some of the christian theology also cosly to this Jewish concept, the kindom of God come to the through the person and work of Jesus, the messiah  and expanse towards his great consummation as God grand salvationto those who recive and trustin jesus as messiah, the messianic kingdom will gadually grow by the expansion of the church, these are the similarities and differnts of jewish and christian .\r\n to evangelize morden jews its better to use there own theology that they are waiting for the messiah, so we can convionce the that Jesus is the messiah to whom for you are waiting, if we success  to convaey the message of christ kingdom, its eay to evangalize morden jews,', 8, '2020-04-21 10:48:27', '2020-04-22 16:01:48', NULL),
(1411, 424, 623, 'christian theology is always connect to the gospel of Christ, Gospel is the announcement that the kingdom of God comes to the earth through the  person and work of Jesus, the messiah .the rule of the God over the world moves forward as the gospel touches the heart of those who believe and bring them in to the salvation that Jesus accomplished, giods kingdom will expand when people come and belive in christ, so that the priority of every beliver is not only waiting for a messianic kingdom, it is impotent that we must hsare the gospel, jesus was preaching about kingdom of god , and those who belive in him will be saved, when he crusifide befor that he made his disiples to preach gospel, and he sent them to preach salvation and bring people to god taht kingdom of God may expan, \r\n  by the gospelization when people will recive christ as messiah kingdom of God will grow, and expands  . so we must change our thinking and give priorities to preach the gospel, and share the christ.', 8, '2020-04-21 11:03:00', '2020-04-22 16:01:48', NULL),
(1412, 432, 654, 'ऐसे अनेक प्रमाण है चlरो लेखक के पास यशु के बारे म विश्वासनिय विवरण लिखने के स्रोतों और प्ररेणा दोनों थे।', 2, '2020-04-21 11:13:01', '2020-04-22 16:01:17', NULL),
(1413, 432, 757, 'सुसमाचार का संयोजन यह बताता है वह किस प्रकार से लिखे गए थे । \r\n१ समानता २ प्रमाणिकता ३ एकता।', 2, '2020-04-21 11:44:13', '2020-04-22 16:01:17', NULL),
(1414, 432, 758, 'चारों सुसमाचार परमेश्वर के राज्य के आगमन के बारे में समान कहानी बताते हैं परंतु प्रत्येक इसे अपने ही रूप में बताता है। \r\nA प्रत्यक्ष कठिनाइयां\r\n१ घटनाक्रम २ विलोपन ३ भिन्न घटनाएं  ४ भिन्न कथन।\r\nB भिन्न प्रभाव \r\n*दृष्टिकोण *विषय।\r\n*सुसमाचार एक ही यशु का वर्णन करती है \r\n*भिन्न रूपों में यीशु के बारे में बात करती है। *यीशु की सेवकाई एक भिन्न पहलुओं को विशिष्टता से दिखाती है।\r\n१ मत्ती  \r\nयशु इसराइल का मसीह रूपी राजा।\r\n२ मरकुस \r\nEshuपरमेश्वर का दुख उठाने वाला पुत्र।', 3, '2020-04-21 12:04:19', '2020-04-22 16:01:17', NULL),
(1415, 432, 759, 'प्रत्येक सुसमाचार बल देते हैं कि यीशु राजा है जो परमेश्वर के राज्य को लेकर आता है। \r\n१ प्रमाण दुष्ट आत्माओं पर यीशु का अधिकार\r\n २ चंगा करने और जीवित करने की यीशु की सामर्थ \r\n३ पाप  क्षमा। करने का यीशु का अधिकार।', 2, '2020-04-21 12:11:45', '2020-04-22 16:01:17', NULL),
(1416, 432, 760, 'स्पष्टवादीता , पुष्टिकरण, और ऐतिहासिक स्रोतों के पुष्टिकरण से गलत शिक्षाओं को नकारने में हमारी सहायता करता है।', 3, '2020-04-21 12:24:12', '2020-04-22 16:01:17', NULL),
(1417, 427, 806, 'Archaeological research has demonstrated that Moses was not the first person to write about the world\'s origin\r\nGod inspired Moses,so he wrote this account..\r\nSome of the ancient texts are well known many people have heard of things like the Enuma Elisha, or the Babylonian creating\r\n\r\nInteraction -\r\nAs we will see throughout this series of lesson, Moses interested with other primeval tradition both negative and positive \r\nOne the one hand , Moses wrote his history of earlier time to counter falsehood with truth. We must always remember that the Israelites Moses led had been subjected to all kinds of pagan influence.\r\n At the Same time Moses accomplished this negative purpose of refuting false myths by interesting positive with the literally traditional of his time', 8, '2020-04-21 16:13:52', '2020-04-22 16:00:17', NULL),
(1418, 427, 807, 'Moses should begin by nothing that this passage has three major steps, namely, a beginning, a middle and an ending.\r\nWe may summarize 1:1-2 the content of these verses as the \"dark chaotic world\" ch1:3-31\r\nFrom a middle section of this material which contains the so called\" six days of creation\" or what we will call the six days of ordering\" creation. \r\nFinally 2:1-3 is the Sabbath day or as we will call it the ideal word', 6, '2020-04-21 16:23:00', '2020-04-22 16:00:17', NULL),
(1419, 427, 808, 'Looking at the first portion of Genesis 1, we se a very important dramitc mention between the chaos covering the earth and the spirit of God .\r\n\r\nThis verse introduce the dramatic tension that flows through this entire chapter, \r\n\" Formless and empty\" or as it said in Hebrew \' tohuwabohu \'this Hebrew expression does not occur frequency enough in the Bible for us to know precisely what it means. \r\nThe second 1:2 Moses wrote\" the spirit of God was hovering over the water\" \r\nHebrews mean\" fly above\" \r\nSo we see very dramatic pictures right at the beginning of this passage.', 7, '2020-04-21 16:31:53', '2020-04-22 16:00:17', NULL),
(1420, 427, 809, 'When Moses described God as entering a state of Sabbath rest , granting a special blessing to that day and marking it holy , he declared that the tension between the chaos and God\'s hovering spirit had been resolved. \r\nGod had subdued the darkness, ruled over the chaotic deep , and delighted in the ideally ordered world . The creation story come to an end with this delightful peace full \r\nNow that we have seen how Mose creations account begin and end we should look at the middle portion of this passage which describes how the tension between the chaotic world and God\'s hovering spirit was resolved', 7, '2020-04-21 16:42:41', '2020-04-22 16:00:17', NULL),
(1421, 427, 810, 'John 1:1-3\r\nJohn say \" In the beginning\" we know that this word come from Genesis 1:1\r\nJohn 1:4-5  John continued to draw upon themes of Genesis 1 especially the theme of the light which God brought to the dark chaotic world on the first day. \r\nYet instead of simply speaking of Jesus as the light of Genesis \r\nA similar motif appears in 2 Corinthians 4:6\r\nHere Paul directly refer to Genesis 1 \" God said.... let\'s....\r\nThe apostle said that the inauguration of Christ s kingdom..that time when Christ face could be seen on earth \r\nThis Main passage we see new Testament writers approach to Moses creation account', 7, '2020-04-21 16:52:43', '2020-04-22 16:00:17', NULL),
