(2122, 583, 753, 'उत्तर = प्ररितो के काम की पुस्तक में लुका पेन्तेकुस्त  के बाद पवित्र आत्मा के कई बार किए हुए  के कार्य की ओर मुड़ता है उदाहरण के लिए हम तीन संदर्भों के ऊपर अपना ध्यान केंद्रित करेंगे हम सबसे पहले एक ऐसी घटना को देखेंगे जो कि सामरिया  शहर के गुमनाम स्थान पर घटित हुई दूसरा हम केसरिया में घटित हुई एक घटना को देखेंगे और तीसरा हम इस उसमें आत्मा को प्रकृति करण के ऊपर ध्यान केंद्रित करेंगे आइए सबसे पहले हम सांवरिया में यरूशलेम के के उत्तर में आत्मा की सेवाएं को देखें।सामरिया :- \r\n\r\n\r\nप्ररितो के काम 8:14-17 मे लुका  ने एक अन्य स्थान पर आत्मा को विशेष तरीके से विश्वास क्यों के ऊपर उतारते हुए लिखा है सुनिए लुकाने वहां पर क्या लिखा है: प\r\nप्ररितो के काम 8:14-17 सामान्य तौर पर प्रेरितों के काम  मैं जैसा कि आज के दिनों में होता है पवित्र आत्मा उन लोगों के ऊपर उड़ेल आ गया जो कि पहली बार विश्वास में आए थे ना कि बाद में किसी समय पर इस संबंध में दृष्टांत यहां पर पेन्तेकुस्त  के दृष्टांत है विश्वास क्यों ने आत्मा को उनके मन परिवर्तन के बाद प्राप्त किया या एक विशेष समय था आत्मा का नाटकीय तरीके से उड़ेला जाना था सांवरियो पर आत्मा इस रीति से क्यों उतरा ठीक है आत्मा के इस असामान्य तरीके से उड़े ले जाने की सबसे उत्तम व्याख्या यहां है कि इसमें बड़ी संख्या में शामिल हों का मसीही विश्वास में आने के बारे में सूचित किया जैसा कि आपको स्मरण होगा कि शो मे प्रेरितों  को परमेश्वर का राज्य मुस्लिम से यहूदी या फिर सांवरिया और फिर पृथ्वी के अंतिम छोर तक विस्तार करने के लिए अधिकृत किया था यार इसलिए मैं यह दिया पेन्तेकुस्त  के दिन एक आरंभिक बिंदु था परंतु सामग्रियों के विश्व विरासत मिलावट थी अर्थात वे यहूदी और अन्य जाति से मिलकर बने थे और उन्होंने परमेश्वर की आराधना पुराने नियम में दिए हुए निर्देशों के अनुसार नहीं किया इसलिए जब सांवरिया में सुसमाचार पहुंचा तो इसने एक नए चरण को यीशु के द्वारा उनके शिष्यों को दिया हुए आदेश की पूर्णता की ओर मुख्य चरण का प्रतिनिधित्व किया यहां जातियों की जातियां सीमाओं के पार सुसमाचार का पहला बड़ा विस्तार किया जाना था पवित्र आत्मा ने इन विश वासियों को अन्य भाषा बोलने के लिए सशक्त किया था ताकि यह प्रेरित हों को और बाकी की कलीसिया को यह गवाही दे सकी किसानों को भी कल ईशा ने पूरी तरह अपना लिया गया है।\r\n\r\n\r\nकैसरिया :-\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nसामरिया के अपरिचित शहर में हुए घटना के सदस्य केसरिया की परिस्थितियों यह संकेत देती है कि वहां पर भी पहली बार सुसमाचार जातियों की सीमा को तोड़ते हुए पहुंचा था इस घटनाओं में अन्य जाति पहली बार मसीह में एक बड़ी संख्या में आ मिले थे विशेषकर रोमी सूबेदार कुरनेलियुस और उसक घाराना।\r\nप्ररितो के काम 10:44-47 भैया वर्णित करता है कि उस समय क्या हुआ जब पतरस ने कुरनेलियुस  के घर आने को सुसमाचार प्रचार किया।\r\n एक बार फिर से एक आश्चर्य कर देने वाला समांतर पेन्तेकुस्त  के दिन के लिए प्रमाणित होता है वह जिन्होंने सुसमाचार में विश्वास किया था वह अन्य भाषा बोलने लगे पतरस ने तो यहां तक की टिप्पणी की कि केसरिया के  विश्व आसियों ने भी पवित्र आत्मा को प्राप्त किया था जैसे कि हमने प्राप्त किया था जो शायद पेन्तेकुस्त  के दिन आत्मा के उदय ले जाने का संकेत देता है पुराने नियम में अन्य जाति परमेश्वर की इस्राएल के साथ बंदी गई बचाव के से बाहर थे और विश्वास योगी यहूदी लगाता है स्वयं को मन फिर आए हुए अन्य जातियों से दूर रहते थे इसलिए यहां आरंभ कि कल ईशा के लिए अचंभे की बात तहरीर जब अन्य जातियों ने मसीह में यहूदी धर्म में पूरी तरह मन परिवर्तन किया बिना मन परिवर्तन किया।\r\n परिणाम स्वरूप पवित्र आत्मा कुर्नेल यूज और उसके घर आने के ऊपर आश्चर्यजनक तरीके से यह प्रदर्शित करते हुए उड़ेल दिया गया क्या ने जातियों के राष्ट्रों के लिए भी अंत में दरवाजों का खोल दिया गया है सुनिए प्ररितो के काम 1:4,15,18 के शब्दों को जहां लोका ने अन्य जातियों के प्रति कलीसिया के प्रतिक्रिया के बारे में वर्णित किया है।\r\n अन्य जातियों का मशीन में आने का तरीका पेन्तेकुस्त  से मिलता-जुलता था इसलिए पवित्र आत्मा नहीं आ प्रदर्शित किया कि उनका मन परिवर्तन वास्तविक था और उनकी अन्य जातियों के साथ राज्य के निर्माण की योजना का आरंभ हो गया था।\r\n\r\n\r\nइफिसुस :- \r\n\r\n\r\nयह घटना प्ररितो के काम 19:1-6 मैं वर्णित की गई है जहां पर हम निम्नलिखित विवरण को पढ़ते हैं:\r\n एक बार फिर से हम पेन्तेकुस्त  के दिन जैसे सामान तारों को यहां पाते हैं यीशु उनके बपतिस्मा के बाद पवित्र आत्मा  इन लोगों के ऊपर आ उतरा और वे अन्य भाषा बोलने और भविष्यवाणी करने लगे इस संदर्भ में लोकानिक्स उसने पवित्र आत्मा को नाटकीय तरीके से उतरते हुए वर्णित किया है जो कि एशिया माइनर एक का एक मुख्य शहर था जो यहूदियों और सांवरिया से बहुत दूर था जैसा कि हमने देखा है कि लोका ने पहले ही आत्मा के कार्य को यरूशलेम से सांवरिया और अन्य जातियों में होते हुए देख लिया था आज सामान्यता तथा यह है कि आत्मा को प्राप्त करने वाले लोग यूहन्ना बपतिस्मा देने वाले के शिष्य थे अनुमान यह किए हुए यदि थे जिन्होंने यूहन्ना बपतिस्मा देने वाले की गवाही कोई होना के प्रकाशन से पहले प्राप्त किया था कि यीशु ही लंबे समय से प्रतीक्षा किए जाने वाला मर्सिया तालुका ने इस घटना पर प्रकाश इसलिए दिया है क्योंकि यहां एक विषय को बंद करता है जिसे दुकाने प्ररितो के काम  की पुस्तक के आरंभ में जोड़ देता है यह ना बपतिस्मा देने वाले और यीशु के बीच के संबंध के ऊपर आपको प्ररितो के काम 1:5 इसमें होगा यीशु ने अपनी शिव काय की तुलना योना बपतिस्मा देने वाले के साथ इस तरह से की है।\r\n एपिस उसमें यूहन्ना के शिष्यों को ऊपर आत्मा के उड़े रे जाने की कहानी यह संकेत देती है कि अभिषेक का आत्मा को लाने का कार्य एक नई अवस्था में पहुंच चुका है यहां तक कि योजना के शिष्य भी मसीह का अनुसरण करने वाले बन गए थे और उन्होंने मसीह की आत्मा को प्राप्त किया था कोई भी चीज मसीह को प्राप्त करने के लिए पूरी नहीं है और पवित्र आत्मा की सामर्थ्य में जीवन यापन करना परमेश्वर की इच्छा अनुसार था।\r\nलुका  ने बिल्कुल स्पष्ट कर दिया कि जैसे-जैसे प्रेरित कनिषा के मिशन का विस्तार करते गए उनका राज्य की सीमाओं पर किया गया कार्य लगातार पवित्र आत्मा के नाटकीय तौर पर उड़ेला जाने के द्वारा प्रमाणित किया गया आत्मा की सामर्थ्य के द्वारा सुसमाचार का प्रसारण सीधा निबंध गति से यरूशलेम से यहूदियों और फिर सांवरिया और फिर पृथ्वी के अंतिम छोर तक हुआ यहां तक कि आज के समय में भी पवित्र आत्मा की सामर्थ्य ही केवल वह तरीका है जिसके द्वारा कलीसिया परिवर्तन हो सकती है और प्रभावशाली तरीके से सुसमाचार प्रचार कर सकती है हमें भी आत्मा की शामत पर निर्भर करने के लिए कुछ करनी चाहिए यदि हम सुसमाचार के संदेशों के लिए प्रभावशाली और धर्म गवाह होना चाहते हैं।', 9, '2020-10-31 03:52:16', '2020-11-05 18:10:12', NULL),
(2123, 583, 754, 'उत्तर = प्ररितो  और उनका अनुसरण करने वाले जिन्होंने मसीह की गवाही पूरे के पुरे  मसीह प्ररितो के काम  की पुस्तक में दी के विभिन्न तरीकों पर हमारी विवेचना हम दो भागों में बैठेंगे सबसे पहले हम मसीह की गवाही देने के लिए विभिन्न रणनीतियों पर ध्यान देंगे। दूसरा हम कई तरह के विभिन्न घाटों का उल्लेख करेंगे जिनमें उन्होंने इस गवाही को दिया आइए सबसे पहले विभिन्न रणनीतियों को देखें जो की प्ररितो के काम  की पुस्तक में प्रयोग की गई है।\r\n\r\n\r\nरणनीतियाॅ:-\r\n\r\n\r\n जबकि यहां पर अनगिनत तरीके है जिनसे हम प्रेरितों  और उनके अनुयायियों द्वारा इस संसार को मसीह को प्रस्तुत करने की रणनीतियों के बारे में वर्णित कर सकते हैं परंतु यहां पर 6 प्राथमिक दृष्टिकोण ओं को सोचना उपयोगी होगा सबसे पहले उन्हें उन्हें अक्सर इतिहास का उपयोग किया विशेष करके जब जीवन मृत्यु और यीशु मसीह के पुनरुत्थान के संदर्भ में कहने की बात आई जो  की संपूर्ण रोमी साम्राज्य में सूची किया जाए थे उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरितों के काम 26:26 पौलुस ने राजा के सब्द :\r\n इस संदर्भ में पौलुस  की मुख्य बात यह थी कि वे मौलिक तो थी जिसे उसने और कलिसीया  ने घोषित किया था  वे  पूरे के पूरे प्राचीन संसार को मालूम थे इस तरह के ऐतिहासिक घटनाओं का उपयोग करना एक सामान्य रणनीति थी जिसे प्ररितो  ने प्रयोग किया जब आयुष वासियों को गवाही दे रहे थे। दूसरा प्रेरितों  ने लगातार पवित्र शास्त्र का उपयोग सुसमाचार के विस्तार की सहायता के लिए किया जबकि यहूदी श्रोताओं की गवाही दी जा रही थी तो प्रेरित अक्सर पुराने नियम का उपयोग करते थे उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरितों के काम 3:22 मे पतरस  ने मूसा की शब्दों को यहूदियों के लिए यह प्रमाणित करने के लिए उपयोग किया की सूखी ही लंबे समय से प्रतीक्षा किए जाने वाला मसीह था और प्रेरितों के काम 23:6 मे पौलुस  ने मृतकों के पुनरुत्थान के यहूदी मान्यता उपयोग किए जो कि पुराने नियम के पवित्र शास्त्र से निकालता है ।\r\n तीसरा जैपनीज आती श्रोताओं को गवाही दी जा रही थी तो प्रेरितों  ने परमेश्वर के प्राकृतिक में दिए हुए प्रकाशन और उन सच्ची मान्यताओं का उपयोग किया जो की मूर्ति पूजा विचारधारा में पाई जाती थी उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरितों के काम 17:24-27 पौलुस  परमेश्वर और मनुष्य की इतिहास के लिए मूर्ति पूजा को किस सामान्य दृष्टिकोण का प्रयोग अध्ययन ए के लोगों द्वारा सुसमाचार की घोषणा के लिए आरंभिक बिंदु के रूप में किया उसने वह पर क्या कहा उसे सुनिए प्ररितो के काम 17:25-27\r\n जिस दृष्टिकोण को पुलिस ने यहां प्रस्तुत किया है वह न केवल मसीही विश्वासी और यह उद्योग के द्वारा माननीय थे वर्ण में मूर्ति पूजा को को भी माननीय सच्चाई तो यह है कि अथेने मे अरियुपगुस पर भी यही संदेश दिया तो भी उसने मूर्तिपूजक साहित्य की ओर संकेत किया जो कुछ उसने प्ररितो के काम की पुस्तक 17:28 मैं कहां उसे सुनिए।\r\nयहाँ पर पौलुस  यूनानी साहित्य का उपयोग  मसीही गीत की गवाही देने के लिए उपयोग किया जब वहां एथेने में मसीह के लिए गवाही दे रहा था।\r\n चौथा प्ररितो  ने जब भी मासी को दूसरे के मध्य प्रस्तुत कर रहे थे तो अक्सर व्यक्तिगत अनुभव का उपयोग किया प्ररितो के काम की पुस्तक  मलूका ने कई बार इसका वर्णन किया है कि पौलुस  इस तरीके का उपयोग किया उदाहरण के लिए और उसने उसके नाटकीय मन परिवर्तन को जो कि दमीसकस  के मार्ग में हुआ था को उपयोग किया जिससे प्रेरितों के काम अध्ययन 9 में  वर्णित किया गया है उसने प्ररितो के काम अध्ययन 22 मैं यरूशलेम में यह उद्योग की भीड़ के आगे अपने अनुभव को स्मरण किया और उसने इसका विवरण राज्य अग्रिप्पा को प्रेरितों के काम अध्ययन 26 में दिया ।\r\n पांचवा प्ररितो  ने कई चीन और आश्चर्य कर्मों की साहित्य के सुसमाचार के प्रचार कर रहे थे को प्रमाणित करने के लिए किया जैसा कि हमने इस अध्याय में पहले देखा प्ररितो के काम की पुस्तक प्ररितो  के द्वारा प्रकट किए हुए अच्छे कर्मों से भरी पड़ी है जब कभी भी आत्मा ने प्रेरितों  को अच्छे कर्म करने के लिए सामर्थ दी तो उसने ऐसा यीशु मसीह की गवाही देने की मैं सहायता के लिए किया ।\r\n छठा प्ररितो  निशु के प्रति गवाही उनकी अटल विश्वास योग्यता के द्वारा दी उन्होंने निरंतर लोगों का ध्यान मशीन से प्राप्त ध्यानपूर्वक आकर्षित के द्वारा खींचने में प्रयोग किया और उन्होंने उसका अनुसरण करने की बोला हट को इनकार कर दिया यहां तक कि जब वे सताए गए या फिर जब उन्हें धमकियां क्या उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरितों  के काम अध्याय 14 में जब दूसरा के लोगों ने पुलिस और वनवास की पूजा करनी चाही तो पुलिस ने जोर दिया कि वह केवल एक मनुष्य था और उसने लोगों का ध्यान परमेश्वर की ओर लगाया और पंडितों के काम अध्याय 4 में जब यहूदी महासभा ने पीड़ितों को धमकाया और सुसमाचार प्रचार करने से रोकने के लिए दोषी ठहराया तो प्रीतो ने चुप रहने में मना कर दिया जैसा कि हम प्रेरितों 5:28-29 मे पढते हैं ।\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nढाॅचे:-\r\n\r\n\r\n ऐसे कई तरीके हैं जिनमें हम विभिन्न राज्यों को सारा सेट कर सकते हैं जिनमें प्ररितो ने प्रेरितो के कामकी पुस्तक  मैं सुसमाचार के लिए गवाही दी परंतु हमारी अपनी आसानी के कारण हम इन समय अवधि को 4 मौलिक श्रेणियों में एक कर देंगे सबसे पहले वहां पर सार्वजनिक भाषण थे यहां पर हमारे पास भी घटनाएं है जिनमें प्रेरित हों ने एक बड़े समूह के लोगों को सार्वजनिक ढांचे में रहकर मासिक दिया चाय व संदेशों या फिर मंडन भाषण हो या फिर किसी अन्य तरह का काव्य हो।\r\n इस तरह के प्रस्तुतीकरण में प्रेरित होने बहुत ही ज्यादा सावधानी से सूरत आंखों के अनुसार अपने शब्दों का चुनाव करके उन्हें भाषण दिया जैसा कि हम पहले ही इस अध्याय में देख लिया है विरोधियों से 1 तरीके से और अन्य जातियों से दूसरे तरीके से बोले।\r\nदुसरा प्ररितो  ने समस्या वाद विवाद के संदर्भ में गवाही दी इस तरह के ढांचे में लोगों को प्रतिवाद करने के लिए निमंत्रण दिया जाता है और प्रेरित तो मैं यह आशा की जाती थी कि वह सुसमाचार का वर्णन करें उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरित ओके काम अध्ययन 19 मे पौलुस ने इफिसुस में तिरनुस नामक स्थान के सभागार में वाद विवाद किया जहां पर कला और नए विचारों को लोगों के आगे जांचा जाता था\r\nतीसरा प्रेरितों के काम और प्रेरितों  और अन्य जिन्होंने उनका अनुसरण किया वे अक्सर पूरे घरों में गवाही दी प्राचीन संसार में घर आने में विशेष तौर पर माता पिता और उनके बच्चे में से ज्यादा लोग सम्मिलित होते थे वहां पर अक्सर संबंधित मित्र और उस घराने की सेवा करण होते थे इसलिए जब वह हम प्रेरित हों के काम की पुस्तक में घर आने के बारे में पढ़ते हैं तो हमें यहां कल्पना करनी चाहिए कि उन्हें उनके संबंधित भी होते थे जैसे बच्चे दादा दादी चाचा और चाची और इसके साथ काम करने वाले कर्मचारी और सेवा कर और कई घरानों ओम ऐसे तो गुलाम भी सम्मिलित थे पूरे घरों के समूह मिलकर लगभग औसतन 15 से 20 लोगों को मिलाकर होता था हम प्रेरित के उदाहरण के हो पाते हैं कि वे पीड़ितों के काम की पुस्तक के कई स्थानों पर घरानों की दवाई दे रहे थे जैसा कि अध्याय 10 में झा पत्र स्नेक रंगीली उसके घर आने को गवाही दी और अध्याय 16 में जब जहां पर पोलूश लुधिया और फिलिप उसके बंदी ग्रह के दरोगा को गवाही दी।\r\nचौथा प्ररितो के काम  मैं गवाही देने के तरीके को व्यक्तिगत सुसमाचार प्रचार के उदाहरण भी उदाहरण के लिए प्रेरित ओके काम की पुस्तक के अध्याय 25 में पोलूशन राजा अग्रिप्पा और व्यक्तिगत तौर पर बातचीत करते हुए अपने शब्दों को विशेष तौर पर अग्रिप्पा के ज्ञान और अनुभव से मिलाया संक्षेप में पीड़ितों ने स्वयं को केवल एक निश्चित तरीके से ही यहां फिर एक निश्चित ढांचे में ही गवाही देने तक सीमित नहीं रखा जब हम प्रेरित ओके काम की पुस्तक समीक्षा करते हैं तो हम उन्हें प्रत्येक अवसर पर लगभग उठाते हुए पाते हैं जिनमें वे सुसमाचार के प्रचार इस तरीके से कर रहे हैं जो कि प्रत्येक श्रोता के लिए उचित लगता है ऐसा करने के द्वारा प्रेरित होने हमारे लिए एक नमूने को प्राप्त किया है जो हमारे सुसमाचार उन तत्वों के ऊपर जोर देने के लिए शिक्षा देता है जिसके द्वारा हम अपने श्रोताओं के साथ ज्यादा शक्ति  साथ तर्क वितर्क करते हुए उन पर जोर दे सकते हैं और और उन विभिन्न तरीके को पता लगा सकते हैं जो कि प्रत्येक विश्वासी के जीवन के साथ संबंधित होता है।', 9, '2020-10-31 04:02:50', '2020-11-05 18:10:12', NULL),
(2124, 583, 755, 'उत्तर = \r\n आवश्यकता:-\r\n मशीह उसकी कलिसीया शा को निर्मित करने के लिए प्ररितो  को अधिकृत किया क्योंकि प्रेरित जानते थे कि वे थोड़े से ही लोग हैं जो कि मसीह के सुसमाचार को पूरे संसार में स्वयं के बल पर नहीं ले जा सकते हैं उन्हें प्रत्येक स्थान पर राज्य के सुसमाचार प्रचार की घोषणा के लिए गवाहों की सेना की आवश्यकता थी हम उन दो तत्वों की ओर देखेंगे जिसने अलीशा को प्रेरित हों के मिशन को स्थापित करने के लिए आवश्यक बना दिया पहला हम प्रेरित हों कि भौमिक सीमाओं को देखेंगे वे सच्चाई कि वे भूमि के तौर पर चीन का कार्य को उन्हें करने के लिए दिया गया था को नहीं कर सकते दूसरा हम उनके अलौकिक सीमाओं को देखेंगे वह सच्चाई की वे सामान्य मानवीय जीवन को यापन करने और भविष्य की पीढ़ियों के लिए गवाही देने के लिए असमर्थ होंगे आइए प्रेरित हो कि भौमिक सीमाओं के आरंभ करें।\r\n\r\n\r\n भौतिक सीमाएं :-\r\n\r\n\r\n जैसा कि हमने पहले ही देख लिया है कि प्ररितो  का कार्य मसीह की गवाही सुसमाचार की घोषणा के द्वारा देने के लिए था परंतु वह स्वयं से पूरे संसार के लिए जीवित पथरिया बनकर नहीं दे सकते थे इस समस्या के समाधान के लिए प्रेरित होने कलीसिया को प्रमाणित गवाही देने के दायित्व सौंपा जैसे जैसे लोग कलिसीया मे प्ररितो  के सुसमाचार द्वारा जोड़ दिए जाते थे वे विश्वासी अपने आप में ही जीवित पथरिया बन गई उन्होंने सुसमाचार को अपने यापन किया परिणाम स्वरुप अपने परिवार और पड़ोसियों के लिए यीशु के गवाह बन गए उनमें से कुछ तो यहां तक कि मिशनरी और सुसमाचार प्रचारक बन गए इस तरह से प्रेरित हों ने प्रत्येक पीढ़ी में प्रमाणित सुसमाचार प्रचार के लिए एक स्वयं को प्रदर्शित करता हुआ नमूना प्रदान किया जिनमें अब कलेश या स्वयं ही सबसे ज्यादा कार्य कर रही है यह सुनिश्चित है कि कल ईशा उसी अधिकार और प्रमाणित असरी कर्म के द्वारा सुसमाचार प्रचार करने की योग्य नहीं हुई जो प्रेरित के द्वारा प्रचार साथ में प्रगट हुए परंतु फिर भी पवित्र आत्मा कलीसिया के जीवन और वचन की प्रमाणित गवाही के द्वारा कार्य करने से प्रसन्न हुआ था आप ही सारे नए विश्वासी इस तरीके से इनमें आज जुड़े थे|\r\n प्रेरितो के काम 11:19-21\r\n\r\n\r\n लौकिक सीमाएं :-\r\n\r\n\r\nप्ररितो इस बात से  अस्वस्थ थे कि यीशु वापस आएगा परंतु वह नहीं जानते थे कि कब जिस समय राजा हिरदेश ने प्रेरित याकूब को प्रेरित के काम अध्याय 12 में मारा तो यह स्पष्ट था कि कम से कम कुछ प्रेरित उस समय तक जीवित नहीं रहेंगे जब तक यीशु पुनः वापस नहीं आ जाता इस लिए प्रेरित अनेक अलीशा को प्रस्तुत रुप से प्रेरिताई देखरेख में सुसमाचार प्रचार के लिए परंतु साथ ही साथ प्रेरित ओं की मृत्यु के बाद कलीसिया के निर्माण के कार्य के चलते रहने के लिए भी इसे प्रशिक्षण किया उदाहरण के लिए पॉलिश के पैसों के प्राचीन ओं को प्रेरित ओं के काम 20:25-28 मैं कहे हुए शब्द को सुनिए।\r\nपौलुस  यह सुनिश्चित करना चाहता था कि कल इसे निरंतर सुसमाचार प्रचार के लिए प्रमाणित तरीके से मसीह के ऊपर निर्भर रहेगी और विश्वास इयों को परिपथ तक लेकर आएगी इसलिए उसने यह सुनिश्चित किया कि उसके अब वह को उनकी मृत्यु के बाद यंत्र सेव काय के लिए तैयार रहना चाहिए प्ररितो  की भौतिक और आलू की की सीमाओं के कारण अलीशा प्रेरित हो कि छोटी अवधि और लंबी अवधि किरण नीतियों के लिए परमेश्वर के राज्य के विस्तार के लिए केंद्रित थी।', 9, '2020-10-31 04:14:18', '2020-11-05 18:10:12', NULL),
(2125, 583, 756, 'उत्तर = प्रेरितों लोग  अभी भी कलीसिया में सेवा देते हैं प्ररितो  लोगों ने पहले अपने सेवक आई के करने के दरमियान वर्णिक अलीशा को स्थापित किया और कल शाम में वह अधिकार दिए जो कलीसिया का जिम्मेदारी भी होता है कि यीशु के सुसमाचार को बाहर में फैलाएं आज कल ईशा के प्रत्येक विश वासियों का भी कर्तव्य और काम है कि वह भी प्रभु यीशु मसीह के सुसमाचार को प्रेरितों  जिस तरह से फैलाया है अर्थात जिस तरह से यीशु के सुसमाचार के लिए वे जोखिम उठाए दुख उठाए परेशानी उठाए उसमें भी वह लोग विश्वास के साथ में प्रभु के सुसमाचार को बढ़ाते गए वैसे ही कल ईशा का भी जिम्मेदारी है कि प्रभु यीशु मसीह के लिए दुख उठाते हुए प्रभु यीशु मसीह के सुसमाचार को आगे बढ़ाएं यह अधिकार प्रभु यीशु मसीह भी हमें और  कलीसिया को देता है कि उसके सुसमाचार को लोगों तक पहुंचाएं और प्रेरितों   लोग भी कलीसिया को अधिकार दिया है यह करने के लिए  पहले किस शताब्दी में प्रेरित  कलीसिया  के न्यू के रूप में उन्होंने काम किया  आज भी हमारे लिए यह महत्वपूर्ण है कि हम भी प्रेरितों  की तरह सुसमाचार के लिए दुख उठाए।', 8, '2020-10-31 04:25:10', '2020-11-05 18:10:12', NULL),
(2126, 584, 937, 'The three members of the Trinity are each eternal and uncreated. There was never a time when any member of the Trinity didn\'t exist. But the Father\'s relationship in the Trinity is unique. The Father is not begotten and doesn\'t proceed from any of the other members of the Trinity. He has never become incarnate, and the other two members of the Trinity have never sent him to do divine work. There\'s one God, yet they exist in some type of mysterious unity as one God, three persons. It\'s clear in New Testament that son gladly and joyfully submits to the Father and Spirit gladly and joyfully submits to Father and to the Son. They are always of one mind. They are always of one will and they work in an absolute perfect unity and harmony. So any idea that there\'s a disagreement among the Triune God is just a creation of man\'s imagination and nothing such can be found in God\'s revealed Word.', 8, '2020-11-01 16:07:24', '2020-11-05 18:09:46', NULL),
(2127, 584, 938, 'Just as the Father\'s relationship in the Trinity is unique, the Son\'s relationship is also unique. The Bible uses language that tells us that the Father \"begat\" the Son. Scriptures like Revelation 5:13,14 are clear that Son is eternal. He has always existed. Because of this theologians say that the Son is \"eternally begotten\" of the Father. New Testament uses the word \"God\", as first person and so when there\'s a father, there has to be a Son, and this relationship goes back to eternity. He\'s not a creature out of nothing but He\'s a Son begotten of his Father from eternity before all worlds. Son is of one substance with the Father, and through the Son all things were made, so that the Father, through the Son, made all things.', 7, '2020-11-01 16:13:26', '2020-11-05 18:09:46', NULL),
(2128, 584, 939, 'When we say that the eternal and uncreated Holy Spirit \"proceeds\" from His Father, we do not mean that the Spirit had a beginning. Just like the Father and Son, the Spirit is eternal, without beginning or end. So, Holy Spirit eternally proceeds from the Father. This is the doctrine of Holy Spirit\'s \"eternal procession\". The Bishop of Constantinople said that the Holy spirit was created being like an angel and that he was subordinate to the Son. Constantinople reaffirmed Nicea, and added that Holy Spirit is Lord. He is life-giver. He is to be worshipped as the Father and the Son. And this is known as the Nicea Creed. By calling the Holy Spirit both \"Spirit of God\" and \"Spirit of Christ,\" Paul implied that the Spirit not only proceeds from God, the Father, but also proceeds from Christ, the Son. Other passages that teach this same doctrine include John 16:13-15; 20:22 and Galatians 4:6.', 8, '2020-11-01 16:28:23', '2020-11-05 18:09:46', NULL),
(2129, 584, 940, 'In 589 A.D, a church council met in the western city of Toledo, Spain and among other things, attempted to solidify the doctrine of eternal procession. Here western theologians added the Latin term \"filioque\", meaning \"and the Son,\" to the Nicene Creed of 381 AD. With this addition, creed clearly articulates that the Holy Spirit \"proceeds\" or \"spirates\" from the Father and the Son. They argued in the filioque clause that means \"and from the Son\"- they affirmed the double procession of the Holy Spirit and the Spirit proceeded from the Father and the Son. Many would say that it was a political matter but the truth was  that a big division came over the very issue of the trinity. And it is very clear that the issue that really divides the East and the West on this doctrine of the Trinity is this issue of the filioque or the double procession of the Spirit.', 8, '2020-11-01 16:45:10', '2020-11-05 18:09:46', NULL),
(2130, 584, 941, 'the word \"Ontological\" refers to being and existence. So, the theological term \"ontological Trinity\"- has to do with the existence and personal interaction of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit apart from their acts of creation and salvation. We refer to the fact that the three persons of the Trinity- Father, Son and Holy Spirit- are equal in substance, power and glory. And none of them are superior in this regard. The Father is God. The Son is God. The Holy Spirit is God. It means God in himself, God as present only to himself and what God in himself beyond all time and space, before creation, God within himself. The ontological Trinity is what from Scripture, we derive as to the relations within God himself beyond all creation. It is the same God at work in creation, but God in an inner life of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit relating to each other eternally.', 8, '2020-11-01 16:53:48', '2020-11-05 18:09:46', NULL),
(2131, 582, 891, 'Problem of Sin:\r\nSin Condemns us and apart from Christ, we have no way to save ourselves from its presence and its consequences. sin is lawlessness and is most fundamentally violation of God\'s law.it means rebellion, transgression and missing the mark.\r\n1john 3:4\r\nGenesis 3 we see that Adam and Eve Rebelled against God by eating the forbidden fruit of the knowledge of good and evil. this act was not an isolated event. it caused the entire human race to become guilty of sin. In Romans 5:12, sin had not entered humanity before the fall, we had never committed sin, not inclined toward sin, not corrupted by sin neither indwelt by sin.\r\none command quickly moved to foreground as a test of their willingness to serve God. And the possibility of breaking this law gave an opportunity for Adam and Eve to sin. but neither Satan \'s temptations nor Eve\'s foolishness excused the sin of our first parents, the both were guilty of choosing evil instead of good.\\\r\nConsequences of sin:\r\nAfter Adam an Eve Sinned, God judged and cursed the entire human race\r\nthis curse affected every aspect of their being\r\n1. it immediately resulted in the spiritual death(John 5:24,25)\r\n2. produced corruption(Jeremiah 17:9)\r\n3. lead to physical death(Genesis 3:19)\r\n4. eternal suffering under God\'s judgement in hell(Mathew 5:29,30)', 8, '2020-11-03 17:49:33', '2020-11-05 18:09:19', NULL),
(2132, 582, 892, 'as  a work of  divine grace, forgiveness involves all three person of the trinity;\r\nthe Father:\r\nForgiveness began with the Father , because He was the one that planned it. Father send the son into the world nd appointed him the redeemer. John 3:16\r\nFather authorized Jesus empowerment as the redeemer of his people. John 10:14-18\r\nFather offered Jesus as a sacrifice Romans 3:25\r\nFather is the great architect of redemption\r\nSon:\r\nJesus atone for Sin by dying on the cross in the place of sinners.\r\nHe received the divine curse  that had been warranted by our sin .His perfect righteousness was credited to our account, so that we could be counted not as sinners, but as obedient children of God.\r\nJohn 10:14-18 ,Galatians 2:20\r\nHoly Spirit:\r\nthe Holy Spirit applies forgiveness to our lives. The spirit reconciles us to God by forgiving all the sins we have committed up to that point.\r\n He gives us new spiritual life by regenerating our spirits. John 3:5-8\r\nSpirit is the one that maintain our faith, that lead us to daily repentance and that continually applies forgiveness to us.\r\nRomans 8;1-5', 7, '2020-11-03 18:02:50', '2020-11-05 18:09:19', NULL),
(2133, 582, 893, 'The process of forgiveness involves an element of individual responsibility. two primary conditions for forgiveness are faith and brokenness.\r\nFaith in God is the perquisite of forgiveness. \r\nBrokenness is genuine sorrow over sin, true regret over violating God\'s law Psalms 51:6,17\r\nwe need to live lives of constant faith and to truly sorry for the sin  we continue to commit  so that we can continue to receive forgiveness and cleansing in a daily basis\r\nforgiveness is always earned , but it is earned by Christ, not by us, And the fundamental means by which all grace is applied to our lives faith. prayer is presented as an ordinary means for applying to God for grace and forgiveness. the second general category of means of forgiveness is the sacraments .\r\nThe Bible teaches that when we express our faith through the Lord supper and baptism , the holy Spirit use this ordinances to apply forgiveness to our lives.', 7, '2020-11-03 18:17:54', '2020-11-05 18:09:19', NULL),
(2134, 582, 894, 'God created human beings to consists of physical bodies and non physical souls. Hebrews 4:12, human consists of two main parts body and soul.\r\nbefore our fall into sin , our bodies and souls  were unaffected by sin and its corrupting powers. but when Adam and Eve fell into sin, sin corrupted not only souls but also their bodies. Gen 3:19\r\nour souls come into the world in a state that the Bible describe as spiritual death, we are under God\'s judgement and have lost all ability to please Him.\r\nRomans 5:12-19\r\nthis corruption results in physical hardship, sickness and eventually death. Sin corrupts all that we are our entire being, body and soul. so the curse leads in our death, which is very tragic and unnatural. God did not create humanity for death.', 7, '2020-11-03 18:28:39', '2020-11-05 18:09:19', NULL),
(2135, 582, 895, 'The redemption of our body is in three stage: Present life, intermediate state and  new life:\r\nPresent Life:\r\nBible teaches that the salvations of our bodies begins with the indwelling of the holy spirit, when we first come to faith, Romans 8:9-11. it does not result bin our immediate bodily resurrections , it does seal us with the guarantee of the full redemption of the bodies in the future. Ephesians 1:13-14\r\nintermediate state: the holy spirit sets us apart for God and purifies us to the sanctification\r\nIntermediate state:\r\nwhen we die, our bodies are temporarily separated from our soul , this stage is often called the intermediate state. the state between our live s on earth now  and the lives we will have in the resurrection. during the intermediate state our souls dwell with Christ in heaven\r\nMathew 17:3\r\nNew Life: Our bodies will receive new perfect life when they are restored to life in the general resurrections. in the resurrection the consequences if sin will , finally and forever be put away from us. Romans 8:23\r\nJust Christ bodies under went changes  in the resurrection, our bodies will also be changed, they will be renewed and perfected .they will be immortal, glorious, powerful and spirit-full.', 8, '2020-11-03 18:44:03', '2020-11-05 18:09:19', NULL),
(2136, 585, 717, 'Missions originated in the heart of God. It is not something we decide to do for God, but God reveals his purpose to us so that we may have a creative part in his mission. God\'s mission, however, is to redeem man from the clutches of evil and save him. Three basic purposes are;\r\n1. To bring glory to God- In Ephesians, Paul stated three times that God\'s eternal plan is for his people to be to the praise of his glory (Eph 1:6,12,14). God stands as both the originator and the goal of the redemptive process. The glory of God is the ultimate goal of God\'s mission. God receives glory when man fully realizes the purpose of his existence, Eph 3:19)\r\n2. To share the good news with the alienated\r\nGod\'s mission includes recreating man spiritually.  \"We are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works , which God prepared before ordained that we should walk in them\" (Eph 2:10) God restores man\'s identity and his purpose for being. The mystery of God\'s mission is clear: \"That the Gentiles should be fellow heirs, and of the same body and partakers of his promise\" (Eph 3:6)\r\n3. To display the wisdom of God to evil powers- Man becomes God\'s display of his wisdom to Satan and his evil beings: \"In order that the manifold wisdom of God might now be made known through the church to the rulers and authorities in the heavenly places\" Redeemed man is the primary exhibit of God\'s grace. God seems to be depending on us to demonstrate his goodness, wisdom, and power. We do not understand what is at sake for God, but we are told that it is supremely important to him and us.', 8, '2020-11-04 15:42:58', '2020-11-05 18:08:51', NULL),
(2137, 585, 718, 'God\'s new strategy was to select one individual as his friend and partner. Christian, are elected in Christ and inherit the promises (Gal 3:13-16). God has called us and selected us to be one of his children. He expects us to walk in obedience to his commands and to serve him through serving the world. God intends for his people to be a disciplined people, holy priests and suffering servants to the world. Both Old and New Testament emphasize that God is forming a holy nation of servant priests. \r\nA disciplined people- The OT sketches God\'s efforts to form a holy people. Holy means to be separated unto God. God called Abraham to leave home and family to become a pilgrim people, and represent God\'s holiness to other nations of the world. They learned that righteousness of God demanded disciplined, obedient people. Israel was to be a parable and proclaimer of God\'s holiness.\r\nA Nation of Priests- At the beginning of the covenant with Israel, God promised that they would be a kingdom of priests. God has decreed that they shall be called \"priests of the Lord\" and \"ministers of our God\". Priest were the models to the whole nation who represented God to man and Also represented man to God, They were a holy priest nation to whom God would reveal that they might transmit the revelation to other nations\r\nA Servant People- the Lord of heaven and earth serves us and expects us to be servant people. We are to love like God, serve like God, and minister to all people like God. The servant passages of Israel clearly expresses the servant role of Israel and ultimately of Christ.', 8, '2020-11-04 15:54:05', '2020-11-05 18:08:51', NULL),
(2138, 585, 719, 'The incarnation- When God spoke at the beginning of time, the world was created, when God spoke by the Word in the incarnation, new humanity was created. The incarnation is the good news that the kingdom of heaven has come in Christ. Almost as startling as the incarnation was Jesus\' becoming a servant. He served  man in the most menial ways. He fulfilled the intentions of the Father as stated in Isaiah. The graphic picture of his servant role is when Jesus took the towel and washed his disciples\' feet. God, who became man, now stooped to serve man that man might catch the vision of serving. \r\n\r\nThe Crucifixion- The self emptying of Christ climaxed with Gethsemane and Golgotha. Philippians 2:8 moves directly from Christ\'s incarnation to his crucifixion: \"Being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even death to the cross.\" Jesus completely surrendered to the Father that the struggle centered on knowing the Father\'s will. He fulfilled the priestly role by his prayers and by death, positioned himself between sinful man and the Holy God as intercessor and represented man to God as well as God to man. By His death, satisfied the Father, subdued Satan and reconciled man to God.\r\n\r\nThe Resurrection- Jesus\' resurrection proved that the evil forces of satan had been conquered throughout the cosmos. In his death and resurrection, Jesus \"spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly\" (Col 2:15). During these times the gospel is to be preached to all nations. Jesus gave the Great commission on basis of resurrection. The resurrection was the condition for world missions. The gospel all our lives may not realize what good news it is. \r\n\r\nThe incarnation tells the masses that God cares; the crucifixion proclaims forgiveness of sin; the resurrection shouts that the victory has been won over sin, death, and Satan.', 8, '2020-11-04 16:10:57', '2020-11-05 18:08:51', NULL),
(2139, 585, 720, 'The mission of Holy Spirit who came to take Jesus\' place, to inspire, to empower, and to guide them. \r\nThe Inspirer of Missions- The mission of Christ could not be accomplished without the mission of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit came to make God\'s people into the body of Christ. He regenerates, sanctifies, teaches, guides, comforts, illuminates, and intercedes. But the most important ministry in relation to his mission is the filling of Christians for service. The Great Commission the Holy Spirit who inspires men to carry it out spontaneously. When God\'s people are revived, they witness. Result is that sinners coming to Christ has not been an outpouring of the Holy Spirit. \r\n\r\nThe Empowerer of the Church- The power they received with the filling of the Holy Spirit was life changing and world changing because it was living, having authority spiritual power. Jesus used the word dunamis, from which we get the word dynamo and dynamite. Holy Spirit gives us the spiritual ability to make an impact on those hearing the testimony. The spirit filled believer never witnesses alone. The Holy Spirit works within the lost person to verify and to personalize the witness of the believer. He makes real the authority and the presence of Christ.\r\n\r\nThe Guide in Missions- As executor of Christ\'s will the Holy Spirit determines the course of missions. Holy Spirit thrust them farther into the fields. Acts 1:8 outlines the Spirit\'s movement: Jerusalem (Acts 2:! to 8:4); Judea and Samaria (Acts 8:5 to 12:25) and the remainder of the world. Holy Spirit redistributes the harvesters from an area where there are many laborers to other places to thrust them out into the world as flaming witnesses.', 8, '2020-11-04 16:25:15', '2020-11-05 18:08:51', NULL),
(2140, 585, 721, 'The church lives with two identities. First, it is the remnant that God has chosen, sanctified and commissioned to be blessing to the world. Second, it is the body of Christ that must fulfill his ministry in the world. \r\n\r\nPriest of God- The Calvary Principle\r\nAs God\'s chosen generation, we live in the election stream of God\'s great purpose. This part of the chapter explores what it means to be chosen to be priests. Formerly we were Christ less, homeless, promise less, hopeless and Godless (Eph. 2:12). Just as the veil in the Temple barred man from the holy of holies, so our sins had created a barrier that kept us separated from God and each other. Old Testament priests, as representatives of God, pronounced blessings on individual people; we bless all nations by who we are and what we do. The Old Testament priesthood was limited to the tribe of Levi; the New Testament priesthood includes all believers. We should be different in our thoughts, talks and walk. \r\n\r\nServants of God- The incarnation Principle\r\nThe church as the body of Christ is incarnate in the world to become the suffering servant. It is not called out of the world but called to go into the world and love like Christ, forgive like Christ, and give itself to the world like Christ. But a more common application of the incarnation principle is to empty ourselves of earthly power and self importance. Proclamation grows most naturally out of life of service. His incarnation best illustrates the servant attitude. \r\n\r\nSons of the King- The resurrection principle\r\nFrom the time God created the world, his intention was to rule over obedient persons. The first church faced almost insurmountable odds. Obstacles like persecution, deception, discord and prejudice that cause the church not to fulfill its co-mission with God. Satan unleashed his attacks in ever increasing force and cunning, but the early Christian would not be silent. As children of King, we announce that kingdom has come. With joy we tell the good news that our Father owns everything.', 8, '2020-11-04 16:43:06', '2020-11-05 18:08:51', NULL),
(2141, 587, 927, 'The word \"trinity\" simply means \"tri-unity\" tree in one , so doctrine of trinity can be define in this way ,God is three divine person in one divine essence .this concepet is also found in the related terms \"trinitarian\" and triune God , when we talk about doctrine of the trinity , tyhe classic lanuage is ,we have one God in three person-one substance in three person .well what to be mean by that ,we are saying there is only one God ,so this is very important thing that christian want affirm, this is the great revelation we get throughout the scripture ,there is only one God not three ,one God but we find is this one God revels who God is in terms of father ,son and holy spirit, the tree person,not the three people but the three person of the one God,so we have the father who sends the son , the son who ,after his death gose to the father ,and they send the spirit .so we can think about the God as father ,son and holy spirit, its dosent mean thyey only suddenly began in creation, \r\nin christia n theology we talk about triune God , the trinity ,one in three ,and three in one , because that is how bible talks about him,', 7, '2020-11-05 01:44:11', '2020-11-05 18:08:27', NULL),
(2142, 587, 928, 'When we speak of three divine person we are referring to the distinct person of the father ,son and the holy spirit , we can define the word \"person\" as distinct , self aware personality existing in relationship with other persons , \r\n Understanding  what is \"person \" means when we talk about triune God is incredibly mysterious, Augustin , toward the end of his famous work on the trinity , says that when we use a term for \"person \"we are simply answering \"three what \"we need a kind of place holder to say \"three -something\" but we really don\'t know what exactly we speaking of , because we often get the order of the analogy between divine and human person reversed , \r\none thing that we can pretty substantially rule out is the modern concept of person as a distinct ego, and individual self consciousness attached to a separated body and a particular person name that others share ,  if we to apply that concept of the modern individual person to the person of triune God. We would risk tritheism , we would have \"God by committee\" so whatever we meant by person, we don\'t mean the modern concept of an individual person is not the trinity ,', 7, '2020-11-05 02:00:58', '2020-11-05 18:08:27', NULL),
(2143, 587, 929, 'Tritheism violates the biblical doctrine of divin simplicity , that God is indivisibly one , but the doctrine of God\'s Threeness doesn\'t , this acn be dificult o grasp until we recognize that although God is three divine persons, he is only one divine essence , and his essence cannot be divided into parts .he is invisibly one , so we should never confuse the trinity with tritheism , \r\nGod\'s simplicity is a very intresting notion because ,on the one hand ,the bible say that God is inconceivable , that is , a man cannot analyze him and sort him out,and on the othar hand theology contains the concept of God\'s \"simplicity\" and as we know we may say that God is reveald him self as trinity , but these tree perzon are one ,the have one essence ,that is he cannot be broken down into what wee\'d call parts, \r\nGod the first person has always been the father of his son, and his son has always been the son of his father ,and that gose back forever ,and ther has always been the spirit of the father and the spirit of the son, and the three are inseperatable , they are always with each other, not oly with each other but  a lot of times ,we can say that the son is in the father ,the father is in the son,', 8, '2020-11-05 02:15:58', '2020-11-05 18:08:27', NULL),
(2144, 587, 930, 'Some time anti Trinitarians cite verse like Colo 1:15 to argue that God hasn\'t always existed as Trinity , this verse say that Christ is the firs born over all creation, Since Christ was born, they argue that unlike the father he must have begun to exist , they believe hens a created divine person rather that an uncreated divine person equal to the father,\r\nwhen we look bake to the old testament we see hints there of the existence of Christ and the holy spirit , one objection of course would be that Christ is refers to as \"the first born over all creation\" in Paul\'s letter to Colossians ,when we look at the term \"first born \"and trace it back in the old testament , you see that first born is not a statement about chronological priority , its actually a statement of what we might call \"ontological \"  priority or supremacy ,', 8, '2020-11-05 02:25:47', '2020-11-05 18:08:27', NULL),
(2145, 587, 931, 'The scripture are clear that Jesus Christ the second person of the trinity , is uncreated and eternal , just like God the father ,he is equal to the father in power and glory sharing equally in all the attributes of the Godhead .and the same things is true of the holy spirit as well , infect every trinitarian formula found in scripture mentions the holy spirit as an equal member of the trinity , together with the father and the son , \r\nThe trinitarian formulas of the new testament helps us to understand that the holy spirit is fully divine and fully personal .there are number of important trinitarian formulas in the new testament , in the gospel as well as in the epistles ,and they help us to see how these new testament writers , the apostles , understood the work of god in Christ, \r\nJesus commands us to baptize people in the name of the father and the son and the holy Spirit ,one name father son and the holy spirit , , so we have one god , three person, Paul in his epistles dose the same thing where he is regularly talking about God,  and the son and the spirit .', 7, '2020-11-05 02:39:08', '2020-11-05 18:08:27', NULL),
(2146, 588, 932, 'As we know the doctrine of trinity teach us that there is one God ,one divine essence -and all three members of the trinity are equally and fully God, however this biblical truth is not fully and explicitly revealed in the old testament , instead much of the Christian doctrine of the trinity comes from the new testament , this truth dosen\'t mean that the old testament is wrong or inferior , but we should recognize that the old testament is incomplete revelation ,God didn\'t reveal himself all at once ,so when we look for the unity of the trinity in scripture ,\r\nwe need to acknowledge that the understanding of the triune God take place through what we call \"progressive revelation\" where God throughout the canon of scripture and moving into the new testament ,is progressively revealing more about who he is and how he functions ,so that when you see the phrase in the old testament \"the spirit of God \"tere is no reason to try to make some type of radical disconnect between say, God the father ,when you speak of the spirit of God ,you are speaking of God, the scripture dosen\'t say that the God is other than us ,above us like in the old testament ,that he no longer dwells among us like in the new testament era in Jesus ,but now the scripture says through the holy spirit that God has taken one step closer and he now in us .', 9, '2020-11-06 01:47:38', '2020-11-16 15:28:33', NULL),
(2147, 588, 933, 'Revelation 1 and 4 says that the one who sit on the throne is God  the father ,john 1 tells us that the Lamb is the word of God who become flesh , Jesus Christ ,the son of God ,both of this chapter clearly teach Jesus uncrated divinity .in Revelations 5 John used the imagery of both a lamb and alion to describe Jesus ,\r\nA lion represents Jesus\'s kingly majesty ;a lamb represents his gentleness ,and more importantly perhaps symbolizes him as a redeemer of sins , so this is wonderful device, revelation 5 placed jesus in the category of divine .he isn\'t one of God\'s creatures ,insted he is equal with God , and as God\'s divine son ,he will conquer all his enemies when he returns.his victory over satan will be complete , an all God\'s peop[le will have peace with God the father and his divine son .\r\nRevelation 4 after the praises of the wonders of God\'s creation suddently the praises stopped because God raised his right hand ,holding a scroll , and a mighty angel asked if any one could open this scroll ,it announced the Lion of Judah ,but once announcement happened what appeared was not a lion but a lamb , and when that lamb got the scroll entre corts of heaven worshiped and praised him ,', 8, '2020-11-06 02:04:43', '2020-11-16 15:28:33', NULL),
(2148, 588, 934, 'The first chapter of John and first verse in particular has been used throughout the history of the church as proof that jesus is God the son, the second person of the trinity , he is God and has been with God since the beginning ,he also involved in the creation of all things .and because he involved in the creation of all things he himself must be uncreated , throughout the centuries of the church this has been text that everione has said \"the teaches the deity of Christ\"john 1:1 is absolutely undisputed,it clearly teaches  the deity of Christ, John begin his gospel unlike the other gospel writers , he begins with the person of christ , \"in the beginning was word\" and the word was with God and then very emphatically \'the word was God\", and of course we read john 1:14, and the word become flesh and dwell among us , and we beheld his glory , the glory as of the only begotten , full of grace and truth , an john begins his gospel by telling us that Jesus is God, in John 10, where christ says i and the father are one ,and its intresting that there Christ place himself before the father , emphasizing his equality with the father .', 8, '2020-11-06 02:20:16', '2020-11-16 15:28:33', NULL),
(2149, 588, 935, 'Searching and knowing is an attribute of a distinct , self aware personality who is in a loving relationship with other self aware personalities , these necessarily implies that the spirit of God is a person , distinct from the person of God , if the spirit of God were simply God \'s impersonal attribute of power of his impersonal force in action ,he couldn\'t \"search \'and know ,\r\nnot every passage of the scripture that talk about the spirit of God identifies the spirit as a distinct person , in john 14-16 we read about the spirit doing things that only a person would do , jesus said that he will send another advocate like himself , the holy spirit ,and the spirit gose on the prosecute the word concerning sin and righteousness and judgment , just like Jesus did, the spirti is a witness just like we are witnessing ,and so the spirit is identified as a distinct person in number of passage ,', 8, '2020-11-06 02:32:09', '2020-11-16 15:28:33', NULL),
(2150, 588, 936, 'Clearly scripture teaches that Jesus is the true and living God , both Paul and John understood ,this, but one of the great ,mysteries of the trinity is that Jesus is not only fully god , he is also fully human , and it is through his humanity through his life ,death and resurrections ,that we are saved  and because the human jesus is also the eternal God ,equal power and the glory of the father ,we can trust that he is worthy and able to redeem us from our sins, if we are to be reconciled to God , then only God can forgive our sin, Jesus was the true God and the true men, two natures in one person, and the reason by he has to be a man is that he has to represent us , he must be tempted in every point like as we are ,he must be the second man the last Adam , and he must also be God ,because only God can forgive sin, because God is the  who has been sinned against in Adam, \r\nthe scripture are clear that jesus is both God and man, he is the second person of the trinity , god the son, its amaze truth that the eternal son of God become human and living among us .', 8, '2020-11-06 02:45:30', '2020-11-16 15:28:33', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2151, 589, 937, 'We have seen that the tree member of trinity are each eternal and uncreated .there was never a time when any member of the trinity didn\'t exist , but the father\'s relationship in the trinity is unique , the father is not begotten and doesn\'t proceed from then any of the other member of the trinity , he has never become incarnate ,and the other member of the trinity have never sent him to do divine work ,one of the intresting challenge of Christians is to understand the inneer working relationship of the triune God ,and there are some things we do know and some things we dont know , we know that whatever it is that makes God ,the father is all of that ,the son is all of that , and the holy spirit is all of that , we also know that the father is not the son, the son is not the spirit and the spirit is not the father , , ther e is one God yet they exist in some type of mystirious unity as one God three person, \r\nit is also clear that then son galdly and joyfully submit to the father and the spirit gadly and joyfully submits both to the father and the son, the fatehr always get his way sice he is the one to whom both of them give athority and give acknowledge authority and give submission ,they are always on one mind ,they are always on one will ,they are always united in any and every issue including redemption .', 9, '2020-11-07 01:47:15', '2020-11-16 15:28:02', NULL),
(2152, 589, 938, 'Just as the father relationship in the trinity is unique ,the son\'s relationship is also unique ,when discussing the relationship between the father and the son, the bible uses the language that tells us that the father \"begat \" the son, this can be confusing , since , human beings this language implies the  son was born like every other person ,but scripture like Rve 5:13, 14, are clear that the son is eternal ,he has always existed , because of this theologians frequently say that the son is \"eternal begotten\" of the father , \r\nusually when the new testament uses the word \" God\"  it\'s usually referring to the first person is the father of his son, so when there is father , there has to be child , and in this case it would be thae son, and this relationship goes back to the eternity ,there was not start in this relationship ,and so the first person is seen , right of the bat , as in relationship to the second person, eternal source , eternal begetter of the son, and so we say , in the Nicen Creed , that the son is \"begotten\"(of the father )not made , so he is not a creature , he is not a creature created out of nothing , he is son begotten of his father from the eternity before all worlds.', 9, '2020-11-07 02:02:23', '2020-11-16 15:28:02', NULL),
(2153, 589, 939, 'Now the Greek term ekpreuomai ,doesn\'t meant exactly the same the same things when said of the spirit that it means when when said of a person, when a chilsd proceeds from his or her parents we know that he or she had a beginning in conception and birth , he or she was created, but when we say that the eternal and uncreated holy spirit ,\"proceed \"from his father , we do not mean that the spirit had a beginning ,just like the father and the son ,the spirit is eternal, without beginning or end, so we  say that the holy spirit eternally proceeds from the father , this is the docrine of the holy spirit \"eternal procession\" and its affirms the divinity of the holy spirit , the council of Constantinople meet and wrote two  -thirds of Nicene Creed and in particular that section developed that section on the holy spirit , \r\nwe believe in the hoy spirit , then nit added \"the lord the giver orf life , who proceeds from the father and the son , and with the father and the son is worshiped and glorified ,divityu of the holy spirit we can ascribes ,in two ways , first , its aford to the holy spirit divine status, and second, its ascribes to the holy spirit divine attributes.', 8, '2020-11-07 02:18:26', '2020-11-16 15:28:02', NULL),
(2154, 589, 940, 'In A.D. 589 a church council met in the city of Toledo, Spain , and among other things attempted to solidify the doctrine of eternal procession , here western theologian added the Latin, terms , \"filioque\" meaning \"and the son\" to the Nicene Creed, of 381, A.D, with addition the creed clearly articulates that the Holy spirit \"proceeds\" or spirates from father and son,  the filioque clause was a clause that western church added very erly on the ecumenical council to explain the relationship of the son to the father , but particularly the son/spirit relationship .what they argue that in gthe filioque clause simply means \"and from the son\" they atrgue that the father and the son there were the filioque comes in  , are then person who then send the spirit , and they are triying to justice to the biblical teachings that the father sends the son , yet the father and the son send the spirit , the exalted Lord jesus christ ascends to the right hand of the father ,and from the throne of authority he ,with the father ,send the spirit, and so the church was tring to thing of the roleof the relationship ,the personal relashanship between the father son, and the holy spirit .', 8, '2020-11-07 02:33:14', '2020-11-16 15:28:02', NULL),
(2155, 589, 941, 'On the one hand ,the word \"ontological\" refers to being and existence , so the theological terms \"ontological trinity \" also known as the immanent Trinity , or the Trinitas ad intra ,in latin, has do with the existence and personal interaction of the father  ,son and the holy spirit ,apart from their acts of creation and salvation , when we speak of the ontological trinity we are referring to its nature ,we refer to the fact that the three person of the trinity , father son and the holy spirit , are equal in substance , power , and glory , of course none of them are superior in this regard , the father is God, the son is God and the holy spirit is God ,we could say it like this: the father is one hundred percent  , the son is one hundred percent God, the holy spirit is one hundred percent God ,', 7, '2020-11-07 02:43:29', '2020-11-16 15:28:02', NULL),
(2156, 591, 897, 'AD 56 or 57 : Near the end of Paul\'s third missionary journey, Paul went to Jerusalem to deliver funds to the poor Christians who were enduring a famine. On their way they stopped in Miletus where Paul met with the the elders from the nearby church of Ephesus. During this meeting Paul revealed that the Holy Spirit had warned him that he would be imprisoned when he arrived in Jerusalem. Believers prophesied Paul\'s coming imprisonment. Paul knew these prophecies were to prepare him for his coming hardships.\r\nThe prophet Agabus bound his own hands and feet as a prophetic sign, warning that Paul would be arrested and bound if he continued to Jerusalem. Paul answered \"I am ready not only to be bound, but also to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus\" (Acts 21:12).\r\nPaul understood that his coming imprisonment would be \"for the name of the Lord Jesus.\"', 8, '2020-11-08 15:37:50', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2157, 591, 898, 'Presumably, it was at this point that Paul delivered to the church the famine relief funds that he had collected during his third missionary journey. Paul hoped that the gift would make Jewish Christians more eager to receive the Gentiles as full brothers in Christ. Paul claimed that the Sadducees opposed him because he was Pharisee and that the gospel he preached agreed with the teachings of the Pharisees on many points. When Paul proclaimed to the Sanhedrin that he preached a gospel based on his vision of the resurrected Christ, he gained some sympathy from the Pharisees. Paul defended himself before the Sanhedrin, explaining why so many Jews had taken offense to his teachings. But the Sadducees did not back down, and the meeting became extremely violent. So, once again, Lysias took Paul into custody. Since Paul was a Roman citizen, he was transported to Caesarea Maritima. Felix: Governor of the Roman province of Judea.', 8, '2020-11-08 15:45:29', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2158, 591, 899, 'Late AD 59: Paul was placed under the authority of a Roman centurion named Julius and put on a ship heading for Asia Minor. A violent storms blew their ship past Cauda, far out into the Mediterrean Sea. Eventually, the ship struck a reef near the Isle of Malta and was destroyed. Paul and everyone else from the ship were stranded in Malta for three months. Paul presented the people of with the gospel and performed miraculous healings. \r\nEarly AD 60: Paul and his companions set sail for Italy. Paul arrived in Rome later that year, and was placed under house arrest. \r\nAD 60-62: Paul lived under house arrest in Rome. He was permitted to receive guests and to teach freely. \"For two whole years Paul stayed there in his own rented house and welcomed all who came to see him. Boldly and without hindrance he preached the kingdom of God and taught about the Lord Jesus Christ\" (Acts 28:30-31).', 8, '2020-11-08 15:52:40', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2159, 591, 900, 'As we will see, Paul was not idle during his time in prison. Rather, he continued to serve actively as a minister of the gospel of Jesus Christ. Paul was an apostle. Jesus had personally called and trained Paul and appointed him to serve as his ambassador, his covenant emissary. Paul\'s appointed tasks were not put on hold when he was imprisoned. On the contrary, in God\'s providence prison was exactly so that Paul\'s imprisonment in Rome would provide the opportunity for Paul to spread the Gospel of Christ to the heart of the western world.', 8, '2020-11-08 15:56:33', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2160, 591, 901, 'Paul\'s experiences of imprisonment were very important for Luke, the author of Acts. He dedicated nearly nine chapters to events related to this period in Paul\'s life.\r\n1. Awareness of Suffering- Paul was aware that his service to Christ would bring severe hardship and suffering into his life. Paul was acutely aware of the difficulties that awaited him in his service to Christ and his gospel, and was willing even to be martyred. \r\n2. Awareness of Purpose- Paul was aware of the purpose of his suffering. The Lord also planned to use this suffering to promote the gospel. Imprisonment would be the means through which Paul accomplished his ministry. Paul presented his testimony to the mob that sought his death (Acts 22:1-21). He testified to the gospel and Christ\'s resurrection before the Sanhedrin, proclaimed the gospel to the Caesarean court- publicly at his hearing and privately to the governor Felix and his wife Drusilla, proclaimed the gospel to the new governor Festus. He regularly preached the gospel of the kingdom of God to all who came to see in Rome.\r\n3. Awareness of Blessing-Paul was very aware of God\'s blessing on his ministry during this time. Paul received and interpreted visions to protect the lives of those on the ship that eventually crashed on the reef. He healed the sick. He ministered to the individual needs of the believers.', 8, '2020-11-08 16:07:57', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2161, 591, 902, 'Paul\'s letters from prison share some important doctrinal foundations. Affirm the same gospel. Share a common way of presenting the gospel. Tend to emphasize the same aspects of that gospel. Common foundation: Jesus Christ is the conqueror  and ruler of all creation. \r\n1. Sovereignty- Christ has the strength and power to accomplish his will, and the legal authority and right to do. When Jesus ascended into heaven, God the Father vested him with sovereignty over all creation  with absolute power. Not everything in creation obeys him as it should. But Jesus has the right to command its obedience, and the power to make it obey. \r\n2. Honor- Christ\'s glory and value demand the responses of respect, emulation and worship. Jesus is God, and God is worthy of the highest honor imaginable. \r\n3. Determination- Christ is determined to return of Christ grew out of traditional Jewish views of the end times. Jesus revealed that this traditional Jewish conception was not entirely accurate. The age to come would replace the present age , but not at all once. Jesus reigns as king from heaven. He wants and plans to rule over inch of creation as fully and gloriously as he now reigns in heaven. Jesus must return in order to deliver our inheritance in the consummated kingdom.', 8, '2020-11-08 16:19:11', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2162, 591, 903, 'When we believe in Jesus, we are united to him in a spiritual, though mysterious way. Paul frequently encouraged his readers through their sharing in Christ\'s kingship. Through our union with Christ: We are united to Christ\'s death. We are united to Christ in his resurrection and life. We are united to Christ in his ascension and kingship. We are seated with Christ in the heavenly places. Paul spoke of our union with Christ in order to encourage believers that they did not suffer alone, and that they did not suffer in vain.  Paul took comfort in the fact that when we suffer for the gospel , our union with Christ ensures that Christ suffers and sympathizes with us.', 9, '2020-11-08 16:25:02', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2163, 591, 904, 'Common foundation: Jesus Christ is the conqueror and ruler of all creation. \r\n Christ has the strength and power to accomplish his will and the legal authority and right to do. When Jesus ascended into heaven, God the Father vested him sovereignty over all creation. Christ\'s glory and value demand the responses of respect, emulation and worship. Jesus is God, and God is worthy of the highest honor imaginable. Christ is determined to return to earth in order to consummate his kingdom. \r\nWhen we believe in Jesus, we are united to him in a spiritual, through mysterious way. Paul frequently encouraged his readers through their sharing in Christ\'s kingship. Paul spoke of our union with Christ in order to encourage believers that they did not not suffer alone, and that they did not suffer in vain. \r\nBecause Christ is both sovereign and just, we are legally and ethically obligated to obey everything as he commands because He is our King. God has commanded that all who are united to his Son must live holy lives because we are United to Christ. This obligates us to treat one another as we would treat Christ himself, as we ourselves want to be treated.', 9, '2020-11-08 16:36:33', '2020-11-16 15:27:29', NULL),
(2164, 592, 761, 'Genesis is the title for the creation account, telling us that God was the Creator .\r\nScripture tells that the Son was at the Father\'s side during Creation and that then Father created the world by or through him. \r\nColossians  1:16 says that about the son\'s involvement in creation.\r\nDuring the first three days of Creation . God formed or gave shape to the world.\r\nDuring the second three days of creation, God filled the empty world, so that his kingdom would be properly ordered and governed.\r\nThe world existed in order to become God\'s special kingdom , under the authority of his Only Son.\r\nHebrews 1:2\r\nHebrews connected the idea that God the son was active in creation with the fact that He was the heir of all things.', 7, '2020-11-09 17:42:54', '2020-11-16 15:45:15', NULL),
(2165, 592, 762, 'The theological term eternal counsel, often called eternal decree refers God\'s plans for the universe, which were set before his work of creation.\r\nit is mentioned in Acts 2:23, Romans 8:28-30.\r\nEternal counsel:\r\nWhat Christ accomplished is central to god\'s plan- that /god ordained salvation in him and that Christ wont fail. Ephesians 1:4.\r\nForeknowledge:\r\nRegarding his foreknowledge . god said that from the beginning , before the created of the world, he knew what was still to come.\r\nGod created the universe in order to display and increase his glory through his kingdom in Christ. Hebrews 1:1-13\r\nPurpose\r\nScripture indicates that before the creation of the world, the persons of the Godhead entered onto a solemn arrangement to secure redemption and to apply it to the fallen creation.\r\n In which the Son Promised to become incarnate and died  in order to redeem fallen humanity from the consequences of sin. \r\nCovenant of Redemption\r\nand the Father promised to accept his Son\'s sacrifice in payment for the redemption of sinners.', 8, '2020-11-09 17:55:06', '2020-11-16 15:45:15', NULL),
(2166, 592, 763, 'God\'s promises are immutable.\r\nCovenant of redemption: the trinity promised to redeem fallen humanity\r\ncovenant of grace:\r\nthe arrangement made between the Father and the son and redeemed humanity\r\n Adamic covenant: Genesis 3:15\r\nredemption was first offered to humanity\r\nNoahic covenant: Genesis 6-9\r\npromise to preserve humanity until the Son\'s redeeming works was complete.\r\nAbrahamic covenant: Genesis 15, 17, 22\r\nPromise that one of Abraham\'s descendants would be the redeemer.\r\nMosaic covenant: Exodus 19-24\r\ninstructions of a sacrificial system\r\nDavidic covenant: Psalms 89, 132\r\npromise that the redeemer would descend from David and bring God\'s kingdom to earth\r\nNew covenant: Luke 22:20\r\nthe work of redemption was and is being accomplished.\r\nThe son of God was the promised redeemer who would bring all God\'s covenant blessing to his faithful people.\r\nJesus performed promised role of dying as a Sacrifice for sin.', 8, '2020-11-09 18:08:00', '2020-11-16 15:45:15', NULL),
(2167, 592, 764, 'Jesus\' redeeming work:\r\nInauguration of Kingdom\"\r\nGod\'s people longed for the day when God would bring his kingdom to earth.\r\nJesus brought the kingdom of God to earth in his days.\r\nwe are still waiting for Jesus to consummate the kingdom\r\nobedience:\r\nJesus role as redeemer includes succeeding were Adam failed .\r\npassive obedience:\r\nJesus submission to a life of humiliation , suffering, and crucifixion\r\nJesus was our substitute\r\nJesus sacrificed on the cross , frees us from God\'s just condemnation\r\nActive obedience:\r\nJesus life of obedience to everything the Father commanded\r\nJesus perfectly kept God\'s law\r\nForensic righteousness:\r\nJesus\' righteous obedience imputed to us.\r\nGod rewards us with salvation on the basis of Jesus merit\r\nResurrection:\r\nJesus conquered death and secured everlasting life for his people.\r\nJesus guaranteed that all who believe in him, will also be resurrected.\r\nAscension:\r\nafter the resurrection. Jesus was taken bodily into heaven.\r\nJesus is mediating and interceding for his people.', 8, '2020-11-09 18:19:33', '2020-11-16 15:45:15', NULL),
(2168, 592, 765, 'At least three crucial events will take place when Jesu returns:\r\nGeneral resurrection:\r\nat Christ\'s return everyone that has died will be resurrected. John 5:28-29\r\nBeliever\'s bodies will be free from corruption and sin. Philippians 3:20-21\r\nBodies of unbelievers will continue to be plagued by the effect of sin .\r\nsin will no longer indwell our bodies.\r\nOur bodies will be glorious.\r\nLast judgement:\r\nJesus will destroy all his enemies and bless all his faithful people.\r\nEveryone human being will be judged.\r\nEvery aspect of each person\'s life will be counted as evidence in the trial. every thought , word and deed will be evaluated.\r\nEvery human being that stand before god on his own merit will be condemned in this judgement and punished with eternal damnation in hell.\r\nBut the good news is that those who have been forgiven by grace through faith in Christ will be acquitted and rewarded with an eternal inheritance John 3:18\r\nMathew 12:36-37\r\nRenewal of creation:\r\nJesus will purge and renew creation.\r\nall creation will be restored to God\'s original plan.', 8, '2020-11-09 18:33:21', '2020-11-16 15:45:15', NULL),
(2169, 590, 771, 'Critical scholars offer three arguments for the objection that author was Jesus\' brother James:\r\nfirstly. they say there is no mention of the author\'s relation to Jesus. They say that its unthinkable that a brother of Jesus would write to the churches and not reveal this familial bond when he identified himself\r\nSecond , some scholars assume pseudonymity because the book gives evidence that the author was aware of Hellenistic  or Greek , culture and James was a Jew from Palestine.\r\nA third argument for peusdonymity points to inconsistencies with the theological ideas that ideas expressed in the epistle of James don\'t match theological outlooks attributed to James in these other New Testament books. Acts 21:17-25, James 2:12 here also James appears to be spokesman for  rather conservative Jewish-Christian position on the Law.\r\nthere is a great deal of support for the traditional outlook that Jesus\' brother James wrote this epistle. James 1:1 the writer didn\'t give any credential beyond  saying that he was a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ. He simply assumed that his name alone would be recognized and would carry sufficient authority. This opening greeting then make strong case for Jesus\r\n brother James because of his status in early church in Jerusalem.\r\nIn the second place, the testimony of the early church confirms this outlook on the authorship of the book. The First epistle of clement written around AD 96 and the shepherd of Hermas written around A D 140 quoted the book of James .\r\nOrigen identified the author of James as the brother of the Lord.', 9, '2020-11-10 17:47:58', '2020-11-16 15:45:36', NULL),
(2170, 590, 772, 'In James 1:1.James identified his readers as the twelve tribes scattered among the nations. This seems to be a reference to Jews who lived outside of Israel\r\nin 2:1 James addressed his audience as Believers in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ\r\nThese verses indicate that James original audience was made up primarily , Of Jewish Christians who lived outside of Palestine. \r\nJames addressed his audience affectionately as brothers. Acts 8:1-4 we learn that in the wave of persecutions following Stephen\'s martyrdom, members of the Jerusalem church were scattered through out the Judea an Samaria.\r\nit is possible then, that James as the leader of the Jerusalem church was writing to the this scattered members of the twelve tribes. James audience was made up of Jewish Christians in similar circumstances\r\nthe vocabulary James used also supports that idea that his original readers were Jewish followers of Jesus.\r\nJames 2:2\r\nSynagogue to describe his audience meetings\r\nJames 5:4 James used the phrase Lord Almighty this phrase comes from a common old Testament for the God of Israel.', 9, '2020-11-10 17:59:34', '2020-11-16 15:45:36', NULL),
(2171, 590, 773, 'Both the New Testament and early church fathers suggest that James lived his life of ministry in Jerusalem and he remained in Jerusalem until he was martyred in Jerusalem in A D 62\r\nDate:\r\nfirst the earliest likely date for the letters composition in AD 44. James wrote his epistle as the leaders of the early church in Jerusalem . Acts 12:17 indicates that Jesus Became a significant leader of the Jerusalem church by the time of Peter\'s release from prison . Peter was released in the year Herod Agrippa died in AD 44.\r\nPURPOSE:\r\nJames 1;2-4  W e read that James was audience was facing trials of many kinds, but James called them to have pure joy in their trials. He explained to produce perseverance.\r\nthe scattered Jews might find it difficult to find joy in Christ , and some of them might even leave their loyalty to Christ, so James  tried to persuade them to be in faith.\r\nJames also taught them to be in faith and not to stray from faith.', 8, '2020-11-10 18:13:07', '2020-11-16 15:45:36', NULL),
(2172, 590, 774, 'Many interpreters have spoken of the book of James as the New Testament book of wisdom. the letter of James also Reflect the content of the influential wisdom books outside of wisdom d of Sirach, also known simply as Sirach and the wisdom of Solomon books were well known in ?James day. and there are striking parallels to both in his letter Sirach 1;26 and James 1:5 are same \r\nMuch like the book of proverbs the the book of James deals with a variety of important themes. And if often spends only a few verses on one theme before moving to another.\r\noccasionally it returns to one or more of its themes later sin the letter but not with any consistency. There is no structure to James. its a only a collection of wisdom sayings with no real order or flow of thought.  \r\nAlthough the book of James resembles wisdom literature in both form and content, it also differs from that genre in a variety of ways.\r\nunlike other wisdom literature, James is later written to specific churches.', 8, '2020-11-10 18:25:48', '2020-11-16 15:45:36', NULL),
(2173, 590, 775, 'James 1:2-4  we see that James audience was facing trails of many kinds , but James called them to have pure joy in their trails. he explained produce perseverance and those who persevere will become mature and complete not lacking anything.\r\nJames 1;5 gives us a window in the heart of the entire epistle to experience pure joy in the midst of trial.\" ask god\" for wisdom and it will be given to you.\r\nJames called his audience to pursue wisdom from God so that they would have joy in their grails\r\nthe heart if the matter was that they need  to pursue wisdom from god. Wisdom from God was the key to receiving joy as they endured their many trials. James directed believers to humble themselves so that God would lift them up. He taught that  humility before God is a path to wisdom. and when Christ\'s followers draw near to God in humble submission , the wisdom they receive bring joy , even as they persevere through trials.', 8, '2020-11-10 18:37:33', '2020-11-16 15:45:36', NULL),
(2174, 594, 905, 'Paul had never actually visited the church in Colosse, but he cared deeply for them nonetheless. Paul\'s relationship with the Colossians from the details in Paul\'s letter to them as well as from his letter to Philemon, who lived in Colosse. For one thing, we read that Paul had an indirect relationship with Colossians through representatives, such as his Colossian friends Epaphras, Philemon, and Onesimus, and his messenger Tychicus. \r\nPaul and the Colossians corresponded with each other and ministered with each other. For instance, besides struggling in prison on their behalf, Paul prayed specifically for the Colossians. As he wrote in Colossians 1:9. Paul had friends that labored alongside him in gospel ministry including: Philemon, Apphia, Archippus, Epaphras- Paul\'s fellow worker and prisoner, a faithful minister of Christ. Onesimus who was a slave who sought out Paul after fleeing from Philemon; ministered to Paul in prison. Paul had a fairly minimal personal relationship with the Colossian church. Even though Paul had not personally met most of the Colossian believers, they shared affection and affinity for one another, making their relationship real and substantial.', 9, '2020-11-11 15:40:00', '2020-11-16 15:47:21', NULL),
(2175, 594, 906, '1) Greek Philosophy- Mysteries, Rites, Secret knowledge- secret wisdom \"Philosophy\" these words are typically applied to occult religions, especially those that were based on religious traditions such as =True mystery, wisdom and knowledge were found only in Christ, and not in pagan religions. False teachers in Colosse were enamored with beliefs and practices similar to those found in Greek religion. Paul objected to the ascetic practices in Colosse.\r\n2. Jewish Law- In Colosse, their use and understanding of Jewish law departed both from traditional Judaism and from proper Christian practices. False teachings were using Jewish teachings and practices in corrupt ways. Paul wrote against abuses of the Mosaic Law, but he did not write against the law itself. \r\n3. Spiritual Beings- The false teachers in Colosse promoted the worship of spiritual beings. \r\na. Angels- Ministering spirits- it is important to recognize their work. According to the false teachers, angels were cosmic powers. This exaggerated view of the power and influence of angels was not uncommon in the ancient world. \r\nb. Rulers and Authorities- Paul emphasized Christ\'s superiority over every power and authority in heaven and on earth. The false teachers attributed to these invisible rulers, actions and abilities that belong to Christ alone. \r\nc. Basic Principles- The basic principles were the basis for the philosophy of the false teachers. The false teachers appear to have combined: Jewish legalism, Pagan Religion, Christianity.', 9, '2020-11-11 15:52:35', '2020-11-16 15:47:21', NULL),
(2176, 594, 907, 'Paul\'s letters were: Personal and Pastoral\r\nHe was motivated by love and concern. Paul\'s letters were not just collections of authoritative teaching. For the churches and people to whom he wrote and Paul\'s letters were also \"occasional.\" It was written to address specific issues in particular times and places.\r\nPaul\'s letter to the Colossians, it is important for us to know something about the occasion that prompted Paul\'s writing. The background to Paul\'s letter to the Colossians from two directions: First,  some details of the his relationships with the Colossian church in general and with individuals within the church. and second, some of the problems in Colosse that concerned Paul.', 7, '2020-11-11 15:59:21', '2020-11-16 15:47:21', NULL),
(2177, 594, 908, 'In the main body of the letter the supremacy of Christianity over the religion of the false teachers. \r\n1. Supremacy of Christ- Christ is the image of invisible God. \r\na. Image of God, Colossians 1:15- In some Greek philosophies, the universe was thought to be God\'s image, the greatest revelation of God. Paul pointed to Christ as the image of God. He adopted this Greek philosophical meaning of the term \"image of God\" to show that Christ was the ultimate revelation of God. \r\nb. Firstborn over all creation, Colossians 1:15- Paul mentioned that Christ is the firstborn over all creation. He associated Christ\'s status as \"firstborn\" with his authority and supremacy.\r\nc. Agent of Creation, Colossians 1:16- Christ was the agent of creation, the one through whom God created the universe. Christ\'s priority as the agent of creation makes him far superior to everything within creation.\r\nd. Supreme Lord, Colossians 1:18- Christ is the supreme Lord because God placed him as head over the church. \r\ne. God incarnate, Colossians 1:19- Christ is God is incarnate\r\nf. Only Reconciler, Colossians 1:20- Christ is the only reconciler between God and man. Jesus Christ is the agent and the means through whom God is purging sin from the world, and making peace with humanity.', 9, '2020-11-11 16:09:20', '2020-11-16 15:47:21', NULL),
(2178, 594, 909, 'A. Reconciliation through the Christian Gospel, Colossians 1:21-23; 2:5- Paul and the Colossians had already experienced reconciliation through the gospel.\r\nB. Altruism, Colossians 1:24- Paul suffered on behalf of the church and his sufferings benefitted the church by: Providing a powerful witness of the gospel, encouraging the church and completing the sufferings of Christ. \r\nC. Divine Commissions, Colossians 1:25- Paul had been appointed to his apostleship by the Lord himself. The false teachers relied on ideas that idolatrous human beings had invented. \r\nD. Revelation, Colossians 1:25-28, 2:2-4- The revelation Paul had received was superior to that attested by false teachers. Paul had spent three years in the desert of Arabia and in Damascus receiving revelations from God (Gal 1:15-18). \r\nE. Empowerment, Colossians 1:29-2:1- God gave power to Christ\'s ministers. The Holy Spirit gifted Paul with astounding gifts to advance the kingdom of God on earth: Words to speak, Opportunities to speak and Miracles to confirm his witness.', 8, '2020-11-11 16:22:13', '2020-11-16 15:47:21', NULL),
(2179, 593, 589, 'Bible related the true story of creation in order to explain how humanity was actually designed to function within the world. Creation ordinances prove how the world is supposed to work and the role that human beings are morally obliged to play. Biblical scholars or theologians often refer to these obligations as creation ordinances, because they are \r\n\r\nMoral requirements established by God\'s works of creation.\r\n\r\nCreation ordinances can be split into two, one is Explicit & the second one is Implicit.\r\n\r\nFor e.g. \r\nGod\'s command to be fruitful & multiply are Explicity\r\nObligations such as keep the Sabbath holy are Implicit', 7, '2020-11-11 23:12:45', '2020-11-16 15:47:49', NULL),
(2180, 593, 590, 'The book of Genesis contains two accounts of Creation\r\n\r\nThe First Creation Account : (Genesis 1:1-2:3)\r\nThe first creation account, we are told that creation was originally formless and empty. \r\n\r\nFirst three days God dealt with the fact that the earth was formless and started giving shape to it\'s various realms. Earth was void and without shape, God separated dry land from the seas.\r\n\r\nNext 3 days, God dealt with the fact that creation was empty and he started filling it wity light, birds and animals\r\nOn the 6th day, God created man in His own image, and gave him authority over his other creatures.\r\n\r\nThe Second Creation Account (Genesis 2:4-25) \r\nMentions about God\'s work on the sixth day, when he created the land animals and humanity.\r\n\r\nGod created the animals from the dust of the earth. Only Adam received the breath from God. \r\n\r\nAdam named all the aimals and this demonstrated his authority over them. God created the first woman Eve from Adam\'s rib. Both were created in God\'s image and were asked to fill and subdue the earth. \r\n\r\nOnly man had the authority to rule as representatives of God in this earth.', 8, '2020-11-11 23:29:09', '2020-11-16 15:47:49', NULL),
(2181, 593, 591, 'Bible teaches that Adam & Eve were created to be superior to the rest of God\'s creatures. Humanity\'s creation itself on the 6th day in itself was a separate act from the rest of creation. \r\n\r\nOnly Adam had the explicity to have had his life breathed into him by God.\r\n\r\nIn Genesis 1:27-25, Adam and Eve\'s superiority over the rest of creation is found in the fact that God created them in his image and appointed them to rule over creation on his behalf.\r\n\r\nGod created man in his own image, male and female. God blessed them and said to them be fruitful and increase in number, fill the earth and subdue it.\r\n\r\nGod created humanity to reflect his glory and his attributes in ways that other creatures couldn\'t. God appointed our first parents to rule over every other creature he had made. So humanity isn\'t just inherently superior, but we have been given a superior role. \r\n\r\nAdam exercised authority over the animals by naming them.\r\n\r\nHuman beings are mentally suprior to animals, it denies the fundamental dignity we have as God\'s image and undermines our authority as creation\'s rightful rulers.', 8, '2020-11-11 23:41:01', '2020-11-16 15:47:49', NULL),
(2182, 593, 592, 'Major Abrahamic religions share the same beliefs about human origins as mentioned in the Bible. Whereas different religions interpret the origins of humanity in their own religious concepts, which are man made and are not supported by history. \r\n\r\nMost of the ethnic religions of the subcontinent does not share Biblical view on the origins of humanity, the myths they follow are mere fantasies and does not support any historic proof.\r\n\r\nWhereas Christian view about human origin are fully supported by historic and archaelogical proofs even in terms of geographical location etc.\r\n\r\nAccording to Scripture, Adam and Eve were real people.  Adam and Eve were historical people. That\'s how the Bible has recorded it, and we believe in the Bible because it\'s inspired by God.\r\n\r\nWorld and history use archaelogy, historical documents and all sorts of accounts passed down by variour traditions. \r\n\r\nThe book of Genesis gives biographical details about Adam\'s life. \r\n\r\nHence we can conclude that Christian views about origins of humanity is the actual fact checked view with historical and archaelogical proofs.', 8, '2020-11-11 23:53:45', '2020-11-16 15:47:49', NULL),
(2183, 593, 801, 'God placed Priestly obligations on Adam & Eve, God also gave them Royal obligations over the rest of creation.\r\n\r\nPriestly Obligations\r\nGarden of Eden served as an earthly sanctuary for the priestly role of Adam.\r\nAs God walked and talked with Adam and Eve, his presence itself implied the precursor of the tabernacle an temple. Since later on when we study the scriptures in detail, God manifested his presence only the High Priest and only in the most holy place of the tabernacle and temple.\r\n\r\nRoyal Obligations\r\nGod appointed Adam and Eve to rule over creation on his behalf. He caommanded them to increase the human race in order to spread its rule over the whole earth. This was humanity\'s royal obligation\r\n\r\nGod ordained humanity as his royal vassals to expand his kingdom beyond their dwelling place in the Garden of Eden. \r\n\r\nBy the grace of God personally I feel we have an obligation and responsibility to fullfill both Priestly and Royal obligation.\r\n\r\nServing God in the opportunities that He give, bearing his testimony and witness and to serve the community and lead them to have trust in God.\r\n\r\nBoth as an individual and family not only to share from the gospel, but also to be physically available for the needs of the community, since God works in mysterious ways through our life to enhance his domion and kingdom in this world. \r\n\r\nOur submission and obedience to his calling and ensuring a closer walk with God is what enables us to represent God as good stewards and ambassadors to fulfill the mandate of Priestly and Royal obligations.', 9, '2020-11-12 00:13:22', '2020-11-16 15:47:49', NULL),
(2184, 597, 910, 'A number of modern scholars have argued that Ephesians was written by one of Paul\'s students, but there are good reasons to accept Paul\'s authorship. The letter states that it was written by Paul. In Eph 1.1- Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God, to the saints in Ephesus. Ephesians closely resembles Paul\'s other letters in both doctrine and language. The connections are especially strong with Colossians, which should not surprise us since Paul probably wrote them both at about the same time. According to Acts 19-21, Paul had planted the church in Ephesus, and had lived in Ephesus for two years. And even after that time, he had continued to maintained close relationships with its elders. It is simply unimaginable that the Ephesians would not have recognized this letter as a forgery.', 7, '2020-11-13 15:37:11', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2185, 597, 911, 'Paul tailored his letter to address the specific problems of a relatively localized group of people whom he knew directly or personally. But in Ephesians he did something different: he responded to the problems of several churches in different locations, many of which he had never met.  The purpose is divided into two:\r\n1. Kingdom of God- Christ\'s kingdom exists in the overlap of the ages: The present age- sin and death and the Age to come- God will pour out ultimate blessings and curses. Our divine King is using his power and authority: Citizenship, the most valuable and well-known citizenship in Paul\'s day was citizenship in the Roman Empire. Inheritance, Paul associated it with inheritance in Christ\'s kingdom; Military Service, Christians engage in spiritual warfare also implied citizenship in God\'s kingdom. Rule over Creation and Sources of our names were because we are a part of His Kingdom and An Ambassador. \r\n2. Challenges- A. Sinful Nature- Sin must not characterize the citizens of God\'s kingdom.\r\nB. Racial Tension- Paul used the imagery of the God\'s kingdom to address tension between Jews and Gentiles in the church. \r\nC. Demonic Forces- The church- God\'s kingdom- is in a cosmic battle with the kingdom of darkness, ruled by Satan and his demons.', 8, '2020-11-13 15:46:55', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2186, 597, 912, 'Paul\'s job as an apostle was to provide authoritative teaching and leadership to the church, and he did this partly by writing letters. But Paul didn\'t just want to spread sound doctrine or record it for posterity. First and Foremost, he wanted to minister to the church in his own day by applying sound doctrine. His letter were pastoral and caring, and they directly to the problems that the church faced in the first century. \r\nThis means that as we study Paul\'s letter to the Ephesians, it helps to begin by asking questions like: To whom was this letter written? And what significant issues were they facing in life? Knowing the answers to the questions like these will help us make greater sense of Paul\'s teachings.', 7, '2020-11-13 15:50:58', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2187, 597, 913, 'A section of praise to God appears next in 1:3-14. This is Paul\'s only canonical letter in which the salutation is followed by such a section of praise to God. Typically Paul followed his salutation with a personal reference or greeting. But as we have seen, there are no personal references of any type in the epistle to the Ephesians. We don\'t know for certain why Paul decided not to include any personal greetings. Maybe he thought that a section of praise would work better in a circular letter. or maybe he wanted to lay the groundwork for the doctrinal sections that followed. Some have seen this section as the beginning of a dialog of prayer that spans the first three chapters. Paul praised God for our inheritance in Christ. \r\nPrayer- The kingdom of God provides the context for Paul\'s prayer. He mentioned God\'s sovereignty when he spoke of: The Father\'s  \"incomparably great power\" and \"mighty strength\". Christ being enthroned above all other rulers and shares his inheritance with us, so that his inheritance is our inheritance too.', 8, '2020-11-13 15:59:32', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2188, 597, 914, '1. Citizenship- The body focuses on the contrast between the righteous kingdom of God and the sinful kingdom of demons and fallen humanity. \r\na. In Kingdom of Darkness, the human race is sinful and fallen. \r\nb. In Kingdom of Light, God sovereignly determined to deliver people so they would inherit salvation.\r\nc. In the nature of citizenship, God has fulfilled the Old Testament ideal of combining both Jews and Gentiles into one kingdom under God\'s sovereign rule. \r\n2. Administration- Just before his arrest in Jerusalem, Paul had warned the Ephesians elders that false teachers would arise from their own ranks. The apostles possessed: special grace from God that empowered them in their ministry, special revelation from God that taught them infallible truth. \r\n3. Code for Living- \r\nA. Ecclesiastical Order in the Kingdom- Paul portrayed the Lord as a victorious king returning from the battle.\r\nB. Purifying the Kingdom- Believers within the kingdom of light have a new nature that they can rely on to overcome their sin. \r\nC. Domestic Order in the Kingdom- Proper relationships of authority must be maintained within all levels of Christ\'s kingdom.\r\nD. Warfare of the Kingdom (6:10-20)\r\nE. Final Greetings- Paul offered a closing blessings and indicated that Tychicus would deliver this letter', 8, '2020-11-13 16:11:24', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2189, 597, 915, 'God requires that we help him expand and grow his earthly kingdom. The kingdom of God is a building, with each Christian being a stone in the structure. The goal of the building is to become God\'s dwelling, so that God will live in the midst of his people. When Paul taught that both Jews and Gentiles would live in God\'s presence as his Temple, he meant that God\'s kingdom was moving toward its final goal. The big picture: Honoring God, Living in his presence, Striving to increase Christ\'s glory rather than our own. We must be humble- no believer is more deserving of blessing than any other. We must embrace all believers as equals in the Kingdom of God. All believers collectively composing Christ\'s body. Like the metaphor of the Temple, the image of the body described the kingdom of God: Christ was seated as king in heaven. He ruled for the benefit of his people, the church. Paul\'s fullest use of the body imagery appears in Ephesians 4:1-6, where he argued for ecclesiastical order in the kingdom. These leaders are to guide the church toward two goals: Unity in the faith, Attaining to the whole measure of the fullness of Christ- to bring all of creation under the rule of Christ.', 8, '2020-11-13 16:20:45', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2190, 597, 916, 'In the Bible, the gospel is cosmic in scope. It is the message that our divine King is using His power and authority to subject his enemies and to conquer sin, to redeem his people from their bondage and to establish them as rulers over the new earth. John The Baptist, Jesus and his disciples have all preached about the nearing Kingdom of God. \"The time is fulfilled, and the Kingdom of God is at hand (Mark 1.15).\" The same gospel is being preached today also. In our understanding, people most often associate the \"gospel\" or  \"good news\" with things like the forgiveness of an individual\'s sins and the promise of an individual\'s everlasting life. And these are the wonderful aspects of our hope.', 8, '2020-11-13 16:34:15', '2020-11-16 15:48:17', NULL),
(2191, 598, 917, '1. Philemon- Paul\'s partner in gospel ministry and the host of the local church. He seems to have had a significant history with Paul that formed a strong bond between the men. Paul meant that he had brought Philemon to faith, and owed Paul a great debt. But they knew each other very well. \r\n\r\n2. Onesimus- Onesimus was Philemon\'s slave, he was a member of Philemon\'s household. But unlike his master, Onesimus was not a believer, at least not initially. But after Onesimus left Philemon\'s household the apostle led him to faith in Christ and grew to love him greatly. Paul expressed his love for Onesimus, in Philemon 10-16 and because he had developed a fatherly love for him. \r\n\r\n3. Witness- Paul mentioned a number of other Colossians in his letter to Philemon: Apphia- as Paul\'s \"sister\" might simply indicate that she was a believer. It is more likely that she was a member of Philemon\'s household- probably his wife. Archippus, may have been the host of the local church met in Philemon\'s house. He was mentioned as person of some influence over Philemon, whether as a local pastor or a part of Philemon\'s household. Epaphras, Paul expected them to serve as familiar witnesses in his appeal to Philemon on Onesimus\' behalf. He had a keen interest in making sure that Philemon would respond properly to Paul\'s letter.', 8, '2020-11-15 15:41:14', '2020-11-16 15:48:42', NULL),
(2192, 598, 918, '\"Onesimus\" is derived from a Greek word meaning \"useful\" or \"profitable.\" But Onesimus had been a useless or unprofitable slave. The initial problem in Philemon\'s household was that Onesimus had caused some loss to Philemon, whether intentionally or unintentionally, through negligence, sloth or malice. And this problem was compounded by the resulting tension between Onesimus and Philemon, including probably Philemon\'s anger and intent to discipline Onesimus, and Onesimus\'s fear. And finally, it culminated in Onesimus\' flight from Philemon. Philemon may have assumed that Onesimus was a fugitive. But Onesimus\' true motives remained to be seen.\r\nUnder Roman law, a master had the right to punish slaves severely, even with heavy beatings. Onesimus fled in fear. God had a positive reason for allowing the situation. The law permitted slaves to leave their masters temporarily to find an advocate or mediator. If Onesimus fled in order to ask Paul to be his advocate and mediator with Philemon, he was not a fugitive.', 8, '2020-11-15 15:48:32', '2020-11-16 15:48:42', NULL),
(2193, 598, 919, '1. Onesimus\' Petition- Scripture provides some details suggesting that Onesimus sought out Paul as his advocate. He knew where Paul was- the church at Colosse had sponsored Epaphras\' mission to care for Paul in prison. He pursued  a meeting with Paul and pursued Paul\'s advocacy. Paul wrote to Philemon only after Onesimus had endeared himself to Paul: Paul converted Onesimus to Christianity. He ministered to Paul in prison. Some scholars conclude that Onesimus was seeking to start a new life far from Philemon, and encountered Paul only accidentally. \r\n2. Paul\'s Agreement- Philemon had the right to punish Onesimus. So, Paul defended Onesimus on the basis of mercy. Godly authority figures carry out appropriate punishments because it is the right thing to do. Onesimus initially stayed with Paul to convince the apostle of his good intent. During this time: Paul preached the gospel to Onesimus. The Holy Spirit brought Onesimus to faith in Christ. He might have remained with Paul without becoming a fugitive. Morally, this would not have been the best solution. The Christian values of charity and reconciliation demanded his return to Philemon.', 8, '2020-11-15 15:56:48', '2020-11-16 15:48:42', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2194, 598, 920, 'Paul presented his petition to Philemon in verses 8-21. The petition represents the main purpose of the letter, namely advocating on behalf of Onesimus before Philemon. Paul had the authority to order Philemon to do the right thing. Instead, he wrote to Philemon in ways that elicited Philemon\'s sympathy and concern. Paul spoke as a weak, elderly man in need of help. \r\nPaul wanted Philemon to respond to this situation with genuine Christian love. Compassion for an elderly man in prison. Compassion for the newly converted brother in Christ who ministered to him, We will explore the petition in some depth, breaking it down into the following six elements:\r\nAn explanation of Paul\'s role as advocate in verses 8-10\r\nAn explanation of Onesimus\'s role as petitioner in verses 11-12\r\nAn explanation of Philemon\'s role as master in verse 14\r\nAn explanation of God\'s role as providential ruler of the universe in verses 15-16\r\nPaul\'s petition itself in verses 17-20\r\n And Paul\'s statement of confidence that the petitions would be granted, in verse 21.', 9, '2020-11-15 16:03:15', '2020-11-16 15:48:42', NULL),
(2195, 598, 921, 'Paul said in Philemon 11-13, that Onesimus who was unprofitable in the past, but is now profitable to you and me, whom Paul has retained and in Philemon\'s absence, ministered him in the bonds of gospel. Paul appealed on the basis of mercy, because Onesimus had sinned and was guilty, hence Paul with mercy appealed for him. It is easier and better to appeal for mercy, as we all have sinned and have no right to make demands. God is extremely merciful and gracious to us. And in verse 12, \"He is very dear to me but even dearer to you, both as a man and as a brother in the Lord.\" Paul\'s high praise of Philemon as a deeply loving Christian, as well as Paul\'s strong advocacy for Onesimus, it appears that Paul expected this to be the outcome.', 8, '2020-11-15 16:20:07', '2020-11-16 15:48:42', NULL),
(2196, 599, 922, 'Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey, around the year A.D 48 or 50. Many of Paul\'s activities in Philippi are recorded in Acts 16:12-40, it was in Philippi that Paul gained his first convert in Europe, the merchant woman, Lydia. And it was in Philippi that he was jailed for an exorcism he performed on a slave girl. He led the Philippian jailer to a profession of faith in Christ. The church was poor and not always able to help Paul financially. But, when they had opportunity, they gave to him generously. Paul referred to the Philippians as his \"dear friends\" (2:12, 4:1).', 9, '2020-11-17 15:34:40', '2020-11-29 05:51:48', NULL),
(2197, 599, 923, 'We approach Paul\'s letters to the Philippians, we have questions like: Who were the Philippians? What was happening in their lives and in Paul\'s life? And why did Paul write to them? The answers to questions like these will help us to understand Paul\'s authoritative teaching in this letter, and to apply it to our own lives. \r\nAs we investigate the background to Paul\'s epistle to the Philippians, we will focus our attention on three matters: First, we will consider Paul\'s relationship with the Philippians. Second, we will mention some details pf Paul\'s suffering in prison and Third, we will explore the conditions in Philippi at the time Paul wrote to them.\r\nRelationship- Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey, around the year AD 49 or 50. The church was poor and not always able to help Paul financially. But when they had opportunity, they gave to him generously. \r\nSuffering in Prison- Paul often suffered greatly. He did not always bear up well under these hardships. At times, he was depressed, even despairing. He spoke frequently of death as welcome relief from his suffering. \r\nConcern for Paul- As soon as the Philippians were able, they demonstrated their concern by sending: A gift to meet Paul\'s earthly needs. Epaphroditus to deliver the gift and minister to Paul.', 9, '2020-11-17 15:43:50', '2020-11-29 05:51:48', NULL),
(2198, 599, 924, 'Paul instructed the Philippians to remain faithful to Christ and to live exemplary lives even in midst of distressing circumstances. \r\na. Importance of Perseverance:- God had planned the Philippians\' sufferings as a means of blessing them. Paul wanted the Philippians to rejoice in the midst of suffering because of the blessings it produces.\r\nb. Help for Perseverance:- It is much easier to endure suffering when we have real people helping us on a daily basis, and suffering right alongside us. Paul sent Epaphroditus back to them in order to ease their minds as well as to minister to them, planned to send Timothy to Philippi. Paul hoped that he would be released from prison, and would come to minister to the Philippians. \r\nc. Examples of Perseverance:- Paul was a positive example of perseverance in the faith: Mindset, Behavior Paul refused to rely on his earthly merits. He depended only on Christ\'s merit, which God credited to him by means of faith.\r\nd. Challenges to Perseverance- Paul encouraged the Philippians not to falter in their faithfulness to God because of: False teachers, Conflict within the church and Personal hardship.', 9, '2020-11-17 15:53:22', '2020-11-29 05:51:48', NULL),
(2199, 599, 925, 'A. Salutation- Paul is the primary author of the letter. The letter also comes from Timothy. Philippians is Paul\'s only letter that never calls explicit attention to the apostolic authority. \r\nB. Thanksgiving- Paul presents a fairly standard statement of thanks, speaking of; the joy the Philippians have brought to him. His expectations for their ultimate salvation. \r\nC. Prayer- Paul\'s prayer is packed with statements that reflect the emphasis of the entire letter: Make proper judgements, perform good works, persevere in faith and practice, bring glory and praise to God. \r\nD. Body- Paul let the Philippians know how much he loved them and how thankful he was for their friendship and ministry included Paul\'s perseverance: a. Present Ministry, 1:12-18- Paul persevered by finding reasons to be joyful despite his suffering. \r\nb. Future Deliverance, 1:18-21- He focused on the possibility that he might eventually be released from prison.\r\nc. Future Ministry, 1:22-26- He looked to the possibility of a future ministry to the Philippians as source of joy. \r\nE. Final Greetings- The mention of believers within Caesar\'s household demonstrates that Paul\'s imprisonment had not hindered his gospel ministry.', 9, '2020-11-17 16:01:43', '2020-11-29 05:51:48', NULL),
(2200, 599, 926, 'In my North Indian Ministries, from the beginning onwards, many people of the Lord, encouraged and motivated me, in different ways. When I was alone, they gave me a parental support, friendly guidance and financial support in my needs too. In church ministry, even after the tenure, the support and love of believers have been a great encouragement to my ministry. Many a times, the timely help from them has been a amazing and much needed help. They did not just send me money; but also their immense love. Being physically present is another good way to help persevere us. After many years, the brotherly love with us, coming to visit us and remembering us in their good times is always a blessing to me. It taught us how to persevere with sound doctrine and practical advice too.', 9, '2020-11-17 16:11:18', '2020-11-29 05:51:48', NULL),
(2201, 600, 842, NULL, 0, '2020-11-18 04:21:09', '2020-11-18 04:33:04', NULL),
(2202, 600, 843, NULL, 0, '2020-11-18 04:34:01', '2020-11-18 04:51:52', NULL),
(2204, 600, 845, NULL, 0, '2020-11-18 04:59:03', '2020-11-18 05:11:56', NULL),
(2206, 600, 847, NULL, 0, '2020-11-18 05:20:08', '2020-11-18 05:32:27', NULL),
(2208, 600, 849, 'In the Old Testament, the law can be understood in 3 dimensions: Ceremonial, civil, and moral. God\'s laws were given to help people love God with all their hearts and minds. When Jesus talked about a new way to understand God\'s law, he was actually trying to bring people back to its original purpose. Jesus did not speak against the law itself but against the abuses and excesses to which it had been subjected.\r\n\r\nChristians have typically insisted that the moral aspects of the Torah remain relevant for example, the Ten Commandments, but not the ceremonial, legal, or ritualistic aspect. But it is necessary to keep in mind that this material is Scripture. Throughout its long history, the Church has taught that the meaning of scripture is never exhausted by a literal or historical reading.', 8, '2020-11-18 05:39:46', '2020-11-29 05:52:44', NULL),
(2203, 600, 844, 'The most basic elements of an ethical situation as facts. There are two special kinds of facts that are particularly important in ethics. First, goals, which are the intended or potential outcomes of our thoughts, words, and deeds. And second, we talked about means, which are the ways we reach our goals. \r\n\r\nEthics has to do with our choices and actions, which form our character even as they express it. Ethics depends on that human habit of reflection that takes into account our interests and values as well as those of others in the process of deciding and doing. Since it calls on perceptive thought, analytical and intuitive reasoning, and prudential judgment, ethics is perhaps more art than science - the art of choosing well and wisely for the good of self and others.', 8, '2020-11-18 04:44:41', '2020-11-29 05:52:44', NULL),
(2205, 600, 846, 'Jesus\'s sermon begins with a description of those who would be blessed by God. For instance,  \" Blessed  are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled \" Matthew 5:6 \r\nThe verse beginning with the word \"blessed\" is commonly called \"Beatitudes\" - meaning a state of supreme happiness. In essence, Jesus is stating that these perspectives would become the mental states of mind for all who would become His disciples. \r\n\r\nChrist taught that His disciples were to be the salt of the earth and light of the world. They were commanded, \" Let your light to shine before men, that they may see your good works and glorify your Father in heaven\". Christians\' examples are to reflect God\'s righteousness to the world around them; and by doing so, their actions would shine like a light.', 8, '2020-11-18 05:10:38', '2020-11-29 05:52:44', NULL),
(2207, 600, 848, 'The Kingdom of God, also called Kingdome of Heaven, is Christianity, the spiritual realm over which God reigns as king, or the fulfillment of Earth of God\'s will.  The phrase occurs frequently in the New Testament, primarily used by Jesus Christ in the first three Gospels. It is generally considered to be the central theme of Jesus\' teaching, but widely differing views have been held about Jesus teaching on the Kingdom of God and its relation to the developed view of the church. \r\n\r\nAccording to the first three Gospels, most of Jesus\' miraculous actions are to be understood as prophetic symbols of the coming of the Kingdom, and his teaching was concerned with the right response to the crisis of its coming. The nationalistic tone of much of the Jewish expectation is absent from the teaching of Jesus.', 9, '2020-11-18 05:28:46', '2020-11-29 05:52:44', NULL),
(2209, 603, 786, 'Human beings are physical representations that remind all creation about God\'s power authority and goodness. \r\n\r\nThe office of God is rooted in the authority God delegated to humanity. God appointed human beings to rule over his creation on his behalf.\r\n\r\nThe nature and our role is first introduced in \r\nGenesis 1:27-28\r\nGod created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him male and female, he created them. \r\nGod blessed them and said \"Be fruitful and increase in number - fill the earth and subdue it. \r\n\r\nGod appointed us humans not only as images of God, but also to govern creation. Important aspect of being God\'s image is that we hold the office of delegated ruler. In theological terms, we are God\'s vice regents. We are also known as God\'s servant or vassal kings.', 8, '2020-11-25 16:09:33', '2020-11-29 05:53:14', NULL),
(2210, 603, 787, 'Scripture teaches us in Genesis 1:26 \r\nGod said \"Let us make man in our image, in our likeness and let them rule over the rest of creation,\r\n\r\nThis scripture teaches that humanity is created in the image and likeness of God\r\n\r\nEverything that humans are, everything that we do images God.\r\n\r\nWe are in the likeness of God ; so its a representational dynamic, not a static copy of himself. When God wanted to make a being that represented him, he made humanity.\r\n\r\nThe meaning of image and likeness overlap because they both desribe an image of greater relaity. Human beings are image and likeness of God because we model God\'s power, authority and goodness. \r\n\r\nGod has appointed humans to rule over the earth as God\'s servant kings and are empowered with necessary qualities and abilities to perform our duties. \r\n\r\nOur role or office as image of God elevates all humanity to the level of royalty. God has appointed us the task of administering his rule throughtout the earth. And that office gives us dignity. Also we need to treat each other with appropriate degree of respect and honor.', 8, '2020-11-25 16:18:02', '2020-11-29 05:53:14', NULL),
(2211, 603, 788, 'Some of our rational ability was lost in our fall into sin, as evidenced in many biblical passages that speak of human beings being irrational and even insane at times.\r\n\r\nIn Deuteronomy 29:2, 3 states that the Israelites could not understand the significance of the miracles God had performed for them.\r\n\r\nAlso as it states in Titus 1:15\r\nTo those who are corrupted and do not believe, nothing is pure. In fact both their minds and consciences are corrupted\r\n\r\nFallen human beings cannot properly evaluate good and evil.\r\nHumans lost the ability to act in moral ways (They claim to know God, but by their actions they deny him) - Titus 1:16\r\nSinful mind is hostile to God, it does not submit to God\'s law,  they are controlled by sinful nature and hence annot please God\r\nHumanity lost the original righteousness and holiness\r\nGod\'s image and likeness in humanity were damaged, but not destroyed.\r\nHumans became irrational and even insane due to the fall\r\n\r\nPaul speaks to us through Colossians 3:10 about the restoration of our rational abilities to Put on the new self, which is being renewed in knowledge in the image of its creator.\r\n\r\nWhen we come to faith in Christ, God begins to restore that aspect of his image in us. As a result we are able to think and understand more correctly, so that our thoughts and reason become more aligned with his.', 9, '2020-11-25 16:35:22', '2020-11-29 05:53:14', NULL),
(2212, 603, 789, 'God created humanity, he entered into a covenant relationship. The covenant between a great emperor or suzerain - God and a vassal or servant king, human beings. \r\n\r\nThe three important aspects of our covenant relationship with God are as follows :\r\n\r\nOur obligation to REFLECT GOD\'S CHARACTER :\r\nDuty to promote PURE WORSHIP \r\nResponsibility to build GOD\'S KINGDOM\r\n\r\n1) Reflect God\'s Character\r\nGod\'s character is utterly pure, holy and righteous, as a result human images are required to reflect the character of God.\r\n\r\nApostle Peter says, Just as he who called you is holy, so be holy.\r\n\r\nNo matter how hard we try to obey God, we need to fully rely on Christ\'s perfect holiness for our standing before God and God still requires us to pursue holiness in our lives by keeping his commandments.\r\n\r\nWe are obligated to pursue holiness and righteousness with our whole person. \r\n\r\n2) Promote Pure Worship.\r\nThough human beings are the real images of God, but our fallen intuition might suggest that it would honor God to worship him through crafted images. \r\n\r\nScripture rejects this idea, God gave clear instructions to Moses that God does not reveal himself in a physical form because he wanted to protect the purity of their worship. He wanted to keep their relationship with God, pure, unadulterated by the idolatrous theology and practices of the surrounding nations. He didn\'t want them to think that God could be spirituall bound by an obect. God is a true God and not to be treated similar to the false gods of the nations\r\n\r\n3) Build God\'s Kingdom\r\nWhen God commanded humanity to fill the earth, God wanted human beings to be spread throughout the world, and demonstrate God\'s presence through our represenation to establish God\'s rule everywhere they go. All human beings are tasked with building God\'s kingdom.', 9, '2020-11-25 16:55:03', '2020-11-29 05:53:14', NULL),
(2213, 603, 790, 'When God created man - in his own image and likeness, God blessed them and said to them to be fruitful and increase in number, fill the earth and subdue it.\r\n\r\nAs God\'s images we as human beings, our task is to fill and subdue the earth, and to rule over its creatures. We are appointed by God as vice regent, as vassal kings to rule over the rest of the creation of God. \r\n\r\nGod epxectes us humans to spread beyond the Garden\'s borders into the rest of the world and to subdue it as they went turning the entire world into God\'s garden sanctuary \r\n\r\nPersonally I feel this is a great task and resposibility and equally an opportunity that God gives each individual in his lifetime to represent alminghty God and to treat others with dignity and justice.\r\n\r\nAs per the ability and talent God has given, to use our time wisely and to minister to others, in serving others and maintaining the dignity and justice that God wants us to treat others. \r\n\r\nEvery human being is valuable to God since we bear his image and irrespective of their background or circumstances each person deserves to be treated with dignity and respect. Every person represents God in some way. And to mistreat God\'s responsibilities is to insult God himself.\r\n\r\nAll human beings represent God, the way we treat other human beings indicated very much our relationship with God. To the extend that we honor other human beings we are honoring God their maker. \r\n\r\nScripture also directls us to uphold justice towards everyone.\r\n\r\nWhen God appointed humanity to build his kingdome he wanted everyone to apply that law fairly and justly.\r\n\r\nOur role as God\'s servant kings obligates us to preserve justice and to show responsbility of maintaining equality and justice for our fellow human beings.\r\n\r\nPreserving justice for all humanity is an important part of our role as God\'s images.', 9, '2020-11-25 17:11:12', '2020-11-29 05:53:14', NULL),
(2214, 601, 850, 'Ethics is the science of morality. Its task is to examine the moral aspect of human nature and behavior, to clarify issues in moral decision making and moral dilemmas, and to facilitate the formation of moral character.  In Micah 6:8 we find a helpful definition of morality. It can be argued on the information found in that passage that morality asks four questions. Not what is normal, legal, or acceptable in a given culture, but what is good. Human beings are born with the rights, to which they have God-given rights. Immoral conduct consists of denying these rights to our fellow human beings. Our relationships and our inter-human conduct must be kind and compassionate.', 7, '2020-11-26 03:12:43', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2215, 601, 851, 'In some ways, making biblical decisions requires to identify many factual details. And we also have to figure out how all these facts related to the ethical questions. Throughout many studies, biblical decisions have been that ethical judgement involves the application of God\'s word to a situation by a person.  This outlook on ethics has reminded us there are three major perspectives to be taken on every ethical issue. A focus on God\'s word, which we have called the normative perspective; a focus on the person, which we have called the existential perspective; and a focus on the situation, which we have called the situation perspective.  The importance of considering facts about God, the world around us and human beings when we make ethical decisions.', 7, '2020-11-26 03:29:02', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2216, 601, 852, 'The development of Christianity\'s influence on the character of society since the  Reformation has been two-fold.  In the realm of state churches and territorial churches, Christianity contributed to the preservation of the status quo of society. In England, the Anglican church remained an all of the thrones, as did the Protestant churches of German states.  in Russia the Orthodox church continue to support a social order founded upon the monarchy, and even the monarch carried out a leading function within the church as a protector. \r\n\r\nThroughout the impulses for the transformation of the social order according to the spirit of Christian ethic came more strongly from the Free churches, state and territorial churches make positive contributions in improving the status quo.', 7, '2020-11-26 03:37:19', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2217, 601, 853, 'Revealed theology is concerned with knowledge about God that is available by way of some special divine revelation, distinct from anything we know by the philosophical arguments. It has a source that is supernatural in the sense of being beyond what the natural order of things is capable of producing. The supernatural encompasses all entities, places, and events that have been invoked to explain phenomena as diverse as lighting, seasons, and the human senses which today are understood.  Supernatural is the more familiar word and is used to describe phenomena or occurrences that imaginary, magical or unreal. Preternatural is a less common word. It is used to mean more, sooner or later than usual or beyond the usual experience. Something happening preternaturally is not always imaginary.', 8, '2020-11-26 03:52:50', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2218, 601, 854, 'The creation mandate is the on-going charge to humanity, in the power and blessing of God, to be fruitful, multiply, and fill the earth and to gently subdue and cultivate the earth.  The creation mandate was given to Adam and Eve within the narrative of the original creation, Alongside the prohibition against eating of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, humanity was blessed with God\'s presence and told to be fruitful, multiply, and fill the earth and to gently subdue and cultivate the earth. Although sin and separation from God followed closely on the heels of this original charge, the creation mandate has been reiterated to the people of God throughout scripture. The final and ultimate reiteration of this mandate is the Great Commission, which Jesus gave to his disciples before his ascension.', 8, '2020-11-26 04:00:49', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2219, 601, 855, 'The basic mission of the church is to share the love of Christ with the world in the hopes everyone will come into a relationship with Jesus. The challenge is that unchurched people are not exactly flocking to most churches and many Christians seem stumped as to why that is. As we consider God\'s being, it is important to remember that all of God\'s essential attributes are immutable, meaning that they can never change. For instance, God cannot be holy one day but unholy the next. He cannot be all-powerful and all-knowing at certain times but limited in his power and knowledge at other times. \r\nWhen we speak about God\'s goodness in the context of ethics, we have in mind his moral purity and perfection.', 7, '2020-11-26 04:13:44', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2220, 601, 856, 'Our God is gloriously incomprehensible.  God is infinite. God has no limits or boundaries. He is not confined to the dimension of space. His love, holiness, mercy, and all His other qualities are unlimited in their scope and expression. God is self-existent, he had no beginning. Because He is the creator, He is the only one who exists outside of the created order. God is eternal. With God, everything that has ever happened or will ever happen has already occurred within His awareness. God encompasses all of eternity.  God is self-sufficient.  God is gloriously incomprehensible, but He is also a personal Sprit. Through His infinite love and goodness. He has provided a way for us to have an authentic, personal relationship with Him. God destroyed the barrier of sin and self-centeredness that separated us from our holy God. God sent the Holy Spirit to dwell in Christians with His presence. Our creator God did al this so we could go beyond just knowing about Him. We can actually enter into a warm friendship and intimate family relationship with Him. What a gracious God we have in Jesus!!', 8, '2020-11-26 04:27:17', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2221, 601, 857, 'God\'s forgiveness and His acceptance are immediate. It\'s a lifelong process of weeding out all the artifacts of iniquity that still remain in us. The long process of sanctification, of personal transformation, is an argument for the power and the depth of sin in our hearts. It\'s humbling to know how long God must work on us in a variety of situations, in a variety of locations, day after day, moment after moment, to get at all of the roots of sin that still live inside us. Praise the Lord for His patience and faithfulness. Praise the Lord that he would continue to exercise the power. We are not done yet because the Lord continues to work on us.', 7, '2020-11-26 04:38:33', '2020-11-29 05:54:26', NULL),
(2222, 595, 766, 'Peter\'s affirmation of Jesus\' anointing to the office of Christ was a direct revelation from god. But Peter\'s confession as the spokesman for the apostles was more than that . it was an authoritative prophetic revelation from God.  It was an infallible confirmation of the fact that Jesus really was the Christ.\r\nTransfiguration:\r\n  It is the event when Jesus was revealed to his disciples in glory. It refers to the fact that his appearance was radically transformed, revealing a portion of his divine glory. Mathew 17:1-8\r\nwhile Jesus \'s appearance was altered , Moses and Elijah appeared with  him, and God\'s voice was heard from heaven, affirming that Jesus was His son.\r\nGod singled out Jesus as being worthy of the greatest honor and obedience. Jesus stood ij continuity with the law and the prophets. Its also meant that he was the greatest anointed one of all and the final Davidic heir who was the bring God\'s kingdom to earth.\r\nit affirms that Jesus was Christ.', 8, '2020-11-26 17:44:30', '2020-11-29 05:54:58', NULL),
(2223, 595, 767, 'First , His temptation taught him obedience:\r\nobedience:\r\nJesu was entirely sinless, he never disobeyed God. but he was also fully and truly human. so he had to learn God\'s righteous requirements and to overcome challenges and temptations throughout his life. Jesus obeyed God both by keeping the requirements of his law, and by submitting to the father\'s plan for his life. and this obedience prepared him for his work as Christ\r\nHebrews 5:8,9\r\nSympathy:\r\nJesus recognized that the things Satan offered him were desirable and his weakened condition from fasting must have amplified his longing for them. And this experience caused him to grow in compassion and understanding for us as we suffer and struggle with temptation in our own lives\r\nHebrews 4:15\r\nImpeccability:\r\nthe word impeccability means inability to sin.\r\nbut because of his human nature, Jesus experienced temptations from a human perspective. He recognized the value of the things offered to him, and keenly understood the benefits they could provide him. Jesus is impeccable, his obedience and sympathy are actually increased , since he learned perfectly from the experience and now responds to us in away that is perfectly suited to our needs.', 8, '2020-11-26 17:57:22', '2020-11-29 05:54:58', NULL),
(2224, 595, 768, 'Jesus Crucifixion was unique because it served as a atoning offering for sin. \r\nAs the Christ it was his responsibility to die on behalf if his people, Hebrews 9:11-21\r\nimputation: it simply means assignment or reckoning.  we are referring to the act in which God assigned the guilt of sinners to the person of Jesus.  but from a legal perspective God counted Jesus as if he had personally committed every sin imputed to him. Jesus offered himself on the cross as a substitute for his people. Bible often refers to him as a sacrifice Romans 3:25. He is also called our ransom in places like Matthew 20:28\r\nJudgement:\r\nJesus death was also a divine judgment against sin\r\nBefore God laid our guilt on him, Jesus couldn\'t die, but our sin was imputed to him on the cross, his death became not only punishable but necessary. It was the only just response God could make to such tremendous guilt.', 8, '2020-11-26 18:08:46', '2020-11-29 05:54:58', NULL),
(2225, 595, 769, 'God\'s anointed Christ conquered death for all of us, when he rose from the grave, through the power of the Spirit, he proved to all creation that he really is  God\'s favored son and the heir to his kingdom.\r\nhe ensured the future resurrection and blessing of all his faithful followers.\r\nPlan of redemption:\r\nGod\'s plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depended on him fulfilling his covenant promises to establish his kingdom on earth under the kingship of a descendant of David, otherwise known as the Christ. Jesus resurrection was a critical step that enabled God to fulfill his covenant promise . this is one of the reason that New Testament calls the resurrection  as an affirmation of Jesus\' role as Christ. Luke 24:45,46\r\nBlessings of salvation:\r\nThe new testament associates Jesus resurrection as a wide variety of blessings, we receive as part of our salvation, it results in our justification, regeneration, opens door to our eternal inheritance, produces good works, and is a true witness to Christ in our bodies and lives. Its the source of the future bodily resurrection of believers.', 9, '2020-11-26 18:21:25', '2020-11-29 05:54:58', NULL),
(2226, 595, 770, 'Jesus governs all things- the churches, nations, creation and the Spiritual world of angels and demons. His rule doesn\'t always seems predictable to us, but he rules according to god\'s hidden plan. The Bible assures as that because of Christ\' rule over all, his followers have nothing to fear. Our eventually victory is assured, nothing can happen to us, that\'s beyond his control and authority. Everything that exists is under his authority and power. From the workings of the entire  universe to the tiniest subatomic particle\r\nJesus is the Christ of my life  , our ministry and our family.\r\nHe rules every aspect of my life. He is the king over all our circumstances  and all areas of our life.\r\nHe controls everything and so there is nothing to fear.\r\nhe has the authority and power of everything that surround us both visible and invisible.', 9, '2020-11-26 18:31:14', '2020-11-29 05:54:58', NULL),
(2227, 606, 858, 'Our paradigm for making biblical decisions has been that ethical judgment involves the application of God\'s word to a situation by a person. When we look at our choices in the light of the norms of God\'s word, we are using the normative perspective. When we pay attention to circumstances, we are using the situation perspective.   And when we consider the persons involved in ethical questions, we are using the existential perspective. In order to please God, people must loyal to the Lord and do the right thing for the right reason. Their motives must be righteous. The loving allegiance of God in terms of loyalty, orientation, and responsibility is a critical aspect of biblical ethics.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:10:26', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2228, 606, 859, 'Jesus laid down his life in order to ensure grace and freedom for everyone who believes in Him. When Jesus dies for our sins, He gave us the freedom to choose the grace He has made available to us. When we choose to accept his gift of grace that our Father has freely given to us, we are cloaked in it and that is all God sees when He looks at us. He sees people cloaked in His grace as perfect, and that gives us freedom.  Our freedom in Christ is wholly dependent on His grace.  The grace our Heavenly Father has given us allows us freedom from the eternal consequences of our sin. Grace gives us the freedom to forgive those who hurt us and to love others unconditionally, things we could not do before.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:18:16', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2229, 606, 860, 'God has called us to the obedience that wells up from the heart into our outward actions. God-honoring obedience originates in the heart of the believers. It flows outwardly to our walk. Jesus often makes this point when he spoke to the religious leaders of his day. He accused them of being whitewashed sepulchers. They were clean on the outside but full of dead bones on the inside. He also told them that they were like cups that were outwardly clean but inwardly putrid. As Paul taught the Romans he provided the measure against which to measure our heart. That measure of the standard is the teaching to which you were committed. To be godly obedient is to inwardly desire obedience and outwardly do obedience. We need God\'s help to renew our hearts so that we walk in life what reflects a clean heart in step with the biblical standard.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:29:09', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2230, 606, 861, 'Faith and belief are the most important in personal belief a may be motivated to adopt and maintain. God knows the best way to answer a prayer and He selects the best time to provide an answer. The prayer beliefs tend to be reinforced through prayer groups and informal support from fellow church members. The strong trust-based prayer beliefs as associated with a greater sense of life satisfaction over time.  It pleased God, in his goodness and wisdom, to reveal himself and to make known the mystery of his will. which was that people can draw near to the Father, through Christ, the Word made flesh in the Holy Spirit, and thus become shares in his divine life.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:38:44', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2231, 606, 862, 'Having told the story of God\'s work of creation, Genesis moves on to tell the story of human work. Everything is grounded on God\'s creation of people in his own image. All creation displays God\'s design, power, and goodness, but only human beings are said to be made in God\'s image. God is a creator who works in the material world, who works in a relationship, and whose work observes limits.  When Jesus entered the grave and defeated death three days later, he exited the tomb with a path to restoration. Through his resurrection, Jesus initiated the curse reversal of creation. Through Jesus, all of creation is being brought to new, so that we can be the caretakers and gardeners of the created potential.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:46:41', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2232, 606, 863, 'Ethical decisions generate and sustain trust; demonstrate respect, responsibility, fairness, and caring; Unethical decisions often result from faulty rationalization and external pressure. Ethical values are influenced by the culture and upbringing of each individual. Ethical scenarios introduce both correct and incorrect ethical decisions and allow us to learn the complexity of ethics.  Ethics should concern all levels of life: acting properly as individuals, creating a responsible organization. The decision about \"right\" and \"wrong\" can be difficult and may be related to the individual context. Ethics provides a set of standards for behavior that helps us decide how we ought to act in a range of situations. Ethics is all about making choices, and about providing a reason why we should make these choices.', 7, '2020-11-28 03:57:07', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2233, 606, 864, 'Decision making requires identifying the factors related to it. It helps create a clear understanding of what needs to be decided and can influence the choice between alternatives. An important aspect of any decision is its purpose or objective. This is different from identifying a specific decision outcome; It is a good idea to be able to approach decision definition from a different perspective. Doing so can capture dimensions of the issue that might otherwise have been overlooked. Involving two or more people can bring different information, knowledge, and experience to a decision. Having a shared definition and understanding of a decision helps the decision-making process by creating a focus for discussion and making them more efficient.', 7, '2020-11-28 04:08:37', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2234, 606, 865, 'A committed person is simply one who regularly keeps their commitments. People spontaneously indicate that both commitments and committed people are highly uncommon in their life. Our words reveal your heart. Bible says that words speak loud enough by themselves. It is out of the overflow of the heart that the mouth speaks. It is the tongue that sets the body on fire. It is not what goes into a man that makes him unclean, what comes out of him. We are always able to justify our actions and give an excuse for behavior. If we really want an honest glimpse into our own hearts, we must listen to our words. Jesus told us in Luke 6, that all people bear fruit. Good fruit comes from those with good hearts, and evil is produced from those with evil hearts.', 7, '2020-11-28 04:24:32', '2020-11-29 05:55:29', NULL),
(2235, 604, 638, 'Individual eschatology is the study of how human beings experience the events of the last days , especially with regards to life , death, the intermediate state  and the final state of our bodies and souls. \r\nIn theology the term \"regenerate\" means recreated or reborn . Those who are regenerate are the spiritual beings .\r\nThe term unregenerate refers to the \" not created\" or \" not reborn\". \r\nThe \" unregenerate\" are spiritually \"dead\". \r\nThe fallen human beings are born spiritually dead, the unregenerate remain in this state of spiritual death because they haven\'t been renewed or regenerated by the Holy Spirit .\r\nThey have spiritual death, moral inability and enmity with God.\r\nRegenerate are those who have been born again through the work of the Holy Spirit. when that happens our spirits are regenerated or given new life.\r\nThey have Spiritual life, moral ability, reconciliation to God.', 8, '2020-11-30 17:50:45', '2020-12-05 17:04:22', NULL),
(2236, 604, 639, 'description of Spiritual death:\r\nwhen we die physically , we are incapable of independent action. but when we die spiritually , our souls are still active in our bodies. the unregenerate continue to think, feel dream , make choices and change the world in almost every way the regenerate do. God cursed humanity with spiritual death, they didn\'t die that day, at least no physically, but they spiritually corrupt. and this spiritual corruption is the essence spiritual death. Romans 7:14-25. sin lives inside our very bodies and even takes control of our minds.\r\nspiritual death effects all the naturally conceived descendants of Adam and Eve except for Jesus ,every human being come sin tot the world spiritually dead.\r\nRomans 8:10\r\nAugustine\'s states of moral ability\r\nhe taught that before humanity\'s first sin , Adam and Eve lived in the state of Posse non peccare\" to be able not to sin \" or\" the ability not to sin\"\r\nand when they sinned , they moved from state of posse non peccare to the state of non posse non peccare  - the inability not to sin. \r\n they and all their naturally generated descendants lost the moral ability to avoid sin.', 9, '2020-11-30 18:06:12', '2020-12-05 17:04:22', NULL),
(2237, 604, 640, 'Regenerate are those who have been born again through the works of the Holy spirit. in order for us to receive God\'s blessings like salvation we have to undergo a second birth- spiritual one\r\nJohn 3;3-6\r\nJesus taught that we can inky have spiritual life if the holy spirit gives birth to our spirits Titus 3:5\r\nwhen the holy Spirit regenerates us , our souls from death to life. and that life never ends. even when our earthly bodies die, our souls will live forever.\r\nMoral ability:\r\nthe term moral ability refers to the capacity to please god and merit his blessings. Augustine taught that when the Holy Spirit regenerates our souls, giving us spiritual life, he revitalizes our moral ability. He moves us back into the state of posse non peccare. the regenerate regain the ability to avoid sin and to please God.\r\nGod prepares good works for the regenerates proves that we have regained our moral ability. The holy Spirit regenerates us , but hasn\'t completely removed the sin\'s corruption or influence from our lives. Sin that lives in us still battles, the Spirit that dwells in us , works in us, we also do good works  because of the spirit\'s influence.', 8, '2020-11-30 18:19:40', '2020-12-05 17:04:22', NULL),
(2238, 604, 641, 'the unsaved the death is an unmitigated catastrophe , it takes them from bad to worse and gives them a foretaste of even greater suffering in the future.\r\n when our souls die they don\'t cease to exist., they continue to exist , think and feel even after our bodies die. For the unregenerate the fact that their souls continue to exist should cause great fear, because when their spirit returns to god, who give it, it is not to enjoy his blessings, kit is to begin suffering their just punishment in hell.\r\nLuke 12:4-5\r\nScripture uses generic term \"hell\", Hebrew word Sheol psalms 9\"17 as a reference to the home f the unregenerate souls. Abbaddon in Job 26:6 means destruction , bowr in Isaiah 14:15 refers to a place of punishment.\r\nthe Greek word hades Luke 10:15 refers to a place of punishment. abyss refers to a prison for demons in Luke 8:31\r\nThe word geeena or \"gehenna\" defines a place of fiery punishment , torment and destruction.\r\nMathew 5:22. James 3:6 suggests Gehenna to the place where unregenerate souls go during the intermediate state.\r\nthe clearest picture we get  of how unregenerate souls experience the intermediate state come from Luke  16:19-32', 9, '2020-11-30 18:34:56', '2020-12-05 17:04:22', NULL),
(2239, 604, 642, 'The regenerate souls have Lord\'s presence.\r\nwhen the regenerate die, their souls are immediately taken into God\'s presence in heaven .Luke 23:43 Jesus says to penitent thief, Revelation 6:9 souls of the Martyrs Paul looked forward to physical death as the  time that his soul would depart to be with Jesus. 2Corinthians 5:8\r\n when the regenerate die, their bodies rest in the grave, but their soul are immediately taken until resurrections. Some Christians have taught \" soul sleep\" , this is the idea that god doesn\'t take our souls to heaven immediately when we die, instead our souls remain with our bodies , unconscious until Jesus\' resurrection . Daniel 12:2\r\nA second experience regenerate souls have is fellowship with other regenerate souls in heaven. Revelation 6:9\r\nthe third experience of regenerate souls during the intermediate sate is that the reach perfect holiness. when our souls are separated from our bodies by physical death, sin loses its hold on us and we eagerly avoid all sin from that point on. Hebrews 12:23, because they are no longer corrupted by sin.', 9, '2020-11-30 18:49:43', '2020-12-05 17:04:22', NULL),
(2240, 605, 927, 'The word Trinity simply means -Tri -unity- three in one. So the doctrine of the trinity can be defined in this way, God is three divine person in one divine sense. this concept is also found in the related terms Trinitarian and Triune God. One God reveals who God is in terms of Father, Son and Holy Spirit, the three persons- not three people, but the three persons of the One God.\r\nso we have , the Father who sends the Son, the son who , after his death and resurrection, goes to the Father, and they send the Spirit, so , we can think about God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit.\r\nBefore the Creation . God was already Father, Son and the Holy Spirit, one God in three persons.\r\nperhaps the most direct way unto understanding this  is there\'s only One God in three persons.\r\nIn Christian theology, we talk about god as Triune- the Trinity, one in three and three in one\r\nGod\'s essence is one , his person is three.\r\nwhen we say that God is one divine essence, we are affirming that, He is the one uncreated Creator, according to Romans 1:25. there is one uncreated Creator and all other things in the universe are part of God\'s created order.\r\nThe doctrine of the trinity is concerned with the persons of the trinity within the one divine essence or God head.', 9, '2020-12-01 17:43:15', '2020-12-14 21:48:41', NULL),
(2241, 605, 928, 'When we speak of three persons, we are referring to the distinct person of the Father, Son and Holy spirit. We can define the word persons as a  distinct self aware personality, existing in relation ship with other persons. according to Dr. Daniel Terrier, God\'s personhood is what should define ours . the modern concept of person as a distinct ego, an individual self -consciousness attached to a separated body and a particular personal name that no other shares.\r\n if we are to apply the concept of  individual person  to the persons of the triune god, We would risk tritheism, . So whatever we mean, by person, we don\'t mean the modern concept of an individual , focused on really, the concept of personality.\r\nthe reality in certain New Testament passages, when Father, Son , Holy Spirit speak to about each other. John 17\r\nthe Father isn\'t just conscious of the Father as a self, but is conscious of himself in relation to the /son an din relation to the Spirit\r\nso communicative agency seems to describe the relation of father, Son and the Spirit as persons to each other.\r\nthey seem to have reciprocal self consciousness that undergirds the other major principle of Trinitarian theology, the unity of divine action ad extra- the trinity of God\'s action in his  relation to the  world.', 9, '2020-12-01 18:00:51', '2020-12-14 21:48:41', NULL),
(2242, 605, 929, 'Tritheism violates the biblical doctrine of divine simplicity- that God is indivisibly one- but the doctrine of God\'s threeness does not .God is three divine persons, He is only one divine essence . his essence cannot be divide into parts. he is indivisibly one.\r\nRev Valery Zadorozhny says that God\'s simplicity is a very interesting notion because, on the one hand, the Bible says that God is inconceivable. That is  a man cannot analyze and sort him out. and on the other hand theology contains the concept of God\'s simplicity.\r\nAccording to Rev Dr. Paul R Raabe, God the First person, has always been the father of his Son, and His Son has always been the Son of his father, and there has always has been the Spirit of the Father and the spirit of the son and the three are inseparable. there is only one God head , one divine nature that\'s indivisible , three persons.\r\nthe very Reve Justin Terry if we read Mathew 10, we see there- the disciple who has the Spirit of god in them- its not just receiving that disciple, they receive the Son of god, when they receive the Son of god, they receive the Father. It\'s one God in three persons.', 8, '2020-12-01 18:16:19', '2020-12-14 21:48:41', NULL),
(2243, 605, 930, 'According to Dr. Carl R Trueman, Christ is the referred as the firstborn over all creation. Firstborn is not a statement about chronological priority. it is actually a statement of what we might call, ontological priority or supremacy. for example, when Moses goes to pharaoh he calls the people of  Israel God\'s firstborn, not ,meaning they were, first tribe on the face of the earth, but they are in God\'s eyes.\r\nDr. Lynn Cohick says that Colossians 1:15-17 Paul explaining how Jesus Christ is God\'s image, he represents God the Father. Paul also speaks of Jesus here as the Firstborn over all creation. and Paul connects Jesus then , with creation. in him, creation happened, and through Him, then, creation stands, exists, continues.\r\nHe is establishing here that both the Father and Son are worthy of our worship that they together along with Holy Spirit accomplish our salvation. \r\nwe have one God and one Lord and that Lord Jesus1 Corinthians 8\r\nDr. Gareth Cockerill says that Colossians 1:15 Jesus is called the firstborn it means  that he is the prince , the one Sovereign over and eventually the heir.\r\nGod has appointed Him the heir of all things and as the /son of God, heir ship is the natural outcome of sonship.', 8, '2020-12-01 18:32:43', '2020-12-14 21:48:41', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2244, 605, 931, 'Every Trinitarian formula found in Scriptures mentions the holy spirit as an equal member of the trinity, together with Father and the Son.\r\nAccording to Rev  Dennies E John son Trinitarian formulas of the New Testament help as to understand that the Holy spirit is fully divine and fully personal. to take two examples, in the Great ?commission , Jesus commands us to baptize people in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the holy Spirit. @ Corinthians 13 the grace of  our Lord Jesus Christ, the Love of God, the and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit, there\'s a blessing in the name of the Triune God\r\nAccording to Dr. Steve Mc Kinion , the Spirit has a very important role in God\'s work, in the economy of god. When god determines that He will create, its not just the Father and the Son, its the Spirit that we even find in Genesis, whos is hovering over the waters.\r\nin the incarnation, it sis the Spirit who comes on Mary ,so that she can conceive and bear the Son.\r\n\'When it comes to Jesus being lifted up on the cross, he is commending his spirit. He is giving his spirit, giving up his spirit.', 8, '2020-12-01 18:47:02', '2020-12-14 21:48:41', NULL),
(2245, 602, 633, 'The Word eschatology comes from the Greek words: eshcatos, meaning, \"last\" or \"final\" and logos in this case means study . So eschatology is the study of last things or the doctrine of the last things. Eschatology covers the entire period of the last days that began with Jesus\' \' life and ministry and that will be completed when he returns. Finally the Eschaton is the ultimate state of the universe, after redemption is complete, when god\'s heavenly kingdom fills the earth. for thousand s of years , the old Testament looked forward to the last days as that time when God would bring his kingdom on the earth. The Jewish Eschatology held that world history could be divided into two great ages. They called the first age \"this age\"- or Olam hazech in hebrew. It was characterized by sin, Exile, suffering and death. this is started when Adam and Even fell into Sin and it correspond s to the age of redemption.\r\nthe second age or ages to come or olam Haba , this was the the future eschaton when God\'s kingdom would fill the earth.it would be characterized by forgiveness, righteousness, peace and eternal life.', 8, '2020-12-03 17:49:59', '2020-12-14 21:48:16', NULL),
(2246, 602, 634, 'according to Genesis 1 and 2, God intended humanity to be is vassals or servant king. Our Job was to rule over creation on behalf of God, the great suzerain or emperor. Human beings were created in the image of god, when God created human beings, in his image, He indicated that His plan was to create an Earthly Kingdoms. \r\nTheologians refers this to cultural mandate because they require humanity to build culture throughout the whole world. that means snot only reproducing to create enough people to fill the world, but also creating human culture throughout the world- things like families and Governments, farming and animal husbandry, arts and sciences.\r\nSo the combined picture of humanity\'s role as servant kings in Genesis 1 and as priestly workers in Chapter2 tells us that human beings are God\'s role and priestly images.\r\nOur job is to extend his rule and until it fills the world and the to cultivate the whole earth and until everything resembles the Garden of Eden.', 8, '2020-12-03 18:00:25', '2020-12-14 21:48:16', NULL),
(2247, 602, 635, 'The Story of humanity\'s fall into sin recorded in Genesis 3. God put Adam and Eve in the Garden to work it and to take care of it. but Satan spoke through the serpent in the garden . he tricked eve into eating the fruit from the tree of knowledge o f good and evil, which God had forbidden. but God mercifully introduced a plan of redemption that would rescue individuals through personal salvation, and would restore the hope of his great messianic kingdom on earth. \r\nGenesis 3:15 I will put enmity between you and the woman and between your offspring and heir , he will crush your head and you will strike his heel\r\nthis offer of redemption the proto evangel ion or first gospel because its is the first offer of salvation after humanity\'s fall into sin.\r\nwhen God promised that the seed of woman would defat the seed of the serpent, he indicate that humanity would be redeemed the curse of sin.', 8, '2020-12-03 18:09:28', '2020-12-14 21:48:16', NULL),
(2248, 602, 636, 'God made five major redemptive covenants beginning with His covenant with Adam\r\nAdamic covenant:\r\nafter Adam had sinned against God in the garden of Eden, god offered him salvation through the proto euangelion or first gospel.\r\nNoahic covenant:\r\nGenesis 6-9 records the flood that destroyed the world in Noah\'s days. Genesis 8:21 to 9:17 explained the covenant God made through Noah, in the context of promising not to destroy the whole creation through flood.\r\nAbrahamic covenant:\r\nGenesis 15,17 and 22 God called Abraham and his descendants to serve him in a special way. It reminded all humanity to fill and subdue the earth.\r\nGod\'s covenant with Abraham created the expectations that God\'s earthly covenant would be consist of a one nation or people, instead it would be eventually include members from all nations on earth.\r\nMosaic covenant:\r\nthis covenant mentioned God\'s commitment to bring the covenant blessings to pass, but if his people ,disobeyed him , he would punish them. Deuteronomy 4:27-31\r\nDavidic covenant\r\n As per this covenant, God would establish the house of David as the permanent dynasty rule over God\'s earthly kingdom Psalm 89:34-37', 9, '2020-12-03 18:21:12', '2020-12-14 21:48:16', NULL),
(2249, 602, 637, 'Jewish eschatology held that world history could be divide into two great ages, they called the first age \"this age\"-olam hazeh in hebrew. it was characterized by sin , exile and death. This age started when Adam an d eve fell in to sin and this corresponds to the age of redemption . the second age \"the age to come\" or olam habah in hebrew . this was the future eschaton, characterized by forgiveness, righteousness , peace , and eternal life\r\nJesus and the writers of the New testament also spoke of this age and the age to come, as the two major eras of history. They largely agreed  and taught that Messiah would end this age of sin and death and usher in the age to from with all its blessings.\r\nMark 10:29, 30\r\nJesus distinguished this age from the age to com just like his contemporaries did. He made it clear that blessings would come to those that follow him. Mark 10:29 Jesus indicated that He was the Messiah that was expected to bring the transition from this age to the age to come\r\nPaul  also distinguished from this age to to age to some 1 Corinthians2:6-8, 2 Corinthians 4:4, 1 Timothy 6:17-19', 9, '2020-12-03 18:34:28', '2020-12-14 21:48:16', NULL),
(2250, 608, 643, 'Personal:\r\nIts necessary for Jesus to return to earth, personally in order to fulfill the promise made to Abraha and David. Jesus really is coming back in person to finish his work. Acst1:11 says two angels appeared and told that Jesus would personally return in future,  to finish his work consummating God\'s kingdoms on earth.\r\nthe apostle Peter was so persuaded of this reality that it became pat of his gospel presentation Acts 3;21\r\nNew testament writers also argued that Jesus will return personally to consummate God\'s kingdom on earth\r\nPhysical:\r\nJesus didn\'t give up his humanity, when he ascended into heaven. he is one person-the second person of the trinity. Acts 1:11. the apostles saw Jesus humanly ascend into heaven and they were told that they would see him return the same way.\r\n\' that can only mean that he will return in his human body. 1corinthains 15:20-23, Philippians 3:20-21\r\nthe new testament consistently teaches that when Jesus returns he will appear in his glorified physical human body- that same body that  rose from death and ascended into heaven .\r\nvisible:\r\nwhen Jesus return in his glorified human body , we will be able to see him. when he appears , we shall see him as he is 1 john 3:2\r\nlook he is coming with the clouds and every eye will see him revelation 1:7\r\nMatthew 24\"27\"230 says that Jesus return will be an earth -shattering event. the radiance of his glory will be like the sun and his heavenly arms will fill the sky and every last human being will have to face him on his judgement throne.', 9, '2020-12-07 17:48:54', '2020-12-14 21:47:46', NULL),
(2251, 608, 644, 'three of the most prominent precursors th Christ return begins with Demonic opposition to God\'s kingdom\r\nDemonic opposition\'\r\nscripture teach that Satan and his demons forces have opposed the spread of God\'s kingdom throughout history. revelation 7:14 calls this the great tribulation, demons will, empower false prophets to performs misleading signs and wonders,\r\ngod\'s demonic opponents use every trick and power they have to hinder his plan for his kingdom.\r\nHUMAN OPPOSITION:\r\nA second precursors to Christ return is human opposition to God\'s kingdom.\r\nRevelation 13 indicate that Satan will use two beats one form the land and other form the sea. they do appear to be human for one thing they influence humanity in a way that implies control over societies and governments. Revelations 13:18\r\na powerful false prophet is mentioned in revelation 16:13, 19:20 and 20:10\r\nhuman opposition to god\'s kingdom also appears in 2 Thessalonians 2:1-10\r\n. which mentions a rebellion and a man of lawlessness, he will be satanically empowered to perform false miracles and will eventually proclaim himself to be God.\r\nWORLD WIDE EVANGELISM:\r\nMathew 24:14, Jesus gave this prophecy despite the various form of serious opposition God\'s kingdom face. the kingdom will successfully present its gospel to the nations.\r\nhe mentioned it in two place once in Great commissions and one after his resurrections before his ascension Acts 1:8\r\nAs a result , be the time Jesus returns , the church will include member from every tribe, nations, language, people.', 9, '2020-12-07 18:05:13', '2020-12-14 21:47:46', NULL),
(2252, 608, 645, 'evangelical interpretive strategies exhibit focus main tendencies.\r\nsome interpretive strategies focus on when the precursors to Christ\' return will appear in history. this strategies ten between preterism and futurism , at one extreme, preterism says that this precursors appear into our past and will not repeat in the future. for example preterist might say that the antichrist was a human being that loved during the fist century and that we should not look for any like him in future. \r\nas the extreme futurist says that precursors of Christ return have \'nit appeared yet, but will appear in the future. for instance a futurist is likely to say that antichrist will live in the last generation before the second coming.\r\nsome might say that the antichrist is a type of human institution that last or is repeated throughout history\r\nother interpretive strategies focus on how the precursors to Christ return will be manifested in history. this strategies tend between historism and idealism. historism relates this precursors to actual people and event throughout history. for instance, during the reformation in the fifteen the century, many historicists believed that the antichrist was the Roman catholic Pope or even the papacy itself. by contrast idealism relates the precursors to Christ return to abstract concepts and general principles.\r\nfor example an idealist might argue that the antichrist is anyone that preaches falsehood and oppose Jesus. this  tendencies overlaps in many  ways.', 9, '2020-12-07 18:21:15', '2020-12-14 21:47:46', NULL),
(2253, 608, 646, 'Historic premillennialism is often called chiliasm which means thousand from the Greek word, chilloi\r\nthe term premillennialism  itself refers to the belief will that Jesus will return to the world before the  millennium begins. And the term historic denotes that this view has been held through church history. historic premillennialism teaches that the church will go through the great tribulation, just prior to Jesus return.\r\n the last point is critical one of the key distinction premillennial is that it looks for Christ to return physically in the earth during the millennium. some premillennial interpreters believe that millennium will be exactly one thousand years, bust most believe that one thousand means metaphorically indicate a long time of unspecified length, similarly, they are not unified on the details pf the gathering or rapture of believers.\r\nthey teach, those believers that remain alive, when Jesus returns will be gathered to him in the air. then they will immediately return to earth with him as part of his victorious military parade and live on earth during his millennial  reign, some of them believe that rapture will also include resurrected believers, but others argue that resurrections of believers wont the place until the final judgement at the end of the millennium. they believe that Satan \'s rebellion will take place , followed by the resurrections of every one that hasn\'t been previously resurrected.', 9, '2020-12-07 18:39:24', '2020-12-14 21:47:46', NULL),
(2254, 608, 647, 'Post millennialism refers to the belief that Jesus will return after the millennium ends. this is a direct contrast to both premillennial systems which says that he will return before the millennium begins another important contrast between premillennial systems and post millennialism is the location of Christ during his millennial reign. premillennialism place Christ physically on earth. but post millennialism locates him physically in heaven while reign spiritually in earth.\r\n in this, the millennial is the age of the success of for the gospel that prepares the earth for Christ return. \r\n1 Corinthians 15:25 they generally believe that Christ will successfully defeat his enemies before he returns. they acknowledge Satan\'s rebellion at the end of millennium and his defeat when Christ return.  they teach that the rapture will include resurrections and still living believers.', 9, '2020-12-07 18:49:07', '2020-12-14 21:47:46', NULL),
(2255, 499, 866, 'One believe and taught that the apostles creed was written in the first century by the twelve Apostles themselves, but we have no evidence any apostles were directly involved the writing of the apostles creed.. \r\nThe the main question is who Didi? \r\nTertullius refer to what is called the old Roman creed which is almost identified to the apostles creed is man named by Rufinus in 390AD and he relate the story that each of the twelve Apostles, including Matthias.. Not much profile for this theory\r\n\r\nIn the writing of early Christian, there are many lists of essential doctrine that resemble the apostles creed\r\n\r\nOne ancient creed that prominent in the manner was the creed from the church in Rome, called Roman creed,  similar apostles creed also', 7, '2020-12-08 04:49:03', '2020-12-14 21:47:21', NULL),
(2256, 499, 867, 'Apostles creed important\r\nFirst, Teaching are foundational to the rest of Christian theology. \r\nMost people are familiar with the large building soild foundation. The foundation is the base on which the rest of the building is created. It is the anchor that holds the building firmly in place, and that provides strength and stability\r\n\r\nSecond, we ll talk about the universal affirmation of these teaching throughout the church. \r\n\r\nThird, We\'  ll speak of the unifying nature of these articles of faith.', 7, '2020-12-08 05:02:15', '2020-12-14 21:47:21', NULL),
(2257, 499, 868, 'Trinity\r\nApostles creed divided three main part, each of which begins with the statement \" I believe \" The believe in God the father. Second belif is Jesus Christ , his only son, our Lord. And the third summarizes belief in the Holy Spirit, his activ minister\r\nThe apostles creed does not specifically mention the word Trinity or spell out it\'s details. But creed was intended to be a summery of believefe, not a comprehensive statement of faith, \r\n\r\nWhen we say that God exists in three person, we are defending the biblical truth that the father, the son and the Holy Spirit are distinct one another, \r\n\r\nPerson\r\nWith regard the father the creed ascribe to him the attributes of being almighty, and mention the historical facts that he is the maker of heaven and earth.', 7, '2020-12-08 05:22:12', '2020-12-14 21:47:21', NULL),
(2258, 499, 869, 'When the creed say  I believe in the holy.. It emphasizes the importance of participating in the church, \r\nFirst century. Some professing believe wanted to avoid  the gathered church as a place for worship , teaching and fellowship. But scripture teaches that the church is important and necessary of Christian\r\n\r\nEven so in the modern world we still encounter Christian who avoid the church. Perhaps it is because they dislike organized religion. Or maybe they have been mistreated by other Christian. \r\n\r\nAccording to Jude part of church job is a conted for the faith to protect the truth and belief that have entered to against those who promote false teaching and practice', 7, '2020-12-08 05:36:22', '2020-12-14 21:47:21', NULL),
(2259, 499, 870, 'Christian theology must be founded on true doctrines and principles if it is to honor God and be useful to his people. Just as  Jesus is the corner ston of the church. \r\nHis teaching is corner ston of theology\r\nAnd just apostles and prophet because the church foundation to theology by introduction Christ to the world, \r\nWe will consider the foundation nature of the apostles creed in two parts. First we took started against which other doctrines can be judged\r\nAnd second we will speak of the way that it serves as the logical basis on which other true doctrine are built.', 7, '2020-12-08 05:48:32', '2020-12-14 21:47:21', NULL),
(2260, 607, 932, 'The doctrine of the trinity teaches that there is one God-one divine essence - and all three members of the Trinity are equally and fully God. this Biblical truth is not fully and explicitly revealed in the old Testament. much of the Christian doctrine of the Trinity comes from the New Testament. we should recognize that the old Testament is incomplete revelation . God didn\'t reveal himself all at once.\r\nDr.  Danny Akin\r\n says that we need to acknowledge that the understanding of the triune God take place through what we call progressive revelation. it is more fully revelated in the new Testament, in triune passages like Mathew 28 the great commission text, or Ephesians 4, in the first six verses, the end of 2corinthians 13, where it is brought together in a very clear package about the Trinity.\r\nDr.  Matt carter says that in the new Testament era , god took a step closer to us through Jesus, and not only was He other than us, but he dwell among us.\r\nand the Old Testament\'s  under sand, dirt at a lot of times, occasionally will come to the surface, but it is the New Testament that sends light n the old Testament as to how we might understand, phrases like let us create man in our own image.', 8, '2020-12-08 17:43:46', '2020-12-14 21:46:53', NULL),
(2261, 607, 933, 'Revelation chapter 1 and 4 says that the one who sits on the throne is god the Father. lamb is the word of God, who became flesh -Jesus Christ, the Son of god. in Revelation 5, John uses a imagery of both lamb and lion to describe of Jesus Christ.\r\nDr. Mark L Strauss says that revelation 5 sums up the whole message of revelation. it is the Lion, lion of tribe of Judah. john turns expecting to see a lion, a powerful lion, instead he sees a lamb, a symbol of sacrifice, a symbol of suffering. and there is a beautiful picture of who Jesus is?\r\nbecause Jesus is the conquering king, he is the messiah.\r\nHe is the Lord of all, yet he accomplished  salvation by suffering and dying as the sacrificial lamb.\r\nwho is the lamb is the beautiful picture as Jesus is Christ as Lord, as sovereign as king\r\nDr. Lai chai Kang says that a lion represents Jesus kingly majesty.\r\nin revelation 4 god raised his right hand holding a scroll and might angel asked , if anyone could open the scroll?\r\n its announced as the Lion of Judah, but once the announcement happened , revelation places Jesus in the category of divine. and as God divine son, he will conquer all his enemies and our enemies when he returns.', 9, '2020-12-08 17:53:38', '2020-12-14 21:46:53', NULL),
(2262, 607, 934, 'The first chapter of John, and the first verse has been used throughout the history of the church as proof that Jesus is god the son, the second person of the trinity. He is God and has been with God since the beginning. he is also involved in the creation of all things and because of that He himself must be uncreated. he is the eternal and uncreated creator. John 1:1\r\naccording to Dr. Daniel B Wallace John 1:11 is absolutely undisputedly, and clearly teaches the deity of Christ.  but the best translation is probably what the revised English bible has \" what God was, the word was\"\r\nand so the last few words of John 1:1 and the word was god says in the most concise way possible that the second person of the trinity is equal in authority, in worship, in attributes as the first person of Trinity and yet they are not the same person.\r\nDr. John Mc Kinley says that in John gospel , he introduced the readers to Jesus with a prologue -first 18 verse-where he says -Jesus is the word, he was with god nd the word was god. calling Jesus the word of God. John also saying he is the son of God. he is the divine agent, and he is the one who brings life.\r\nDr. Jeff Lowman makes it clear that john begins his gospel unlike the other gospel writers, he begins with person of Christ.', 9, '2020-12-08 18:06:04', '2020-12-14 21:46:53', NULL),
(2263, 607, 935, 'Searching and knowing is an a tribute of a distinct , self aware personality, who is in a loving relationship with other self aware personalities. this necessarily implies that Spirit of God is a person, direct from the person of God.\r\naccording to Dr. Craig S Keener 2Crinthians 13 and John 14-16, we read about the Spirit doing things that only a person would do. for example  Jesus says that he will sent other advocate like himself, the Holy spirit and the spirit goes on to prosecute the world concerning sin and righteousness and judgement, just like Jesus did. \r\nThe Spirit is a witness just like we are witnesses nd so on\r\nRev Mike Osborne says that Bible speaks so clearly that the Holy Spirit searches hearts. the Holy spirit imparts gifts, the Holy  Spirit teaches, Jesus sis telling us, what the Holy Spirit does. He is teaching, he is revealing all truth, guiding, comforting and leading.\r\n In 1 Thessalonians he says, don\'t quench the  Holy Spirit\'s fire, or don\'t\' put out  the fire. the holy spirit is nota force. he is not an \"it\" but a \" he\"\r\nDr. Peter Walker says that New testament says on several occasions , yet the holy Spirt is like a force- Acts 2-rushing wind- but the holy spirit is more than a force. Paul describes on one occasion, we can grieve the holy spirit. we can only grieve a person.', 9, '2020-12-08 18:18:58', '2020-12-14 21:46:53', NULL),
(2264, 607, 936, 'Scripture says that Jesus is the true god , living God. John understood this. but one of the great mysteries of the trinity is that Jesus is not only fully god, he is also fully human. and it is through his humanity, through his life, the death and resurrections that we are saved. because the human Jesus is also the eternal God equal in glory and power to the Father, we can trust that he is worthy and able to redeem from our sins. \r\nDr. Derek Thomas says that if we are to be reconciled to God, then only God can forgive sin.\r\nwhy the God man because as it was necessary for the mediator to be god and Man?\r\nHe was true god and True Man, two nature in one person and the reason why he has to be a man is that he has to represent us. he must be tempted in every point like as we are. He must be the second man, the last Adam, but he must also be God, because only God can forgive sin , because God is the one who has been sinned against God.\r\nDr. J Scott Horrell says that 2 Corinthians 5 talks about How God was reconciling the world to himself in Christ. Christ became the perfect substitute for us, because he is fully human yet without sin, he can be our complete substitute.\r\nJesus is both god and Man , he is the second person of the Trinity, god the son. its an amazing truth that the eternal son of god became human and lived among us. he died and rose again so that we might be God\'s children and Jesus; brothers and sisters.', 9, '2020-12-08 18:30:57', '2020-12-14 21:46:53', NULL),
(2265, 512, 875, 'There are many things we might say about the God authority as a creator. But we will focus on just three of its basic characteristics\r\nHis authority is absolute, exclusive and exhaustive. \r\n1) Absolute, The father authority is absolute in the sense that he has complete freedom to do whatever he wants with his creation. Scripture often compare his absolute authority to a Potter authority over his clay. We find this description in places like Is 29:16\r\nRomantic 9:20\r\n2) Exclusive, Besides having absolute authority, the father also has exclusive authority over everything he has created. The father authority as creator is exclusive in the sense that no creature possess absolute authority. Absolute authority belongs only to the creator, and God is the only creator. And beyond this when we view the Trinity economically, \r\n3) Exhaustive, In addition to having absolute and exclusive authority the father also has exhaustive authority over the universe. When we say that God\'s authority is exhaustive, we mean that it extended over every thing he has created, in every detail. And there are at least two important implications of this fact', 7, '2020-12-09 04:50:25', '2020-12-14 21:46:25', NULL),
(2266, 516, 886, 'The concept of the church in the new Testament actually finds its roots in the old Testament\r\nThe NT speak many times church using the Greek word ekklesia. This is from Septuagint, ekklesia and it Hebrew counterpart is frequently used to identify the gathering nation Israel. \r\nDut 9:, 10\r\nNT where ekklesia has become a technical term referring to the church, this word use for assembly of OT Israel. Act 7:30 we see Stephen say about that\r\nGreek word transition assembly is ekklesia which more common transition Church this is indicated that assembly of Israel was the OT equivalent and predecessor to NT \r\nNT or OT not same, but they are connected, Rom 11 Paul say about relationship between OT and NT', 7, '2020-12-10 04:45:24', '2020-12-14 21:45:56', NULL),
(2267, 516, 887, 'Important to recognize that Jesus did not establish a church that was totally new. His church has great continuity with Old Testament church\r\nWe see word oikodomeo mean build can building something new, or rebuilding and restoring what already excited. \r\nWe see same thing in Rome 11\r\nWe look at the continuity between the old testament and new testament churches, important is recognized the center role that Jesus plays in trying these two church together, \r\n1) broadly speak the new testament presents Jesus fulfilled of God\'s promises of Israel.. We see in Rome 8:1-\r\n2) Jesus is the founder of the New testament church the one who brought the restoration and renewable that transform the failure OT church into NT church ep 5: 24', 7, '2020-12-10 04:59:59', '2020-12-14 21:45:56', NULL),
(2268, 516, 888, 'When we realize that Jesus ordained the NT church as restoration and development of OT Israel. One important was a fundamental continuity between Israel in OT and Christian Church in NT. \r\nSome point\r\n1) Purpose\r\nTheologian often summarizes the history of world three stage \r\nCreation, fall and redemption\r\nGen 1-2 we see creation\r\nGen 3 we see fall \r\nThe rest of stage of redemption in which God working to restore humanity to a perfect condition, and \r\nIn the mordent world the church still toward this goal restorating by preaching the gospel \r\n2) Believers and unbelievers\r\nThe OT and NT, the church of God was never perfect. In the church \r\nWe see OT in blessing of God5 covenant Leviticus 26, and Deuteronomy 27-30\r\nAs same kind of things is is true of assembly of the followers of Jesus, the NT church, there are always unbelievers mixed among the believers in our churchs\r\n3) Obligation\r\nThe people of God both responsibility of loving God', 7, '2020-12-10 05:19:22', '2020-12-14 21:45:56', NULL),
(2269, 516, 889, 'Catholic mean universal, it includes all Christian in all congregation. Catholic word Latin mean whole and complete it not reference Roman Catholic Church\r\nApostles creed speak of Catholity of church, it does not mind of organization of all Christian community, rather it  talking about the unity of the holy Spirit that exist between all legitimate Christian Church despite our meant to extend the name church \r\n1 cor 1:2 \r\nVisibility of Catholic Church\r\nWhen we component our understand of Catholic with our understand of the visible church we can defend the visible Catholic Church as. One worldwide fellow ship of all people in covenant with God', 7, '2020-12-10 05:31:52', '2020-12-14 21:45:56', NULL),
(2270, 516, 890, 'When we combine our understand of Catholic with our understand of the visible church we can defend the visible Catholic as, one worldwide fellowship of all people in covenant with God under the headship of Christ. \r\nSurly this one of spirit rather than of church government, \r\nThere is no single denomination whose government extended overall all Christian congregation. Rather the unity of visible church is based on the fact that each church is in covenant with the same God. \r\nAnd under the covenant headship of the same Christ\r\nWhile there are many way to talk about the Catholic of the invisible church we ll focus on just two, first the visible church is universal because there is only one Savior, and second, the invisible church in universal because there is only one true religion that can lead us to that savior l', 7, '2020-12-10 05:49:11', '2020-12-14 21:45:56', NULL),
(2271, 612, 937, 'The three member of the trinity are each eternal and uncreated. there was a never a time when any member of the Trinity didn\'t exist. \r\nAccording to Dr. Danny Akin whatever it is that makes God, the Father is all of that, the Son is all of that and the Spirit is all of that. we also know that the Father is not the Son, the Son is not the spirit, and the Spirit is not the Father. there is one God, yet they exist in same type of mysterious unity as one God - three  persons. they are always of one mind, they are always of one will, they are always united in any and every issue including redemption. it is the Father who  initiated salvation, it is the son who accomplished our salvation and it is the Spirit who applies our salvation and they work in an absolute perfect unity and harmony, not just in that area, but  they operate in that way in every area. so an idea that there\'s any disagreement among the triune God is a creation of man\'s imagination. It  is nothing revealed in God\'s Word.\r\n\r\nDr. .Steve Blakemore \r\nThe Father\'s authority is always an authority of love ,and desires the son to be glorified, just as the Son then desires to be glorified.', 8, '2020-12-15 17:47:31', '2021-01-09 14:51:04', NULL),
(2272, 612, 938, 'Father\'s relationship in the Trinity is unique, the Son \'s relationship is also unique. Scripture like Revelation 5:13, 14 makes it clear that the Son is eternal. He has always the Father, the son is eternally begotten of the Father.\r\nDr. Paul R Raabe says that the Son is one substance with the Father and through the Son all things were made so that the father through the son made all things.\r\nDr. Thomas J Nettles says that in the incarnation God who is eternally generating  the Son overshadows Mary at the point that the Holy Spirit conceives in her our human nature, so everything that relates to our being an dour actions as human, Jesus took himself, he had a full human nature, we could not be redeemed if he had not assumed our full human nature. though he emptied himself of the external manifestation of the glory, he had with the father, he did not empty himself, he did not empty himself of any of the central attributes of his central being as  the Son of God. he still was omnipotent, omniscient,  had immutable holiness, had perfect knowledge as the Son of God while redemption was taking place.', 8, '2020-12-15 18:00:20', '2021-01-09 14:51:04', NULL),
(2273, 612, 939, 'when a child proceeds from his or her parents we know that he or she had  a beginning in creation or birth. The eternal and uncreated Holy spirit proceeds from his Father, do not mean that the Spirit had a beginning.\r\nThis is the doctrine of the holy Spirit\'s eternal procession  and it affirms the divinity of the Holy spirit. Dr. Jim  Maples says that Constantinople in 381 A D said that Holy Spirit was a created being like an angel, but the Macedonian\'s  affirmed Nicene Creed and added words concerning the Holy Spirt, they said he is Lord, he is life giver. He is to be worshipped  as the father and Son and it is this form of the creed that most people today recognize.\r\nDr. Tim Foster says that what Nicene Creed  says about the Holy spirit is every simple.\r\nthe holy Spirit was not created , because if the Spirit was created , he was not God so just as the Son is begotten of the father, so the spirit proceeds from the Father.\r\nBy calling the Holy Spirit both the Spirit of God and Spirit of Christ, Paul implied that the Spirit not only proceeds from God, the Father but also proceeds from Christ the Son .\r\nJohn 16:13-15,  20:222', 8, '2020-12-15 18:15:22', '2021-01-09 14:51:04', NULL),
(2274, 612, 940, 'in 589 A D a church council met in the city of To lento, Spain and attempted to solidify the doctrine of eternal procession. the theologians added the latin term filioque meaning \"and the Son\" to the Nicene Creed of 381 AD. with this addition, the creed clearly articulates that the  Holy Spirit \"proceeds\" or \"spirates \" from the Father and Son. \r\nDr. Stephen J Wellum says that filioque clause was a clause that t he western church added early on the ecumenical councils to explain the relationship of the son to the Father, but particularly the Son -Spirit relationship. what they argued in the filioque clause - and filioque simply means and from the son, they argued that the father and Son- there\'s where the filioque comes in -a re the persons who then send the Spirit.\r\nand they are trying to do justice to the Biblical teachings that the father send the son , yet the father and Son send the spirit.\r\nDr. Jim Maples says that the filioque  the latin word meaning and the son was added to the creed. and  what they affirmed was the double procession of the Holy spirit that the spirit proceeded from the Father and the Son.', 8, '2020-12-15 18:30:44', '2021-01-09 14:51:04', NULL),
(2275, 612, 941, 'the word ontological means being and existence. so ontological trinity also known as the immanent trinity or the trinitias ad intra in latin. he has to do with the existence and personal interaction of the father, son and holy spirit apart from their acts of creation and salvation. the word economic refers to the management of a home\r\nDr. David  Correa refer to the fact that the three person of the trinity , Father, son and the holy spirit are equal in substance , power and glory. The Father is one hundred percent god, Son is one hundred  god and  Holy spirit is one hundred God.\r\nDr. J Scott Horrell says that ontological trinity describes God in himself. the common God word is immanent trinity. before creation, God ad intra-god within himself, the ontological trinity is what, from scripture, we derives as to the relation within himself beyond all creation.\r\nDr. Simon Vibert says that ontology being\" within the being of God \"there are three persons who are coexisting, co loving, within one another.\r\nDr. John Mc Kinley\r\nthe ontological trinity is the same God at work in creation, but God in an inner life of the life, Son and holy spirit and holy spirit relation to each other eternally.', 9, '2020-12-15 18:46:02', '2021-01-09 14:51:04', NULL),
(2276, 611, 648, 'There were at least two schools of thought concerning the resurrection of the dead . the Pharisees believed that there would be a general resurrection of the righteous and the wicked. But the Sadducees denied that there would be physical resurrection of the dead. In fact, when Paul was arrested and taken before the Jewish court called the Sanhedrin, he appealed to this controversy in order to defend himself. Acts 23:6-8\r\nPharisees accepted the entire Old Testament as inspired scripture, but Sadducees accepted only the five books written by Moses. The general resurrection is taught in Passage like Isaiah 26:19 and Daniel 12:2. So the Pharisees affirmed it but the Sadducees denied it because they don\' t believe in other books than Pentateuch.\r\nJesus, Paul and the rest of the early church clearly took the side of the Pharisees and to refute the \r\nSadducees even more strongly., Jesus proved that they had misread Moses himself. Mark 12:18\r\nMathew 8:11 says that believers like Abraham , Isaac and Jacob were to be resurrected then the general resurrection was true as well.\r\n2Timothy 2:18 Paul accused two believers that the resurrection had already taken place.\r\nPaul  say s 2 Corinthians 15:17 .', 8, '2020-12-17 17:49:38', '2021-01-09 14:50:35', NULL),
(2277, 611, 649, 'God\'s divine authority is God\'s legal and moral right to carry out his will. God has the authority to do something, it means that its perfectly within his rights to do it and that he commits no wrong of he does it.\r\nAuthority over hell: God has complete authority over hell. when unregenerate souls suffer in hell during the intermediate state, they suffer because God us punishing them and when they are drawn out of hell in order to face judgement  because God has summoned them into his court. God rules over hell and that he complete control over Satan. the demons and the unregenerate souls he imprisoned there. 2Peter 2:4,9\r\nHeaven:\r\nwhile some Christians have been confused about God\'s authority over hell. None should be confused about his authority over heaven.\r\n heaven is God\'s throne room, the place where His authority and glory are manifested. god put its simply in Isaiah 66:1 Heaven is my throne\r\nOut of all creation, heaven is where God manifests his authority most directly. the old Testament prophet Micaiah also saw him in 1King 22:19 and 2Chronicle 18:18\r\nStephen the first Christian martyr had a similar vision in Acts 7:55,56', 8, '2020-12-17 18:02:56', '2021-01-09 14:50:35', NULL),
(2278, 611, 650, 'The general resurrection effect on creation will be felt in at least three different realms\r\nNatural world:\r\nwhen humanity fell into sin, part of god\'s curse on us included a curse in earth itself. it became a place of danger and death and the ground itself resisted humanity\'s attempt to cultivate it. as god said to Gen 3:17,18. but in romans 8Paul looked forward to God\'s miraculous resurrection of the regenerate as the solution to this problem. He thought that, in the resurrection then earth itself would be rescued through the resurrection of  redeemed humanity. Romans 8:19, 23\r\nHell - when the unregenerate are resurrected their souls will be removed from hell and returned tp their bodies on earth so that they can face God\'s judgement. Satan and the other demons will also have been removed by this point in the eschaton. Revelation 20:7,8. 2 peter 2:4 say that the fallen angels are being held in gloomy dungeons until the judgment\r\nheaven:\r\nthe resurrected regenerate will be return into the earth to appear before God\'s judgement throne.\r\nbesides that after the resurrection , Jesus throne will, be on earth not in heaven. Mathew 25:31 says that when Jesus return He will bring all the angels with him and Mathew 24:31 says that there tasks will be to collect the resurrected regenerate from the farthest.', 8, '2020-12-17 18:15:16', '2021-01-09 14:50:35', NULL),
(2279, 611, 651, 'the three Parties that will face the final judgment are \r\n1. Fallen angels :\r\nboth 2 Peter 2:4 and Jude 6 reports that the demons used to be angels that held authority from God, but they rebelled against him and abandoned their heavenly home and their authority . as a result they are now changed in gloomy dangerous , awaiting Christ\'s judgment\r\nUnregenerate:\r\nwhen the resurrected human race appears before the Christ; judgement throne , the Lord will separate the unregenerate from the regenerate.  Paul talked about this in Romans 2:5-8\r\nand John saw vision in Revelation 11:18, 20:11-15. Jesu himself talked about in Mathew 25\r\nRegenerate:\r\nafter Jesus separates the regenerate from the unregenerate, he will also render judgment on the regenerate. Romans 2:7, Revelation 11:8, Mathew 25\r\nJesus compared the unregenerate to goats and the regenerate to sheep.  some Christian mistakenly believe that regenerate wont be judged at all John 5:24. believers wont be condemned in the final judgement, if the regenerate were to be missed the judgment, they would not receive the everlasting  judgment.', 8, '2020-12-17 18:29:04', '2021-01-09 14:50:35', NULL),
(2280, 611, 652, 'God will consider every evidence in the final judgement in order to ensure that perfect judgment is upheld. He will evaluate everything we have done , thought, and said. he will look into our secret motives. he will consider the covenant that govern over relationship with him and the revelation we received in life. he will listen to witnesses, and weigh all circumstances. nothing will be left out, nothing will be inadmissible. all of this will be done in pursuit of perfect justice, so that every reward and every punishment will perfectly fit everyone who is judged. Ecclesiastes 12:14, Mathew 12:36, 2 Corinthians 4:5, Psalms 16;12, Proverbs 24:12\r\nNot every one will be held to the same level of accountability. rather we will be each be judged according to our own situations. for example, those who have sinned more blatantly and with more knowledge of God\'s requirements will be judged more harshly. Luke 10:13,14\r\nPsalms 50:4-6 indicates that Christ will held us more accountable obligations in the judgment and James 3:1 says that teachers within the church will be judged even more strictly.', 8, '2020-12-17 18:40:54', '2021-01-09 14:50:35', NULL),
(2281, 617, 589, 'The Bible relates the true story of creation in order to explain how humanity was actually designed to function within the world. The Bible contain the appeal to the creation accounts to prove how the world is supposed to work and what role human beings are morally obligated to play. \r\nTheologians often refer these obligations as \' creation ordinances\'. They are moral requirements established by God\'s work of creation.\r\nSome times, creation ordinances are explicit. For example; God\'s command to be fruitful and to multiply is very vivid. It is a direct command to the human beings. But there are implicit ordinances like\' keeping Sabbath day holy. The creation accounts don\'t say explicitly about keeping Sabbath. But in Exodus 20:11, Moses clarified that God\'s pattern of working six days and resting on the seventh. \r\nThus the creation ordinances shed light on the rules and obligations for the function of creation on this earth. Violation of them will lead to Chaos and calamity.', 8, '2020-12-18 09:42:10', '2021-01-09 14:50:00', NULL),
(2282, 617, 590, 'The two accounts of creation ( Genesis 1:1-2:3 and 2:4-25)give us a general picture of how and why we are created. Both these accounts compliment each other. The first narrative give a picture of the whole creation process when the second is the kind of a window to the sixth day of creation. \r\nIn the first account we see God giving form and shape to the formless and empty universe. First three days God did it by separating light and darkness, forming sky and the atmosphere and finally separating dry land and water. Next three days God filled the sky with heavenly bodies like sun, moon and stars on fourth day; birds in the sky and sea creatures on fifth day and on sixth day creating all living creatures on land and finally man. Man was made in God\'s image to represent and rule on his behalf. \r\nThe second narrative concentrate more on creation of human race. Though animals also made from dust; only man was given the breath of God. In order to give a help mate to Adam, the first woman was made out of Adam\'s rib. Adam named he; which shows his authority in marriage but they were made equal in tasks assigned to them.\r\nThus the Bible contains the most unique accounts of creation compared to any other document available.', 9, '2020-12-18 09:54:00', '2021-01-09 14:50:00', NULL),
(2283, 617, 591, 'The Bible evidently says man as a superior being above all other creatures. According to Genesis 1:27, it is a separate act of creation. After making man God said\' very good\' where as \' good\' is used in relation to other creations. The superiority is vivid in genesis 2:7, where we see only Adam receiving the breath of God.\r\nThe superiority of man is evident in the fact that he is made in the image and likeness of God. man is also appointed to rule over the creation on behalf of God. God created humanity to reflect his glory and attribute. Man is given a superior role to administer God\'s rule over earth. Adam exercised this authority by naming all creatures. later the scripture confirms human superiority by putting him almost on the level of angels ( psalms 8:5).\r\nThough we see many people deny and destroy the distinction between human and animals; supporting evolution theory; we know that the humans are mentally superior to the animals. Humanity was created to be superior to the rest of creation in both dignity and authority.', 9, '2020-12-18 10:03:55', '2021-01-09 14:50:00', NULL),
(2284, 617, 592, 'India is a pluralistic society with different religious beliefs and cultures. The major religion Hinduism itself is pluralistic in relation to the myths on creation and origin of man. Though there are variations with different accounts, they have something common in them.\r\nHinduism believe creation as eternal and go through a process of creation and destruction. So beginning refers to the beginning of a phase. \r\nConcerning the origin of man, the major concept is Brahma created human and whole life. He created man of his soul. One of the stories mention that Brahma split in to two to create man and woman. According to the \"Matsya purana\', sage Manu was the first man created by Brahma. He was born through the union between Brahma and Shatrupa.( goddess). The rest of the race was born from the union of Manu and Ananti. \r\nRigveda has a different account of human origin which says human race was born from the five children ( Four male and one female)of lord prajapati; as Brahma was earlier called.\r\nHinduism also talk about cycle of rebirths. The theory evolution has a greater place in Hindu mythology of creation and human origin.\r\nThe biblical record of human origin is totally different from the Indian or Hindu concept. The union of god and goddess, process of evolution and cycle of rebirths are in contrast with the biblical concept of God making man in his own image and likeness.', 0, '2020-12-18 10:18:08', '2020-12-18 10:18:08', NULL),
(2285, 617, 801, 'God made man with a purpose and entered in to a covenant relationship. garden of Eden as a sanctuary, a precursor to the Tabernacle or Temple; Adam and Eve had a priestly ministry to fulfill. As Adam and Eve were chosen to look after the garden, the old Testament priests were chosen to look after the tabernacle/ Temple. As priests represented God in the Temple; Adam and Eve represented God in the Garden of Eden. \r\nGod appointed Adam and Eve to rule over the creation on his behalf. The great King of heaven ordained humanity as his royal vassals to expand his Kingdom beyond the initial boarders his dwelling In the garden through multiplying and replenishing the whole earth. Ultimately the humanity was to turn the whole planet in to God\'s earthly sanctuary as an extension of his heavenly Kingdom. \r\nGod\'s covenant with Adam and Eve is binding to humanity. We are supposed to fulfill our priestly duty to serve God, worship him, cultivate and guard creation and to turn the whole world in to a sanctuary. Also we are supposed to exercise the divine authority given to us as the children of God. \r\nWe fulfill the priestly responsibility in the Church through rendering sacrifices of praise and Obedience obedient to him proclaiming his goodness. By preaching the gospel and manifesting the power of the Holy Spirit, we exercise the divine authority and establish the Kingdom of God. As we are supposed to represent and rule on behalf of God; in different capacity we represent God in life and spiritual disciplines and exercise divine authority in establishing the Kingdom of God on earth through establishing his Church. Thus both in life and ministry we fulfill the priestly and royal responsibility assigned to us.', 9, '2020-12-18 10:35:58', '2021-01-09 14:50:00', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2286, 620, 638, 'Individual Eschatology is the study of how individual human beings experience the events of the last days especially with regard to life , death , the intermediate state and the final state of our bodies and souls. \r\nthe term regenerate means recreated or reborn, we could refer to those who are regenerate as the spiritual living. the theological term unregenerate means not recreated or or not reborn.\r\nthe unregenerate are spiritually dead.\r\nthe unregenerate remains in the state of spiritual  death because they haven\'t\' been renewed or regenerated by the holy spirit.\r\n the regenerate are those who have been born again through the work of the holy spirit. when that happens , our spirits are generated or given new life.', 8, '2020-12-23 17:49:16', '2021-01-09 14:49:10', NULL),
(2287, 620, 639, 'Spiritual death:\r\nwhen we die physically our souls are separated from our bodies . our bodies are incapable of independent action, and eventually decay to the point that they return to dust. but when we are dead spiritually our souks are still alive in our bodies, the unregenerate continue to think feel dream, make choices and engage with the world in almost every way the regenerate do. \r\nwhen God had made with Adam , God cursed the, they didn\'t die that day, at least not physically, but they became spiritually corrupt.\r\nand this spiritual corruption  is the essence of spiritual death, in Romans 7:14 -25, we read Paul referred to this as our sinful nature. He described by saying that sin lives inside our very bodies and even take control of our minds. spiritual death effects all the naturally conceived descendants of Adam and eve.\r\nJohn3:5-7 , Romans 8:10, Colossians 2:13 indicated that every human being except for Jesus comes into this world Spiritually dead. Adam was our representative and therefore we share in his punishment.\r\nMoral inability:\r\nAugustine the bishop of Hippo, taught that before humanity\'s first sin, Adam and Eve lived in the state of Posse non peccare, this Latin Phrase means \"to be able not to sin\" or the ability not to sin.\r\nAn when Adam and Eve sinned, they moved from the state of Posse non peccare to the state of non ;posse non peccare- the inability not to sin,\r\nthey and all their naturally generated descendants lost the moral ability to avoid sin.\r\nJohn8:31-44', 9, '2020-12-23 18:02:56', '2021-01-09 14:49:10', NULL),
(2288, 620, 640, 'The regenerate are those who have been born again through the work of Holy spirit. when that happens our spirits are regenerated or given new life. \r\nSpiritual life:\r\nthe Bible teaches that all human beings are born into a state of spiritual death, so in order for us to be regenerate to have spiritual life our souls have to pass from death into life, John 5:24 and Ephesians 2:4 and 5 in order for us to be receive God\'s blessings like salvation , we have to undergo a second birth, a spiritual one John 3:3-6.\r\nJesus taught that we can only have Spiritual life when the Holy spirit gives birth to our spirit. an d that life never ends , even when our earthly bodies die. our souls will forever.\r\nMoral ability: the term moral ability refers to then capacity to please God and merit his blessings.\r\nbut Augustine also taught us when the Holy Spirit regenerates our souks giving us spiritual life, he revitalizes our moral ability, he moves us back into state of posse non peccare  that means the regenerate regain the ability to avoid sin and to please God.\r\nGod prepares good works for the regenerate. the sin still battles in our, but the Spirit indwells in us Romans 7:23', 9, '2020-12-23 18:14:32', '2021-01-09 14:49:10', NULL),
(2289, 620, 641, 'When our souls die, they don\'t cease to exist. Our souls continue to exist, to think and feel even after our bodies die. for the unregenerate the facts that their souls continue to exist  should cause great fear. because when their spirit returns to God who gave it, its not to enjoy blessings, kits to begin suffering their just punishment in hell Luke 12:4-5\r\n Hell\r\n scripture uses many different words:\r\n1. Sheol in psalms 9:17, the home to unregenerate souls\r\n 2. Abaddon means destruction , Job 26:6\r\n3. Bowr or pit a place of punishment in Isaiah 14:15-19\r\n4. Hades or heydeez a place of punishment in Luke 10:15\r\n5. Abusso or Abysss a prison for  demons Luke 8:13\r\n6. Gehenna or geena  a place of fiery punishment , torment or destruction. Mathew 5:22\r\nthe clearest picture we get of how the unregenerate souls experience the intermediate state torment Luke 16:19-31.', 9, '2020-12-23 18:25:16', '2021-01-09 14:49:10', NULL),
(2290, 620, 642, 'Regenerate souls continue tom exist just like unregenerate souls, but with lost of blessings\r\nLord\'s presence:\r\nwhen they die, their souls are immediately taken into God\'s presence in heaven . Luke 23:43 Jesus told the penitent thief.\r\nRevelation 6:9 \r\nwhen they die, their bodies rest in the grave, but their souls are immediately taken , where they remain until the last resurrection, we have also learned about soul sleep. which means that god does not take our souls to heaven  immediately when we die, instead our souls remain with our bodies unconscious until Jesus returns\r\nFellowship:\"\r\n Revelation 6:9-10 says that we have fellowship with one another an s we speak together with one voice as they appeal to god to hasten the final judgement\r\nPerfect holiness:\r\n when our souls are separated from our bodies  by physical death, sin loses its hold on us and we eagerly avoid all sins from that point on. Hebrews 12:23 says the spirit of righteous men are made perfect.', 9, '2020-12-23 18:35:58', '2021-01-09 14:49:10', NULL),
(2291, 618, 791, 'As per the Westminster Shorter Catechism, \r\nSIN is any want of conformity or transgression against the law of God\r\nDisdain and complete disregard for God\'s law were central to humanity\'s first sin and they continue to characterize human race.\r\n\r\nWhen God created the first human race - Adam and EVe, they were sinless, they had no predisposition to sin and no reason to sin. God had given them authority to rule over other creatures that God had created. Clearly God had given them clear prohibition - they were forbidden to eat the fruit of the tree of the Knowledge of Good and Evil. There were 99% possibilities and probabilities to obey God and 1% prohibition to round it up to 100%. But unfortunately Adam and Eve fell for that 1% to round the negativity to 100%, leaving aside every other probability in obeying God - and hence disobeyed the very first covenant made with humanity represented through Adam and Eve. \r\n\r\nIn Genesis 3:1-7, the serpent tempted Eve to eat the forbidden fruit, and she gave some of it to Adam and he ate too. Immediately they realized they were naked and felt shame. that\'s the origin of sin in the human race. \r\n\r\nGod confronted Adam and Eve and cursed them because of their disloyalty. These events are summarized as The Fall of mankind, sin caused humanity to fall out of God\'s favor and blessings. \r\n\r\nAs a result of the fall, God judged man and women and indeed the whole of creation. Mankind experienced death because of the sin.\r\n\r\nAdam and Eve sinned and their sin was the origin of sin in the human race.', 9, '2020-12-24 15:59:06', '2021-01-09 14:51:33', NULL),
(2292, 618, 792, 'The idea of authorship of human sin is important since many opponents of Christianity have accursed God of authorizing humanity\'s fall into sin.\r\n\r\nAfter Adam and Eve ate the forbidden fruit, God judged the serpent, Adam and Eve. In the course of that judgement, Adam and Eve both tried to shift the blame to someone else. Adam was the first one to try to shift the blame to someone else. \r\n\r\nAdam blamed Eve, Eve blamed the serpent - \r\n\r\nAdam didn\'t deny eating the fruit, but he did attempt to avoid being held responsible. First he blamed his wife, who had givem him the fruit to eat. And second he blamed God, since God had created Eve. \r\n\r\nBoth Adam and Eve tried to arguie that the blame or authorship of their sin should be placed on someone else. And they tried achieving this to avoid punishment. But God didnt agree with their reasoning. \r\n\r\nAs far as God was concerned Adam and Eve were guilty as they chose to disobey his command.\r\n\r\nFrom scripture we can understand that the ultimate author of sin was the serpent, as it was the first character to come up with the idea to sin and first one to try to cause humanity to  sin. But Adam and Eve also contributed free choices to this event and in this sense authored human sin.\r\n\r\nBoth Adam and Eve knew God\'s command and freely chose to disobey him. There was no compulsion from any internal and external power. Their minds and choices were their own. In this way, humanity was culpable for their sin, and God was not.', 8, '2020-12-24 16:17:33', '2021-01-09 14:51:33', NULL),
(2293, 618, 793, 'Original sin refers to the consequences of humanity\'s first sin. Adam\'s disobedience negatively impacts every human being that naturally descends from him. \r\n\r\nOriginal sin, is sin that at bottom a person has possessed since he was born And a person cannot evade this sin. Every person who is born must accept this because people are born from a sinful lineage.\r\n\r\nWhen they sinned they lost ther sinless ability and retained only the ability to sin. The corruption they suffered removed their capacity to please God and to merit his blessings and left them only with the ability to sin.\r\n\r\nJesus and Paul compared this state of moral corruption to being slaves of sin. We all remain in this state of corruption until God saves us. \r\n\r\nJesus compared this corrupt state to a bad tree that can only produce bad fruit. He didn\'t mean that unsaved fallen humanity never does anything outwardly good. \r\n\r\nThe mind of sinful man is death, as their mind is hostile to God.\r\n\r\nFallen humanity, our corruption extends to every facet of human nature, every faculty of our human nature gets affected.\r\n\r\nThe 3 main areas werhein sin affects corruption\r\n\r\nCONCEPTS\r\nDue to sin both Adam and Eve\'s concepts were corrupted they disobeyed God and believed the lies of the serpent. But the most terrible thing about these corruption is that they were passed on to all human beings.\r\nSin has damaged humanity\'s capacity for conceptual thought and caused us to believe that false ideas are true. Sin has made us insane in many ways, we don\'t value what God and we commit ourselves to evil\r\n\r\nBEHAVIORS\r\nAdam and Eve\'s behavior was probably the most obvious aspect of their sin they ate the forbidden fruit. Humanity sinned and God has given us over to our sinful appetities.\r\nBehaviroal pattern was completely affected. Humanity was filled with every kind of wickedness and disobedience against God and with other humans.\r\n\r\nEMOTIONS :\r\nLove is an emotion that motivates us to bedience. Sinful corruption affects our emotions preventing us from loving God and our neighbors preventing us from ma', 9, '2020-12-24 16:37:36', '2021-01-09 14:51:33', NULL),
(2294, 618, 794, 'God told Adam that if he ate the fruit of the Tree of the knowledge of good and evil he would die. \r\n\r\nAfter the sin God cursed Adam to physical death. As covenant head of the entire human race, his rebellion against God affected our entire human race and fell under the shoadow of his guilt and consequently under the curse of death.\r\n\r\nPaul said that all sinned because God reckoned Adam\'s guilt not only to Adam, but to the rest of natural humanity too.\r\n\r\nAdam didn\'t die immediately when God cursed him, not physically though, but he died spiritually when he was cursed and his descendants are spiritually dead as well.\r\n\r\nPaul says in Ephesians that we are dead in our sins and our transgressions in which we used to live when you followed the ways of this world.\r\n\r\nAs natural descendants of Adam and Eve, the fallen nature was automatilly passed on to  the descendants - all the character of the fallen nature is manifested in all of humans which originated at the Fall of mankind. Human beings suffer immediate spiritual death. But worst if we never come to faith in Christ. If we are redeemed from sin\'s curse by God\'s grace, we will continue to suffer both spiritual and physical death. The curst of death and sin is very real.\r\n\r\nBut by God\'s grace we can over come sin\'s influence if we trust in the salvation that was appointed by God, by faith in the Lord Jesus Christ who will enable us to overcome all the sins and curses due to the fall of mankind.', 8, '2020-12-24 16:53:58', '2021-01-09 14:51:33', NULL),
(2295, 618, 795, 'When God created Adam and Eve he gave them the full liberty and choice either to obey God or to disobey Him. God who in his foreknowledge knew everything beforehand. But both Adam and Eve knoews God\'s command and freely chose to disobey him. There was no compulsion from any internal or external power. Their minds and choices were their own, in this way humanity as a whole was culpable for their sin. Sin was passed on to the descendants.\r\n\r\nSin crept into humanity through the disobedience of one man. Furthermore the fallen nature of mankind was evident in all facets of human nature. Violenece, wickedness and self interest preceded over all other relationships, resulting in pain, sorrow and death Sin took the precedence over everything else and damaged man\'s relationship with God and also reflecting in the behavior pattern with the rest of the humans.\r\n\r\nThough God is love, yet God gave mankind the free choice or will power to chose their paths whether in obedience or disobedience, thereby going through the consequence of sin and pain and death.\r\n\r\nBut inspite of all the curses and disobedience God\'s love was evident in Genesis 3:15 wherein God also promised a future redemption for all of mankind. \r\n\r\nThere was a ray of hope for all humans to go through the plan of salvation that God himself had authorized.\r\n\r\nThough our God is a loving God, yet he allows all humans to go through a process. God allows us to learn through our mistakes and in the process have faith not in our own abilities but to blindly trust in God\'s path of salvation and beliefs. \r\n\r\nAll humans have to go through this life cycle, where the main turning poing it whether to Obey God or Disobey God - accordingly we will meet our destiny. \r\n\r\nOur choices and decisions determine our destiny in life. If we are willing to be governed by a loving God or whether we choose to rebel against God and his will,\r\n\r\nBut in all these processes of life that we go through all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, but there is hope if we believe in the salvation that God has appointed for us, we are freely justified through the redemption in Christ Jesus.\r\n\r\nHaving faith in God and his promises we are able to overcome Sin, Pain and even Death and get ready for the eternity that God has prepared for us.\r\n\r\nThis journey of ours in this world is temporary and we look forward to spend our entire eternity with God.', 9, '2020-12-24 17:11:41', '2021-01-09 14:51:33', NULL),
(2296, 621, 727, 'Ans. :-  उद्देश्य :--  एक अर्थ में, मरकुस और अन्य सारे सुसमाचार ओं का एक ही उद्देश्य था यीशु के जीवन और शिक्षाओं के सच्चे ऐतिहासिक अभिलेख को सुरक्षित रखना। ईस्वी 50 के बाद से यीशु के जीवन, मृत्यु और पुनरुत्थान के गवाहों और प्रेरितो की मृत्यु होती जा रही थी। इसलिए, उनकी गवाही को सुरक्षित रखने की आवश्यकता बढ़ती जा रही थी। जैसे यूसेबीयस और अन्य आरंभिक कलीसियाई लेखकों ने बताया, मरकुस का आंशिक उद्देश्य यीशु की सेवकाई के पतरस के अभिलेख को सुरक्षित रखना था। \r\n           परंतु इस अभिलेख को सुरक्षित रखना ही मरकुस का एकमात्र उद्देश्य नहीं था। प्रत्येक सुसमाचार लेखक के सामान ,मरकुस केवल यह नहीं चाहता था कि उसके पाठक यीशु के बारे में जाने। वह यह भी चाहता था कि वे यीशु के जीवन से बातों को सीखें जिन्हें वे अपने स्वयं के जीवन में लागू कर सकें। परंतु उनके जीवन कैसे थे ? \r\n         रोम में  ईस्वी 60 का दशक मसीहियो के लिए वास्तव में एक कठिन समय था। आपको याद करना होगा कि उस समय तक रोमी कानून में यदि आप एक यहूदी थे तो आप एक मान्यता प्राप्त धर्म के अंग थे, इसे वैध धर्म, एक स्वीकृत धर्म माना जाता था। और इसलिए यहूदी मसीहीयो पर अधिक अत्याचार नहीं हो रहे थे क्योंकि रोमी सोचते थे, \"वे तो यहूदी धर्म के ही भाग हैं।\" परंतु क्या होता है जब रोमी अधिकारियों को यह अहसास होने लगता है, \"अरे इस नये धर्म में तो गैर-यहूदी, अन्यजाति सब शामिल है और यहूदी धर्म से बहुत अलग है?\" अचानक ही यह एक सुरक्षित या स्वीकृत धर्म नहीं रह जाता है और रोमी अधिकारी उसके बारे में चिंतित हो सकते हैं। अब, ईस्वी 60 के दशक के आरंभ में रोम में ठीक यही होता है या पहचाना जाता है वर्ष 59 में न्यूरो थोड़ा पागल हो जाता है, उसके राज्य के पहले पांच वर्ष कुछ अच्छे थे परंतु उस समय से ईस्वी 68 में उसकी मृत्यु तक, वह अधिक से अधिक अप्रत्याशित होता जाता है। फिर ईस्वी 60 के लगभग पौलुस रोम में पहुंचता है और वह मसीह के लिए मरने और यह दिखाने का इच्छुक है कि उसका नया धर्म सम्राट नीरो सहित सब लोगों के लिए है। और यह संभव है कि जब नीरो का इससे सामना होता है तो उसे यह अहसास होने लगता है कि यह एक ऐसा धर्म है जो मुझे पसंद नहीं है।अधिकार मेरे पास है और ये मसीही घोषणा कर रहे हैं कि \"यीशु प्रभु है।\" अतः ,जब 18 जुलाई, सन 64 में रोम में आग लगती है और उसका दोष नीरो पर लगाया जाता है, तो नीरो वह दोष इस नए समूह पर लगा देता है जो मसीही कहलाते हैं, और उसके बारे में हम जो सुनते हैं वह दु:खद है। ( डॉ.पीटर वॉकर ) \r\n        जिन वर्षों के दौरान संभवतः मरकुस लिख रहा था, उस समय रोम की कलीसिया रोमी सम्राट नीरो के अधीन अत्याचार सह रही थी। नीरो ने ईस्वी 54 से 68 तक राज्य किया। वह  ईस्वी 64 में रोम में आग लगाने का दोष मसीहियो पर लगाने और उन्हें भयानक तरीकों से दण्ड देने के लिए कुख्यात है। \r\n               नीरो के शासन के अधीन, रोम ने कलीसिया पर घोर अत्याचार किया। आरंभ में, रोम का गठन एक गणतंत्र के रूप में किया गया था। बाद में, जूलियस कैसर की हत्या के बाद, अग्स्तुस ने सेना की कमान संभालकर रोम नगर पर अधिकार कर लिया और सीनेट को भंग कर दिया। अतः रोमी गणराज्य रोमी साम्राज्य बन गया और  अग्स्तुस उस का पहला सम्राट बना। यहीं से रोमी क्रूरता के इतिहास की शुरुआत होती है। यथार्थ में, मसीहीयो पर अत्याचार के मामले में नीरो सबसे बुरा नहीं था। अन्य सम्राटों ने मसीहियत पर और भी घातक हमले किए और ऐतिहासिक अभिलेखों के अनुसार बहुत से मसीहियो को क्रूस पर कीलों से ठोककर मारा गया या जीवित जला दिया गया। आरम्भिक कलिसिया में बहुत से मसीह शहीद हुए थे जिन्होंने अपनी मृत्यु के द्वारा परमेश्वर की करुणा और धार्मिकता की गवाही दी।( डॉ.स्टीफन चान )   \r\n         इस अवधि के दौरान रोम और उसके आस-पास मसीहीयो के लिए जीवन कई प्रकार से कठिन था। और जैसा हम देखेंगे, मरकुस ने उनकी परिस्थितियों में सेवा के लिए अपने सुसमाचार को तैयार किया। मरकुस के लिखने के उद्देश्य का वर्णन करने के बहुत से तरीके हैं परंतु इस अध्याय में हम इस विचार पर ध्यान केंद्रित करेंगे कि मरकुस ने अपने सुसमाचार को रोम के अत्याचार से पीड़ित मसीहीयो को बल देने के लिए लिखा। \r\n               मरकुस के सुसमाचार ने यह स्पष्ट किया कि रोमी मसीहीयो ने जिन कठिनाइयों और परीक्षाओं का सामना किया, यीशु पहले ही उनका सामना कर चुका था। उसे रोमी न्यायालय में अन्यायपूर्ण तरीके से दोषी ठहराया गया। उसे रोमी सैनिकों द्वारा पीटा गया और उसे एक रोमी क्रूस पर लटकाया गया। परंतु अपने कष्टों के बीच में यीशु विजयी रहा। और मरकुस अपने पाठकों को भरोसा दिलाना चाहता था कि यदि वे विश्वासयोग्यता से यीशु के पीछे चलते हैं तो वे भी विजयी होंगे। हां, उन्हें कष्ट सहना होगा। परंतु उनका कष्ट उनकी महिमा का मार्ग होगा, जैसा यीशु के लिए था।', 9, '2021-01-07 04:35:50', '2021-01-09 14:52:06', NULL),
(2297, 621, 728, 'Ans. :--  संरचना एवं विषय सूची :-- एक बड़े स्तर पर मरकुस का सुसमाचार पांच मुख्य भागों में विभाजित है। पहला, मरकुस अपने सुसमाचार की शुरुआत  1:1-13 में यीशु की मसीहा के रूप में संक्षिप्त घोषणा के साथ करता है। दूसरा, 1:14-8:26 में एक बड़ा भाग मसीहा की सामर्थ का वर्णन करता है। तीसरा, 8:27-30 में एक छोटा परंतु प्रमुख केंद्रीय भाग प्रेरितो द्वारा मसीहा की पुष्टि को प्रस्तुत करता है। चौथा, एक और बड़ा भाग 8:31-15:47 में मसीहा के कष्ट के बारे में बताता है। और पांचवा, एक लघु और उपसंहार है जो 16:1-8 में मसीहा की विजय के बारे में बताता है। हम मसीहा की घोषणा से शुरू करेंगे और मरकुस के इनमें से प्रत्येक भाग को कुछ गहराई तक देखेंगे। मसीहा की घोषणा :-- सुने मरकुस 1:1 में सुसमाचार की शुरुआत कैसे होती है :\r\nपरमेश्वर के पुत्र यीशु मसीह के सुसमाचार का आरंभ।  जब मरकुस ने  यीशु को \"मसीह\" के रूप में संबोधित किया तो उसने इब्रानी शब्द मसीहा के यूनानी अनुवाद का प्रयोग किया। इसी प्रकार परमेश्वर का पुत्र शब्द एक और संकेत था कि यीशु परमेश्वर का मसीहा है। पुराने नियम और तत्कालिन यहूदी धर्म विज्ञान में मसीहा को राजा दाऊद का वंशज होना तथा जो शाही सिहासन को इस्राएल को वापस लौटाएगा और देश को पृथ्वी पर परमेश्वर के राज्य की ओर लाएगा। \r\n        इस शुरुआती घोषणा के बाद मरकुस 1:2-11 में यूहन्ना बपतिस्मा दाता के द्वारा यीशु के बपतिस्मा का संक्षिप्त अभिलेख दिया गया है। यीशु के बपतिस्मा के अंत में पवित्र आत्मा यीशु पर उतरा और स्वर्ग से पिता परमेश्वर की आवाज यह घोषणा करते हुए सुनाई दी कि यीशु उसका प्रिय पुत्र था। इस प्रकार आत्मा और पिता दोनों ने यह प्रमाणित किया कि यीशु वास्तव में बहु-प्रतीक्षित मसीहा था। \r\n       यह सोचना बहुत ही रुचिकर है कि यीशु के समय के यहूदी मसीहा के आगमन के संबंध में क्या सोचते थे। और वास्तव में, उनके बीच में बहुत सारे विविध प्रकार के विचार थे। मृत सागर चर्मपत्रों से हमारे पास कुछ अभिलेख हैं जो यह दिखाते हैं कि कुछ लोगों का यह विश्वास था कि दो मसीही आएंगे, एक याजकीय मसीह और राजकीय मसीहा। परंतु मुख्यधारा की अपेक्षा यह थी कि मसीहा दाऊद का पुत्र होगा और वह राजनैतिक शांति स्थापित करेगा और रोमियो को खदेड़ देगा। मेरे विचार से धार्मिक केंद्र का यह मानना था कि यदि इस्राएल व्यवस्था का पालन करेगा तो राज्य आ जाएगा। वे यीशु को इस संबंध में वास्तव में एक ख़तरा मानते थे क्योंकि इस व्यवस्था को मानने की अपेक्षा उसे तोड़ने को बढ़ावा देता लगता था। कम से कम उनकी दृष्टि में। उसके पास वह राजनीतिक बल नहीं था जिसकी उन्होंने अपेक्षा की थी फिर व्यवस्था के संबंध में भी उसने वह नहीं किया जिसकी उन्हें अपेक्षा थी, और इसलिए मेरे विचार में वे उसके कारण दुविधा में थे। वास्तव में, मेरे विचार में अंततः वे यीशु को व्यवस्था विवरण 13 के अर्थ में एक ऐसे व्यक्ति के रूप में देखते थे जो चिन्ह, चमत्कार और आश्यकर्म तो करता था परंतु एक झूठा भविष्यवक्ता था और उसे मार डाला जाना चाहिए था। (डॉ थांमस श्रइनरn) \r\n         परंतु यीशु उस प्रकार का मसीहा नहीं था जैसी बहुत से लोगों ने अपेक्षा की थी। सामान्यतः, पहली सदी के यहूदीयो का सोचना था कि मसीहा इस्राएल में आएगा और शासन को अपने हाथ में लेगा परंतु मरकुस 1:12-13 में, हम देखते हैं कि यीशु के बपतिस्मा के तुरंत बाद पवित्रआत्मा ने उसे शैतान द्वारा परीक्षा के लिए मारूभूमि में भेज दिया। अंत में वह विजयी हुआ। परंतु परमेश्वर की योजना के अनुसार विजय का उसका मार्ग कठिनाई से भरा लंबा रास्ता था। यीशु की मसीहा के रूप में घोषणा के बाद, मरकुस 1:14-8:26 में मरकुस ने मसीहा की सामर्थ का वर्णन किया।', 9, '2021-01-07 04:38:59', '2021-01-09 14:52:06', NULL),
(2298, 621, 729, 'Ans. :- इन यहूदी अपेक्षाओं को मन में रखते हुए, आइए हम कष्ट सहने वाले सेवक के रूप में यीशु की सेवकाई की ओर मुड़ते हैं। \r\nयीशु की सेवकाई :---  यीशु एक चौकाने वाला मसीहा था क्योंकि उसने मरने के द्वारा जय पाई। सुसमाचार के आरंभ में, मरकुस ने संघर्षों का वर्णन किया जो अंततः उसकी क्रूस की मृत्यु की ओर ले गए। और सुसमाचार के उत्तरार्ध में पहले यीशु के आने वाले कष्ट और उसकी मृत्यु का विषय हावी है और बाद में उसके वास्तविक कष्ट और मृत्यु का विषय। \r\n           और यीशु के कष्ट पर महत्व के समानान्तर  उसकी सेवा पर भी महत्व दिया गया है। उसने बहुत से विभिन्न प्रकार के लोगों को चंगा किया और उनके बीच से सेवकाई की। उसने पापियों को छुड़ाने के लिए छुडौती के रूप में अपना जीवन दे दिया। उसने परमेश्वर लोगों के लाभ के लिए प्रत्येक मोड पर परमेश्वर की इच्छा को पूरा किया। \r\n     एक जगह जहां मरकुस ने कष्ट और सेवा के इन विषयों को सामर्थी रुप से जोड़ा, वह मरकुस 10:35-45 है। इस  परिच्छेद में, याकूब और यूहन्ना ने यीशु के राज्य में आदर के स्थान मांगे। तब दूसरे 10 चेले महिमा की इस महत्वकांक्षा पर क्रोधित हो गए। परंतु यीशु ने उन बारहो को डांटा। उसने उन सब से सेवा के एक जीवन का अनुरोध किया और अपने स्वयं के जीवन को एक आदर्श के रूप में पेश किया। \r\n        सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व की मांग केवल इतनी है कि  अगुवा सेव काई के जीवन में सक्रिय रहे और पौलुस के शब्दों में, अपने जीवन को दूसरों की सेवा में, दूसरों की सहायता में, और दूसरों को तैयार करने में उण्डेल देने का इच्छुक हो। और इसलिए अगुवा केवल आज्ञा ही नहीं देता कि जाओ और करो- हां, आज्ञा दी जाती है परंतु लक्ष्यों और उद्देश्यों को पूरा करने के मार्ग में अगुवा अगुवाई करता है। और यह मुझे परिश्रम करने के बारे में पौलुस की बात का स्मरण दिलाता है। उसने कहा कि उसने इस संबंध में अपने सारे साथियों से बढ़कर परिश्रम किया। और यह आपको इसका अद्भुत अहसास दिलाता है कि एक सेवकरूपी अगुवा होने का क्या अर्थ है, लोगों के बीच उतरना और बोझ उठाने में सहायता करना और छुटकारे के लिए बोझ उठाना। ( रेव्ह. लैरी कोक्रेल) \r\n         मरकुस 10:45 में इस प्रकार के नेतृत्व के बारे में यीशु के स्पष्टीकरण को सुने :\r\n          क्योंकि मनुष्य का पुत्र इसलिए नहीं आया, कि उसकी सेवा टहल की जाए, पर इसलिए आया, कि आप सेवा टहल करें, और बहुतों की छुडौती के लिए अपना प्राण दे। (मरकुस 10:45)\r\n              यीशु ने स्पष्ट किया कि नेतृत्व परमेश्वर और  अधीनस्थों की सेवा का एक रूप है। नेतृत्व महिमा का एक अवसर नहीं है। इसके विपरीत, इसका परिणाम बहुधा अगुवो के लिए कष्ट के रूप में होता है। यथार्थ में, यीशु जानता था कि दूसरों की सेवा का उसका मिशन अंततः उसे मृत्यु की ओर ले जाएगा। परंतु उसने इसी मिशन को अपनाया और उसने अपने चेलों को भी इसे अपनाने की आज्ञा दी। \r\n     मरकुस के सुसमाचार में, वह विशेष रूप से इस बात के लिए उत्सुक प्रतीत होता है कि हमें इस बात का संदेश मिले कि यीशु ही वह कष्ट सहने वाला सेवक है जिसके बारे में पुराने नियम में भविष्यवाणी की गई है। सुसमाचार के पूर्वार्द्ध में राजा के रूप में इसकी भूमिका पर अत्यधिक बल दिया गया है, और उत्तरार्द्ध  में हम यीशु को कष्ट और मृत्यु की ओर बढ़ते हैं। और संभवतः एक मुख्य पद मरकुस 10:45 है, \"मनुष्य का पुत्र इसलिए नहीं आया, कि उसकी सेवा टहल की जाए, पर इसलिए आया, कि आप सेवा टहल करें, और बहुतों की  छुड़ौती के लिए अपना प्राण दे।\" और वह विशेष पद उस संदर्भ में आता है जिसमें चेलों को उस प्रकार के सेवक बनने का प्रोत्साहन मिलता है कि हमारे अधिकारों का प्रयोग न करने के विषय में यीशु हमारा आदर्श है, और हम सुसमाचार के प्रसार और परमेश्वर के राज्य के प्रसार की खातिर अपने अधिकारों को त्याग देते हैं। और मरकुस वास्तव में हमें इसका एक आदर्श देता है कि कैसे हमें अधिकार नहीं जताना चाहिए, हमें स्वर्ग में सबसे अच्छे स्थानों की प्रतीक्षा नहीं करनी चाहिए जैसे कि प्रायः चेलों की प्रवृत्ति थी। इसके विपरीत, हम मसीह के आदर्श का पालन करते हैं जिसने स्वेच्छा से दूसरों की खातिर स्वयं को बलिदान कर दिया, और हम उसी आदर्श का पालन करते हैं। (डॉक्टर साइमन विबर्ट)  \r\n    अतः यदि हम यह सोचते हैं कि प्रभु यीशु द्वारा दी गई यह सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व की आज्ञा व्यवहार में कैसी है, तो हम सुसमाचारो में देख सकते हैं कि यीशु कैसे उस प्रकार के नेतृत्व का प्रदर्शन करते हैं। विभिन्न प्रकार के लोगों और उनकी आवश्यकताओं से सामना होने पर जो पहली बात हम यीशु को करते हुए देखते हैं, वह यह है कि यीशु बहुत बहुत ध्यान से सुनते हैं। वे लोगों से उनके स्थान पर मिलते हैं। यीशु उनके जीवन की भौतिक आवश्यकताओं, उनके जीवन की भावनात्मक  आवश्यकताओं पर ध्यान देते हैं, वह केवल उनके शब्दों को नहीं बल्कि उनके अर्थ को समझने के लिए बहुत ध्यान लगाकर सुनते हैं। उदाहरण के लिए, मरकुस के सुसमाचार में हम एक पिता के साथ मुलाकात को देख सकते हैं जिसका पुत्र वर्षों से दुष्टआत्मा से ग्रस्त था, इतना कि वह स्वयं को घायल करता था और पिता को यह महसूस नहीं होता है कि अब वह कोई आशा रख सकता है। वह कहता है, \"मेरे अविश्वास का उपाय कर।\" आत: उसे झिड़कने की बजाय यीशु उसका उत्तर देते हैं और उसके पुत्र को चंगा करते हैं। और फिर हम सुसमाचार के इस मोड़ को देखते हैं, कि यीशु कहां जा रहे हैं? यीशु अपने अनुयायियों के पापों का मूल्य चुकाने के लिए अपना प्राण देने यरुशलेम जा रहे है। बलिदानी प्रेम की यह अभिव्यक्ति उस समय की संस्कृति के अर्थ में स्वयं के लिए किसी भी महिमा को अलग कर देती है, और फिर अपने अनुयायियों के लिए अपना प्राण देना। सुसमाचार में यीशु इस प्रारूप का प्रदर्शन करते हैं कि वह किस प्रकार लोगों की परवाह करते हैं और उनकी सुनते हैं, और फिर वह कहां जा रहे हैं, अपना प्राण देने यरूशलेम जा रहे हैं। यही सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व है। (डॉक्टर ग्रेग पेरी)', 9, '2021-01-07 04:42:19', '2021-01-09 14:52:06', NULL),
(2299, 621, 730, 'Ans. हम अपनी वर्तमान सेवकाई मे सेवक संबंधी अगुवाई को प्रभु यीशु और प्रेरितो की सेवकाई को ध्यान मे रख कर सेवकाई को दर्शा सकते है।\r\n     सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व की मांग केवल इतनी है कि  अगुवा सेव काई के जीवन में सक्रिय रहे और पौलुस के शब्दों में, अपने जीवन को दूसरों की सेवा में, दूसरों की सहायता में, और दूसरों को तैयार करने में उण्डेल देने का इच्छुक हो। और इसलिए अगुवा केवल आज्ञा ही नहीं देता कि जाओ और करो- हां, आज्ञा दी जाती है परंतु लक्ष्यों और उद्देश्यों को पूरा करने के मार्ग में अगुवा अगुवाई करता है। और यह मुझे परिश्रम करने के बारे में पौलुस की बात का स्मरण दिलाता है। उसने कहा कि उसने इस संबंध में अपने सारे साथियों से बढ़कर परिश्रम किया। और यह आपको इसका अद्भुत अहसास दिलाता है कि एक सेवकरूपी अगुवा होने का क्या अर्थ है, लोगों के बीच उतरना और बोझ उठाने में सहायता करना और छुटकारे के लिए बोझ उठाना। \r\n          क्योंकि मनुष्य का पुत्र इसलिए नहीं आया, कि उसकी सेवा टहल की जाए, पर इसलिए आया, कि आप सेवा टहल करें, और बहुतों की छुडौती के लिए अपना प्राण दे। (मरकुस 10:45)\r\n              यीशु ने स्पष्ट किया कि नेतृत्व परमेश्वर और  अधीनस्थों की सेवा का एक रूप है। नेतृत्व महिमा का एक अवसर नहीं है। इसके विपरीत, इसका परिणाम बहुधा अगुवो के लिए कष्ट के रूप में होता है। यथार्थ में, यीशु जानता था कि दूसरों की सेवा का उसका मिशन अंततः उसे मृत्यु की ओर ले जाएगा। परंतु उसने इसी मिशन को अपनाया और उसने अपने चेलों को भी इसे अपनाने की आज्ञा दी। \r\n     मरकुस के सुसमाचार में, वह विशेष रूप से इस बात के लिए उत्सुक प्रतीत होता है कि हमें इस बात का संदेश मिले कि यीशु ही वह कष्ट सहने वाला सेवक है जिसके बारे में पुराने नियम में भविष्यवाणी की गई है। सुसमाचार के पूर्वार्द्ध में राजा के रूप में इसकी भूमिका पर अत्यधिक बल दिया गया है, और उत्तरार्द्ध  में हम यीशु को कष्ट और मृत्यु की ओर बढ़ते हैं। और संभवतः एक मुख्य पद मरकुस 10:45 है, \"मनुष्य का पुत्र इसलिए नहीं आया, कि उसकी सेवा टहल की जाए, पर इसलिए आया, कि आप सेवा टहल करें, और बहुतों की  छुड़ौती के लिए अपना प्राण दे।\" और वह विशेष पद उस संदर्भ में आता है जिसमें चेलों को उस प्रकार के सेवक बनने का प्रोत्साहन मिलता है कि हमारे अधिकारों का प्रयोग न करने के विषय में यीशु हमारा आदर्श है, और हम सुसमाचार के प्रसार और परमेश्वर के राज्य के प्रसार की खातिर अपने अधिकारों को त्याग देते हैं। और मरकुस वास्तव में हमें इसका एक आदर्श देता है कि कैसे हमें अधिकार नहीं जताना चाहिए, हमें स्वर्ग में सबसे अच्छे स्थानों की प्रतीक्षा नहीं करनी चाहिए जैसे कि प्रायः चेलों की प्रवृत्ति थी। इसके विपरीत, हम मसीह के आदर्श का पालन करते हैं जिसने स्वेच्छा से दूसरों की खातिर स्वयं को बलिदान कर दिया, और हम उसी आदर्श का पालन करते हैं। (डॉक्टर साइमन विबर्ट)  \r\n    अतः यदि हम यह सोचते हैं कि प्रभु यीशु द्वारा दी गई यह सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व की आज्ञा व्यवहार में कैसी है, तो हम सुसमाचारो में देख सकते हैं कि यीशु कैसे उस प्रकार के नेतृत्व का प्रदर्शन करते हैं। विभिन्न प्रकार के लोगों और उनकी आवश्यकताओं से सामना होने पर जो पहली बात हम यीशु को करते हुए देखते हैं, वह यह है कि यीशु बहुत बहुत ध्यान से सुनते हैं। वे लोगों से उनके स्थान पर मिलते हैं। यीशु उनके जीवन की भौतिक आवश्यकताओं, उनके जीवन की भावनात्मक  आवश्यकताओं पर ध्यान देते हैं, वह केवल उनके शब्दों को नहीं बल्कि उनके अर्थ को समझने के लिए बहुत ध्यान लगाकर सुनते हैं। उदाहरण के लिए, मरकुस के सुसमाचार में हम एक पिता के साथ मुलाकात को देख सकते हैं जिसका पुत्र वर्षों से दुष्टआत्मा से ग्रस्त था, इतना कि वह स्वयं को घायल करता था और पिता को यह महसूस नहीं होता है कि अब वह कोई आशा रख सकता है। वह कहता है, \"मेरे अविश्वास का उपाय कर।\" आत: उसे झिड़कने की बजाय यीशु उसका उत्तर देते हैं और उसके पुत्र को चंगा करते हैं। और फिर हम सुसमाचार के इस मोड़ को देखते हैं, कि यीशु कहां जा रहे हैं? यीशु अपने अनुयायियों के पापों का मूल्य चुकाने के लिए अपना प्राण देने यरुशलेम जा रहे है। बलिदानी प्रेम की यह अभिव्यक्ति उस समय की संस्कृति के अर्थ में स्वयं के लिए किसी भी महिमा को अलग कर देती है, और फिर अपने अनुयायियों के लिए अपना प्राण देना। सुसमाचार में यीशु इस प्रारूप का प्रदर्शन करते हैं कि वह किस प्रकार लोगों की परवाह करते हैं और उनकी सुनते हैं, और फिर वह कहां जा रहे हैं, अपना प्राण देने यरूशलेम जा रहे हैं। यही सेवकरूपी नेतृत्व है। (डॉक्टर ग्रेग पेरी)', 9, '2021-01-07 04:58:10', '2021-01-09 14:52:06', NULL),
(2300, 621, 731, 'Ans. हम प्रभु यीशु को अदुस्य रूप मे सहायक पवित्र आत्मा के रूप मे राज्य करते हुए देख रहे है। \r\nअदुश्श्स्पष्टतः  मसीहा का विचार पुराने नियम में अत्यधिक महत्वपूर्ण है। मसीहा अभिषिक्त है, अभिषिक्त राजा है। यह सोचना रुचिकर है कि यदि इस्राएल आज्ञाकारी होता और उसने मानवीय राजा की मांग नहीं की होती तो मसीह कैसे आता? परंतु उन्होंने मांगा। इसलिए, आप ऐसे \"अभिषिक्त\" को देख सकते हैं जो अपने लोगों की परवाह नहीं करते थे और जो संसार में परमेश्वर के न्याय की स्थापना नहीं कर रहे थे और जो स्व-केंद्रित थे। और इसलिए पुराने नियम यह चाहत उभरती है। \"काश, कोई ऐसा मसीहा होता जो वास्तव में वैसा ही होता जैसा एक मसीहा को होना चाहिए !\" और आने वाले राजा की तस्वीर को देखते हैं, परंतु विशेषतः यशायाह में इस आने वाले राजा और पवित्र आत्मा के बीच एक रुचिकर संबंध है। पुराने नियम के लोग केवल मसीहा को ही नहीं बल्कि यह भी चाहते हैं कि पवित्र आत्मा आकर उन्हें तोराह (व्यवस्था) को मानने में सामर्थ बनाएं। अतः यह देखना रुचिकर है कि विशेषतः यशायाह  किस प्रकार मसीहा और आत्मा के बीच संबंध को चित्रित करता है। वह आत्मा से परिपूर्ण होगा; वह पवित्र आत्मा द्वारा अभिषिक्त है। वह आत्मा के द्वारा बोलता है। और इसलिए प्रेरित आश्चर्यचकित प्रतीत नहीं होते हैं जब जी उठने पर यीशु उनसे कहते हैं, \"पिता की प्रतिज्ञा के लिए यरूशलेम में प्रतीक्षा करो।\" यह कुछ इस प्रकार है कि, \"अरे, अंततः मसीहा ने कुछ ऐसा कहा है जिसकी हमने मसीहा से अपेक्षा की थी: \'मैं पवित्र आत्मा को भेजने जा रहा हूंँ।\"\' परंतु उन्हें यह समझ नहीं आया कि परमेश्वर के न्याय और आत्मा के इस युग को लाने के लिए मसीहा को क्या कीमत चुकानी होगी। उन्होंने यशायाह 53 को  यशायाह 11 से नहीं जोड़ा। उन्होंने इस तथ्य को नहीं जोड़ा कि न्याय शांति और आत्मा के इस जीवन को लाने के लिए मसीहा को मरना होगा।\r\n       मरकुस का सुसमाचार यीशु के चरित्र और सेवकाई के उन पहलुओं पर प्रकाश डालता है जिन पर दूसरे सुसमाचारों में अधिकांश बल नहीं दिया जाता। यह हमें यीशु को अपने चारों ओर के शक्तिशाली, ऊर्जावान, सक्रिय स्वामी के रूप में दिखाता है। परंतु यह हमें इस बात को भी दिखाता है कि अपनी सामर्थ्य में भी यीशु ने स्वेच्छा से कष्ट सहने वाले सेवक की भूमिका को चुना। और मरकुस हमें  हमारे प्रभु के उदाहरण के प्रति विविध  प्रत्युत्तरो की ओर बुलाता है। वह चाहता है कि हम आश्चर्य में यीशु के चरणों पर गिरे, चुपचाप उसकी सुने, और पूरी आज्ञापालन के साथ उसके वचनों का प्रत्युत्तर दें। वह चाहता है कि हम यीशु के समान ही परमेश्वर के राज्य के लिए कष्ट सहने के लिए तैयार रहें।और वह चाहता है कि हम यह जानते हुए उत्साहित हो कि जब यीशु ने क्रूस के द्वारा अपने शत्रुओं पर जय पाई तो उसने हमारे लिए भी विजय सुनिश्चित की। और इसी आशा में हम उस दिन तक स्थिर रहते हैं जब यीशु  अपने राज्य की पूर्णता में अपनी महिमा के साथ हमें वह विजय देने के लिए लौटेगा।', 9, '2021-01-07 05:17:33', '2021-01-09 14:52:06', NULL),
(2301, 622, 786, 'The office of the image of God is rooted in the authority God gave to humanity to rule over his creation on his behalf.( genesis 1:27-28). So we hold the office of delegated ruler. In theological terms we are God\'s vassal regents - his administrative deputies or in ancient near eastern terms, his servant or vassal kings. \r\nHuman beings are physical representatives that remind all creation of God\'s power, authority and goodness. And through us, he manifest his rule over the world and all its creatures.\r\nThe nature of our role is first introduced in Genesis 1: 27-28. \" Be fruitful and increase in number; fill the earth and subdue it. Rule over the fish of the sea and the birds of the air and over every living creatures that moves on the ground. Just after the creation of man, God commanded his office and explained the nature of his role on the earth.', 7, '2021-01-08 09:38:53', '2021-01-09 14:52:36', NULL),
(2302, 622, 787, 'The first thing scripture tells about humanity is that we are the image and likeness of God. The term likeness further defines that we are not exact copies of God. So it is a representational dynamic, not a static copy of him. \r\nThe Hebrew words for Image and likeness are not identical. Image can be a curved or cast idol. By contrast likeness never represent an idol. It describes the appearance or sound of one thing by comparing it to another. \r\nAlthough not identical, the meaning of image and likeness overlap because they both describe a model or sketch of a greater reality. In a similar way human beings are images and likeness of God because we model God\'s power, authority and goodness. Many theologians argue that collective meaning while image points to our similarity with God, Likeness distinguishes between God and humanity, to avoid wrong assumption that we are exactly like God. \r\nOur office as the image of God elevates all humanity to the level of royalty; with a task of administering God\'s reign on earth. This gives us great dignity, but still under divine authority.', 8, '2021-01-08 09:49:37', '2021-01-09 14:52:36', NULL),
(2303, 622, 788, 'It is understood that image of God included the capacity to think rationally. Man used his rationale to give appropriate names to the animals in Genesis 2:19-20. Some of these rational abilities we lost by fall, as evidenced in many biblical passages that speak human of being irrational and insane at times. According to Deuteronomy 29: 2-3, the mind of Israel could not understand the significance of the miracles of God performed for them. In John 8, Jesus referred the unbelievers as the children of the devil who is the father of lies. As a result they believe lies and are incapable of accepting the truth. \"The gentiles live in futility of their thinking. They are darkened in their understanding and separated from the life of God because of the ignorance that  is in them due to the hardening of their hearts. ( Ephesians\".  (4:17-18)\r\nOur fall in to sin damaged our ability to think and to understand the world from God\'s perspective; though it was not destroyed completely.  Paul told in Colossians that the restoration of our rational abilities is an aspect of the image of God being renewed in us. \r\nThrough Christ God begins to restore the lost image of God in man by which we are able to think and understand more correctly, so that, our thought and reason become more aligned with his.', 8, '2021-01-08 10:02:32', '2021-01-09 14:52:36', NULL),
(2304, 622, 789, 'The three aspects of covenant relationship with God are : 1. Our obligation to reflect God\'s Character. 2. Our duty to promote pure worship. 3. Our responsibility to build God\'s Kingdom. \r\nMan is supposed to reflect the pure, holy and righteous character of God. 1 Peter 1:15-16. Ass fallen man fell short of reflecting God\'s character; we rely entirely on Christ\'s perfect holiness for our standing before God. \r\nAny form of false worship especially of idol is prohibited in scriptures. Moses reminded that God had not revealed himself in a physical form to protect the purity of worship. He wanted Israel\'s relationship with God pure through worship, unadulterated by the idolatrous theology and practice of the surrounding nations. \r\nWhen God commanded to fill the earth, he was instructing us to place the images of himself every where through out the world and demonstrate God\'s rule every where they go. The scripture says fallen human beings as against God\'s kingdom and belongs to the enemy Satan\'s Kingdom ( Ephesians 2: 1-2). Until we come to the faith in the Lord Jesus Christ, we continue to struggle against God\'s Kingdom. Nevertheless, all human beings are tasked with building God\'s Kingdom especially the spiritual kingdom in the New Testament perspective.', 8, '2021-01-08 10:15:48', '2021-01-09 14:52:36', NULL),
(2305, 622, 790, 'As the image of God, man was created to protect,  felicitate and  rule the world and all the creation on his behalf. But man lost his image and accordingly his authority and failed to fulfill his responsibility  towards God and the creation. but God in his mercy reinstated man to relationship with him. But in Christ Jesus and through the Covenant of grace only man is perfectly restored to perfect relationship with God to fulfill God given responsibilities. \r\nAs  ministers of the New Testament, primarily we are entitled to represent God or Jesus Christ in reflecting our Character to the world. In a lost world of sin and Chaos; We are appointed as light in the darkness. \r\nSecondly we are supposed to maintain true worship of God according to the scriptures. It\'s our responsibility to convince the world of their futile ways of false worship and lead them to the true worship and right relationship with God .\r\nAnd ultimately our responsibility is to expand the Kingdom of God through the preaching of the gospel of Jesus Christ. The devil\'s Kingdom is defeated and Christ\'s Kingdom is established through turning people from their wicked ways and to the saving knowledge of Jesus Christ. We use God given authority and the power of the Holy Spirit to establish God\'s Kingdom, His Church. \r\nIn my ministry I try to reflect God\'s character, to establish true worship and to establish God\'s Kingdom through preaching the gospel by using the divine authority vested on me.', 8, '2021-01-08 10:33:51', '2021-01-09 14:52:36', NULL),
(2306, 625, 791, 'According to west minister shorter catechism, sin is any want of conformity, or transgression of, the law of God. \r\nAdam and Eve were created sinless. In their covenant relationship with God; they were given responsibilities. In addition they were given prohibition; not to eat the fruit of the knowledge of good and evil. Covenant blessings were promised for obedience and covenant curses for disobedience. \r\nSadly, deceived by the serpent, man disobeyed God and God confronted him and cursed them for their disloyalty.( Genesis 3: 1-24). The theologians describe the whole episode as the \'fall\" with the idea that Adam and Eve\'s sin caused humanity to fall out of God\'s favor and blessings. \r\nBecause of the covenantal curse God did not end the obligation of Adam and Eve but made it painful and harder.  Even worse Adam and Eve would both experience death because of their sin. \r\nThere fore we see the origin of sin in human race through the disobedience of Adam and Eve in the  garden of Eden.', 8, '2021-01-11 09:39:12', '2021-01-11 17:14:14', NULL),
(2307, 625, 792, 'By \'authorship of sin\' we mean the person who is ultimately caused and to be blamed for the original sin. Christianity is attacked by opponents blaming God as the author of sin and claim such God is not worthy to be worshiped and man should not be punished for sin. \r\nThe scripture clearly speaks of the root of sin. Adam and Eve tried to argue that the ultimate blame, or the authorship of their sin, should be put on some one else. But their reasoning was not sufficient for God not to punish them. God punished serpent for deceiving the woman; Eve for mistrusting God and being deceived; and Adam for misled by Eve and eating fruit. \r\n in this account we might say that the ultimate author of sin was the serpent because it was the first character to come up with the idea of sin; and the first one to try to cause humanity to sin. \r\nThe scripture emphatically says that God is not culpable or guilty of sin. 1 John 1:5 says \' God himself is the ultimate standard of good and evil. There is no ultimate moral standard out side him that can judge. Besides this scripture portions like Deuteronomy 25:16, Psalms 5:4 James 1:13 make clear that God cannot be tempted by sin. God is free from sin and he hates sin. \r\nAccording to Augustin Adam and Eve were empowered to avoid sin completely. But they also had the ability to sin. According to the evangelical thinking, the fallen angel authored sin by taking the form of a serpent ands deceiving Eve and Adam, causing them to transgress the command of God.', 8, '2021-01-11 09:53:13', '2021-01-11 17:14:14', NULL),
(2308, 625, 793, 'In traditional systematic theology, the term \'original sin\' refers to the consequences of humanity\'s first sin.  It is the condition in which Adam\'s natural descendants are born as a result of Adam\'s fall in to sin. \r\nMan lost his original ability not to sin by fall. The corruption Adam and Eve suffered removed their capacity to please God and to merit his blessings. They were left with only the ability to sin and merit God\'s curse. \r\nBy means of faith and repentance God restored their ability to avoid sin but unfortunately this was not extended to the descendants. The generation were born with the moral corruption to being slaves to sin. \r\nSin is pervasive in our lives. It corrupts every part of humanity\'s being- Body, soul, minds, desires, thoughts and everything else. And as a result also it corrupts everything that flows from our being - everything we think, do and feel. When we come to the faith in Christ, god renew us in ways that restore our ability to please him in all these areas. \r\nSin has damaged human capacity for conceptional thought, and caused us to believe that false ideas are true. we don\'t value what God value and we commit ourselves to evil Ephesians 4:17-18.\r\nAdam\'s sin served as the model for all the behavioral sins that have plagued humanity since then. This sinful behavior caused God to destroy the entire human race with a flood. Romans 1:29-32.\r\nThe sinful corruption also affect our emotions, preventing us from loving God and our neighbors as we should . Also it prevents us from manifesting other righteous emotions that flow from this love. This affected the social and family relations as well. Pervasive corruption is one of the terrible consequence of man\'s fall.', 9, '2021-01-11 10:08:44', '2021-01-11 17:14:14', NULL),
(2309, 625, 794, 'Adam was warned of death penalty if they eat the fruit of the prohibited tree in the garden. after the fall God, cursed man with physical death. As the covenant head, Adam\'s guilt passed to rest of humanity. ( Romans 5: 12-17).\r\nThe scripture does imply that Adam died spiritually when he was cursed, and that his natural descendants are spiritually dead before they come to faith.  Ephesians 2:1-2. Spiritually dead means to engage in sin and live against the spiritual kingdom of God, separated from the presence and fellowship of God. \r\nAll fallen human beings share this spiritually dead condition until we receive spiritual life in Christ. Paul explained this in Romans 8:10. As a result of Adam\'s sin, human race is born spiritually dead; and eventually suffer physical death. But by faith in Christ we are redeemed from sin and made alive in spirit. By God\'s grace we can struggle against sin\'s completely escape it in future.', 8, '2021-01-11 10:18:39', '2021-01-11 17:14:14', NULL),
(2310, 625, 795, 'It is a natural question raised by common people that how a loving God allow sin and pain and death in to the world. The Bible makes it  clear that God mad made man with freedom of choice. Man originally had the ability not to sin. He was also waned of the covenantal curses which will follow their disloyalty.  But unfortunately man chose to sin mistrusting God and disobeying his command. As a result of sin man had to suffer the covenantal curses as a consequence of his sin instead of enjoying covenantal blessings. \r\nGod is righteous and he cannot cancel the consequence of sin. But postponed the physical death which had to effect immediately. So God in his everlasting love and mercy restored Adam and Eve to himself and allowed them to continue in their covenantal obligations. \r\nAlso God promised the redeemer and replaced the covenant of grace in place of the covenant of works to redeem humanity through his son Jesus Christ. As man was unable to fulfill the covenantal requirement God himself fulfilled them in Christ and opened the way for man\'s redemption. \r\nSo it is not God who caused sin , pain and death but was the choice of man. But God found solution for it in Christ Jesus.', 9, '2021-01-11 10:34:39', '2021-01-11 17:14:14', NULL),
(2311, 623, 927, 'The word trinity simply means tri-unity which means three in one. the doctrine of trinity can be defined as as God is three persons in one divine essence. this concept is also said as Trinitarian and triune God. As per the very Rev Dr. Justyn Terry One god reveals who god is in terms of Father, Son and the Holy Spirit- three persons-not three people but the three persons of the one god. We have the Father who sends the Son, the Son who after his death and resurrection, goes to the Father and they send the Spirit. so we can think about god as the Father ,and Son and the holy Spirit. Before the creation ,God was already the father, Son and the Holy Spirit, one god in three persons. Perhaps the most direct way unto understanding , this is there\'s only one God n three persons. In Christian theology, we talk about God as triune- the trinity, one in three and three in one.\r\nGod\'s essence is one: His person is three. \r\nWhen we say that God is one divine essence, we are affirming that he is the one uncreated creator. As per Romans 1:25, there is one uncreated creator and all other things in the universe are a part of god\'s created order, the doctrine of the trinity is with the person of the trinity within the one divine essence, or Godhead.', 8, '2021-01-11 17:43:39', '2021-02-02 21:32:48', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2312, 623, 928, 'when we speak of three divine persons, we are referring to the distinct persons of the Father, son and holy Spirit. we can define the word, person as a distinct self aware personality existing in relationship with other persons. According to Dr Daniel Treier, god\'s personhood is what should define ours. the modern concept of person as distinct ego a, an individual self consciousness attached to a separated body and particular personal name that no other share. individual person to the persons of the triune God, we would risk tritheism.  the reality in certain new testament passages when Father, Son and holy Spirit speak to and about each other and when  john 17 , we have the different persons being spoken of as \"in\" one another, loving one another, in same way that we can get a bit of a grasp of. \r\nthe father isn\'t   just conscious as a father as self , but the is conscious of himself in relation to the Son and in relation to the spirit, and so to the son is conscious of himself as the son , begotten eternally by the father speaking and so forth. so the communicative agency seems to describe about the relationship about Father sin and the holy spirit.\r\nDr J Scott Horrell says that there is a greater density to the reality of the /son and the know the spirit, as well as the father. the point in of this is that the idea of person was  in its embryonic stage.', 8, '2021-01-11 17:56:21', '2021-02-02 21:32:48', NULL),
(2313, 623, 929, 'Tritheism violates the biblical doctrine of divine simplicity- that is god is indivisibly one-but the doctrine of God\'s threeness does not, this can be difficult to grasp until we know that god is three divine persons-He is only one divine essence.\r\nRev Valery Zadorozhny says that God\'s simplicity is a very interesting notion because , on the one hand the bible says that God is inconceivable . that is man cannot analyze him and sort him out. Na do the other hand theology contains the concept of God\'s simplicity.\r\nRev Dr Paul R Raabe says that there has always been the Spirit of the Father and the Spirit of the son and the three are inseparable. There is only one God head, one divine nature that\'s invisible and three persons.\r\nThe very Rev Dr Justin Terry says that when we read Matthew 10. we see a disciple who has the spirit of God in them, it is not just receiving that disciple they receive the Son of god. and when they  receive the son of god,  they receive the Father. Its one God in three persons.', 8, '2021-01-11 18:05:33', '2021-02-02 21:32:48', NULL),
(2314, 623, 930, 'Dr.  carl R trueman say that Christ is referred to as the firstborn  over all creation. firstborn is not a statement about chronological priority. it is actually a statement of what we might call ontological priority or supremacy. So for example , when Moses goes to Pharaoh, He calls the people of Israel, God\'s firstborn, not meaning they were the first tribe on the face of the earth, but they are supreme in God\'s eye. \r\nDr. Lynn Cohick says that Colossians 1:15-17 says that Paul explaining How Jesus Christ is God\'s image. He represents God the Father. Paul also Speaks of Jesus here as the first born overall. And Paul connects Jesus , then with creation. Notice here that in him creation happened and through  him, then, creation stand exist, continues. He is establishing here that both the father and the son are worthy of our worship and that they together along with the Holy spirit accomplish the salvation.\r\nwe have one God and one Lord and that Lord Jesus.\r\nDr.  Gareth says that in Col0ssians passage 15, Jesus is the prince, the one sovereign over , and eventually the heir.\r\ncreation is that God ahs appointed him the heir of all things  and there  is  sense in which  as the son of god, heirship is the natural outcome of sonship.', 8, '2021-01-11 18:17:26', '2021-02-02 21:32:48', NULL),
(2315, 623, 931, 'Every Trinitarian formulas found in scripture mentions the Holy  spirit as the equal member of the trinity together with the father and the son. dr. Dennis  E Johnson says that Holy Spirit is fully divine and fully personal. to take two examples in the great commission , Jesus command as to baptize people in the name of the Father , Son and holy spirit. 2Corinthains 13 says that grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God and fellowship go the Holy Spirit. DR. Steve Mc Kinion says that Spirit then is that he has  important role in God\'s work. in the economy of God. when God determines that He will create , its not just then Father and The son , Its the spirit that we even find in genesis whos is hovering over the water. In incarnation it is the Spirit whom comes on Mary so that She can conceive and bear the son. when it comes to Jesus being lift on the cross, he is commending his  spirit, he is giving his spirit, giving up his spirit  .', 7, '2021-01-11 18:27:11', '2021-02-02 21:32:48', NULL),
(2316, 428, 811, '2:4-17\r\nIn Garden\r\nHere we see , God put adam in the Garden of Eden, Adam was privilege by God grace. He was to keep the garden on God behalf\r\n\r\n2:18-25\r\nCondition Enhance\r\nWe see designate humanity condition enhanced\r\nIn this material God added even greater blessing to Adam life. \r\nGod give one helper for Adam, Great partner eve\r\n\r\n3:1-20\r\nCondition cursed\r\nHumanity condition cursed\r\nHere we see tempting serpent. And the results of his temptation. Eve falls prey to the serpent\'s TEMPETAION\r\n\r\n3:22-24\r\nWe see here is \"out of the garden\r\n\r\nThe problem is Adam eating from the tree \r\nThat point on human being would no longer to live to the garden', 7, '2021-01-12 04:50:30', '2021-02-02 21:33:44', NULL),
(2317, 428, 812, 'As we see 2:4-17 and 3;22- \r\n2:7 we see Adam live in great place.. Full of diveine blessing. Wonderful, life living water, precious metals and stone \r\nThe narrative end we see in 3:24 is \r\nGod expelling Adam and Eve from the garden. \r\n\r\nWe see here in two tree in garden tree of life, tree of the knowledge of God \r\n\r\nThird different between beginning and ending of narrative is in humanity condition in 2:15 \r\n3:23 we see God banished in outside  ther situation is so difficult\r\n\r\nThis three contracts between the opening and closing section of 2-3 chapter', 4, '2021-01-12 05:03:06', '2021-02-02 21:33:44', NULL),
(2318, 428, 813, 'The Gene 2 make it clear that Moses believed God had promised Abraham and his descendants the land that was once know as the land of eden \r\n\r\nHoliness\r\nThe primary was in which Moses conveyed the holiness of Eden was to describe it in terms that he also used to describe the tabernacle. \r\nSeven aspects of Eden indicated that it was a holy place of God special presence, much like the tabernacle. \r\n1) 3:8 Moses used a special expression when he said that God \" Was walking in the garden \" \r\nWe see in same thing in levit 26\r\n2) 2:9  we see tree of life a central feature of the garden of Eden \r\nWe see in Moses tabernacle was most likely a stylished represent of the tree of life\r\n4)  the presence of cherubim and angel 3:24 in E', 2, '2021-01-12 05:23:09', '2021-02-02 21:33:44', NULL),
(2319, 428, 814, 'Paul\r\nPaul summarizes his view point succinct in Rom 5:14 \r\nPaul said Adam was a type of one to come \r\nPaul put it here Adam one trespass results in condition for all people but Christ one act of righteous resulted in justification for all men. \r\nAdam mide sinner. But the obedience of the one man Christ made us righteous\r\nAdam sin to our lives. The only way for paradise lost to be found again is through the righteous obedience of Christ. \r\n\r\nMatthew\r\nMatthew in particular Drew attention to the way Christ retraced and reversed Adam sin in his account of Christ s temptation in matt 4:1 \r\nTemptation parallel both the experience of Adam and Eve in the  garden  and the challenge Moses brogan to the Israel as he wrote about Adam and Eve\r\nThe first place we look Jesus temptation connect it with Israel \r\n2) length of Jesus spent in the wilderness paralleled the experience of Israel just as Israel was in the wilderness for forty years, according to Matthew 4\r\nChrist  was in the wilderness forty years\r\n3) hun', 5, '2021-01-12 05:43:13', '2021-02-02 21:33:44', NULL),
(2320, 428, 815, 'Paul say in 2 cori 11\r\nWe see here  Paul appealed to Eve\'s negative example to warn against the worst kind of disloyalty  tuning from the true gospel of Christ. Just as Moses used the story of Eve temptation to warn Israel to move faithful towards the promised land, Paul used the same story to warn believers in his day about the basic loyalty required of who follow Jesus Christ\r\n\r\nJames 1:12 we see Simlar explain\r\nIt is clear alluded Gen 2-3 in James 1:14 \r\nFocus on human desire as the power behind enticement to sin \r\nSecond James say thos who test will received the crown of life by contrast the results of sin is that \"give birth to death\"', 3, '2021-01-12 05:58:22', '2021-02-02 21:33:44', NULL),
(2321, 624, 675, 'Hermeneutics is a common word in theological  and biblical studies. the word hermeneutics derives from the family of Greek terms , the name, the  \' hermes\' the mythological messenger of the god. the family of Greek words related to the hermeneu, which means  interpret or explain. when we refer to hermeneutics we have in mind the interpretation or explanation of some kind of message or communication. biblical hermeneutics is the study of interpreting the meaning and the significance of the scripture. when we read the scripture , then we are involved ourselves in hermeneutics at least informally. informal approaches to the bible are of great value , and these lessons will build on what most of us already understand. one particular aspect of biblical hermeneutics is that we try to see to relate all the data of scripture together under the assumption that they don\'t contradict one another, but rather they speak - while of the variety of God\'s revelation, they also speak one in agreement with itself.', 8, '2021-01-12 17:42:20', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2322, 624, 676, 'Biblical authors themselves often interpreted other scriptures with an orientation toward factual and logical analysis. Romans 4:3 to 5says what does the scripture says ? Abraham believed God and it was credited to him as righteousness. Paul carefully analyze the meaning of the word \"credited or reckoned\" as the Greek term Logizomi may be translated . from his knowledge of Greek, he argued that, wages are not credited as gift , but as an obligation. but then he noted that, for anyone who trust god their faith not works, is credited as righteousness. so he conclude on the base of these reasoning that Genesis 15:6 indicates that Abraham was granted righteousness as a free gift through faith. \r\nHere Apostle Paul handles Genesis 15, with meticulously factual and logical analysis. as this example ,illustrates, time and again biblical authors presented this kind of careful interpretation of scriptures. \r\nAnd their approach to scripture, indicates that scientific biblical hermeneutics is firmly rooted in scriptures themselves.', 7, '2021-01-12 17:50:37', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2323, 624, 677, 'Devotional hermeneutics focus primarily on the divine authorship of scripture. 2 Timothy 3:16 tells us the scripture were inspired by God, or more literally were God-breathed. this fact makes biblical hermeneutics distinct from general hermeneutics. we must interpret the scriptures devotionally as the living word of God himself. time and again Biblical authors indicates that the followers of Christ are to read the Scripture as the word of God, in the presence of God, in ways that bring about extra ordinary and even supernatural experience of God. Hebrews 4:12, here the author of Hebrews refer to a portion of Psalms 95 calling it the word of God . earlier in Hebrew 4:7, he quote the same psalms saying that god himself spoke through David, in Hebrew 7, he introduced Psalms 95 with the words as the holy Spirit says. notice how after acknowledging the divine authorship of the Psalms the writer of Hebrews describes the experience of reading scripture. he said that Scripture itself is living and active, it penetrates the depths of our inmost being and judge the thoughts and attitudes of the heart with a blade that is sharper than any double edged sword. but in this passage, the write of Hebrew indicates that scripture actually dissects and analyzes us, his highly intellectual analysis of scripture didn\'t turn away from devotional hermeneutics.', 9, '2021-01-12 18:01:37', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2324, 624, 678, 'Two important aspects of Organic inspiration that help us orient ourselves to the task of interpretation\r\n1. the fact that the holy spirit was the divine source of scripture \r\n2. the fact that he worked through human means to produce scripture\r\nDivine sources : as the one who inspired all scripture , the holy spirit has intimate knowledge of Bible\'s meaning and the way to communicate the meaning. so preparing to interpret the scriptures involve s dealing personally with the holy spirit as their ultimate author.\r\nHuman means:\r\nGod the scriptures composed by means of human authors. he revealed himself through the gifts and abilities of different human beings. without a doubt, the holy spirit could have illuminated any influence or presence of any human writers in scripture. \r\nMathew , Mark , Luke and John have narrated the life, ministry , death, crucifixion and resurrection different way, all are not identical. this is not the shortcoming of scripture , its the product of the way the holy spirit to close to inspire the scriptures.', 7, '2021-01-12 18:11:47', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2325, 624, 679, 'Illumination may be defined as the The holy spirit\'s work  of conveying a proper understanding of scripture to human being. through his illumination, the holy spirit grants us knowledge of his word. and this knowledge isn\'t purely cognitive. it also impacts our imaginations, intuitions, emotions , will, motivation, desires, moral conscience- any part of us that contributes to our understanding or scriptures can be illumined by the spirit. human beings are so deeply affected by sin that we cant understand the things of God on our own. we desperately need   God himself-the Holy spirit to illumine us . without the work of holy spirit, we have no hope of grasping the thought of God as we should. the illumination of the spirit is a subject that has seldom been addressed at length.', 8, '2021-01-12 18:18:55', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2326, 624, 680, 'Exegesis means drawing out of biblical texts especially by looking at the things like then historical context, literary forms, grammar, setting, vocabulary , and so on. every involvement we have with te exegesis of scripture prepares us further interpretation of the bible. the knowledge , skills and attitudes  we develop from encounter with the biblical influence as the next time, we go to scripture , for instance every time we study biblical vocabulary and grammar, we increase our ability to handle theses aspect of scripture more responsibly. when we work to understand the literary types of scriptures such as narratives. laws, poetry, prophecies and the like. we are better equipped to understand them at a later time. and we learn about the ancient history of the bible. we are prepared to come back to the scriptures for fur further understanding, every effort we pit into the exegesis, of scripture helps prepare us for further studies. being an exegete of scripture has helped me to understand well, teach well,  preach well of the scripture. it has  helped us a lot in our pastoral ministry while we conduct bible study classes and expository preaching.', 8, '2021-01-12 18:28:32', '2021-02-02 21:34:39', NULL),
(2327, 341, 776, 'Identifying the author of hebrew is not as simple as it is with many other new testament books because the author never identify himself. \r\nThroughout the medieval period, most leading scholars continued to believe that Paul wrote Hebrew. But during the Reformation, protest reformers question many ecclesiastical traditions, including the traditional view of pauline authorship. Martin Luther suggest that apollos was the author. John Calvin didn\'t suggest an alternative, but he instead that the book could not have come from Paul\r\nPaul mentioned all his authorship in his all other book. But he would have failed to identify himself had he written Hebrew\r\n\r\nSome subject not connect Paul other book, exp. Melchizedek, tabernacle, high priest,...', 5, '2021-01-14 04:58:07', '2021-02-02 21:35:15', NULL),
(2328, 341, 777, '1) Jewish\r\nHeb 1:1 we see the original audience Jewish\r\nHere author referred to how God had revealed himself to Israel in the OT \r\n\r\n2) Hellenistic\r\nWe know that the audience was in large part Hellenistic. The content of Hebrew indicates that audience was familiar with  theological teaching that were more common among Jews living outside of Palestinian then among  more traditional Jews \r\nWe confidence say that is that is major audience of Hellenistic Jews who lived outside of Palestine\r\n3) Immature\r\nHeb 5:12 \r\nWe understand the audience was theologically immature the book of Hebrew contains some of the most advance, sophisticated theological teaching in the NT \r\n4) Persecuted\r\nThe original audience of Hebrew was persecuted b. There wars two well known  time of persecution for Christian during the first ad 49, ad 64 \r\nAs we read through book of Hebrew original audience faced persecution was the more of them would suffer, perhaps even more  severely, in the future, \r\n5) Near Apostasy \r\nHeb 10: 26-\r\nWe need to be clear here that the author of Hebrew', 8, '2021-01-14 05:18:09', '2021-02-02 21:35:15', NULL),
(2329, 341, 778, 'To look further at what we mean by the intensity of the authors exhortation, we look at two issues, first the frequency of exhortation in the book, and second the author rhetoric style associated with his exhortation let\'s begin by examing the frequently of exhortation\r\n \r\nFrequency\r\nOne many occasion the author used what Greek grammarian call the hortatory subjunctive these verb form urge  translation\" let us\" \r\nHeb 4:4\r\n\r\nRhetorical style\r\nOne rhetorical device, called synkrisis in Greek is a detailed comparison between two or more thing designed to convince audience to affirm the speakers point of view. \r\nAnother rhetorical device in the book of Hebrew is known as exempla are lists of illustration or examples that follow one after the other to build a persuasive argument for a particular in Hebrew 11\r\n\r\n1) Reject local teaching', 8, '2021-01-14 05:34:41', '2021-02-02 21:35:15', NULL),
(2330, 341, 779, 'Hebrew 5:12 \r\nWhen we see this word \" By this time you ought to be teachers \"  They should have made great doctrinnal progress. But as the author noted they needed to be taught \" the elementary truth of God\'s word all over again \"\r\nInterestily enough even though the audience was theological immature, the book of Hebrew contains some of the most advanced, sophisticated theology \r\nIn NT \r\nWe learn passages Luke 4: 16, act 13: 15, 1 tim 4\r\nThat scripture to their congregation, so the author of Hebrew wrote some of these most theological sophisticated NT', 5, '2021-01-14 05:47:27', '2021-02-02 21:35:15', NULL),
(2331, 341, 780, 'Ceremonial food \r\nBasic Teaching \r\nAngel\r\nFaithfulness to Jesus\r\nTo accomplish the goal of calling his audience to faithful service to Jesus, the author of Hebrew organized his exhortation into five major division, \r\n1:1-2\r\nSupremacy of Christ over angel revelation\r\nWe mention early in this that the book of Hebrew spoke against false belief about angel, A human of Jewish writing often excited angel as powerful, \r\n3:1-4\r\nIt was obvious to everyone that follows of Jesus were not observing the sacrificl service God had ordanint through Moses. The local Jews community called for Christian to return to Moses \r\n4:14- \r\nIn this division the author argument that Jesus was the supreme Royal priest after the order of Melchizedek. Apparently the local Jews community wanted the original audience to reject\r\n8:1-11:8\r\nThe teaching of local Jews community raised dubts about the Christian clain that Jesus had come to mediate', 6, '2021-01-14 06:04:40', '2021-02-02 21:35:15', NULL),
(2332, 627, 796, 'The covenant of grace has its root in God\'s eternal plan of history, which theologians refer to as his \' eternal counsel\' or \'eternal decree\'. God planned to redeem sinners through Christ\'s death on our behalf which was made before the creation of the universe. Ephesians 3:11 speaks of God\'s eternal purpose, historically accomplished by Christ. 2 Thessalonians 2:13 says that we were chosen for salvation \' from the beginning\'. 2 Timothy 1:9-10 speaks of the grace that was given to us\' before the beginning of time. \" The Lord and Father of Jesus Christ.... chose us in him before the creation of the world..\" Ephesians 1:3-4.  Ephesians 1:11 says that we are predestined according to his plan. It means that God\'s eternal decree of salvation was fore ordained. There are differences of opinion regarding predestination of specific people but all agree that God\'s decision to save sinners was made as part of his eternal counsel, before the foundation of the world.', 8, '2021-01-14 09:40:22', '2021-02-02 21:35:45', NULL),
(2333, 627, 797, 'God\'s eternal plan of redemption involves the work of all three persons of Trinity. The Bible says it was Father\'s plan to save us ( Eph. 3:10-11). According to Paul it was father\'s eternal purpose to accomplish our redemption through Christ. ( Ephesians 1:4, @ Thessalonians 2:13, 1 Peter 1:20)\r\nCorrespondingly the son agreed to add a perfect human nature to his perfect divine nature, so that he could die on behalf of sinners.  2 Timothy 1:9 says that we received grace in the son before the beginning of time. John 17:4-5 supports this perspective. \r\nThe Holy Spirit agreed to enable and empower the son\'s work, and to apply salvation to those whom the son redeemed. 2 Thessalonians 2:3\r\n All three persons of Trinity were and are involved in our salvation. The father planned, chose us and gave us his son. And the son has come and taken human nature, offered himself as the sacrifice and rose again fulfilling God\'s righteousness. The Holy spirit brings our stony hearts to life, make them tender to God\'s word, give us the ability to believe and trust in Christ.\r\nThe father is the planner, the son is the accomplisher and the Holy spirit is the applier of our salvation.', 8, '2021-01-14 09:51:49', '2021-02-02 21:35:45', NULL),
(2334, 627, 798, 'Covenant grace is the eternal plan of God to redeem fallen humanity through the atonement made by his son Jesus Christ. God eternally decreed it and fulfilled it in the covenant of grace by sending the Son and the spirit to do the work. He also appointed the Son to the office of Messiah or Christ which was necessary for redemptive work. Acts 2:36, John 5:36; 6:38. \r\nJesus became incarnate for the specific purpose of dying on the cross to save us from sins. Philippians 2:5-8.  According to Hebrews 2:13-17, the son had come to die as an atonement for the people  that father had previously given him, in fulfilment of their eternal counsel. Romans 8:3-3, Galatians 4:4-5.\r\nThe Holy spirit fulfills his part in eternal counsel through enabling and empowering the son\'s incarnation. \r\nAmidst all the tragedies and untoward incidents, the believer is assured eternal redemption through Jesus Christ which is the eternal counsel of God fulfilled through the covenant of grace.', 8, '2021-01-14 10:02:12', '2021-02-02 21:35:45', NULL),
(2335, 627, 799, 'The covenant of works took the form of a typical Suzerain - vassal treaty; where God was Suzerain and Adam served as the head or representative of God\'s vassal people. But in addition to these parties, God the son joined covenant as the mediator. As the mediator, the son intercedes for the covenant people. He reconciled us to God by taking both the blame and the punishment for our sins. He suffered the covenant curses and earned the covenant blessings. through obedience and loyalty and shares them with the sinners he redeems. \r\nThe son began to mediate the covenant of grace from the garden of Eden and continued to mediate ever since. No one was ever saved through works but by grace. The old testament ordinances were symbols that God\'s people performed in faith Colossians 2:17.\r\nAs mediator of the covenant of grace, Jesus fulfilled the covenant of works on our behalf. , and guaranteed that we should receive its blessings. Redemption is possible under the covenant of grace because Jesus is not just our representative but he is also our mediator, both God and man. Hebrews 9:15, 1 Timothy 2:5-6. The unique providence of God through Jesus Christ makes the redemption possible; and no other covenant or mediator sufficed the demands for the redemption of mankind.', 9, '2021-01-14 10:15:38', '2021-02-02 21:35:45', NULL),
(2336, 627, 800, '1. Adamic Administration. The covenant of grace was instituted with Adam in Genesis 3:15 immediately after the fall. This period is called so because Adam was the covenant head. This administration provided human beings with the immediate opportunity to reconcile relationship with God by which they were able to fulfill obligations.\r\n2. Noahic administration. The covenant was confirmed with Noah after the flood, expanding the covenant blessings adding promise of not destroying the earth with flood again and gave the sign of rain bow. They were asked to continue the covenant obligation Genesis 9:1.\r\n3. Abrahamic Administration. Continuing the terms with Noah, God added promise to make Abraham\'s descendants to a mighty nation, and to bless all the nations through them. Genesis 17: 1-21. \r\n4. Mosaic Administration. With Moses God built on the Abrahamic administration and gave the first extensively codified version of his law. They were given the task of building his Kingdom through out the world. Under this administration Israel was supposed to redemption to all nations through the message of God\'s truth. Exodus 19-24.\r\n5. Davidic administration. This period is mentioned in 2 Samuel 7 and Psalms 89 and 132. God confirmed Mosaic administration and revealed that the greatest covenant blessing would be fulfilled under the Kingship of David and his dynastic heirs. Psalms 89:3-4. \r\n6. The New covenant. This is the period of Jesus\' administration. This term comes from Jeremiah 31:31. The Hebrew and Greek words \' Chadash\' and \' Chainos\' usd for \'new\' can also be translated as \'renewed\', which means God was renewing his previous covenants in Jesus Christ. The idea is that God is renewing or re affirming his covenant with his people through a fresh covenant administration, not that he ha abandoned the covenant he has sworn to keep.\r\nThe goals of each administration was to establish the', 9, '2021-01-14 10:35:39', '2021-02-02 21:35:45', NULL),
(2337, 626, 796, 'Even before God created the world, He knew humanity would fall into sin, accordingly God created a plan to save us\r\n\r\nGod\'s in his foreknowledge to redeem humans from the corruption and consequences of sin, planned even before the universe was created. The timing is mentioned in several scriptures\r\nEphesians 3:11 speaks about God\'s eternal purpse, historically accomplished by Jesus\r\n2nd Thessalonians 2:13 says that we were chosen for salvation from the beginning. \r\n\r\nIn Ephesians 1:3-4\r\nThe God and Father of our Lord Jesus chose us in him even before the foundations of the world to be holy and blameless in his sight\r\n\r\nGod\'s eternal decree of salvation was foreordained even before the world began.', 7, '2021-01-14 16:24:23', '2021-02-02 21:36:19', NULL),
(2338, 626, 797, 'God\'s eternal plan of redemption involves the workd of all three persons of the Trinity.\r\n\r\nThe Father originalted the agreement because of his desire to redeem fallen humans from the curse of sin. Scripture says that it was the Father\'s plan to save us. It was the Father\'s eternal pupose to accomplish the redemption of mankind through his Son Jesus. Scriptures : Ephesians 3:10-11\r\n\r\nThe Son agreed to come in the human nation added to his perfect divine nature, into this earth and fulfill the wishes of the Father and to die on behalf of the sinners.\r\n\r\nThe Holy Spirit agreed to enable and empower the Son\'s work and to apply salvation to those whom the Son redeemed\r\n\r\nApostle Paul indicates in the 2nd Thessalonians 2:13 that the Father\'s choice was made from the beginning and the plan involved the sacrifice of our Lord, and also Holy Spirit\'s agreement to perform the sanctifying work of applying salvation. \r\n\r\nThe Father is the planner, the purpose, the giver of the Son.\r\nThe Son is the accomplisher of our salvation\r\nHoly Spirit is the applier of our salvation, convering the hearts and minds and bringing them into the presence of God by believing and trusting in Christ\r\n\r\nHence all 3 persons of the Trinity have a very important role in God\'s eternal counsel.', 9, '2021-01-14 16:43:10', '2021-02-02 21:36:19', NULL),
(2339, 626, 798, 'God\'s eternal counsel is his plan of what will happen in history. And the covenant of grace fulfills part of that plan. The implementation of the eternal counsel in history throught the covenant of grace.\r\n\r\nFather eternally decreed our redemption in Christ and fulfilled the decree in the covenant of grace by sending the Son and the Spirit to do their work. God also appointed the Son to the office of Messiah which was necessary to his redemptive work.\r\n\r\nJesus Christ came to do his saving work, he was executing the Father\'s plan. The Father also gave the Son his Spirit without limit to fulfill the mandate. God the Son fulfilled his eternal agreement to redeem humanity. He veiled his divine glory, addead a full human nature to that of divine, lived a perfect life and died an atoning death. Jesus became incarnate only for the purpose of dying on the cross to save mankind from their sins.\r\n\r\nHoly Spirit also fulfilled his part in God\'s eternal counsel. Enabled and empowerd the Son\'s incarnation and subsequent work by conceiving the Son\'s human nature. Holy Spirit empowered Christ\'s death on the cross and was also instrumental in the resurrection.\r\n\r\nGod\'s eternal counsel regarding the redemption of makind is a great comfort to everyone including believers. It reminds us that the tragedies including the murder of Jesus Christ are just a plan in God\'s will that opens the door to salvation through the covenant of grace.', 9, '2021-01-14 17:01:52', '2021-02-02 21:36:19', NULL),
(2340, 626, 799, 'God the Son (Christ) the second person of the Trinity, joined the covenant as its mediator. As a mediator the Son intercedes for Gods covenant people.  He reconciles us to God by taking both the blame and the punishment for our sins. He preserves the integrity of the covenant and the lives of the people by suffering the covenant curses on our behalf.\r\n\r\nThrough his obedience to the covenant\'s requirement of human loyalty the Son earns the covenant blessings and then shares them with the sinners he redeems.\r\n\r\nChrist began to mediate the covenant of grace when it was first made back in the garden of Eden, when God first offered redemption to Adam and Eve. Ad he\'s continued to mediate ever since. No one was ever saved on the basis of his or her own merit, since no works of obedience can erase our sin. \r\n\r\nNo one was ever saved on the basis of sacrificed animals, since it would not be sufficient substitute for a human being.\r\n\r\nThe Son\'s mediation of the covenant of grace centered around his incarnation, life of perfect faith and obedience, his death on the cross and his resurrection from the dead, his ascention into heaven. As mediator of the covenant of grace, he fulfilled the covenant works on our behalf and guaranteed that we would receive its blessings.\r\n\r\nAdam\'s sin had thrown the entire human race under the curse of sin and death. But the Son\'s role as a mediator fulfilled both covenants, the covenant of works and the covenant of grace.\r\n\r\nAdam\'s history revolved around sin, failure, condemnation and death. All humanity was condemned in Adam. It did not provide a mechanism for mercy and forgiveness. it did not provide a mediator. There was nothing that could do within the covenant of works to reverse our condemnation.\r\n\r\nJesus succeeded where Adam had failed Jesus righteous works benefited us because the covenant of grace does provide mechanism for mercy and forgiveness. Jesus history centers on obedience, righteousness, justification and life.', 9, '2021-01-14 17:21:11', '2021-02-02 21:36:19', NULL),
(2341, 626, 800, 'The covenant of grace was governed or administered by various covenant representatives. Considering the historical administration of the covenant of grace it is important to recognize that different theological traditions define these administration in different ways.\r\n\r\nAdamic Administration\r\nAdam representated the covenant head of all humanity, its also known as Adamic administration of the covenant. This administratio provided human beings with immediate opportunity to reconcile our relationships with God. This goal is evident not only through God\'s refusal to destroy mankind, but also in the subsequent account of Adam\'s faithful descendants. The descendants of Adam, began to call on the name of the Lord, shows that they were determined to fulfill the covenant obligation to God\r\n\r\nNoah Administration\r\nThe covenant with Noah was confirmed after the flood. It expanded the covenant blessings also incorporating Adamic covenant. God promised to never aain destroy the earth with flood waters and also provided a rainbow as sign to this covenant. God also reaffirmed his kingdom purposes for humanity by giving Noah and his family the same command to be fruitful and increase in number to fill the earth, the same command he gave to Adam and EVe.\r\n\r\nAbrahamic Administration\r\nAbrahamic covenant administration included the terms from Noahic administration. God promised Abraham to turn his descendants into a might namtion and to bless all nations through them especially the nation of Israel\r\n\r\nMoses \r\nMosaic administraation is provided in great detail throughtout the books of Leviticus and Deuteronomy.  Not only did God build on the Abrahamic administration but also provided structure for the nation of Israel as a nation by providing them codified version of the law.\r\n\r\nDavid :\r\nDavidic administration is described in the book of Samuel and in Psalms\r\nGod confirmed the Mosaic administration but also revealed the greatest covenant blessing that would be fulfilled under the kingship of David and his dynastic heirs\r\n\r\n\r\nJesus\r\nThe final covenant representative, the administration is referred to as The New Covenant. This name originally comes from the book', 9, '2021-01-14 17:41:14', '2021-02-02 21:36:19', NULL),
(2342, 628, 932, 'the doctrine of trinity teaches us that there is one God- one divine essence- and all three members of the trinity are equally and fully God. this biblical truth is not fully and explicitly revealed in the old testament. much of the Christian doctrine of the trinity comes  from the new testament. the old testament is incomplete revelation, god dint reveal himself all at once\r\n\r\nDr. Danny akin says that we need to acknowledge that the understanding of the triune God, takes place through what we call, progressive revelation. its more fully revealed in the New Testament. in triune passage like Mathew 28, that great commission text, or Ephesians 4:1-6, end of 2 Corinthians 13 we see that all three are brought together in very clear package.\r\nDr. Matt Carter say that in the New Testament era, God took a step closer to us through Jesus. and not only was he other than us, but he dwell among us.', 8, '2021-01-18 17:44:18', '2021-02-02 21:36:48', NULL),
(2343, 628, 933, 'Revelation Chapters 1 and 4 says that the one who sits on the throne is God the Father, the lamb is the word of God who became flesh-Jesus Christ , the son of God. in revelation 5 John use imagery of both the lamb and lion to describe Jesus. DR. Mark l Strauss says that Revelation 5sums up the whole message of revelation, it is the .lion , the lion of the tribe of Judah. John turns expecting to see a lion a powerful lion, instead he sees a lamb a symbol of sacrifice , a symbol of suffering. and there is a beautiful picture of who Jesus is, because Jesus is the conquering king, he is the messiah, he is the Lord of all, yet he accomplishes salvation by suffering and dying as a sacrificial lamb. who is the lam is the the beautiful picture of whos Jesus Christ is as Lord, as a sovereign , a s a king.\r\nDr. Lai-Chang Kang says that a lion represents Jesus kingly majesty. In revelation 4 God raises his right hand  holding a scroll and a mighty angel asked if anyone could open then scroll, its announced as the lion of Judah, revelation 5  places Jesus in the category of divine  and as God divine son, he will conquer all his and our enemies when he returns.', 8, '2021-01-18 17:54:39', '2021-02-02 21:36:48', NULL),
(2344, 628, 934, 'John 1:1 has been used throughout the history of the church as proof that Jesus is God the son., the second person of the trinity. he is God and he has been with god since the beginning. He is also involved in the creation of all things and because of that, he himself must be uncreated. He is the eternal and uncreated creator. \r\nDr. Daniel B Wallace says that the translation that is customary, but the best translation is probably what the revised English bible \" what god was, the word was\". so the last few words of John 1:1 and the word was God, says in the most concise way possible, that the second person of the trinity is equal in authority, in worship, in attributes, as the first person of the trinity, yet they are not the same person. John1:1 affirms the deity of Christ.\r\nDr. John Mc Kinley says that in Johns\' gospel he introduced the readers to Jesus with a prologue- first eighteen verses- Jesus is the Word, the word was with God, the Word was God.\r\ncalling Jesus the word of God, john  is also saying he sis the son of God, he is the divine agent, and he is the one who brings life.\r\nDr. Jeff Lowman says that John begins his gospel unlike the other writers , he begins with person of Christ.', 9, '2021-01-18 18:04:58', '2021-02-02 21:36:48', NULL),
(2345, 628, 935, 'the scripture s don\'t speak of the holy spirit as frequently as they do of the divinity of the son. but there are a few place in scripture where the uncreated divinity of the holy spirit can be clearly seen. t\r\nDr. Glenn R Kreider says that its because of the way Jesus talks about the Spirit that in the upper room discourse, he says \"I will ask the Father, he will send another comforter, who will be -who you already know , because he is like me- he will be with you- he will be in you-\r\nthe central pivotal text is in Acts % given in the story of Ananias and Sapphira. Peter says \" you have lied to the holy spirit, a little bit later in the same discourse, you have not lied to men, you have lied to God. so to lie to the Holy spirit is to lie to God.\r\nRev Dr. Emad  a Mikhail says that In Geneis1:1-2, there are two things here. first the scripture does not tell us that God created the holy spirit, he is not created, he is the creator. he is God, this is the first thing, the second thing is that the spirit of God participated in the process of creation.\r\n1 Corinthians 2:9-11, we see that  in order of the holy spirit to reveal God, he has to be equal to God. he has to know God\'s thoughts.\r\n third way showing us the divinity of the holy spirit is in his role in believers life and the church.\r\n1 Corinthians 3:16 the dwelling of the holy spirit in us makes as a temple for God, because the spirit God.', 9, '2021-01-18 18:16:27', '2021-02-02 21:36:48', NULL),
(2346, 628, 936, 'Scripture teaches that Jesus is the true and living God, both Paul and John understood this, but one of the great mystery of the trinity is that Jesus is not only fully god, he is also fully human. and it is through his humanity, through his life , death and resurrection , that we are saved. and because the human Jesus is also the eternal god, equal in power and glory of the father. we can trust that he is worthy and able to redeem us from our sins.\r\nDr. Derick Thomas says that if we are to be reconciled to God, then only god can forgive sin, Why God -Man, because it was necessary for the mediator to be both god and man?\r\nthat he was true god and true man, two natures in one person and the reason why he has to be man is that , he has to represent us. he must be tempted in every point like as we are. he must be the second man, the last Adam, but He must also be God. because only god can forgive sin, because God is the one who has been sinned against in Adam.\r\nDr. J Scott Horrell says that 2 Corinthians 5 talks about how was reconciling was the world to Himself in Christ, as Christ becomes the perfect substitute for us, because he is fully human yet without sin, he can be our complete substitute. \r\nJesus is both God and man.', 9, '2021-01-18 18:28:14', '2021-02-02 21:36:48', NULL),
(2347, 613, 633, 'In the old testament ,God\'s kingdom was expected to unfold in three phase , the initial creation of the universe and its creatures ,a long period of redemption neccessiated by humanity\'s fall into sin ,the evrlasting eschaton , the ultimate state of the univers after redemption is complite ,when the God\'s heavenly kingdom fill the earth ,just like the term \"eschatology \" the theological terms \"eschaton\"comes form greek word eschatos ,naturly the event we study in eschatology are the one that take place duding the section \r\ninline with this old testament understanding of the history we will explore the old testamnet expectation for the culmnination of God\'s kongdom in three parts , the nplans God reavealed at creation , hopes he reavealed through the history of redemption ,and the prophetic picture of the eschaton ..', 8, '2021-01-20 07:15:44', '2021-01-29 16:03:15', NULL),
(2348, 613, 634, 'Most christians are familiar with God\'s work of creation detailed in genesis 1-2, genisis one teach us that god created and orderd the heaven and the earth , he created all the realm within the universe such as the waters , the dry land ,and the heaven ,and he created the all the creatures that inhabite these realm ,such as fish ,land animal ,and birds .and of course ,he created human beings to rule over and administer the entire creation ,including both bearth itself and all its creatures ,\r\ngod intented humanity to be his \"vassals\" or servent kings , our job was to rule over creation on behalf of God ,the great zuzerain or emperor ,this idea is strenthened by the fact that human beings were created in the image of god ,God did\'nt wwant just one couple that would merely mange the things he\'d created rather he wanted them to populate the world and to turn it into his earthly kingdom , theologians generally refers this verses as the \"cultural mandate \" because they require humanity to buld culture throughout the whole world', 8, '2021-01-20 07:32:15', '2021-01-29 16:03:15', NULL),
(2349, 613, 635, 'All christian should know the story of humanity\'s fall, because of huaman sin God cursed all humanity , and great hope of his eschatological kingdom appeareed to be lost ,but god mercifully introduced a plan of redemption that would you rescue individual through personal salvation ,and would restore the hope of his great messianic kingdom on earth , this plan of redemption was first introduced in Genesis 3:15 , when God said this to the serpent , \r\n\"i will put enimity between you and women , and between your offspring and hers , he will crush your hesd ,and you will strike his heel, \r\ntheologians call this offer of redemption the \"proto-euangelion \" or first gospel , because its first offer of the salvation after humanity fall into sin,', 8, '2021-01-20 07:43:09', '2021-01-29 16:03:15', NULL),
(2350, 613, 636, 'In old testament God made five major redemptive covenant , begin with his covenant with Adam , \r\nAdam :after Adam has sinned God in the garden of Edan ,god offered him salvation trough the prato -euangelion, or the first gospel ,this created the expecation that god\'s plan for his world wide kingdom would eventually come to pass ,but its growth would be charecterised .\r\n   Noah :Genesis 6-9 records the flood that destroyed the world in Noah\'s days ,within this account god made in genesis 8:21-9:17 expalins the covenant God mde throgh Noah ,the context of promising  not to flood earth again , god also establish the broder stability of nature \r\n     Abraham : according to passage like genisis 15-17 and 22 God called Abraham and his desendants to serv him in a special way , it remaint all humanity task to fill and subdue the earth \r\n     Moses: moses wrote about God\'s covenant within him in many place , several times ,he confirmd that the mosaic covenant incorporated and continued the early covenants , \r\n     David :the historical record of God\'s covenant with David is mentioned in 2 samuel 7 nd many more details are recorded in pasalms 89,132,', 8, '2021-01-20 07:59:59', '2021-01-29 16:03:15', NULL),
(2351, 613, 637, 'In the centuries between the close of the old testament and the coming of christ rabbis and other jewish theologians devoloped a view of eschatology that gained wide acceptance , in its simplest form , jesish eschtology held that wold history could be divided into two great ages , they called the first age \"this  age \" or olam hazeh in hebrew , it was charecterized by sin, exile , suffering and death ,this age started when adam and eve fell into sin and its corresponds to the age of redemption we identified earlier , \r\njewish theologians called the second age \"athe age to come\' or olam haba in hebrew , this is the future eschaton , when god\'s kingdom will filled the earth , it would be charecteriszed by the forgiveness , righteousness ,peace, and eternal life , \r\njesus and the writers of new testament also spoke about this age , and the age to come \" as two major eras of the history .and they largerly agreed on the way other jewish theologians charachteriezed these ages', 8, '2021-01-20 08:13:16', '2021-01-29 16:03:15', NULL),
(2352, 631, 932, 'As we know the doctrine of the Trinity teachers that there is one God - one divine essence and all three members of the Trinity are equal and full of God. The biblical truth is not fully and explicitly revealed in the Old Testament. \r\nMuch of the Christian doctrine of the Trinity come from  the New Testament. \r\nThis is mean is that ot is wrong or inferior but we should recognize that the ot is incomplete revelation. God didn\'t reveals himself all with a simple question. Why does the Trinity seem to be more clearly express in the NT\r\nWe see Mathew 28 father son holy Spirit, and eph 4, 2 cor. 13 we see three person same time', 6, '2021-01-25 05:04:03', '2021-02-02 21:37:22', NULL),
(2353, 631, 933, 'Revelation 1:4 say that the one who sits on the throne is God the father. And John 1 tells us that the lamb is the word of God who became flesh \r\n\r\nA lion represent Jesus kingly majesty a lamb represent his generation and more important perhaps symbolizes him as a redeem of sin so this is a wonderful device. \r\nSomething happen in heavenly Court. Rev 4 after the praise of the wonders of God\'s creation, suddenly the praise stopped because God raised his right hand, holding a scroll, and a might angel asked if anyone could  open this scroll... \r\nRevelation 5 place Jesus in the category of diven. He isn\'t one of God\'s creatures Instead he equal with God. And as God\'s divine son \r\nHe will conquer all his ans and our enemy when he return. His victory over Satan will be complete, and all God\'s people  will have peace with', 7, '2021-01-25 05:18:17', '2021-02-02 21:37:22', NULL),
(2354, 631, 934, 'The first chapter of John, and the first verse in particular, has been used throughout the history of the church as proof that Jesus is God the son, the second person of the Trinity. \r\nHe is God and has been with God since the beginning. \r\n\r\nRudolf bultmann who is  main scholar of Christian  say John 1:1 absolutely undisputed. It clearly teache the deity of Christ. \r\n\" What God was, the word was\" In essence what  the Greek  is doing is it\'s saying that in terms of his essence, what he visit \r\nDr. John mckinley say \r\nJohn gospel he introduced the readers to Jesus with a prologue', 6, '2021-01-25 05:29:39', '2021-02-02 21:37:22', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2355, 631, 935, 'The scriptures don\'t speak of the divinity of the holy Spirit as frequently as they do of the divinity of the son, but there are a few places in scriptures where the increased divinity of the holy spirit can be clearly seen. How can we denomination he divinity of holy spirit from scriptures \r\n\r\nDr. Glenn say - \r\nNT not  spend a great deal of time explicitly addressing the question of the divinity if the holy spirit, \r\nWe see a appear in a Jesus Christ baptism time, and presentation of the temple, those kind of events, \r\nJesus talk about spirit that in the upper room discourse, he say \" I will ask the father, he will send another comforter who will be with you, who you already know because he is like me, he will be with you, he will be in you, but \r\nThe act 5 we study then we see in Story ananias and sapphira when thy lied to the apostle.. They Peter say them \" You have lied to the holy spirit \" And a little bit later in the same discourse, \" You have not lied to men, you have lied to God \"', 8, '2021-01-25 05:47:05', '2021-02-02 21:37:22', NULL),
(2356, 631, 936, 'Scriptures teach that Jesus is the true and living God. Paul and John understand this thing. \r\nJesus is true God and true man, two natures in one person. And reason why he has to be a man is that he has to represent us. He must be tempted in every point like as we are. \r\nHe must be the second man, the last Adam, but he must also be God. Because only God can forgive sin, because God is the one who has been sinned against in Adam. \r\n\r\n2 cor 5 talk about how God was reconciing the world to \r\nWe see it in vived coler as Christ because the perfect substitute for us because full human with out sin', 8, '2021-01-25 06:00:20', '2021-02-02 21:37:22', NULL),
(2357, 633, 937, 'The three members of the trinity are each eternal and uncreated. there was a never a time, when any member of the trinity didn\'t exist. but the father\'s relationship in the trinity is unique. \r\nDr. Danny Akin says that whatever it is that it makes God, God the father is all of that, the son is all of that and the spirit is all of that.\r\nwe also know that the father is not the son, the son is not spirit and the spirit is not father. there is one god , yet they exist ins same type of mysterious unity as one God. three persons. they are always of one mind ,one will, always unite din every issue including redemption. its is the father who initiated salvation, it is the son who accomplishes our salvation and it is the spirit who applies our salvation and they work in an absolute perfect  unity and harmony, not just in that area, but they operate in that way in every area. so any idea that there\'s ever a disagreement among the triune God is a creation of man\'s imagination. it is nothing that you  an find in God\'s revealed word. the father \'s authority is always an authority of love, it is an authority that loves the son, desires the son to be glorified, just as the son then desires the sin to be glorified.', 8, '2021-01-25 17:49:59', '2021-02-02 21:37:48', NULL),
(2358, 633, 938, 'The Father\'s relationship in the trinity is unique, the Son\'s relationship is also unique. Scriptures like revelation 5:13, 14 are clear that the Son is eternal. he has always existed. the son is eternally begotten of the father\r\nRev Dr. Paul R Raabe says that the Son is of one substance with the Father, and through the Son all things were made, so that the Father through the Son made all things.\r\nDr. Thomas J Nettles says that in the incarnation God who is eternally generating the Son overshadows Mary at the point that the Holy Spirit conceives in her the human nature.\r\nSo everything that relates to our being and our action as a human, Jesus took to himself, he had a full human nature. we could not be redeemed if he had not assumed our nature. though he emptied himself of the external manifestation of the glory he had with the father, he did not empty himself of  any of the central attributes of his eternal being as the son of God. he still was omniscient and omnipotent. He still had immutable holiness. he still has perfect knowledge as the son of god of why redemption was taking place.', 8, '2021-01-25 17:59:23', '2021-02-02 21:37:48', NULL),
(2359, 633, 939, 'When a child proceeds from his or her parents, we know that he or she had a beginning in conception an birth. the eternal and the uncreated Holy spirit proceeds from his father, don not mean that the Spirit had a beginning, just like the father and the son, the spirit is eternal without beginning or end. this is the doctrine of the holy spirit\'s eternal procession and it affirms the divinity of the holy spirit.\r\nDr. Jim Maples says that but they added to the Nicine Creed words concerning the Holy spirit. they said he is Lord. he is life giver, he is to be worshipped as the Father and the Son/. and it is this form of the creed that most people today recognize.\r\nDr. Tim Foster says that  what it says about the holy spirit is very simple. It says I believe in the Holy Spirit. All that council said about the holy spirit was i believe in holy spirit because that council was really concerned to combat against Arianism and try to understand the divinity of the Son of God. it also means that \"the Lord the giver of life who proceeds from the Father and the Son and with father and the son. he is worshipped and glorified. the Holy Spirit was not created  because if the spirit was created , he was not God, so just as the Son begotten of the Father, so the spirit proceeds from the father', 8, '2021-01-25 18:11:49', '2021-02-02 21:37:48', NULL),
(2360, 633, 940, 'In 589 AD, a church council net in the western city of Toledo, in which they attempted to solidify the doctrine of eternal procession, theologians added the latin term, filioque meaning \"and the Son\" yo the Nicene Creed of 381 AD.  with this addition , the creed clearly articulates that the holy spirit proceeds or spirates from the father and the Son.\r\nDr. Stephen J Wellum says that the filioque clause was a clause the western church added. to explain the Son/Spirit relationship. filioque means \" and from  the Son\", they argued that the father and the son, there\'s where the filioque comes in- are the the persons who then send the spirit and they are trying to do justice to the biblical teachings that the father send the son,  het the father and son send s the spirit. the spirit\'s work is to glorify the son, to bear witness to the son , so that in the divine economy and divine relations of the persons , the father son and spirit work together. but there is an ordering -Father sends the Son, father and Son sends the Spirit and all three together bring about all of God\'s actions and works for his glory .\r\nDr. Jim Maples  says that word filioque was added to the creed, that . the latin for \" and the son \" and what they affirmed was the double procession of the Holy spirit, that the spirit proceeded from the father and the Son.', 8, '2021-01-25 18:26:08', '2021-02-02 21:37:48', NULL),
(2361, 633, 941, 'The Word ontological refers to being and existence. ontological trinity also known as the immanent trinity or the trinitas and intra - together has to do with the existence and personal interaction of the father and the holy spirit, apart from the acts of creation and salvation, the word economic refers to the management of a home, \r\nDr. David Correa says that refers to the fact that three persons of the trinity - father, son and the holy spirit are equal in substance ,power and glory. the father is one hundred God, so is son and the holy spirit. \r\nDr J. Scott Horell says that ontological trinity describes god in himself, the common word is immanent trinity, before creation God ad intra, God within himself, the ontological trinity is what from scripture we derive as to the relation within god himself beyond all creation. \r\nRev Dr. Simon Wibert  says that ontology being within the being of god. there are three persons who are co-existing, co-living within one another.\r\nDr. John Mc kinley says that the ontological trinity is the same  god at work in creation , but God in an inner life of the father, son and the holy spirit, relation to each eternally.', 8, '2021-01-25 18:38:00', '2021-02-02 21:37:48', NULL),
(2362, 630, 927, 'The word Trinity simply means \" Tri _ unity\"\r\nThree is one, so the doctrine of the Trinity can be defined in this way \r\nGod is three divine person in one divine essence. \r\nThere is only one God, not three. One  . One God  but we find is this is one God reveals who God is in terms of father, son and holy spirit_ the three person  not three people but the three person of the one God. \r\nWhen we say that God is one divine essence, we\'re affirming that he is the one uncredited creator. According to Rom 1:24\r\nThere is one uncredited created, and all other things in the universe are a part of God\'s creation order.', 5, '2021-01-26 04:57:30', '2021-02-02 21:38:16', NULL),
(2363, 630, 928, 'When we speak of three divine person were refer to the district person of the father, son, and holy spirit\r\nWe can define the word person as a distinct self aware personality existing in relationship with other person but with respect to the Trinity, what is a person\r\nWe need to acknowledge the mystery that we don\'t know and can\'t possibly contemplate the fullness of what it means to be a person of the triune God', 3, '2021-01-26 05:10:30', '2021-02-02 21:38:16', NULL),
(2364, 630, 929, 'Tritheism violent the biblical doctrine of divine simplicity - that God is individual one but the doctrine of God\'s  threeness does not. This can be difficult to grasp until we recognize that although God is three  divine person, he is only one divine essence and his essence cannot  be divided into part. He is individly one. So we should never confused. \r\nMathew 10 when we study we finding that.. Jesus say at his disciples in my name they receive me \r\nThe disciples who has the sprint of God in them it\'s not just receiveing that  disciples, thy receive the son of God and when they receive in son of God, they also receive in father.', 4, '2021-01-26 05:23:45', '2021-02-02 21:38:16', NULL),
(2365, 630, 930, 'Colo 1:15 to argue that God hasn\'t always existed as Trinity. This verse say that Christ is the firstborn over all creation. Since Christ was born thy argue that unlike the father, he must have begun to exist. \r\nWhen we took Colossians we see firstborn.. What mean \r\nIs not a statement about chronological priority. It s actually a statement of what we might call ontological priority or supremacy, so for example, when Moses gosh to pharaoh, he call the people of Israel God\'s firstborn, not meaning they were the first tribe on the face of the earth, but they are supreme in God\'s eyes \r\nSo exegetically while some challenge have been raised to the eternity of the son,', 4, '2021-01-26 05:35:02', '2021-02-02 21:38:16', NULL),
(2366, 630, 931, 'New Testament help us understand that the holy Spirit is fully divine and fully person. To take two example, in the Great commission, Jesus command us to baptize people in the name of the father and of the son and holy spirit \r\nSo we have one God three person and Jesus is really echoing or alluding to the blessings that Aaron and his sons as priest were to give, we\'re to place upon Israel in the old Testament, that blessing that we know so well the Lord bless you and keep you, the Lord mark his face shine upon you', 4, '2021-01-26 05:46:20', '2021-02-02 21:38:16', NULL),
(2367, 629, 681, 'there is a theological basis for giving attention to the biblical writers in our exploration of the meaning of the scripture\r\n1. Writer \r\nthe holy spirit choose to communicate his word through human writer. Focusing on the human writer help us understand many features of scripture. \r\n2. Audience: divine accommodation : God design his revelation to be understood by its first audience;\r\nuniversal accommodation in scripture appear most dramatically in Anthropomorphisms\r\nGod accommodate his revelation to the specific people to whom he spoke. \r\n3. Document:\r\na. organic inspiration means the holy spirit inspired human authors to write scriptures. only the original texts of scripture have the full authority of God.\r\nresponse to objections made about , copies and translation of scriptures: the original document of the old testament dint exist even in Jesus days. Jesus and his apostles believed the scriptures they had were trustworthy.\r\nmany decades of scholarly research devoted to studying and comparison ancient copies of scripture are available today. Divine accommodation  means the word s, grammar and style of scripture rose out of the cultural and linguistic conventions of its day.', 8, '2021-01-26 17:48:24', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2368, 629, 682, 'the doctrine of  Organic inspiration teach that the holy spirit inspired human authors to write scripture, but it odes not say that every copy made of that document will be perfect or that every translation made from that document will be perfect. because the doctrine of organic inspiration extends only to the original text of scripture, only those documents have the full authority of god himself, the changes that have taken lace in these texts  as they have been copied over in the centuries are not inspired by God.\r\none of the most important thing for followers of Christ to remember is that the original documents of the old testament didn\'t exist in Jesus day either.\r\nbut Jesus and his apostles still believed that the scripture they had were trustworthy and adequate to lead the people of God.\r\nDivine accommodation: the doctrine of accommodation implies that everything in scripture including things like its words, grammar and literary style rose out of the cultural and linguistic conversations of its day. John 20:16 Jesus said to her \"Mary\" she turned to toward and cried out in Aramaic \"Rabboni \"which means teacher, by first using Rabboni John created an moment of hesitation that heightened the drama of Mary\'s response. John texts leads his readers to imagine the actual sound of Mary\'s joyous cry to help them appreciate her joy in the Risen Savior. Literary devices and conventions like these demonstrate the importance of accommodations in the original documents of scripture .', 8, '2021-01-26 18:01:09', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2369, 629, 683, 'if we want to know the original meanings of biblical passage , we have to look at the passage itself. \r\nwe must do all we can do to rasp the original meaning of biblical passages - what the spirit of /god and the writers he inspired intended. So , when god gives us the opportunity, we should also become familiar as much as possible with the original, languages of Scripture: Hebrew and Aramaic in the old Testament and Greek in the New Testament. \r\nTo understand the real meaning if  a text, the original manuscripts are needed and more helpful, and now that we know that the scholars have access to the original manuscripts , it boosts our confidence. It helps us to know the real intents of the holy spirit to the the then real audience .\r\nIn order to determine whether we have the original words of scripture, we can look at the collective remaining manuscripts  we have and compare them with one another, and we can see how  much change has taken place overtime.  and the good news when it comes to the bible is we have so many manuscripts of the bible that we can compare them ,we can see how they have developed over time, and we can look and see what the original texts actually was.', 8, '2021-01-26 18:13:32', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2370, 629, 684, 'when we interpret the bible, we are the subjects and the objects of our study is the Bible\r\nthree major approaches towards the objects and subjects of knowledge \r\n1. objectivism: it is possible to arrive at impartial knowledge\r\n2. subjectivism: knowledge is always influenced by personal biases\r\n3. dialogism: emphasizes the interplay between objectivism and subjectivism\r\nobjectivism: \r\nobjective scholars believe that they can interpret scriptures impartially.\r\nbackground: scientific rationalism - Rene Descartes promoted reason as the supreme judge of truth. Francis Bacon applied rational, logical thinking to the study of the physical world\r\nStructuralism : they used to use rational and scientific objectivity to obtain an exhaustive understanding of everything they study.\r\nSubjective: they recognize that human beings , the world , matters of faith are often  too complex to be discerned by scientific rationalism.\r\nmodern subjectivism gained prominence partly in response to the objectivism of the enlightenment.  \r\nDavid hume believed that our emotions and mental categories always influence our thinking.\r\nImmanuel Kant\r\nRomanticism: argued that poetry, drama , music and visual arts provide an understanding of reality that is far superior to rational scientific discourse\r\nDialogical: refers to the idea  that interpretation involves  a type of dialogue or discussion between the reader and the text.\r\nFrederic Schleiermacher\r\nthomas Kuhn', 8, '2021-01-26 18:29:24', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2371, 629, 685, 'Philosophers like David Hume  , 1711 to 1776 argued that reason and scientific study cant lead us to objective knowledge of the world. he and others believed that our emotions, desires and mental categories always influence our thinking , making impartial objectivity, impossible.\r\nthe German philosopher , Immanuel Kant, lived from 1724 - 1804 also made tremendous contribution subjective thought, Kant argued that we can\'t know objective reality as it really is.\r\nSubjectivism has become widespread , especially in the interpretation of art an literature. Subjective interpreters argued that since we cant discover the objective understanding of the world around us , then the meaning of art an literature, including the bible w be must be located  within us. so rather that speaking of objective meaning in art and literature, subjectivists talk about how music, paintings, books and the like are viewed by different cultures, different ethnic groups, different, genders.', 7, '2021-01-26 18:40:28', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2372, 629, 686, '1.authority dialog and objective\r\nthe authority dialog model acknowledge s that objective truth can be founded i the text of scripture. Bible is God\'s word and revelation to us. and everything it says is objectively true and meaningful. \r\n2 timothy 2:15 \r\nthe authority -dialog model helps us avoid thinking that we can be utterly objective  when we approach the scripture. \r\n2. authority dialog and subjective\r\nthe authority dialog model acknowledges that our perspectives and believes influence the way we interpret scripture. it agrees with scripture and subjectivism that the personal, subjective input we bring to interpretation invaluable\r\nif we don\'t submit our subjectivity yo the authority of scripture, our interpretations of the Bible will be severely hindered. investigating the bible is a lifelong process in which scripture changes us and causes us to grow and mature in our Christian faith', 7, '2021-01-26 18:49:59', '2021-02-02 21:38:46', NULL),
(2373, 634, 826, 'Christian ethics teaches us how to live. Christian ethics asks what the whole Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God\'s approval and which ones do not. This means that Christian ethics teaches us how to live. It is important to study Christian ethics so that we can better know God\'s will, and so that each day we can walk in a manner worthy of the Lord , fully pleasing to him.\r\n\r\nChristian ethics teaches us how to live for the glory of God. Such life will have a character that glorifies God and, results that glorify God and behavior that glorifies God. Obeying God brings numerous blessings to our daily lives.', 8, '2021-01-27 02:37:40', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2374, 634, 827, 'When we evaluate actions from a moral point of view, we can do this in two very different ways. We can consider them as morally right or wrong, but we can also judge them as morally good or bad. Even though the distinction between right and good is generally recognized by moral philosophers, there is a tendency in contemporary ethics either to oversimplify it or to blur it all together. Most philosophers focus on motives when they investigate this problem. Actions can be evaluated in various respects, When we evaluate them from a moral point of view, we can do this in two very different ways. We can consider them as morally right or wrong, but we can also judge them morally good or bad.', 8, '2021-01-27 02:46:38', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2375, 634, 828, 'Scripture shows that God intends His law to function in three ways, which Calvin crystalized in classic form for the church\'s benefit as the law\'s threefold use. \r\nIts first function is to be a mirror reflecting us both the perfect righteousness of GOd and our own sinfulness and shortcomings. The law is meant to give knowledge of sin, and by showing us our need of pardon and our danger of damnation to lead us in the repentance and faith of Christ.\r\nA second function is to restrain evil, Though the law cannot change the heart, it can to some extent inhibit lawlessness by its threats of judgement. Thus it secures civil order and serves to protect the righteous from the unjust.\r\nIts thrid function is to guide the regenerate in to the good works that God has planned for them. The law tells God\'s children what will please their heavenly father. The Christian is free from the law as a system of salvation, but it under the law of Christ as a rule of life.', 8, '2021-01-27 02:58:58', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2376, 634, 829, 'All of the morality aims at the same thing but there are several basic ways to get there. If we can grasp the basic ideas of each of the different approaches to ethics, we will be in a better position to make a sound ethical decision. There are other ways in which moral philosophy can be categorized, but establishing ethical theories into their three schools is a useful way to understand ethics.  The 3 different perspectives are virtue ethics, consequential ethics, and deontological ethics. Each approach provides a different way to understand ethics.  In most cases, all the 3 perspectives are needed to be considered in order to reach the best ethical decision.', 8, '2021-01-27 03:12:31', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2377, 634, 830, 'God approves of right actions because they are right and disapproves of wrong actions because they are wrong. So, morality is independent of God\'s will; however, since God is omniscient He knows the moral laws, and because He\'s moral, he follows them. God creates moral standards form scratch any source of guidance. Moral standards exist independently of God, and God merely endorses them. To clarify, the first option is that God is the sole author of morality, and something becomes good when simply wills and pronounces that is good. Christian morality consists of living one\'s life with guidance and inspiration form the Christian scriptures and traditions.  As the moral values come from religions, transmitted through sacred texts and religious authorities.', 8, '2021-01-27 03:23:09', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2378, 634, 831, 'The first one is general revelation. God has given revelation to all people showing the He exists. There are two primary aspects of general revelation and the first os creation. Creation boasts that there is a God.  David said the heaven declares the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands. When we look at creation and how beautiful, complex, and dynamic is it, it boasts of a greater being and intelligent design. The Psalmist says, \"The fool says in his heart there is no God\" It is foolish to think that there is no God. The second form of general revelation is natural or conscience. Because God has made man in his image, there remains a conscience, a moral code. The third one is  special revelation comes in the form of angels. Throughout the Old and New Testament, we have seen many accounts where God revealed himself through angels.', 8, '2021-01-27 03:35:10', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2379, 634, 832, 'The existential perspective centers on human beings, the persons that make ethical decisions. It poses questions like \"How must we change in order to please to God\" and what kind of people please Him. The term existential has been used in different ways by various philosophers. But the existential perspective focus on issues like our character, our nature, the kinds of people we are and ought to be. The existential perspective explains the relationship between being and goodness in terms of the three basic stages of biblical history. First, the period of creation, looking at God\'s own goodness, and the fact that human beings were inherited good when God first created us. Second, we will turn to the period of the fall, exploring the way sin damaged humanity\'s goodness. And third, we will speak of the period of redemption, when God restores those who are faithful to him and empowers them for goodness.', 8, '2021-01-27 03:50:16', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2380, 634, 833, 'In 1 John verse 6 spoke against walking in the darkness. Verse 7 offers the contrast and calls believers to live according to God\'s way. John 1:8-9 speaks of John the Baptist, stating \" He was not the light but came to bear witness about the light. The true light, which gives light to everyone, was coming into the world. The second half of this verse states what takes place when believers do walk in the light; fellowship and cleansing from sin. First, believers will have community and friendship with one another. Second, believers will experience forgiveness. Even though a person is forgiven of sins when he or she first believes in Christ, Christians still commit sins and find themselves in need of forgiveness as believers.', 8, '2021-01-27 03:57:23', '2021-02-02 21:45:34', NULL),
(2381, 636, 638, 'Individual eschatology is the study of how how individual human beings experience the events of the last day , especially with regards to life ,death the intermediate state ,and the final states of our bodies and souls , ,as we know humanity currently lives during the last days , also known as \"the eschaton\" as a result , we all suffer the hard ship of this age ,like sin and death ,but these problems are partially alleviated by the blessings of the age to come ,like forgiveness and salvation ,  in humanity present state believers or the regenerate ,experience the effects of both ages simutaneously while unbelivers  or the unregenarate  over whelming experience the hard ship  of this age , \r\nin theology the term regenerate means \"recreated \" or reborn , so we could refers to those who regenerate as the spiritually \"living\" , if follow then  that the theological term \"unregenerate \"means to not recreated , or reborn, inother word ,unregenerated are spiritually \"dead\"', 8, '2021-02-01 07:16:50', '2021-02-02 21:46:05', NULL),
(2382, 636, 639, 'Scripture tells us that fallen human beings are born spiritually dead , the unregenerate remain inthe state of spiritual death because they haven\'t been renewed or \"regenerated \"by holy spirit , there are three charecteristic of the unregenerate , spiritual death , the moral inability, and their enymity with God, \r\nspiritual death , :iits tempting to think of spiritual death in ways that parallel spiritual death , but this acan be a little misleading , when we die phuycically our souls are seperated from our bodies , our bodies are in capable for indipendent action, and eventully decay to the point that thay return to dust, \r\nthe moral inability reffers to the fact that unregenerate human beings have , no capacity to please God or merit his blessings , most significantly , they cant appeal to him for forgiveness or work their way to salvation , Austin the bishop of hippo  who lived form AD 345-430, famously taught that before humanity\'s first sin, Adam and Eve ,lived inthe state of posse non peccare ,this latin phrase can be transilated literally as \"to be able not to sin \"  in the thological use \"the ability not to sin\"', 8, '2021-02-01 07:31:10', '2021-02-02 21:46:05', NULL),
(2383, 636, 640, 'The regenerate are those who hsve been born again through the work of the holy spirit , when that happens ,our spirit are regenerated or given new life , the bible teaches that all human beings are born into a state of spiritual death , so in order for us to be regenerate ,to have spiritual life ,our souls have to pass from death into life , human beings are first born when they receive physical life , but in order for us to receive God\'s blessing like salvation ,we have to undergo a second birth , a spiritual one , ,spiritual life is that  everlasting , some transilate it as eternal, when the holy spirit regenerates us our souls pass from death to life , and that life never ends , ebven when our earthly bodies die, our souls will live forever , \r\nmoral ability reffers to the capacity to please God and merit his blessing, our moral ability dose not enable us to earn our salvation ,things like forgivness ,righteousness and eternal life are free gifts from our god, based only on christ merit , but our restored moral ability dose empower us to obay God\'s will and to pursue the blessings of the kingdom,', 8, '2021-02-01 07:45:58', '2021-02-02 21:46:05', NULL),
(2384, 636, 641, 'As we saw in our discussion of spiritual death when our souls die they don\'t cease to exist , they dont even cease to be conscious ,and the sasme things is true when our souls are seprated from our bodies by physical death ,our souls continue to exist ,think and feel even after our bodies die, fror the unregenerereretad the fact that thire souls continue to exist should cause great fear , because when their spirit returns to God who gave it , its not to enjoy his blessing , its to begin suffering their just punishment in hell as jesus taught Luke 12:4-5, people should belive in hell for two reasons , first reason , that jesus christ spoke many times about hell , for example, he said, people should not fear onw who kill the body and nothijng else, but we should fesr the one who can kill the body and also throw the soul into hell ,', 7, '2021-02-01 07:56:46', '2021-02-02 21:46:05', NULL),
(2385, 636, 642, 'Obviously regenerate souls continue to exist just like unregenerate souls do ,but your experience is far different ,where as unregenerate souls suffer a foretaste of thier finall punishment , regenerate souls enjoy a foretaste of their final blessings , in several place biblical writers and charecters expressed their belief that when the regenerate dies , their souls are immidietly taken into God\'s presence in heaven , example Luke 23:43 jesus told to penitent thiefe onthe cross that very day they would be togethre in paradies , Revelation 6:9 speak of souls of the martyrs being close to the altar in God\'s heeavenly tabernacle ,', 7, '2021-02-01 08:06:03', '2021-02-02 21:46:05', NULL),
(2386, 637, 648, 'In jesus day there were at least two school of thought concering the resurrection of the dead ,the pharisees believed that there would be a general resurrection of the righteous and the wicked .but sadducees denied that there woud be a physical resurrection of the dead .in fact when paul was arrested and taken befor the jewish court ,called the sanhedrin, he appealed to his controversary in order to defend himself ,\r\n    The controversery between the pharisees and sadducees rooted in each group\'s understanding of scripture , the pharisees accepted the entire old testament as inspierd scripture , but the sadducees accepted only the five books written by Moses ,wich often called as Pentateuch , the general ressurection is clearly taught in passage like  Isaiah 26:19, Daniel 12:2, so the pharisees afirmed it ,but the sadducees denied it becuse they didnt see the doctrin of the resurrection , \r\n With regards to controversary , Jesus ,Paul,and rest of the early church clearly took the side of Pharisees. and refute the sadducees even more strongly ,', 8, '2021-02-02 07:15:10', '2021-02-02 21:46:40', NULL),
(2387, 637, 649, 'We can define God\'s divine authority in a variety of ways ,here we will describe it as God\'s legal and moral right to carry out his will ,when we say that the god has the authority to do somthing ,we mean that its perfectly within his rights to do it , and that he commits no wrong if he dose it , our discussion of God\'s divine authority in the general ressurection will divide into two parts, his authority over hell, and his authority over heaven, \r\nHell: it is important to recognize that God has complete authority over hell, when unregenerate souls suffer inhell during the intermediate state , they suffer because God is punishing them , and when they re drawn out of hell in order to face judgment , its because God has summoned into his court , \r\nHeaven : some crhistians have been confused about God\'s authority over hell ,none shoud be confused about his authority over heaven, heaven is God\'s throne room, the place wher his authority and glory are manifested more openly than anywhere else .', 8, '2021-02-02 07:27:10', '2021-02-02 21:46:40', NULL),
(2388, 637, 650, 'The general ressurections effect on creation will be felt in at least three different realms ,in natural world , hell , and in the heaven, \r\nNatural world :God uses eschatological event to alter how the natural world work in order to prepare it for his presence , God in his ordinary providence maketh use of means ,yet is free to work without above and against them at his pleasure , when the confection speaks of means , it has in mind things like cause and effect ,human vilation and physical laws of the univers .\r\nHell:when the unregenerated are the ressurected their souls will be removed from hell and returned to thier bodies on earth so that they can face God\'s judgement , but it wont be just the unregenerate that will have been emptited from hell , satan and the other demons will also have been removed by this point in the eschaton ,\r\nheaven :Like the resurected unregenereted , the resurrected regeneret will return to earth in order to appear befor God\'s judgment throne , heavenis a wonderful place , so it is easy to wonder why we would ever want to leave it, but god neveer intended us to live there forever .', 8, '2021-02-02 07:41:44', '2021-02-02 21:46:40', NULL),
(2389, 637, 651, 'Scripture mention three separate parties or groups that will face the final judgement , Fallen angels , unregenerate , and regenerate ,\r\nFallen angels :both 2 peter 2:4,and Jude 6, report that the demons used to be angels that held authority from God, but they rebelled against him, and abandoned thier heavenly homes and their authority , as a result they are now chained in gloomy dungens awaiting christ judgment .The elect angels that is , those who havent fallen wont be included int the judgment .\r\nthe second of the parties facing judgment will be unregenerate ,several pasage of scripture teach that when the resurrected human race appears before christ\'s judgment throne , the lord will seperated the unregenerate from the regenerate , \r\nthirdly Regenerate : after jesus separates unregenerate fromthe regenerate , he\'ll also render judgment on the regenerate , we see this in many place , including romans 2;7, Revelation 11:18,', 8, '2021-02-02 07:53:17', '2021-02-02 21:46:40', NULL),
(2390, 637, 652, 'God will consider every imaginable piece of evidence , in order to ensure that perfect justice is upheld , He\'ll evaluate everithing we\'ve done , though , and said .He\'ll look in to our secret motives , he will consider the covent that govern our rlationship with him, and thr revelation we recived in life , he will listen to the witness , nothing will be left out , and nothing will be inadmissble ,and all of this will be done in pursuit of perfect justice , so that every reward and every punishment will perfectly fit everyone who is judge , as we have mentioned how ever , not eveyone one will be held to the same level of accountability , rather we will each be judged according to our own situation ,', 7, '2021-02-02 08:01:53', '2021-02-02 21:46:40', NULL),
(2391, 635, 687, 'Theologian Thomas Aquinas insisted that the literal sense of Scripture was foundational to all its other senses. In his /summa Theologica , he insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal sense of a passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as spiritual meaning without being taught elsewhere in Scripture in the literal sense.\r\nIn Renaissance scholars begin to study classical  literary, philosophical, and religious texts in their original languages. in the medieval church most believers affirm that God \'s full intent in Scripture was known through a four fold Approach. The model following the literal, analogical, and the allegorical. The Quadriga or the four fold sense of\r\nscripture has a long and ancient history and tradition within Christian Church.\r\nBut scholars like William Whittaker says , they don\'t reject the idea of Quadriga, that there\'s four meaning, but we reject the idea that there is four meaning s or sense of to scripture. \r\nThere\'s just one and its historical, literal and grammatical.\r\nDuring the Renaissance, Protestants continued develop the idea that had been championed by Aquinas. they said that all the spiritual aspect of a text that were intended by the author for his original audience are actually aspects of its literal sense. they believed that Scripture\'s original sense or meaning  is both singular and complex.', 8, '2021-02-02 17:52:32', '2021-02-02 21:47:08', NULL),
(2392, 635, 688, 'Biblical elaborations are places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture. sometimes an elaboration is stated as a repetition of a facet of the original meaning . \r\nat other times , a biblical elaboration may be stated as a clarification of things that they weren\'t entirely apparent or well understood. at other times , a biblical elaboration can be expansion of the meaning of a  particular passage\r\n you shall not murder Exodus 20:13\r\nthe first biblical elaboration mentioned is primarily a repetition of these exact words in Deuteronomy 5:17\r\n the second type of elaboration we listed was clarification and we find it in Numbers 35:20-25\r\nthe third type of biblical elaboration is expansion  ,in which scripture provide additional information about the passage or topic: Mathew5:21-22\r\nAccording to Jesus elaboration , unrighteous anger violates the same principle that murder does. Anger is not as bas as murder, but it offends the same aspect of God\'s character.\r\nJesus invites us to go back to the principles behind exodus that its not just enough not to commit the sin , but we should not want to commit the sin, That is Jesus is not only is interested in our behavior but in our character, not just on what we do, but in who we are.', 9, '2021-02-02 18:03:25', '2021-02-02 21:47:08', NULL),
(2393, 635, 689, 'The more we study the scripture, the more we see that the bible elaborates on itself on and over. The prophets and Psalmists regularly refer back to Moses  Law. Jesus continually referred book of the Old Testament. And New Testament writers did much the same tome and again . We may have difficulty to understand , how biblical authors come to their conclusions. But in each case , biblical elaborations confirmed other parts of the  bible by repeating them, by clarifying them and even by expanding on their original meaning. and they did all of theses under the inspiration if the Holy spirit. And for this reason , as we explore the meaning of Scripture, we must acknowledge and submit ourselves to all the places where the scripture elaborate on themselves. \r\nAll legitimate applications of scripture must be derived from and consistent with the Bibles\' original meaning and elaborations.  we may make mistakes when we interpret the scripture, and apply them. \r\nif we make mistakes our applications should always subject to modification and improvement.\r\nand while the applications to scripture to our lives is necessary. we should never treat our applications as if they were infallible like the Bible.', 9, '2021-02-02 18:14:24', '2021-02-02 21:47:08', NULL),
(2394, 635, 690, 'The writer , the document and the audience are interdependent guide s to meaning, if we over emphasize the author, we often fall in to a intentional fallacy. it relies too heavily on what we think we know about a writer and his intentions and de-emphasizes the thinks we learn about the documents and audience. we might make unwanted assumptions about the author\'s intentions by speculating about what he was thinking when he wrote.\r\nA second type of mistake  we make is over emphasizing the document. this kind of error , is often called a graphic fallacy. the term graphic , comes from the Greek word, graphe which means writing. graphic fallacy overemphasizes the document itself.  we might commit this fallacy by thinking that we can sufficiently grasp the meaning of a passage  merely by analyzing its vocabulary, grammar and stricture without regard to its author or original audience\r\na third type of interpretive mistake we make is over emphasizing the audience. its called an affective fallacy, because its focus too heavily on how scripture effects its audience. the fallacy is often committed when an interpreter speculates too much about the mind set of the original audience and fails to pay enough attention to the writer and the document.\r\n we should access to resource that help us understand all its features. our information should not be limited, for our interpretations to be more generalized.', 9, '2021-02-02 18:28:02', '2021-02-02 21:47:08', NULL),
(2395, 635, 691, 'Using an authority dialogue approach, to the meaning of scripture, in this model acknowledges that objective truth can be found in the text of scripture as long as the methods comply with biblical standards. An authority dialog model is that it highlights the fact that all interpreters come to biblical writings with different sets of concerns, assumptions, backgrounds, and questions. each of us read scripture differently, because God has gifted each of us differently. we all have strengths and weakness. \r\nAn historian may summarize the meaning of Genesis 1 differently than an artist. An artist might talk about the beauty and the goodness of the stars in the night sky.\r\nthe personal strengths of the  interpreters lead each to bring out important but different aspect of the passage\' original meaning.\r\n\r\nwhen we summarize the  original meaning of Scripture , we look at the needs of different audiences. sometimes we summarize a passage to preach a sermons to adults, sometimes  preparing a bible study  for children, sometimes when wrestling with a particular problem or even just for our own spiritual growth. different audiences need different needs, so the multiple summaries are important to interpretation.', 8, '2021-02-02 18:40:31', '2021-02-02 21:47:08', NULL),
(2396, 638, 655, 'The historical analysis is concerned with the \" Acts of God\' found in the Bible.  Historical analysis looks at the scripture as a window to history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible.  \" Biblical Theology is the theological reflection drawn from the historical analysis of Acts of God reported in scripture. The Holy Spirit has lead the Church to pursue the exegesis of the scripture in three mail ways such as, Literary analysis, Historical analysis and thematic analysis. The Bible reports of many different kinds of historical events, but biblical theology primarily asks, \'what do the scriptures say that God has done? \'.', 8, '2021-02-03 09:37:29', '2021-03-10 15:57:02', NULL),
(2397, 638, 656, 'Factual historical analysis is concerned with how the events reported in scripture fit within the larger enviornment of the ancient near east. A factual approach to historical analysis is concerned with question like the date of Exodus, circumstances for Israel\'s monarchy, evidence of certain battles. The goal is to establish reliable account of the facts of history. \r\nBiblical theologians are more interested in the theological significance of the Acts of God. According to Thomas Aquinas, theology has two main concerns: any thing that refers directly to God and anything that describes other subjects in relation to God. Theological reflection teach us about God himself and what do mighty acts of God reveal about the charecter and will of God. \r\nBoth these analysis go hand in hand but differs in the area that when the factual historical analysis gives us the information and facts about divine acts in history; theological historical analysis gives the insight of God, his purpose, charecter and will in the event.', 8, '2021-02-03 09:48:59', '2021-03-10 15:57:02', NULL),
(2398, 638, 657, 'The Christian theologians have always tried to fulfill the great commision by reformulating Christian theology in ways that communicate well with their contemporary cultures. Significant cultural shifts happened in the 17th century that led theologians to emphasis historical interest as never before. The enlightenment of 17th century can be root of the cultural shifts of modern era. \r\nBiblical theology is a Christian response to a prominent intellectual movement in the modern period, often called \' modern historicism\'. For instance, avalanches of archiological discoveries shed much light on the ancient cultures of the world. The science of geology became a endeavor to discern the age and development of the earth. Even biology became historical in its focus on many biologists began to view their field in terms of Darvinian evolution. Similar shifts towards modern took place in nearly every academic discipline including theology.', 8, '2021-02-03 10:01:19', '2021-03-10 15:57:02', NULL),
(2399, 638, 658, 'Many modern Christians without denying the value and importance of scientific research, continued to hold that the scripture are true in all that they claim, including history. \r\nModern Evangelical Theologian Charles Hodge defined biblical theology as an exegetical discipline, the study of the facts of scripture. And also he defined systematic theology as the discipline that takes the facts discerned in biblical theology and arranges them in relation to each other. In contrast with the critical theologians, Hodge believed in the authority of the scripture. He taught that Christians are obligated to base systematic theology on the findings of the biblical theology. Followed by Benjamin B Warfield, it was explained that the teachings of scripture were already logically organized in the Bible itself. \r\nHe argued that the Bible never contradicts itself; all of its teachings are harmonious; though the authors described theology in different perspective and style. Thus they described systematic theology as an effort to bring all the theologies of the Bible in to a unified system. \r\nGeerhardus Vos agreeing to Hodge and Warfield, went further to say that the various theologies of the scripture had a common focus on the history of redemption. \r\nThe efforts of such theologians to establish the authenticity of biblical theology and the initiative to connect with systematic theology harmoniously has caused modern Christians to approach scriptures through biblical theology.', 8, '2021-02-03 10:14:55', '2021-03-10 15:57:02', NULL),
(2400, 638, 659, 'At each stage of biblical history, God had specific purposes or goals that guided his acts or word revelation. The ultimate purpose of Revelations were to establish God\'s Kingdom on earth. Romans 11:36. Jesus taught us to pray towards this end. Matthew 6:10. \r\nThe opening chapters of the scripture describe the way of God began to turn the chaotic world in to his Kingdom through out the world. Adam and Eve were given the obligation of expanding the Kingdom of God.  The rest of the OT reports how God chose Israel and commissioned them to lead the rest of humanity in spreading the Kingdom of God.\r\nDespite all the failures in different administrations, God did not give up his grand purpose. Through incarnation, death, resurrection and glorious return of Christ, God is fulfilling the task originally given to humanity. Revelation 11:15 describes Christ as the one who will bring God\'s Kingdom to earth as it is in heaven. \r\nAll the divine actions found through out the Bible, find culmination in the work of Christ who will bring ultimate glory to God through the establishment of his Kingdom in the new heaven and the new earth.', 8, '2021-02-03 10:26:20', '2021-03-10 15:57:02', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2401, 641, 834, 'God created this world and has full authority over everything He created. God then delegated some of His authority to human beings. God has authority over everything and that His perfect nature demands that anything He says must be trustworthy. He also saw that Christ Himself obeyed the Father\'s Word totally, fulfilling the scriptures to the minutest detail, thus setting an example for us. And because God delegated some of His authority to various human beings, we get to see His authority every day. God delegates authority in marriage. God delegates authority in the home. God delegates authority in the church. God delegates authority in the workplace. God delegates authority to civil government.', 7, '2021-02-06 02:37:57', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2402, 641, 835, 'Ethics in the Bible refers to the system or theory produced by the study, interpretation, and evaluation of biblical morals, that are found in the bible. It comprises a narrow part of the larger fields of Christian ethics., which are themselves parts of the larger field of philosophical ethics. Bible provides patterns of moral reasoning that focus on conduct and character in what is sometimes referred to as virtue ethics. Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God\'s approval and which ones do not. Christian ethics teaches us how to live.  Christian ethics asks what the whole Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God\'s approval and which ones does not.', 7, '2021-02-06 02:49:41', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2403, 641, 836, 'As it is said in Phillippians 3:10 - I want to know Christ - yes, to know the power of his resurrection and participation in his sufferings, becoming like him in his death. Certain decisions are easily guided by God\'s will. Simply doing what\'s right will drive most day-to-day interactions. However, when it comes to bigger life transactions. Sometimes, God\'s will may not be as immediately clear. Following are the four ways to help us to understand God\'s will through a difficult decision.\r\n\r\n1. Looking to the scriptures for answers.\r\n2. Using objective counsel to tell us what we need to hear.\r\n3. Learning on the peaceful control of Jesus Christ.\r\n4. Coming to know His heart to understand His will.', 7, '2021-02-06 03:00:04', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2404, 641, 837, '\" I am under obligation both to Greeks and to barbarians, both to the wise and to the foolish. So, fo my part, I am eager to preach the gospel to you also who are in Rome. For I am not ashamed of the gospel, for it is the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes, to the Jew first and also to the Greek\" - Romans 1:14-16.\r\n\r\nThe message of salvation in Jesus Christ is like a vast fortune that has been entrusted to us. These words reveal Paul\'s compelling sense of obligation in the proclamation of the gospel. These three \"I am\'s\" reveal his eagerness and excitement for the spread of the gospel.', 7, '2021-02-06 03:12:13', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2405, 641, 838, 'Scholars generally recognize three languages as original biblical languages: Hebrew, Aramaic, and Koine Greek. The Old Testament was originally written almost entirely in Hebrew, with a few short elements in Aramaic.  The Vulgate is usually credited as being the first translation of the Old Testament into Latin directly from the Hebrew Tanakh rather than from the Greek Septuagint. Wisdom, Ecclesiasticus, Maccabees, and Baruch are included in the Vulgate and are purely Vetus Latin translation.  The Adamic language, according to Jewish tradition, is the language spoken by Adam in the Garden of Eden. Biblical languages are studied more widely. The very first translation of the Hebrew Bible was into Greek.', 7, '2021-02-06 03:27:52', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2406, 641, 839, 'According to the Bible, God revealed the Ten Commandments to the Hebrew leader Moses at Mount Sinai. These commandments, which have been preserved in the Old Testament in Exodus 20: 1-7 and Deuteronomy  5:6-21, established a code of ethical conduct.  God gave Moses the Ten Commandments on Mount Sinai to serve as principles of moral behavior for the human race. The 10 Commandments are commonly divided into two parts. Because the commandments establish a covenant, it is likely that they were duplicated on both tables. Jesus said: Don\'t think that I came to destroy the law or the prophets. I didn\'t come to destroy, but to fulfill.', 7, '2021-02-06 03:41:13', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2407, 641, 840, 'Mosaic laws are divided into three categories: moral, civil, and ceremonial.  These were specifically given for the culture of the Israelites, which includes everything from murder to restitution and dietary restrictions. Second, are ceremonial laws.  In the view of Westminister Divines, only the moral laws of the Mosaic Law, which include the Ten Commandments and the commands repeated in the New Testament directly apply to Christians today. The law of Christ is a New Testament phrase that most likely refers to the two commandments which are mentioned by Jesus \"you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength.', 7, '2021-02-06 03:49:21', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2408, 641, 841, 'If Jesus did not come to abolish the law, does that mean all the Old Testament laws still apply today? God\'s laws were given to help people love God with all their hearts and minds. Throughout Israel\'s history, however, these laws had often been misquoted and misapplied. By Jesus\'s time, religious leaders had turned the laws into a confusing mass of rules. When Jesus talked about a new way to understand God\'s law, he was actually trying to bring people back to its original purpose. Jesus did not speak against the law itself but against the abuses and excesses to which it had been subjected. Some of those in the crowd were experts at telling others what to do, but they themselves missed the central point of God\'s laws. Jesus made it clear that obeying God\'s laws is more important than explaining them. It\'s much easier to study God\'s laws and tell others to obey them than to put them into practice.', 7, '2021-02-06 04:02:20', '2021-03-10 15:57:33', NULL),
(2409, 640, 660, 'The word \'Synchronic\' derived from two Greek words \'sun\' and \'chronos\' which means \' together with\' and \'time\' respectively. So the meaning can be derived as \'together in time or \' at the same time\'. The term \' synchronic\' is used to indicate how biblical theologians often explore sets of events in Old Testament history that happened in time. \r\nAs a movie is divided in to different scenes which tell a portion of the large story; the old Testament history is approached with similar approach. \r\nin Synchronic synthesis, biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular periods of time in the OT as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of its entire history. Biblical theologians when they apply synchronic approach some times, they focus on relatively short time frames and other time on long term periods of history. For example some times, the sacrifice of Issac is dealt seperately from Abraham\'s story; other time his life as a whole story. Some times biblical theologians treat the whole OT as a synchronic unit, to know what God did and said in the OT period.', 8, '2021-02-08 09:41:17', '2021-03-10 15:58:04', NULL),
(2410, 640, 661, '\'Synthesis\' simply means \'combining different components of some thing in to a whole\'. In this approach, we describe the ways different components of theology revealed in a particular period of history fit together in a coherent, logical structure. \r\nBy the middle of the 20th century critical theologians began to deny the logical character of the OT. Based on a number of linguistic and cultural assessments they argued that the Greeks focused on abstraction and logical order, much like systematic theology.  And by contrast they suggested that the Hebrew mind set looked everything in terms of historic dynamics. From this point of view, the OT did not focus on logical systems or on theological relationships between beliefs.\r\nBut in contrast to the denial, the logical character of OT is affirmed because of two reasons. Primarily, recent studies have largely discredited the kinds of contrast between Greek and Hebrew minds; but concluded they were very similar to each other. Secondly, the OT theology displays substantial concern for logic and rational thought. \r\nThough the OT theology does not employ the standards of formal western philosophical traditions; God\'s revelation in scripture were not random, disconnected or contradictory.', 9, '2021-02-08 09:53:57', '2021-03-10 15:58:04', NULL),
(2411, 640, 662, 'The poets of the OT were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history. First they gave attention to \'the world\' they wrote about. In the first place poetry often opened windows to the past as they provided objective facts about God\'s acts and words. Eg: Song of Moses and Miriam in Exodus 15. \r\nSecondly OT poetry provided windows to contemporary historical information from the writer\'s own time. Eg: Psalms 1:1- Meditation of the law. \r\nThirdly OT poets turned their readers attention towards the future. Isaiah 40:1-11, return of exiles. In one way or other the OT poetry often conveyed information about the revelatory acts and words of God in the past, present and future. \r\nLike poets, the authors of narratives also stood between two worlds. On the one side, they wrote about world that was the subject of their \'text\' or \'the world\'. Narrative, largely focus on the past, only rarely mention the present or the future. \r\nOn the other side the authors of the narratives also dealt with their \' world\', the world in which their readers lived.  They wanted their readers to think, act and feel in certain ways within their own worlds in the light of past events. \r\nThe OT narratives were doxological, theological, poetical, polemical, moral and motivational. They were didactic, designed to teach early readers about their lives.', 8, '2021-02-08 10:07:10', '2021-03-10 15:58:04', NULL),
(2412, 640, 663, 'Since the enlightenment in the 17th century, historians expect OT narratives to be like modern journalistic historic writings. Three modern standards that are often mistakenly applied to OT narratives are : primarily, historical accounts should be comprehensive. But the OT authors felt no compulsion to include every significant fact because it was written with a didactic purpose. Though they did not meet the criterion of comprehensiveness in good history writing; their narratives are true and authoritative records of the past. \r\nSecondly, the OT authors were only as precise as their didactic purpose required. They were only precise as they needed to reach their didactic goals. The lack of modern precision does not mean that the accounts are untrue. \r\nThirdly the OT narratives are not objective by modern standards. God inspired OT authors to shape the opinions of their readers. This goal influenced what they omitted, what they included and how they described them. \r\nThe OT narratives were not designed to meet modern standards of history writing. They only present fully reliable historical information that will enable us to construct synchronic synthesis of OT theology.', 8, '2021-02-08 10:19:07', '2021-03-10 15:58:04', NULL),
(2413, 640, 664, NULL, 0, '2021-02-08 10:21:11', '2021-02-08 10:21:11', NULL),
(2414, 639, 692, 'as theologians teaches  God is immutable meaning that he doesn\'t change, since God is immutable and since loyalty and obedience to him are usual obligation, there are strong connections between the impact. Scripture was intended to have on its original audience and its it modern audience.\r\nSo God listens to our prayers, grieves over our sins, delights in our faithfulness, so being said that God is essentially immutable, but relationally mutable.\r\nimmutability might be translated unchangeable, descriptor of God as still point in ever turning universe.\r\nThere are three critical aspects of God\' s immutability\r\n1. First God\' eternal counsel \r\nEverything God ahs done is doing, and will do is part of a unified plan. God knows everything and he is using that knowledge to direct history towards the end for which he created it . Isaiah 46:10\r\nthe immutability of God\'s plan assures us that if we look closely enough, god\'s way in ancient times accord with his ways today. at some level god\'s level for his ancient people and his will for us are similar. because they both fit within his one immutable purpose in his creation.\r\n2 Character\r\nGod reveals different aspects of his character, prominently at same time than others. some he displays his mercy, sometimes his wrath, sometimes he reveals his omniscience and other times he hides. but the full range of his attribute, his eternal nature remains the same.\r\nJames 1:17 \r\nGod\'s immutable character helps us to see that there will be always be significant connections between the original and modern applications of the scripture\r\n3. covenant promises:\r\nwhen God promises,  his word is immutable, otherwise he is free to change his mind\r\nNumbers 23:19\r\nEvery revelation  of God in scripture assumes that god will keep his covenant and his covenant promises. the original audience is supposed to understand every passage of scripture in this light and modern audience should do the same.', 9, '2021-02-09 17:53:07', '2021-03-10 15:58:33', NULL),
(2415, 639, 693, 'Three ways in which modern people are very similar to the Scripture original audience are\r\n1.Sinful ways:\r\nGenesis 1:27 when god created humanity, he created in hid image. all humans are rational , linguistic religious and moral vice regents of God . All human being have sinned and so they are not using  above capacities to glorify God the way we should. 1kings 8:46 \r\nin systematic theology, there is a teaching known as total depravity. which means totality of means being and his thinking, feeling and has all been tainted with sin so  that everything he does is a defiance of God\'s commandments and standard.\r\n2. Religious divisions\r\nreaders of scripture have fallen into these religious groups,\r\nunbelievers are people who make themselves the enemies of God by refusing to submit to him.\r\nfalse believers make superficial commitment to God. \r\nbelievers are people who make sincere , faithful commitments to god, and who are there by redeemed from sin and saved from god\'s eternal judgement.\r\n3 .Classes\r\nwe might be classified according to particular traits or attributes we have. some people are old , young, male , female , rich and poor. we might be parents, sibling, servants , friends or any else. because these some classes of people exist in every age, they form meaningful connections between the original audience and all subsequent audiences. ancient and modern rich people can draw similar applications from passages about wealth. same in all fields', 9, '2021-02-09 18:05:42', '2021-03-10 15:58:33', NULL),
(2416, 639, 694, 'Christians have summarized the bible\'s perspective on world history in threes stages:\r\n creation , when God first made the world, fall: when humanity first committed sin and was cursed by God and redemption : the period following the fall in which god redeems us from our sin. Son after fall, God began the slow process of redemption\r\nthe most obvious epochal developments occurred between the old and new testaments:\r\n but theologians also commonly identify , epochs according to God\'s various covenants throughout the bible, especially, those associated with Adam, Abraham , Noah, Moses and David in the Old Testament and with Jesus in the New Testament.\r\nIn Moses\' time, they required sacrifices and the tabernacle. in Solomon\'s they required sacrifices and the temple.\r\nin the early New Testament , they required Jesus death on the cross and in later New Testament, they ceased to be offered altogether.\r\nBiblical history is like a growing tree. every tree from a seed, becomes a sapling and eventually develops into a grown tree. every thing that tree will become is contained in the initial seed but over time the tree has to grow and develop in order to become fully mature. Redemption has grown and developed throughout biblical history. these developmental model teaches us that the whole Bible is relevant and authoritative for us, but also that older revelation must always be applied in light of later revelation.', 8, '2021-02-09 18:17:31', '2021-03-10 15:58:33', NULL),
(2417, 639, 695, 'The old testament reports many theological changes that took place through out history. these changes can be considered as a growing tree. A healthy tree goes though many changes as it grows, from a small tree to full mature tree. \r\ntheologians have found different ways to divide the history recorded in the old testament. Time doesn\'t pass in sharply segmented periods. Transitions between epochs generally took place gradually and the epochs themselves often overlapped. the divisions theologians draw depend on the criteria they use.\r\n when  a tree goes through stage of growth, it different parts don\'t grow at the same time or at the same place. sometimes some parts of a tree grow slowly and some quickly. similarly some parts of Old testament theology advanced slowly and other proceeded at a medium pace and others changed rapidly.', 9, '2021-02-09 18:26:27', '2021-03-10 15:58:33', NULL),
(2418, 639, 696, 'New testament believer should never apply the scripture as if they lived in earlier period of history. in earlier Mosaic Epoch , Israel sacrificed at Moses tabernacle, God has commanded to offer sacrifice only at the temple. in historical context sacrificing at Moses tabernacle would violate God\'s will. one God commanded a new  way for sacrifice in worship , he expected his people to never to return to the old ways. about the modern application of the old testament theme of sacrifice in worship, we have to be aware that we live in epoch of the new covenant. as the New testament explains repeatedly, Christ \'s once for all time- perfect sacrifice has superseded every earlier form of sacrifice. his death on the cross changed how god\'s faithful people are supposed to offer sacrifice in God\'s worship.\r\nif we lived in Moses day but prefer to follow  a tribal patriarch as God\'s people did in the covenant age of Abraham , in every age we need to follow the military leader ship that God has established  for that age. we no longer wage physical war ,like Joshua did, because the new testament only calls us to spiritual warfare.', 9, '2021-02-09 18:40:45', '2021-03-10 15:58:33', NULL),
(2419, 642, 619, 'Two types of evidence emerge that strongly suggest the person wrote both books. \r\nExplicit Evidence  acts 1:1 here the writer spoke of his former book , meaning that Acts is the second of at least two volumes . he also indicate that he wrote his book to person named Theophilus. Acts 1:1-4\r\nin Luke 1:1-4 we read > Most excellent Theophilus. once again this passage refers to some one name Theophilus . But there is no reference to second book. both Acts and the third gospel is dedicated to Theophilus. Book of Acts refers to former book. these facts provide strong evidence that the author of these books produced at least two volumes with the gospel of Luke being the first volume and  Acts  being the second volume.\r\nImplicit evidence: Luke 1:1-4 states that the author had invested a variety of source and had made an orderly account dedicated to Theophilus . An number of scholars have noted that the accounts in Luke \'s gospel and the book of acts are ordered and shaped in similar ways. there are also several similarities in the compositional structure of the books. the books proceed in an episodic style, and both are roughly of same length , each filling a standard size scroll. beyond this, there is a similar chronological length in each book. both Luke and acts cover  roughly same number of years and the themes are parellell.', 8, '2021-02-12 17:45:58', '2021-03-10 16:00:17', NULL),
(2420, 642, 620, 'From the second to the fourth century AD, the early Church testified that Luke the travelling companion of Paul , was the author of both the gospel of Luke and the Acts. \r\nManuscripts: very old manuscripts , Papyrus, discovered in1952 in Egypt and include some of our earlier New Testaments manuscripts evidence. Between AD 75-200, includes large portions of both eth gospel of Luke and John. after the conclusion of Gospel of Luke, the manuscripts contains the word \"Evangelion ata Lukan\" or the gospel according to Luke\r\nsecond proof is the Muratorian Fragment dated around AD 170 to 180 is the earliest document listing the New Testament books that the early church consider to be Canonical. After affirming, Luke authorship of Luke, it explicitly points to Luke as the author of Acts as well\r\nthird , so called Anti- Marcionite Prologue describes  the authorship of Luke and Acts . Luke moved by the holy spirit , composed whole of this gospel .\r\nEarly church leaders:\r\nThe church Father Irenaeus , AD 130- 202, believed that the Luke was the author of the third gospel and Acts\r\nClement of Alexandria , who lived from around  AD 150-215 also referred the same. \r\nTertullian who lived from A D 155-230  and great Church historian Eusebius said that Luke has made mention of the census in the Acts.', 8, '2021-02-12 17:58:35', '2021-03-10 16:00:17', NULL),
(2421, 642, 621, 'Luke wrote about the early church keeping in mind the threefold outlook of history, which are god prepared the world, sin and  redemption.\r\nLuke awareness of this important Old Testament idea is evident in many place .In Acts 4:24-30, Peter and John spoke of creation as evidence of God\'s royal lordship. in !4:15-17 Paul and Barnabas spoke of creation as the basis for God\'s rule over the nation.\r\nLuke\'s awareness of Humanity \'s fall into sin also come to the foreground in the book of Acts. he wrote of the misery of sin in many places throughout Acts. Acts 2:38\r\nthe book of Acts repeatedly illustrates that everything in creation was effected by sin.\r\nand in the end he frequently displayed his belief that the redemption was coming to the world through the saving word of Christ.\r\nIsrael\r\nGod\' s choice of Abraham: God said that Abraham would father a great nation, become famous, and receive many spiritual and material blessings. he freqeuntly reported how the blessing of salvation in Christ came to the Jews, fulfulling the promise of God.\r\nSecond the exodus by Moses:\r\nthe understanding of the relationship between Moses and the Christian church as God\'s deliverer\r\n\r\nin the third place , we see David\'s dynasty as he wrote frequently in Acts', 9, '2021-02-12 18:13:42', '2021-03-10 16:00:17', NULL),
(2422, 642, 622, 'deep Connection between the Jews and the Church:\r\nJesus was Jewish, and so were the apostles , first members of the Church were almost all Jews. many people in the early church attended  temple worship, met in synagogues and maintained appreciation for many Jewish customs. the early church and Jewish community were both committed to the same scriptures. \r\ndifferences:\r\nMost fundamentally Christians and unbelieving Jews disagreed over the person and the work of Jesus.\r\nsecond they differed on the authority of Hebrew scriptures.\r\nthe third place , they differed over how they deal with gentiles.\r\nunderstanding the similarities and differences of the Christians and Jews  would help us evangelize the Jews in a better way.  when we know that they follow the same Scriptures as the Christians do, we should try to make them understand that it is the savior , Jesus who they were waiting for.\r\nAnd that Gentiles were to be included in the plan of redemption when Abraham was blessed to be blessing.', 9, '2021-02-12 18:26:18', '2021-03-10 16:00:17', NULL),
(2423, 642, 623, 'the Gospel message has two sides . On is the objective side and other the subjective side.\r\nthis side announced that then final phase of God\'s kingdom expands towards its great commission as God grants salvation to those whom we receive and trust in Jesus as the messiah. the rule of God over the world, moves forward as the gospel touched the hearts of those who believe and brings them into the salvation as Jesus accomplished. on Many occasions Luke stresses the importance of people personally embracing the truth of Christ so that it transformed their lives. The Christian gospel cuts to the heart of those who hear it. it is not just a bare acknowledgement of facts, but a heart felt life transforming embrace of the savior. so we should change our priorities to spreading the message of gospel to as many as possible. and we should adopt the thinking of the power of gospel which frees every mankind from the slavery of sin.', 8, '2021-02-12 18:36:47', '2021-03-10 16:00:17', NULL),
(2424, 643, 897, 'Near the end of paul\'s missionary journey probably around the year AD 56 or 57 Paul and his travaling companions were making their way from asia minor to Jerusalem , primarly by boat ,their intension was to deliver funds to the poor christians in Jerusalem who were enduring a famine .on their way they stopped in the Miltus were pul met with the elder from the near by church of Ephasus ,during this meeting Paul reaveled that the holy spirit had warned him that he would be imprisoned when he arrived in Jerusalem , Again when he was staying in Caesarea Maritima paul was warned yet not to go to jerusalem, in a well known Dramatic scens the prophat agabas bound his own hands and feet as prophatic sign ,warning that paul woud be artested and bound if he continue to jerusalem, , but Paul undeersood that his coming imprisonment was would be \"for the name of the lord Jesus\" that the holy spirit was going to use Paul\'s coming imprisonmemnt as the means to advance the gospel and minsiter to the church .', 8, '2021-02-15 15:42:04', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2425, 643, 898, 'We are familiar with the event preceding Paul\'s arrest we ar ein a possition to investigate the circumstances of his arrest in Jerusalem, When Paul arrived inJerusalem ,he stayed with a beliver named Mnason and was well recived by the church ,,the next day Paul visited James who was the brother of Jesus and the author of new testament book of James , the elders of the church in Jerusalem also gather to meet Paul , we know that paul was very concerned with the role these founds wich coollected from rom would pay not only adding the poor christian in jerusalem , but also in reconcilling Jewish and gentiles belivers .while paul was inthe court of israel , he was recognized by some jewish from Asia minor , these were very likely unbelieving jewis,rather than christian convert from judaism, Erlier the same jews had seen Trophimus who had accompained paul to Jerusalem ,Asian jes knwo that he was gentile , so when theyb sow paul inthe court of Israel, they wrongly assumed that Trophimus has also enter in to the court, and they were outraged , these issue led pau\'s arrest in Jerusalm.', 8, '2021-02-15 15:55:38', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2426, 643, 899, 'Paul was a prisoner of Rome , he had to be transported under roman guard , so has placed under authority of a roman centurian name Julius and put on a ship heading to asia minor , they were traveling ship  to italy , but climat was not good , it was winet season, wether had become dangerous for sailing ,the dangers of sailing at tis time pule to advise centurian julius not to put out for Italy, ,in any event captain and owner of the ship convinced julius that their journey would be successful , but on the way the ship distroyed , the sailors,soldiers , prisoners ,and everyone else from the ship was strained on Malta, Paul performs may miracles in malta, his healing ministry bergan, when he heald the father of phubaious , pubalious was the cheaf pficial of Malta , afeter some mothes paul and his vcompainous tkaen to Rom, he arrived in rom later AD 60,and was placed under house arrest , paul lived in house arrest in rome for two years,', 8, '2021-02-15 16:08:53', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2427, 643, 900, 'Now we have surveyed the background of Paul\'s imprisonment , we are in a position to explore his on going ministry during his imprisonment , Paul was an apostele, Jesus had personlly called and trained PAul and Appointed him to serve as his ambassador , his covenant emissary , and as strange it may sound to us today , God himself has orchestrated events so that paul\'s imprisonment in Rome would be provide the opertunity for paul to spread the gospel christ to the heart of the western world Paul\'s experiance in the imprisonment were verey important ,paul was aware that his service to christ  was about to bring severe hardship and suffering into his life , and also paul was actuly aware of dificulties that awaited him in hgis servicfe to christ and his gospel .', 7, '2021-02-15 16:18:24', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2428, 643, 901, 'The book of Acts teaches us at least three major theames appear on may occasion ,:First paul was aware that his service to Christ was about to bring sever harshipmand suffering top his life , In acts 19-28 Luke discribed Paul as well informed about his coming hardship, Paul new that he would be imprisoned and suspected that he would even be put to death , \r\nsecondly his awareness of purpose : in the second place , paul was well aware of the purpose of his suffering, he new that if God planed  to let him suffer , thye lord also planed to use this suffering to promote the gospel , pual believed that God would use his hardship to spread the christian gospel .\r\nThirdly his awareness of blessing: Paul was very aware of God\'s blessing on his ministry during this time, Luke also tells us that his ministry include other tnings thta contributed ton his ability to proclaim the gospel and apply it to the lives of individuals .', 7, '2021-02-15 16:29:45', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2429, 643, 902, 'Paul emphasis on Christ Kisngship over creation is perhaps more pronounsed in his letter from prison than in any of his other writings , we will focus on three aspects of christ kingship that appear frequently in the prison epistels , \r\nSovereignty :when we say that christ is Sovereign we meant that he has the strength ahd power to accomplish his will , and that he has the legal authority and right to do so, according to paul when jesus ascended into heaven,God the father vested him with this type of sovereignty over all creation, jesus ois so powerful and so authoritive now thyat his soverignty extended over all other kigs and ruls as well\r\nDetermination : to understand Paul\'s outlook on the return of christ ,we must remember that this teaching about the end times grew out of traditional jewish vews  of the nd times, the present age was to be followed byb the age to come , wich the bible also refers to asthe kingdom of God and the kingdom of heaven, \r\nHoner:Christ is horned becuse he is perfect , holy and righteous , and he is horned because he holds a position of highest authority and because he exicutes that authority justly and righteously , he also honerd because he himself is the most valuble being in all of creation, the one whome God value .', 8, '2021-02-15 16:44:36', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2430, 643, 903, 'According to Paul when we beleive in Jesus we are united to him in a mysterious spiritul way ,and because we are united to Jesus ,we are continued as if we were jesus .Example, Jesus blameless before God ,and because we are united to him, we also counted as blameless before God with all our sin being forgiven, through our union with christ , we are united to christ\'s death so that we also died with him, and we are united to christ in his resurrection and life so that we are also raised with him, we are also united to christ his ascention, and kingship so that we have return in to glory  we will rule with him , according to Paul now we are seated with christ ,inthe heavenly places , being united to him in his present kingship over all creation, as aresult we share his honer and his blessings in a spiritual way right now though our earthly circumstances may not reflect it.', 7, '2021-02-15 16:54:07', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2431, 643, 904, 'Pauls letter from prison share some important doctrinal foundation .most basically they all affirm the same gospel ,but beyond that they all share a common way of presenting that gospel , and they tend to emphasize , the same aspect of that gospel , is is not to say that thy are identical to one another ,but there is a big picture that units them , a common foundation of that fact that jesus christ is the conqueror and ruler of all creation, our discussion of the theological unity of thye prison epistles will emphasize three main doctrines :firstly jesus christ is king of creation, Paul emphasize christ kingship over creation is perhaps more pronounsed in his letters .his sovereignty ,', 8, '2021-02-15 17:03:08', '2021-03-10 16:00:50', NULL),
(2432, 648, 624, 'Luke plainly stated that he had a twofold purpose as he wrote. \r\nLuke declared that he had historical intention, desire to write a true and reliable historical account of the church of the first century. On the other hand, he declared that he had a significant theological intentions,  a desire to convey and confirm the significance of the gospel message.\r\nHistorical account:\r\nLuke 1:1-3,  \r\nLuke referred to\" the things that been fulfilled among us\", that is historical events that had taken place, Luke also mentioned that , he had consulted , eye witnesses and \"the at he had carefully investigated the details he recorded. he also took take to write an orderly account, so that the truth he reported would be communicated clearly and accurately. \r\nGospel message: Luke intentioned the theological purpose of conveying the reality and power of the gospel message in the book of acts. Luke 1:3-4 Luke wrote the history of Acts to confirm what Theophilus and others had been taught . Luke viewed the world and all of history through the lens of Christ\'s Lordship and kingdom. He saw the Old Testament hopes and promises being fulfilled though Jesus and Church. and he wanted Theophilus to view his record of events in the early church through this lens, to see how Christ through the Spirit of God had established and was continuing to build upon the kingdom of God in Christ. \r\nThis purpose have helped us to understand the book of acts better as we know that Luke was giving a record after lot of studies and confirmation . and viewing everything being concluded in Christ Lordship and kingdom finally gives us a hope and faith in Him.', 8, '2021-02-15 17:54:06', '2021-03-10 16:01:24', NULL),
(2433, 648, 625, 'the two facets of structure of acts are \r\n1. summary statements: Luke made many authorial comments throughout acts . He gave background information, he revealed the intentions of Characters hearts, described settings and son on. he did this to ensure that his message was presented clearly and faithfully. \r\nstatements for six periods of time in his history:\r\n success of the gospel in Jerusalem. In Judea and Samaria, From Samaria to Syrian Antioch, in Cyprus, \r\nPhrygia and Galatia, In Asia, Macedonia and Achaia and From Jerusalem to Rome\r\nChurch Growth:\r\nwhen Luke described the growth of the church, he regularly mentioned two pairs of dynamic forces. he wrote  about internal growth referring to the positive effects of the gospel within the Christian community. then he refers to the tension which means the problems, questions , controversies and struggles that occurred within the church. the second pair of elements is Eternal growth and opposition . Four stages of growth by structural statements:\r\n1. Pheonicia, Cyprus and Antioch Acts 9:32 -12:25\r\n2.Cyprus  Phrygia and Galatia Acts 13:1-15:35\r\n3 . Asia, Macedonia and Achaia Acts 15:36-21:16\r\n4. Rome Acts 21-28:31\r\n\r\nthis patterns encourage we believers everywhere that no matter how great the tension or how terrible the opposition god is always working through the gospel   for the internal and external growth of his church.', 8, '2021-02-15 18:09:43', '2021-03-10 16:01:24', NULL),
(2434, 648, 626, 'As we attempt to find ways to apply the book of Acts in the modern , we need to do two things. on the one hand we need to avoid the error of thinking that Luke recorded everything we might ever want to know about this period of church history. there are many questions he left unanswered , so we need to avoid looking for every answers in our modern problem in the book of acts. on the other hand we need to remember that, each account in the book of Acts is there to help Luke accomplish his two fold purpose , so we need to read everything in the book in the light of how it helps Luke further this goal, we should constantly be asking questions like , what does these things teach me about the early church? and what doctrines does it encourage me to adopt.\r\nif we hope to understand the book of acts in  a responsible way, we must take into account the kind of Literature it is and the way this kind of Literature communicates its idea. \r\nFirst Luke was selective in what he reported, he designed Acts to be episodic and he communicate din implicit ways.', 8, '2021-02-15 18:21:13', '2021-03-10 16:01:24', NULL),
(2435, 648, 627, 'One of the chief ways to evaluate  and apply any biblical narrative to see our live sis to see how God reacts to actions that takes place. above all others , his words and actions are perfectly infallible. as a result, we should always take not of the things in the books of Acts that god approve sand blesses and the things that he disapproves or curses.\r\nwhatever god bless must be good and whatever he disapproves or cursed must be evil. as we read the book of Acts, we should seek to emulate the beliefs , attitudes and behaviors that are pleasing to god and to avoid those that oppose him. \r\nGod approves of obedience and repentance as Paul did.  He blesses Cornelius\' god fearing nature. He approves Peter\'s submission to God\' s word , at the same time God disapproves dishonestly and greed of Ananias and Sapphira. He also disapproves of Simon who thought of buying the power of Holy spirit with money. God approves faith, love, obedience, humility, peace, trust while he disapproves ignorance, stubbornness, greed and selfishness. we should pay attention to these things to follow in our live as well as guide others the same to be blessed by our God.', 7, '2021-02-15 18:34:29', '2021-03-10 16:01:24', NULL),
(2436, 648, 628, 'Besides living in a different time than the original audience of Acts, we also have different circumstance, which has different cultures and personal situations. \r\nIn acts 5:42, we read that the church met in private homes, on this basis some Christian insist that the church today must meet in home sand not in buildings. and in Acts 6:1 we find that then church in Jerusalem was providing food for widows. As a  result some Christian insist that every church  must provide food for widows. \r\nthere is nothing inherently wrong with church today meting at home or serving food to widows. but we must recognize that  this practice were conditioned upon the circumstance of the first century church. for example, persecution necessitated there meetings in homes. where there is little or nor persecution , the church doesn\'t need to meet in homes, as our circumstances resemble thiers .This may be legitimate application of biblical principles. but as our circumstances are different, we may be obligated to same principle sin different ways. different application of the same principles we see even in the books of acts itself. in act 2:44-45, Luke described that the members of the church in Jerusalem as owing their resources in common , yet within the book of acts itself , we find that many of the churches established by the Apostle Paul met in the homes of wealthy  citizens or city leaders. \r\nso the same biblical principles must be applied in ways that are appropriate to the current circumstances.', 8, '2021-02-15 18:50:02', '2021-03-10 16:01:24', NULL),
(2437, 644, 905, 'Paul\'s relationship with Colossian church , Paul did not have the same relationship with every Colossian Christian ,so we will focus first on his relationship with the church in general and then on his relationship with specific individual .\r\nChurch :Now paul had traveled trough Phrygia during his second and third missionary Journey , but for some reason he had not visited the Collosian church ,possibly he had been to Collosse prior to the establishment of the church there ,or perhaps he had visited the city but not had the oppertunity to meet the cburch, , neverthless we are able to lern somthing about Paul\'s relationship with the Colossian from the details in the Paul\'s letter to tyem as well as fom his letter to philomon , who lived in Colosse , we read that Paul had an indirect relationship with the Collosssians through respectives ,Second althogh  they had not meet face to face , he has relataion with colossian friends , Epaphras, Philomon, and Onesimos , and his messenger Tychicus ,Paul and Collosisans had correspondence each other, they ministerd each other ,', 8, '2021-02-16 15:43:06', '2021-03-10 15:56:21', NULL),
(2438, 644, 906, 'While Paiul was in prison , Epaphras came and meet him and told him about some falls teaching that were threttening the churchs of Lycus valley ,including gthe cbhurches in the coloss, som inorder to diffend thye church against these falls teaching ,Paul wrote his letter to the Colossians , First fallesd teaching in colossians was mixed christianity with element of Greek philosophy , second it depended heavely on jewish Law , and third there were many spiritual being that vchristiance were requierd to venerate and to appere,\r\nGreek Philosophy :in the firstvcentury mediterranean world , there was non sharp distiction between religious specification on the one hand and intelectual study on the other hand ,the world philosophy was typically applaid to occult religions , espaclly tyhose are based on religious tradition, these tradition involved specific mysteris and rites as well as knowledge and wisdom.\r\nJewish laws,:as we have seen , Paul upheld the Mosiac law, and he was willing to accept and participate in many traditional jewish practice for the sake of the gospel, if the falles teachers inthe colloos had employed the law in a valid way paul would not have critizised ,his critisizm indicate that the fals teachers were using jewish teachings and practice in corrept wqay \r\nSpiritual beings :Colosians s church was courting the worship of spiritual poweres in evident in atleast three ways, Paul wrot the worship of angel, matter of rulers and athority, delt with problem related to the basic priciples of the world', 8, '2021-02-16 16:00:00', '2021-03-10 15:56:21', NULL),
(2439, 644, 907, 'Paul was writting letters was an aspects of his authoritive ministry as Christ representative , another part of being an apostel was pastoring churches and individuals , we can understand the background to paul\'s letter to the Colossian from  two direction .First ,his relationship with the church in general, and with individual inthe church ,and second investigate some of the problems in the Colosse that concernd paul, Pauls have no direct and face to face relation with colossian church, but he had connection with them in general throgh the letters, also he has connection with some friends in the church ,that was an indiduval relation, \r\nsecondly , he came to know that there are some falls teaching is going on in collose, Greek phylosophy , the wrong practice of mosiac law and rights, and some nagel worships , when he came to know these all he wrote the letter to them .', 7, '2021-02-16 16:10:32', '2021-03-10 15:56:21', NULL),
(2440, 644, 908, 'Falls teachers were trying to persuade the Colossian church to worship Cosmic, spiritual powers , and they were encoraging as ascetic life style , thinking that such harsh living would placate the spiritual powers and reap some benifit from these falls gods,so paul began to refute these heresies by showing that chrusit is suprime over all so called gods, \r\non the one hand Paul insisted Christ is the king of all creation and that he posses all perfection and authority , on other hand he taught the basic principle of the world are incapable of delivering the blessings of the salvation and unworthy of reverence ,Paul listed many important aspectes of supremecy of Christ ,in Colossian 1:15-20\'he spoke the christ as the image os the God ,in Colo 1:15,first born over all creation ,1:16,Suprime Lord,1:18,God incarnate :1:19,and only reconciler in Colo1:20,', 7, '2021-02-16 16:20:20', '2021-03-10 15:56:21', NULL),
(2441, 644, 909, 'After demonstrating the suprimecy of christ over the spiritual power ,Paul asserted the suprimacy of Christ ministers , Paul argued that christ was suprim to the fals gods , christ ministers were supirior to those who served the fals God , the falls teaching in collose encorage people to placate demon, and perhaps they also offerd reconcilation with God, but in realitty no reconcilation over took place for them because their so called gospel had no power to save, , by contrast , Colossiance believers had alrady experienced the true reconsilation that comes through the true gospel preached by God\'s minijsters, paul began to focusing on the reconcialtion that the colocians had alrady experince through the gospel, we rad in Colo:1:22-23,  he had reconciled you by Christ\'s physical body through death to present you holy in his sight , without blemish and free from accusation ...this nis the gospel that you heard ,', 7, '2021-02-16 16:31:24', '2021-03-10 15:56:21', NULL),
(2442, 645, 910, 'A number of moden scholars have suggested that Paul did not actually write this letter , they bhave argude Ephesiance was written by one of Paul\'s student in order to continues paul\'s legacy and apply his teaching in new ways , it is simply to belive that an admirer of student of Paul would have contredicted Paul\'s own teaching by forgining his name in this matter, beyond this Epesians resembls Paul\'s other letters in both doctrin and language , the connections are especlly strong with colossians wich should not surprise us since paul probably wrote them both about the same time ,\r\nFinnally according to Acts 19-21 Paul had planted the church in ephesus and he had lived in Ephasus for two years ,and even after that time he had continued tom maintain close relationship with its elders ,its simply unimagnable that the Epheciance would not have the recognaze this letter as forgery .', 7, '2021-02-17 15:40:52', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL),
(2443, 645, 911, 'Normally ,Paul tailored his letters to adress the specific problems of a relatively localized grope of people whom he know directly or personally , but in Epecians he did somthing different , he responded to the problems of several churches in several locations , many of wich he had never met, now pauls purpose in writing this letter was to deal with the problems of all these churches , but his strategy was not to address each issues individualoly , \r\n first he addresed the theame of the kingdom of God  in his letter , second the several challenges in the church in terms of God\'s kingdom ,', 6, '2021-02-17 15:47:58', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL),
(2444, 645, 912, 'Pauls job as an apostle was to provide autheritive teaching and leadership to the cbhurch and he did this partly by writing letters , but paul did not just want to spread the sound doctrine or record it for posterity , he wanted to minister to the curch ,in his own day by applying sound doctrine , this means that as as we study Paul\'s letter to the Ephesians ,it helps to begin by asking questions like :to whom was this letter writen, as we took the background of Paul\'s epistles to Ephesians we can foucus of his auther ship, identifing orginal audiance , and his purpose to wrot this letter .', 5, '2021-02-17 15:55:49', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL),
(2445, 645, 913, 'Paul taught to Epshsian church about praise and prayer , A section of praise to god next in Eph.1:3-14, this is paul\'s only canonical letter in which the salutaion is followed by such a nsection of praise to God , typically Paul followed his salutation with personal reference or greeting, figur out Paul\'s motives for including this praise may be hard nbut recognizing its contents is easy , we might foucus on such tnhings as its strong trinitarian theology , throughout these verses , explicity honering the work of the Father , the son, and the holy spirit , or its emhasis on salvation through the atonment of Jesus Christ, or the revelation of the mystery of the gospel , the promise of our future Glory , Paul praised God for the benevolence toward his people, he praised God for the our inheritance in christ .\r\nPrayer: Prayer for paul\'s readers found in Eph 1:15-23,Pauls prayer consist essentially of three parts , his thanksful for the believers to whom he wrote , a two fold petition that the holy spirit would enlighten them , and extended explaination of that enlightmrnt .', 8, '2021-02-17 16:08:52', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2446, 645, 914, 'Paul\'s teaching about Citizenship in God\'s kingdom of light can be divided into three section : first focus on the fallen human beings are born in to the kingdom of darkness and are enemies of God by nature , second details the way God grants us Citisonship in his kingdom by transfering us from the kingdom of darkness ,  to kingdom of light , thirdly nature of our citizonship in the kingdom of light , \r\npaul reminded his readers that human race is sinful and fallen, we are spiritually dead , we have evil nature , we serve God\'s enemy , and as a result we are liable to fall under God\'s wrath on the day of judgment ,\r\n secondly: administration it should be obvious that every kingdom need some sort of administrative structure , kingdom cant fuction well if they have only a king and citizenry , other govenment officers musat exist through the king administrative ,his kingdom,\r\n   thirdly code for living :this code for in the kingdom contains many different instruction on Christian behavior , but it can be summarized in the following ways , we raed the ecclesiastical order in the kingdom in Eph. 4:1-16, purifying the kingdom, in 4:17-5:20,warfare of the kingdom in 6:10-20, the section of ecclesiastic order in the kingdom, foucus primarly on possition of leadership , influence , and authority in the cbhurch.', 8, '2021-02-17 16:24:19', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL),
(2447, 645, 915, 'To help to understand how to build the kingdom of God on the earth , Paul employed a number of metaphors , Each one offered insight in to how the citizens of God\'s kingdom are to relate to one another and to Christ, as well as into how are we to corporate in growing God\'s kingdom \'Pauyl taught that the Gentail christiance were fully Citizens in kingdom ,with equal  status of the jewish Christian Citizen , and to emhasize this fact, described the kigdom of god as build as abuilding with each Christian being a stone in the stucture without distinction among us , \r\nNow the goal of the building was to become God\'s dwelling so that God could live in the midst of his people , the nation of israel , Realized a blessings like this in the old testament ,', 6, '2021-02-17 16:32:27', '2021-03-10 16:02:05', NULL),
(2448, 645, 916, NULL, 0, '2021-02-17 16:33:17', '2021-02-17 16:33:17', NULL),
(2449, 646, 917, 'Many different individuals are mentioned in Paul\'s letter to Philemon, butbwe will disccus about those who directly or indirectly involved in the content of paul\'s letter , \r\nPhilemon , : Philemon\'s home town is not mentioned in Paul\'s letter to him , but colossians: 4:9 indicate that Philemon\'s slave Onesimus was a resident of Colosse , since Onesimos lived with his master Philemon at the time Colossians was written , Philemon must also have lived in Colosse .Philemon him self appere tom have been actively involved in ministering tom other believers in Colosse , Paul spoke of the way Philomon had lovelingly refreshed the hearts of other Believers ,Paul spoke of Philemoin as his partner in gospel ministry .\r\nOnesimus :the second person we should intriduse is Onesimus ,according to Philemon 16, Onesimus was Philemon\'s slave , althogh it is unclear what kind of slave he was and in what specific capacity he served Philemon, Onesimus unlike his master ,Onesimus was not a believer , at least not intially , but after Onesimus left Philemon\'s nhouse hold  to seek Paul\'s help the apostle led him to faith in christ and grew to love him grately Paul expressed bis love for onesimus in Philemon 10-16.\r\nWitness :Paul mentioned Appia and Achrippus  in the letter address , The mention of Apphia as Paul\'s sister might simply indicate that she was a believer ,', 8, '2021-02-18 15:47:38', '2021-03-10 16:10:20', NULL),
(2450, 646, 918, 'Pauls letter are in a position to address the problem it self , what exactly went wrong that requierd Paul\'s  intervention , Its no secret that some workers are not good workers and some serevnts are not good servants and that some people refuse to accept their responsibilities and fulfill their obligation , and unfortunately it appears that Onesimus was one of these people ,and his failures, according to verese 18, Onesimus may actually have caused a significant loss for Philemon, many intrepreters understand this verse to imply that Onesimus had stolen from Philemon, which was a comen crime from house hold slaves , But Onesimus might also have incurred a loss for Philemon in other ways , In any case Phjilemon had right to be angry , and Onesimus probably had good reason to be afrad of Philemon, under roman law, \r\nThe intial Problem in Philemon\'s house hold that was Onesimus had caused some loose to Philemon ,whether intentionally or unintentionally , and these problem was compounded by the resulting tension between Onesimus and Philemon,', 8, '2021-02-18 16:01:07', '2021-03-10 16:10:20', NULL),
(2451, 646, 919, 'As we consider The mediation between Philemon and Onesimus , we look at two matters :First Onesimus petition that Paul become has an advocate , SEcond Paul\'s agreement to advocate for Onesimus , Onesimus petition:During this time Paul was in prison as we have said in prior lessons , it is most likely that he was imprisoned in Rome , althogh it is also possible that he was in caeserea, Maritima, According to some scholars this distance was too great for Onesimus to have sought out \r\nPaul asa advocate or mediator , as result onesimus was seeking to start a new life far from philemon and encounderd Paul only accidently , Scripture dose not tell us what onesimus was thinking when he fled from Philemon, nor dose it tells us how he came to meet Paul in prison, For one thing Onesimus went to the city where Paul was imprisoned , and he should have known full well that Paul resided three because the church at colosse had sponsed Epaphras mission to care for Paul in prison,', 7, '2021-02-18 16:12:37', '2021-03-10 16:10:20', NULL),
(2452, 646, 920, 'Paul presented his petition to Philemon in verses 8-21, the petition represents the main purpose of the letter ,namely advocate on behalf of Onesimus befor philemon , we will explore in some depth ,breaking down in six elements , 1) An explanation of Paul\'s role as an advocate ,2)Onesimus role as petitioner ,3)Philemon;s role as master ,4)God\'s arole as providential ruler of the universe ,5)Pule\'s petiton ot self ,6)and Paul\'s statement of confidents thatvpetition would be granted ,\r\nPaul as Advocate : Because Paul was an apostle of Christ , he had the authority to order Philemon to do the right thing , but instead he wrote to Philemon in ways that elicited Philemon\'s sympathy and concern ,\r\nOnesimus as petitioner :The onesimus that Paul described here was very different man from the one who had come to him asking for a mediator , onesimus have been a worthless slave ,but he had been comverted to christ ,he had repented of his sin, showing his good faith by putting forth earnest effort to care care for Paul in prison\r\nPhilemon as master :here Paul acknowledged Philemon\'s authority over Onesimus and reavealed his own motivation for making an appeal to Philemon insted of commanding him, Paul wanted Philemon himself to chose to do the right things , \r\nGod as ruler :Paul remained Philemon of God\'s role as providential ruler over univers ,\r\nPetition:Paul asking to Philemon for the forgivness to Onesimus ,', 8, '2021-02-18 16:30:07', '2021-03-10 16:10:20', NULL),
(2453, 646, 921, 'Paul appealed to Philemon favor , as Paul himself were the one who needed Philemon\'s grace , he ndid not argue that onesimus deserve to be restored , and he did not ask Philemon to show christ like mercy to Onesimus , Figuratvly speaking Paul did not stand beside Onesimus as his defence attorney persuading Philemon to be merciful for Onesimus , sake, insted , he stood infrond of onesimus as his father and protector ,shielding him from philemon, and providing reason that philemon should be merciful for Paul sake , according to this view i found that its better to chose appeal for mercy than make a demand ,', 7, '2021-02-18 16:39:26', '2021-03-10 16:10:20', NULL),
(2454, 647, 922, 'Philippi was an important city in the Roman province of Macedonia ,Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey somewhere the year AD 49 or 50,befor he reached Philippi he had been ministering inAsia, Many of Paul activities in Philippi are recorded in Acts 16:12-40, Pauls ministry bin Philippi was so succesfull that even when he left the city ,the Philippian Christian supported Paul sending him monetray gifts at various times when he was in finacial need ,The church in Philippi loved Paul, and theybregularly helped him with financial gift ,according to Philippi 4:1018,the philippians also sent Paul a gift clos the time thatvhe wrote his letter ton them, and just as philippians loved him ,he felt strong affection for them as well , he Loved them for their commitment to the lord, and for the way they had been his partner in gospel ministry , they were his close friends , there are caring , supportive relationship between Paul and the Philippians .', 8, '2021-02-19 15:42:52', '2021-03-10 16:11:38', NULL),
(2455, 647, 923, 'It is always important to know somthing about Paul\'s circumstances , and those of the people to whom he wrote ,The church in the Philippi faced at least three types of problem ,First they appear to have face the persecution from those out side the church , second they were threatend byt the possibility of falls teaching similer to that wich had infiltrated other churches, and third they struggled with conflicts between one another in the church, \r\nPaul mentioned the persicution they were under going in Philippians 1:27-30, some years earlier ,just after he had planed the church in Philippi Paul had encounterd gtreat resistence from jews in the neighboring Macedonian city  of thesalonica, these angry jews accuseed Paul and otheer believers of viloting Roman law , as a result Paul was forced to flee the city by night to avoid Futur persicution by Jews ,as well as atreest by civil goverment .whatever the pecifica nature of tghe persicution in Philippi it is at least clear that the church was truly suffering at the hand of unbilievers ,\r\nthe second problem faced by the Philippians was the treat of the false teaching, to adress all thse problems he wrote the letter to Philippi .', 8, '2021-02-19 15:57:49', '2021-03-10 16:11:38', NULL),
(2456, 647, 924, 'Paul included a long section of Exhortation for the philippians also to preserve in his letter to Philippians , Phil:1:27-4:9, here Paul instrected them to remain faithful to Christ and to live exemplary lives even in the midst of distressing circumstances , our treatment of Pauls exhortation will descus on four manin topic ,the important of perservance , the help for perseverance that ministers provide , Pau\'s own example of perservance ,\r\nImportance of perservance :in Philippians 1:27-29, Paul aknowledge that Philippians struggles with hardship and encoraged them with these words , the philippians suffering were distressing and pain ful,but thyey were not beyond God\'s control , On the contrary , God himself had planed their suffering as means of blessing them, \r\nas we have seen in other lession ,jesus work for suffereing other will not be finished until the returns ,inthe mean time he complited his appointed suffering through the church, God had not just permited the philippians to suffer ,he had granted them to suffer , .', 8, '2021-02-19 16:13:24', '2021-03-10 16:11:38', NULL),
(2457, 647, 925, NULL, 0, '2021-02-19 16:14:41', '2021-02-19 16:14:41', NULL),
(2458, 647, 926, 'Philippians church loved Paul and he also loved them, he felt strong affection for them as well he loved them for thier commitment to the lord, and for the way they had been his partners in gospel ministry , they were his close friends ,people whose fellowship he enjoyed and whose presence he missed , Paul and his friends have caring and supportive relationship between them, many were caring Paul, Throghout his long ministry Paul often sufferd greatly , he was repeatedly whipped, beaten with rods, and hunted by assassins, he was imprisoned many times,and once he was even stoned felt for dead, at the tiem he was depressed and even despairing , but his friendship and love of philippians encouraged him,\r\n  i also hve many experiens in my ministry when i was needed many friends was with us,it helps us to do ore for christ,in our truble, in our proiblems their help encoraged us,', 7, '2021-02-19 16:26:10', '2021-03-10 16:11:38', NULL),
(2459, 649, 630, 'the book of Acts represents the holy spirit as the one that empowers the church to live transformed lives and to evangelize the world.\r\ntwo aspects of Jesus teaching about the Holy Spirit before the Pentecost\r\n1. timing \r\nWhen we read Acts 1:4-8, Jesus announce the coming baptism of the Spirit, then the apostles asked if \r\nJesus was about to restore the kingdom of Israel based of Old testament prophesies , the Israelites beloved that Messiah would come and restore everything , save them from sins, enemies and begin His reign as the king and kingdom.\r\nIsaiah 44:3-4 , he spoke about restoration, saying that God pour out His Spirit on the Land. Jesus explained that God\'s kingdom would unfold over time by Spreading throughout the nations. the holy spirit was about to be poured on the church. Jesus had only inaugurated and td the blessings of the Spirit would equip his Church, as it continue to live in a sinful way, till He returns.\r\nPurpose:\r\nthe spirit was to be poured out to empower the apostles to Christ\'s Godly and faithful witness.\r\ntwo dimensions of the spirit\'s ministry power and godly witnesses. the holy spirit\'s power was also manifested in dramatic ways in people\'s lives.\r\nSamson in judges 14-15\r\nJesus mentioned that the spirit would cause the disciple to be godly witness.', 8, '2021-02-22 17:48:45', '2021-03-10 16:12:09', NULL),
(2460, 649, 802, 'In the book of the Acts, Luke mentioned many times about the dramatic work of the Spirit after Pentecost:\r\n1. Samaria: Acts 8:14-17\r\n the unusual outpouring of the spirit is tat it mark the first time Samaritan converts come to Christianity in large numbers. Jesus commissioned the apostles to expand the kingdom of god from Jerusalem to Judea to Samaria to the ends of the earth. Jerusalem in Judea was the starting point of Pentecost. So, when the gospel reached Samaria, it represented a new stage, major step i the expansion of the gospel. the holy spirit empowered the believers to speak in tongues.\r\n2.Caesarea:\r\nthe situation here marked the first time the gospel moved another ethnic boundary. in this case, gentiles where converted to Christ in significant numbers for the first time, the Romans centurion Cornelius and his household. Acts 10:44-47\r\n3.Ephesus:\r\nActs 19:1-6 Luke described a dramatic outpouring of the Holy spirit in Ephesus a major city in Asia minor, far from Judea and Samaria. the recipients of the Spirit were disciples of John the Baptist. this story of the Spirit\'s outpouring on them in Ephesus indicated , now Jesus work of bringing the Spirit had reached a new level.', 8, '2021-02-22 18:01:23', '2021-03-10 16:12:09', NULL),
(2461, 649, 803, 'the authority of the apostles:\r\n1. Function:\r\nthe word apostles refers apostolos means sent , it refers to messengers, to agents send to accomplish missions. the apostles were appointed to the office of the church, they were authorized to speak to all matters related to the establishment and governs of the Christ church, their words were to be received because they served as faithful stewards of Jesus will and teachings\r\nBlessing:\r\nGod blesses the apostles by giving them converts almost every time they preached the gospel. Peter sermon on the day of Pentecost expanded the church from 120 to 3000 people. this kind of blessing continued throughout the book of Acts. this external numerical growth of the church was evidence of God\'s approval and power  \r\nMiracles: one of the main functions of miracles is to prove that God\'s messengers speak the truth and bear God\'s delegated authority. the number of miracles in Acts done by apostles proved that their testimony concerning Christ was true\r\nthey healed the sick (acts 5:16). they restored the lame Acts 14:8, they restored the dead Acts 9:40, they afflicted the wicked, escaped from prison, survived ship wreck.\r\nrevelation: the holy spirit gave guidance to the apostles leading them to flesh out the truth of the gospel to make decisions of the church a s a whole , shaping structural elements that allowed the church the grow into maturity.', 8, '2021-02-22 18:16:00', '2021-03-10 16:12:09', NULL),
(2462, 649, 804, 'All truly spiritual Christian will experience the dramatic manifestation of the spirit that occurred at Pentecost. since those early days of the Christian church, the presence of the holy Spirit has been applied to the church empowering us for service. we should always expect the holy spirit to be present in the lives of believers. \r\neven today the power of the holy spirit is the only means by which then church can be transformed and effectively spread the gospel. we too must seek to depend on the Spirit\'s power if we are to be godly and effective witness to the gospel message..\r\nthe Holy spirit helps us to stand strong in weakness , over come temptations, helps us to intercede. holy spirit helps me to overcome fear. the gifts of the holy spirit helps us to serve each other in church, edify each and encourage each other. Holy Spirit helps me to witness cross of Christ and the message fearlessly and faithfully.\r\nby the Power of the holy Spirit, we can conquer against all the wiles of the evil', 8, '2021-02-22 18:33:27', '2021-03-10 16:12:09', NULL),
(2463, 649, 805, 'My church is carrying out the mission of the expanding the kingdom of God by depending on the power of the holy spirit and the teachings of the apostles . Our lives transformed by the Holy spirit are  \"living letters\". we live a life based on Christ and pass on His message to the near and dear ones.\r\nour church can always improve our efforts to witness more in power and godly witnesses by spreading the message of the Kingdom of God by living it out and by sharing to others.\r\nI can personally help my church by being a true follower of the Christ and His teachings, depending on the power of the Holy Spirit to empower me to perform  miracles, to be a faithful witness of His love wherever we are , whether in our church, outside the church, in our pagan neighborhood. \r\nwe need to recognize the perishing souls, pray for them witness to them, teach them , serve them as Christ would do. we need to go among the masses and be true witness.\r\nI do it as I am into Full time ministry and Pastoral ministry sharing the Gospel through all possible mediums and ways, trusting The Holy Spirit to empower me .', 8, '2021-02-22 18:44:14', '2021-03-10 16:12:09', NULL),
(2464, 650, 897, 'around the year Ad 54-57, Paul and his travelling companions were making their way from Asia Minor to Jerusalem to deliver fund to the poor Christians in Jerusalem who were in famine.  on their way, they stopped in Miletus, where Paul me with the elders from the nearby church of Ephesus. during this meeting Paul revealed that Holy spirit had warned him that he would be imprisoned when he arrived in Jerusalem . Acts 20:22-24. believers prophesied Paul\r\ns coming imprisonment, but the holy spirit compelled Paul towards this .\r\nso Paul knew that this prophesies were not intended to dissuade him from his course but rather to prepare him for his coming hardship. \r\nfrom Miletus, Paul and his company sailed to Cos, then to Rhodes, then to Patara. in Patara they found a ship that took them to Cyprus and then  to Tyre. In Tyre, the Holy spirit moved many more believers to warn Paul of his coming hardship in Jerusalem. during his stay in Caesarea, Paul was warned yet again not to go to Jerusalem. the prophet Agabus bound his own hands warming that in this manner Paul would be arrested and warned.\r\nPaul knew that God was planning to use his arrest and imprisonment to further the gospel. Acts 21:30', 8, '2021-03-09 17:45:49', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2465, 650, 898, 'when Paul arrived in Jerusalem, he was received by the church and elders. Paul delivered to them the famine relief fund. he hoped that when the Jewish Christian received the gift from the gentiles, their thankfulness would make them more eager to receive the gentiles as full brothers in Christ. instead of rejoicing in the generosity of the gentile Christians and affirming Paul\'s ministry, James and the elders informed Paul that certain rumour had reached them regarding his teachings and doctrines. it was rumoured that Paul taught Jewish Christians living among gentiles to disregard traditional Jewish practices such as circumcision.\r\nJames and the elders came up with a solution that , they though would satisfy the Jewish Christian in Jerusalem. they suggested that Paul demonstrate his commitment to the Mosaic law by participating in the ritual for the temple in Jerusalem. they urged him to undergo purification rites with four men who had taken Nazirite vows, to show Paul\'s, obedience to the law and submission to Jewish traditions. Near the end pf Paul\'s week of purification, he was spending time in the inner court of the temple. while Paul was in the inner court to Israel, he was recognized by some Jews of Asia minor who were unbelieving Jews rather than Christian converts from Judaism. these same Jews had seen Paul with Trophimus, who had accompanied Paul to Jerusalem, so when thy saw Paul in the court of Israel, they wrongly assumed that, Trophimus had also entered that court and they were outraged. in response to this the Jews roused the city against Paul and angry dragged him from the court of Israel intenting to kill him. but when the commander of the Roman garrison in Jerusalem heard that the city was rioting, he rushed to quell the disturbance and chained Paul and took him to custody.', 9, '2021-03-09 18:05:34', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2466, 650, 899, 'Paul was a prisoner of Rome. he had to transported under Roman guard, so he was placed under the authority of a roman centurion named Julius and put on a ship heading for Asia Minor. the ship sailed from Caesarea, and then in Myra they boarded a ship which headed for Italy. before long , however they were caught in a violent storm  which lasted for two weeks. during this time Paul ministered to those on board and encouraged them that god had revealed to him that they would all survive.\r\nEventually, the ship struck a reef near the isle of Malta. \r\nDuring his stay on Malta, three months , he performed many miracles of healings in Malta. . then in the early part of AD 60. winter passed , Paul and his companions and guards once again set sail for Italy. from Syracuse they sailed to Rhegium on the southern tip of main land of Italy. Paul was finally moved on to Rome. he arrived in Rome, later in AD 60 and was placed under house arrest for two years from AD 60-62, he was under guard but he was also permitted to guest and teach freely.', 8, '2021-03-09 18:17:32', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2467, 650, 900, 'Paul\'s ministry in prison included suffering in behalf of others. suffering is a hardship, when the goal and product of suffering is the advancement of God\'s kingdom though the promotion of the gospel. suffering is rightly thought of as a form of Christian ministry.\r\nPaul \'s suffering in prison proved opportunity for him to preach the gospel and it also encouraged others to proclaim the gospel as well. Philippians 1:14. Paul was willing to got prison and even to die, if doing so would promote the gospel. Paul\'s pint here was that his imprisonment allowed him to promote the gospel in new places and to new people. thereby more and more people to faith in Christ.', 7, '2021-03-09 18:25:45', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2468, 650, 901, 'book of Acts teaches us three things about Paul\'s ministry:\r\n1. Awareness of suffering. in Acts 19-28, Luke described as well informed about his coming hardships a, as he knew that he would be imprisoned and suspected that he would even be put to death. he was aware of all the difficulties and ready to be martyred.\r\n2. Awareness of purpose\r\nPaul believed that he would use this hardships to spread the Christian gospel. he knew that any sacrifice would be worthwhile.\r\nit would be god\'s way of promoting gospel.\r\n3. awareness of blessing:\r\nPaul was very ware of God\'s blessing on his ministry during the sufferings. in /acts 19-28, we see that Paul\'s gospel testimony spread with the dramatic blessings of God\'s spirit', 7, '2021-03-09 18:34:13', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2469, 650, 902, 'sovereignty:\r\nas per Paul, when Jesus ascended into heaven God the Father vested him with sovereignty over all creation , Christ is sovereign means that he has the strength and power to accomplish his will and has legal authority and right to do so.\r\nhis sovereignty extended over all other kings and rulers as well. he rules over all creation with absolute power\r\nhonor:\r\nChrist is honored because he is perfect, holy and righteous. He holds a position of highest authority and he executes this authority justly and righteously.\r\nDetermination:\r\nPaul knew Christ\'s plan to rule over all creation. he confidently asserted that Christ was determined to consummate his Kingdom', 7, '2021-03-09 18:45:08', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2470, 650, 903, 'according to Paul, when we believe in Jesus, we are united to him in spiritual mystical way. we are counted as if we are Jesus,  Jesus is blameless before God, and because we are united to him, we are also counted as blameless before God. in Colossians 3:1-4\r\nthrough our union with Christ, we are united to Christ death and resurrection. if so that we are also raised with him , we are also united to Christ in his ascension and kingship, so in gory we will rule with him. Ephesians 2:6,7\r\n As per Paul ,we are seated with Christ in the heavenly places, so united with him in present kingship over all creation.  we share his honor and blessing sin a spiritual way right now.  we also share with Jesus in his kingship to suffering. Colossians 1:24', 7, '2021-03-09 18:52:49', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2471, 650, 904, 'Paul\' s use of the idea that Jesus Christ is the king of the creation. as well as believers union with Christ in his kingship. Jesus is the conqueror and ruler over all creation.\r\nChrist as king:\r\nChrist is the perfectly righteous and just king. because Christ is both sovereign and just, we are obligated to obey him.\r\nUnited to Christ:\r\nChrist indwells us by His Spirit, giving us a new nature , compelling us to do good works and conforming us to Christ, transforming us and motivating us to obey Christ.\r\nFor at least three reasons our new nature, God\'s commands , our union with one another, our union with Christ obligate us and enables us to live ethically according to the standard that God has set down for us in Scripture.', 7, '2021-03-09 19:04:59', '2021-03-10 16:13:22', NULL),
(2472, 652, 665, 'The Term \' Diachronic\' derives from two Greek words; \'dia\' means \' through\' or \' through out\' and \'Chronos\' which means \' time\'. Diachrony is a concern with the passage of time. In biblical theology, the term \'diachronic\' points to the ways Old Testament theology transformed, changed or developed through time.\r\nDiachronic approaches to the Bible concentrate on how theological structures progressively unfolded through time. This is in contrast with the synchronic synthesis. Though these approaches differs, they are also interdependent. So biblical theologians move back and forth between synchronic and diachronic work in variety of ways. Both these approaches are necessary to understand the theology of the Old Testament properly.', 8, '2021-03-10 09:38:25', '2021-03-10 16:02:37', NULL),
(2473, 652, 666, 'We use the term development to suggest two main ideas. First, changes in Old Testament theological structure always move toward fulfilling God\'s purposes of history. Secondly, we speak of development because theological changes only occur through God\'s providential involvement in history.\r\nDivine purposes; The scripture affirms that everything in history including shifts in theology, always accomplish God\'s unfailing purposes for his creation Isaiah 46:10. It fulfills the long term and short term goals of God in history. And ultimately it leads to God\'s glory and establishment of his Kingdom. \r\nDivine providence: Though the Old Testament theological development often appears like a road seen from distance, actually on a close look, the road of the Old Testament is filled with steep inclines, fast descents, and sharp turns to the left and right. These abrupt shifts are caused by God\'s providence, his complex involvement with his creatures.', 8, '2021-03-10 09:47:19', '2021-03-10 16:02:37', NULL),
(2474, 652, 667, 'The covenants with Adam and Noah known as universal because they were made between God and all humanity. The theological structure of the covenants tell us much about the relationship between God and all people. \r\nFollowing the universal covenants, God established national covenants with special people Israel: the covenants with Abraham, Moses and David. God narrowed down his covenantal attention to just one ethnic group establishing Israel to lead the rest of humanity in service to God.\r\nThe people of Israel were promised by the prophet about a New and final covenant God would make with his people Jeremiah 31:31. This covenant is also known as the covenant of fulfilment. The New covenant charectarises every particular theological structure from Christ\'s first coming to his glorious return. \r\n We see the six major divine covenants so permeated the theology of the times in which they were revealed that they provide us with ways of understanding the different emphases of major historic epoch in the Old Testament. Adam\'s covenant introduces the age of foundations; Noah\'s covenant began an age of natural stability; Abraham\'s covenant established promises for Israel; Moses\' covenant introduced God\'s law; David\'s covenant emphasized Kingship and the New covenant brought all of these earlier covenants to their ultimate fulfillment.', 8, '2021-03-10 10:02:28', '2021-03-10 16:02:37', NULL),
(2475, 652, 668, 'In a broad sense biblical theology applies the term\' Typology\' to any diachronic development of topics in OT theology. In a narrow sense, we can summarize the meaning of typology as follows: \' Biblical typology is the study of diachronic developments between theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in scripture\'.\r\nIn simple language typology is the study of types in three important passages NT writers spoke of particular theological structures in the OT as Types of the theological structures of the NT faith. \r\nI Romans 5:14, Paul declared Adam as \' a pattern of the one to come\'. Paul observed that Adam was a type of Christ. \r\nIn 1 Peter 3:20-21, Christian baptism is presented as a NT counterpart to Noah\'s flood. Colossians 2:17 speaks of OT ceremonial law as a shadow of the things to come, which is fulfilled in Christ.', 8, '2021-03-10 10:11:40', '2021-03-10 16:02:37', NULL),
(2476, 652, 669, 'The aspects of Old Testament worship, like sacrifice, temple, Passover are types of the things to come. When we apply these aspects of Old Testament worship and practices, they should be presented as the shadow of the things to come. They were types of things to come and to be fulfilled in Jesus Christ as the messiah. \r\nOld Testament sacrifices foreshadowed the great sacrifice or atonement to be made by Jesus in the fulness of time. The old Testament temple was a shadow of the heavenly Tabernacle through which Jesus had to enter through with his own sacrifice. It also depicted the New Testament believer who is the living temple of God. The Passover meal also was fore shadow of the true pass over lamb, Jesus Christ who is to be sacrificed for the redemption of mankind. \r\nIn every aspects of Old Testament worship we see the fore shadow of Jesus Christ, the mediator who reconcile God and man. In this perspective we need to apply them in the Church services today under the New covenant.', 8, '2021-03-10 10:22:33', '2021-03-10 16:02:37', NULL),
(2477, 654, 670, 'On the one hand the NT reports many revelatory acts of God, such as Christ\'s earthly ministry and the ministry of the Holy spirit in the first century Church. It also foretells the acts of God, like the glorious return of Christ. \r\nAlso the NT reports word revelations associated with the Acts of God. God the father spoke; Christ also spoke and at times angels and humans by the spirit of God, revealed God\'s word as well. \r\nThe NT records report both the actions and words of Jesus, the Apostles and other Christians in The NT because God reveals himself through the intersections between his actions and his words. The NT biblical theologians have drawn attention to the two fold manner in which God revealed himself. Old and New Testaments biblical theology, both focus on God\'s act and word revelation.', 7, '2021-03-11 09:38:36', '2021-05-10 22:57:50', NULL),
(2478, 654, 671, 'In the development of OT theology, each time God reveals himself acting or speaking in history, to one degree or another, his new revelations reconfigured existing theological structure. \r\nAs NT history moved forward, theological structures underwent diachronic changes. But because diachronic changes in NT history were not as dramatic as changes in the OT. NT biblical theologians have shifted their attention in a new direction. Instead of focusing on different historical periods, they have tended to treat the entire period of NT history as a whole. The theological unity created by the NT brief history, uniform circumstances and single covenant makes it difficult to do extensive diachronic study. So instead of dividing the history of God\'s NT act and word revelations in to small segments, they have focused on the ways, different NT authors provided different perspectives on the entire period.', 7, '2021-03-11 09:48:33', '2021-05-10 22:57:50', NULL),
(2479, 654, 672, 'The OT eschatology also developed in significant ways over time. These diachronic developments in the OT set the stage for what the NT biblical theologians have discovered about eschatology in the New Testament as well. \r\nOld Testament eschatology began in seminal form in the days of Adam and was refined further in the days of Noah. In Abraham\'s time, Israel\'s blessings to the world became the means by which God would bring history to its ultimate end.  Moses connected this hope with Israel\'s glorious return from  exile. The covenant with David set his dynasty and Jerusalem at the center of these glorious last days after exile. And also there was a brief period of hope, Israel\'s continuing rebellion caused the OT to end with the hope of the eschaton postponed to the distant future. Upon these background developed the New Testament eschatology.', 7, '2021-03-11 09:58:11', '2021-05-10 22:57:50', NULL),
(2480, 654, 673, 'Biblical theologians have demonstrated that in one way or another every NT teaching was shaped by Jesus\' three phase view of last days. \r\nFirst, the NT authors spoke of the entire period as the last days. Hebrews 1:1-2. Second, NT writers designated the extended period of Church history as the last days. 2 Timothy 3:1-5. Third, the NT authors described the consummation of the Kingdom at Christ\'s return as the \' last days\'. John 6:39.\r\nThe NT writers believed that all revelations from God from time to time of Jesus\' earthly ministry until his return in glory took place in the last days. Jewish religious leaders and general Jewish population raged against Christianity precisely because of the Christian outlook on the messianic last days. Christians believed that Messiah had already come, but in a way that unexpected. Such a messianic scenario was utterly contrary to what Jews believed in that day. This brought a break between first century Judaism and Christianity. And for these reasons NT authors were deeply pre occupied with Jesus\' three phase eschatology.', 8, '2021-03-11 10:08:54', '2021-05-10 22:57:50', NULL),
(2481, 654, 674, 'The concept of the Kingdom of God has a great impact upon my life and  ministry. The past, present and the future aspects of the Kingdom of God has lasting effect in life and ministry. The past aspects of the Kingdom of God of God make me to understand how God has been establishing and reigning in his Kingdom through his servants. The ultimate purpose of God in ever dispensation was the expansion of his Kingdom all over the earth. \r\nThe present aspect of the Kingdom of God is that Christ inaugurated his Kingdom through his appearance, death and resurrection from the dead. The present aspect of the Kingdom of God gives me the understanding of the significance of living according to the Kingdom principles. \r\nThe futuristic aspect of the Kingdom enable me to prepare for the advent of Christ and to prepare others for it through preaching the good new of the Kingdom of God.\r\nThe applying of the concept of the Kingdom of God to all aspects of life results in focusing on the glory of God and the Kingdom which is to come than laboring for this world and personal glory.', 8, '2021-03-11 10:21:18', '2021-05-10 22:57:50', NULL),
(2482, 656, 806, 'The literature of the ancient near east is very important to our purpose first because other primeval account were widely available to Moses ,and second Moses actually interacted with other primeval accounts .\r\nAvailability :Archeological research has demonstrated that Moses was not the first person to write about the world\'s origins .to be sure God inspierd Moses ,so that his account has true .But Moses wrote in a day when many nations and gropes in the near east had already written many myths and epic about primeval history .\r\nmany of ancient documents were actually available to Moses in his youth. Moses was educated in the royal courts of Egypt ,and his writing indicate that he new the literature of the ancient world .as Moses wrote his divinely inspierd and true account of the primeval period , he was quiet aware of other literary tradition in the ancient near east ,\r\nInteraction :Moses intracted with other primeval tradition both negatively and positively .on the one hand Moses wrote his history of the early times to counter falsehood with truth ,we must always remebeer that the Israelites Moses led had been subjected to all kinds of pagan influence .they were tempted to belive that the world resulted from the effect and the strugless of many gods.', 8, '2021-03-15 01:46:20', '2021-05-10 22:57:27', NULL),
(2483, 656, 807, 'The basic over view of Gen .1:-2:3, in his record of the ideal cration , most evangelical think about the opening chapter of the bibel they think about the all the contirversies that surrounded its interpretetion , moses cration account begins with 1:1-2:3 we amy summarize the content of these verses as the Dark chaotic world ,chaptere 1:3-31 from a middil section of this metiriel wich contains the socalled \"six day of creation\" these verses intruduse dramatic tension that flows through this entier chapter ,on one side of the tension the world is formless and empty , the second element in the dramatic tension also appears in 1:2,Moses wrote that the spirit of God was hovering over the water ,and in second chapter begins with summery statemetnt that God had finished his creative work ,and its concludes in 2:2-3,', 6, '2021-03-15 02:01:32', '2021-05-10 22:57:27', NULL),
(2484, 656, 808, 'Looking at the first portion of Gen 1,we see a very important dramatic tension between chaos covering the earth and the spirit of God .\" now the earth was formless and empty ,darkness was over the surface of the deep , and the spirit of God was hovering over the waters (Gen.1:2) this verse intruduse the dramatic tension that flows through this entier chapter .on one side of the tension , the world is formless and empty ,in the hebrew many scholers beileve that it meant that the world was inihabitable ,hostel toward human life , much like desert of wilderness is inhospitable to buman life .\r\ntyhe second element in the dramatic tension also appears in 1:2 ,Moses wrote that the \'Spirit of God was hovering over the water \'the hebrew terms used here is \"Merachefet\" wich means to \"fly above \" or to \"encircle above\" so we see very dramatic picture right at the beginning of this passage . on one side we see chaos on the earth .', 6, '2021-03-15 02:14:53', '2021-05-10 22:57:27', NULL),
(2485, 656, 809, 'This section is structured very simply .its begin in 2:1 with summery statement that God had finished his creative work ,and it concludes in 2:2-3 ,with God at rest , we reads thses words in Gens 2:2-3, by the seventh day god had finished the work he had been doing ;so on the seventh day he rested from all his work .and God blessed the seventh day and made it holy .When Moses discribed God as entering a state of Sabbath rest , granting sa special blessing to that day and making it holy ,he declared that the tension between the chaos and God\'s hovering spirit had been resolved , God had subdued the darkness , ruled over the chotic deep , delighted in his ideally orderd world, thge creation story comes to an end with this delightfully peaceful vision of the universe in perfect harmony .', 6, '2021-03-15 02:24:20', '2021-05-10 22:57:27', NULL),
(2486, 656, 810, 'Several occasions the New testament speaks of the first coming of Christ as God\'s re-creation , his reshaping of the cosmos .consider first the opening word of the gospel of John.1:1-3, in the beginning was the word , and the word was with God , and the word was God, he was with God in the beginning \r\nthe John gospel starts off, \"in the beginning \" we all realize that these words cpme from the opening words of Gen1:1,from the outset John put his readers in the framework of the creation account in Genesis .then john went on the say that Christ was the person of the trinity who made all things ,he was the Word of God spoken at creation , by whom the world was first made ,at this point John continued to draw upon the themes of Gns.1, especially the theme of the light wich God brought to the dark chaotic world on the first day ,yet insted of simply speaking of Jesus as the light of Gen.John pointed of the incarnation christ as the light shining into darkness of the world caused by sin,', 7, '2021-03-15 02:37:14', '2021-05-10 22:57:27', NULL),
(2487, 655, 866, 'It was once believed that the Apostles\' Creed was written by the twelve Apostles in the first Century. But there is no evidence. In the writings of the early Christians there are many lists of essential doctrines that resembles the Apostles\' creed. The writings of the Church fathers like Origen and Irenius indicated the existence of written rules of faith; to preserve the faith. They included the affirmation of important doctrines, as well as moral teachings and traditions. \r\nSome ancient doctrinal lists are in the form of creed which were often recited in liturgical settings. In early centuries each congregation had its creed, summarising essential biblical truth. By the third or fourth century, some creeds rise to prominence and being used in multiple churches. \r\nThe Roman creed was one among the prominent. This creed is so similar to the Apostles\' creed. Many Scholars believe Apostles\' creed as a later version of Roman creed. \r\nRegardless of its origin, the churches especially wester churches began widely using it. In the early centuries its wordings varied from Church to church. But in the eighth century the wordings was generally standardized in to the form that we recognize and use today.', 8, '2021-03-15 09:43:53', '2021-05-10 22:57:07', NULL),
(2488, 655, 867, 'The early church relied on scripture as the basis for all its doctrine and practice. But they still depended on the traditional teachings of the church to summarize and protect the teachings of the scripture. \r\nBit it was necessary to formulate concise, clear statements on what the church believe, especially because of the false teachings. Also the scripture are very long and literacy was limited; so it was necessary for people to understand and comprehend the faith without reading the entire Bible. The canon of scripture was formally listed around 397 AD; so the authoritative faith of the Church was passed through creeds. The Apostles\' creed still summarizes the essential teachings of the Bible in a simple way. The problem of human infallibility also made important for the Church to record its rule of faith in written form.\r\nThe goal of preserving the Church\'s traditional teaching was to ensure that the churches did not stray from the original meaning of the scripture, in order that believers would rightly understand the teachings of the Apostles, that they would firmly grasp and live by the words of Christ.', 8, '2021-03-15 09:54:06', '2021-05-10 22:57:07', NULL),
(2489, 655, 868, 'Christian faith affirms that there is only one God, and that this God exists in three persons, namely the person of the father, the son and the Holy Spirit. The mention of these three name together and on equal terms implies that while the father, the son and the Holy Spirit are all distinct persons, they are only one God. Apostles\' creed does not mention the term \'Trinity\' or spell out its details, but people understood God as one and in three persons. \r\nThe Trinity is commonly stated this way: God has three persons, but only one essence. By the term \'person\' we mean a distinct, self aware personality. And by the term \' essence\' we are referring to God\'s fundamental nature of the substance of which he consists. \r\nRegarding father, the creed ascribes to him the attribute of being Almighty, the maker of heaven and earth. The creed describes more about the earthly life and ministry of Jesus Christ; The incarnation, life, suffering, death, burial, resurrection, ascension in to heaven and finally his role as a judge.\r\n Of the Holy Spirit , the creed says the he caused the virgin Mary to become pregnant with Jesus. The creed also associates the Holy spirit with the Church, and with our experience of salvation both now and in future.', 9, '2021-03-15 10:06:04', '2021-05-10 22:57:07', NULL),
(2490, 655, 869, 'Since first century there were people who refuse to participate in the visible church activities while believing in Christian doctrines. Hebrews 10:25. But the scripture teaches that the Church is important and necessary for Christians. Christians are supposed to form an actual, physical community, and this community is extremely important to every believer. It is not to be limited to spiritual fellowship, but to function as a family or neighborhood. \r\nThe creed affirms the fact that Christ appointed his church to protect and proclaim the gospel and other truths of the Bible. Jude 3 - 4 mentions that part of Church\'s job is to content for faith, to protect the truths and beliefs that have been entrusted to it against those who promote false teachings and practices. The Church cannot abandon this call, but keep trying to preserve doctrine in the Church as we believe\' I believe in the Church\'.', 9, '2021-03-15 10:14:46', '2021-05-10 22:57:07', NULL),
(2491, 655, 870, 'In Ephesians 2:19-21 Paul spoke of the church as a building founded on the Apostles and prophets. Without a solid, true foundation, the church could not be built in a way that honored God. \r\nIn a similar way, Christian theology must be founded on true doctrines and principles if it is to honor God and be useful to his people. Just as Jesus Christ is the corner stone of the Church, his teachings are the corner stone of Christian theology. And just as the Apostles and prophets became the Church\'s foundation, by introducing Christ to the world, the Apostles\' creed is foundational to theology because it introduces us to the teachings of the Apostles recorded in scripture. \r\nThe Apostles creed functions as a doctrinal standard because it presents several of the biggest, most important ideas of Christianity. Using the creed as a standard helps us remain faithful to scriptures amidst false teachings.\r\nAlso the Apostles\' creed provides a logical basis for all that we believe. First, it helps us to see the relationship between the various different beliefs in our theological systems. And second, it helps us to think about doctrines that are more remote from scripture in ways that harmonize these less central ideas with our fundamental beliefs.', 9, '2021-03-15 10:27:00', '2021-05-10 22:57:07', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2492, 657, 811, 'Genesis 2:2-3:24 divide into two major section , these sections are indicated primarily by changes in topic and charecters ,we should walk through these steps and summarize thier basic content .\r\nIn garden :the first dramatic step of our story appears in 2:4-17 , where we read that God put Adam in the garden of Eden .the passage tells us the all garden was Adam\'s splendid place to dwell and to work ,\r\nthe second step of narative consists of 2:18-25, wich we designate humanity\'s condition enhanced ,in this material Goid added even greater blessings to Adam\'s life .\r\ncondition cursed :the third step of the major section is 3:1-21, wich we will call humanity\'s condition cursed ,this material begins in 3:1 with the introduction of a new topic and character .the tempting serpent , temptaion and the result of his temptation .\r\nout of garden :the forth element in the overarching structure of this passage is 3:23-24, wich we have entitled \'out of the garden \'God drove adam from the garden and placed cherubim and flaming sword to gard the entrance to Eden garden .', 8, '2021-03-16 01:46:25', '2021-05-10 22:56:45', NULL),
(2493, 657, 812, 'with the four major divisions of this passage in mind we may now look more closely at Gen 2-3.to see the dramatic symmetry that this passage displays .by juxtaposing different elements in these sections Moses revealed the central concern of his narrative .to explore the symmetries of this narrative , we will look first at the balance which exist between the beginning and ending of our narartive ,\r\nbeginning and ending :the first contrast is in location ,the account begins in 2:7 with God placing Adam lived and worked in a place full of divine blessing ;wonderful vegetation ,life-giving water ,precious metals and stones surrounded him on every side , by contrast the narrative ends in 3:24 , with God expelling Adam and Eve from the garden .this geographical contrast make its clear that the most desirable place of human beings to be on earth was in eden garden .\r\nthe second is on the special trees of the garden , although 2:4-17 mentions two trees ,tree of Life and tree of the knowledge of Good and evil ,by the time we come to 2:17 attention moves just to the one tree , the tree of knowledge ,the tree held the power to give human beings the experiential knowledge of goodness and sin .', 8, '2021-03-16 02:02:11', '2021-05-10 22:56:45', NULL),
(2494, 657, 813, 'By focusing on the test of loyalty in the garden of Eden ,Moses stressed a parallel requirment of loyalty for the Israelites whom he held toward the Promised land , As Mose led the Israelites to the promised land ,the frequently warned them that God required them to be faithful to him ,Moses succinctly summarized his teaching on this matter in the eighth chapter of Deuteronomy .From this passage its clear tht God required Israel to be loyal to him in order that they might enter and posses the land of canaan .in fact all through the wilderness wandering of the nation ,God tested the Israelites to teach them how to be holy .Moses also made it clear that once the Nation of Israel comne to the holy land , they had to remain loyal to God or they would lose this privilage ., Moses knew that the Israelites were prone to rebel against God\'s commands ,Just like Adam and Eve , and because of these tendancies Mose focused on Adam and Eve\'s test in the garden to warn that God required loyalty of everione who wished to dwell in Canaan .', 7, '2021-03-16 02:17:13', '2021-05-10 22:56:45', NULL),
(2495, 657, 814, 'One way in wich the new testament speaks of the salvation Christ brings to the world is in his earthly ministry .the inaguration of the kingdom Christ retraced and reversed what Adam and eve had done in the garden of Eden .we will look at the aspects of the new testament teaching by looking first how this theme appears in the letters of Paul, and second in the gosple of Matthew .\r\nPaul :Paul summarized his view point succinctly in rom 5:14, DEath reigned from the time of Adam to the time of Moses even oveer those who did not sin by breaking a command , As did Adam ,who was a pattern of the one to come .Paul said Adam was a type of one to come ,the rest of Romans 5 makes it clear that \"the one to come \" was christ , Listen to the way Paul summarized the matter in Rom:5:18-19,how paul put it here ,adams one tresspass was condemnation for all men , for all people ,but christ one Act of righteousness resulted in justification for all men, \r\nMatthew : Matthew in particular drew attention to the way Christ reacted and reversed Adam\'s sin in his account of christ temptation in Mathew 4:1-11, in a number of different way the sory of Christ \'s temptation parallels both experience of Adam and eve in the garden of Eden .', 7, '2021-03-16 02:32:30', '2021-05-10 22:56:45', NULL),
(2496, 657, 815, 'Moses wrote thjis all to teach some genaral theological themes to Israelites whom he held, he told them about the origin and the nature , and the result of the sin in the world , and these were the very important , moses didnt write the primeval historyb simlply to inform Israel about such general histroical and theological issues ,insted ,Moises wrote his primeval history to give his people practical instrection about current relegious and social problems .\r\nthese same things we can see in the current world, the effect of Adams sin bring the currepted thoghts to all bhuman beings , in this world people rebel againist God a s adam did, the currepted mind forcing people to do evel .fight each other , disobidint to God , People are used to tempt to do sin , \r\nthe same way people lose the relation between each other , the fellowship and sinciarity we losed ,', 8, '2021-03-16 02:44:29', '2021-05-10 22:56:45', NULL),
(2497, 653, 905, 'General\r\nPaul had an indirect relationship with the Colossians through representatives such as his Colossian friend Epaphra, Philemon and Onesimus and his messenger Tychicus. Paul and Colossians had corresponded with each other and ministered to each other  and Paul prayed specifically for the Colossians , The Colossians ministered to Paul as well. Epaphras and Onesimus visited Paul in Prison\r\nIndividuals:\r\nPaul had a number of Friend in Colosse, many of who had labored alongside Paul in gospel ministry. three such friends were Philemon, Apphia and Archippus, we see it in Philemon 1:2\r\nPhilemon was a close friend of Paul, he was a part of Philemon\'s family, perhaps his son.  Paul referred to Epaphras as a fellow prisoner , and another friend named Onesimus who ministered to Paul in prison was also close to him.', 8, '2021-03-16 17:45:31', '2021-05-10 22:56:22', NULL),
(2498, 653, 906, 'Errors in Colosse church:\r\nGreek Philosophy:\r\nColossians 2:1-4\r\nPaul s words says that the Colossians valued mystery, wisdom and knowledge, all of which Greek philosophy and religion typically valued. False teachers in Colosse tried to mix the church teachings with Greek traditions and were supposed to bring wisdom and strengthen believers against temptation.\r\nJewish Laws:\r\nthe false teachers in Colosse stressed certain practices derived from the Old Testament Law. this included observance of the Jewish calendar, such as religious festival. new moon celebrations, and the sabbath day as well as dietary restrictions.\r\nthe false teachers had corrupted theses laws using them in idolatrous worship and pagan ascetism and they were advocating a form of Christian circumcision\r\nSpiritual beings:\r\nthis church was courting the worship pf spiritual powers in three ways: \r\nworship of angels, address of rulers and authorities and problems related to the basic principles of this world', 8, '2021-03-16 17:56:29', '2021-05-10 22:56:22', NULL),
(2499, 653, 907, 'it is important to understand the background of Colossians as we seek for Paul\'s reasons for writing this letter because the false teachers in Colosse appeared to have combined ?Jewish legalism, pagan religion  and Christianity and to have encourage the worship of astral or cosmic powers commonly known as basic principles or Stoicheia. the background says that the church in colosse face some real challenges in the first century, unlike other churches they apparently had never received apostolic training, although the church had been planted by godly men, it had not been solidly granted in the theology of the apostles. this made the Colossians Christians particularly vulnerable to false teaching. so when the false teacher began bombard them with corruption of Judaism and pagan idolatry, it was hard for them to tell the difference between truth and heresy. Wisely, they recognized their problem and appealed to Paul for help.', 8, '2021-03-16 18:07:36', '2021-05-10 22:56:22', NULL),
(2500, 653, 908, 'supremacy of Christ:\r\nPaul listed many important aspects of the supremacy of Christ in Colossians 1:15-20\r\nPaul began by saying that the Christ is the image of the invisible God. Colossians 1:15, 16. when then false teachers look to the planets and elements as the image of god, Paul pointed to Christ as the mage of god. \r\nSecondly Paul mentioned that Christ is the firstborn over all creation Colossians 1:15, 16\r\n the first born was the one who had the greatest right of inheritance  and Paul associated Christ status as \" firstborn\" who has with his authority an supremacy over all creation, and said nothing about a time when Jesus did not exist.\r\nPaul said that Christ was the agent of creation, the one through whom god created the universe, Paul insisted that  chose other powers were inferior to him and subject to him Colossians 1:16\r\nfourthly he says that Christ is the supreme Lord because God placed him as head over the church. Colossians !:18\r\nfifthly , Paul explained that Christ is god incarnate, Colossians 1:19, which means all the fulness of God dwell in Christ making Him the incarnation if the supreme God.\r\nFinally, Paul spoke of Christ as the only reconciler between God and Man, Colossians 1:19,20', 7, '2021-03-16 18:21:08', '2021-05-10 22:56:22', NULL),
(2501, 653, 909, 'in Colossians 2:21-25, Paul argued that because Christ was superior to the false gods, Christ ministers were superior to those who serve the false gods. his arguments consisted of five main ideas:\r\nfirstly, Christ\'s ministers are superior because they preach a gospel that actually reconciles believers to God.\r\nSecondly Paul pointed to his onw altruism, speaking of his suffering in behalf of the church. Colossians 1:24\r\nthird, Paul speaks of his divine commission, he had been appointed to his apostleship by the Lord himself. Colossians 1:25\r\n fourth, the revelation that he had received was superior to that which was attested by the false teachers, Colossians 2:4\r\nfifth Paul wrote of the superior empowerment of Christ\'s ministers speaking of the fact that God gave power to his ministers. Paul did not labor in his on strength.', 8, '2021-03-16 18:30:09', '2021-05-10 22:56:22', NULL),
(2502, 658, 816, 'These chapters in Genesis touch number of different topics , and include both narrative and genealogies , as we look at Gen 4:1-6:8,we will see this portion of genisis divided into two part ,the first section ,we have \'early vioence and hope \'the second section \'Later violence and hope\'\r\nEarly violence and Hope :the early senario of violence and hope of deliverance in Gen 4-5 divides into four parts , and these part from two parallel sets of narretives and genealogies ,the second narrative forms a genealogy wich forms a corresponds to second genealogy ,\r\nNarratives >in the first place Moses began to writing a story about sinful Cain in 4:1-16,this passage is wellknown record of the time when Cain broke into a fit of Jealous rage and murdered his brother Abel ,the second step of the story found in verse 2-7,the even that led Cain to murder abel , the third section of this narrative verse 8 forms the turning point of this story , the forth section of narrative appears in verse 9-15 and describes the curse and protection God gave ton Cain .\r\nso we can see the chpter 4 begin with the story of Cain terrible sin,he was so currepted by sin that he actully murdered his righteous brother .', 8, '2021-03-17 01:46:59', '2021-05-10 22:55:59', NULL),
(2503, 658, 817, 'when we look more closely to this verses ,it becomes apparent that 6:1-8 divided into three steps : first verses 1-3,concern charecters known as the \"sons of God\" second vs 4-7 focus on the character known as the \"Nephilim\" following these two steps , Moses added an after word in vs 8,mentioning Noa once.\r\nMoses related these chapters of his primeval history to his Contempory world by describing the violence of early human history in ways that closly resumbled the experience of violence wich israel had endured . he pointed out that the troubles which Israel faced were very similer to the rubles of the primeval world ,\r\nnow to explore how Moses established these similarities , we will look once again in two major section of Gensis .\r\nEarly violence and hope :narravtives .we should began by examing the story of sinful Cin in gen 4:1-16,inthe story Moses focusd on at least five concern that allowed the Israelites to connect this passage of thiere own story , in the first place Moses mention the occupation of Cain and Abel to creat the these association ,Listen to the way Cain and Abel were initially distinguished , Moses and Israelites were quit aware of this kind of tention and how it led to serious trails while they were in the Egipt .', 7, '2021-03-17 02:04:17', '2021-05-10 22:55:59', NULL),
(2504, 658, 818, 'Moses focused on at least five concerns that allowed the Israelites to connect this passage to their own day, first place , the occupation of Cain and able, as this passage Cain was settled agriculturalist , a farmer , while Able was a shepherd .in ancient and modern times , it has been commen for tensions to arise between settled ,agriculturlly based society and nomadic shepherd ,and as the book of gensis it self indicate .moses and Israelites wer quit aware of this kind of tension and how it led to serious tarils while they were in Egipt, in Gen 46:33-34,Joseph instructed his brothers in his way when thay came to Egypt .joseph instrution were give us insight in to the why Moses mentioned that Cain was a farmer and Abel a shepherd .he wanted his Israelites readers to associate Cain , the agriculturslist with Egyptians and he wanted Israel to associate themselves with Abel ,the victimized shepherd .', 7, '2021-03-17 02:18:19', '2021-05-10 22:55:59', NULL),
(2505, 658, 819, 'Moses contrasted the lives of Cain and abel in  may aspects , both are having tottaly different charecter, here moses was hoping to accomplish the contrasting varios charecter between Acin and abel , Cain had a sinful charecter , he was joules with is own brother , he was not resdy to please God with wich he had ,he had a mind to kil his brother ,Cain dishonored God with his normal sacrifies ,but Able was a man who is alway ready to pleas god with wjhat we have , \r\nit is not easy to say wich charecter is most like me , some time i feel am like Cain , because many time i failed to plaese God with what i am having , jelouse to others , murmuring againist God, by my charecter dishonher God , Here moses hoping that we understand the various character ,and be like Abel , who plaese God ,', 7, '2021-03-17 02:30:21', '2021-05-10 22:55:59', NULL),
(2506, 658, 820, 'To understand the corruption and the violence of man kind ,we whave to look at the primeval history, by Adam\'a and Eve\'s sin, man become corrupted, they rebeld agianst God , because of this human beings came under the curse ,they lost their relationship with god, God expel them from the Place where they were had to be, but still god put an option to reconnect with him, he put a thred of hope to human being to come back to god , God promised to Eve about a son who will save all huaman being , but in between this , by doing sacrifice human being could connect with Gos again, in the primeval history People were connect to God with sacrifice , tey were offering sacrifice to contact and please God , \r\nbut today we have the hope throgh the sacrifition of his son Jesus christ , now we are conected with God through christ ,by his sacrifical death we recconected with God.', 8, '2021-03-17 02:43:19', '2021-05-10 22:55:59', NULL),
(2507, 661, 871, 'The scripture clearly says that the polytheistic belief is part of human sin and alienation from true one God. Romans 1:20-23. Many passages asserts God\'s singularity. \r\nIt is clear from the scripture that all monotheistic religions like Judaism and Islam, do not worship same God. They disagree over his character, divine actions and even over his nature. Such monotheistic religions deny the Trinitarian faith of the Bible. \r\nInstead of using \'Yahweh\', the Apostles\' creed use \'God\' which indicates that the God of Christianity is the only one deserves to be called God as we read in 1 Kings 8:60. Though the other religions believe that they worship actual gods, the Bible says they worship imaginary beings or even demons- inferior, created spirits who ruled by the Christian\'s God. 1 Corinthians 10:20. \r\nThe Bible insists that only the Christian\'s God is true, divine and he will only judge the world; and only He has the power to judge us.', 8, '2021-03-17 09:39:28', '2021-05-10 22:55:35', NULL),
(2508, 661, 872, 'By \'simplicity\' it means the essence of God that we have in mind- his fundamental nature, the substance that makes up his being. Theologians use this in a technical way to mean that God\'s essence is not a composite of different substances but a unified whole consisting of only one substance. \r\nWhen theologians say that God has a simple, unified essence, they were denying that the father, son and the Holy Spirit were three separate gods that some how joined together in the Trinity.  Instead the affirm that these three persons had always existed together as only one God. \r\nSo Christians do not believe in three Gods, but only one God- one divine being- who exists in three persons. Traditional Christian worship has always been fully Trinitarian: we worship all persons of Trinity, and we offer songs of praise and prayer of petition to each one of them. To neglect any person of the Trinity in favor of another is to neglect God himself.', 8, '2021-03-17 09:49:10', '2021-05-10 22:55:35', NULL),
(2509, 661, 873, 'The word \'ontological\' means \' relating to being\'.  So we look at the Trinity in terms of being and essence in it; how the three persons of the Trinity are integrated with one another, and how they are single in essence. \r\nFrom the perspective of ontology, all three persons of God interact with each other, how they relate to one another as individual persons. In this view, each persons has different responsibilities, a different level of authority and a different assigned role with different task to perform. \r\nThe father, son and the Holy Spirit engage in conversation with each other, make agreements with each other, and interact in many other ways. Both ontological and economic perspectives, the father is said to be the first person of the Trinity. In ontological perspective the Son is said to be generated by the father, and the Holy Spirit said to proceed from the father. From the Economic perspective, the father is said to be the first person because he has the authority over the other two persons.', 8, '2021-03-17 09:58:17', '2021-05-10 22:55:35', NULL),
(2510, 661, 874, 'In the broader sense, scripture sometime refers to God as being the father of everything he has created. Deuteronomy 32:6, Isaiah 43:6-7. Our human relationship to God as creator is often expressed in terms of relationship between a father and a son. This shows our origin and his authority. \r\nIt was common in ancient near east to consider Kings as the father and people as their children. So God is considered as the Royal father or imperial father who takes care of people, provides and protects them . This concept is expressed in the Lord\'s prayer. \r\nMore often in the OT, God is portrayed as the family head of the nation of Israel. Deuteronomy 1:31. Hosea 11:1. The same concept is prevalent in the NT too. Matthew 1:12. Passages like 1 Timothy 3:15, the Church is referred to as God\'s house hold and family. \r\nIn al these perspectives God\'s fatherhood is referred and understood by the people through the scriptures. .', 8, '2021-03-17 10:08:03', '2021-05-10 22:55:35', NULL),
(2511, 661, 875, 'Absolute: God\'s authority  over the creation is absolute in the sense that he has complete freedom to do what ever he wants with his creation. Romans 9:20-21, Isaiah 29:16. \r\nExclusive: God\'s authority as creator is exclusive in the sense that no creature possesses absolute authority. father also has the authority over the other persons of the Trinity. John 5:26-27. \r\nExhaustive: By saying God\'s authority as exhaustive, we mean that it extends over everything he has created, in every detail. This means two things: primarily, every one is under God\'s authority- human, angels and demons. Secondly, everything is under his authority - all his creation function in his will. There is no segregation between sacred and secular in relation with divine authority.', 7, '2021-03-17 10:16:22', '2021-05-10 22:55:35', NULL),
(2512, 659, 821, 'Noah\'s flood displays a relatively clear literary pattern, although it is possible to discribe this pattern in numer of ways , a systematical five steps Drama .\r\nInitial covenant :the five steps this narrative appears in Genesis 6:9-22,and we will call it tyhe \"Initial covenant \"with Noah,in this portion of narrative ,Moses noted that Noah was man in a world that God gone sour ,God spoke to Noah revealed why he planed to destroy the human race .\r\nEnduring covenant :in this passage God return to Noah after the flood and made another covenant with him .God decided to give humanity the oppertunity of a new order in the world .\r\nEscape from water :Noah prepared the ark and brought animal of every kind in to it ,and flood water began to burst into the world , but Noah ,his family ,and the animal he had gathered were safely sealed in the ark .\r\nExit to dry land :Noahs flood forms a dramatic counterpoint o the second part , its describes Noah\'s exit to dry land in Gen 8:6-19,after the flood had began to subside ,Noah longed for dry land to appears so that he could leave the ark .after a period of waiting ,dry land appears and God commanded Noah to leave the ark. \r\nDriven remberence :these veres began with the discription of the flood raging and didtriying every living things on the earth .', 8, '2021-03-18 01:46:53', '2021-05-10 22:55:13', NULL),
(2513, 659, 822, 'Moses\' record of the sons of Noah in chapter 9-10 of Genesis consist of a title and two main section ,in line with title Moses ;record of Noah\'s sons divides into two section , in the first place ,the story in 9:20-29 setforth the distinctions among the sons .and the second place 10:1-32 describes the Noah;s son and their descendants .the out look on the distiction among Noah\'s son is confirmed by chapter 10.the distribution of Noah son,looking to the generation wich came along after the days of Noah , in Gen 10,moses giving a sample listing of thge places where the sescendant of Ham ,shem ,and Japheth ,went throughout the world , according to gen 10, Japhethites occupied territories to the north ,northeast and northwest of Canaan.with a few exception the Hamits moved to north Africa ,and special son of Ham ,the Shemites of semitic people largely occupied the territories of the arabian Penisula .\r\nDefeat of Babel :the story of Babel divides into five symmetric steps .majority of humanity togethr ,but God dispersed over the earth as he confused human language ,secondly thay intented to build a city great tower reaching to heavan so that they would be famous for all time and utterly invisable .God investigated the city and its tower , once God saw the city and proud plan of its inhabitance , he ditermined to bring an end to the city of babel', 8, '2021-03-18 02:03:27', '2021-05-10 22:55:13', NULL),
(2514, 659, 823, 'There are at least eight connection between Noah and Moses . In the first place , Moses drew a connection between himself and Noah in the motif of violence .Noah\'s flood came because of the world was filled with violence .As Exodus 1-2,maake clear the Egyptien had inflicted much violence on the people of israel,\r\nthe second  association appears in Mose\'s use of the term \"ark\" the hebrew word for Noah\'s ark throghout Genisis is \"tevah\" intrestingly enough the only other place where Moses used the term tevah was in exodus 2:3,5.thre he reffers basket in witch his mother placed him as an ark.\r\nin the third place , the importance of divine covenants also establishes Noah as a precursor of Moses .as we have seen according to Gen 6:18,Noah enterd into covanant with God on behalf of entire human race .Moses\'s primary service to IOsrael was to mediate a divine covenant as Exodus 19-24.Illustaret so well , \r\nthe central role of judgment through water also established a forth connection between the two men ,in Gen 6-9,God deliverd Noah and his famly .and much same way as Exodus 13-15 teel us ,Moses braoght Israel out of Egypt by passing trough the waters of red sea.\r\nGod sent wind todrive back the water in both days of Noah and the days of Moses .\r\na sixth connection appears in the emphasis put on animals .God command Noah to bring animal in to ark, Exodus mention the many animals that left Egypt with Israelites \r\nsevent connecton divain remeberance of bothbn Naoh and Moses .\r\nfinally , the blessing of anature also associatees Noah with Moases .', 8, '2021-03-18 02:20:43', '2021-05-10 22:55:13', NULL),
(2515, 659, 824, 'In the inauguration of the kingdom , Christ accomplished a great salvation on the behalf of his people in ways that corresponded to the themes Moses emphasized in GEn 6:9-11:9,\r\nCovenant :on the one hand Christ brought deliverance to his people by means of covanent that rescue them from the judgment of God , as we have seen Noah played a special role as the mediator of a covenant and Moses drew upon fact as he explained his own ministry to Israel, ina similer fashion , the new testamment teachs that Christ is our deliverer because he mediated a new covenant when when he came to this earth .\r\nvictory :in adition bring a new covenant ,Jesus \'earthly ministry fullfiled the theme of victory in holy war , Moses focused of the theme of holy war as apart of the new order after the flood .he established the new odrer of the world requierd Israel to move forward onto the conquest of Canaan .', 8, '2021-03-18 02:31:55', '2021-05-10 22:55:13', NULL),
(2516, 659, 825, 'In our usual fashion , we will aproach the question of modern application by following the new new testament\'s discriptions of the three stages of Christ\'s kongdom.first the flood and the deliverance and the resusl the new order applay to the Inauguration of the kingdom in the first coming of Christ .then will turn to the relevance of thse matters for the continuation of the kingdom throughout the history of the church , and finally we will examine how the new testament applies this portion of the primival history to the cunsumation of the kingdom when christ return in glory .\r\nInauguration :in the inauguration of the kingdom, Christ accomplished a great salvation on the behalf of his people in ways the corresponded to the theme Moses emphasized in Gen 6:9--11:9.\r\nContinuation :the new testamnt describe the time between the first and second coming of Christ in at least two ways that relate to the last chapter of Moses\' Primival history .these perspsctive relate directly to the Important of baptism and spiritual warfare in the Christian life .\r\nConsummation :In light of the way the new testament applies the last chapter of primeval history to the inaguration and the continuation of the kingdom , its not surprising to discover that the consummation of the kingdom is also described in terms of Noah;s flood and warfare of the new primeval order .', 8, '2021-03-18 02:47:18', '2021-05-10 22:55:13', NULL),
(2517, 665, 842, 'Christian ethics asks what the whole Bible teaches us about which acts, attitudes, and personal character traits receive God\'s approval and which ones do not. \r\n1. The ultimate basis for Christian ethics is the moral character of God : God delights in his own moral character, which is supremely good, unchanging, and eternal. His moral standards for human beings flow from  his moral character, and therefore they apply to all people in all cultures for all of history. God is love , so he commands us to love. He is holy, and he commands us to be holy. He is merciful, and he commands us to be merciful. He is truthful, and he commands us not bear false witness. \r\n2. Christian ethics is essential to the proclamation of the gospel: Evangelism in New Testament clearly include a call to repentance. Jesus clearly told his disciples\r\n3. Christian ethics teaches us how to live for the glory of God: The goal of ethics is to lead a life that glorifies God. Such a life will have a character, result, and behavior. All though we are justified by faith in Christ alone and not by works.', 8, '2021-03-22 00:17:18', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2518, 665, 843, 'God\'s word tells us that wisdom originates in him. He is the source and fountain of wisdom. He also gave us his Spirit who is at work in us, helping  us, helping us to desire wisdom, teaching us the way of wisdom through the word, and enabling us to walk in it. So when we have a decision to make, we need to turn to God\'s Word.  Ethical decisions generate and sustain trust; demonstrate respect, responsibility, fairness and caring; and are consistent with good citizenship. These behaviors provide a foundation for making better decisions by setting the ground rules for our behavior. The holy Spirit of God and Jesus has a place in our decision making. He is our guide, our source of light and revealer of the way of truth.', 7, '2021-03-22 00:24:15', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2519, 665, 844, 'It is important to set goals to measure the areas we hope to grow and improve. Goals give us purpose, and a motivation . Goals are mile stones in life.  As it\'s said in Proverbs 16:9 \"In their hearts humans plan their course, but the Lord establishes their steps\". Proverbs 19:21 \" Many are the plans in a person\'s heart, but it is the Lord\'s purpose that prevails.\" Proverbs 21:5 \"The plans of the diligent  lead to profit as surely as haste leads to poverty\". lead to profit Like any professional are in life, Biblical goals are very much important in our spiritual growth and  to take biblical decisions. First of all, we know that the Bible has a supernatural ability to change people. The Bible has a specific ability to change people for the better. It does not happen immediately . It\'s not like reading one verse. We are not perfect. But the change happens over time. The more we meditate the God\'s word, the more we absorb it, the more the Bible will change us into better person.', 7, '2021-03-22 00:38:54', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2520, 665, 845, 'Whether  we find that intimidating or enticing, we need some guardrails to keep us from getting lost in Revelation\'s prophecies, metaphors, and apocalyptic imagery. Revelation was written in a particular time  and place to particular people. Traditionally, interpreters have approached Revelation in four primary ways. 1. Preterist 2. Histrorist 3. Futurist 4. Idealist. \r\n\r\nWe should resist \"Revelation-made-easy\" approaches. If we are unwilling to live with an any uncertainty. The first Christians were blessed for obeying Revelation (1:3) and the book is described as an unsealed book. even for people living in John\'s day (22:10).  Revelation does not progress in a neat linear fashion. The book is filled with prophetic, apocalyptic visions designed to make a dramatic impact on the reader, not to present a precise chronological sequence of the future events. For example, notice that sixth seal(6:12-17) takes us to the end of the age.', 7, '2021-03-22 00:52:52', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2521, 665, 846, 'The teachings of the Sermon on the Mount have been a key element of Christian ethics. For centuries, the sermon has acted as a fundamental recipe for the conduct of the followers of Jesus. The last verse of chapter 5 of Matthew is a focal point of the sermon that summarizes its teachings by advising the disciples to see perfection. The Greek word telios used to refer to perfection also implies as end, or destination, advising the disciples to see the path towards perfection and the kingdom of God. It teaches that God\'s children are those who act like God.  The teaching of the sermon are often referred to as the Ethics of the Kingdom: they place a high level of emphasis on \"purity of the heart\" and embody the basic standard of righteousness.', 6, '2021-03-22 01:09:27', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2522, 665, 847, 'When the Lord Jesus taught  His disciples the beautiful prayer known as the Lord\'s Prayer, He began \" Our Father in heaven, hallowed be Your name, Your kingdom come, Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven\". Spiritual kingdom in people\'s heart is certainly a part of it. Paul said\" For the kingdom of God is not a matter of eating and drinking, but of righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit. And thus, the Church is one form of Kingdome of God, a community of people who through faith in Christ and the indwelling of the Holy Spirit serve Christ in the world. Jesus referred to His own miracles as \"signs of the Kingdom\" for they were bright sports in the darkness.', 7, '2021-03-22 01:16:32', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2523, 665, 848, 'The kingdom of God is one of the key elements of the teachings of Jesus in the New Testament.  Drawing on Old Testament teachings, the Christian characterization of the relationship between God and humanity inherently involves the notion of the Kingship of God. The Old Testament referes to \"God the judge of all\" and the notion that all humans will eventually be judged is an essential element of Christian teachings.  The new Testament is written against the back drop of Second Temple Judaism. The view of the kingdom developed during that time included the restoration of Israel to a Davidic Kingdome and the intervention of God in history. The coming of the kingdom of God involved God finally taking back the reins of kingdom.', 6, '2021-03-22 01:23:26', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2524, 665, 849, 'Blessed is the man who walks not in the counsel of the wicked, not stands in the way of sinners, not sits in the seat of scoffers; but his delight is  in the law of the Lord, and on his law he meditates day and night. Jesus very clearly said \" Don\'t think that I came to destroy the law or the prophets. I did not come to destroy, but to fulfill. How to delight yourself in the Lord?? The word delight is an action of taking a great pleasure in God, to please Him or to be His pleasure.  In Romans 13:1-2, Obey the government, for God is the one who has put it there. There is no government anywhere that God has not placed in power. So those who refuse to obey the law of land are refusing to obey God.', 6, '2021-03-22 01:30:13', '2021-05-10 22:54:52', NULL),
(2525, 664, 876, 'Often the term \' Son of God\' is used to denote beings that are not divine. Angels were addressed so. Job 1:6. The nation of Israel was also called God\'s son. Exodus 4:22. Human Kings were also addressed so.  ( 1 Samuel 7:14). Adam is called the son of God. Luke 3:38. God\'s faithful believers also called sons of God. Matthew 5:9. \r\nBut Jesus\' unique sonship is clear in the gospel of John. We are told that Jesus is the eternal word of God, meaning he is both God himself, and the only begotten of the father. John 1:1-3. In John 8:18-23, Jesus claimed his pre existence with the father. Jesus insisted that he and the father are one. John 10:30. \r\nMany of the NT passages refer to the divine sonship of Jesus. Romans 1:3-4, 8:3, Hebrews 1:1-3. The NT teaches that Jesus is God the son, one of the three persons of the Trinity. Through out the NT, the son is subordinate to the authority of the father. The early Church confessed that Jesus is fully divine; and it was a critical aspect of the Christian faith, and even today it is the hall mark of Christianity.', 8, '2021-03-22 09:41:11', '2021-05-10 22:54:03', NULL),
(2526, 664, 877, 'The Greek word \' Kurios\' was a common word meaning \'ruler\' or \' master\' used as a polite form of address. Matthew 10:24.\r\nSame time Nt also used the word \'Kurios\' as a name for God. The septuagent used the term kurios 6700 times, to denote the sacred name of God \'Yahweh\', revealed to Israel in Hebrew Bible. \r\nWhen Philippians 2 said , \' every knee shall bow and every tough shall confess that Jesus is Lord\'; it says, Jesus Christ is not just a master, he is to be identified with the Lord God of Israel. \r\nRomans 10:9 and13 reaffirms the saving power of God at work through Jesus Christ.  Matthew 3, Mark 1, Luke 3 and John 1 quoting from Isaiah 40 equate Jesus with God in the old Testament. \r\nConfessing Jesus as Lord, the Church was reaffirming the biblical teaching that Jesus Christ is God, and that as God he is a full member of the Trinity, with the same divine attributes as the father and the Holy Spirit.', 7, '2021-03-22 09:51:06', '2021-05-10 22:54:03', NULL),
(2527, 664, 878, 'Since the early centuries many people raised doubt about the humanity of Christ, unable to comprehend God in the form of human beings. Following features clarify the humanity of Jesus Christ. \r\n1. Generation: When the Apostles\' creed says that Jesus \'was conceived\', it has in mind that Jesus began in the same way that all human beings after Adam and Eve begun; as a tiny baby in mother\'s womb. \r\n2.Body: Jesus\' sufferings, crucifixion, death and burial demonstrated that he was a real man with a physical human body on that could be beaten, bleed, abused, killed and buried in a tomb. \r\n3. Soul: Scripture regularly says that human beings consists of a mortal body that houses an immortal soul. Hebrews 4:12, 1 Thessalonians 5:23. Jesus spoke of his soul in Luke 23:46. When we die. our souls are separated from our bodies and continue in a conscious state. Same experience Jesu too passed through. \r\n4. Resurrection: The resurrection proves that Jesus was real human being because it reaffirms that his full, glorified human existence included the reunion of his real human body with his real human soul.\r\nSo the natural human experiences of birth , human body, soul and resurrection proves the humanity of Jesus Christ.', 8, '2021-03-22 10:03:34', '2021-05-10 22:54:03', NULL),
(2528, 664, 879, 'The English word \' Christ\' is a translation of Greek \' Christos\' which in turn is a translation of the OT Hebrew word \' Mashiach\' or \' Messiah\'\', meaning \' the anointed one\'. \r\nIn the OT, it was a broad term which can be applied to any one whom God appointed to serve him in a special capacity. Eg; Prophets, Priests, Kings etc. \r\nIn later history of Israel, the prophets prophecied of an anointed King in Davidic line. Jeremiah 23:5-6, Zechariah 12-13. The NT speaks of Jesus as the Christ in over 500 places. John 1:41 and 4:25-26 speak of Jesus as the Messiah. Jesu explicitly admitted to being the messiah declared by the prophets. \r\nAccording to the OT prophecies, the messiah had to be human. Only human son of David could be an atoning sacrifice for his people. Hebrews 2:14-17, Isaiah 53. Also the messiah had to be human that he had to be the second Adam that he had to succeed where Adam failed in building the Kingdom of God. \r\nThrough his righteous life, atoning death, empowered resurrection and heavenly enthronement, Jesus succeeded where Adam failed. Thus he fulfilled the human office of the messiah, restoring human race to the important role of building the Kingdom of God.', 9, '2021-03-22 10:16:33', '2021-05-10 22:54:03', NULL),
(2529, 664, 880, 'Christian theology consistently maintained that Jesus is fully human in every respect. But Jesus is a perfect human being where as the rest of us are flawed. Jesus was born without sin, and lived a perfectly sinless life. Hebrews 4: 14-15.\r\nAnother important aspect is the dual natures of Christ; perfectly human and perfectly divine. It is described as \' hypostatic union\'. which deals with the union of a divine nature and a human nature within the one hypostatic or person of God the son. \r\nGod the son has always possessed full divinity with all its attributes. And when he was conceived and born as a human being, he added to his person all the essential attributes of a human being, such as a body and soul. Philippians 2 : 5-7. Jesu has only one person, same time has two distinct natures. Jesu possesses every attribute that is essential to divinity and humanity. \r\nMore over Jesus\' two natures are distinct from each other. Each nature remains entirely unchanged. This hypostatic union gives us a mediator who can be approached at any time as the one who knows our nature.', 8, '2021-03-22 10:26:47', '2021-05-10 22:54:03', NULL),
(2530, 660, 897, 'Around the year AD 56-57, when Paul and his travelling companions were going from Asia Minor to Jerusalem to deliver fund  to poor Christians for famine, they met the elders of the Ephesus church. During this meeting Paul revealed about this imprisonment in Jerusalem as told by Holy Spirit. Acts 20:22-24. In Tyre and in many cities Holy Spirit moved many more believers to warn Paul of the coming hardship in Jerusalem.\r\nduring his stay in Caesarea Maritima , Paul was warned yet again not to go to Jerusalem, prophet Agabus bound himself with a belt, depicting that Paul would be imprisoned in Jerusalem this way. But Paul was determined to go to Jerusalem  and preach gospel , as he thought that God was planning to use his arrest and imprisonment to further the gospel. Acts 21:30', 9, '2021-03-22 17:44:17', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2531, 660, 898, 'Paul arrived in Jerusalem with the funds and stayed with Mnason and he was well received by the elders. Paul hoped that this fund for poor gentile Christians would bring them all together, but the leaders informed Paul about the rumor that Paul was disregarding the Jewish practices and practices. so the elders of the church suggested Paul to undergo purification rites with four men who had taken Nazirite vows to show obedience to law and traditions. as Paul was fulfilling the rites, and as he was sitting in the inner court of the temple, he was recognized by some Jewish from Asia minor, who had seen them with a gentile Jew Trophimus who had accompanied Paul with Jerusalem. so they thought that Trophimus was also inside the court defiling the inner temple. in response the Jews aroused the city against Paul and angry mob dragged him from the court of Israel intenting to kill him. but when the commander of the Roman Garrison heard about it , took him to custody and when he realized that Paul was a Roman citizen,  he was given special protection.', 8, '2021-03-22 17:54:52', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2532, 660, 899, 'Paul was a prisoner of Rome and was taken under Roman centurion Julius to be presented before Caesar. from Jerusalem to Rome. He had advise the sailors not to put sail now for Italy, due to violent wind and storm , he wanted to reach Rome and Preach gospel. But the captain and Sailors convinced the roman centurions to set sail. At the beginning , it was well, but very soon , they were caught in a violent storm. During this time Paul ministered to those on board and encouraged that God revealed to him that they would all reach safely. Eventually, the ship struck near the Isle of Malta. the ship was stranded on Malta, Paul and his companions remained in Malta for three months and was cared by the residents of Islanders.\r\n during his stay in Malta, and he was bitten by a poisonous snake, the natives initially took this a sign that Paul was murderer and expected him  to die. Paul was safe and natives changed their mind about Paul. he performed many miraculous healings in Malt. so many believed in gospel.  in AD 60, Paul was finally moved on to Rome, he arrived there later in AD 60 and was placed under house arrest. he was under guard and was allowed to teach for two years.', 0, '2021-03-22 18:07:02', '2021-03-22 18:07:02', NULL),
(2533, 660, 900, 'Paul was not idle during his time in prison, rather he continued to serve actively as a minister of the gospel of Jesus Christ. Paul was apostle, Jesus was personally called and trained Paul and appoint him to serve as his ambassador. Paul \'s appointed tasks were not put on hold when he was imprisoned . on the contrary in god\'s providence, prison was exactly were God wanted Paul to b eat this time in his life.\r\nGod himself had planned events so that Paul\'s imprisonment in Rome would provide the opportunity for Paul to spread the gospel of Christ to the hearts of the western world.', 7, '2021-03-22 18:16:08', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2534, 660, 901, 'The book of acts teaches us three things about Paul\'s ministry during his imprisonment.\r\nAwareness of suffering:\r\nIn Acts 19-28, Luke described Paul was well informed about his coming hardships, Paul knew that he would imprisoned and suspected that he would even be put to death.\r\nAwareness of Purpose:\r\nPaul believed that God would use his hardships to spread he Christian gospel. HE knee that it would be worthwhile because it will be God\'s ways of spreading the gospel of salvation in Christ.\r\nAwareness of blessing:\r\nPaul was very aware of God\'s blessing in his ministry during his time. In Acts 19-28, Luke states that Paul\'s gospel testimony spread with the dramatic blessings of God\'s spirit', 6, '2021-03-22 18:23:57', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2535, 660, 902, 'three aspects of Christ\'s kingship:\r\nSovereignty:\r\nChrist is sovereign, which means he has the power and strength to accomplish  his will and he has the legal authority and right to do so.\r\nJesus is so powerful and so authoritative now that his sovereignty extends over ll other kings and rulers as well, whether they are on the earth or on spiritual realms.\r\nhonor: \r\nChrist is honored because he is perfect, holy, righteous, and he is honored because:\r\n1. he exercises his highest authority righteously and justly\r\n2. he is the creator and sustainer of the creation.\r\n he is the most valuable thing in all creation.\r\ndetermination:\r\nPaul knew Christ\'s plan to rule over all creation. he confidently asserted that Christ was determined to consummate his kingdom. he commonly wrote of believers having a future inheritance and great reward on his return.', 7, '2021-03-22 18:34:22', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2536, 660, 903, 'as per Paul, when we believe in Jesus, we are united to him in a mystical , spiritual way, we are counted blameless as if we are Jesus. because we ere united to him God forgives us all our sins.\r\nColossians 3:1-4\r\nThrough our union with Christ, we are united to him in his death resurrection ,and ascension and when he returns we will reign  with him glory.\r\nEphesians 2:6-7\r\nAs per Paul , we are sated with Christ in the heavenly places, being united to him in his present kingship over all creation. we share his honor and glory. \r\nPaul also appeals to our union with Christ kingship among with suffering Colossians 1:24', 8, '2021-03-22 18:44:07', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL),
(2537, 660, 904, 'The main theological truth that underlies the theological unity of Paul\'s prison epistles is the fact the Jesus Christ is the conqueror and ruler over all creation.\r\nChrist as king:\r\nChrist is a perfectly righteous and just king , his judgements and commands are perfectly ethical, so that we also have an ethical obligation to obey him.\r\nUnited to Christ:\r\nOur union in Christ with Christ obligates us to obey and live ethically as Christ indwells us by his Spirit, gave us a new nature and compels us to do good works.  \r\nOur new nature, God\'s command , our union with one another, our union with Christ enables us to live ethically, according to the standard that god has set down for us in scripture.\r\nPaul\'s prison epistles are theologically united by the Paul\'s rich and multi faceted doctrine of the kingship of Christ\'s over all creation including believers\' union with Christ and our responsibility to live ethically.', 8, '2021-03-22 18:56:37', '2021-05-10 22:53:35', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2538, 663, 910, 'the letter to Ephesians was written by Paul. we can affirm it by following ways\r\n1. Ephesians 1:1 says that \" Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God......\". this shows the letter is written by Paul\r\n 2.2 Thessalonians 2:1-3 in the early church some false teachers forged letters under the name of other people, but the elders would have discovered the forgery and rejected the letter\r\n3.  the  letter of Ephesians closely resembles  Paul\'s other letters such as  Colossians . the connection s in doctrines are so powerful that even if Paul had not stated his name in the letter, it is difficult to imagine the church ever crediting it to anyone else.\r\n4 Paul had planted the church of Ephesus and lived there for two years, and had close relationship with the elders, so it is impossible that the Ephesians would not have recognized his letter as a forgery  if it was one.', 8, '2021-03-23 17:48:03', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2539, 663, 911, 'Ephesus was a capital city of the Roman province of Asia, here he had clashed with worshippers of the Pagan god , Artemis and with many occult practice, we see it in \r\nEphesians 5:11. he taught strongly against fruitless deeds of darkness  . in Ephesians 6:11-12, he insisted that Christians battle against the false pagan gods. The city of Ephesus was the nurturer of Artemis. in Ephesians 5:27-29 Paul spoke of Christ as the feeding or nurturing.\r\nin Ephesians , he responded to the problems of several churches in different locations . Paul\'s purpose in writing this letter was to deal with the problems of all this churches. to teach the newly found church about the Christian faith and turning from pagan religions.\r\nhe wanted to teach them about\r\n1. Kingdom of God\r\n2.challenges that come in living as Christians.', 7, '2021-03-23 18:03:35', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2540, 663, 912, 'When we study the background of the Ephesians , we come to know that Paul had founded the church of Ephesus and that he had lived there for two years, he had good relationship with the elders even after that, Ephesus was a prominent city of Roman provinces, where the pagan worship was at its peak . the Artemis the nurturer was dominant there. while in Ephesus, Paul had a clash with the worshippers of Artemis.  in Ephesians 5:11 and6:11-12, Paul insisted against the false pagan worship. the churches in the Lycus valley , Colossians ,Laodiceans and Hierapolis had the issues of pagan worship . Paul\'s epistle to the Ephesians is is only canonical letter without any personal references, this also indicates that Paul wanted his letter to circulate to a variety of churches  beginning with the church in Ephesus.', 0, '2021-03-23 18:19:21', '2021-03-23 18:19:21', NULL),
(2541, 663, 913, 'Paul \'s teaching of praise and prayer are similar. \r\nBoth has Trinitarian theology that we read in Ephesians1:, it\'s emphasis on salvation  through the atonement of Jesus Christ in verse7, mystery of the gospel in verse 9, promise of our future glory ensured by the holy Spirit in verse 11-14\r\nin verses 4and 5, Paul honored God for his sovereign rule, in verses 5-7 Paul praise God\'s benevolence towards his people. in verse Paul praise God for our inheritance in Christ.\r\nPrayer\r\nPaul\'s prayer included Trinitarian theology\r\nverse 19 and 20 emphasizes that salvation comes through the atonement of Jesus Christ.\r\nverse 17-19furtherrevekation of gospel.\r\nverse 18 speaks of the hope of our future glory.\r\nverse 19 speaks of God\'s sovereign rule and his benevolence verse 22 and 23 speaks of Christ rules as king for the benefit of the church\r\nverse 18 Paul spoke directly of Christ\'s glorious inheritance in the saints', 7, '2021-03-23 18:34:09', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2542, 663, 914, 'Pauls\' teaching in Ephesians regarding:\r\n1. Citizenship:\r\nPail\'s teaching on citizenship can be divide into three sections\r\n1. Ephesians 2:1-3 focus on the fact that fallen human beings are born into the kingdom of darkness and are enemies of God by nature \r\n2. Ephesians 2:4-10 God grants us citizenship in his kingdom, by transferring us from the kingdom of darkness to light\r\n3. Ephesians 2:11-22 nature of our citizenship in the kingdom of light\r\nAdministration\r\n A kingdom cannot be run by merely king and citizenry. it needs different levels of leaders to make laws, to execute laws and to judge those who violate theses laws.\r\nIn God\'s kingdom of light, it is manifested in the church. the church is to be ruled by the elders and theses elders are accountable to one another and to God.\r\nCode for living:\r\nthis contains different instructions on Christian behavior\r\nEcclesiastical order in the kingdom Ephesians 4:1-16\r\nPurifying the kingdom Ephesians 4:17-5:20\r\nDomestic order in the kingdom. Ephesians 5:21-6:19\r\nwarfare of the kingdom Ephesians 6:10-20', 8, '2021-03-23 18:44:01', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2543, 663, 915, 'In god\'s kingdom , good works play a specific role, they are tools by which God expands and purifies his kingdom, receives glory and ministers to his people. we are all citizens of the kingdom of god. each of us a are a stone in the building.\r\nChrist has the position of preeminence, being the cornerstone of the foundation. in which all other stones rely.\r\nthe goal of this building is to become god\'s dwelling so that God could live in the minds of his people.\r\nthe good works god has prepared for us consists largely of services to one another, for the purpose of building up the church, so that it becomes a fitting kingdom for the Lord of the universe to rule .Ephesians 4:11-13. so Christians should work to build the kingdom of  God.', 7, '2021-03-23 18:57:53', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2544, 663, 916, 'Gospel or good news is the forgiveness of an Individual\'s sin and the promise of an individual\'s everlasting life. it is the message that our divine king is using his power and authority to subject  his enemies and to conquer sin. to redeem his people from their bondage and to establish them as rulers over the new earth. \r\nPaul often spoken of the gospel of the kingdom . Paul spoke of the ages to come, they generally described it in terms of the kingdom of the god. they saw it as the time when God\'s kingdom should be manifested on earth as it is in heaven', 7, '2021-03-23 19:08:13', '2021-05-10 22:58:11', NULL),
(2545, 666, 776, 'Majority of interpreters believe that Paul was not the author of the book of Hebrews due to the following reasons :\r\n\r\n1) The book of Hebrews - the author is anonymous normally whenver we read any epistle of Apostle Paul, it was his nature to identify himself as the author, particulary to avoid forgeries and spreading false teachings under his name.\r\n\r\n2) A detailed study of the book of Hebrews - it emphasizes on topics that do not receive any attention or reference in Paul\'s episltles. \r\n\r\n3) The strongest contention of doubt came from the fact that the author of the book of Hebrews distances himself from the first generation of Jesus followers. Whereas the contention of Paul was that he received the gospel directly from Jesus. \r\n\r\nHence from the above reasonings we can understand from the interpreters that Paul was not the author of the book of Hebrews.', 8, '2021-04-23 15:19:58', '2021-05-10 22:58:33', NULL),
(2546, 666, 777, 'While studying the book of Hebrews - the five important factors or characteristics of the original audience for whom the book is addressed is as follows :\r\n\r\n1) Jewish \r\nMajority of the original audience was Jewish Hebrews.\r\n\r\nAs mentioned in Hebrews 1:1 \r\nIn the past God spoke to our forefathers through the propohets and in various ways. \r\n\r\nHere the author refers to the Old Testament Israelites as \"our forefathers\" as the ancestors of the author and his audience.\r\n\r\n2) Hellenistic :\r\nA large part of the originial audience were Hellenistic. The familiarity of the audience with the teachings that were common to Jews living outside of Palestine as referred to in Hebrews 13:24 wherein it mentions, \"those from Italy\"\r\n\r\n3) Immature \r\nThe original audience of Hebrews were referred to as immature. As they have been believers for a long time but they could not make further progress in their beliefs and faith. The author notes that they need to be taught the elementary truths about God all over again as mentioned in Hebrews 5:12\r\n\r\n4) Persecuted\r\nThe original audience were under persecution. The Roman rulers - emperors persecuted the Christians for their beliefs. They already faced persecution in the past and it was an ongoing process of suffering in the present and the possiblity of future persecution, with the severity increasing. \r\n\r\nHebrews 10:32-35 - exhorts the believers to not throw away your confidence since it will be richly rewarded.\r\n\r\n5) Near Apostasy.\r\nDue to the severity of the persecutions many of the believers were are the brink of embracing apostasy turning away from Christ entirely.\r\n\r\nThe author of Hebrews warned such category of believers, warining them not to turn away from Christ., wherein no sacrifice for sins is left.', 8, '2021-04-23 15:37:37', '2021-05-10 22:58:33', NULL),
(2547, 666, 778, 'The author of Hebrews wrote to exhort his audience. Exhortation implies to summon the speakers side or to take the speaker\'s point of view.\r\n\r\nThe frequency of the exhortation co-relates to the urgency of the messages. These messages were implicit at times and at least 30 times they appear explicitly.\r\n\r\nTo urge - to implore, encouraging - exhorting the audience to do this or that. using imperatives, direct orders, associated with persuasive oratory.\r\n\r\nThe book of Hebrews has been characterized as highly rhetorical, can be categorized as follows \r\n\r\n1) Rhetorical devise called synkrisis in Greek - comparison between 2 or more things to convince the audience to affirm the speaker\'s point of view.\r\n\r\n2) Another rhetorical devise of Exempla, list of illustrations or examples that follow one another to build a reasonable argument to prove a point.\r\n\r\n3) The third rhetorical devise - Expression - gol wahomer - can be translated as light to heavy. Such arguments begin with simple explanation graduating to more complex conclusion. \r\n\r\nThese examples help us to see the urgency of the author\'s intentions using different rhetoric styles to convince the audience.', 8, '2021-04-23 15:53:48', '2021-05-10 22:58:33', NULL),
(2548, 666, 779, 'The original audience of Hebrews were immature. \r\nHebrews 5:12 Though by this time you ought to be teachers, you need someone to teach you the truths all over again.\r\n\r\nThey have been belieers for over a long time, and they should have made doctrinal progress. But the author finds out that they need to be taught all over again starting from the elementary truths of God\'s word.\r\n\r\nIn other words they are unable to grow in the doctrine that they have learned, the very reason may be that they are unable to apply the basic truths and the doctrine in their personal life. Since the more you learn spiritual truths, you should be able to apply it to your practical life and automatically God would enable you to grow in the Word.\r\n\r\nSince they have been believers over a long period of time but they have faltered somewhere in their lives, hence they need to be taught all over again.\r\n\r\nAs parents we should be able to explain our faith and beliefs to our children, as neightbors to our friends and people staying around us. \r\n\r\nBelievers over a stronger period of time, but no growth factor. Dull of hearing and not able to apply the teachings to their personal life.\r\n\r\nAs a person, my individual faith life also needs to grow in the grace of God. The more we learn about the doctrinal truths, combining with faith when we apply it to our individual lives, God does the rest by helping us to grow. Studying the word and praying for God\'s providence and grace is the way forward to enhance one\'s personal faith in God.', 9, '2021-04-23 16:13:02', '2021-05-10 22:58:33', NULL),
(2549, 666, 780, 'The author of Hebrews wrote to exhort his audience to reject local Jewish teachings and to remain faithful to Jesus. \r\n\r\nEarly Christians faced rejection and persecution, they were suffering for their faith, tempting may to accept the teachings of the local Jewish community that were contrary to Christian faith.\r\nThe author primarily dealth with erroneous beliefs, and practices, specifically those that developed in Jewish communities.\r\nSome of the false topics that the author to the book of Hebrews wrote about were \r\n\r\nCeremonial Foods \r\nBasic Teachings\r\nAngels \r\nMelchizedek\r\nSupremacy of the New Covenant in Jesus\r\nExercise Practical perserverance to face challenges to their faith \r\n\r\nSome of the mainstream false teachings influencing Christians are as follows :\r\n\r\n1) Prosperity Theology\r\n2) No sufferings for true Christians\r\n3) Local Culture influence on our faith life, families and next generation\r\n4) Manipulation of God\'s Word or doctrince to suit their personal choice\r\n5) Compromising or adjusting with upcoming trends or life styles - contrary to the doctrine or word of God.\r\n\r\nFacing the challenges that question the foundations of one\'s personal faith life, we can always go back to the Scriptures and study and examine our personal ives in light of the truths wherein God wants us to always continue and grow.\r\n\r\nNever compromise or adjust with any pattern, style or behavior that is contrary to the Scriptures, for it is God\'s Word, preparing us for eternity.', 9, '2021-04-23 16:32:16', '2021-05-10 22:58:33', NULL),
(2550, 670, 781, 'The book of Hebrews refers to the Old Testament nearly 100 times to support his theological views.\r\n\r\nFive main categories wherein the author referred to the quotes from the Old Testament are as follows :\r\n\r\n1) Factual Backgrounds\r\nAuthor recalled historical details from the Old Testament and quoted few words and furthermore incorporated, culminating into his presentation of the Christian Faith. \r\nFor e.g. Heb 7  speaks about Melchizedek - reference to the OT - book of Genesis, furthermore comparison between Jesus and Melchizedek.\r\n\r\n2) Theological Outlooks\r\nThe author checks on certain theological outlooks which were still true in his own days, which were affirmed by OT scriptures and beliefs.\r\n\r\nFor e.g. Hebrews 1: 7 - author speaks about angles as ministering spirits with reference to Psalms 104:4\r\n\r\nHebrews 12:29  wherein author referred to God as consuming fire, further strengthening his teaching that God is still a consuming fire in Christ.\r\n\r\nThese writings prove that most of the factual backgrounds hold grounds even in New Testament times. \r\n\r\n3) Moral Obigations \r\nThe moral obligations placed by God in the OT times still holds true to the NT times, and these were standards for God\'s people.\r\n\r\nHebrews 3: - wherein author indicated OT reference of Psalms 95, taught Israel not to rebel against God.\r\n\r\nHebrews 13:6 - quoted Psalms 118 - the author urged his audeince to confess confidence in God.\r\n\r\n4) Eschatological predictions\r\nThe predictions made about the last days in OT times shows that God\'s final judgments and blessings are fulfilled in Christ.\r\n\r\nFor eg.\r\nHebrews 10: refers to Jeremiah 31 to show how the new covenant of Christ eliminates the need for further sacrifices.\r\n\r\n5) Dynastic Ideals\r\nThe author refers to a number of dynastid ideals which were established from David;s lineage being fulfilled in Christ Jesus.\r\n\r\nFor e.g. Heb 1:5 quoted Psalms 2:7 wherein God adopted a descended of David as his royal son to rule the nations, fulfilled in Ch', 9, '2021-05-07 15:25:48', '2021-05-10 22:58:54', NULL),
(2551, 670, 782, 'The local Jewish community gave excessive honor to angels. This was a challenge to those who followed Christ. \r\n\r\nThe author respoded to these challenges in 5 steps as follows :\r\n\r\nRefrences from Hebrews Chapters 1 & 2 \r\n\r\n1) Author asks the audience to follow Jesus because he is God\'s supreme source of revelation. God who spoke in OT times through angels and prophets have in these last days spoken to us through His Son - Jesus Christ.\r\n\r\n2) Jesus is greater than angels, because he alone is God\'s Son., whereas angels are spirits sent to serve those who inherit salvation in Christ. \r\n\r\n3) Message of Salvation first announced by Jesus, such a great salvation, violation of instructions from angels received just punishment, how much more would be the punishment for those who ignore the salvation revealed by Christ.\r\n\r\n4) Supremacy of Christ, Jesus is now the ruler over the angels. Furthermore all believers will reign over them. Though initially God made humans little lower than angels, but destined them to rule over all of creation, as the final glory revealed in Christ Jesus.\r\n\r\n5) Jesus is Abraham\'s descendant. and will share his glory with all of Abraham\'s descendants, but angels will not be a part of it. Jesus humanity and divinity broke the power of the fallen angel - the Devil. And all these were done to free mankind from the fear of death. \r\n\r\nHence the author concluded that Jesus the Son of God was far more superior to the  angels.', 9, '2021-05-07 15:44:41', '2021-05-10 22:58:54', NULL),
(2552, 670, 783, 'Hebrews Chapter 8 to 11 focuses on New Covenant\r\n\r\nThe new covenant as prophesied by Jeremiah 31 cannot fail because it is far superior than the old,, the reasons are as follows :\r\n\r\nA) It is found on better promises\r\nBetter promises offer the complete transformation of God\'s people and the final eternal forgiveness of their sins.\r\n\r\nThe author elaborated on the fact that Jesus heavenly royal priesthood was far superior to the Levitical priesthood. The sacrifices of earthly tabernacle could not resolve the problem of sin but it was a constant reminder every year. \r\n\r\nB) Christ came as high priest !!\r\nChrist came as high priest and offered himself as a priestly atonement and opened access to the throne of grace in heaven (Holy of Holies) \r\n\r\nFurthermore the author explained why Jesus sacriice was necessary and used the exmaple of a Will. A will is always initiated by someone\'s death similarly Moses covenant was initiated with death and blood of animals. But the new covenant had to be initiated by death and blood of Christ in the sanctuary of God\'s heavenly place.  Hence this covenant was far superior since Jesus himself as the royal priest offered himself to fulfill the covenant requirements of the New Covenant. \r\n\r\nThe inheritance of this will is forgiveness to all who believed & were cleansed by the blood of Jesus. \r\n\r\nJesus dd away with sin once for all because his blood is not sprinkled in man made sanctuary. He entered heaven by the sacrifice of himself. \r\n\r\nJesus died as a ransom to set his people free from judgment. \r\n\r\nLevitical sacrifices could not bring final forgiveness of sin. Whereas Jesus accomplished that by his own sacrifice on the cross, hence there was no need for any more animal sacrifices.', 9, '2021-05-07 16:04:12', '2021-05-10 22:58:54', NULL),
(2553, 670, 784, 'The book of Hebrews refers to the Old Testament nearly 100 times, these references were very important for the author, hence they appear in every major division of the book. Considering that the Old Testament teachings still hold true and are further enahnced to our current day beliefs.\r\n\r\nFactual Backgrounds :\r\nAuthor recalled historical details and then further incorporated the facts into the presentation of Christian faith, which holds true to even to current day belief system and are fulfilled in Christ Jesus\r\n\r\nTheological Outlooks\r\nAuthor focused on theological beliefs affirmed about God himself and other matters in close relation to God.\r\n\r\nMany of the references about Angels, Melchizedek - King of Salem, Descendants of David\'s dynast would be called God\'s Son etc all were fulfilled by Jesus Christ and holds true to our faith and belief system.\r\n\r\nMoral Obligations\r\nGod had plaed moral obligations in Old Testament times and they still remain as standards for God\'s peopel in New Testament times. \r\nFor eg. Psalms 95 verse 7 taught Israel not to rebel against God, which still holds true until today.\r\n\r\nBook of Proverbs 3:11 urged Israel not to be discouraged when God disciplined them - holds true to today \r\n\r\nAll these passages points out that Old Testament moral obligations continued to be in force for followers of Christ.\r\n\r\nEschatological Predictions :\r\nMany references about the last days wherein God would gather his perople together from exile. Many of the old testament predictions are fulfilled in God\'s final judgments and blessings in Christ Jesus \r\n\r\nHebrews 10: speaks about the New Covenant in Christ as prophesied by Jeremiah 31 - which eliminates the need for further animal sacrifices.\r\n\r\nOld Testament teachings are still applicable to present day life and ministry, and they are further enhanced and fulfilled through our Saviour Jesus Christ.\r\n\r\nIn contrast the book of Hebrews throws more light and increases the stature of our bliefs and faith in Christ Jesus, teaching us that we are indeed a blessed generation, far more superior that OT believers. Due to our adoptions as Sons & Daughters of God almighty through the sacrifice of Jesus Christ.\r\n\r\nOld Testament is a shadow of things to come being fulfilled through the life of our saviour Jesus Christ.', 9, '2021-05-07 16:24:15', '2021-05-10 22:58:54', NULL),
(2554, 670, 785, 'As we study in the book of Hebrews 5:6 onwards we learn that \r\n\r\nThough He was a Son, yet he learned obedience by the things which He suffered. \r\nAnd having being perfected, He became the author of eternal salvation to all who obey Him. \r\n\r\nChrist Jesus overcame sin during His earthly life and suffered for the sins of the mankind by offering himself as a sacrifice, such a great salvation providing access the throne of grace.\r\n\r\nJesus Christ is superior to Angels, to Moses, Melchiedek, far above all principalities and powers fulfilling all the requirements of the law and offering himself as a sacrifice, as a ransom to pay the price of Sin for manking and transform those who believe to become children of God\r\n\r\nThis salvation is common and open to all irrespective of our background or circumstances and welcomes one and all to have faith in God.\r\n\r\nGreatest salvation every available to mankind without any pre-requisite conditions, availble to everyone who believes - The New Covenant which is far superior with which we as humans who affirmly believe in Christ Jesus will also destined to reign with him in eternity. \r\n\r\nInviting one and all to receive such a great Salvation which was first proclaimed by Angels, and prophets but in these last days declared to us through His Son Jesus Christ. \r\n\r\nTo leave aside every local teachings and beliefs, and inviting one and all to receive Christ Jesus as their personal saviour and to commit their lives destined for eternity with our Lord.', 8, '2021-05-07 16:44:18', '2021-05-10 22:58:54', NULL),
(2555, 671, 905, 'Paul had an indirect relationship with the Colossians with representatives , such as his Colossians friends Epaphras, Philemon and Onesimus and his messenger Tychicus.\r\nsecond  although they had not met face to face, Paul and the Colossians corresponded with each other. \r\nthird Paul and the Colossians ministered to each other and besides struggling in prison on  their behalf. Paul prays specifically for the Colossians. Fourth the Colossians ministered to Paul as well. Colossians men Epaphras and Onesimus visited Paul in prison\r\nIndividuals:\r\nPaul had a number of friends from Colossians. three such friends were Philemon, Apphia and Archippus.  Philemon 1:2\r\nPhilemon was a close friend of Paul, he was also part of Philemon\'s family, perhaps his son.\r\nAnother friend of Paul from Colosse was Epaphras, who remained with Paul imprison when Paul sent his letter to the  Colossian church. Paul\'s friend Onesimus was also from Colosse who was a slave , who have sough out Paul after running from Philemon and ended up serving Paul in prison.', 8, '2021-05-13 17:42:11', '2021-08-11 05:54:45', NULL),
(2556, 671, 906, 'problems in colosse church:\r\n Greek philosophy:\r\nthe false teaching in Colosse seems to have mixed Christianity with elements of Greek Philosophy Colossians 2:1-4 Paul speaks that Colossians valued mystery, wisdom and knowledge , all of which Greek philosophy and religion typically valued. Colossians 2;8 suggests that the false teachers in Colossians were with beliefs and practices based in Greek religion and occults. they were not truly converted . they accepted Christianity along with Greek practices.\r\nJewish Law:\r\nthe false teachers in colosse included observances of the Jewish calendar such as religious festivals, new moon celebrations and the Sabbath day, as well as , dietary restrictions. the false teachers corrupted these laws using them in idolatrous worship and pagan asceticism, they also propogated circumcision.\r\nSpiritual beings:\r\nthe Colossian church was courting the worship of spiritual powers in three evident forms.\r\n first Paul wrote about the worship of angels , second addressed the matter of rules and authorities, third he dealt with the problems related to the basic principles of this world.', 9, '2021-05-13 17:55:11', '2021-08-11 05:54:45', NULL),
(2557, 671, 907, 'it is important to understand the background of Colosse church, as the false teachers in colosse appear to have combined Jewish legalism , pagan religion and Christianity and to have encouraged the worship of theses astral or cosmic powers, commonly known as basic principles of stoicheia\r\nThe church in colosse faced some real challenges in the first century. unlike other churches they apparently had never received apostolic training. although the church had been planted by godly men, it had not been , solidly grounded in the theology of the apostles. this made the Colossian Christians particularly vulnerable to false teaching. so to correct these false doctrines and teaching Paul write this letter to the church of Colossians.', 8, '2021-05-13 18:05:06', '2021-08-11 05:54:45', NULL),
(2558, 671, 908, 'Christ as supreme\r\nColossians 1:15-16 Paul says that Christ is the image of the invisible God.\r\nSecondly Paul mentioned that Christ is the firstborn over all creation. Paul associate Christ\'s status as firstborn, with his authority and supremacy over all creation and said nothing about a time when Jesus did not exist.\r\nThird , Paul said that Christ was the agent of creation. The one through whom God created the universe.\r\nfourth Christ is the supreme Lord, because God placed him as a head over the church. Colossians 1:18\r\nfifth, Paul explained Christ is God incarnate. Colossians 1;19 all the fulness of God dwell in \r\nChrist, the incarnation of the supreme God.\r\nfinally Paul spoke of Christ as the only reconciler between God and man. Colossians 1;19-20', 8, '2021-05-13 18:15:30', '2021-08-11 05:54:45', NULL),
(2559, 671, 909, 'Paul argued that because Christ was superior to the false gods, Christ ministers were superiors to this who served the false gods. \r\nPaul\'s argument consisted of five ,main ideas.\r\nChrist ministers are superior because they preach a gospel that actually reconciles believers to God. They stand before God clothed in righteousness of God.\r\nSecondly, Paul pointed to his own altruism, speaking of his suffering in behalf of the church Colossians 1:24\r\nthirdly Paul spoke of his divine commission . Paul had been appointed to his apostleship by the Lord himself . Colossians 1:25.\r\nfourth, the revelation that Paul had received was superior to that attested by the false teachers. Colossians 2:4\r\nfifth, Paul wrote of the superior empowerment of then Christs ministers, speaking of the fact that god gave power to his ministers. Paul did not labor in his own strength, rather God empowered and motivated ;Paul to work and to suffer as his apostle Colossians 1:29', 9, '2021-05-13 18:26:58', '2021-08-11 05:54:45', NULL),
(2560, 673, 910, 'we can consider the following points to believe that Paul wrote Ephesians\r\n1. firstly , the letter states that it was written by Paul . Ephesians 1:1\r\nPaul , an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God, to the saints, in Ephesus.\r\nnow in the early church  some false teachers forged letters under the names pf other people, but whatever the church discovered a letter to be forgery, they rejected it.\r\n2Thessalonians 2:1-3\r\nsecondly, it is simply too hard to believe that an admirer or student of Paul would have contradicted Paul\'s own teaching by forging his name in this manner. \r\nthirdly beyond this Ephesians closely resemble Paul\'s other letters in both doctrines and language. The connections are strong with Colossians .\r\nfinally, As per Acts 19-21 Paul had planted the church in Ephesus and he lived there for two years, he had continued to have good relationship with the elders, so then the Ephesians would have recognized this letter as a forgery , if it was one.', 8, '2021-05-17 17:41:29', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2561, 673, 911, 'Ephesus was the capital city of the Roman province of Asia which correspond roughly to the modern region known as Asia minor. From Acts 19, we see that Paul was in Ephesus  and he had clashed with the worshippers of the pagan goddess Artemis and with many occult practices . In Ephesians 5;11 he taught strongly against fruitless deeds of darkness and in Ephesians 6:11-12 he insisted that Christians battle against the false pagan gods\r\nSecondly, we know that the city of Ephesus was considered to be the nurturer of Artemis\r\nin Ephesians 5:27-29, Paul spoke of Christ as the feeding or nurturing the church. \r\nPaul had come to know about the false prophets, false teachers and the false doctrines they taught. he also was aware of the false practices around Ephesians an the nearby churches, like Colossians and Laodicea. so to address all the problems he wrote to Ephesians and also thinking that the letter to circulate to a variety of churches beginning with the church in Ephesus, nut continuing on to churches which he was unfamiliar.\r\nSo Paul wrote letter to Ephesians because of above said reasons.', 9, '2021-05-17 17:55:19', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2562, 673, 912, 'in the first century Christians had to be taught how to live in ways that were appropriate for the kingdom of Christ, many Christian  had been converted from pagan religions, as we see in Ephesians , that they worshipped goddess Artemis the most , they had many pagan practices, false teachings. . they had spent much of their lives following the ways Satan before they came to faith in Christ. and they found it difficult to change the ways they thought, felt and behaved. so the apostle Paul wrote his epistle to the Ephesians. in the first century , number of churches grew in the Lycus valley like churches of Colosse and Laodicea. these churches also face similar problems of people following and practicing occult religions and pagan gods.\r\nso in the letter to Ephesians, while we study the background of Paul writing the epistle, we understand the epistle better and we understand that Paul responded to all the abobe problems of several churches in different locations many of which he had never met. so his purpose in writing this letter was to deal with the problems of all these churches. \r\nPaul his letter contains no personal references, and he wanted his letter to circulate to a variety of churches beginning with the church in Ephesus, but continuing on to churches to which he was unfamiliar.\r\nhe directly addressed his challenge by painting a sweeping cosmic portrait of life in the kingdom of god in Christ.', 9, '2021-05-17 18:11:06', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2563, 673, 913, 'a section of prayer is seen in Ephesians 1:3-14 we see honoring the work of the Father. the son and the holy Spirit. \r\nits emphasis on salvation through the atonement of Jesus Christ in verse 7\r\n the revelation of the mystery of gospel in verse 9\r\n the promise of our future glory ensured by the gift of the holy Spirit in verses 11-14\r\nin verses 4 and 5 Paul honored god for his Sovereign rule\r\n in verse 9 and 10 Paul also praised God for his sovereign rule over all creation that will eventually bring all under the head ship of Christ.  verses 5 to 7 Paul praised God\'s benevolence toward his people in verse 14, Paul praised god for our inheritance in Christ\r\nthe next section is of Paul\'s prayer 1:15-23\r\n Paul repeats all these elements it includes honoring strong trinitarian theology\r\nin verse 17\r\n salvation through Christ in verse 19and 20\r\nfurther revelation of gospel in verses 17-19\r\nhope of our future glory verse 18\r\nGod\'s Sovereignty in verse 19\r\nGod\'s benevolence in verse 19\r\nbenefit of the church verse 22 and 23\r\nverse 18 Paul spoke of Christ\'s glorious inheritance in the saints.', 9, '2021-05-17 18:25:22', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2564, 673, 914, 'Paul\'s teaching to Ephesians \r\non citizenship: it can be divided in to three sections \r\n1: Ephesians 2:1-3 focuses on the fact that fallen human beings are born into the kingdom pf darkness and are enemies of god by nature\r\n2 Ephesians 2:4-10 details the ways God grants us citizenship in his kingdom by transferring us from kingdom of darkness to the kingdom of light\r\n 3 Ephesians 2:11-22 discusses the nature of our citizenship in the kingdom of light\r\nAdministration:\r\nevery kingdom needs an administrative structure. kingdoms function well only if they have a king and citizenry. human governments include different levels and types of leadership such as those who make the laws, to execute the laws and to this judge violation of the laws and the same is true in God\'s kingdom of light a sit is manifested in the church.\r\n the bible teaches that the church is to be ruled by elders and that these elders are accounted to one another and to God.\r\nCode for living\r\nthis code for living in the kingdom contains many different instruction on Christian behavior\r\n1. Ecclesiastical order in the kingdom Ephesians 4:1-16\r\n2 purifying in the kingdom 4:17 to 5:20\r\n3. domestic order in the kingdom 5:21-6:9\r\n4 .warfare in the kingdom 6:10-20', 9, '2021-05-17 18:36:51', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2565, 673, 915, 'Paul described the kingdom of God as abuilding with each Christian being stone in the structure . Christ has the position of preeminence, the apostles and prophets held positions of high authority under Christ. All other Christian stones in the structure. without distinction among us. \r\nthe goal of this building was to become God\'s dwelling so that god could live in the midst of his people. as we build God\'s kingdom today, we must focus on the big picture of honoring god and living in his presence and of striving to increase Christ\'s glory rather than our own. and we must be humble toward one another recognizing that no believer is more deserving of blessing than any other. the good works god has prepared for us consists largely of service into one another for the purpose of building up the church so that it becomes a fitting kingdom for the Lord of the universe to rule. Ephesians 4:11-13\r\nthe second goal is to attaining to the while measure of the fulness of Christ.\r\n so we Christians must work to build the kingdom of God for God\' glory.', 8, '2021-05-17 18:48:52', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2566, 673, 916, 'Paul preached the kingdom of God. as for us all gospel is the good news, we often relate it with the forgiveness of an individual sins and the promise of an individual\'s everlasting life. but in the Bible , the gospel is cosmic in scope . it is the message that our divine King is using His Divine  power and authority to subject his enemies and to conquer sin , to redeem his people from their bondage and to establish them as rulers over the new earth. that is why Jesus and the gospel writers so often, spoke of the gospel of the kingdom. \r\nso it has just made me more happier to have the gospel of my salvation and to have a king with such power and authority to redeem me from my sins and make me enter His Kingdom.', 8, '2021-05-17 18:59:15', '2021-08-11 05:54:09', NULL),
(2567, 674, 917, 'the three main people involved are 1. Paul 2. Philemon and 3. Onesimus\r\nColossian 4:9 indicates that Philemon\'s slave Onesimus was a resident of Colosse. Onesimus lived with his master Philemon at the time Colossians was written , Philemon must also have lived in Colosse. we read it in Philemon 19 You owe me your very self. \r\nPaul meant that he had brought Philemon to faith although it is possible that he had literally saved Philemon\'s life in some or other way.  Philemon owed Paul a great debt.\r\nThe Bible doesn\'t explain how Paul came to Philemon, but when we read Philemon verse 7 , he had refreshed the hearts of other believers, verse 17 says that Philemon was a great partner in Paul\'s ministry and Philemon was the host of the local church m verse 2. from these verses we can clearly make out that Paul knew Philemon to be a good man  and they knew each other well. \r\nfrom Philemon verse 11, we read that Onesimus was the slave of Philemon , when was useless to Philemon.  and when we read further verse 18, many interpreters say that Onesimus  might have stolen from Philemon or might have cause a severe loss to Philemon. And his failures , negligent angered his master Philemon, so much so that Onesimus had greatly feared punishment from Philemon and so in order to avoid his punishment, Onesimus left Philemon\'s household. later on Onesimus meets Paul, who shares the gospel to Onesimus and he was changed by the love of Christ, and Paul called Onesimus as son. And he writes letter to Philemon pleading to accept Onesimus back as he is a changed person now.', 9, '2021-05-24 17:48:53', '2021-08-11 05:53:34', NULL),
(2568, 674, 918, 'Onesimus was one of such type of people who refuse to accept their responsibilities and fulfill their obligations . and this negligent angered his master Philemon ,so much s that Onesimus had greatly reared punishment from Philemon . and to avoid this punishment Onesimus left Philemon household.\r\nwhen we read Philemon 11 , formerly he was useless to you, here Paul says that Onesimus had proven useless. when we read verse 18, Onesimus might have causes significant loss to Philemon or might have stole something from Philemon. he had the right to be angry with Onesimus and under Roman law, masters had right to punish slave severely even with heavy beatings. when we read verse 15, apparently Philemon himself had not intended for Onesimus to leave and probably did not approve of his leaving. Paul suggested that Paul had a positive reason for allowing the situation. through this time of separation God changed Onesimus so that he became a great benefit to Philemon. so this is the problem compounded by the tension between Onesimus and Philemon which Paul resolved to solve through the letter', 8, '2021-05-24 18:01:29', '2021-08-11 05:53:34', NULL),
(2569, 674, 919, 'Onesimus was seeking to start a new life  far from Philemon and encountered Paul only accidently.  Paul wrote to \r\nPhilemon , only after Onesimus endeared himself to Paul. in Paul\'s letter to Philemon he indicated that he had converted Onesimus to Christianity and Onesimus had  ministered to Paul in prison. Paul defended Onesimus only after  he had proven himself to Paul . since he stayed with Paul long enough to secure his advocacy, it suggest he had fully intended to seek Paul\'s help in this situation.  Onesimus was changed man now,  and it was appropriate that he return to Philemon , so he left for Colosse Paul\'s letter of advocacy. Paul\'s letter to Philemon , legally Onesimus might have remained with Paul without becoming a fugitive,\r\nPaul sent Onesimus back to Philemon for their reconciliation to be accomplished through a face to face meeting in which Onesimus repented and asked for Philemon\'s forgiveness and in which Philemon graciously forgave and accepted Onesimus and to be reconciled as brothers in Christ.', 9, '2021-05-24 18:18:23', '2021-08-11 05:53:34', NULL),
(2570, 674, 920, 'there are six aspect of Paul\'s petition \r\n Paul as advocate : Philemon 8-10\r\nPaul wanted Philemon to respond to this situation with genuine Christian Love , asking him to have compassion on an elderly man in prison and on the newly converted brother\r\nOnesimus as petitioner Philemon 11-13\r\n Onesimus had been a worthless slave but he had been converted to Christ , repented of his sibs and mended his ways, showing his  good faith in his effort to care  for Paul in prison\r\nPhilemon as master, verse 14\r\nPhilemon\'s past love an d faithfulness to the church encouraged Paul to think that Philemon would be loving and faithful to Onesimus as well\r\nGo as ruler\r\nPail reminded Philemon as God\'s role as providential ruler inverse 15 and 16. he suggested that in this case, god had orchestrated events to bring Onesimus and Philemon into conflict in order that Onesimus would be forced to seek Paul\'s advocacy\r\nPetition he appealed to Philemon for  a personal favor as if Paul himself were the one who needed Philemon\'s grace, he hoped that Philemon would extend mercy to Paul s spiritual son Onesimus\r\n   confidence\r\nPaul ended with the statement of confidence in verse 21, Paul expresses  his belief that Philemon would do as the apostle asked', 9, '2021-05-24 18:33:47', '2021-08-11 05:53:34', NULL),
(2571, 674, 921, 'in Philemon 8-10 we read that Paul was an apostle of Christ, he had the  authority to order Philemon to do the right things m but instead he wrote to Philemon in ways that elicited Philemon\'s sympathy and concern. Paul wanted Philemon to respond to this situation with genuine Christian love. \r\nhe adopted this strategy because he wanted Philemon to remember that God loves and forgives all believer sand he has encouraged him to reflect the same love towards one another patiently bearing with one another. \r\nI find it easier to make demands than appeal for mercy. because convincing some one to be merciful is really a tough job. but I shall choose to select the tougher path in appealing for mercy because that would definitely need some patience and kindness, but in the end to follow Christ\' way of compassion shall be fruitful till eternity', 8, '2021-05-24 18:45:49', '2021-08-11 05:53:34', NULL),
(2572, 675, 922, 'Paul had planted the church in Philippi during his second missionary journey. Paul\'s activities in Philippi are recorded in Acts 16:12-14\r\nit was in Philippi that Paul gained his first convert in Europe the merchant woman Lydia and in Philippi he was jailed for an exorcism performed on a slave girl and the well known Philippian jailor professed faith in Christ. Even when he left the city, the Philippians Christians supported Paul sending him monetary gifts at various times , when he was in financial need. Philippians 4:15-16\r\nthe Church in Philippi loved Paul and they regularly helped Paul with financial gifts. Philippian 4:10,18\r\nalthough they were poor, when they had an opportunity, they helped Paul.\r\nPhilippians loved Paul , he felt string affection for them as well. He loved them for their commitment to the Lord. they were his close friends , people whose fellowship he enjoyed and whose presence he missed. Philippians 1:4-8\r\n\'In Philippians 2:12, 4:1 Paul referred to the Philippians as his dear friends. so we conclude that both Philippians and Paul were deeply bonded with each other', 8, '2021-05-31 17:45:15', '2021-08-11 05:52:32', NULL),
(2573, 675, 923, 'when we know the background of Philippians we understand that Paul had\r\n1. a good relationship with the Philippians . \r\nPaul planted the church in Philippi and we come to knw that both the Philippian Christians and Paul had deep bond of love  with each other. they helped him always.\r\n\r\n2. suffering in prison:\r\n then when we read the background that Paul was suffering in prison and while he was suffering he wanted the people to face the sufferings of life in similar he wanted them to be joyful \r\nPhilippian 1:20\r\n3.conditions in Philippi: \r\nconcern for Paul. \r\nthe church in Philippi had a strong loving relationship with the apostle Paul and when they heard about this sufferings in prison, they were dismayed and worried about him. they worried for his life and out of deep concern for the apostle, the Philippians devoted themselves for prayer on Paul\'s behalf\r\n5. problems for the church:\r\n then when we understand the background, we come to know that there were some problems in the church\r\na) they appear to have faced persecution from those outside the church\r\n they struggled with conflicts between one another in the church\r\n they were threatened by the possibility of false teaching', 9, '2021-05-31 18:01:03', '2021-08-11 05:52:32', NULL),
(2574, 675, 924, 'types of exhortations to persevere:\r\n1. the importance of perseverance Philippian 1:27-2:18\r\nThe Philippians suffering were distressing and painful, but they were not beyond God\'s control, on the contrary god himself had planned  their suffering as the means of blessing them and so it was vital that they persevere through theses difficult times. \r\n\r\n2. the help for perseverance that ministers provide  2:19-30 \r\nPaul offered Philippians practical help for persevering through the hardships they were enduring by sending ministers to care for them. Paul knew that his letter would teach the Philippians how to handle suffering but also understood that it is much easier to endure suffering when we have real people helping us in our daily basis and suffering right alongside.\r\nPaul\'s own example pf perseverance 3:1-16\r\nPaul offered himself as a positive example of perseverance on the faith.  Paul had earthly benefits but did not rely on it, but depended only on Christ\'s merit which God credited to him by means of faith.\r\nhe also made it clear merely professing faith is not sufficient to guarantee our salvation, we must also persevere.\r\ninstructions regarding challenges to perseverance 3:17 to 4:9\r\nPaul encouraged the Philippians not to allow false teachers or conflict within the church or personal hardships to cause them to falter in their faithfulness to God.', 9, '2021-05-31 18:17:19', '2021-08-11 05:52:32', NULL),
(2575, 675, 925, 'the structure and content of Paul\'s letter to the Philippians has six main sections:\r\n1. the salutation  in 1:1-2\r\n2. the thanksgiving 1:3-8\r\n3. Prayer for Philippians \r\n4main body of the letter\r\n final greetings:\r\nsalutation:\r\nit identifies Paul as the primary author of the letter and states that the letter also comes from Timothy\r\n2. thanks giving:\r\n it presents a statement of thanks , speaking of the joy Philippians have brought to Paul and of his expectations for their ultimate salvation\r\n3. Prayer\r\nPail offered a prayer for the Philippians 1:9-11. he prayed that the Philippians would express their Christian love by living in ways that honored God\r\n4. body\r\nwhen Paul wrote to the Philippians he was suffering greatly and his very life was in jeopardy, he was beset by troubles and anxiety.\r\nbut even in his suffering he encouraged Philippians to suffer joyfully and to persevere in faith and holy living. he wanted them to rejoice in his blessings.\r\nfinal greetings:\r\nhe lastly closes the letter with Paul\'s greetings in Philippians 4:21-23', 8, '2021-05-31 18:31:37', '2021-08-11 05:52:32', NULL),
(2576, 675, 926, 'As Paul exhorts Philippians to persevere in troubles but he send ministers to the churches to lighten their burdens. encouraging each other through words and deeds is important in the timing of suffering. \r\n yes many times we as a missionary family have been comforted by many people in our ministry. many times we have been financially helped in tough times and more than that during the death of our elder son, we were comforted by the love of our people we were ministering to in Delhi, India.\r\nIt was a very tough time for us, but the love of the people, especially my believer sister Serina and  her continuous presence, encouraged us greatly. and the tougher darker days were shorted and lighter with their comforting words and deeds. it made us understand the what impact our presence or words can make in the lives of others when they go through similar situations.\r\nit kept us on the track and kept us mentally, spiritually sound and as time passed we persevered it all by His grace and came back stronger in our lives than ever. now we are blessed with two more children, but the love of the people help us persevere in all this.', 9, '2021-05-31 18:43:48', '2021-08-11 05:52:32', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2577, 677, 962, 'Here Paul touched on the inspiration of the scriptures when he said that \"all scripture is God\'s breathed \"the holy scripture that paul had in mind here and that Timothy had know From infancy were not the new testament ,but the old testament o, of course many dimension of new testament theology are quit simple but Paul made it clear that understanding scripture is not always easy ,Timothy was to be a worker who correctly handle the word of truth ,Pauls metaphors indicats that grasping the theology of the new testament ofen required hard work ,i am motivated to study word work hard to learn and study word of God so that i can handle word of God in right way , by hard working i can learn about the right context of each word so that interpretation will be right and the proper message i can convey .scripter is God\'s voice ,if we know wat was God\'s message we must meditate and learn the custom ,history purpose of the writing and all, for that studing and hard working is important .', 8, '2021-06-01 01:45:56', '2021-08-11 05:51:52', NULL),
(2578, 677, 963, 'Throughout the history people claiming to be followers of Christ have had different understanding of what it means to say that the new testament was inspired or breathed out by God ,some people belives holy spirit inspired biblical writers in the same way the secular poets musicians might be moved to wright .they think new testament consist only of the personal reflection and opinion of its human rights .\r\n  Most Evangelical christians believe in Organic inspiration .it means impossible to seprate the work of God\'s spirit and the work of the human authors of scripture .according the view the Holy spirit moved human authors to write and supervised and directed their words ,as a result the word of scripture are the word of God ,at the same time the holy spirit uesd the personalities ,experiences ,outlook and intentions of human authors as he guided their writings ,so the word of scripture is very much the word of its human authors .\r\nthe organic inspiration what we really mean is that the scripture hasn\'t been droped from heaven into our laps and the writers were some kind of automation .', 8, '2021-06-01 02:02:29', '2021-08-11 05:51:52', NULL),
(2579, 677, 964, 'The new testament was written during the during the time when God;s people were transitioning between old and new testament faith ,for this reason many of the issues adressed in the new testament ,for them it was a big task ,people who were following the tradition and some were form paganism ,there was many of the foundational theologis issues ,new testament authors was tringing to teach the people about the present situation ,what God did for the salvation how sacrifican is not effective for the salvation, what was the need of curcumsition in old testament and why we no need to follow that , that all the old covenant ended with christ death, the same way in our time many words implication is not possible as it is in the scripture because somes are according to old culture and some acording to jewish culture ,so here we can follow the word and take the message acording ot the circumstances that we have ,', 9, '2021-06-01 02:19:16', '2021-08-11 05:51:52', NULL),
(2580, 677, 965, 'We all know the commen experience that people aren\'t exactly the same ,even people who live in the same culture are different ,the fact that bible is written 2000 years ago is relevant and important because it was written in a culture at a particular time that fact that God\'s word is what make it relevant to us today ,because God chose to speak by his grace and mercy to us careful study can help us with continue and discontinue between ourslfe and new testament ,there are many ephocal continues between ours and day of new testament one of the best way to see these connections is to realize the christians today serve the same God that followers did inthe first century , but discontinue is in new testamnt time church was bilt on then foundation  apostels and prophets with christ jesus him self, but its discontinue now , so many things in our age are different from the new testament age ,', 8, '2021-06-01 02:35:13', '2021-08-11 05:51:52', NULL),
(2581, 677, 966, 'Continues an discontinues between today and new testament age are different ,in the new testemant age evangelism wasin a different way ,it was according to their situation , when they were evangelizing situation o nthe jerusalem and entier israel was different every evangelist in in this age must know about it , other wise if we try to follow the same pattern wich they follow we will fail in evangelism , another thing was culture ,according to time change we devoloped new culture some thisng is still continues but many culture and traditiond change if we are not aware about it this will not help us in evangelism ,many of the culturalmout look in our day and in new testament day are vastly different ,and we have to work very hard to work overcome the obstacles they present to interpreting and applying new testament theology .', 9, '2021-06-01 02:44:39', '2021-08-11 05:51:52', NULL),
(2582, 678, 967, 'Passage like 1 chro.29:11 and Timo 6:15 speak of the entire creation as God\'s kingdom because God always rules and always will rule all that hw has made .as God enthroned in heaven ,he receives reports ,hears prayers ,delibreats make plans and issues royal decrees .he hides spiritual creatures tondo his bidding on earth ,but when ever hard situations happeneds in the earth people used to think where is God when its hurts or when its happens , especially problems like disaster ,persicutions ,sickness and etc, but my responds to this issue is many things are man made in this world God even had unwavering sovereiginity over all things but still God given an option to chose good and bad , and still God demanding people will trust him more .God has given this world to men but if we distroy his creation the nature will respond that will comes as disaster ,and God put a living style and how we all are responsible to protect our body and have a orderd life style otherwise sickness will effect, many things are man made problem when men rebbel to God\'s creation .', 8, '2021-06-02 01:47:34', '2021-08-11 05:50:46', NULL),
(2583, 678, 968, 'New testament theology emphasize that Jesus\'s stratagy was two fold in the inagurations of kingdom on one side he inflicted the judgment of God on God\'s spiritual enemies .throughout his ministry Jesus disempowered evil spirit by casting them out of their positions of power .The defeat of God\'s spiritual enemies was so important to christ\'s inagural work .through his death Jesus broke the power saitan had over human beings ,demonic power and authorities lost their position of prominence when Jesus set his people free from the dominion of sin by dying on the cross .\r\nthis is our hope that the same way when christ will come to take his church to heaven as he promised  now here in the earth curch having lots of struggles ,saitan is always disturbing the church , but one day agin God will punish him and God will save the church for ever ,that is our hope , if we stay and be faithful and trust in God he will fullfil his faithful promis to us .', 8, '2021-06-02 02:03:20', '2021-08-11 05:50:46', NULL),
(2584, 678, 969, 'God\'s deliverance is a blessing of his people always ,as we learn in the new testament Jesus miracle was an example for his deliverance , in his time many people were struggiling from nay sickness ,demons attack and many other poverty an all, but in many Place jesus did miracles an deliverd them ,these happened in every time still in my life , there was many stuggles in my life which was always pulling me to down fron grow up, some poverty , finacial crisis , and many other issues , but as we trust in God gradually he made our journey easy ,he delived me from the struggles , even when we are suffering from some serious sickenses we canot even goand share the word of God to anyone , because if we still having problems and what hope we will share to the people so we ask the deliverance to God and he deliverd us and we share the testamany to the people .', 8, '2021-06-02 02:13:45', '2021-08-11 05:50:46', NULL),
(2585, 678, 970, 'The biblical out look will help us to live a faithful life because God give a faithful promis to fallen humanity , when God created human was perfect ,he has always communication with Adam and Eve but man kind did sin aganest God after sin creation and human race under curse ,but God chose Abraham and his descendants to full fill the kingdom commission he had first given to Adam and Eve. God gave a Promis land to Abraham\'s decendend , in the time of moses and Josua God furtherd the Israelites privilages and responsibility by giving them victory over the canaaites .and throgut the Israel\'s history God was faithful to them , but his demand was be faithful to him, its indeed to be fithfuil to God ,if Israel is not faithful they will sruggle it was God\'s commandment , the same way there are many chans and opertunity to go away from God , if so God will punish , so Many biblical examples giving a message to us how should be faithful to God in fallen world.', 9, '2021-06-02 02:28:14', '2021-08-11 05:50:46', NULL),
(2586, 678, 971, 'New testament declaration about Jesus this is an announcement that the \"king has come \"here the Lord ship declared ,and scripture telling that he is king, a royal priest who will established the kingdom , and rule over the world ,he came to the world to inagurate the kingdom of God , bible mensioning him as king ,means the one who have authority over everithing , and the one who are able to order ,in our evangelism minsitry we can use the term king , and we can prove it he is able to save you and punish you, becaus eking haas authority over every human being ,\r\nmany time his name used as \"savior\" that implicat Jesus name can save you when from the wrath of God , if we use his name as an evangelical tool that vwill be efective ,becaus his name it self have power to save people .', 8, '2021-06-02 02:40:14', '2021-08-11 05:50:46', NULL),
(2587, 679, 972, 'It isn\'t difficult to see that God chose covenant representative in old testament times. God deal with each of the people whom he chose ,but differently as he made his covenant .but all of the represent other before God in the judgements of God\'s heavenly royal court .but in the new testament Christ is the new covenant representative .God engaged him in special way on behalf of his Church every Jew and Gentile thatv God identifies with Christ .\r\nCovenent arragment help us to understand new testament in better way , Christ is the for a new covenant ,that those whonare called may receive the promised eternal inheritance ,the fact that Christ is the God\'s chosen covenant representative for the church helps us understand one of the most important characteristics of new testament theology .every fact of new testament theology is closely tied to the person and the work of Christ .so it is to explain how he intreceeding for us befor God , and no need to teach a sacrifice like old testament .', 8, '2021-06-03 01:44:01', '2021-08-11 05:50:17', NULL),
(2588, 679, 973, 'The new covenant comes comes because of the people failures in God\'s kingdom, people wikkedness and sin came before God Even he gave them an old covenant ,they failed to full fil it, so God put a new covenant with the people , he will forgive the ultimattly , their wickedness and sin remember no more ,God give a light ot the world that is Christ, Christ not came to the world for his own failure but for people sin, i deal with my failure through Christ , by him and through him i can enter in to the kingdom of God , in old testament God made many covenant with people but they failed but God forgave their sin and brig them to the light , still we have hope in christ is able to bring us to God from our failure .', 8, '2021-06-03 01:59:14', '2021-08-11 05:50:17', NULL),
(2589, 679, 974, 'God frequently called the nation of Israel to move beyond their outward association with his covenant and to circumcise their hearts .in other words they were to love him deeply by writing his law on their heart ,but in new covenant age God promised to intervene in such a way that this would be a reality for all of his covenant people .by this verse personally i encoraged to keep word of God in my heart , if i compare to isralites people having God covenant with them still they astray from God ,but God said keep his low in the heart so that u can be seprated from the world ,and from the sin, in new testament God deal with my heart not with sacrifies ,so keeping and writing his word in heart its help me in temptation in every curcumstances i faced, the promise console me ever time , its giving hope of change in life,', 8, '2021-06-03 02:10:20', '2021-08-11 05:50:17', NULL),
(2590, 679, 975, 'We should know how that covenant with God also entails test of loyalty ,these requirment of obedience reveal the condition of the hearts of those in covenant with God , we shouldn\'t surprised when scripture tells us that God tested the loyalty of his people in every biblical covenant ,including the new covenant .when we say that God test our loyality as part of life in new covenant ,we are obligated to be loyal to him, because sinner has never earned salvation by his own work , we will never reach the perfection needed to receive God\'s eternal blessings by our own efforts .we have to obey God because he loves us, some specific way to we can fulfil the covenant obligation , obeying him, loving him, be fathful to him with all joy,', 7, '2021-06-03 02:25:39', '2021-08-11 05:50:17', NULL),
(2591, 679, 976, 'In new testament ,Jesus enjoys the blessings of God throughout the continuation of his kingdom as he reigns over all of creation at the right hand of the father ,and will be blessed even more at the consumation of his kingdom when he receive his eternal inheritance of ruling over the new creation ,as true believer we will also receive our eternal inheritance , this is a God promis in christ , it will motivate us , because the salavation and the hevanly blessing is tottaly free and by grace , Christ did everithing for our salvation ,its motivate us to live for his , and we are happy to share this good news , God promised us to his heavenly blessings and inheritance through Christ ,even we are in the world having nothing but still we have a great blessings in Christ .it is our Hope .', 8, '2021-06-03 02:36:46', '2021-08-11 05:50:17', NULL),
(2592, 684, 922, 'Paul had planted the church in Philippi, during his second missionary journey. His activities in Philippi are recorded in Acts 16:12-40. He gained his first convert in Europe , the merchant woman Lydia. he performed an exorcism on a slave girl , for which he was jailed.\r\nthe well known Philippian jailor prophesied faith in Christ. Even when he left the city, the Philippian Christians supported him by sending him mandatory gifts, many times when he was in financial need.\r\nPhilippians 4:15-16 although , they were poor, when they had the opportunity, they gave to Paul.\r\nSo we realize that the Philippians loved Paul well, and he too had strong affection for them as well. he loved them for their commitment to the Lord. They were his close friends, whose fellowship  he enjoyed and whose presence he missed. Philippians 1:4-8 \r\n Philippians 2:12 and 4:11, Paul referred to the Philippians as his dear friends.', 8, '2021-06-14 17:39:55', '2021-08-11 05:48:54', NULL),
(2593, 684, 923, 'when we come understand the background , we come to know that there was a strong relationship between the Philippians and Paul. then we come to know about the sufferings of Paul in prison.\r\n when the church heard about his suffering in prison , they were dismayed and worried about him . when we come to know the background, we become aware of three types of problems  the church faced\r\nfirst, they faced persecution from outside the church,  secondly the possibility of false teaching and they struggled with conflict between one another in church. As Philippians were deeply distressed for Paul, fearing of even his death, they prayed for him and Paul had thanked them for their prayers and financial help . fearing about false doctrines, Paul had prepared them to reject any false teachings.', 8, '2021-06-14 17:51:13', '2021-08-11 05:48:54', NULL),
(2594, 684, 924, 'Paul \'s exhortations ahs four main topics:\r\n1. the importance of Perseverance1:27 to 2:18\r\nPaul says that God has planned the suffering as a means of blessing us , if we persevere in our troubles through faith and living righteously.\r\n2. the help for perseverance that ministers provide 2:19-30\r\nPaul offered the Philippians practical help for perseverance through the hardships they were enduring by sending ministers to care for them. Paul knew that real people helping us on a daily basis and suffering alongside is better, so he send ministers along with the letter.\r\n\r\n3. Paul\'s own example of perseverance 3:1-16\r\nPaul offered himself as a positive example of perseverance in the faith , both with regard to his mindset and behavior\r\n4. instructions regarding challenges to perseverance 3:17-4:9\r\nPaul encouraged the Philippians not to allow false teachers or conflict within the church or personal hardship to cause them to falter in their faithfulness to God. he focused on the ways that false teachings could invade the church and threaten its perseverance', 8, '2021-06-14 18:02:05', '2021-08-11 05:48:54', NULL),
(2595, 684, 925, 'the overall structure an content of Philippians have six main points\r\n1. salutation 1:1-2\r\n it identifies Paul as the primary author of the letter and states that the letter also comes from Timothy\r\n2 section of thanksgiving 1:3-8 . it presents a fairly standard statement of thanks, speaking of the joy to Philippians have brought to Paul.\r\n3. Paul\'s prayer for the Philippians 1:9-11. he offered a prayer that Philippians would express their Christian love by living in ways that honored God.\r\n4. the main body of the letter 1:12-4:20\r\na description of Paul\'s perseverance, his exhortations , his affirmations\r\n5. his perseverance\r\nPaul exhorted the Philippians by his example, he thanked them , he affirmed them\r\n\r\n6. Paul\'s final greetings 4:21 to 23\r\n the letter closes with final greetings, and specifically from Caesar\'s household', 7, '2021-06-14 18:13:03', '2021-08-11 05:48:54', NULL),
(2596, 684, 926, 'Paul send many friends in Philippi and he was encouraged by them. It is much easier to endure suffering when we have real people helping us on a daily basis and right along side us.\r\nYes being a minister , many have ministered to us in the time of  our needs, be it physical and financial or spiritual counselling.\r\nI would like to mention my college teacher Pastor P T Thomas who have impacted my life a lot from my college days. he guided us well in scriptures, patiently listen to our questions and then clearing our doubts. he has explained to us the basic and fundamentals matters of faith perfectly especially regarding perseverance. he has always encouraged us to bear the cross with pride and to endure all hardships for Christ with a smile on our face and joy in our hearts and hope in our words.\r\n so I in our ministry had been able to persevere  all sorts of troubles as much as possible with no complaints knowing that it will all be counted worthy one day.', 8, '2021-06-14 18:26:15', '2021-08-11 05:48:54', NULL),
(2597, 687, 866, 'As per John Oswalt, by 200 A D, Tertullius refers to what is called the old roman creed , which is almost identical to the Apostles Creed, the first reference to the Apostles creed is by man named Rufinus by 390 A D and he relates to the story that each of the twelve apostles including Mattiathias gave one of the affirmations in the creed. the writings of the early church fathers indicate that written rules of faith summarized the beliefs and practices of the churches  that produced them. Origen included a rule of faith in the beginning of his work On First principles. Rules of faith like this were intended to preserve the teachings of the church were used to train its people, especially its leaders. theses rules varied from congregation to congregation. the ancient creed that rose to prominence in this manner was the creed from the church in Rome often called the Roman Creed. this is so similar to the Apostle creed, so that many scholars believe the Apostle creed to be simply a later version of the Roman creed', 7, '2021-06-22 17:47:38', '2021-08-11 05:47:55', NULL),
(2598, 687, 867, 'purpose of creation and use of the Apostles creed:\r\nscripture doctrine:\r\nthe early churches believed that whole Bible was Christ\'s word. the early church relied strongly on scripture as the basis for all its doctrine.\r\ntraditional teachings:\r\nfalse teachers created many problems in the church, in response to these circumstances, godly Christian leaders created short summaries of the central teaching of scripture, so that all Christians would know and affirm the basic content of the faith.\r\nthe antiquity of errors which means the errors that had crept into the church a long time ago, it was the problem of human fallibility that made it important for the church to record its rule of faith in written form.\r\nOrigen and other early church fathers wrote down the Church\'s rule of faith to make sure that Christians throughout the world would be bale to compare the doctrines to the traditional ones\r\nApostles creed:\r\nit was largely used to confirm the new Christians believed the fundamental teachings of the bible. since many congregations had different creeds, there was sense in which they also had different minimum standards of beliefs, so the early church recognized the need for a creed that would be acceptable to every congregation that called itself Christians. the apostles creed rose to prominence in order to fulfill this need.', 8, '2021-06-22 18:18:45', '2021-08-11 05:47:55', NULL),
(2599, 687, 868, 'Apostles creed is divided into three main parts , each of which begins with the statement \" I Believe\"\r\nthe first session speaks of God the Father, the second session is about belief in Jesus Christ , his only son our Lord and third section summarizes belief in the Holy Spirit.\r\nin all cases, however the division of the creed, according to the persons of God has been universally recognized by the church, this formula is Trinitarian, that is its based on the the belief that there is only on god and this God exist in three persons, namely the persons of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. the Trinity is commonly stated this ways- god has three person but only one essence.\r\nby the term person it means distinct, self ware personality, by essence God\'s fundamental nature of the substance of which he consists.\r\nFather:\r\nthe creed  ascribes to him the attribute of being almighty, and mentions that he created heavens and earth\r\nChrist:\r\nthe creed mentions Jesus incarnation, his life on earth, suffering, death , burial and resurrection and ascension into heaven and his role as the one who will judge all humanity on the last day.\r\nHoly spirit; the creed says that He cause the virgin baby to become pregnant with Jesus, other than that it merely affirms its existence as distinct person of God', 8, '2021-06-22 18:33:25', '2021-08-11 05:47:55', NULL),
(2600, 687, 869, 'When the Creed says that \"I Believe in the church, it means that we affirm the belief that church is both holy, sanctified and catholic meaning universal and we affirm our belief that there is a communion of saints that is the fellowship of believers that historic Christianity has insisted on the importance of the church.\r\nParticipation:\r\nit emphasizes the importance of participation in the church. scriptures teaches that church is important and necessary for Christians, those who originally use the Apostles Creed , they used it in church services, they came to be joined to its numbers, to be included in the gathers to be baptized, this is the model that the creed puts forth for us to follow. it means Christians have spiritual communion with each other through Christ and his Spirit and our community is to be like a family.\r\nPreservation:\r\n affirming the fact that Christ appointed his church in part to protect and to proclaim the gospel and other truths. As per Jude part of the church\'s job is to contend for the faith, to protect the truths and beliefs that have been entrusted to it against those who promote false teachings and practices.\r\n we have to keep trying to preserve doctrine in the church', 8, '2021-06-22 18:46:15', '2021-08-11 05:47:55', NULL),
(2601, 687, 870, 'Foundation is the base on which the rest of the building is created . it is the anchor that holds the building  firmly and that provides strength and stability for entire structure . In Ephesians 2:19-21 Paul spoke of the church as building founded on the Apostles and prophets. without a solid true foundation, the church could not be build in a way that honored God.\r\nChristian theology must be founded on true doctrines and principles , if its is to honor God and useful to his people. Jesus is the cornerstone of the church, His teachings are the Cornerstone of theology. Apostles creed is foundational to theology because, it introduces as to the teachings of the apostles recorded in future', 8, '2021-06-22 18:53:13', '2021-08-11 05:47:55', NULL),
(2602, 690, 871, 'Christianity has always placed such a strong emphasis on knowing the God of the Bible, that means gods of other religions are imposters or false gods. Christianity says that Cod is the only true God , who has the power to create , to destroy and to save.\r\nThe singularity of God , the fact that He is the only god that exist , God\'s singularity , the doctrine that the God of the Bible is the one and only true God.\r\nsingularity:\r\n when Polytheism is the belief in multiple gods and atheism is the belief that no gods exist, as Scripture teaches  all people know deep down in their hearts that universe could not possible have without the hand of a divine creator. \r\nPaul even went so far as to say that we refused to glorify him or to give him thanks.\r\nFrom old Testament , we come to see that in Psalms 86:10 you alone are God, 2 kings 19:10You alone , On Lord are God, and in New Testament we read There is only one God. \r\nfrom all these verses we assure that there is only one true God.\r\nChristianity beliefs is the Trinitarian formula that God the Father, Jesus Christ his only son and the Holy Spirit are three different persons in same Godhead.\r\nBible insists that only the Christian God is truly divine, only the Christian god will judge the world, and only the Christian God has the power to save us.', 9, '2021-06-29 17:52:01', '2021-08-11 05:53:02', NULL),
(2603, 690, 872, 'when we speak of God\'s simplicity, it is his essence that we have in mind, His fundamental nature , the substance that make up his being. God is simple in the sense that his essence is nota composite of different substances, but a unified whole consisting of only one substance\r\nGod has a simple ,unified essence, they were denying that the Father, Son and holy spirit were three gods that somehow joined together in the trinity. three persons had always exist together as only one God.\r\nwe believe in only one God one divine being who exist in three persons. for example water is a pure substance, when we add dirt to water it becomes mud, which is a complex substance composed of two substances mud and dirt. God\'s essence is just like absolutely pure water, it is composed of only one substance.', 8, '2021-06-29 18:04:44', '2021-08-11 05:53:02', NULL),
(2604, 690, 873, 'two different perspectives of the trinity:\r\nontological trinity  : in this we focus on God\'s being, the world ontological means relating to being, we are looking at the trinity in terms of being or essence, how the three persons are integrated with one another and how they share a single essence.\r\nand economic trinity:\r\nthis is when we consider how God\'s persons interact with each other, how they relate to one another as individual persons. economic means relating to household management . in this view each persons has different responsibilities, different level of authority and a different assigned role with different tasks to perform. \r\nsimilarity in both ontological and economic perspective: the Father is said to be the first person, because the son is generated by the Father and the holy spirit is said to proceed from the Father.\r\nin economic trinity Father has authority over son and Holy spirit.\r\nthe son\'s kingship is subordinate to Father\'s Kingship', 8, '2021-06-29 18:19:48', '2021-08-11 05:53:02', NULL),
(2605, 690, 874, 'the Fatherhood of God has three main aspects :\r\ncreator:\r\nscripture sometimes refers to God as a being the Father of everything he has created . Deuteronomy 32:6, Luke 3:38\r\nas human fathers are patient towards their children God\'s general fatherhood over creation motivates him to show great patience with our fallen world and with sinful humanity in particular. \r\nKing:\r\nIn the ancient near east, it was common for people to refer to human kings as their father and for kings to refer to people as their children .  we see in Bible as Israelites referring to David as their father.\r\nwhen we read Mark 11\"10 and Acts 4:25-26, we see that God is our Father, he protects us a Father would. We also see it in Lord\' prayer\r\nFamily head:\r\nwhen we look in old Testament we see god leading His people like a parent or elder leading a family taking decisions for the family.\r\nGod is portrayed as the family head of the nation Israel. in Luke 11:11-13 we see Father answers our prayers in the same way the human fathers provide for their children , Father loves us as we have been born into His family Hebrew 12:5-10\r\n He disciples us like Father would do.\r\nchurch id also referred to as God\'s family and household.', 9, '2021-06-29 18:34:51', '2021-08-11 05:53:02', NULL),
(2606, 690, 875, 'God \'s authority as the creator:\r\nhis authority is absolute:\r\nthe fathers\' authority is absolute in the sense that He has complete freedom to do whatever he wants with his creation. Scripture often compares absolute his authority to a potter\'s authority over his clay\r\n Jeremiah 18:1-10\r\nExclusive: \r\nThe fathers\' authority as creator is exclusive in the sense that no creature possesses absolute authority. Absolute authority belongs only to the creator and God is the only creator. John 5:26, 27\r\nExhaustive: God\'s authority extends over everything he has created in every detail.\r\nfirst every one is under God\'s authority, no one and nothing is free from the obligation to obey God\r\nsecond, because God has created all things, no aspect of creation is morally neutral, that means everything has been created for a purpose and assigned it a moral character.', 9, '2021-06-29 18:47:26', '2021-08-11 05:53:02', NULL),
(2607, 691, 876, 'Jesus\' unique sonship is clear in the Gospel of John. Jesus is the eternal word of God meaning that He is both God himself and the only begotten of The Father. joihn8:18-23, Jesus said that as the Father\'s Son  he had come from above, that he had not originate din this world.\r\nJohn 10:30 where Jesus insisted that he and the Father are one/\r\nJoh 5:18 Jesus spoke of himself as God\'s Son, he meant that he was equal with God the Father. when Bible says the Jesus is the son of God, it means that he is both unique and divine. \r\nJesus \'s divine sonship is mentioned in Romans 1:3-4 and 8:3\r\nHebrews 1:1-3 says that as the Son of God, Jesus created the Universe and is the exact representation of the Father\'s being. \r\nJesus is identified as God\'s son  in a special way that indicates his eternal, divine nature.\r\nAs the Son of God, Christ is equal in power and glory to Father and the Holy Spirit. all three of God\'s persons- including the Son are infinite, eternal and unchangeable.', 8, '2021-07-08 17:43:47', '2021-08-11 05:46:16', NULL),
(2608, 691, 877, 'when the New Testament calls Jesus Lord, it is translating the Greek word Kurios which in English means Sir, which is used merely for human being in Mathew 10:24, Luke 12:36-37\r\nBut in romans 10:19, 13, Paul quoted Joel 2:32 in order to prove that everyone who calls on the name of Jesus will be saved . but in this verse from Joel in the Hebrew Old Testament , the name of the Lord was Yahweh, the proper name of God. \r\nwhen Paul said that Jesus is Lord, he meant that Jesus is Yahweh, the Lord and God of the old Testament.\r\nIn Matthew 3, Mark 1, Luke 3 and John 1, where Jesus is the Lord from Isaiah 40 whose path John the baptist has prepared. Hebrews 1:10, where the Lord Jesus is the God that Psalm 102:24-25 credit with creation the world.\r\nWhen we say in Apostles Creed, Jesus is Lord, we are affirming the biblical teaching that Jesus Christ is God, and that as God ,He is a full member of the Trinity, with the same divine attributes as the Father and the Holy spirit.', 7, '2021-07-08 17:59:57', '2021-08-11 05:46:16', NULL),
(2609, 691, 878, 'Several of Jesus\' Experiences prove that he was truly human because only human beings have such experiences\r\nGeneration:\r\nJesus was conceived, Jesus began in the same way that all human beings after Adam and Eve have begun : as tiny baby in his mother\'s womb . we read in Matthew 1:18,  Luke 2:5-6, Galatians 4:4 and Hebrews 10:5\r\nJesus could not have been truly human being because he did not have a human father. but the first humans also did not have a human Father. \r\nBody:\r\nAccording to accounts Of Jesus\' arrest and crucifixion in Mathew 27, Mark 15, Luke 23 and John 18-19, Jesus suffered under Pontius Pilate in a variety of ways. Jesus\' sufferings , crucifixion , death and burial demonstrated that he was areal man with a physical human body- one that could be beaten, one that could bleed , one that could be abused by soldiers.\r\nSoul:\r\nIt speaks of our souls by many different terms, but the most common are soul and spirit. Hebrews 4:12 and 1 Thessalonians 5:23\r\nLuke 23: 46 Jesus spoke of his own soul or Spirit as he was dying.\r\nResurrection:\r\nJesus was real Human being because it reaffirms  that his full, glorified human existence included the re union of his real human body with his real human soul. the resurrection of his body occurred when his human soul was introduced into his perfected human body.\r\n1 Corinthians 15:20-23', 9, '2021-07-08 18:16:49', '2021-08-11 05:46:16', NULL),
(2610, 691, 879, 'Christ is the Jesus\'s office rather than a title.\r\nit sis similar to king or judge.\r\nthe English translation Christ is the Greek word Christos and Hebrew word Mashiach or \"messiah\" meaning anointed one.\r\nin the Old testament, the term \"anointed one \"  was a broad term that could be applied to anyone whom god appointed to serve him in a way special capacity. !chronicles 16:22 identifies prophets as anointed ones\r\nin 1Samuel 26:9, 11, David referred to Saul as the anointed one, because he was the king of Israel.\r\none of the most important use is for descendants of David that served as kings. Psalms 89:3-4\r\nmany prophets foretold that god would send a great Messiah or anointed one in the future, he would be great king , descendant from David, he would restore and reunite the kingdoms of Israel and Judah.\r\nFulfillment in Jesus:\r\nthere are two passage sin John\'s gospel. where Jesus is called Messiah and when John explains that Messiah means the same things as Christ. John 1:41 and John 4:25-26. \r\nhe explained that the normal word for messiah in Greek was christos here translated in English Christ. next reason  is that only human son of David could be an atoning sacrifice for his people.\r\nHebrews 2:14\r\nthird the Messiah had to be human was that he had to be a second Adam.', 9, '2021-07-08 18:34:15', '2021-08-11 05:46:16', NULL),
(2611, 691, 880, 'Jesus has a human nature, we mean that he possesses all the attributes and traits that are essential to be humans- things like a physical human body and rational human soul.\r\nJesus is fully human in every respect: he has a body and soul, he was subject to sickness, injury and death, he had normal physical limitation and son on.\r\nJesus differ from human being in some important ways, for one things Jesus is a perfect human being, whereas the rest of us are flawed, every other human being has sinned Psalms 130:3 but Jesus is different , he was born without sin and lived a perfect sinless life.\r\nthe Bible specifically speaks of his sinlessness in passages like \r\nHebrews 4;14-15\r\n in the beginning God created humanity with the ability to sin, but we have to admit even before they sinned , they were still human, so it is possible to have human being that does nots sin. Sinfulness is not an essential attribute of humanity.\r\nthis is why we say that Jesus\'s human nature includes all the attributes and traits that are essential to being human.\r\nAnother thing that makes Jesus different is the fact that he sis the only person that possess two nature , a human nature and divine nature. every other human being has only one nature- human nature. but Jesus is both God and being both fully human and divine at the same time.\r\nthe technical term we used to define the existence of both human and divine nature in the person of Christ is Hypostatic union.', 9, '2021-07-08 18:49:22', '2021-08-11 05:46:16', NULL),
(2612, 692, 881, 'Apostles creed affirm the Holy Spirit\'s divinity in two ways:\r\nfirst its Trinitarian structure equates the Holy Spirit with the Father and the Son and in important ways. \r\nstructure:\r\nthe Trinitarian formula express the belief that there is only one God and that He exists in three persons, namely the person of the Father, the Son ad the Holy Spirit. the affirmation of the articles related to the holy Spirit and the third and final immersion. \r\nSecond the creed\'s description of Jesus conception indicates that the holy spirit is divine. \r\nJesus conception:\r\nJesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit. Luke 1:35\r\nThe Holy Spirit is equated to the power of the most high. only God can have the power of the Most High, so by alluding to this verse as an example of the holy Spirit\'s work the Apostles creed affirms the Spirit\'s full divinity.', 5, '2021-07-12 17:45:11', '2021-08-11 05:46:53', NULL),
(2613, 692, 882, 'the biblical basis for believing in the divinity of the Holy spirit in four ways:\r\n names:\r\n the Holy Spirit is called by  a host of names in Bible, the name that implies His divinity in the most implicit way is the name Holy Spirit. it is God who is repeatedly referred to as the Holy one. 2  Kings 19:22, God Himself is referred by the me Holy Spirit Isaiah 63:10,11\r\nthe other names include Spirit of the Lord, Spirit of God, Spirit of the living god, the spirit of Christ, the spirit of Jesus Christ, the spirit of your Father.\r\nacts 5:3-4\r\nAttributes:\'\r\nGod possesses communicable attributes, which he shares with his creatures for example God possesses the attribute of reason which we human beings also possess, but god possesses certain in communicable attributes which none of his creatures can possess like omnipresence, omnipotence and omniscience. so if we prove that Holy Spirit possess some of theses divine in communicable attributes , we can prove that Holy spirit is God.\r\nThe Holy spirit is proven to be god by His omnipotence 1Samuel 10:46\r\nWorks:\r\nthe third evidence is the type of work He does.\r\nHoly Spirit performs many works which is appropriate to God alone .\r\n1 He creates new life Romans 8:11\r\nhe applies salvation to us Romans 5-8\r\nHoly spirit inspired the writing of the scripture, in recognizing that the word of the Holy Spirit is the word of God, we acknowledge ,the holy spirit is God himself\r\nFormulas:\r\nTrinitarian formula, by mentioning the Father the Son and the Holy Spirit as equal partners, the bible indicates that the Holy spirit is just as divine as the Father and Son Romans 15:13', 7, '2021-07-12 18:03:21', '2021-08-11 05:46:53', NULL),
(2614, 692, 883, 'The Holy spirit has too many personal attributes :\r\nfour we will see\r\n1. the Holy spirit has a will, the faculty he uses to plan , choose and desire. any being that do these things cannot be mere power or force. 1 Corinthians 12:11. he wants to give people gifts, and others to have gifts. impersonal powers don\'t have plans and desires. therefor holy spirit is a person\r\n2. the Holy Spirit has the attribute of intelligence, through which he possesses knowledge and ability to teach others, by searching and knowing mind, he also gives wisdom. 1 Corinthians 2:10-12.imersonal forces don nit have intelligence or can think\'\r\n2. The holy spirt has emotions , internal feelings and affections. we can see His love in Romans 15:30. his joy in 1Thessalonians 1:6, his grief in Ephesians 4:30. impersonal forces don\'t have joy or grief\r\n3. The spirit has the attribute of agency. he has will the ability to intend things and to act on his own accord. the holy spirit offers testimony in Joh 15:26, he fellowships in Philippians 2:1\r\nthe Holy Spirit prays for believers, romans 8:26. no impersonal powers prays for anyone', 7, '2021-07-12 18:17:48', '2021-08-11 05:46:53', NULL),
(2615, 692, 884, 'the distinctions between the Holy Spirit and the other persons of the trinity can be see in the passages that show them interacting with one another in ways that emphasize their differences\r\n John 16:7 , in this passage Jesus says the Holy spirit would not come unless the Holy Spirit first went away. he distinguished himself from the Spirit by saying that one goes before the other comes. each had an appointed role and the role of the spirit would not begin unless Jesus had complete his earthly work and ascended into heaven. this makes it clearly that the Spirit is distinct from the Son.\r\nthe spirit also does things that distinguish from the Father, like his past as a counselor, the Holy spirt is our attorney, assisting our prayers by pleading for us.\r\nWhen Paul taught that the holy spirit intercedes on our behalf, with the Father, he demonstrated that they are distinct persons- one interceding and the other hearing the intercession.', 7, '2021-07-12 18:30:42', '2021-08-11 05:46:53', NULL),
(2616, 692, 885, 'ontological trinity:\r\nwe are focusing on God\'s being and existence. the Holy spirit is equal in power and glory to the Father and the son. all three of god\'s persons are infinite, eternal and unchangeable and has same essential divine attributes  such as wisdom, power , holiness , justice goodness and truth.\r\nEconomic Trinity:\r\nhow God\'s persons interact with each other. each person of Godhead had different responsibilities , different authority, and  different assigned role.\r\nThe spirit\'s authority is subject to the higher authority of the Father and the son. The spirit\'s role is to largely carry out their instructions and to bring them glory.\r\nin both trinity, the third person is Holy Spirit.\r\nin the ontological trinity he is the third person, as he is breathed out by the Father, who is the first person  and by the son , who is the Second person.\r\nin economic trinity he s the third person, as the Holy spirit is said to be the third person because he holds the third rank, being subordinate both to the father  and to the Son. Luke 11:13 holds the lowest rank from the perspective of the economic Trinity, even though he is still fully God and has absolute sovereign authority over every aspect of creation.', 8, '2021-07-12 18:48:20', '2021-08-11 05:46:53', NULL),
(2617, 693, 886, 'The new  Testament speaks many times  of the church using the Greek Word Ekklesia, in the Old Testament ekklesia is frequently used to identify the gathered nation of Israel Deuteronomy 9:10, 31:30, judges 20:2. even in the /new testament , where Ekklesia has become a technical term referring to the church , the word is still used to refer to the assembly of the Old Testament Israel. the Greek word translated assembly is ekklesia, which is more translated \"church\".1 Peter 2:9 , Peter also called the church by name  that applied to Israel in the Old Testament.\r\nIn Romans 11, Paul used two metaphors to talk about the relationship between the Old Testament assembly of Israel and the Christian church. He spoke of them as as a batch of dough, an an olive tree. Frist Paul said that the Old Testament assembly was the first-fruits of the same batch of dough from which the New Testament church had been made. Second Paul said that the Old Testament assembly was the root of the tree and that the New Testament church was the branches of this same tree. Old Testament church , consisting primarily of Jews, was the bulk of the tree :roots, trunk, and many branches.', 8, '2021-07-23 17:43:20', '2021-08-11 05:56:03', NULL),
(2618, 693, 887, 'Jesus only mentioned the church by the name ekklesia on three occasions recorded one in 16:18 and two in !8:17. the word oikodomeo translates as build, refer to building something brand new restoring. we see in romans 11\r\nin the context of Jesus ministry, the only church that existed was Jewish synagogues and the temple in Jerusalem , bust Jesus still called them the \"church\"\r\nIn Old Testament disputes , were handles by elders, Jesus affirmed this principle in his day to remind the audience that they are obligated to resolve the disputes within the assembly. in the minds of Jesus and Matthew, just like in the mind of Paul, the church of the New Testament was a development of Israel\'s Old Testament assemblies, Jesus did not come to replace Israel with the church, he came to rescue and restore Israel in the form of the New Testament church.\r\nFirst the New Testament presents Jesus as the fulfillment of god\'s promises to Israel. He is the culmination of the Old Testament church, its most perfect member and minister.\r\nand second Jesus is the founder of the New Testament church  , the one who brought the restoration and renewal that transformed the failing Old Testament church into New Testament.', 8, '2021-07-23 17:56:39', '2021-08-11 05:56:03', NULL),
(2619, 693, 888, 'Jesus ordained the New Testament church as the restoration and development of the Old Testament Israel, one important implication is that there is fundamental continuity between Israel and the Christian church, \r\nPurpose: In Genesis 1-3 , we read how God made the whole world and specially human beings with a mandate , but de to humanity\'s fall into sin , the entire world was corrupted.\r\nthe final sate of the period of redemption will be the new heavens and the modern church is still striving toward the goal of restoring creation\r\nBelievers and unbelievers:\r\nin the old testament the and the new testaments the church of God was never perfect. in the Old Testament, some ancient Israelites were faithful to god and revived God\'s blessing. but many others rebelled against god in unbelief and fell under his divine curses. same is with the followers of Jesus.\r\nPaul encouraged people in the church to examine themselves \r\nObligations :\r\nthe people of God  both the testaments were given the responsibility of loving God , spreading his kingdom throughout the world and bringing him glory .Old testament church is taught to Love God from heart to obey law from heart, similarly in New Testament church is called to love God and to obey his law\r\n secondly both  Testament churches are obligated to spread God\'s kingdom.\r\na third obligation shared by Old Testament Israel and the New Testament church was to glorify God.', 9, '2021-07-23 18:13:37', '2021-08-11 05:56:03', NULL),
(2620, 693, 889, 'We can define the visible catholic church as the one world wide fellowship of all people in covenant with god under the headship of Christ. the fellowship is one of the spirit rather than of church government. the unity of the visible church is based on the fact that each church is in covenant with the same god. and under the covenant leadership of the same Christ.\r\nthe marks theta enable Christians to distinguish genuine congregations of the visible catholic church :\r\n1 preaching of the word of God.\r\nGod has given the bible to the whole visible church and Holy Spirit is given to help us understand the bible. Hebrews 4:11-13\r\nThe second mark of the church is the right administration of the sacraments of baptism and the Lord\'s supper, these sacraments belong to the entire visible church not to just one denomination.\r\nthe third traditional mark of the visible catholic church is the formal church discipline, such as excommunication, the discipline is extended both to protect the church and to drive the offender to repentance. Matthew 16:19', 9, '2021-07-23 18:24:39', '2021-08-11 05:56:03', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2621, 693, 890, 'we can define the visible catholic church as one world wide fellowship of all people and covenant with God under the leadership of Christ. this fellowship of all people in covenant with god under the headship of Christ. this fellowship is one of the spirit rather than the church government. we can embrace was church if its a part of the visible church by looking at three traditional marks:\' \r\n first is the preaching of the word of God:\r\nGod ahs given Bible to the whole visible church and heh has given the whole visible church his holy spirit to help us understand the Bible, we read in Hebrews 4:11-13\r\nThe second mark of the church is the right administration of the sacraments of baptism and the Lords supper. these sacraments belong to the entire visible church not just to one denomination or another.\r\nthe third traditional mark of the visible church is the formal church discipline, such as excommunication.\r\nMatthew 13:24-30\r\n discipline is intended both to protect the church and to drive the offender to repentance.', 8, '2021-07-23 18:35:27', '2021-08-11 05:56:03', NULL),
(2622, 694, 891, 'problem and origin of sin:\r\nsin uses words like lawlessness, rebellion, transgression, offense, evil, missing the mark and a variety of other words to describe the things that are sinful. but when the scripture speaks of sin abstractly, it means to rise above the others: lawlessness. sin is most fundamentally a violation of God\'s law.  in Genesis 3, we see the account  when our fist parents Adam an Eve rebelled against God by eating the forbidden fruit of the tree of the knowledge of good an evil. it cause the entire human race to become guilty of sin. God created man good. we had the  ability an opportunity to sin. but even came under deception an Adam following her choose willfully to sin. but nether Satan\'s temptations and Eve\'s foolishness excused the sin of our forefathers. they were both guilty of choosing evil over good. their willingness to serve God and obey him was the real question , they failed in it. that\'s the problem of sin. \r\n if we love God, we choose to obey Him.\r\nConsequences of sin:\r\nafter Adam and Eve sinned God judged and cursed the entire human race. this curse effected every aspect of their being. It immediately produce spiritual death John 5:24-25\r\nit also produced corruption in our beings both body and soul Jeremiah 17:9\r\nit ultimately lead to physical death genesis 3:19\r\nsin finally earned humanity eternal suffering under God\'s judgement in hell\r\n the consequences also spread to entire human race Romans 3:9-12', 8, '2021-08-02 17:46:54', '2021-08-11 05:56:39', NULL),
(2623, 694, 892, 'Divine grace grace involves all three persons of the Trinity\r\nthe Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit\r\nForgiveness began with the father because he was the one that planned it. The Father sent the Son into the world and appointed him as the redeemer John 3:16\r\nthe Father authorized Jesus\' empowerment as the Redeemer of his people Joh 10:14-18\r\nthe Father who offered up Jesus as a sacrifice\r\nthe Father is the great architect of redemption.\r\nTh son\r\nJesus atoned for sin by dying on the cross in place of sinners. he received the divine curse that had been warranted by our sin. And his perfect righteousness was credited to our account, so that we would be counted not as sinners , but as obedient children of god.\r\nGalatians 2:20\r\nHoly Spirit\r\nthe Holy Spirit is the person of Trinity who actually applies forgiveness to our lives. our sins are actually not forgiven until the Holy Spirit does his work.\r\nthe spirit reconciles us to God by forgiving all the sins we have committed up to that point. He gives us new spiritual life by regeneration our Spirit. the spirit continues to applies forgiveness to us throughout our lives, he maintains our faith, that leads us to repentance and that continually applies forgiveness to us.', 9, '2021-08-02 17:58:06', '2021-08-11 05:56:39', NULL),
(2624, 694, 893, 'the re process of forgiveness ordinarily involves an element of individual responsibility.\r\nthe role of individual responsibility divided into two parts:\r\n1. conditions: first it speaks of faith in God as a prerequisite for forgiveness. Scripture often refers to this type of faith as the fear of god. the second ordinary conditions of forgiveness found in scripture is brokenness. brokenness is genuine sorrow over sin: the true regret over violating god\'s aw. David recognized that in order to receive God\'s forgiveness , he needed to share God\' perspective. God\'s mercy is given to those who forsake their sin\r\n we need to be live lives of constant faith and to be truly sorry forth sins we continue to commit , so that we can continue to receive forgiveness and cleansing on  a daily basis. \r\nbasis is always the merit of Christ, which he earned by his obedient life and sacrificial death on the cross.\r\nforgiveness is always earned , but it is earned by Christ. prayer is presented as an ordinary means for appealing to God for grace and forgiveness. the Bible commonly speaks of prayers of confession and repentance as expressions of faith through which the holy Spirit applies forgiveness to us.\r\nthe bible teaches that when we express our faith through the Lord\'s supper and baptism, the Holy Spirit uses these ordinances to apply forgiveness to our lives.', 9, '2021-08-02 18:12:20', '2021-08-11 05:56:39', NULL),
(2625, 694, 894, 'God created human beings to consist of physical bodies and non physical souls. Hebrews 4:12. human beings consists of only two main parts : body and soul. before our fall into sin, our bodies and our souls were unaffected by sin and its corrupting powers. but when Adam and Eve fell into sin, sin corrupted not only their souls, but also their bodies. genesis 3:19 our souls come into the world in a state that the bible describes as spiritual death, we are under God\'s judgement and have lost all ability to please him. this corruption results in physical hard ship, sickness and eventually death\r\nsin corrupts all that we are our entire being, body and soul. physical death is a  blessing to believers , but its tragic and its a universal experience, but its also unnatural. God did not create our bodies for death, he created us for life.\'', 8, '2021-08-02 18:23:18', '2021-08-11 05:56:39', NULL),
(2626, 694, 895, 'Redemption of our bodies in three stages :\r\nfirst the believers experience during our present life on earth\' \r\nsecond the intermediate state of our bodies and the third the new life resurrection\r\nPresent life:\r\nthe Bible actually teaches that the salvation pf our bodes begins with indwelling of the Holy Spirit when we first come to faith romans 8:9-11 .although it does not result in our immediate bodily resurrection , it does deals us with the guarantees of the fill redemption of our bides in the future,\r\nthe holy spirit\'s indwelling presence throughout our lives, he sets us apart for God and purifies through the process of sanctification.\r\nintermediate state:\'\r\n when we die our mortal bodies are temporarily separated from our soul, this stage called the intermediate stage- the state between our lives on earth now and the often lives we all have in the resurrection. during the intermediate state our souls dwell with Christ in heaven.\r\nMatthew 17:3\r\nPaul taught in Romans 6:2-11. for another our bodies lie in the grave in an unconscious state being incapable of nay thought , action or feeling whether good or bad. \r\nNew life:\r\n our bodies will receive new, perfect life when they are restored to life in the general resurrection . the consequence of sin will finally and forever be completed put away from us. Romans 8:23. just as Christ\' body underwent changes his resurrection our bodies will, also be changed .they will renewed and perfected, they will be immortal, glorious, powerful and spiritual.', 9, '2021-08-02 18:39:50', '2021-08-11 05:56:39', NULL),
(2627, 696, 917, 'three main people involved in Paul\'s letter to Philemon are \r\nPhilemon: Colossians 4-9 indicates that Philemon\'s slave Onesimus  was a resident of Onesimus was a resident of Colosse. Onesimus lived with his master Philemon at the time Colossians was written , Philemon must also have lived in Colosse. in Philemon 7, Paul spoke of the way Philemon had lovingly refreshed the hearts of other believers. Paul thought so highly of Philemon that in verse 17, he spoke of Philemon as his partners in gospel ministry.\r\nin verse Philemon as the host of the church.\r\nPhilemon had strong bond between them. in many other ways, we see that Philemon  owed Paula a great debt.\r\nOnesimus:\r\nthe second person was Onesimus, he was Philemon\'s slave, not clear what kid of slave.\r\nBecause Onesimus was Philemon\'s slave, he was a member of Philemon\'s household. but unlike his master, Onesimus was not a believer.\r\nOnesimus left Philemon\'s household to seek help from Paul.\r\nPaul expressed his love for Onesimus in Philemon 10-16\r\nwitnesses:\r\nthird is Paul himself\r\nso we see how Onesimus who is the slave of Philemon comes to prison with Paul and Paul transforms his life. Philemon was like son to Paul. but now Onesimus is also given birth in Christ so Paul is like father to Onesimus too.', 8, '2021-08-04 17:52:24', '2021-08-11 05:57:07', NULL),
(2628, 696, 918, 'we see that some servants are not good servants, and some that people refuse to accept their responsibilities and fulfill their obligations. Onesimus was one of these people, who was negligent and angered his master Philemon, so in order to avoid punishment, from Philemon, Onesimus ran from Philemon\'s household\r\nPhilemon 11\r\nformerly he was useless to you.\r\nmany interpreters understand this ways to imply that Onesimus had stolen from Philemon , which was a common crime among household slaves. Philemon had a right to be angry and Onesimus had a good reason to be afraid of Philemon. \r\nPhilemon himself had nit intended for Onesimus to leave and probably did not approve of his leaving.\r\nbut Paul suggested that God has  a positive reason for allowing the situation, through this time of separation. God changed Onesimus so that he became  a great benefit to Philemon. the initial problem in Philemon\'s household was the Onesimus had caused some loss to Philemon .\r\nSo Paul tried to resolve the matter by writing a letter to Philemon by making understand and forgive Onesimus by pleading to him.', 8, '2021-08-04 18:02:36', '2021-08-11 05:57:07', NULL),
(2629, 696, 919, 'Paul wrote to Philemon only after Onesimus had endeared himself to Paul. in his letter he indicated that he had converted Onesimus to Christianity. Paul defended Onesimus only after Onesimus had prove himself to Paul.\r\nPaul preached gospel to Onesimus and he saw genuine conversion in him, repentance of sins. once Onesimus had gained Paul\'s favor, so it was appropriate that he return to Philemon, . so Onesimus left  for Colosse bearing Paul\'s letter of advocacy. According to Paul\'s letter to Philemon , legally Onesimus might have remained with Paul without becoming a fugitive.\r\nPaul sent Onesimus back to Philemon because he wanted any gift from Philemon to be voluntary rather than compulsory and because he wanted Philemon and Onesimus to be reconciled to be brothers in Christ.', 8, '2021-08-04 18:12:48', '2021-08-11 05:57:07', NULL),
(2630, 696, 920, 'Six aspects of Paul\'s petition verse 8-21. \r\n1. Paul \'s role as advocate:\r\nPaul was a apostle of Christ, he had the authority to order Philemon to do the right thing. he wrote to Philemon in ways that elicited Philemon\'s sympathy and concern.\r\n2. Onesimus as petitioner\r\n the Onesimus that Paul described here was a very different man from the one who had come to asking for a mediator.\r\n3. Philemon as master:\r\n\r\nPaul acknowledge Philemon \'s authority over Onesimus and revealed his own motivation for making a n appeal to Philemon instead of commanding him\r\n4. God as ruler verse 25nd 16\r\nPaul reminded Philemon of god\'s role as providential ruler. God orchestrated events to bring Onesimus and Philemon into conflicts in order that Onesimus would be forced to seek Paul\'s advocacy.\r\n5. Petition:\r\nhe appealed to Philemon for a personal favor, as if Paul himself were the one who needed Philemon\'s grace.\r\n6. confidence:\r\nPaul ended with a statement of confidence in verse 21. Paul had confidence that Philemon would grand his request  first Philemon respected and loved Paul .second Philemon loved the church, Onesimus had just joined.', 9, '2021-08-04 18:27:46', '2021-08-11 05:57:07', NULL),
(2631, 696, 921, 'Paul adopted the strategy  of reconciliation rooted in forgiveness and mercy. creating unity and love where hostility existed before is very important and Christ like.\r\nit  it assumes that there is a source of conflict between us, but that we have put aside the conflict in order to pursue something better, namely, mutual peace with one another, mutual love for one another and mutual ministry to on another.\r\nit is easier to appeal for mercy. making demands is very difficult to be met with . but to ask for mercy pleading the love and mercy of Christ.\r\nbecause we are united in Christ, we all are forgiven and blessed. so we have no basis for resenting or refusing to be reconciled to any believer.', 8, '2021-08-04 18:36:53', '2021-08-11 05:57:07', NULL),
(2632, 697, 619, 'two types of evidence that strongly suggest \r\nExplicit:\r\n in acts 1:1 here the writer spoke of his former book meaning that acts is the second of at last two volumes. he also indicated that he wrote this book to  a person named Theophilus Luke 1:11-4\r\nboth Acts and the third gospel are dedicated to Theophilus and the book of facts refer to a former book. these facts provide a strong evidence that the author of these books produced at least two volumes with eth gospel of Luke being the first volume and acts being the second volume.\r\nImplicit\r\nLuke 1:1-4states that the author had investigated a variety of sources and had made a s orderly account dedicated to Theophilus. A number of scholars have noted that the accounts in Luke\'s Gospel and the book facts are ordered and shaped in similar ways. there are also similarities in the compositional structure in similar ways of the books.  and there is a similar chronological length in each book . Both Luke and Acts cover roughly the same number of years. and there are parallel themes between the books as well.', 8, '2021-08-09 17:44:52', '2021-08-11 05:57:37', NULL),
(2633, 697, 620, 'the evidence for common authorship in Acts and the gospel of Luke, we are ready \r\nthe early church testified that Luke , the travelling companion of Paul, was the author of both Acts and the gospel of Luke.\r\nManuscripts:\r\none very manuscript referred to as Papyrus, on it was written includes some of our earliest New Testament manuscript evidence. it includes large portions of the gospel of Luke and the gospel of John. after the conclusion of the gospel of Luke the manuscript contains the words, evangelion lkata loukan or the gospel of Luke\r\nsecond the Muratorian Fragment died around AD 170 to 180, is the earliest known document listing the New Testament books that early church considered to be canonical. after affirming Luke\'s authorship f the gospel of Luke, it explicitly points to him as the author of facts as well. early church leaders:\r\nthe church father Irenaeus, who lived from around Ad 130 to 202, lived that Luke was the author of the third gospel. here Irenaeus referred to acts as the book that recorded the gospel preached by Paul. Clemet of Alexandria who lived from around Ad 150 to 215 also referred to Luke as the author of facts\r\nand Tertullian who lived from AD 155to 230. \r\nTHE GREAT CHURCH HISTORIAN EUSEBIUS AD 323 mentioned Luke as the author of Luke as well as Acts. Luke has mentioned of the census in the acts.', 9, '2021-08-09 17:58:12', '2021-08-11 05:57:37', NULL),
(2634, 697, 621, 'Luke\'s awareness of the important Old Testament idea is evident of God\'s \r\nroyal Lordship over the earth. in Luke 14-15, Paul and Barnabas spoke of creation as then basis for God\'s rule over the creation. Luke 7:49, Stephen asserted that god had created the world to be his royal footstool. listen to Paul\'s words in Athens in acts 17:24-27\r\nLuke\'s awareness of humanity\'s fall into sin also comes to the foreground in the book of Acts. Luke wrote of misery of sin in many places throughout the acts. the book do Acts repeatedly illustrates that everything in creation- the physical world , our economic strictures,\r\nIsrael:\r\nfirst consider how God\'s choice of Abraham informed Luke\'s history. on the other hand , Abraham would father a great nation, become famous and receive many spiritual and material blessings. \r\nGod\'s call went far beyond what Abraham and his descendants would receives.\r\nLuke also focused on how Jewish Christians brought the gospel of Christ to the gentiles. in the second place , Luke\'s outlook in acts also showed his understanding of the relation ship between Moses and the Christian church .\r\nLuke was influenced by the Old Testament record of David\'s dynasty', 9, '2021-08-09 18:12:43', '2021-08-11 05:57:37', NULL),
(2635, 697, 622, 'there is a deep connection between the Jews and Christians\r\n1. Jews was Jewish and the so were the apostles, and the first church itself consisted of entirely Jewish converts. so the mind of the early church , loyalty to the promised Jewish Messiah implied a certain faithfulness to Judaism,. as per the book  of Acts, many people in the early church attended temple worship , met in synagogues to hear the scriptures and  maintained appreciation for many Jewish customs.\r\nthe early church Jewish community at large were both committed to the same scriptures. \r\nfundamental differences:\'\r\nChristians and unbelieving Jews disagreed over the person and work of Jesus. the church proclaimed that Jesus was the Messiah who had conquered  death and was restoring all creation, beginning with his own resurrection from the death. \r\n second the Hebrew Bible they disagreed vigorously  over the correct interpretation of the Hebrew scriptures for the coming Messiah were fulfilled in Jesus, but unbelieving Jews denied this understanding.\r\nthe early church and the Jewish people of the first century differed over he they viewed gentiles.  so when we understand what and how Jews think and believe Jesus, we can share gospel to them in a better ways', 9, '2021-08-09 18:27:32', '2021-08-11 05:57:37', NULL),
(2636, 697, 623, 'The kingdom of the God comes to earth though the person and work of Jesus. Luke believed that Jesus had begun the final phase of God\'s kingdom on earth and that he would one day return to finish what he had started. God\'s kingdom expands towards its great consummation as God grants salvation to those who receive and trust in Jesus as the Messiah. the rule of God over the world moves forward as the gospel touches the hearts of those who believes a nd brings them into salvation that Jesus accomplished . so our thinking and focus should be to expand his kingdom in all possible ways.\r\nour priority in all things should be to share his gospel in every manner possible. \r\nit should be main aim of our life. the passion to save many from hell fire should rule us.\r\nIf this thought drives our life, we will passionate to save many from clutches of Satan and many will receive Christ and so the God\'s kingdom will expand.', 8, '2021-08-09 18:40:15', '2021-08-11 05:57:37', NULL),
(2637, 698, 697, 'Paul was very self conscious of his Jewish heritage . Philippians 3:5 and Galatians 1:14 states about it very clearly.\r\nPaul was highly educated  educated in the tradition of Judaism. Acts 22:3, he had even been a student of one of the most famous rabbis in Jerusalem, Rabbi  Gamaliel. Paul was highly trained and sophisticated in his understanding of Jewish theology and scripture. Paul\'s Jewish background \r\n1. First both as  Jew and a Jewish Christian , Paul believed in the author of Old Testament scriptures. he trusted and submitted to them . the Hebrew Bible continued to be Paul\'s Bible.  secondly. Paul also held firmly to the Jewish belief that God would one day send the Messiah, the great son of David, who would end Israel\'s suffering an extend the kingdom of god to al the gentiles nations. the reason Paul convened to Christianity was that the he believed Jesus to be this long awaited Messiah. \r\ntheses pillars of Jewish  faith -complete submission to the scriptures and hope in Messiah were essential dimensions of Paul\'s Christian outlooks. so Paul\'s central Christian beliefs depended on his Jewish heritage.', 9, '2021-08-10 17:44:47', '2021-08-11 05:58:07', NULL),
(2638, 698, 698, 'Paul \'s contact with the gentiles was enhanced by the fact that he enjoyed full Roman citizenship. he had born into it. Paul actively asserted 6his rights as a roman citizen in order to promotes the gospel and to defend himself, Paul\'s letters to gentiles churches even demonstrate his willingness to serve  the customs for the sake of Gentiles to gain them \r\n1 Corinthians 9:21\r\nPaul knew Gentile culture so well that to be able to walk fine line of adapting his behavior to gentiles customs while still obeying Christ\'s law.\r\nPaul also quoted pagan philosophers . he was well educated in the philosophies and religions of the Greeco-Roman world.\r\nPaul\' awareness of gentile culture make it acceptable to gentiles. he strictly remained Jewish in his basic orientation. but it equipped him in to minster to the gentiles. he was well prepared to bring the gospel to them in effective ways. Romans 11:13, he was called the apostle to the gentiles.\r\nPaul was also prepared to minster to the gentiles and to even fight for them.', 9, '2021-08-10 18:07:34', '2021-08-11 05:58:07', NULL),
(2639, 698, 699, 'Paul \'s first missionary journey:\r\nin acts 13-14. it began when God told the church in Syrian Antioch to set aside Paul and Barnabas for a special work. the Holy Spirit led these men through the isle of Cyprus, they moved forward on an evangelistic tour of Asia minor, after preaching in Jewish synagogues, Paul began preaching in to the gentiles too. Paul planted  number of churches in journey, in the region of Galatia, after travelling to the east as far as Derbe.\r\nSecond journey:\r\nPaul\'s second missionary journey appears in Acts 15:36-18:22\r\nApostles and church leaders in Jerusalem selected Paul and Barnabas to deliver letters in Antioch, Syria, Cilicia, Galatia that Gentiles need not circumcise or to keep the laws of Moses to have salvation.\r\nPaul has a falling with Barnabas so they parted ways, so he took Silas. Paul continued to reach in Asia, but Holy spirit prohibited him and he had a Macedonian vision, he a man begged to preach gospel, so he responded by sailing for that province, Paul had planted many church in Greece, in Philippi and Thessalonica in the north\r\nThird :\r\nActs 18:23-21:17\r\nPaul went from Syrian Antioch through Galatia and Phyrgia and then established a thriving ministry in Ephesus. Paul returned Jerusalem after his third journey\r\nfourth journey:\r\nacts 27-28\r\nPaul traveled by a ship. between Crete and Isle of Malta, terrible storm completely destroyed the vessel which held Paul and  a number of other prisoners.\r\nthe crew, the guards , Paul and his companions were shipwrecked on the isle of Malta. they he went to Rome.', 9, '2021-08-10 18:27:01', '2021-08-11 05:58:07', NULL),
(2640, 698, 700, 'Paul\'s eschatology:\r\nterminology:\r\nthe term eschatology comes from Greek word eschatos which means last or end.\r\nStructure : \r\nthe origins \r\nPaul held the same basic two age view of history\" this age \"\r\nwhich is this current age of evil,  and the other one \" the age to come\"\r\nPaul used the expression \" the age to come : to refer to the future age when final judgements and blessing will come to the human race.\r\n\r\nDevelopment:\r\nPaul explained the transition from this age to the age to come was not a simple shift from one age to the next, it involved a period of overlap when both ages occurred simultaneously.\r\nPaul was confident that when Christ would return in glory this evil age would end and the age to come would arrive in fulness with ultimate blessings and final judgements for his enemies.\r\nthemes  \r\nPaul taught that final stage of the kingdom of God began when Jesus ascended to his heavenly throne.\r\na second aspect of eth age to come which is already present with us is the foretaste of our eternal inheritance in the Holy Spirit. Paul taught that when Christ ascended to his throne of high, he poured out the holy Spirit on the  church as the foretaste of Paul also pointed that Christ inaugurated the new creation associated with the age to come.', 9, '2021-08-10 18:42:38', '2021-08-11 05:58:07', NULL),
(2641, 698, 701, 'Paul\'s eschatology that opens up as already yet paradigm gives us hope for the future. as \r\n2 Corinthians 4:16-18 says we are wasting away outwardly, but inwardly we are renewed every day. Romans 8:18, says our present sufferings are not worth comparing with the glory that will be revealed in us. theses troubles are momentary. Jesus will eventually win this present evil age and create the world as a glorious gift for his children.\r\nwe are wasting away because of the troubles of this life. but the blessings that still lie ahead of us are so astounding that they utterly overshadow any trails we experience in this life.\r\nso this blessed hope fills our hearts with hope and joy even in the midst of all these troubles. and we are not downcast and devastated. Jesus has already conquered death and one day he will recreate the whole world and we will have freedom from sin and the power of the spirit enable us to delight in this daily inward renewal so that we fix our eyes on our eternal hope in \r\nChrist.\r\nOur foretastes of the age to come help us look forward to the full banquet that awaits us at Christ\'s return.', 9, '2021-08-10 18:54:45', '2021-08-11 05:58:07', NULL),
(2642, 699, 624, 'Luke plainly stated that he had a twofold purpose as he wrote.\r\nhistorical account:\r\nLuke had declared that had historical intentions , a desire to write a true and reliable historical account of the church of the first century.\r\nLuke 1:11-3 we see Luke\'s concern with true history, he referred to the things that we have been fulfilled among us. he had also mentioned that had consulted eye witnesses and carefully investigated the details he had recorded. he also took care to rewrite an orderly account so that the truth he reported would communicated  would e communicate d clearly an  accurately. Luke was a historical \r\nGospel message:\r\nLuke\'s recess the theological purpose of conveying the reality an power of the gospel message in the book of Acts\r\nLuke 1:3-4Luke wrote the history of acts to confirm what Theophilus and others had been taught. Luke viewed the world and all of history through the lens of Christ\'s lordship and kingdom. He saw the Old Testament hopes and promises are being fulfilled through Jesus and the church.\r\nso we see that the book of Acts written by Luke gives us insights about the days of Jesus, the accounts collected are reliable. \r\nand it gives us proper conclusions. these are foundational to the church.', 9, '2021-08-16 17:51:18', '2021-09-20 12:49:47', NULL),
(2643, 699, 625, 'structural pattern:\r\nsummary statements:\r\nLuke made many authorial comments throughout acts. he gave background information, revealed the intentions of Characters hearts, described settings and so on. he did this to ensure that his message was preached presented clearly faithfully.\r\nLuke made many statements for six periods of time in his history, the success of the gospel in Jerusalem, in Judea and Samaria, from Samaria to Syria Antioch, in Cyprus, Phyrgia, and Galatia in Asia, Macedonia and Achaia. \r\nChurch growth:\r\nhe wrote about internal growth and tension within the church, he wrote about external growth and preposition from outside the church. \r\nthis was a form of qualitative growth furthering the spiritual maturity of the individuals and of the church as whole.\r\n\r\nthe second pair of elements in Luke\'s structure pattern of church growth is external growth an opposition. by externally growth, it means  church increased numerically by adding new numbers.\r\nLuke regularly pointed out that internal growth and tension yield external growth and opposition an that external growth and opposition cause internal growth and tension.\r\n understanding the pattern encourages believers everywhere that no matter how great the tension or how terrible the opposition. God is always working through the gospel for the internal and external growth of his church.', 9, '2021-08-16 18:07:55', '2021-09-20 12:49:47', NULL),
(2644, 699, 626, 'if we hope to understand the book of Acts in a responsible way, we must take into account the kind of literature it is and the ways this of literature communicate its ideas.\r\n\r\nFirst Luke was selective in what he reported Luke only wrote 28 fairly short chapters. as we apply the book of acts in the modern world , we need t avoid the error of thinking that Luke recorded everything we might ever want to now bout tis period of church history. we need to remember that each account in the book on Acts is here to help\r\nEpisodic:\r\nActs is a successful collection of smaller stories and accounts. its is important to note that each of its individual narratives. we must pay attention to both the big and small picture understanding how each episode contributes to the greater goal\r\nImplicit:\r\nnarrative discourse is the dominant type of literature in the four gospel and more importantly for these lessons in the book of acts.\r\none of the chief ways to apply any biblical narrative to our lives is to see how God reacts to actions that take place. we should seek to emulate the beliefs , attitudes and behaviors that are pleasing to God.', 9, '2021-08-16 18:22:27', '2021-09-20 12:49:47', NULL),
(2645, 699, 627, 'one of the chief way to evaluate and apply Biblical narrative to our lives is to see how God reacts to cations that take place . above all others , his words and actions are perfectly infallible. we should always take note of the things in the book of Acts that God approves and blesses as well as the things that he disapproves or curses. Whatever god blesses must be good and whatever he disapproves or curses must be veil. Luke also so heavily rely on the outlooks of prominent church leaders. the trustworthy people such as Apostles , prophets and other respected church leaders, the actions were appropriate and their witness true. our responses would be to responding our hearts to this call and to model our behavior and thoughts after hearts.\r\n when characters in the books of Acts are condemned by the Apostles o the church, we can refer that their actions were evil and that we should not follow their examples.\r\n we should pay attention to these things as we have God whose words and actions are infallible.', 8, '2021-08-16 18:37:10', '2021-09-20 12:49:47', NULL),
(2646, 699, 628, 'Continuities or similarities:\r\n the continuities between Christians and in the periods by saying that we have same triune God who exists in the person of the Father , the Son and the Holy Spirit. we have same goal to build the kingdom of God in Christ and the same gospel . the same message of salvation  and redemption that requires us to respond to faith, repentance and obedience, we have the same God as the Christian in the first century\r\ndiscontinuities :\r\ndiscontinuities between Luke\'s world and ours in two ways. we live in a different time that did and the world has changed quite a bit since the first century, so that we have different circumstance, different sustains from those whom Luke first wrote this book difference in application :\r\nwhere there is little or no persecution, the church does not need to meet in home. our circumstance resemble theirs, these may be legitimate applications of biblical principles. different applications of the same princiles evn in the book ofavts itself, inacts 2:44-45 luke described the members of the church in Jeruslame as owin yheir resources in commomthe same rincile \r\nappllication :\r\nwe seek to submit to god\'s stated mission rather thatn to pursue our own world, the gola, amd the prinviples behind itremain same in every generation.\r\nthe summons to obedience is for jews and gengtiles, rich an dpoor , male an femle, respected and despised. it overcomes altyoes of resstance and barriers.for its is the word of the reigning Christ, so matter what age we live in , We must be bold in authentic an dbold in proclaiming an d affirming the gosple of the apostles, calling everyone to repentance and faith in christ.', 8, '2021-08-16 18:57:03', '2021-09-20 12:49:47', NULL),
(2647, 700, 702, 'Paul and Barnabas began their first missionary journey in AD 46, setting sailing to the isle of Cyprus. they proclaimed gospel from synagogue to synagogue as they moved to the western city of Paphos. From Cyprus Paul and Barnabas sailed to Perga and then moved inland to Antioch in the region of Pisidia. many of the Jews responded positively. but within a week unbelieving Jews incited the city against Paula and Barnabas and had them out of town. then they moved further cast in the province of Galatia. when they preached in the synagogue, many Jews and gentiles came to faith. but the church was not firmly established. because Paul and Barnabas quickly left the city, when they were planned go be murdered by unbelieving Jews.\r\nthe they moved to Lystra, where he heled a lame man , an people started worshipping them as God hermes and Barnabas for Zews . They travelled east in Galatia as far as Derbe.\r\n\r\nthe assembly in Jerusalem addressed some of the issues in these churches \r\n they also  had certain Problems in the church of Galatia, as the gentiles was also being saved. and the false teachers were guiding them wrongly even regarding circumcision.\r\nso due to theses problems , play wrote the letter to the Galatia. so understanding the first missionary journey of Paul and the problems in the church, we can better understand the reason behind Paul writing the Letter.', 8, '2021-08-17 17:48:36', '2021-09-20 12:53:16', NULL),
(2648, 700, 703, 'three historical events of Paul\'s call and training:\r\nthis explains how Paul had the authority to resist circumcising gentiles. it begins with a description of how Paul had loved the traditions of Israel in Galatians 1:13-14. but his attitude changed. Paul had spent three years in Arabia, learning the gospel and Christian doctrines, directly from Jesus, his refusal to require gentile circumcision did not result from natural reasoning or personal preference, but Jesu had taught Paul himself.\r\nMeeting with the leaders. \r\nGalatians 2:1-10 states that Paul\'s meeting with the church in Jerusalem, fourteen years after an earlier private meeting with Peter, Paul met with the leaders of the church n Jerusalem, the other apostles agreed that God had given Paul the specific role of spreading the gospel to the gentile world.\r\nConflict with Peter:\r\nthis account is found un Galatians 2:11-21. describes a conflict with Peter Syrian Antioch, at an earlier time, Peter had freely associated with uncircumcised believers. at some time later, Peter became to fear for his reputation among Strict Jewish believers in Jerusalem. Paul wrote about his conflict with Peter, to prove that even Peter had submitted to his correction in this matter.', 9, '2021-08-17 18:00:27', '2021-09-20 12:53:16', NULL),
(2649, 700, 704, 'Direct theological arguments that Paul offered for his doctrine of Justification by faith in Galatians 3:1-4:31\r\nFrist Paul appealed to the early experience if the Galatians\r\nGalatians 3:1-5 says that Galatians had received many incredible blessings form the Holy Spirit, when Paul had been first among them. Both they and he knew that theses blessing had not come to them because they had kept the law, but because they had believed the gospel.\r\n\r\nsecond Abraham\'s saving faith:\r\nhe argues in 3:6 to 4:11 that God had blessed Abraham because of Faith, not because of Abraham\'s obedience to God.\r\nAbraham had not earned the blessing of salvation through fleshly human effort.\r\nGenesis 15:6  made it clear, he was made just because of faith not because of circumcision.\r\n\r\ncurrent experience of the Galatians believers\r\nGalatians 14:12-20. Paul was deeply concerned that they had lost the joy, a fruit of the Holy Spirit, that they should have been enjoying. and they have terrible spiritual condition\r\n\r\nfourth  story of Abraham\'s wives and sons:\r\nSarah was barren and was past child bearing. Galatians 4:31 states that faith in God\'s promise is the way of salvation . by trusting God to fulfill his word, Sarah had a son.', 8, '2021-08-17 18:15:46', '2021-09-20 12:53:16', NULL),
(2650, 700, 705, 'the false teachers in the Galatia actually taught that some believers in the church were better that here because , everyone stood before God on the basis of their own merit.\r\n\'but they were wrong the truth is that we are clothed with Christ, we are in Christ, Jesus because we are united to Christ in this way. god looks Christians as if they were Christ himself.\r\nPaul taught that the transition form this age of judgement to the coming age blessing takes place through the life, death and resurrection of Christ. \r\nby His  obedience to the law Christ fulfilled the requirement for all believers\r\nby his death enduring curses he fulfilled the requirement that sin has to be punished\r\nby his resurrection , Christ and those for whom he dies were vindicated before father as worthy of glory. on that basis God considers them to have died to the curse of the law, with Christ and to have been raise with Christ in the new life of the age to come,', 8, '2021-08-17 18:25:53', '2021-09-20 12:53:16', NULL),
(2651, 700, 706, 'in Galatians 5:1-15 Paul had called to Galatians to remain true to their freedom in Christ. his position is truly balanced.  Christian faith is free from burdens and he wanted them to remain free as its is good because the burdens of legalism are so dangerous.\r\nChristian freedom is with an affirmation of Christian moral responsibility. we should not use the freedom we have as license for disregarding God\'s moral law.\r\nChrist had released us from  bondage of sin and law as the means to justification and power of righteous living, but he stull demanded that they follow the command of God. we  should not take the freedom we have and got as the mean to violate the God\'s holy character, which was very foundation of the law.\r\nwe are to be grateful to God for the freedom that we have in Christ, but when we have the freedom while enjoying we should not do anything that hurts the heart of God.', 8, '2021-08-17 18:37:54', '2021-09-20 12:53:16', NULL),
(2652, 701, 630, 'in Acts 1:3-11, we will see how Jesus taught on two aspects the Holy Spirit:\r\ntiming\r\n Jesus announced the coming of the coming baptism of the Spirit, Jesus explained that God\' s kingdom would unfold over time by spreading throughout the nations Jesus explained to his apostles that the Holy Spirit was about to be poured out on the church. Jesus had only inaugurated the kingdom and the blessing of the Spirit would equip his church as it continued to live in a sinful  world prior to his return.\r\nPurpose: two dimensions of the Spirit\'s  ministry Power and godly witnesses. first Jesus said that the Apostles would receive power through the baptism of the Spirit. Jesus mentioned that the Spirit would cause the disciples to be godly witnesses. the Spirit would act in them as he had acted through others in the old Testament like in Micah 3:8. the holy spirit would also perform acts of power to authenticate the truth of the apostles\' message and he would give them words to speak to those who opposed them.', 8, '2021-08-23 17:42:05', '2021-09-20 12:54:51', NULL),
(2653, 701, 802, 'the work of Holy spirit acts 2:1-4 after the day of Pentecost\r\nsignificance: Pentecost was time of celebration , connected with Passover, exodus 12 and Leviticus 23, time of year when Israel remembered their deliverance form Egypt. Pentecost was held about 50 days after Passover, celebrated God\'s provision of sustenance in the land of promise. the Jews also remembered the giving of God\'s law to Moses during the celebration of Pentecost. Jeremiah 31:31-3. the writing of the law in the heart was a work of the Spirit if God promised in the Old Testament and fulfilled in the New Testament,\r\nthe arrival of the Spirit on the first Christian Pentecost indicated that the church had also received the writing of God\'s laws o the heart, equipping the church for bold witness. the gift if the Spirit at Pentecost began the great harvest of salvation and the inward renewal of God\'s promise to establish the Messainic kingdom of God.\r\nSamaria: Acts 8:14-17 \r\nthe unusual outpouring of the Spirit is that it marked the first time in Samarian converts . Jesus commissioned the apostles to expand the kingdom of God from Jerusalem to Judea to Samaria to the ends of the earth. \r\nCaesarea: first time the gospel moved across another ethnic country. acts 10:44-47 peter preached the gospel to Cornelius\r\nEphesus :\r\nActs 19:1- a major city in Asia Minor far from Judea and Samaria. the recipients of the Spirit were disciples of John the Baptist. it indicated that Jesus\' work of bringing the Spirit had reached a new level.', 9, '2021-08-23 17:59:43', '2021-09-20 12:54:51', NULL),
(2654, 701, 803, 'Jesus appointed the apostles to continue his ministry, to extend his kingdom from Jerusalem to the very ends 0 the earth through the gospel.\r\nfunction : apostle  means one who is sent. the apostle were authoritative ambassadors. they were appointed not only to the mission of spreading the gospel but also the office of the church.\r\nto speak to all matters related to the establishment and governance of Christ\'s church.\r\nthey words were to received because they served as faithful stewards of His will and teaching.\r\nBlessing:\r\n God blessed the apostle by giving them converts almost every time they preached gospel. Peters sermon of the day of Pentecost explained the church from 120 to 3000. their external , numerical growth was evidence of God\' approval and power. acts 5:38-39. Gamaliel\'s words, \r\n\r\nMiracles:\r\none of the main function of miracles is to prove God\'s messengers speak the truth and bear god\' s delegated authority. apostles proved that their testimony concerning Christ was true. they escaped from prison, they raised dead, they survived shipwreck, they healed sick, \r\nRevelation:\r\n the Holy Spirit gave evidence to the apostles leading them to flesh out the truth of the gospel, to make divisions for the church as whole shaping the structural elements that allowed the church to grow into maturity.', 9, '2021-08-23 18:13:57', '2021-09-20 12:54:51', NULL),
(2655, 701, 804, 'The Holy Spirit empowers people today to live transformed lives by guiding us in our daily lives. the holy Spirit works though the church authentic testimony in life and word. the gift of the Holy Spirit at Pentecost began the great harvest of salvation and inward renewal of god\'s people to establish the messianic kingdom go God. \r\nthe Holy Spirit empowers us for service. the holy Spirit is present in lives of believers. the Holy Spirit was given to empower the apostles to be his godly witnesses and gives us power to bless the spread the gospel . He transforms lives. he imparts gifts.\r\nThe Holy Spirit gives us power to endure trails and overcome temptations, He helps us in our weaknesses. He performs miracles when we pray.\r\nI ma empowered by the Holy Spirit to be a faithful witness of Christ, till today. he had helped endure the fiery trials in my life. when I am weak he strengthens me. he enables me to pray.\r\nhe always guides me , fills me with power and peace I can not comprehend. he has given me gifts to expand the Kingdom and be fruitful.\r\n1 Corinthians 12, Romans 12 Ephesians 2', 9, '2021-08-23 18:31:50', '2021-09-20 12:54:51', NULL),
(2656, 701, 805, 'our church is carrying out the mission of expanding the kingdom of God, by being living letters to the entire world. we are aroma of Christ. we are witnessing Christ by being good example to others. we spread the gospel in all possible ways. we have church structure based in the basic doctrines of Christ.  we conduct revival meetings, outreach meetings among youth and kids.\r\nwe preach, propagate and practice the teachings of Christ in our deeds and words. by being human we fail in many things and many times. we can still do better , there is always room for improvement. we  can do things more sincerely and whole heartedly for the Kingdom of God, we need to trust god more and pray more and go out and witness more fearlessly. being in India, its not easy any way, physically, financially.\r\nI can personally help my church in witnessing by praying more, witnessing more and living according to the word of God, many people don\'t read Bible, let them read me ,such be my life this I pray. \r\nI need to be more bold and burdened b the love of God for perishing souls, to witness about His love more sincerely. I can serve God faithfully being a Pastor, exhort and encourage people to be good witnesses where ever they are. empowered by the Holy Spirit and following the role model of Apostles all this would be easier.', 8, '2021-08-23 18:43:59', '2021-09-20 12:54:51', NULL),
(2657, 702, 707, 'three main problems that plagued in Thessalonica and ramifications\r\nPersecution:\r\nWhen Paul had fist brought the gospel to Thessalonica , the believers there had become the object of violence, enduring serious , life threatening persecution. Acts 17:5 the unbelieving Jews in Thessalonica were so aggressive that they were not satisfied to drive Paula and Saul of out of their city and they followed the missionaries to Berea to trouble them even after. \r\nPaul wrote to the believers in that city, he was very concerned about this persecution and the effect it was having on them.\r\nFalse prophets: the Thessalonian church had also come under the influence of false prophets. suffering Christians tend to orient their entire lives toward the second coming of Christ. when Christians are highly concerned with this, they often become susceptible to false teachers of false prophets who have extreme views regarding the second coming. Paul reminded them to examine every prophecy they heard. he instructed theme to test everything and to hold on to good .some even forged letters under Paul\'s name , Paul added words written by his own hand as a signature that distinguished his letters. \r\nPractical Christian living:\r\nin Paul\'s letter to the Thessalonians , discouragement and irresponsibility\r\nChristians then believed that Jesus return was around the corner and when he seemed to delay it let to discouragement and people became sad about the death of the believers too. Paul assured them that the departed Christians had died physically and they were still alive with Christ and they would accompany Christ when he returned.\r\nand to those who ignored daily life and the responsibilities of the daily life. he exhorted to lead a quiet life and to do do own business and work with own hands, Paul had encouraged attention to daily affairs.', 9, '2021-08-24 17:50:47', '2021-09-20 12:53:50', NULL),
(2658, 702, 708, 'Paul\'s second missionary journey in acts 15:36, his trip he began  Syrian Antioch, Paul chose chose Silas as his companions. during the journey when he reached Thessalonica. 1 Thessalonians 3:1-2, while Paul was in Athens he sent Timothy back to Thessalonica to encourage the believers there. . shortly after that Paul and his company left Thessalonica. apparently Timothy told Paul about a number of serious misunderstanding and practical problems that had risen in the Thessalonians from Corinth shortly. \r\nin Thessalonica the problems were manifold but interrelated. persecution had opened the door for misconceptions about the immediate return of Christ and those false beliefs lead to practical problems such as discouragement and irresponsibility. so Paul hoped that his letters to the Thessalonians church would hep them deal with theses difficult problems.\r\nFrom the time Paul fist preached in Thessalonica until he wrote his epistles to them persecution had been prominent in this church, so when understand all this we come to know that Paul had written these epistles addressing all the problems that plagues Thessalonians and the ramifications for it.', 9, '2021-08-24 18:03:54', '2021-09-20 12:53:50', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2659, 702, 709, 'Paul\'s purpose in writing the second letter to Thessalonians:\r\nPaul expressed his confidence and joy in this church, as well as his gratitude and their faithfulness in his absence, yet Pal also know that serious problems had raised during his absence  so he instructed the Thessalonians not just once but at least twice by writing this two letters . his chief concern was to they be faithful to Christ and to his teaching, living responsibly , in their daily lives and gaining a proper outlook in the Lord\'s return.\r\nMain points in the letter:\r\n\r\nSalutations:\r\nthanksgiving and encouragement:\r\nPaul encouraged them how exemplary their faith was and hw had he boasted to other churches about their perseverance.\r\n2thessalonians 1:4\r\nPrayer:\r\n1:11-12 Paul explained that the constantly prayed for the Thessalonians and they were not lone in their struggles.\r\nInstructions:\r\n2:1-17 Paul instructed them about Christ\' return3:1-5 Paul asked them to pray for safety and success in ministry. 3:6-15 Paul issued a warning against irresponsibility, telling them to work and to earn their own livings.', 8, '2021-08-24 18:16:37', '2021-09-20 12:53:50', NULL),
(2660, 702, 710, 'Paul wrote the first letter to Thessalonians addressing the problems in the church of Thessalonica like persecution, false teaching and irresponsible living. Paul gave them a number of instructions regarding the second coming of Christ. he wanted then to live s Christ wanted. he mentioned a number of areas in which they should guard against sin and increase their obedience to Christ. he commanded me to engage in meaningful labor. he taught them against false teachings.\r\nwhereas in second letter. he taught them about Eschatology, doctrine of salvation, \r\n similarity: Paul expressed his confidence and joy in this church as well as his gratitude for their faithfulness in is absence in bit these letters. Paul also knew that serious problems also arisen in his absence, so he instructed the Thessalonians not just once but twice by writing these two letters, his chief concern was that they be faithful to Christ and his teaching, living responsibly in their daily lives and gaining a proper outlook on the Lord\'s return.', 8, '2021-08-24 18:31:56', '2021-09-20 12:53:50', NULL),
(2661, 702, 711, 'Paul wanted to preach in the province of Asia, but the Holy Spirit did not permit him, so the company went from Phyrgia to the coastal part of Troas . in a dream , Paul saw a man who begged him to come to Macedonia. this was not in his plan. in response to this dream Paul and his companions immediately sailed for Macedonia. Paul waned to preach the gospel to the northern Asia and Bithynia , but the Holy Spirit prohibited him, \r\nPaul\'s famous Macedonian vision , he immediately obeyed Holy Spirit setting aside his plans and in light to god\'s call. similarly we should respond positively when God calls us to things we have not planned , we should always be ready to listened to the guidance of the Holy spirit and obeyed the voice of God. we should seek his voice before we do anything, we may have many plans for our life and ministry, nut w should always move ahead according to the plans of God as they are perfect and better that in our lives, we should always have an open heart and attitude submissive to follow his plans and instructions in our lives.', 8, '2021-08-24 18:47:35', '2021-09-20 12:53:50', NULL),
(2662, 703, 712, 'Paul began this journey around the year AD 52 OR 53, he started in Antioch in Syria and strengthened the believers throughout Galatia and Phyrgia, the Paul arrived in the coastal city of Ephesus in the province of Asia or Asia minor,  there he encountered twelve disciples of John the Baptist. Paul and his compatriots came into conflict with the artisans who crafted the shrines of Artemis , the patron goddess of Ephesus.  After this event, Paula nd his travelling companions spent several months in Macedonia and Achia. Paul and his companions travelled to the neighboring city of Asses , they stoppped in Mirylene, Chios, and Sarnes, then passing through Rhodes, Patara and Cyprus, they landed in Tyre . so when he travelled in all these churches he came to know the problems there like damaged relationships, sexual misconduct, abuses in worship and rejection of Paul\'s apostolic authority. so decided to write letters to Corinth to give detailed study of right things and corrections needed in each fields.', 9, '2021-09-01 17:42:45', '2021-09-20 12:52:08', NULL),
(2663, 703, 713, 'Paul\'s purposes in writing the letter was to guide them on four major problems. \r\ndamaged relationships: Paul received a report that the believers in Corinth were turned against each other by identifying themselves with which ever teacher they held in highest esteem .Paul guided them to meet the need of the saints and take care of the poor.\r\nsexual  misconduct: many Corinthians believed that because Jesus had come, sexual matters were no longer significant 1corinthians 5:1-2\r\nsome believers in Corinth went to the opposite extreme preferring ascetism and sexual abstinence even within marriage. 1 Corinthians 7:2-5\r\n abuses in worship: \'there was misconduct in ownership, mistreatment of the poor in Lord\'s supper, issues also with regard to these : gender roles , use of spiritual gifts and meat sacrificed to idols. he taught that man\'s head is Christ and woman\'s head is man. he taught that spiritual gifts i worship, and believers should listen to one another and \r\nRejection  of Paul\'s apostolic authority:\'\r\nthe greatest problem. \r\ndivisions in church, immorality and Christian associations', 9, '2021-09-01 17:58:45', '2021-09-20 12:52:08', NULL),
(2664, 703, 714, 'both letters include the  correction from Paul\'s side regarding divisions within church, immorality and Christians associations. both letters Paul taught about treating the other saints with respect, collection for needed saints. in both he taught about eschatology regarding the resurrection  of all believers. in both letters Paul referred to his apostleship, but in first letter he dealt with love, meat sacrificed to idols, , spiritual gifts, matters relation to worship , gender roles, marriage remarriage and all things regarding sexual immorality were taken in 1corinthians. \r\nin 2 Corinthians Paul explained his reason not visiting Corinthians, he proved an extensive defense in the nature of his ministry then he dealt with collection,  he constantly warns them to test whether they are in faith or not . \r\nin both the letters, he proves his apostleship,  teaches eschatology, about collections. purpose of both letters is to correct on divisions, immorality and Christian associations.', 8, '2021-09-01 18:18:10', '2021-09-20 12:52:08', NULL),
(2665, 703, 715, 'Error in the Corinthians regarding faith and preeminence of Christ:\r\nSome Corinthian Christians acted as if Christ had brought in the kingdom of God in nearly all its fulness so that they were already enjoying many of the most of the eternal blessings that God has in mind for believers. they also acted as if Jesus had appointed them as a rulers over his newly established earthly kingdom, this appears to have been  the opinion especially of those Corinthians who wielded authority in the church. They imagined that Christ had given then his power because , they were wiser and more spiritual than the others and they looked down than on other s who in their eyes had not merited such great great rewards.  1 Corinthians 4;7-10. they had thought they had \r\n merited status and honor but in fact it was Christ which had given them these things , they knew that Christ loyal followers would, reign the restored earth with him one day, but they foolishly thought that their reign had begun even through Christ had not yet returned as king.', 8, '2021-09-01 18:30:54', '2021-09-20 12:52:08', NULL),
(2666, 703, 716, 'when  we read Leviticus 10:1-3 we read about Aarons\' son offering strange fire before the Lord, which he commanded them not. and so fire went out from the Lord and they died before the Lord. similarly Paul says in 1Corinthians 7, to worship God in manner worthy to Him. we should revere the Lord. and refrain from abuses in worship. we should have the fear of the Lord. God says be Holy as he is holy. we should conduct worship with each other in fear and holiness. we should deal with the use of Spiritual gifts in worship, we should avoid any disorder in worship, any sort of quarrel, fights and abuses should not be in worship. 1 Corinthians 11-4:5 Paul teaches of head coverings in worship.\r\na healthy fear of god should be their in our heart and in our dealing as we honor the Lord of Lords and God of gods.  we should not only practice and revere God , when we are in worship, but also in our personal secret lives.\r\nwe should give more value to God and love with all our heart.', 8, '2021-09-01 18:49:25', '2021-09-20 12:52:08', NULL),
(2667, 704, 927, 'God is 3 divine persons in one divine essence. In other words one substance in 3 persons, can be explained as follows : \r\n\r\nTrinity simply means \"Tri-Unity\" three in one. Related terms - Trinitarian / Triune God. \r\nGod reveals who God is in terms of Father, Son and Holy Spirit through the Scriptures.\r\nTriune God is  a mystery in itself, but Scripture reveals to us in depth the greatness of our God. \r\nFather who sends the Son, the Son who after his death and resurrection goes to the Father and they send the Spirit.\r\nBefore creation God already existed as Father, Son and Holy Spirit. God is One Uncreated Creator \r\nIn the ccreation God interacts with the world as Father, Son and Holy Spirit.', 8, '2021-09-03 15:26:50', '2021-09-20 12:51:26', NULL),
(2668, 704, 928, 'Using human mind it is very difficult to comprehend ior contemplate the fullness of what it means to be a person of the Triune God.\r\nThese can only be understood in the light of the Scriptures as follows :\r\nReciprocal, self conciousness of the persons within the Godhead.\r\nFather conscious of the self in relationship to the Son and in relation to the Spirit, and similarly the Son and the Holy Spirit - vice versa\r\nRelationship of the Father, Son and Spirit as persons to each other, they have a reciprocal, self consciousness that undergirds the major principle of Trinitarian theology, the unity of divine action\r\nFather, Son and Holy Spirit speaks to the world ultimately through one voice in Jesus Christ.\r\nOne substance - 3 persons. One person putting on different masks.\r\nDoctrine of Christ - fully divine, fully human in one person, one personal consciousness.', 8, '2021-09-03 15:38:50', '2021-09-20 12:51:26', NULL),
(2669, 704, 929, 'God is inconceivable - man cannot analyze him and sort him out. \r\nTheology explains the concept of God\'s simplicity. God has revealed himself as Trinity, but these 3 persons are one, they have one essence. They cannot be separated or broken down into parts.\r\nThe Son is in the Father, the Father is in the Son, there is only one Godhead, one divine nature, that\'s indivisible and three persons.\r\nIn the book of Mathew chapter 10, Jesus says whoever receives one of my disciples in my name, they receive me, and whoever receives me, receives the One who sent me, in other words, the dsciple who has the Spirit of God in them, it\'s not just receiving the disciple, they receive the Son of God. And when they receive the Son of God, they receive the Father. It\'s one God in three persons. \r\nThe Divine Threeness does not violate the doctrine of God\'s simplicity, since they are undivided and interrelated.', 8, '2021-09-03 15:54:23', '2021-09-20 12:51:26', NULL),
(2670, 704, 930, 'Jesus Christ is God\'s image, as explained by Apostle Paul in Colossians 1:15-17. Christ represents God the Father. \r\nJesus represents the way in which God the Father, God the Son and God the Holy Spirit are accomplishing our salvation. \r\n1st Corinthians 8 where Paul talks about God the Father and also Jesus Christ the Lord, it is not to be understood that Jesus is a secondary diety, one one of the angels. But Paul is establishing here that both Father and the Son are worthy of our worship and that they together along with the Holy Spirit accomplish our salvation.\r\nWe have one God and one Lord and that Lord Jesus Christ.\r\nJesus is part of creation, Jesus is God, has always been God.\r\nJesus is the prince, the one sovereign over and eventually the heir. All things were created by him and for him. God has appointed him the heir of all things. As the Son he is the full revelation of God, fully identified with God in creation, but also the heir of all things. Sonship through his incarnation, his redeeming work on the cross, his taking seat at the Father\'s right hand, he enters into his heirship of all things which he has created.', 8, '2021-09-03 16:11:23', '2021-09-20 12:51:26', NULL),
(2671, 704, 931, 'Holy Spirit is fully divine and fully personal - One God 3 persons, can be explained through the scriptures as follows : \r\nLooking at the Great Commission in Mathew 28, Jesus commands us to baptize people in the name of the Father, and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit - One name - Father, Son and Spirit. \r\nJesus is echoing or alluding to the blessing that Aaron in the Old Testament placed upon Israel. That the Lord bless you, keep you. the Lord make his face shine upon you. The Lord turn his face toward you and give you peace. Thus we are putting on the name of God making the claim that we now belong to him in the name of the Father, The Son and the Holy Spirit.\r\n\r\nThe Spirit is fully divine equal with the Father and the Son, fully personal, no less than the Father and the Son.\r\n\r\nApostle\'s Paul\'s  benediction at the end of 2 Corinthians 13: The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit. Again there\'s a blessing in the name of the trune God. Grace extended through the sacrifice of the Son, motivated by the love of God the Father and then apply to us by the Holy Spirit as he has fellowship with us and communes with us. He brings in our hearts what the Son has accomplished for us in grace, which is what the Father has purposed for us in love.\r\n\r\nFather and the Son and Spirit is at work in salvation, just as they were at work in creation when the Spirit of God hovered upon the deep.\r\n\r\nIn the incarnation Father sending the Son and the Son being born of a woman, it is the spirit who comes on Mary so that she can conceive and bear the Son.\r\n\r\nGod is at work in Christ to reconcile the world to himself and the Spirit is active in every aspect of God\'s work, creation, revelation, incarnation, ultimately our own salvation our regeneration.', 9, '2021-09-03 16:31:15', '2021-09-20 12:51:26', NULL),
(2672, 705, 697, 'Paul was very self -conscious of his Jewish heritage before he became a Christian. Philippians 3:5. Paul was religious conservative, fully dedicated to preserving and pursuing Israel\'s traditions. Galatians 1:14Paul was highly educated in the traditions of Judaism and trained under famous Rabbi Gamaliel.\r\neven after he became a Christian Paul was deeply indebted to the same heritage.\r\nPaul the Christian, carefully followed the traditions of his fathers. even after Jews had severely persecuted Pau because of his faith in Christ, Paul\'s ethnic identity and loyalty were so string that he still wanted desperately to save them. Romans 9:25\r\nPaul\'s Jewish background influence ion his theology:\r\nfirst both as a Jew and as a Christian , Paul believed in the author of the Old Testament Scriptures. he trusted and submitted to them without reservation. Paul would have never believed anything that contradicted the teachings f the Old Testament. Paul was fully rooted in the monotheism of Old Testament and believed whole heartedly in the moral requirements of the Hebrew Scriptures.\r\nin the second place, Paul was held firmly to the Jewish belief that God would one day send the messiah, the great son of David, who would end Israel\'s suffering and extend the kingdom of god to all the gentile nations. the reason Paul convened to Christianity was that he believed Jesus to be this long awaited Messiah. Paul did not hesitate to call Jesu Christ, Paul dud not see Christianity as the replacement of Judaism, instead he saw Christianity as the branch  of Judaism which recognized Jesus was the true Messiah.', 9, '2021-09-13 17:47:09', '2021-09-20 12:50:48', NULL),
(2673, 705, 698, 'the pillars of Jewish faith- complete submission to the Scriptures and the hope in the Messiah were essential dimensions of Paul\'s Christian outlooks. \r\nPaul lived not only in Jewish Palestine, but at different times in his life he lived in the gentile world as well. according to acts 21:39, Paul came from the gentile city of Tarsus in Cilicia. in Acts 21:39Acts 9:30. 11:25 indicate that Paul again lived in Tarsus as an adult. Paul\'s contact with the gentile world was enhanced by the fact that he enjoyed full Roman citizenship. Acts 22:28 we see that Paul hadn\'t purchased his citizenship but had been born into it. Paul\'s awareness of Gentile culture did not lead Paul to alter Christianity to make it acceptable to Gentiles. He remained distinctly Jewish in his basic orientation. Paul\'s contact with the Gentile world affected him in at least two ways. on the one hand, it equipped him to minister to Gentiles outside the church. Better than many , he knew the values and beliefs of Gentiles and was well prepared to bring the gospel to them in effective ways. Romans 11:13, Paul called himself \"the apostle to the Gentiles. Paul was also prepared to minister to Gentiles within the church and even to fight for them .Paul played an important role in convincing the apostles and elders that Gentile converts did not need to be circumcised. Paul insisted that Christ had destroyed the wall between the Jews and Gentiles. A central theme in many of Paul\'s epistles was that Jesus had flung open the door of salvation to the gentiles nations so that any gentiles who was in Christ was counted as a full blooded Jew and perfect law keeper n God\'s eyes.', 9, '2021-09-13 18:03:23', '2021-09-20 12:50:48', NULL),
(2674, 705, 699, 'Paul\'s first missionary journey in Acts 13-14 began when God told the church in Syria  to set aside Paul and Barnabas for special work. The Holy Spirit led them through the isle of Cyprus, they moved from Cyprus to Asia Minor. he successfully planted many church on this Journey, he preached to Jews an when he faced much resistance , he preached to Gentiles also.\r\n Paul\'s second missionary journey:\r\n Acts 15:36- 18:22 the church selected Paul and Barnabas to send a letter to churches in Antioch, Syria, Cilicia, Galatia Paul had a falling out with Barnabas and parted ways with him and teamed with Silas. first they two travelled to Syria and then through Cilicia until they reached Galatia.\r\nhe wanted to preach the gospel t the north in Asia and Bithynia, Holy Spirit prohibited him. Paul\'s famous Macedonia vision,\r\nthird journey: Paul\'s third missionary journey appears in Acts 18:23-21:17 . Paul went from Syrian Antioch Galatia and Phrygia and established a thriving ministry in Ephesus.  when Paul returned to Jerusalem after his third journey, he was arrested by romans as Jews falsely accused him.\r\n\r\nFourth Journey:  after spending two years in prison, Paul appealed to Caesar. Acts 27-28 Paul traveled most of his journey by ship between Crete and the isle of , Malta, a terrible storm completely destroyed the vessel. the crew and the guards , Paul and his companions were shipwrekced on the isle of malta after three months Paul reached Rome and was of house arrest Paul  .', 9, '2021-09-13 18:23:20', '2021-09-20 12:50:48', NULL),
(2675, 705, 700, 'Paul\'s eschatology:\r\nterminology:\r\nthe term eschatology comes from the Greek word eschatos,  the doctrine of the last days or end times. \r\nstructure:\r\norigins:\r\nthis age and ages to come\r\nPaul directly referred to the age in which he lived as \"this age\" on at least twelve occasions\r\nsimilarly Paul used the expression \" the ages to come\" to refer to the future age, when final blessing  and judgements will come to the human race.\r\nDevelopment:\r\nPaul explained it , the transition from this age to the age to come was not a simple shift from one age to the next, initiated it involved a period of overlap when both ages occurred simultaneously.\r\nthe age to come had been inaugurated through the death , resurrection and ascension of Christ.\r\nThemes: Paul\'s view of eschatology as \" already and not yet\".\r\nsome aspects had been already begun but some remains.\r\nfinal stage of the kingdom of god began when Jesus ascended to his heavenly throne\r\n second aspect of age to come which is already present with us is the foretaste of our eternal inheritance in the holy spirit. finally Paulk also inaugurated the new creation associated with the age to come.\r\nPaul looked forward to the return of Christ as the time when the new creation would come in all its fulness.\r\nImplications:\r\npractical focus: 1, union with Christ\r\n2 divine purpose\r\n3 Christian hope', 9, '2021-09-13 18:42:26', '2021-09-20 12:50:48', NULL),
(2676, 705, 701, 'Paul admitted that we are outwardly  wasting way a because of the troubles of this life, but he also declared that we are being inwardly renewed day by day because of the blessings of the age  to come that are already  ours. Freedom from sin and the power of the spirit enable us to delight in this daily inward renewal so that we fox our eyes on our eternal hope in Christ. Our foretaste of the age  to come help us look forward to the full banquet that waits us at Christ\'s return.\r\nRomans 8:18\r\nOur present sufferings are not worth comparing with the glory that will be revealed in us. \r\nOur present distresses are only temporary. Jesus win eventually end this present evil age and recreate the world as a glorious gift for his children. \r\n the blessings that still lie ahead of us are so astounding that they utterly overshadow any trails we experience in this life.\r\n so this already not yet paradigm help us shape our understanding of suffering that it is temporary and it gives us hope for the future by pointing out that we have already begun to enjoy many benefits of the age to come. we have the foretaste of our eternal inheritance\r\n2 Corinthians 4:16-18', 9, '2021-09-13 18:55:47', '2021-09-20 12:50:48', NULL),
(2677, 706, 702, 'First missionary journey of Paul began by sailing to isle of Cyprus., then they moved to the western city of Paphos. From Cyprus , Paul and Barnabas many of the Jews responded positively. they moved further cast in the province of Galatia.  Paul and Barnabas travelled east in Galatia as far as Derbe. The assembly in Jerusalem addressed some of the same issues as Paul\'s letter to the Galatians. \r\n there were two main problems in Galatian churches: the influx of Gentiles into these churches and the rise of false teachers. \r\nMost of the Jews in Galatia rejected the gospel, when Paul faced this widespread opposition and he came to realize that god wanted him to focus on reaching Gentiles.  and the influx of Gentiles actually caused serious problems in the Galatia churches and these problems incited Jewish false teachers to rise in reaction.\r\nthe influx of Gentiles led to all kinds of theological and Jewish identity. so Paul wrote letter , in the book of Galatia, so we know that he wrote this letter after his missionary journey. he wrote it to suggest ways against influx and false teachers.', 8, '2021-09-20 17:47:16', '2021-11-17 04:24:30', NULL),
(2678, 706, 703, 'Paul \'s  call and training in 1:11-17, Pail\'s meeting with church leaders in Jerusalem in 2:1-10 and Paul\'s conflict in Syrian Antioch in 2:11-21\r\n Call and training: the account of Paul\'s call and training explains how Paul had the authority to resist circumcising Gentiles. it begins with a description of how Paul had loved the traditions of Israel . Paul had sent three years in Arabia learning the gospel and Christian doctrines directly from Jesus.\r\nMeeting with Leaders:\r\nit reports Pauls\' meeting with the leaders often church in Jerusalem . fourteen years after and earlier privet meeting with Peter , Paul met with the leaders of the church in Jerusalem,\r\nconflict with Peter: Paul\'s; third historical account found in 2:11-21 describes with conflict with peter in Syrian Antioch. Peter had feely associated with uncircumcised believers. peter began to fear for his reputation among the some strict Jewish believers from Jerusalem,\r\nPaul wrote about his conflict to prove that when Paul had submitted to his authoritative correction in this matter. \r\n so all this aided in proving the  authority of Paul.', 9, '2021-09-20 17:59:13', '2021-11-17 04:24:30', NULL),
(2679, 706, 704, 'direct theological argument for his doctrine of Justification by faith. Frist Paul Appealed to the early experience of the Galatians early experience: Paul focused on the Galatians experience of the Christian faith:\r\n Galatians had received many incredible blessings from the Holy Spirit when Paul had fist begun among them . and both they and he knew that these blessing s of the spirit did not come because they kept the laws of God. God had freely given them because they had believed the gospel.\r\nAbraham\'s faith: \r\nhe argued in 3:6-4:11, that God had blessed Abraham because of faith but because of Abraham\'s obedience to God\'s law. Abraham had not earned blessing of Salvation through fleshly human efforts.\r\nGenesis 15:6 made it clear that Abraham was justified by his faith in God\'s word and not then basis of his circumcision \r\n current experience:\r\nPaul addressed the Galatian\'s current experience and to the biblical account of \r\nAbraham\'s wives and sons:\r\nFifth in God\'s promise is the way to salvation for Christian\'s just as Isaac was born to Sarah because of Abraham\'s faith. God had promise Abraham an heir through his wife Sarah, Hagar bore the child Ishmael to Abraham , but Ismael was a child of the flesh. (Galatians 4:31)', 8, '2021-09-20 18:16:26', '2021-11-17 04:24:30', NULL),
(2680, 706, 705, 'Paul taught that transition from this age of judgement to the coming age of blessing takes place from through the life, death and resurrection of Christ. by his own obedience to the Law, Christ fulfilled the Law\'s requirements for all believers. by his death in place of believers - enduring the curses of the laws on their behalf. Christ had fulfilled the requirement of the law that sin be punished by death\r\n by his resurrection on behalf of believers. Christ and those for whom he died were vindicated by the Father as worthy of glory. as a result, when believers are united to Christ by faith, God looks upon them as if they Christ himself, n that basis considers them to have died to the curse of the law with Christ and to have been raised with Christ into the new life of the ages to come. \r\nbecause we are united to Christ in this way, God looks upon Christians as if they were Christ himself.', 8, '2021-09-20 18:27:25', '2021-11-17 04:24:30', NULL),
(2681, 706, 706, 'Freedom in Christ:\r\nGalatians 5:1-15 Paul called on the Galatians to remain true  to their freedom in Christ. this position is carefully balanced.\r\nin the first place, he stressed the need to maintain Christian freedom. Christian faith is free from burdens , and he warned them to remain free because the burdens of legalism are so dangerous.\r\nChristian freedom is with an affirmation of Christian moral responsibility. we should also not use their Christian freedom from Jewish traditions as a license for disregarding god\'s moral law . \r\nChrist had released us from bondage to the law as the means to justification and power for righteous living, we still demanded that they follow the commands of god. we should think that their freedom from circumcision included freedom to violate God\'s holy character , which was the very foundation of the law.\r\nwe should balance freedom in Christ with proper respect for God\'s moral law.\r\nGod has set us free to love him, to live freely and we should not misuse this freedom to keep sinning and make no sense of God\'s law and disrespect.\r\nfreedom comes with responsibility.', 8, '2021-09-20 18:42:13', '2021-11-17 04:24:30', NULL),
(2682, 707, 932, 'Biblical truth about Trinity is not fully and explicitly revealed in the Old Testament, instead much of the doctrine for Trinity comes from the New Testament.\r\n\r\nUnderstanding of the Triune God takes place through progressive revelation where God reveals himself through the New Testamen about who he is and how he functions. \r\n\r\nThough in the Old Testament God spoke to us through various meanw but there seems to be a distance. But whereas in the New Testament times, God took a step closer to us through Jesus who dwelt among us and fullfilled the mandate of the Father. Furthermore through the Holy Spirit, God is even more closer to us, he is within us and the implications are ublieveable for a believer. We have the power of the resurrection of Jesus that lives inside of us. \r\n\r\nBut it\'s the same God in the Old Testament, but there is a clearer revelation provided by God first through the manifestation of his Son, Jesus Christ and thereafter the indwelling of the promised Holy Spirit among believers. \r\n\r\nHence more than Old Testament, New Testament brings about a clearer revelation about the Triune God.', 8, '2021-09-21 15:12:55', '2021-11-17 04:23:47', NULL),
(2683, 707, 933, 'Revelation Chapter 5, John used the imagery of both the Lamb and the Lion to describe Jesus. \r\n\r\nAs we read - John is shown a scroll being held by God the Father on the throne, and a mighty angel asked if anyone could open this scroll. As John sees no one - he starts weeping - since the scroll holds the secret to the world\'s future.\r\n\r\nBut suddenly one of the elder said, Do not week, the Lion of the tribe of  Judah, the root of David has prevailed to open the scroll. \r\n\r\nLion is a symbol of royalty, from the tribe of Judah - picture of a reigning king from the line of David. \r\nLion represents Jesus Kingly Majesty \r\n\r\nJohn truns around expecting to see a lion, but instead he sees a lamb a symbol of sacrifice & suffering. \r\n\r\nThis is the beautiful pickture of who Jesus is, because he is the conquering King, he is the Messiah. He is Lord of all, yet he accomplishes salvation by suffering and dying as the sacrificial Lamb.\r\n\r\nLamb represents his gentleness and more importantly symbolized him as a redeemer of sins. \r\n\r\nOnce Jesus takes the scroll, the entire court of heaven worshiped and praised him demonstrating the divinity of Jesus Christ, in the presence of the God the Father.', 9, '2021-09-21 15:30:23', '2021-11-17 04:23:47', NULL),
(2684, 707, 934, 'The first chapter of John and the first verse has been used throughout history as proof that Jesus is God the Son, the second person in the Trinity.\r\n\r\nThe first verse says that \r\nIn the beginning was the Word, the Word was with God and the Word was God.\r\n\r\nBefore there was anything else, in the very beginning there was the Word. Jesus is God since the very beginning. He is also involved in creation of all things. He himself is uncreated. He is the eternal and uncreated Creator.\r\n\r\nJohn 1:1 Teaches us about the deity of Christ\r\nRevised English Bible says : What was God was, the Word was. In essence the Word is identical to God.\r\n\r\nThen it says the Word was God, it says in the most concise way possible that the second person in the Trinity is equal in authority, in worship, in attributes to the first person of the Trinity.\r\n\r\nThis verse identifies Jesus as a divine person, He is fully God himself. \r\n\r\nJohn 1:1 undisputedly teaches us about the deity of Jesus, that Jesus is God.', 8, '2021-09-21 15:43:17', '2021-11-17 04:23:47', NULL),
(2685, 707, 935, 'The Spirit of God is a person, distinct from the person of God as mentioned in a number of passages from the Scripture.\r\n\r\nExplicit Trinitarian passages from the Scriptures for instance\r\nMathew 28 : Baptizing in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\r\nJohn 14-16 Spirit functioning only as a person would do. Jesus says that he will send another advocate like himself, the Holy Spirit, that would convict the world concering sin and righteousness and judgement, just like Jesus did\r\n\r\nSome of the Attributes of the personality of the Holy Spirit from the Scripture : \r\nThe Holy Spirit is a witness just like we are witnesses.\r\nHoly Spirit as a person, having personal qualities, attributes etc.\r\nHoly Spirit imparts gifts\r\nHoly Spirit teaches - revealing all truth\r\nHoly Spirit comforts, guides, leads\r\nHoly Spirit has emotions and can be grieved as explained by Apostle Paul in Ephesians,\r\n1st Thessalonians says - Do not quench the Holy Spirit\'s fire, or don\'t put out the fire.\r\nJesus promises in John 14  that he is going to send another one who\'s like Jesus going to bear witness to hiim.\r\nJesus clearly explains that Holy Spirit is a person like Jesus, not just an impersonal force.', 8, '2021-09-21 15:53:57', '2021-11-17 04:23:47', NULL),
(2686, 707, 936, 'Jesus is not only fully God, but he is also human. And it is through his humanity - through his life, death and resurrection we are saved. Jesus is also the eternal God, equal in Power and glory to the Father.\r\n\r\nIt was necessary for the man Jesus also to be God due to the following reasons :\r\n\r\nIf humans are to be reconciled to God - the God only can forgive sins\r\nBeing human - he has to represent us. He must be tempted in every point like as we are and yet without sin.\r\nAt the same time He must be God, since only God can forgive sin. \r\nSince Adam sinned against God, it was absolutely necessary for Jesus to be both man and at the same time God.\r\nGod was reconciling the world to himself through Christ. Christ became the perfect substitue for us.\r\nChrist\'s death on the cross has infinite value for all who believe.\r\nFather an the Son effectuating that substitution we need. That\'s why Jesus is worshipped as the Lamb who was slain,  since he purchased mankind with his blood.\r\n\r\nJesus is both God and Man. He is the second person in the Trinity. Eternal Son of God became human and lived among us. He died and rose again that we might be the children of God.', 8, '2021-09-21 16:08:42', '2021-11-17 04:23:47', NULL),
(2687, 709, 707, 'the three main problems that plagued in Thessalonians:\r\npersecution:\r\nwhen Paul had first brought the gospel to Thessalonica , the believers there had become the object of violence, enduring serious , life threatening persecution. Acts 17:5\r\nthe unbelieving Jews were brutal to Paul and the believers in Thessalonica, but Paul strengthened them through his letter. \r\nFalse prophets:\r\nthe Thessalonian church had also come under the influence of false prophets.\r\nsuffering Christians tend to orient their entire loves to ward the second coming of Christ. \r\nwhen Christians are highly concerned this the return of Christ, they were led by the false prophets. so Paul instructed the Thessalonians to test everything and to hold on to the good.\r\nChristian living:\r\nsome concerns in Christian living discouragement and irresponsibility:\r\nsome believers there thought that Jesus return was around the corner and some were discourage when it was delayed. and many number of believers passed away and their dear one were worried about the eternal fate , which led to doubts and then to discouragement.\r\nPaul assured them that although the departed saints had died physically, they were still alive with the Christ, and that they would accompany Christ when he returned.\r\n some thought that Jesus would come soon so, they lived irresponsibly , not working for their living. but Paul had encouraged attention to daily affairs', 9, '2021-09-27 17:47:46', '2021-11-17 04:22:16', NULL),
(2688, 709, 708, 'Paul second missionary journey is recorded in Acts 15:36 thought Acts 18:22. this trip began in Syria around AD 49. Paul chose Silas as his traveling companion , while Barnabas and Mark made their way to Cyprus. Paul and Silas first went through Syria and then into Cilicia, they travelled into Galatia. they stopped first in Debra and then in Lystra, where Timothy joined them. From Lystra ,the party continued through Galatia and into Phyrgia. In  a dream Paul saw a man who begged him to come to Macedonia. In response to the dream Paul and his companions sailed to Macedonia. from Athens Paul travelled to Corinth. it was during his second missionary journey that Paul had written this letter two of them to Thessalonians . Paul was in Athens he sent back to Thessalonica to encourage believers there. this would have been around ad 49 , shortly after Paul an his company left Thessalonica then Timothy had informed Paul a number of serious misunderstandings and practical problems that had arisen in then Thessalonian church. so he wrote these two letters', 8, '2021-09-27 17:59:49', '2021-11-17 04:22:16', NULL),
(2689, 709, 709, 'Paul \'s purpose in writing the second letter to the Thessalonians to confront the problems that the believers that  they are facing and Paul to guide them regarding the problems. in them he encouraged them by telling them again how exemplary faith was, first two part salutation and closing. in the third section Paul explained that he constantly prayed for the Thessalonians and they were not alone in their struggles.\r\nInstructions:\r\nPaul instructed about Jesus; return. next in 3:1-5 Paul asked Thessalonians pray for safety and success in ministry for him and his coworkers.\r\n his chief concern was that they faithful to Christ and to his teaching, living responsibly in their daily lives and gaining proper outlook on the Lord\'s return.', 7, '2021-09-27 18:10:23', '2021-11-17 04:22:16', NULL),
(2690, 709, 710, 'Paul\'s two letters to the Thessalonians are similar in two ways:\r\nPaul express his confidence and his joy in this church, as well as his gratitude for their faithfulness in his absence. Paul addressed about the serious problems in both the letters, his chief concern was to they be faithful to Christ and to his teaching, living. responsibly on their daily lives and gaining a proper outlook on the Lord\'s return. \r\n difference:\r\nhis second letter to Thessalonians mentioned about man of lawlessness, apostasy, rebellion etc. \r\nPaul was still deeply impressed with the Thessalonians  and he also encouraged them by telling them again how exemplary their faith was and how he boasted to other churches about their perseverance', 7, '2021-09-27 18:22:14', '2021-11-17 04:22:16', NULL),
(2691, 709, 711, 'Paul wanted to preach in the province of Asia, but the holy spirit did not permit him. but in dream Paul saw a man who begged him o come to Macedonia in response to this dream , Paul and his companions immediately sailed for Macedonia.\r\nPaul immediately responded  to this dream by sailing for that province.\r\nsimilarly, we may have some idea and plan of our own , but when we are guided by the holy Spirit, we should obey he that gentle voice  and follow .\r\nthe plans that God has is perfect for us. though it is very difficult to come out of our comfort zone and go another way from our secured plan , but God has something lese and much beautiful waiting for us. like how Paul planted many churches in Greece, including those in Philippi and Thessalonica in the north.\r\nwe should respond positively when God calls us to things we haven\'t planned.', 7, '2021-09-27 18:32:50', '2021-11-17 04:22:16', NULL),
(2692, 711, 712, 'Paul began his journey around the year ad 52. he started it in Antioch in Syria, he strengthened the believers throughout Galatia and Phyrgia. In Ephesus , Paul encountered twelve disciples of john the Baptist. Paul and his companions came into conflict with the artisans who crafted shrines of Artemis. the artisans rioted threatening the safety of some of Paul\'s associates. After this event , Paul and his traveling companions spent several months in Macedonia and Achaia. Paul and his companions disembarked at treas. Paul was preaching and young man named Eutychus fell asleep and died Paul wrote his first letter from Ephesus to Corinth, but after that the briefly visited Corinth, during which he grieviously was offended by a member of he church. subsequent to his visit, he wrote a letter that is now lost to us called sorrowful letter. Problems is Corinth:\r\n\' damaged relationships\'\r\n Paul received a report that the believers in Corinth were tried against each other by identifying themselves with which teacher they held in esteem.\r\n\'Sexual misconduct:\r\nmany Corinthians believed that because Jesus had come, sexual matters were no longer significant from this attitude\r\nabuses in worship\r\n: the mistreatment of the poor during the Lord\'s supper gender roles\r\nRejection of apostolic authority \r\n all this deep concerns about the people in Corinth', 8, '2021-10-04 17:47:31', '2021-11-17 04:21:21', NULL),
(2693, 711, 713, 'Paul\'s purpose behind writing the first letter to the Corinthians  was to address the main three issues and to give some clarified instructions there were divisions in the church, responses to the reports immorality and Christian associations.\r\n\' the main points in the letter was to instruct then regarding the division that Jesus is their primary leader and all other leaders are as Christians servants. \r\nsecond instructed them regarding the immorality . he wanted the Corinthian church to practice proper church doctrine even excommunicating offenders of necessary. he wanted then to maintain their relationships with unbelievers in the world, but to distance themselves from the gross sinners. \r\n then he guided them regarded the marriage, to involve sexual involvemnet.in marriage and not practice asceticism  and sexual abstinence. \r\nhe discusses about meals. in next chapters 11, he turned related to worship , gender roles, which he nd mistreatment of the poor. in chapter 12, he addressed problems related to the uses if spiritual gifts. \r\nin  chapter 13, he wrote about letter.\r\nhe addressed all this  issues to instruct them in right way.', 9, '2021-10-04 18:06:28', '2021-11-17 04:21:21', NULL),
(2694, 711, 714, 'Paul wrote both the letters addressing the issues in the church of Corinth. \r\nIn both the letters he wrote about the rejection of Paul\'s authority. \r\n in first letter he instructed them about damaged relationships within the church, second sexual misconduct. abuses in worship, rejection of Paul\'s apostolic authority. \r\nin second letter to Corinth, he wrote about \r\n Paul\'s conduct, he defines his ministry, he instructed regarding collection for Jerusalem, continues defining of Paul\'s  ministry, he discusses about Paul\'s upcoming visit. \r\nPaul applied the heart of his theology to their problems in both the letters calling on believes to reevaluate their over realized eschatology, there by to repent their sin , to learn humility, to honor one another, to hope , to strive of the future state of the kingdom of God.', 7, '2021-10-04 18:22:32', '2021-11-17 04:21:21', NULL),
(2695, 711, 715, 'Some Corinthian Christians acted as if Christ had brought in the kingdom of God in nearly all its fulness so that they were already enjoying any ,if not most of the eternal blessings that God has in mind for believers , they isolated as if Jesus had appointed them as rulers over his newly established earthly kingdom, this appears to have been the opinion especially of those Corinthians who wielded authority in Church. they imagined that Christ had given them this power because they were wiser and ore church spiritual that the others, and they looked down n others who in their eyes had not merited such great rewards they thought they had merited status and honor but in fact was Christ who had given them these things . they knew that Christ\'s loyal followers would reign on the restored earth with him one day, but they foolishly thought that their reign had begun though Christ and not yet returned as king.\r\nthis was the error in Corinthian church.', 7, '2021-10-04 18:36:46', '2021-11-17 04:21:21', NULL),
(2696, 711, 716, 'a healthy fear of God influence the way we conduct worship with each other.\r\n our God is the same now and then , in old testament and new Testament.\r\n in Leviticus 10:13. God says  \" those who comes near to me must regard me as holy. but now in the worship services , there is now fear of god. God is being patient with us.  in His abundance of mercy, we are not perishing. \r\nPaul taught people to do everything in a orderly way.\r\nwe should be aware of the presence of the holy God, Paul taught them not to indulge in sexual sin , and to flee from idolatry and not mix pagan worship with Jesus Christ. \r\nhe taught them to use the spiritual gifts wisely.', 5, '2021-10-04 18:55:28', '2021-11-17 04:21:21', NULL),
(2697, 712, 717, 'Missions originated in the heart of God. it is not something we decide to do for God, but God, reveals his purpose to us so that we may have a creative part in his mission. God sums up our awesome responsibility  and the faith he puts in us in three basic, purposes of is mission and ours.\r\nTo bring glory to God-\r\n God\' eternal plan is for his people to be to the praise of his glory(Ephesians 1:6, 12, 14). God stands as both the originator and the goal of the redemptive process. the glory of god is the ultimate goal of God\'s mission. God receives glory when manfully realizes the purpose of his existence , consciously praises God for his grace and joyfully demonstrates god\'s grace by being filled with all the fulness of God. \r\n to share the good news with the alienated :\r\nGod\'s mission includes recreating man spiritually. God restores man\'s identity and his purpose for being. the mystery of god; mission is clear, :that the gentiles should be fellow heirs and of the same body and partakers of his promise\r\nto display the wisdom of God to evil powers:\r\nRedeemed man is the primary exhibit of God\'s grace, we are not informed about all, the conflict between the two kingdoms. God intends for man to bring glory to him, to share the good news with the alienated and to display God\'s wisdom to evil powers. this is the purpose of Christian mission.', 8, '2021-10-11 17:47:35', '2021-11-17 04:20:37', NULL),
(2698, 712, 718, 'mission of God\'s people:\r\nEvery person in God\'s election chain is a link to the rest of the world. the saved are elected  to help bring the lost to a knowledge to God. we are elected as responsible partners of god to help win the victory over Satan.  Mission is God\'s choice. From generation to generation , God tried to mold his people into a nation of servant- priests. God\'s pattern for making man his partner in world redemption is clear: election, covenant, and man\'s responsible obedience by faith. when man fails to respond, God remains faithful to his promise and purpose. \r\nGod\'s role for his people:\r\nGod intends for his people to be a disciplined people, holy priests, and suffering servants to the world. God is forming a holy nation of servant priests. \r\ndisciplined people:\r\n\r\nNation of priests:\r\nPriests had many duties, they had two main functions. they represented God to man, they represented   man to god. God intended Israel to perform these same two functions in relation to the nations.\r\nservant people:\r\nthere is no way a people can become a holy nation of servant priests without suffering. God\'s people must be willing to suffer if they are to inks to bring men to god. to reject role of a suffering servant is to reject the destiny of bringing salvation to the nations.\r\nAs God\'s people our mission is to go out to the nations to take the message of salvation as disciplined  and servant people', 9, '2021-10-11 18:04:33', '2021-11-17 04:20:37', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2699, 712, 719, 'The bible teaches that God actually became man.\r\nGod himself had to break through the end of the glass. Jesus was God\'s break through. he fulfilled all the purposes of God that Israel had failed to fulfill by becoming the disciplined Son in the incarnation the suffering servant and the high priest in the crucifixion and the king of heaven and earth in the resurrection.\r\nthe incarnation:\r\nthe incarnation proves God\'s commitment to man and his partnership in mission. it demands that every person have a chance to hear the gospel. when God spoke at the beginning of time, the world was created , when God spoke by the word in the incarnation, the new humanity was created. it cost God nothing to speak the world into existence, but it cost him his Son to create the new humanity. the incarnation is the good news that the kingdom of heaven has come in Christ.\r\nthe crucifixion - the self emptying of Christ climaxed with Gethsemane and Golgotha. Philippians 2:8 \r\nJesus the lamb of God was slain before the foundation of the world. Jesus , as Priest represented man to God as well as God to man. he opened the door for every believer to become the priest. Jesus death was an act of atonement which in the old Testament meant the covering of sin through. on the basis of atonement, Jesus reconciled man to god. communion between man and god was made possible through it.\r\nthe resurrection: \r\nwhen Jesus emerged from the tomb, a new age broke on the world. Mankind had a new king. Jesus revealed through the resurrection that he had gained all authority in heaven and earth. he ruled over sin and death, every created power, he had reconciled the world to God.', 9, '2021-10-11 18:22:27', '2021-11-17 04:20:37', NULL),
(2700, 712, 720, 'Holy spirit : the inspirer of missions:\r\n the Mission of Christ could not be accomplished without the mission of the Holy Spirit. the Holy Spirit came to make God\'s people into the body of Christ. \r\nChrist had come to be with man, the holy Spirit came to be in him. through the spirit , God enables man to become all he wants him to be . the Holy spirit performs varied ministries within the body of the Christ, he regenerates , sanctifies, teachers, guides , comfort, illuminates and intercedes. most important ministry in relation to his mission is the filling of Christians for service. the Holy Spirit is the executor and the administrator of the Great commission\r\n the empowerer of the church: the lordship  of Christ gives the believer authority to bear witness to everyone. the presence of the holy Spirit gives him the spiritual ability to make an impact on those hearing the testimony. the Holy spirit works within the lost person to verify and to personalize the witness of the believer. he makes there the authority and the presence of Christ\r\nthe guide in missions: the Holy spirit determines the course of the missions . in acts we read twelve times that witness ,might have been confined and the disciples might have been satisfied with gains already made. but each times the Holy Spirit thrust them further into the fields.\r\n Christian are to witness wherever the holy Spirit has placed them until he directs them to another place .', 8, '2021-10-11 18:38:37', '2021-11-17 04:20:37', NULL),
(2701, 712, 721, 'the church lives with two identities . it is the remnant that God has chosen, sanctified and commissioned to be a blessing to the world. it is the body of Christ that must fulfill his ministry in the world, we have received the promises of God and in us all the families of the earth shall be blessed. \r\n priests of God:\r\n the Calvary principle: formerly we were Christless, homeless, hopeless, godless but now we have been made one with each other. as the royal priest hood, we receive both the promises and the responsibilities of the new covenant,  royalty we live in the blessed ness of the kingdom, we function as representatives servants of God:\r\n the incarnation principle:\r\nthe church as the body of Christ is incarnate in the world to become the suffering servant. it is not called out of the world but called to go into the world and love like Christ, forgive like Christ and give itself to the world like Christ.\r\nsons of the king - the resurrection principle:\r\nAs God\'s children we have an intimate relationship and an interest in the kingdom. Jesus shared his heart with children of the kingdom to involve them in establish it. With joy we tell then good news that our father owns everything.', 9, '2021-10-11 18:53:49', '2021-11-17 04:20:37', NULL),
(2702, 713, 806, 'background \r\navailability:\r\nMoses was not the first person to write about the world\'s origins .  inspired Moses so that his account is true Moses wrote in a time when many nations and groups in the Near east had written many myths and epics. some of these ancient texts are well known like Enuma Eltish, or the Babylonian creation story, Gilgamesh Epic or the Babylonian flood story.\r\nThese an many other documents from the ancient world dealt with origins an dearly history of the universe. but many of the  ancient Near Eastern documents were actually available o Moses in his youth. Moses was educated in the royal courts of Egypt and his writings indicate that he knew the literature of the ancient world. As Moses wrote his won divinely inspired and true account of the primeval period , he was quite aware of the other literary traditions in the ancient Near Eats/ Interaction\r\nMoses wrote his history of the early times to counter falsehood with truth. we must always remember that the Israelites Moses had been subjected to all likings of pagan influence. They were tempted to believe that the world resulted from the efforts and struggles of may gods. they either rejected the true faith of their patriarchs or they mixed this truth with the religious beliefs of there nations. Moses wrote this account of the primeval times to tach the people of God the way things actually happened. Moses accomplished this negative purpose of refuting false myths by interacting positively with the literary traditions of his time.', 9, '2021-10-12 17:49:45', '2021-11-17 04:19:32', NULL),
(2703, 713, 807, 'basic overview Genesis 1:1-2:3\r\nit has a very intentional , complex structure. the passage begins with the the world in chaos and God about to move upon it. \r\noriginal meaning\r\nliterary structure\r\n the first portion :Dark chaotic world\r\n Genesis 1:1we see a dramatic tension between the chaos covering the earth and the spirit of God. the opening1-2 sets the stage by giving a title in verse 1 and by describing the initial condition of the world in verse 2.\r\nideal world:\r\nit begins with a summary statement that God had finished his creative work, nd in concludes in 2:2-3 with God at rest. by the seventh day God had finished the work he had been doing, so the seventh \r\nday he rested from all his work. God blesses the seventh day and made it holy.\r\nsix days of ordering:\r\nthis passage teaches that God restrained the chaos by ordering the world according to wondrous six plan described in 1:3-31. the central focus of this material becomes clear when we see that Moses repeatedly introduced the cations with phrase. \"and God said\": this the because God is the main character of this material and his powerful word is the focus of theses verses.', 8, '2021-10-12 18:06:24', '2021-11-17 04:19:32', NULL),
(2704, 713, 808, 'literary structure of Dark chaotic world:\r\nGenesis 1 we see a very important, dramatic the opening of 1-2 sets the stage by giving a title in verse1 and by describing the initial condition tension between the chaos covering the earth and the Spirit of God.\r\nthe opening of 1-2 sets the stage by giving a title in verse 1 and by describing the initial condition  of the world in verse 2.\r\nthe verse introduces the dramatic tension that flows through this entire chapter. on one side of this tension the world is formless and empty or as it is said in Hebrew , tohu Wabohii. the second element in the dramatic tension in 1:2 Moses wrote that the Spirit of god was hovering over the waters. the Hebrew term is used here is merachehet which means to fly above encircle above. we sees choose on the earth. on the other side  we see the spit of God hovering above the chaos. in effect God was ready to move into action to remedy the chaos which covered the earth, many scholars believer that it ,man that world was uninhabitable, hostile toward human life, so in the beginning we see that an uninhabitable ,dark, primordial, chaotic deep covered the entire earth.\r\nthe second element in the dramatic tension also appears in 1:2.', 8, '2021-10-12 18:21:07', '2021-11-17 04:19:32', NULL),
(2705, 713, 809, 'the ideal world: literary structure\r\n this section is structured very simply . It begins in 2:1 with a summary statement that God had finished his creative work and it concludes in 2:2-3 with God at rest, by the seventh day God had finished the world he had been doing: so on the seventh day, he rested from all his work. and God blessed the seventh day and made it holy\r\nwhen Moses described God as entering a state of Sabbath rest, granting a special blessing to that day and making it holy. he declared that the tension between the chaos and God\'s hovering spirit had been resolved . God had subdued the darkness  ruled over chaotic deep and delighted in his ideally ordered world. the creation story comes o an end with this delightfully peaceful visions of the universe in perfect harmony', 7, '2021-10-12 18:30:33', '2021-11-17 04:19:32', NULL),
(2706, 713, 810, 'the New Testament use creation speaks of the first coming of Christ as God\'s recreation , his reshaping of the cosmos. when we look into first the opening words of the Gospel of John in the beginning was the world and the word was with God, through him all the things were made form the outset , in the beginning God created the heavens and the earth (Genesis 1:1) then John put his readers in the framework of the creation. then John went on to say that Christ was the person of the Trinity who made all things. he was the world of God, spoken at creation, by whom the world was first made\r\nthen  John subtly shifted from Genesis to  another set of events that paralleled the creation account John 1:4-5\r\nin Genesis 1 especially the theme of the light which God brought to the dark chaotic world on the first day. John pointed to the incarnation of Christ as the light shining into the darkness of the world caused by  in, by shifting from creation to the coming of John revealed that in Christ shining against the sinful darkness of the world, God moved against then chaos of the world.', 8, '2021-10-12 18:45:10', '2021-11-17 04:19:32', NULL),
(2707, 714, 811, 'overview Genesis 2:4 to 3:24, \r\nin garden: the first dramatic step in 2:4-17, the whole place was Adam\'s splendid place to dwell Adam was given the privilege by God\' grace. He was given the job to keep the garden on God\'s behalf.\r\ncondition enhanced:\r\n2:18-25. designate humanity\'s condition enhanced. God added even greater blessings to Adam\'s life. introducing a new problem . it s not good for man to be alone. God dealt with his problem. God formed a woman and brought her to Adam. God enhanced the wonderful creation that he had made already for Adam and Eve.\r\nCondition  cursed:\r\n3;1-21. a new topic and character, the tempting serpent. 3:1-21 deals with the serpent\'s temptation and the results of his temptation . eve falls prey to the serpent\'s temptation so that she and Adam eat from the forbidden fruit and fall under divine curses.\r\nOut of garden: Genesis 3:22-24\r\n God speaking about the problem of the Tree of Life.  to deal with potential problem of Adam eating from this tree, god drove Adam from the garden and placed cherubim and  a flaming sword to guard the entrance to Eden. Human beings have access to the garden of Eden apart from the a direct intervention by God.', 8, '2021-10-19 17:42:34', '2021-11-17 04:18:39', NULL),
(2708, 714, 812, 'to explore the symmetries  we will look at the the balance which exists between the beginning and  ending of our narrative, then symmetry at the two middle portions of the story\r\nbeginning and ending:\'\r\nGenesis 2:4-17 and 3:22 and 24 the account begins with 2:7 with God placing Adam within the paradise of garden all divine blessings , life giving water, narrative ends with God expelling Adam and Eve from the garden.\r\nsecond 2:4-17 states about tree of Life and tree of the knowledge of good and veil. 3:22-24, God was no loner concerned about the tree of the knowledge of God and evil, free access humanity once ad to garden had been lost\r\n3;15, we see humanity\'s commission god commissioned Adam to blessed work in the garden with no pain and no difficulty.3:23, we see God banished Adam and eve from the garden and condemned them to difficult toil outside of the garden,\r\nMiddle portions: 2:18-25, and 3:1-21 humanity\'s relationship with God. harmonious relationship with Adam and God. 2:18. God expressed concern for Adam and brought Adam a perfect partner in Eve,  a third contrast in humanity\'s relationship with evil.', 9, '2021-10-19 17:56:16', '2021-11-17 04:18:39', NULL),
(2709, 714, 813, 'Moses concerned the primeval Garden of Eden 6 and Israel\'s conquest of Canaan, first Moses portrait of the garden of Eden, his focus on the requirement of loyalty from Adam and Eve, and third his depiction of the curses placed on Adam and Eve. Moses description the Garden of Eden in ways that identified Eden with Promised land. From Moses\' perspective , the land to which he was leading in his day was actually the location of the primeval Eland called Eden. identity of Eden an holiness of Eden,\r\nLoyalty:\r\n the theme of loyalty was crucial to Moses\' story about Eden, Eden was a place of tremendous blessing, required moral responsibility. Moses wanted Israelites to remember that the promised Land to which they were going to remember that the promised Land to which they were going also required Israel to ne loyal to God\'s commands\r\nConsequences: to see the Adam\'s and Eve\'s death pain and exclusion \r\ndeath : Moses explained that God threatened Adam and eve with death as s consequence of sin. the Lord had told these Israelites they would surely die in the desert and not one of them was left Except Caleb \r\nMoses certainly wrote of hic consequence of Adam\'s sin to explain how death came into the world, but his purpose was also more directly related to the experience of the Israelites to whom he wrote.', 9, '2021-10-19 18:13:50', '2021-11-17 04:18:39', NULL),
(2710, 714, 814, 'Paul in romans 5:14 death reigned from time of Adam to , romans 5:18,19 says just as the result of one trespass was condemnation for all men, so also the result of one act of righteousness was justification that brings life for all men, Adam\'s one trespass resulted in condemnation, but Christ\'s one act of righteousness resulted in justification for all men, because the disobedience of one man  Adam made us sinners, but the obedience of the new man , Christ made us righteous. the only way for paradise lost to be found again is through the righteous obedience of Christ. we cannot enter the paradise of salvation as individuals standing on our merits before God. we need an absolutely perfect representative to enter paradise before us and Christ is that representative we find the salvation  of eternal life in God\'s presence only because. Christ was fully obedient. Jesus earned the right to enter into paradise and those who place their faith in him shall enter.\r\nMatthew:\r\n4:11Jesus was led by the spirit into the Desert. 4:2 Christ was in the wilderness for forty days, hunger was an important feature in Christ\' temptation. Jesus Christ was the last Adam who succeeded where in the wilderness , and for this reason , he entered paradise.', 9, '2021-10-19 18:30:59', '2021-11-17 04:18:39', NULL),
(2711, 714, 815, 'in Genesis 2:18, we see a harmonious relationship between Adam and God. god and human race in intimacy and at peace. nut after sinning the disharmony replaces the initial harmony. similarly in the modern world , we see, when we sin we against god we become far from in all aspects. there is no peace between we and God. all our relationship with God is broken . second disharmony we see in human relationships. \r\n we see in genesis 2:23, how there is deep relationship between fist husband and wife, then in Genesis 3:16 God pronounced a curse on this relationship that strife would be between the man and woman, the woman would desire her husband, and he will rule her ,same we see now in the modern world not only husband wife relationships but all human relationships are distorted and in strife and quarrel due to sin. greed , violence and strife are present everywhere.\r\nbrother is against brother, and so we see that sin has ruined every relationships. only Christ is the way which can bring us closer to each other and to God.', 8, '2021-10-19 18:43:55', '2021-11-17 04:18:39', NULL),
(2712, 715, 806, 'availability\r\nMoses was not the first person to write about the world\'s origins. God inspired Moses , so that his account is true. some of these ancient are well known like Enuma Eltsh or gilhagmaesh Eic. theses and many other documents from the ancient world dealt with the origins and early history of the universe. but many of these ancient Near Eastern documents were available to Moses in his youth. Moses was educated out if the curtain the royal courts of Egypt and his writings indicate that he knew the literature of the ancient world. \r\nInteraction: \r\nMoses wrote his history of the early times to encounter falsehood with truth. we must always remember that the Israelites Moses led had been subjected to all kinds of pagan influence, they were tempted to believe that the world resulted from the efforts and struggles of many gods, they either rejected the truth faith of they patriarchs or they mixed this truth with there religious belief of other nations. Moses wrote his account of the primeval times to teach the people of the God the way things actually happened . he accomplished this negative purpose of refuting false myths by interacting positively with the literary traditions of his time.', 9, '2021-10-22 17:41:59', '2021-11-17 04:17:38', NULL),
(2713, 715, 807, 'overview of Genesis 1:1-2:3 \r\nthree major steps: \r\nbeginning\r\ndark chaotic world:\r\ndescribes the initial condition of the world the earth was formless and empty tohu wabohii in hebrew. the world was uninhabitable , hostile toward human  life.\r\nideal world: begins with 2:1 with a summary statement that God had finished his creative work and it includes in 2:2-3 with God at rest, by the seventh day God had finished the work he had been doing, so on the seventh day rested form all his work. the tension between the chaos and God\'s hovering spirit had been resolved . God subdues the darkness , ruled over the chaotic deep and enlightened in hid ideally ordered world. Six days of offering: God restrained the chaos by ordering the world according to  a wondrous six day plan the central focus is \" and God said: God is the main  character of this and his powerful word is the focus of these verses.\r\n it is good , it is very good. Moses made it very clear that God was wonderfully pleased with what he had done.', 8, '2021-10-22 17:54:56', '2021-11-17 04:17:38', NULL),
(2714, 715, 808, 'literary structure: \r\nDark chaotic world:\r\nGen 1:1-2\r\nopening the stage by giving a title in verse1 and by describing the condition of the world inverse 2. it starts with the dramatic tension that flows through this entire chapter, one one side of the world  , the world if formless and empty , tohu wabohii. it means that the world was uninhabitable, hostile toward human life, much like a desert or wilderness is inhospitable, dark primordial , chaotic deep covered the entire earth. the second element in the dramatic tension also in 1:2 Moses wrote that spirit of God was hovering over the waters. the Hebrew term used here is merachefet which means to fly above orto encircle above. on one side we see chaos on the earth, on the earth side, we see the spirit of God hovering above the chaos . in effect , God was ready to move into action to remedy the chaos that covered the earth.', 8, '2021-10-22 18:04:08', '2021-11-17 04:17:38', NULL),
(2715, 715, 809, 'literary structure: it begins in 2:1 with a summary statement that God had finished his creative work and it concludes in 2:2-23 with god at rest. by the seventh day God finished the work he had been doing, son on the seventh day he rested form all his work and God blesses the seventh day and made it holy. Moses described god as entering a state of Sabbath rest, special blessing to that day and making it holy, he declared that the tension between the chaos and God\'s hovering spirit had been resolved . God subdued the darkness, ruled over the chaotic deep and delighted in his ideally ordered world. the creation comes to an end with this delightfully peaceful vision of the universe in perfect harmony.  Moses creation account begins and ends we should look at the middle portion of this passage which describes how the tension between the chaotic  world and God\'s hovering spirit was resolved.', 8, '2021-10-22 18:12:08', '2021-11-17 04:17:38', NULL),
(2716, 715, 810, 'the new Testament writers speaks of  the first coming  of Christ\'s as God\'s re-creation, his reshaping of the cosmos. creation a lens of or interpreting the inauguration of Christ\'s kingdom. opening words of the gospel. john 1:1-3. John\'s gospel starts off with \" in the beginning:\" this come form Genesis 1:1. John put his readers in the framework of the creation account in Genesis\r\n2 Corinthians 4:6. there Paul explained the glory of his ministry in this way. Paul directly referred to Genesis 1 These words he focused first in the original ordering of creation with appearance of light but then drew attention to important parallel to the creation story. the apostle said the the inauguration of Christ\'s kingdom that to me when Christ\'s face could be seen on earth-was best understood when was related to the prototype of God\'s creative work.', 8, '2021-10-22 18:25:58', '2021-11-17 04:17:38', NULL),
(2717, 716, 816, 'literary structure of Genesis 4:1-6:8 Moses writes with a unified purpose\r\n is early violence and hope:\r\n narratives: the story begins with Cain and Abel living harmoniously later we see a different circumstance. Cain is banished from good land , from his family and form God\'s special presence CGenesis 4-5 convey a pattern of early violence\r\nCain murdered his brother . God warned Cain, but sin mastered Cain becomes the first murderer in human history. verse 9-15 we see God\' curse and protection to Cain. \r\n later violence hope:\r\nGenealogies : the first genealogy record\'s Cain \'s sinful lineage. second genealogy lists Seth\'s righteous lineage in 5:1-32a strong contrast in Seth\'s lineage Enoch and Cain\'s lineage Lamech. Lamech was a murderer and took pride in his murderous exploits. \r\nGenesis 6:8 we see sons of God and Nephilim \r\n these verses describes threatening events that took place n the earth. \r\nwe see a violation of women. \r\nthe corruption of human race had grown to such proportions that sin utterly dominated humanity. \r\ngod saw how Nephilim terrorized the world and determined that it was time to intervene with massive , world wide destruction. \r\nin verse 8 we see even though god determined to destroy humanity because of the corruption of sin, there was one man who provided hope.', 9, '2021-10-23 16:48:06', '2021-11-17 04:16:31', NULL),
(2718, 716, 817, 'Moses reacted the chapters of his primeval history to his contemporary world by describing the violence of early history in  ways that closely resembled the experiences of violence which Israel had endured. he pointed out that the troubles which Israel faced were very similar to the troubles of the primeval world. Genesis 4:1-6:8. scenario of early violence and hope of deliverance and second scenario  of late violence and hope of deliverance in 6:1-8\r\nfirst connection: Moses mentioned the occupations of Cain and Abel to create these associations . he wanted Israelite readers to associate Cain the agriculturalist and the Egyptian . Israel, shepherd \r\nsecond connection; Moses original audience appearance in the motif of the sacrifices offered by Cain an Abel. Abel offered sincere bringing fat portions, Cain\'s sacrifice in insincere ritual. \r\nthird way Moses created connections with Israel\'s experience was through motif of murder Egyptians killed the infants of Israelites. fourth way Moses created describing Cain\'s location on the earth. god cursed Cain for murdering Abel, he banished Cain from the fertile land.\r\nfifth way Genesis 4 and 5 connected Cain with Egypt and Abel. Cain protection even though Cain killed his brother Abel, God still protected him from harm.', 9, '2021-10-23 17:04:03', '2021-11-17 04:16:31', NULL),
(2719, 716, 818, 'the themes of occupations , worship and murder , location and protection all indicate that Moses wanted his Israelite readers to apply this story to their lives by associating Cain with their the Egyptians and by associating Abel with themselves as the people of God. Moses mentioned the fact that his readers would identify themselves not only with Abel, but also with Seth , Adam\'s son who replaced Abel. Israel would have associated themselves with righteous Seth. through the theme. he also wanted his readers to see that they themselves were like Abel and Seth. he wanted his readers to identify themselves with Abel and Seth to recognize that Cain and in his line Lamech was a murderer and so were Egypt. and Seth was righteous and so was Enoch. how the lineage of Seth was blessed nd numerous just like Israel was numerous in Egypt. \r\n to draw connections between early violence and hope of deliverance. Moses wanted his readers to respond positively to the connections by worshipping God sincerely just like how Abel did.', 7, '2021-10-23 17:21:44', '2021-11-17 04:16:31', NULL),
(2720, 716, 819, 'Moses contrasted the lives of Cain and Abel. we all want to say and believe that we are like Abel, but it is true that somewhere sometimes we also become like Cain. being born again in Christ I am like Abel. like Abel. I ought to offer sincere sacrifice to God. I ought to serve and Worship God .\r\nMoses was hoping to accomplish by contrasting various characters that like his readers we should not be like Cain who  offered insincere sacrifice   and then when God asked him to have victory over sin, he surrendered to sin and murdered his own brother. and later he was banished by God. he was a wanderer. he was cursed by god. so we should identify ourselves with Abel and follow our god, worship him. then we will be blessed by Him.\r\n Moses was hoping that Israelites would identify themselves as Abel and worship God by  offering living sincere sacrifice to God and offer sincere devotion to God.', 7, '2021-10-23 17:32:31', '2021-11-17 04:16:31', NULL),
(2721, 716, 820, 'despite the corruption and violence of mankind , a thread of hope van be traced throughout god\'s relationship with us. we can see a thread of hope in Noah. even though God determined to destroy humanity because of the corruption of sin, there was one man who provided hope. Genesis 6:2, Noah found favor in the eyes of the Lord, the deliverance came through him. similarly , we have hope n Jesus. Jesus is the thread of our hope, we saw  primeval world delivered through Noah, similarly we see violence everywhere in the world. but we have hope in Jesus , his second coming will bring the world to eternal deliverance. this gives a lot of hope. New testament craws connections between the primeval worlds of violence .', 7, '2021-10-23 17:43:14', '2021-11-17 04:16:31', NULL),
(2722, 717, 811, 'overview Genesis 2:4-3:24\r\nin Garden: God put Adam in the garden of Eden. whole garden was splendid place to well and to work. then the concerns goes down to the creation of Adam and the commission to work within the garden.\r\nCondition enhanced: Gen 2:18-25 God added even greater blessings to Adam\' s life. God makes a    suitable helper for Adam \r\ncondition enhanced: the introduction of a new topic and character, the tempting serpent. 3:1-21 deals with serpent\'s temptation and the results of his temptation. Eve falls prey to the serpents\' temptation so that she and Adam eat from the forbidden fruit and fall under divine curses. \r\nout of garden:\r\n 3:22-24 God speaking about the problem of tree of life. God drove Adam from the garden and placed cherubim and  a flaming sword to guard the entrance to Eden. \r\nhuman beings would no longer have access to the garden of Eden from a direct intervention by God.', 8, '2021-10-25 17:47:12', '2021-11-17 04:15:31', NULL),
(2723, 717, 812, 'dramatic symmetry: \r\nbeginning and ending Genesis 2:4-17 and 3:22 -24\r\nthe first contrast is in location God placing him in paradise garden, divine blessings, wonderful vegetation , life giving water\r\nby contrast narrative ends in 3:24  God expelling Adam and eve from the garden, \r\nsecond contrast special trees of the garden end of the narrative God no longer concerned with the tree of knowledge of God and evil. but exclusively with the tree of life. this difference is in humanity\'s commission  God commissioned Adam to blessed work in the garden  with no pain and difficulty. god banished Adam and Eve from the garden and condemned them to difficult toil outside of the garden. \r\nmission portions: one contrast focuses on humanity\'s relationship with god, God concerned with Adam and brought human a perfect partner. yet disharmony replaces the initial harmony between God and human race. Adam and Eve disobeyed God\'s command . second contrast we see in human relationships. a third contrast appears in humanity\'s relationship.', 7, '2021-10-25 18:00:14', '2021-11-17 04:15:31', NULL),
(2724, 717, 813, 'Moses connected the primeval Garden of Eden and Israel\'s conquest of Canaan at three elements of hic story: fist Moses\' portrait of the Garden of Eden , second his focus on the requirement of loyalty from Adam and Eve, and third  his depiction of the cursed placed on Adam and Eve. Moses described the garden of Eden in ways that identified Eden with the promised Land. first the identity f Eden. and second the holiness of Eden.\r\nidentity Moses referred back to Genesis 2 to teach Israel that the land of Eden, fertile crescent was the land God promised to Israel, the land to which he was taking them. \r\nHoliness: the primary ways Moses conveyed the holiness of Eden was to describe it in terms that he also used to describe the tabernacle. many aspects indicate that it was a holy place of God\'s special presence, much like tabernacle. Moses spoke of  service in Eden with language that he used else where for Levitical service in the tabernacle. Moses noted the holiness of Eden was his focus on gold onyx. Fourth connection between the garden of Eden and the tabernacle is the presence of gospel', 8, '2021-10-25 18:17:03', '2021-11-17 04:15:31', NULL),
(2725, 717, 814, 'Paul gives his view point in Romans 5:14. he wrote death reigned from the tie of Adam to the time of Moses. Just as the result of one trespass was condemnation for all men , so also the result of one cat of righteousness was justification that brings life for all men. because the disobedience of one man , Adam made us sinners , but the obedience of one man Christ made us righteous, the only way for paradise life lost it to be found again is through the righteous obedience of Christ, we cannot enter as individual the paradise of salvation standing in our own merits before God. Christ was fully obedient to the father, Jesus earned the right to enter into paradise and only those who placed their faith in him may enter along. \r\nMatthew:\r\nMatthew 4:2, location of Christ\'s temptation connects it with Israel as the Israelites followed Moses. Jesus spent in the wilderness. Christ was the last Adam who succeeded where the first Adam failed, Christ overcame temptation in the wilderness reversing Israel\'s failure. and for this reason he entered eternal paradise. Paul and Matthew taught that salvation in Christ inaugurates a return to paradise', 8, '2021-10-25 18:31:33', '2021-11-17 04:15:31', NULL),
(2726, 717, 815, 'Moses recounted how the evil f sin damaged relationship between God and his people, as well as relationships between people. same we can see in the modern world. when we see, our relationship with God is also effected severely. our hearts become cold and hard to him. we no longer notice and admire his live and gradually we grow hard to his commandments, his command seem burdensome to us. \r\nSam we way we can see relations with people. sin breaks our relationship with peoples. we become cold and cruel to our fellow human beings, we human relationships are characterized by difficulty and struggle, we can only see strife , quarrel and wars everywhere, selfish ness and ego is all and everywhere. \r\n when we receive issues in to our life then only we see our relationships with God and with fellow human beings healed and rebuild and repaired and restored', 9, '2021-10-25 18:41:45', '2021-11-17 04:15:31', NULL),
(2727, 718, 821, 'five step drama of flood of deliverance:\r\n initial covenant: Genesis 6:9-22 Got noted that Noah was righteous man. God spoke to Noah and revealed why he planned to destroy the human race. \r\nGod planned to start all over again by delivering one man and his family.\r\nEnduring covenant :\r\nGod returned to Noah after the flood and made another covenant of a new order in the world. \r\ntwo covenant as long as the earth endure , seed an harvest, cold an heat m summer n winter  day an night will never cease. second covenant: promises that flood will never again destroy the earth and the God setting his bow in clouds. \r\nEscape with water: Genesis 7:1-16 Noah and his family and the animals he had gathered were safely sealed in the ark.\r\nExit to dry land: Noah\'s exit to dry land, dry land appear after leaving\r\nDivine remembrance:\r\nGenesis 7:17, 8:5 description of flood raging and destroying every living thing n the earth. flood begun to subside. God remembered Noah and all the animals\r\nGod had great mercy, God did not forget the covenant he made with Noah and those with him.\r\nMoses wrote about the flood as a story of deliverance.', 9, '2021-10-26 17:44:32', '2021-11-17 04:14:38', NULL),
(2728, 718, 822, 'Sons of Noah:\" Moses record of the sons of the Noah in chapters 9 and 10. 9: 20-29 set forth divisions among the sons. led to a major distinction among the descendants of Noah. Noah cursed Canaan , the son of Ham. Canaan would be the lowest of slaves to his brothers. he pronounced blessings on his other sons Shem and Japheth. Moses included this story in his description of the new order after the flood flood because the entire human race came from the three sons of Noah, the distinctions made here led to the dynamic of human relationships seen from this time forward in Biblical history. defeat of babel:\r\n humanity over the earth as he confused human language they intended to build a city with a great tower reaching to heaven . the account pf Noah\'s son shows that the new order includes complex interactions among the different groups of human beings. it also includes more defiance of God a s well as God\'s eternal defat of those who defy him, Although theses strictures of the new order may seem strange to our modern ears , we see that they rather to plainly to the experiences of the Israelites to whom Moses fist wrote these chapters.', 8, '2021-10-26 17:56:59', '2021-11-17 04:14:38', NULL),
(2729, 718, 823, 'there are eight connections between Noah and Moses first: Moses drew  connection between himself and Noah in the motif of violence. Moses deliverance from Egypt came in response to the violence inflicted on the people of Israel. \r\nsecond connection appears in Moses uses the term ark Moses pointed to the fact that both he and Noah had been delivered from the water deaths by means of an ark or tevah. \r\n thirdly the importance of divine covenants also establish Noah and precursor of Moses. Moses was chosen to lead the people of Israel into a special covenant with Yahweh as they came to Mount Sinai\r\nthe central role of judgement through water also establishes a fourth connection between the two men. \r\nMoses thought Israel out of Egypt by passing through the waters of Red Sea, fifthly God set wind to drive back the waters in both the days of Noah and the days of Moses, Exodus 14:21 the Red Sea, the Lord drove back the sea with a string east wind.\r\nsixth connection is on emphasis on animals. just as God ordained Noah to bring animals into the world of his day, God also ordained that Moses should bring animals into the promised land\r\nseventh the theme of divine remembrance also joins Noah an Moses. same way God declared to Moses that he delivered Israel form Egypt because he remembered his covenant. divine remembrance played a vital role in the flood and in the exodus finally the blessing nature also associated Noah and Moses. in a similar fashion Moses told Israel that in the Land of promise nature would remind blessing and beneficial in the same way.', 8, '2021-10-26 18:15:13', '2021-11-17 04:14:38', NULL),
(2730, 718, 824, 'in the inauguration: in the inauguration of the kingdom , Christ accomplished a great salvation on the behalf of his people:\r\ncovenant: Christ brought deliverance to his people by means of covenant that rescued them from then judgement of God. Noah played a special role as the mediator of covenant and Moses are upon this fact a she explained his own ministry to Israel. new Testament teaches that Christ is or deliverer because he mediated a new covenant when he came to this earth. Mohan delivered from judgement the mediator a divine covenant in the inauguration of the kingdom Jesus delivered those who trusted him from judgement by meditating the new covenant through his blood, which he she on the cross. Victory:\r\n Jesus earthly ministry fulfilled the theme of victory in holy war. Moses focused on the theme pf holy are as part of the new order after the flood , he established the the new order and described Christ\'s victory at the inauguration of the kingdom in Colossians 2:15\r\nJesus not only delivered through his new covenant but be was also victorious the spiritual forces if darkness in his death and resurrection followers of Christ look to Christ; earthly ministry as the beginning of the final victory promised long ago in the book of Genesis.', 8, '2021-10-26 18:31:20', '2021-11-17 04:14:38', NULL),
(2731, 718, 825, 'Genesis 6:9-11:9 in the light of the inauguration  continuation and consummation and in inauguration of the kingdom , Christ accomplished a great salvation on the behalf of his people. Jesus delivered those who trusted him from the judgement by deviating the new covenant through his blood , which he shed on the cross. Jesus not only delivered though his new covenant , but he was also victorious over the spiritual forces of darkness in his death and resurrection. \r\nNew Testament connects Noah\'s deliverance through then flood to our deliverance through baptism , also teaches that just as the primeval world was delivered to warfare, Christian baptism delivers us to engage in spiritual warfare every day of all lives. the new testament teaches us to view the return of Christ in terms of Noah\'s flood. in an even greater way, when Christ returns in glory, there will be cataclysm that utterly disrupts the world as we know it. the wicked will be removed from the earth and all who follow Christ will be delivered into a grand and eternal new heavens and earth. when Christ returns in glory, this divine purpose will  be fully realized, when Christ return in glory, this divine purpose . in Christ God has delivered us from the tyranny of sin, just as he delivered the primeval world through Noah, he also has sets on a path that requires a period of conflict and struggle as we  when Christ brings ultimate victory to his people.', 9, '2021-10-26 18:50:05', '2021-11-17 04:14:38', NULL),
