(2817, 738, 979, 'Teching of Jesus \r\nTo understand what Jesus taught about that relevance and authority of the old testament for his follower, we look briefly into two sides of his teaching, we started with some passg \r\nMost of sermons of mountain \r\nMathews- 5: 21- \r\nMathew 5:31- jes5 spok divorce \r\nMathew- 5:38 revenge \r\nMathew 5 : 43\r\nLove and enemy in the way \r\n\r\nMany believes believe jesuJesus rejecte old testament teaching in favor of his own more comprehensive instructions, now if this popular outlook is anywhere near the truth  , then we have good reson to think that Jesus come to turn \r\nJesus Christ talking many old testament teaching.. But Jesus not say old testament reject... \r\n\r\nTeaching of Paul\r\nBy and large, evangelical Christan depend heavily on the  letter of Paul in the new testament\r\nBut we misunderstood Paul attitude toward the relevance of the old testament for Christian living, gala 3:1_5 \r\nThis passage the people contr8 work of the law  and faith many have assumed this contest set the moral reaching of old testament against the moral instructions of new testament or Christian way to life', 8, '2021-12-15 16:30:15', '2022-01-15 15:21:58', NULL),
(2818, 738, 980, '1) Ancient world \r\nOne of our principles task is to interpret the old testament as much as we can in terms of the ancient world to which it was first given, but even if we hard at this, we will never do it perfect, we so deeply influenced by the world in which we live today that we\'ll always come to the ancient texts of scripture with mordan perdition that we can\'t fully escape, \r\nPut simply the original means of an old testament passage is the impact  that God and his inspired writing designed the text to have on its first audience, in a very important sense, we\'re not hearing God and other, so as we study the old testament, it\'s were overheard them speaking others \r\n\r\n2) Historical Development \r\nUnlike the holy book of some other religion, the theological teaching of the old testament developed over time, the old testament refers to the many ways God reveals truths about himself, the world people beginning in primarily time, it then continue with God  revelations in the days of Israel patrika in the days of moses \r\nAnd also during the time when Israel enter promise land, \r\nBiblical faith didn\'t remain the same through the century but it was not as if God stared one relagin, \r\n3) Our World\r\n\r\nAs Paul put it in 1 corporate 10;11 the old testament was written down for our instructions this fact required us to recognize the responsibility we have as followsrs of Christ in the modern world \r\nIf we going to apply the old testament effectively today we must always ask ourselves question', 8, '2021-12-15 16:50:18', '2022-01-15 15:21:58', NULL),
(2819, 738, 981, 'Many Christians believe mistake believe that Jesus rejected old testament\r\nBut when we see in sermon of the mountain prach we finding Jesus teaching not against old testament\r\n\r\nWhen we see Jesus say \' you have heard it said, but i say to you \' he was not saying that the old testament law is now being nulli8. In fact he say quite the opposite very explain that \' i have come to fulfill law\" But what Jesus is doing is he is using a well known rabbinic teaching authority \' you\'re heard different people in the traditional say these things about  the teaching of the law but i say to you\' and come with a present authority with additional authority with this well known teaching technique, not to discount the old testament law, but to say something very important theology\r\nMathew 5:19\r\nNotice what Jesus said in this passage, if people fail to keep, or if they encourage others to ignore even the least of the commands they will be least in the kingdom of heaven \" And whoever keeps and teaching others to keep, even the least of the be commandment will be grat in the kingdom of heaven \r\nWe all tend to be very selected in apply the old Testament to our lives,', 8, '2021-12-15 17:07:55', '2022-01-15 15:21:58', NULL),
(2820, 739, 982, 'Pas 93: 1-2 we find this praise of God.. \r\nAs we see here the first fact is God created and sustains everything led the psalmist to declare.. The Lord reigns he is the supreme king over all creation. Time and again the old Testament acknowledge this fundamental belief. God created band sustains all things, and he is sovereign over all in this border sense, this reign bor kingdom is universal and unchanging\r\nThis believe the unwavering, universal region of God as the createor and sustains is one of the most striking difference between the faith of ancient Israelites and the religious of the nation that surrounded them. Neighboring religion commonly taught that various gods compled for sovereignty and that power of these gods ebbed and flowed with changing circumstances. In some cases gods were thought to rise and fall from position of power with the annual cycle of the season. In other cases, God were believe to rise and fall from position of heavenly authority as their favored nations experience victores and defeats in war \r\nBut such concert were not part of biblical faith yahweh the God of Israel, was the sole c', 0, '2021-12-16 02:30:46', '2021-12-16 02:30:46', NULL),
(2821, 739, 983, 'This is important subject \r\nAncient Israel to the earliest stages of how the kingdom of God development on earth, \r\nAs we\'re about to see the primeval history emphasizd how God acted as king at this time.. How he exetrted his Royal authority to create. To arrange and to populate the visible world so that it would become the place where he would extend his Sovereign rule from heaven \r\nGods goals for historic was not that the earth would stay in this conditions, rather the entire earth was to become like the holy garden of eden so that one day. His visible glory would fill the entire creation, the condition of the earth at the end of the first weekvwas just the starting point for all that the old Testament teaching about the developing. Historic kingdom of God\r\nSo far we\'ve seen that the primeval period began with God prepare the world to be place bof his development. Historil kingdom, now we should tune to a second crucial fact of the biblical record the role God orders for people at the stage of world history', 0, '2021-12-16 02:50:43', '2021-12-16 02:50:43', NULL),
(2822, 739, 984, 'If Adam and Eve had not fallen into sin, the path of humanity service as the image of God would have been a rather straightforward matter. But when quick sin enter in the humanity  straight path into  a long twisting road of trouble, yet despite these trouble earlier in the primeval history God assured the humanity that his purpose for them would not fail\r\nThere much that could be said about the progress of God kingdom during the primeval period, but we\'ll summarizes it in three steps. In the first place we\'ll speak of the cosmic treason that took place, second we\'ll look at how the corruption of humanity increased and led to serve divine judgment. But third we\'ll find that God established a long term strategy that would enable humanity to fulfill his kingdom purpose let\'s look first at the cosmic treason that took place against the divine king', 0, '2021-12-16 03:08:18', '2021-12-16 03:08:18', NULL),
(2823, 739, 985, 'Universal reign of God as the creator and sustains is one the most strikeing difference between the faith of ancient Israelites and religion of the nation that surrounded them. Neighboring religion commonly taught that various gods competed for sovereignty and that the power of these gods ebbed and flowed \r\nAncient Israelites understood the story of genius in 1:1- it helps to know that throughout the ancient world. Both divine and human kings were ofetn honored as great architect and building, along lines, a number of  interpret have shown that the creation to be his massive Royal Palace, every time \r\nGod rest in the\r\nIn this other way the opening of the book of Genius taught ancient Israel. How heir god the divine architect and building first', 0, '2021-12-16 03:28:03', '2021-12-16 03:28:03', NULL),
(2824, 739, 986, 'Gods kingdom come in three main stage\r\nInauguration, continuation, consummation\r\nWe may call the second stage of Gods kingdom in Christ the continuation of the kingdom, this is the long period of time in which the kingdom of God grown throughout church history, during this time, Christ continue to enlarge his kingdom through the proclamation of the Gospel throughout the world. And he calls his followers to make spreading the kingdom of God their highly priority. Just bas Jesus declare in Mathews 6:33\r\nEvery days of our lives we are seek the expansion of God kingdom, Gods righteous rule throughout bthe entire world. \r\nThe third stage if God\'s kingdom in Christ is the future consummation of the kingdom. This is the time when Christ return and fulfilled God\'s plan to turn the whole world into his kingdom. Revelation 11:15\r\nWhen Christ return to earth, he will defet every enemy of God and his kingdom of world will became the kingdom of God and of this Christ and from that time forth he shall region ever', 0, '2021-12-16 03:48:05', '2021-12-16 03:48:05', NULL),
(2825, 740, 987, 'We should mention from the outset that old Testament describe many different relationship as covenant  relationship between friend , spouses, political leaders tribs and.. Etc\r\nThese human interaction were called covenant in the old Testament because they formally bound people to each other with mutual obligation, \r\nInsights into the nature of God\'s covenant by looking briefly at his covenant in Moses, looking God covenant with Moses is important because the scripture reveal so much more about this covenant than any other covenant in the old testament\r\nWhen we look at the covenant God made with Moses, it quickly became apparent that it was structurd in ways that resemble ancient near eastern suzerain vassal treaties. Moses covenant covenant consisted of the same three elements that we\'ve seen in these treat and his resemble help understand that.. Exodus 19.4\r\nGod give covenant for nation \r\nThis word we see basic threefod dynamic of royal benevolent vassal loyalty and the consequences of blessing and curses that character Suzanne vassal treaties\r\n1) God reminder the Israel of the divine benevolent \r\n2) God also called for human loyalty as he initiative his covenant \r\nMuch', 8, '2021-12-16 15:38:59', '2022-02-16 16:56:31', NULL),
(2826, 740, 988, 'Abraham \r\nAbraham was the patriaarch Or father of every Israelite. And for this reson, God made his first national covenant through Abraham. We find direct reference to this covenant in Gen 15-17 the first mention of God covenant with Abraham \r\nThe covenant with Abraham set his descendants apart from the rest of humanity, through this covenant, God chose Israel to take a leading role in spreading his kingdom throughout the world, Abraham descendants would possess the promised land. They would increase in number, and they would spread the kingdom of God to the ends of the earth, and for this reason, Abraham covenant many be describe as both God\'s covenant bof promise to Israel and his covenant of Israel elation gen 15\r\nGod covenant with Abraham established God\'s kingdom purpose for Israel as his chosen people \r\n\r\nMoses\r\nIt\'s important to realize that Israel covenant in the days of Moses was not country to God\'s covenant  with Abraham. Moses did not see himself as providing an alternative to God covenant with Abraham on the country Moses frequently appealued to the cov with Abraham as the basis of his own kingdom work exdo. 32\r\nAs we see national covenant with Moses was nit a brand new covenant that replace the covenant with Abraham rather it was built on and furthered the covenant of election that God had made with Abraham \r\nWe find the primary record \r\nSo we see that econd covenant with of Israel \r\n\r\nDavid \r\n\r\nWhen Israel become a full fledege kin5 God made a covenant with king David God\'s covenant with David was not out of according with God\'s covenant at mount Sinai rather it was built on and furthered the covenant with Moses, as Solomon made clear 2 chro 6.16', 8, '2021-12-16 15:59:02', '2022-02-16 16:56:31', NULL),
(2827, 740, 989, 'Look briefly at how this is true for each of the universe covenant, first in the covenant with Adam and then in the covenant with Noah \r\nAdam \r\nWhen Adam Eve sinned God prepared the world humanity, taking it from chaos into order, and he formed a splendor garden place, and they have all Privlagy on the human race that extended even beyond the fall into sin \r\nIn second place the covenant with Adam also required human loyalty in addition to requiring Adam and Eve to serve bhim as his imagine b, God tested their loyalty with bthe tree of the knowledge of Good and evil, as God commands in Gen 2:16- \r\nAdam life with God involved all of the dynamics of God covenant, and as we know from book of Genesis, what was true for Adam and Eve  at God Royal discretion through this period of history life with God continue to involve divine benevolent human  loyalty band consequences \r\nNoah \r\nGod benevolence prepare bthe way for Noah covenant when God determined to destroy the human race in his righteous judgment, he also determined to save Noah and his family, as we read in Gene.6:8 \r\nGod showed much kindness bto Noah and his family  and similar kindness extended beyond the day of Noah to his descendants \r\nThere were consequences for human acting of obedience and disobedience during the period of Noah covenant Noah himself was faithful to God, so God was please with his sacrifice after the flood and blessed him with world', 8, '2021-12-16 16:19:05', '2022-02-16 16:56:31', NULL),
(2828, 740, 990, 'God covenant engaged different group of people in various ways \r\nWithin covenant \r\nBoth believer and unbelievable are including within God\'s covenant \r\n\r\nThe universe covenant in Adam and Noah bound all people to God both believer and unbelievers \r\n\r\n\r\nThe national covenant in Abraham, Moses and David including all people of Israel whether they had received eternal salvation or not \r\n\r\nOld Testament prophet indicate that ultimate the new covenant will only includ true believer  jer 31 \r\nAs this passage reveals every person counted in the new covenant will know the Lord and be permanently forgive of their sins. None of thir transgressions will ever be counted against bthem \r\nStill as we think about how Jeremiah prophecy was fulfilled b, we must keep in mind a feature of the new covenant age that we\'ve mention serevrl time in this series the new testment explain that the new covenant in Christ is fulfilled in three stage it was inauguration in Christ  first adivs it contains throughout the history of the church and it will reach its consummation only when Christ return in glory\r\nThis unfolding of new covenant age in', 8, '2021-12-16 16:37:37', '2022-02-16 16:56:31', NULL),
(2829, 740, 991, 'It would be difficult to overcome the important of divan covenant for understanding the old Testament, every biblical author know that God administered his kingdom through covenant . And as a result. The framework of God covenant. We serve Christ who has brought the new covenant and as his followers we must leran all we can from God covenant in the old testament so that we many live for him in this age renewang our commitment to the policy of covenant \r\nWhen we truly obedience this covenant from God we must finding God\'s grace and blessing in our life and our relationship and ministry... \r\nBecause all God covenant for humanity and blessing of from heaven', 7, '2021-12-16 16:50:25', '2022-02-16 16:56:31', NULL),
(2830, 741, 992, 'The old testament consists smaller elements and segment that come together to from larger section when from enter book \r\nOld testament author touched on many subject as they  applied the theology of God\'s kingdom and the policy of God covenant to his people through their books. And as we approach the old testament, we can highlight any of these theme that are significant for our lives \r\nWhen we approach the old testament canon as if it were a mirror, we look primarily for what the old testament bhasha to say about our concerns and our question, even if those theme are only secondary or mirror aspects of the scripture themselves, we call this straight  thematic analysis because we look for the ways the old testament reflect on theme or topics that are important to us as we seek to be faithful to God \r\nTo understand how the old testament canon often serves as a mirror in themselves analysis, we ll toch on two issue. First the basic or justification for this straight and second, the focus of this strategy, let\'s look first at the basic of thematic analysis\r\nThere arevat least two factor that serve as the basic for approach the old testament as a mirror of our interests, first the characters of scripture itself support this approach and second, many biblical examples affirm it, consider first how the characters of scripture support using thematic analysis', 0, '2021-12-17 02:32:46', '2021-12-17 02:32:46', NULL),
(2831, 741, 993, 'The word synchronic refers to events happening within a set period of time, biblical theologians create synchronic snapshot by focusing on a period of biblical history and then summarizes the complex network bof God act and work that took place during that period. These summarizes treat segment of time in biblical history as synchronized units or slices of time \r\nOld testament history like a river. It\'s history was not divided into distinct segment so, creating synchronic snapshots of old testament history is always somewhat artificial, much like dividing a flowing river into district segment is artificial, still it can be beneficial to speak of different segment of a river, and in the same way it can be beneficial to divide old testament history into period of times and to summarizes what God did and said during those period, we can do this in many different ways \r\n\r\nDiachronic trace \r\n The term diachronic simple mean through time it refers to the ways something has developed or change over time, so a diachronic trace focus on the ways Gods action and words connect with each other through time, from one period to another\r\nWe can summarizes the process bof established a diachronic trace in the way when we compare the formative events of various period bof biblical history with each other, we recognize that there are alignment or affinity between events in one period and events in another. Biblical theologians note these association and', 0, '2021-12-17 02:52:48', '2021-12-17 02:52:48', NULL),
(2832, 741, 994, 'Proper way to interpretation for on e the finde text grammar or design of biblical text, but at the same time exegesis must also be historical . And historical setting of author and original audience of the passage \r\n\r\nWriter\r\nChrist followers understand that God inspire band superintended the writing of the entire old testament canon, but as we saw earlier in this series this inspiration was human author to create the book of the canon, so we should also be concerned with this human side as we read the old testament we\'ll look at this fact of literally analysis in two way one the one hand, we should be aware if a number of dangers and on the other hand, we should see a number of benefit of this approach to the old testament \r\n1) many interpret tended to focus on author in way that left them entangled in webs of psychological and sociological speculation they did this in part by assuming too much about the precise identify of the writer \r\nAudience\r\nOriginal audience of the book of kings and chronicle we know for instance that that the audience of king was still in exile from the emphasis of the book of king at least some of original recipient needed to be convinced that God was just in sending his people into exile, and we know that  some bof the audience needed to understand that repentance was required before Israel could return to the promise land\r\nDocuments\r\nThe documents of scripture', 0, '2021-12-17 03:12:46', '2021-12-17 03:12:46', NULL),
(2833, 741, 995, 'The term diachronic  simply means  through time it refers to the ways something has developed or changed over time so a diachronic trace focuse on the ways Gods action and words connect with each other through time from one period to another. \r\nOne of the most difficult but rich featured of interpretation the old testament is the fact that it doesn\'t describe just one period of history. It touch on events over thousands of years as we learn what God did and asid over this long stretch of history it\'s obvious even to a casual reader, that God revelation represent many kinds of theological developed \r\nWhat God revealed in the time of the Adam covenant.. That simple did not repeat what had been revealed nearly. Same way Moses covenant and David covenant \r\nBut divine revelation during these ages did not contract each other. Revelation in later period incorporated and built on organic growth. Old testament faith grew much like a seed grow into a saplings then into a small tree and then into a mature tree Describe these developed in old testament in old testament history is the process we call diachronic tracing \r\nTo illustrate what we mean let\'s consider just one fact of what God covenant with Abraham emphasis we\'ll look at God promise to give Abraham the land of canon in Gene 15: 18 \r\nBut the understand the significant of this promise from God to Abraham in old testament history \r\nDuring the David covenant was David house would prove to be Fathifu to the Lord and the kingdom would expand and bring re', 0, '2021-12-17 03:32:45', '2021-12-17 03:32:45', NULL),
(2834, 741, 996, 'The information about the author of a biblical book is a great help to us as we try understand it means and this is because the holy Spirit in the way that only the sovereign God could used human individual in their personality bin their background of experience to deliver his inerrant word, and often when we can get some sense of how he formed the human author to be the one through whom he would speak the word it enriches our understand of the text. I think of Psalm 51, a classic the David repentance and of his recognize his terrible sin against his faithful soldiers uriah with God \r\nTo illustration what we mean let\'s consider what we know about two important old Testament author the author of 1 and 2 kings and the author of 1 and 2 chronicle. We don\'t know precisely who\'s these author were. We don\'t know there names or exactly when they wrote, \r\nWe certainly don\'t know much about their psychological tendencies so to rely heavily on these sorts of consideration as we interpret their book runs the risk of building bour interpretation on little more than speculation \r\n   By contract the author of chronic often called the chronicle wrote after Israel release from captivity we know this because among other things the genealois in 1 chronicle 9 \r\nAnd 2 chronicle 36 \r\nWe know that both of these author were among the educated elite of Israel. Both author referrred to information n the', 0, '2021-12-17 03:52:48', '2021-12-17 03:52:48', NULL),
(2835, 742, 977, 'There are two factors that cause us to feel distant from the old testament .on one hand God gave these scriptures to humanity through a process known as organic inspiration, and on the other hand God design the old testament to full fill it purpose through a process known  as divine accommodation,\r\norganic inspiration: it\'s Comen to describe the historical evangelical view of the divine inspiration of scripture as organic inspiration while we affirm that Holy spirit inspired the scripture we use the terminology to indicate that his inspiration was not divorced from the personalities ,experiences and the intention of human authors ,that is to say ,under the special infallible supervision of the holy spirit ,the human authors themselves determined what to write ,\r\nDevine accommodation :these describe the fact that every time God revels himself to humanity ,he speaks tom un in finit human terms ,because God is transcendent and incomprehensible, whenever he reveal him self he condescends to us in ways that finite human beings can understand, otherwise we wouldint able to understand him at all  ,now it is important to realize that in the old testament God didnt accomadate himself simply to the limits of the humanity in a broad genaral sence .', 8, '2021-12-22 16:46:54', '2022-02-16 16:56:11', NULL),
(2836, 742, 978, 'It is good to admit that old testament often seems distance us as moden people ,but the reality can also lead to confusion  and it can even discourage us from studying this part of the bible ,there are many ways to cataloge the main kinds of distance us and the old testament ,but here we will just speak about three, theological distance  ,second cultural distance and third personal distance ,these three kinds of distance interconnect in many ways ,\r\nTheological : theological distance we are thinking primarily the difference between the revelation that old testament authors received and the fuller revelation that we have received in Christ .every christian  realize that the old testament presents theological view points that dont at first ,appear to correspond with the teachings of the new testament .\r\nCultural :cultural distance between ourselves and the old testament exists because human culture are constantly changing , social structures shift , many of the difference will make us feel  as we are in a different world ,even though we are simply in a slightly different era,\r\nPersonal:when we speak of personal distance we reffers to the fact that people who lived in a days of the old testament were different from morden people ,many of the gaps between us and them involves very personal ,human factors .', 9, '2021-12-22 17:04:00', '2022-02-16 16:56:11', NULL),
(2837, 742, 979, 'We should say one thing that is old testament is relevant for Christian today ,but it is quiet another to put this truth into practice , sometimes its not difficult to see  how the old testament applies to us ,but other times its very difficult to because of the distance between us and old testament ,now its reassuring that holy spirit helps followers to christ study and apply the old testament ,he teaches us inthe way that go for beyond what we could accomplish in our own strength ,but its our responsibility to study the old testament as deeply as we can so that we can apply it rightly to our lives today,\r\nthe apostel Paul spoke of this responsibility in his epistle ,as paul telling that we must approach the old testament as a worker ,meaning the labourer who works diligently to rightly handle God\'s word .,there are lots of ethical part to apply in our day btoday life from the old testament ,jesus also taught us fro m the old testamnet and telling that follow the sripture , there are lots of moral content there to adopt from old testament ,', 9, '2021-12-22 17:17:08', '2022-02-16 16:56:11', NULL),
(2838, 742, 980, 'The application of the old testament in our lives today must account for the many historical developments that took palce inthe biblical faiths, both in the old testament and in the new testament .the old testament ,the new testament ,and our Christian faith today  reprfesent our religion one faith ,ye we must always acknowledge that the various kinds of developments took place\r\nThere are many ways to discribe the devolopments ,but we will highlights three major types, Epochal developments, cultural developments and personal developments,\r\nEpochal:biblical history is lengthily account of the way God disclose himself to his people progresivly as history moved towards its divinely ordained end in christ, there are many deferent ways to explore the history ,but major is epochs corresponded to God\'s covenant with his people,\r\nCultural: we must take into account to the similarities and differences betweenthe culutal patterns of our own world .\r\nPersonal:there are both considerable similarities and considerable diffrents between the people of the old testament and the people living in our contemporary world,if we hope to apply the encient texts nof the old testmanent properly we need to ask questiance like how do our personal lives to comapre with those waht we seen in the old testmanennt .', 8, '2021-12-22 17:33:43', '2022-02-16 16:56:11', NULL),
(2839, 742, 981, 'Jesus didn\'t disagree with the old testament but showed that the oral tradition  of the scribes and pharisees fell short of old testament standards, Jesus didn\'t contrast his own views with those of the old testament, instead he opposed the false interpretation of  the day and reasserted the true teaching of the old testament ,\r\nWhen Jesus presents these; you have heard what it was said, but now i tell you to statement in the sermon of the mount, I think that the best way to look at this is that Jesus is getting at the true meaning of the law, not that he is introducing something new , but he is expositing in a sense ,what God was aiming at all along ,so that when the lay says you shall not commit adultery God never intened his peop-le think that they could get away with looking lustfulloy at there own views, this is wat jesus teaching on mount sermon, it was emhasising odt testment in a veryb good way .', 7, '2021-12-22 17:46:28', '2022-02-16 16:56:11', NULL),
(2840, 743, 982, 'The word of telling that God is supreme king over all creation, in Psalm 93:1-2 we find the praise of God the creator, the lord reign he is robed in majesty .........he has put on his strength as his belt ,yes the world is established, it shall never be moved, your throne is established from of old, you are from everlasting, as we have seen the fact that God created and sustain everything, time and again the old testaments acknowledge this fundamental belief, God created and sustain all things and he is sovereign over all, this belief in the unwavering universal reign of God as the creator and sustainer is one of the most striking differences between the faith of ancient Israelites and the religions of the nations that surrounded them  , Yahweh the God of Israel was the sole creator and sustainer of all , the unwavering ruler over the entire creation even over powerful spiritual creatures or so called gods, through every cycle of nature ,every defeat and victory in battel ,God\'s kingship is universal and unchanging .', 8, '2021-12-23 03:15:25', '2022-02-16 16:55:48', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2841, 743, 983, 'The book of Genesis opening chapter reveals that the earth was made to be the place of God\'s royal rule in many ways, but here we will discuss on just two , first how God\'s initial preparation of creation reflected his royal purpose for the earth, and second hoe God set the stage for the ongoing expansion of his kingdom on earth .\r\nGod\'s initial preparation: to understand what the primeval history has to say about God\'s initial preparation for his kingdom on earth , we have to set aside for the moment any of our modern questions,in the creation story begins with these familiar words in Genesis:1:1, In the beginning God created heaven and the earth ,the opening word established the fact that god created two realms or levels heaven above and the visible earth below. And God began to construct the earth into aplace for his glorious reign to extend from heaven to earth ,earth stood in sharp contrast with God\'s glorious heavenly throne room , heaven is filled with the blinding glory of God ,but the earth was not at this time , the Hebrew word translate that \"without form\" and void, this was the primeval history first established the place of God\'s kingdom.', 8, '2021-12-23 03:32:33', '2022-02-16 16:55:48', NULL),
(2842, 743, 984, 'If Adam and had not fallen into sin the path of humanity\'s service as the image of God would have been a rather straight forward matter ,adam and Eve would have had children of their own and so on, and together a large number of God\'s priestly ,royal image would have filled the earth ,subdude it and had domination according to the commands of God but as we know all too well, this did not go in this direction, sin quickly turned humanity\'s straight path in to a long twisting road to trouble ,yet despite these troubles early in the primeval history ,God assured the humanity that his purpose for them would not fall. there is much more that could be said about the progress of God\'s kingdom during the primeval period, but we will summarize it in tree steps ,first cosmic treason, second corruption of humanity and the third find that god established a long term strategy that would enable  humanity to fulfill his kingdom purpose,  \r\nThe cosmic treason :instead of faithfully fulfilling what their divin king had commanded ,Afdam and Eve succumbed to satan\'s temptation and rebelled by eating the forbidden fruit.\r\nCorreption and judgment :Adam and Eve fell into sin,it was no longer true their descendants would faithful to God ,in fact none of them could serve as God\'s faithful images .\r\nLong term strategy :God announced the hope of the victory over evil for redeemd ,', 9, '2021-12-23 03:50:06', '2022-02-16 16:55:48', NULL),
(2843, 743, 985, 'The story of Israel in the old testament includes both positive accomplishments and abysmal failure. nearly every generation of Israel rebelled against God in one way or another and suffered under God\'s temporary judgments. But time and again  God brought Israel to repentance and renewed them  still toward the end of the old testament Israel\'s sin become so great so that God brought a great cures of exile from the promised land for hundred of years Israel had no homeland no priesthood and temple ,there was three period of  progress of God\'s kingdom during ancient Israel promise, exodus and conquest and empire,  when we spoke about the promise we have in mind the generation of Israel\'s patriarchs ,during the cdays of Abraham, isaac Jacobe and son\'s,God made many promise about future of the nation ,and another Exodus and Conques : we will recall when Moses and Israelites passed trough the red sea  ,they sang a song of praise to God that appears in Exodus :15:1-18,the third is Empire:israel had human kings during the ancient progress ,God used the kings to spread God\'s kingdom on earth , but sadly Israel\'s kings failed to fullfill this calling from God, in fact 1and 2 king makes it clear that many of Israel\'s king rebellion againist God.', 8, '2021-12-23 04:06:37', '2022-02-16 16:55:48', NULL),
(2844, 743, 986, 'WE often hear that the old testament was oriented toward the physical world, but new testament faith is oriented toward immaterial heavenly realities and inward spiritual matters , Christ-followers has citizenship in heaven rather than in the earthly kingdom because at this moment our king Jesus is in heaven at the right hand of the father ,the new testament faith is not disconnected from the God\'s earthly kingdom , rather new testament proclaims   Jesus fulfilled the last stage of God\'s kingdom on earth, God  establishing a physical kingdom on earth through his people as well as a spiritual kingdom in heaven, but in earthly kingdom the followers of God to seek righteous rule throughout the entier world by expanding of God\'s kingdom, in finnaly Christ willc ome and fullfill god\'s paln to turn the whole world into the his kingdom, as followers of Christ we look back to all that he accomplished in the inaguration nof the kingdom,', 7, '2021-12-23 04:19:51', '2022-02-16 16:55:48', NULL),
(2845, 744, 987, 'We should mention from the outset that the old testament describes many different relationships as covenants, these human interactions were called covenants in the old testament because they formally bound people to each other with mutual obligation. The old testament compares these diverse covenant relationship to God\'s relationship with his people .Kingship in the ancient near east context was largely grounded in the concept of covenant .so we had evidence of treaties, ancient treaties ,in which a king or an overlord,or what\'s called a suzerain enter in to the agreement with some people  who become essentially his servants or his vassals .the first treaties emphasize Royal benevolence, the kindness that great kings showed to their vassals. the second major focus of suzerain vassal was on the requirements of vassals loyalty , vassal were to observe the lows of the king and offer tributes of precious jewels , metals . third major focus of the suzerain vassals was the consequences of the blessing and cures ,these three central features of suzerain vassal treaties help us understand more clearly of the fundamental dynamic of the relationship between God and his covenant people in scripture.', 8, '2021-12-23 16:48:17', '2022-02-16 16:55:22', NULL),
(2846, 744, 988, 'We will also look at the national covenant in chronological order  being with covenant in Abraham , then the covenant with Israel in Moses and finally the covenant in David ,\r\nAbraham: he was the father of every Israeliets ,and for this reason God made his frist national covenant in Genesis 15,17, this was the normal way of indicating the beginning of a covenant relationship ,then the number of years later  God affirmed or renewed his covenant with the father .\r\nMoses : it is important to realize that israel\'s covenant inthe days of Moses was not contrary to God\'s covenant with Abraham ,Moses frequently appealed to the covenant with Abraham as the basis of his own kingdom work.in exodus 32:13 as we seen here the national covenant with Moses was not a brand new covenant that replaced the covenant with Abraham .Rather it was built on and furthered the covenant of election that God had made with Abraham.  ofcurse there ad been rules and regulations for God\'s people befor the time of Moses , but like every new nation one of israel\'s chief needs at this time was a system of law ,a body of regulations to govern them .for this reason the covenant with Moses may rightly be called God\'s covenant of law .', 8, '2021-12-23 17:03:52', '2022-02-16 16:55:22', NULL),
(2847, 744, 989, 'It is true that the biblical record of each old testament covenant has distinctive emphases, Yet when we keep in  view the border biblical portrait of old testament history we can see the dynamics of God\'s  interactions with his people remained consistent under each administration of each covenant .during the primeval history God established a covenant of foundations with Adam and covenant of natural stability with Noah , but God\'s interaction with Adam didn\'t simply amount to the establishment of kingdom foundation , and under Noah\'s covenant intraction between humanity and god involved much more than stability of nature ,As in all divine covenants ,God administered life under these covenants through the dynamic of divine benevolence, human loyalty and consequences for obedience and disobedience .the biblical covenants display Gods benovelance in several ways , one simple way we can see this is that god ,without having any obligation decided to enter into a relationship with his people .......also devine benovalance is displayed in how God gives many blessings for his people to enjoy .', 7, '2021-12-23 17:17:36', '2022-02-16 16:55:22', NULL),
(2848, 744, 990, 'Everyone familiar with the old testament knows that it often distinguishes between differents of individuals and groups of people ,men and women ,old and young the powerful and the week to mention in just few now of course ,its impossible for us to deal with all of his variety in details ,number of large group of people that often took center stage in what the bible tells us about divine covenants ,the division of humanity first by nothing how both belivers and unbelivers are included within God\'s covenants.\r\nWithin covenants :Christian often asume that the only true believers have ever enterd into covenant with God ,but this is hardly the case ,you will remember that we speak of God\'s covenant becaus ethay bound all people to god.\r\nIncluded and excluded:all of humanity was included inthe universal covenants woith Adam and Noah ,no groups were excluded foundation and the natural stability established in these covenants . but this sistuation changed when God chose Israel as his special covenant people .the national covenants with Abraham ,Moses and David primarily included the physical descendend of Abraham only relativly few Gentoiels who were adopted into israel .', 8, '2021-12-23 17:32:21', '2022-02-16 16:55:22', NULL),
(2849, 744, 991, 'All biblical covenant shows God\'s benevolence in many ways, in Genesis we can see Even after Adam failed to pass the test , God continue to show his loyalty to the human race , but in may place God demanded to be obedient to be blessed, otherwise disobeying the covenant will be a cure for the people, the true covenant showed the tru believers in immeasurable, including forgivness of sin and eternal fellowship with God, all biblical covenant contains the result either for blessing or curse,   there ary many things to happening in the church and also in athe personal life , if we dispobay the covenant it will be cure for us then and now , but we obey that will be a blessing today also, believers have an obligation to live differently  from the universe ,christian must follow scripture ,rules and regulations to test and prove their faith ,', 8, '2021-12-23 17:45:05', '2022-02-16 16:55:22', NULL),
(2850, 745, 992, 'Whenever we realized it or not when we read the old testament it is impossible to divorce ourselves entirely from priorities that rise out of our experience .we look to the old testament as God\'s royal word to his people .there are least two factors that serve as the basis for approaching the old testament as a mirror of our interest,First the character of the scripture it self support this approach second many biblical example affirm it .\r\nThe character of Scripture: old testament books consist of many smaller   elements that fit together into segment these segments comes together for larger section and these section from entier books ,unfortunately many well-meaning Christians often think of the meaning of old testament passages are too narrowly .they act as if every passage presents a very thin laser beam of instruction, and there is only one way to summarize it properly ,some themes are quit important ,\r\nBiblical example: Every one familiar with the story of Jephthah and Samson in the book of Judges know that the book did not present these men in a consistently favorable light,in fact the author of the book of Judges repeatedly draw attention to the moral failures no Israel\'s leaders during this period of history , this way tuse canon as mirror justified', 8, '2021-12-24 03:20:40', '2022-02-16 16:54:52', NULL),
(2851, 745, 993, 'In the last century one popular form of theological historical analysis came to be known as \"biblical theology \"it can refers to several different approach to the scripture ,this prominent from the biblical theology generally focuses on two main steps :first biblical theologian create a \"synchronic snap short \" of the historical period, second they explore what we may call a \"diachronic trace \"\r\nSynchronic snapshot: The word synchronic refers to events happening within a set period of time biblical theologians create a synchronic snapshot by focusing a period of biblical history and summarizing the complex network 0f God\'s acts and words that took place during that period .these summaries treat segments of time in biblical history as synchronized units or slices of time.\r\nDiachronic Trace :the term \'diachronic \" simply means \"through time \" it refers to the way something has developed or changed over time ,so a diachronic trace focus on the ways God\'s action and the words connect with each other through time ,from one period to another .', 7, '2021-12-24 03:34:59', '2022-02-16 16:54:52', NULL),
(2852, 745, 994, 'Evangelical Christian often refer to the proper way of interpreting the scriptures as \"grammatico-historical exegesis \".by this we meant that exegesis or interpretation should focus in the first place ,on the gramenr or the design of the biblical text ,but the same time exegesis also the historical.to see the focus of literary analysis , we will look at three consern firt from writter ,second audience , and third the document or text .\r\nWriter :Christ followers understand the that god inspierd and superintended the writing of the entier old testament canon.God used background thoughts ,feelings and intentions of hischosen human authers to create the book of the canon ,so we should also be concerned with this human side as weread the old testament ,\r\nAudience: There are dangers and benefits when we consider the writers of the old testament books, we also need to aware of the dangers benifits of focusing on the orginal audiance ,on the one hand there are dangers when interpreters speculate too much about the audiance  .they imagin their precis identification ,\r\nDocument: the document of the scripture are the primary focus of literary analysis because they are the fully authoritative word of God ,so we need to pay special attention to the dagers and the benifits of document it self.', 8, '2021-12-24 03:49:23', '2022-02-16 16:54:52', NULL),
(2853, 745, 995, 'Diachronic trace focus on the way God\'s action and words connect with each other through time ,from one period to another , we can summarize the focus of establishing a diachronic trace in this way :one of the most difficult but rich features of interpreting the old testament is the fact that it doesn\'t describe just one period of history it toches on events over thousand of years ,as we learn what God did and said over this long strech of history,its obvious even to a casual readr ,that Go\'s revelation represent many kinds of theological developments .If i read scripture diachronic trace will help me tom interpret and think what  God was speaking according to time, what was his action as the era change , many of the scripture was God talked was based on the  current situation and the culture , diachronic trace will help to realize this, some particular words and laws were only for the auudiance whom are on that time and  for them who are obligated to obay the particular scripture ,', 8, '2021-12-24 04:00:48', '2022-02-16 16:54:52', NULL),
(2854, 745, 996, 'To understanding  the scripture in the proper way we need to know the intention of the writer , for example the auther of 1 and 2 king, and the author of 1and 2 Chronicles , we dont know precisely who these authors were we dont know their name or when exactly they wrote ,we certainly don\'t know  much about their psychological tendence ,at the same time we can derive valuable information about both of these authors from old testament itself ,for example we know that the author of kings wrote during Israel\'s exile in Babylon,the last scenes of 2 kings 25:23-27 clearly indicate that the author wrote some time after 562 BC, after Jehoiachin had been released from prison in Babylon, so the books of kings almost  certainly was completed befor the release of israel captivity in Bbael .by contrast the author of chronicles often called chronicler ,wrote after Israel\'s release  ,\r\nhere the several interpretes have rightly conclude that the auther of chronicles emphazied the practical steps \r\nIsraels need,and auther of Kings consider somthimg differetn, he need to keepma history, this is how we need to realaize the intention of the writer to understand the scripture .', 7, '2021-12-24 04:19:19', '2022-02-16 16:54:52', NULL),
(2855, 758, 655, 'test', 0, '2022-06-29 15:25:21', '2022-06-29 15:25:21', NULL),
(2856, 758, 656, 'test', 0, '2022-06-29 15:25:27', '2022-06-29 15:25:27', NULL),
(2857, 758, 657, 'test', 0, '2022-06-29 15:25:37', '2022-06-29 15:25:37', NULL),
(2858, 758, 658, NULL, 0, '2022-06-29 15:25:41', '2022-06-29 15:25:41', NULL),
(2859, 758, 659, NULL, 0, '2022-06-29 15:25:45', '2022-06-29 15:25:45', NULL),
(2860, 771, 675, 'Hermeneutics is a common word in theological and biblical studies, but we don’t use if often in our daily lives. Many of us will notice the word hermeneutics derives from the family of Greek terms that includes the name Hermes, the mythological messenger of the gods. The word itself derives from a family of Greek word related to the verb hermeneuo which means interpret or explain. Broadly speaking hermeneutics refers to the interpretation or explanation of some kind of message or communication. Friedrich. S who lived from 1768-1834 is often called the father of modern hermeneutics.', 7, '2022-08-29 14:38:48', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2861, 771, 676, 'People living in biblical times weren’t modern scientists. But this doesn’t mean that they were unintelligent or irrational. On the contrary their sophisticated, architectural accomplishments, extensive maritime travel, innovative agricultural programs and countless other cultural achievements demonstrate that people in biblical days dealt with facts and thought rationally about the world much like modern scientists do. Abraham believed in God and it was credited to him as righteousness. Now when a man works his wages are not credited to him as a gift, but as an obligation. However, to the man who doesn’t work but trust God who justifies the wicked.\r\nPaul carefully analyzed the word credited as the Greek term therefore he argued that wages are not credited as a gift but as an obligation.', 8, '2022-08-29 14:48:48', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2862, 771, 677, 'Time and again the authors indicated that followers of Christ are to read the scriptures as the word of God, in the presence of God, in the ways that bring about extraordinary even supernatural experiences of God. Heb.4:12, the author referred to a portion of Psa.95 calling it the word of God. Hew.4:7, author quoted from the same Psalm saying God himself spoke through David. Hew.3.7, he introduced Psalm 95 as the Holy Spirit says. Therefore here the Hebrews writer describes the experience of reading scripture. He treated OT scriptures with a depth of insights that exceeds many other NT authors. Still his highly intellectual analysis of scripture didn’t turn him away from devotional hermeneutics.', 7, '2022-08-29 14:57:02', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2863, 771, 678, 'Origen encouraged  Gregory of Neocaesarea in the letter of Origen to Gregory: Origen told Gregory to devote himself to the divine reading. Origen’s approach to scripture was deeply influenced by neo-platonism which is expressed in the works of the Jewish OT interpreter Philo of Alexandria. Believers needed a faith fixed firmly on God if they wanted to discover the Bible’s hidden truth. According to him the most necessary element for comprehending scripture is praying to understand the divine words. People like Origen emphasize the fact when you read the Bible it’s really important that you gain the spiritual meaning of the text. So as to improve our ability to understand the meaning of the biblical words.', 8, '2022-08-29 15:06:37', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2864, 771, 679, 'Illumination is the spiritual sense is turning on the light of understanding in some area. Throughout the ages people in every culture and religion have claimed some kind of revelation from God. When this revelation or enlightenment deals with understanding and applying knowledge already given we call it illumination. The most basic level of enlightenment is the knowledge of sin and without that knowledge everything else is pointless. Psa.18:28, and Psa. 119 some of the verses establishes the basic method of God’s illumination. Regular study of the word of God will give direction and understanding in the issues of life.', 8, '2022-08-29 15:16:17', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2865, 771, 680, '1. We get big picture and the largest story. Historically and currently certain groups have zeroed in one chapter or in one verse of the Bible in order to develop their worldview. These efforts prevent the meaning of scripture because it ignores the canon. \r\n2. It reveals what God is saying rather than what we want him to say. It begins with hands empty and open to God.\r\n3. Grasp scripture and apply it appropriately.\r\n4. Enjoy the diversity of genres and styles. Through exegetical studies of scripture the brilliants and clarity of God’s story comes alive with dynamism and surprising depth.\r\n5. Organic discoveries are the best.', 8, '2022-08-29 15:24:56', '2022-09-08 02:38:26', NULL),
(2866, 772, 681, 'The holy spirit chose to communicate his words through the personalities, experiences, emotions and patterns of human thought of biblical writers. Focusing on the human writers help us to understand many features of the scripture. Eg the different ways of 1 Sam and 1 chro tell the story of the king an ship of David. There is incredible value of knowing about the writers original context to understand a particular part of the scripture. The authority of the scripture is in what is written, not our imagination or reconstructing the author’s background. So their words are true though we fully don’t understand it the background of the writer.', 8, '2022-09-01 14:40:06', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2867, 772, 682, 'The doctrine of organic inspiration teaches that holy spirit inspired human authors to write scriptures. But it doesn’t say that every copy made of that document will be perfect because of the doctrine of organic inspiration extends only to the original texts of scripture only those scriptures have the full authority of God himself. The most important thing for followers of Christ to remember is that the original document of the OT didn’t exist in Jesus day either. Hebrew version of OT books exited at that time.  Aramaic and multiple versions were available. \r\nModern Christian have the advantage of many decades of scholarly research devoted to studying and comparing ancient copies of the scriptures. Therefore Bible is far reliable than the text we have most other ancient writings.', 7, '2022-09-01 14:48:24', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2868, 772, 683, '1 The plethora of NT manuscripts is a great benefit when trying to determine the original reading of the NT. For its easier to sift through and evaluate the various extant. \r\n2 The NT has many more backing manuscripts up do classical texts such as those written by Herodotus. The textual critiques draw the right conclusion from this fact.\r\n3 The scribble variants do not over turn any doctrine where one word or clause in a verse may be scrutinized to determine the best reading among many variants, the entire sweep of the NT assures us that doctrines stand on bedrock.', 7, '2022-09-01 14:58:34', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2869, 772, 684, '1. Some people tend toward an approach we call objectivism. Objectivists believe that under the right circumstances it’s possible to arrive at impartial are the objective knowledge.\r\n2. Other people tend toward an approach called subjectivism. Subjectivists believe that our knowledge is always influenced by our personal biases, making impartial objectivity impossible.\r\n3. Some people have found a middle ground that we might call dialogism. This approach emphasizes the constant dialogue between objective reality and our subjective perspectives.', 7, '2022-09-01 15:03:28', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2870, 772, 685, 'Critical-Biblical scholars influenced by subjectivism often argue that no objective meaning can be found in a biblical text. So instead of teaching their students to discover the original meaning of the scripture, they encourage readers of the Bible to create their own meaning by using the scriptures to suit their own purposes. They believe NT writers don’t care about what OT text meant in a objective sense. A critical subjectivist interpreter argue that we should do the same thing that we shouldn’t worry about what the objective meaning of scripture. Bible should be used to promote our own social, political, and religious agendas.', 8, '2022-09-01 15:09:17', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2871, 772, 686, 'Objective and subjective approaches to meaning to oppose each other in some fundamental ways. But they have something very important in common. Objectivism tends to over estimate how reliable our rationale and scientifically objective views are. Subjectivism tends to over estimate how reliable our personal intuition and opinion. A dialogical model help us deal more adequately with our own weaknesses and with the divine authority of the Bible. An authority dialogue model acknowledges that objective truth can be found in the text of scripture. It also avoids some serious dangers associated with objectivist extremes. It also helps us remember that rationale and scientific judgments must always be viewed in submission to the authority of scripture.', 8, '2022-09-01 15:16:23', '2022-09-08 02:39:14', NULL),
(2872, 773, 687, 'Thomas insisted that the literal sense of scripture was foundational to all its other senses. In his summa theological part 1 question 1 article 10 he insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was rooted in the literal sense of a passage. He also taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated as a spiritual meaning without being taught else where in scriptures in the literal sense. So the reformers of the 16th century developed the idea that had been championed by Aquians. They said that all the spiritual aspect of a text that were intended by the author for his original audience are actually aspects of its literal sense. They believed that scripture literal sense or original meaning is both singular and complex. They broadened that it includes everything the author intended the literature of scripture to convey.', 8, '2022-09-02 15:41:15', '2022-09-08 02:39:40', NULL),
(2873, 773, 688, 'Three types of biblical elaborations are: 1. places where one part of scripture directly or indirectly comments on an aspect of the meaning of another passage in scripture, because all scripture is inspired and infallible, these elaborations always accord and confirm the original meaning. 2. Stated as clarification of things that weren’t entirely apparent or well understood. Clarification of the commandment against murder in Num.35. Moses distinguished between murder and accidental man slaughter. 3. It can be an expansion of the meaning of a particular passage. Ex.20:13, you shall not murder and  Deut.5:17. Both stated in the form of repetition. In the context of the elaborations add something to its meaning.', 8, '2022-09-02 15:56:00', '2022-09-08 02:39:40', NULL),
(2874, 773, 689, 'Our knowledge of the author helps us to understand the document and original audience. The documents word and grammar gives us information about the writer and the audience. The personal strength of the interpreter lead each to bring out important but different aspects of the passage. It includes assessing, synthesizing and applying God’s inerrant word by means of exegesis and theology. Literally units and text hierarchy, text criticism, translation, clause and text grammar, word and concept studies, historical and literary context, biblical and practical theology help us to interpret scripture relevantly in our spheres. We must respond appropriately and we must engage in the task depending upon God.', 8, '2022-09-02 16:13:14', '2022-09-08 02:39:40', NULL),
(2875, 773, 690, '1. The writer - if we over emphasize the author we often fall into an intentional fallacy. 2. The document - over emphasizing the document is a kind of error often called a graphic fallacy. The graphic fallacy over emphasizes the document itself to the relative exclusion of contextual consideration. 3. The audience interdependent guides to meaning. This is often called an affective fallacy, because it focuses too heavily on how scripture affects its audience. \r\nEvidential fallacy which fails to approach the text with the presumption that it is accurate. \r\nSuper knowledge fallacy which occurs when one is approaching difficult text, practices textual amendation to accommodate the critique occurancy.', 8, '2022-09-02 16:22:37', '2022-09-08 02:39:40', NULL),
(2876, 773, 691, 'One valuable aspect of an authority model is that it highlights the fact that all interpreters come to biblical writings with different set of concerns, assumptions, background and questions. Each of us reads scripture differently because God has gifted each of us differently. We all have strengths and weaknesses and we process information in a variety of ways based on our unique knowledge and peoples’ gift and background. historians might describe the order in which God created light and dark, water and dry land differently than an artist. But an artist might talk about the beauty and goodness of the stars in the night.', 8, '2022-09-02 16:27:28', '2022-09-08 02:39:40', NULL),
(2877, 774, 692, 'God’s immutable means that he’s unchanging in his being,perfection, purpose and promises. So his being, his nature, his essence, his perfection, the degree to which he possesses those characteristics his purposes. This doesn’t say that God doesn’t relate to us in a dynamic relational, personal way. So he listens to our prayers, he grieves over our sin, he delights in our faithfulness. We are so aware of impermanence and transcends of just about everything in our lives, world, relationship and even in our own fleeting lives. He is rock solid, trustworthy, which can function as an anchor for the soul when the mountains shake and everything appears to be falling. Immutability doesn’t imply that Gods is inactive.', 8, '2022-09-03 02:38:22', '2022-09-08 02:40:02', NULL),
(2878, 774, 693, 'Sinful images: all human beings, no matter when or where they live, are sinful images of God. Gen.1:27 talks God created man in his own image. But at the same time all human beings have also fallen in to sin.\r\nReligious divisions: this division of humanity includes all those who haven’t heard God’s special revelation to Israel and to church. False believers make superficial commitments to God.\r\nClasses: human beings are classified according to particular trades or attributes. Some people are old, young, male, female, rich, poor, powerful or weak. According to our jobs we are classified and same thing was also true of the original audiences of scripture. They form meaningful connection between all original and subsequent audiences.', 8, '2022-09-03 02:45:00', '2022-09-08 02:40:02', NULL),
(2879, 774, 694, 'Theologians identified epochs according to God’s various covenants throughout the Bible, especially, those associated with Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses and David in the OT and with Jesus in the NT. The laws regarding atoning sacrifices required different things at different points in redemptive history. In Moses’ time they required sacrifice at the tabernacle and in Solomon’s day they required sacrifices at the temple. In the early NT they required Jesus’ death on the cross and in the later NT they ceased to be offered altogether. And now we no longer have to offer sacrifices because Christ is now our sacrifice.', 7, '2022-09-03 02:51:53', '2022-09-08 02:40:02', NULL),
(2880, 774, 695, 'Outline: it is one of the most popular ways to divide OT history by associating each epoch with one of God’s covenants. God’s covenants with his people always entailed significant theological shifts, and therefore provide helpful boundaries for epochal divisions.\r\nImplications: the division of the OT into ages makes it clear that God wanted his people to understand and apply theological themes in different ways at different times. NT believers should never apply this scripture as if they live in earlier periods of history. Once God commanded a new way for sacrifice and worship, he expected his people never to return to the old ways.', 7, '2022-09-03 02:57:54', '2022-09-08 02:40:02', NULL),
(2881, 774, 696, 'First he argued that Christ has inaugurated the new covenant that Jeremiah has predicted in 31. He said The new covenant was making old system of sacrifice obsolete. Many well meaning Christians take this to mean that followers of Christ should simply discard the OT and pay no attention to its teaching. The new covenant requires us to reorient the practice of sacrifice. We should never serve God as if we still in the old days. \r\nTheme of leadership in battle. Kings receives direction from God. One might prefer to follow a local judge unlike others that would be a sin. One is violating God’s command for epoch.', 7, '2022-09-03 03:08:57', '2022-09-08 02:40:02', NULL),
(2882, 775, 761, 'Roles of Father and Son—Scriptures teach us that the son was at the father’s side during creation and that the father created the world by or through him. Joh 1:1-3, Hebrews 1:2. In colo 1:6 Paul explicitly says that creation was accomplished by the son or as as some translation put it “through “ him. At the beginning of creation the Son existed as the Logos the true word. The word become flesh. Paul, tells us not only did the son create the world he brought everything into being. We are told in the genesis that God spoke- it’s was a verbal creation. Therefore, we understand the universe in the same way— from the creator to the creation from God to human beings Son was present.', 5, '2022-09-13 13:38:21', '2022-10-09 02:32:01', NULL),
(2883, 775, 762, 'God’s eternal counsel regarding Christ has two aspects: foreknowledge and purpose. These aspects are evident in Isaiah 46:10.\r\nForeknowledge— God said that from the beginning that is before he created the world, he knew what was still to come. It refers to God’s knowledge prior to creation of events that would occur in the course of the history. Is 42:9, 45:11-13 acts 15:17.\r\nPurpose— God said my purpose will stand still and I’ll do all that I please. God’s eternal counsel should be an incredible source of comfort for all believers. He designed creation to display his glory and to be a suitable place for humanity to live under his benevolent reign.', 3, '2022-09-13 13:46:20', '2022-10-09 02:32:01', NULL),
(2884, 775, 763, 'Redemption is rooted in promises between the Father and the son. Redemption was first offered to humanity (Gen.3:15) commonly known as Adamic administration also identified as the first gospel.\r\nCovenant of renewal: in the nohaic administration God promised to maintain the creation (Gen.6-9).\r\nCovenant with Abraham: this covenant assigned special privileges and obligations to Abraham’s family (Gen.15:17-22).\r\nCovenant with Israel: in mosaic covenant sacrifices were visible confirmations of the promises the father and son had made before  creation (Ex.19-24).\r\nDavidic covenant: in this covenant grace is mentioned in 2 Sam.7, Psa.89,132.\r\nFinally the sixth administration is the new covenant and it began in the days of Jesus and will continue until his return (Lk.22:20; Heb.9:15; 12:24).', 3, '2022-09-13 13:56:14', '2022-10-09 02:32:01', NULL),
(2885, 775, 764, 'There are four aspects of Jesus’ redeeming work.\r\nInauguration of kingdom: Jesus taught that he had brought the kingdom of God to earth in his day (Matt.12:28). He has only begun that work, so we are still waiting for him to finish what he has started.\r\nObedience: Jesus lived a life of perfect obedience to the father culminating in his death on the cross.\r\nResurrection: by raising from the dead Jesus conquered death itself and secured the everlasting physical life for everyone who has faith in him (1 Cor.15:20-21).\r\nAscension: he ascended into heaven in order to be enthroned as king (Heb.12:2). Jesus completed his sacrifice in the heavenly temple to remain in his father’s presence meditating and interceding for his people.', 4, '2022-09-13 14:04:07', '2022-10-09 02:32:01', NULL),
(2886, 775, 765, 'General resurrection: at Christ’s return everyone that has died will be resurrected. Both the dead and the righteous will be given new bodies that will endure forever (Jn.5:28-29).\r\nLast judgment: immediately after the general resurrection Jesus will exercise his authority and power as king by destroying all his enemies and blessings all his faithful people at the last judgment (Ecc.12:14; Rev.20:12-13).\r\nRenewal of creation: this renewal heaven and earth will completely remove the presence and curse of sin (Rev.22:3). All the effects of humanity’s fall will be eradicated so that God’s people will live without sin.', 2, '2022-09-13 14:10:24', '2022-10-09 02:32:01', NULL),
(2887, 776, 766, 'Apostolic confession- Peter’s affirmation of Jesus’ anointing to the office of Christ was a direct revelation from God. It was an authoritative prophetic revelation from God. Mathew records peters confession in 16:15-17. People should have been able to deduce that Jesus was the Christ simply by looking at his miracles. \r\nTransfiguration- its the name theologians have given to the event when Jesus was reveled to his disciples in glory. It refers to the fact that his appearance was radically transformed, revealing a portion of his divine glory. Matt 17:1-8, Luke 9:28-36, 2 Peter 1:16-18.  His face shone with glory and his clothes became dazzling white. When Peter suggested that the disciples build shelters for Jesus, Moses and Elijah, God singled out Jesus as being worthy of the greatest honor and obedience.  The transfiguration is an amazing scene where Jesus goes up on the mountain and his three disciples go with him and they get display of the glory of Christ.', 5, '2022-09-14 00:15:07', '2022-10-09 02:32:37', NULL),
(2888, 776, 767, 'There are mainly three purposes.\r\nObedience— Heb 5:8-9 Jesus was entirely sinless, he never disobeyed God. But he was also fully and truly human. And his obedience prepared him for his work as Christ. Heb 5:9 it made him an acceptable sacrifice to God. So that he can become a source of eternal salvation. \r\nSympathy— Heb 4:15 Jesus faced and resisted the temptation to sin, giving Christians great comfort because he was fully human in every way. He experienced temptation but did not succumb to it.  \r\nImpeccability— it means inability to Sin. He never had an evil thought or desire or said a sinful word. 2 Cori 5:21, Heb 4:15, 1 John 3:5. All three persons of God always have been and always will be impeccable. But this doesn’t mean his temptation any less real. Because of his human nature Jesus experienced temptation from a human perspective. So, his obedience and sympathy aren’t lessened in any way.', 5, '2022-09-14 00:25:35', '2022-10-09 02:32:37', NULL),
(2889, 776, 768, 'Imputation—it’s referring to the act in which God assigned the guilt of sinners to the person of Jesus. So when we say that our sin was imputed to Jesus we mean that god blamed him for our sins. Jesus offered himself on the cross as a substitute for his people. Christ role as our substitute is reflected in the fact that Bible often refers to him as a sacrifice. Ro.3:25, Eph. 5:2\r\n\r\nJudgement-Human death is always a divine judgment against sin. Death entered the human race when Adam sinned in Genesis 3. It has continued ever since because Adam’s sin has been imputed to us. Jesus’s death was also a divine judgment against sin. But once our sin was imputed to him on the cross, his death became not only possible but necessary. As a part of this judgement Jesus also remained under the power of death for three days before his resurrection. But the good news is that has born the full wrath of god against our sin, so that there isn’t any divine judgement remaining to threaten us.', 5, '2022-09-14 00:34:00', '2022-10-09 02:32:37', NULL),
(2890, 776, 769, 'Plan of redemption- God’s plan to redeem humanity and the rest of creation depended on him fulfilling his covenant promises to establish his kingdom on earth under the kingship of David’s descendent, otherwise known as the Christ Luke 24:45-46, Ro. 1:1-4\r\n\r\nBlessings of salvation- Jesus’s resurrection with a wide variety of blessings we receive as a part of our salvation. It results in our justification, which is the forgiveness of our sin Ro.4:25. He is risen from the death and this means that he is someone who we can actually know and meet. It means Jesus’s power is available to us. \r\n\r\nResurrection- gives us incredible hope for the future and it is the pattern of what happens to us when we die. And we see in Jesus’s resurrection God’s pledge that death is not the end and that after the grave comes new life, resurrection the bodily life and we have a great eternal inheritance through resurrection.', 5, '2022-09-14 00:41:22', '2022-10-09 02:32:37', NULL),
(2891, 776, 770, 'Confession is the belief in the heart that a certain is true and it is a decision that one is willing to make an open commitment and let others know. It is a statement professing or declaring the conviction. Having faith in god, I’m strong in the lord and his might power, I’m a chosen generation and a royal priesthood, god has a plan for me are some of the verses that strengthen in our circumstances.As Christians, our faith in God and his sovereignty give us a safe place spiritually, mentally, and emotionally to address our concerns. We use biblical affirmations to build us up when we feel the most torn down and when our faith is at it’s weakest point.', 4, '2022-09-14 00:53:24', '2022-10-09 02:32:37', NULL),
(2892, 777, 655, 'Historical analysis looks at scripture as a window to history, exploring events lying behind the Bible. \r\nLiterary analysis— it looks at the scripture as a picture, a literary Portrait designed by their human writers to influence readers in particular ways.\r\nHistorical analysis— biblical theology approaches the scriptures primarily with it. It’s especially concerned with acts of God found in the Bible. It looks at scripture as window to the history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible. The Bible reports many different kinds of historical  events. But biblical theology primarily asks what do the scriptures say that God has done? Because Christian answer this question in different ways. The arrangement and modifications of pieces according to this method proponents reveal something of the author’s intention and the means by which he hoped to achieve them.\r\nThematic analysis— this looks at the Bible more as a mirror that reflects our interest and questions.', 4, '2022-09-26 12:44:23', '2022-10-09 02:33:01', NULL),
(2893, 777, 656, 'Factual historical analysis— its concerned with questions like the date of the exodus under Moses, the historical circumstances that gave rise to Israel’s monarchy, evidence of certain battles and other crucial events. The goal of this straightforward. It’s to establish a reliable accounts of the facts of history by combining what we learn from scripture and the data we gather from extra biblical sources.\r\nTheological historical analysis— biblical theologians are more interested in the theological significance of the acts of God reported in the scripture. To understand, we should turn to basic definition of theology found in the works of Thomas Aquinas that indicates what most Christians mean when they speak of theological reflection. Christian tend to agree with aAquinas that theology has 2 main concerns. On the one hand a theological matter is anything that describes other subjects in relation to God. The former category is what traditional theology calls theology proper. And the latter category includes matters such as the doctrines of humanity, sin, salvation, ethics, the church and  the like.', 5, '2022-09-26 12:55:35', '2022-10-09 02:33:01', NULL),
(2894, 777, 657, 'Cultural changes— cultural shifts can be traced back to the enlightenment of the 17th century. Christians have always explored the acts of God reported in scripture. Modern historicism is the belief that history holds the key for understanding ourselves and the world around us. One of the most enlightened figures who expressed this cultural shift was the German philosopher who lived from 1770 to 1831. He is best known for his proposal that every aspect of reality is caught up in the logical pattern of historical progress known as the dialect. He thought the entire universe was so ordered by God that it followed a divinely ordained historical logic. From his point of view we understand every item in the world best when we see it in the light of this rational pattern of history. Avalanches and archaeological discoveries shed much light on the ancient cultures of the world. The science of geology became an endeavor do discern the age and development of the earth not simply to understand the way it is at the present time.', 5, '2022-09-26 13:05:54', '2022-10-09 02:33:01', NULL),
(2895, 777, 658, 'Biblical theology helps clarify the Bible’s main purpose— some people approach God’s word as if it were a collection of independent stories or an assortment of advice and counsel or even a universal cookbook with recipes for the good life scattered across its sixty six books. But these approaches fail to bring to light the central purpose of scripture.\r\nBiblical theology helps guard and guide the church— reading scripture rightly means knowing where each book fits into its overarching narrative. And knowing the overarching narrative helps us read and understand accurately each event, characters, or lesson that’s been given to us as part of God’s progressively revealed word. \r\nBiblical theology helps us read understand and teach the Bible the way Jesus said— Luke 24 Jesus says he himself is the imperative key. If we fail to read and understand scripture in a way that leads us to Jesus then we will miss the point of the Bible and as a result we will reach others to commit the same error. \r\nBiblical theology helps us in our evangelist outreach— people first need to grasp that the Christian world wide accompanies a lot of transformation of mindset. We must start with God and creation to see what’s gone wrong and from there we will be able to understand what God has been doing throughout history, which will help us discover why God sent Jesus.', 7, '2022-09-26 13:18:01', '2022-10-09 02:33:01', NULL),
(2896, 777, 659, 'As Paul says all things are from God in the beginning and all things continue there existence now through the sustaining power of God. And all things are to him that is they are all for God’s glory and praise. In the word,  God so orders the history of his creation that it will ultimately bring him immeasurable Glory. When God’s will is done as perfectly on earth as it is in heaven every creature will bow before God and honor him as the divine king, the supreme creator of all. At that time the ultimate goal of history will be full filled. Despite failure God did not give up on his grand purpose God sent his eternal son into the world and through his death god rectified the failures of the past and redeemed a people for himself from all the nations of the world. Rev. 11:15 Christ is hailed as the one who will bring God’s kingdom to earth as it is in heaven.', 5, '2022-09-26 13:24:30', '2022-10-09 02:33:01', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2897, 778, 660, 'The word synchronic drives from two Greek words- the preposition sun which means with or together with and the noun chronology which means time. When it’s applied to historical events it describes occurrences that place together in time or at the same time. \r\nIllustration— most popular movies convey the flow of a story from beginning to end. They depict how one event leads to another and another and so on. Yet even though the movie is one whole unit is broken down into smaller parts called scenes, each scene represents a synchronic movement in the movie a period of time in the film. \r\nBiblical theology— biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular periods of time in the Old Testament as if they were scenes in a movie rather than on the flow of its entire history. Sometimes they focus on relatively short time frames and other times they focus on longer periods of history. Biblical theologians also deal with larger period of time like Abrahams life in Gen 11 through 25 a time that spanned around 175 years.', 7, '2022-09-27 01:39:19', '2022-10-09 02:33:36', NULL),
(2898, 778, 661, 'The concept of synthesis is not difficult to grasp. Basically, it simply means combining different components of something into a whole.  Different components of theology revealed in a particular period of history fit together in a coherent, logical structure of Old Testament in a particular time. \r\nDenial— based on the number of linguistic and cultural assessments they argued that the Greeks focused on abstraction and logical order much like systematic theology. Old Testament theology displays substantial concern for logic and rational thought . No substantial way of looking at life us free from careful logical reflection.\r\nLogical coherence— theology is expressed in a variety of genres. Different Old Testament authors emphasized different aspects of their faith and nowhere does the ot present an all encompassing logical system of theology. God’s revelation not only gave his people insight into particular events but also led them toward logical synthetic way of understanding, behaving and feeling about him themselves and rest of the creation.', 6, '2022-09-27 01:49:58', '2022-10-09 02:33:36', NULL),
(2899, 778, 662, 'Two worlds- poets of the Old Testament were interested in two different worlds that tell us about history. On the other hand they gave attention to the world they wrote about- what we call that world. When writing about that world they provided objective facts about God’s acts and words. Poetry often opened windows to the past. For example Moses and Miriam sang at the Red Sea. Old Testament poetry often provides windows to contemporary historical information from the writer’s own time Psalm 1. Old Testament poets turn their reader’s attention towards the future Isiah 40:1-11. Isiah predicted the time when the exiles of Judah would return to their land. Old Testament poetry often conveyed information about the revelatory acts and words of god in the past, present and future. They focused on their world by designing their text to influence the lives of their original leaders in particular ways.', 6, '2022-09-27 01:57:44', '2022-10-09 02:33:36', NULL),
(2900, 778, 663, 'Discerning information- Modern evangelicals often make the mistake of expecting Old Testament narratives to be like modern journalistic historical writings. Historians must seek to be as exacting as their counter parts in signs like chemistry and biology. There are many ways to summarize the rigorous standards, trust worthy historical accounts must be comprehensive, precise and objective. It is easy to confuse fact and fiction when historians do not reach the standards to some extent. It narratives did not completely follow these modern ideals. They did not propagate religious fantasies nor did they present historical errors. When we explore the genre of narrative in synchronic synthesis we face many different kinds of imprecision. Numbers of people, measurements, geographical references often do not meet modern scientific standards. But this lack of modern precision does not mean the accounts are untrue.', 5, '2022-09-27 02:05:57', '2022-10-09 02:33:36', NULL),
(2901, 778, 664, 'Scripture plays a foundational place in all of life, and scripture itself stresses the need to faithfully interpret in order to stand un ashamed before the Lord. Interpreting scripture faithfully necessitate that we view scripture as God’s word, assumes that scripture’s truth are knowable, requires that we respond appropriately, demands that  we engage in the task depending on God. It includes assessing, synthesizing and applying God’s inerrant word by means of exegesis and theology. We must be willing to let our understanding and application are truth be conformed the Bible’s declarations. Moses stressed that right relationship with the Lord is only possible when one hears God’s word in a way that leads to fearing and following him. David highlighted that God’s words are perfect, righteous, true and sweet that revise the soul, gives wisdom, rejoice the heart and lightened the eyes.', 5, '2022-09-27 02:20:52', '2022-10-09 02:33:36', NULL),
(2902, 779, 665, 'The English term diachronic drivers from two Greek words. First is the preposition dia which often means through or throughout and second is the Greek noun chronic means time. Diachrony is a concern with time. Biblical theology transformed, changed, or developed through time. It helps to see how diachronic approaches to the old testament relate to the synchronized synthesis. on the other hand the interdependence between dichronic any synchronized approaches.', 1, '2022-09-30 10:09:42', '2022-10-09 02:34:07', NULL),
(2903, 779, 666, 'Development -- we use the term development instead of simply of diachronic changes to suggest two main ideas. First, change in the old testament theological structure always moves forward or fulfilling God\'s purpose for history. Second, theological changes only occur through God’s providential purpose behind every change in the old testament theology. \r\nDivine purpose -- from one end to the other the scriptures affirm that everything in history including shifts in theology always accomplishes God\'s unfailing purpose for his creation. For Example, David\'s kingship had immediate purposes in God\'s plan for Israel, it was one step toward the greater goal of extending God\'s kingdom to the ends of the earth. \r\nDivine Providence-- when we pay no attention to details, old testament theological development often appears like a road of theological changes that looks smooth and straight. But when we take a closer look we see that the road of the old testament is actually filled with steep inclines, fast descents, and sharp turns to the right and the left. \r\nFor example- much ceremonial practices God required of Israel. Surprisingly some aspects of these laws appear to be marks of holiness precisely because they stand in contrast with the practices of other cultures.', 7, '2022-09-30 10:22:50', '2022-10-09 02:34:07', NULL),
(2904, 779, 667, 'Universal covenant -- the covenant with Adam and Noah is universal because they were made between God and all the people. So this tells us much about the relationship between God and all the people. \r\nNational covenant -- God established a national covenant with his special people of Israel. The covenants with Abraham, Moses, and David. The covenant with Abraham emphasized promises for the numerical increase of Israel and possession of the promised land. \r\nNew covenant -- last major covenant mentioned in OT Jer 31:31. This was designed to govern Go’s people when God fulfilled his promises to re-establish his people after exile and to spread his kingdom to the end of the earth.\r\nMajor theological period-- six major divine covenants premeated the theology of the times in which they were revealed that they provide us with ways of understanding the different emphases of major historical epochs in the old testament. Coming of Christ, redemption, resurrection, the ascension of the holy spirit, the work of the Apostles, the spread of the faith and Jesus\' return.', 7, '2022-09-30 10:35:09', '2022-10-09 02:34:07', NULL),
(2905, 779, 668, 'The typology is used in a variety of ways in other disciplines such as science and literary studies. Every trace of historical stages of a topic forms typology in the general sense of the word. Typology is the study of types. Drives from the Greek word tupos which is used 15 times in the new biblical typology the study of dichroic developments between the theological structures closely associated with a significant person, institutions and events in the scriptures. For example, Paul said about Adam in Romans5:14 a pattern of the one to come.  The Greek word translated pattern herd is tupos. The one who is to come is Christ. 1 Peter 3:20-21 Greek term translated corresponding to that is the antitype. So in Christ baptism is presented as a new testament counterpart to Noah\'s flood.', 6, '2022-09-30 10:45:13', '2022-10-09 02:34:07', NULL),
(2906, 779, 669, 'A change in sacrifice-- 1peter 2:5teaches all Christians are priests whereas in OT only select Levites had this privilege. In the old testament period, their main function was to carry out the worship of God through the offering of sacrifice. In NT Jesus fulfilled the OT sin offering through his death. \r\nDifferent ways to worship- offer God your whole self. Paul says Christians received the mercies of God through Christ\'s work as a gift. In OT God was pleased by the costly sacrifice and expressed this by calling it a smooth aroma. \r\nIn OT the temple was developed as a place where the Torah was read and prayers were said to God. In NT there was no special place to gather. People gathered in homes and other places to learn from the word of God. Worship the Lord in truth and in spirit. \r\nThe feasts of ancient Israel for the most part were focused on the sin offerings but rather on Thanksgiving for present and past blessings. In NT no such feast is observed.', 6, '2022-09-30 11:01:56', '2022-10-09 02:34:07', NULL),
(2907, 780, 670, 'Act Revelation and Word Revelation - this twofold concept of Revelation have characterized Biblical theology of the New Testament as well. On the one hand, the NT reports many revelatory acts of God such as Christ\'s earthly ministry and the ministry of the Holy Spirit in the first-century church. It also foretells acts of God that will take place in the future such as the glorious return of Christ. But on the other hand, NT also reports word revelation associated with the acts of God. God the Father spoke, Christ also spoke, and at times, angels and humans, by the spirit of God revealed God’s word as well. That\'s why NT narratives report both the actions and words of Jesus, the Apostle and other Christians in the new testament because God reveals himself through their actions and their words. OT paid attention to the logical interconnections between what God did and said.', 5, '2022-09-30 22:41:37', '2022-10-09 02:34:41', NULL),
(2908, 780, 671, 'Diachronic developments-- as the new testament history moved forward, theological structures underwent diachronic changes. At the close of the OT period, God\'s word through the prophets looked forward to the blessings of God when Israel returned from exile. When Christ appeared these theological concerns with return from exile shifted toward understanding how God had begun to pour out these blessings in Christ. \r\nObstacles -- new testament history presents at least three major obstacles to extensive diachronic analysis. By comparison with the OT, the NT covers a very short period of history. Old Testament deals with over 1600 years of history extending from the time of Abraham who lived around 2000BC by comparison to the NT history is very short. The entire NT represents only around 100 years of history. Although the NT introduces the most significant diachronic development so far in history.', 7, '2022-09-30 22:53:14', '2022-10-09 02:34:41', NULL),
(2909, 780, 672, 'John and Jesus-- John the Baptist and Jesus both announced that the arrival of the kingdom of God in the last days was near (Mk 1.15). The phrase kingdom of God doesn\'t appear in the OT but this announcement of the kingdom drew from an association between God\'s reign and what Moses and the prophets called the last days or the end of Israel’s exile (Is 52.7-10). Isaiah described the return from exile with an image of God leading his people back to Jerusalem. The good news declared the ruins of Jerusalem was your God reigns. Isaiah announced that when God restored his people in the last days he will demonstrate that he reigns victorious over all the nations and their idols. John the Baptist held a view of the last days that was very similar to the Jewish contemporaries. He believed God\'s kingdom on earth would come through the messiah acting quickly and decisively, exercising judgment against sinners and pouring out numerous blessings on God\'s repented people.', 7, '2022-09-30 23:03:34', '2022-10-09 02:34:41', NULL),
(2910, 780, 673, 'New Testament eschatology-- as modern followers of Christ we entered the Christian faith with the modern cultural background. We all came to Christ with views of life that are very different from the background of NT writers and because of these differences we often have to work very hard to grasp the mindset, the worldview that governed the ways knew NT authors conceived of their faith. \r\nImportance- Jesus’s three-phase eschatology was so prominent in the hearts of early Christians that we find it either explicitly or implicitly on every page of the NT. Jewish religious leaders and the general Jewish population raged against Christianity precisely because of the Christian outlook on the messianic last days. Christians believed that Messiah had already come, but in a way that was unexpected. Such a messianic scenario was utterly contrary to what most Jews believed in that day.', 5, '2022-09-30 23:14:06', '2022-10-09 02:34:41', NULL),
(2911, 780, 674, 'God does not distinguish between secular and spiritual but only between that which is of the flesh and spirit (Rom. 8). God doesn\'t measure the significance of ministry as men do. Christians can\'t take pride in the magnitude of spiritual ability for they are gift of God. Christian vocation is not a matter of consecration but calling. Corinth Christians were disturbed about their circumstances after being saved. Our calling is not set in cement (Rom.7.29). \r\nBear good fruit - patience, honesty, self-control create respect from others and leave a lasting impression because they reflect God\'s character.\r\nSow seeds of good fruit - kindness and generosity are the two important seeds.\r\nBe courageous - share testimony \r\nForgive - it is giving God permission to enter in and heal the situation. It is a huge act of love.\r\nPray and seek - pray often for God\'s kingdom to come into our own lives instead of someone else.', 6, '2022-09-30 23:28:52', '2022-10-09 02:34:41', NULL),
(2912, 798, 655, 'Historical analysis looks at Scripture as a window to history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible. Biblical theology approaches the Scriptures primarily with historical analysis. It looks at the Bible as a window that gives access to history.', 0, '2023-04-28 00:33:13', '2023-04-28 00:33:13', NULL),
(2913, 798, 656, 'The goal of factual historical analysis is rather straightforward, to establish a reliable account of the facts of history by combining what we learn from Scripture with the data we gather from extra-biblical sources. \r\nFactual historical analysis goes along with theological historical analysis since theological historical analysis is establishing the theological significance of the acts of God reported within Scripture. \r\nTwo main concerns are; a theological matter is anything that refers directly to God, and the second is a theological matter is anything that describes other subjects in relation to God.', 0, '2023-04-28 00:38:10', '2023-04-28 00:38:10', NULL),
(2914, 798, 657, 'The Enlightenment of the 17th century led to the formal discipline of biblical theology. German philosopher Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was known for his proposal that every aspect of reality is caught up in logical patterns of  historical progress known as the dialectic. The entire universe, he thought, was so ordered by God that it followed a divinely ordained historical logic. This and other forms of historicism rose to prominence in the modern period for many reasons.', 0, '2023-04-28 00:41:10', '2023-04-28 00:41:10', NULL),
(2915, 798, 658, 'Modern historicism inspired many critical theologians to approach the Scriptures with new questions and priorities. For example, Johann Gabler believed that the findings of the biblical theology might be of some interest from time to time, but modern Christians should believe only those parts of the Bible that pass the standards of modern rational and scientific analysis. Through various interpretations and perspectives Theologians cited biblical texts to establish doctrines and practice.', 0, '2023-04-28 00:47:26', '2023-04-28 00:47:26', NULL),
(2916, 798, 659, 'Biblical theologians work very hard to see the seeds of New Testament revelation in the initial stages of the Bible and then trace how these seeds grew as further act and word revelation brought successive stages of growth in the kingdom of God, leading to the New Testament. Biblical theology has demonstrated that the developments of biblical history were organically unified.', 0, '2023-04-28 00:51:38', '2023-04-28 00:51:38', NULL),
(2917, 799, 660, 'Synchronic: occurrences that took place together in time or at the same time. In synchronic synthesis, biblical theologians concentrate their attention on particular periods of time in the Old Testament: Brief moments & Long periods. Synchronic approaches concentrate on the theological perspectives established by the end of the time in view. The term is derived from to Greek words: the preposition sun which means \'with\' or \'together with\' and the noun chronos which means \'time\'.', 0, '2023-05-01 00:48:56', '2023-05-01 00:48:56', NULL),
(2918, 799, 661, 'Synthesis: combining different components of something into a whole. Synchronic synthesis describes the ways different components of a particular period of history fit together in a coherent, logical structure. A popular denial of the Old Testament\'s logical character and why we affirm its logical coherence can be illustrated with Adam\'s fall and his disobedience brought death to everyone joined to Adam. Christ\'s single act of obedience brought life to everyone joined to Christ. For this reason, Adam was a \'pattern\' or type of Jesus.', 0, '2023-05-01 00:55:13', '2023-05-01 00:55:13', NULL),
(2919, 799, 662, 'Two Worlds: \r\n1. That world: The world that they wrote about. Poetry often opened windows to the past (Exodus 15) to the present (Psalm 1), and to the future (Isaiah 40). \r\n\r\n2. Their world: The world of their readers. Old Testament writers designed their texts to influence their original readers in particular ways. \r\n\r\nNarratives: \r\nTrue stories about historical people, places and events that reveal many details about history through; words and speeches, characters, places, and connections. \r\n1. That world: The subject of their text. \r\n\r\n2. Their world: The world in which their readers lived.', 0, '2023-05-01 00:59:34', '2023-05-01 00:59:34', NULL),
(2920, 799, 663, 'Modern historical standards: comprehensive, precise, and objective. Authors of the Old Testament narratives wrote in ways that were determined largely by their didactic purposes.', 0, '2023-05-01 01:01:18', '2023-05-01 01:01:18', NULL),
(2921, 799, 664, 'Old Testament authors wrote their texts within a context of beliefs and theological structures that they shared with their characters as well as their readers. God has disclosed these theological frameworks through two kinds of extra-biblical revelation. First, he disclosed hem through general revelation of God in all things, and second, he gave them through special revelations not found in Scripture.  They felt no need to explain certain things explicitly because many theological structures were already in place.', 0, '2023-05-01 01:06:35', '2023-05-01 01:06:35', NULL),
(2922, 800, 665, 'Diachronic: From Greek \r\nDia = through or throughout\r\nChronos = time\r\nDiachronic points to the ways Old Testament theology transformed, changed or developed through time.', 0, '2023-05-04 00:32:21', '2023-05-04 00:32:21', NULL),
(2923, 800, 666, 'The reason the term \'development\' is used instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes is because everything in history, including shifts in theology, always accomplishes God\'s unfailing purposes for his create. God\'s goals for history exist not only as broad, long-term goals, but also as particular, short-term goals: David\'s king (short term) and all-encompassing kingdom purpose (long term).', 0, '2023-05-04 00:34:43', '2023-05-04 00:34:43', NULL),
(2924, 800, 667, 'Universal Covenants = between God and all humanity. Adam - the covenant of foundations, Noah - the covenant of stability. \r\n\r\nNational Covenants = between God and Israel. Abraham - covenant of promise, Moses - covenant of law and David - covenant of kingship. \r\n\r\nNew Covenants = the last major covenant mentioned in the Old Testament. The covenant of fulfillment: first coming of Christ, spread of faith, and Jesus\' return. \r\n\r\nThe six major divine covenants provide us with ways of understanding the different emphases of major historical epochs in the Old Testament.', 0, '2023-05-04 00:38:07', '2023-05-04 00:38:07', NULL),
(2925, 800, 668, 'Broad definition: any diachronic development of topics in Old Testament theology. Biblical typology is the study of diachronic developments between the theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in Scripture. \r\nExamples: Romans 5:14 (Adam and Christ)\r\n1 Peter 3:20-21 (baptism and Noah\'s flood)\r\nColossians 2:17 (Law and Christ)\r\nJohn 3:14-15 (serpent and crucifixion)', 0, '2023-05-04 00:40:30', '2023-05-04 00:40:30', NULL),
(2926, 800, 669, 'The aspects of Old Testament worship share the same ultimate goal as the church today: to extend God\'s glorious kingdom throughout the Earth. The new covenant was about the first coming of Christ: redemption, resurrection, ascension, Holy Spirit, and work of the apostles. To spread faith, and reiterate Jesus\' return. The important factors here are not to live as if they were in an earlier covenant age, and to never forget the wisdom of what God had taught in earlier ages.', 0, '2023-05-04 00:54:49', '2023-05-04 00:54:49', NULL),
(2927, 801, 670, 'Old Testament biblical theology and New Testament biblical theology have a mutual interest in God\'s twofold revelation: \r\n- act revelations (Christ\'s earthly ministry, Holy Spirit in early church, return of Christ)\r\n- word revelations (God the Father, Christ, angels and humans, by the Spirit)\r\nTemporal associates of act and word revelations: \r\n- subsequent, simultaneous, antecedent', 0, '2023-05-08 00:48:01', '2023-05-08 00:48:01', NULL),
(2928, 801, 671, 'Diachronic Character - As New Testament history moves forward, theological structures underwent diachronic changes. When Christ appeared, theological concerns shifted toward understanding how God had begun to pour out his blessings in Christ. \r\nNew Testament - fairly consistent: no extreme changes, consistently marginalized, no tremendous wealth or poverty, no large group travel, no wide scale obedience and disobedience.', 0, '2023-05-08 00:51:11', '2023-05-08 00:51:11', NULL),
(2929, 801, 672, 'Traditional: Systematicians have normally discussed the teachings of Scripture in five major categories: theology proper, anthropology, soteriology, ecclesiology, and eschatology. We need to become familiar with Old Testament eschatology. Adam: two crucial elements of Old Testament eschatology: humanity was created in God\'s image and two groups of people are competing for control of the world.', 0, '2023-05-08 00:53:29', '2023-05-08 00:53:29', NULL),
(2930, 801, 673, 'Jesus\' teaching about the last days represented one of the most decisive ways in which Christians had broken with first century Judaism. New Testament authors spoke of the entire New Testament period as \'the last days\'', 0, '2023-05-08 00:55:12', '2023-05-08 00:55:12', NULL),
(2931, 801, 674, 'My perception of the kingdom of God is that salvation is a gift to all humankind. It is up to us to share the gift of salvation to everyone. When I perceive the way that I do my ministry, I think of a common phrase that everyone mentions in conversation, \"you only live once.\" My rebuttal to that comment is, that \"you only get one chance at eternity.\" There is no do-over or makeup once you are dead. Therefore, to the world and church around us, it is so imperative to preach the message of the kingdom of God and how the Bible teaches us to turn from sin and apply the teachings set forth regarding the end times. \r\n\r\nLooking at all who God uses, they all had flaws. If we look at Moses, Jonah, Abraham, they all came with one imperfection or another, however these were some of the greatest change makers in history. My ministry applying what I have learned about the kingdom of God is that God doesn\'t hold you accountable for your shortcomings and mistakes even when man does. God uses people like me to advance the kingdom of God because the kingdom of God is for people like me. We would never need a church if we didn\'t need God or salvation, just as a sick person would never need to see a doctor or walk into a hospital. The kingdom of God is for everyone, not just the perfect and upright.', 0, '2023-05-08 01:01:08', '2023-05-08 01:01:08', NULL),
(2932, 802, 655, 'Biblical theology is theological reflection drawn from the historical analysis of God reported in Scripture. To understand what is meant by \'historical analysis\' a few broad perspectives should be examined. First of which is Literary analysis. Literary analysis is a literary portrait designed to influence readers in particular ways. Next is historical analysis. Historical analysis is a window to history, exploring historical events lying behind the Bible. Then there is thematic analysis, which is a mirror that reflects our interests and questions. Biblical theology approaches the Scriptures primarily with historical analysis. \r\n\r\nHistorical analysis looks at the Bible as a window that gives access to history. When the focus of exegesis shifts from traditional theological themes to the historical events described in the Bible, a very different set of priorities and concerns emerge. While sound biblical theology does not contradict sound systematic theology, it nevertheless leads to significantly different theological perspectives. Historical analysis is primarily concerned with the acts of God. The Bible reports many different kinds of historical events, but biblical theology primarily asks, \"what do the Scriptures say that God has done?\".', 7, '2023-05-10 00:28:33', '2023-05-10 02:19:10', NULL),
(2933, 802, 656, 'Theological tendencies of factual historical analysis and theological historical analysis go hand in hand as well as have some differentiators. First there is factual historical analysis, which is how the events in Scripture fit within the larger environment of the ancient Near East. Then there is the theological historical analysis which Biblical theologians are interested in the theological significance of the acts of God reported in the Scripture. A theological matter is anything that; refers directly to God (theology proper) and describes other subjects in relation to God. \r\n\r\nThe goal of factual historical analysis is rather straightforward. It is to establish a reliable account of the facts of history by combining what we learn from Scripture with the data we gather from extra-biblical sources. As important as such factual concerns may be, biblical theology is more concerned with theological historical analysis. Biblical theologians are more interested in the theological significance of the acts of God reported in Scripture.', 7, '2023-05-10 00:35:54', '2023-05-10 02:19:10', NULL),
(2934, 802, 657, 'The cultural changes that took place in the 17th century that led to the formal discipline of biblical theology can be traced back to the Enlightenment of the 17th century. Biblical theology is a Christian response to modern historicism: the belief that history hold the key for understanding ourselves and the world around us. Christians have always explored the acts of God reported in Scripture. But in the modern period, significant cultural shifts took place that led theologians to emphasize these historical interests as never before. Simply put, biblical theology is a Christian response to a prominent intellectual movement in the modern period, often called modern historicism. \r\n\r\nOne of the most well-known Enlightenment figures who expressed this cultural shift was the German philosopher Greg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, who lived from 1770 to 1831. Hegel is best known for his proposal that every aspect of reality is caught up in logical patters of historical progress known as the dialectic. The entire universe, he thought was so ordered by God that it followed a divinely ordained historical logic. From his point of view, we understand every item in the world best when we see it in the light o f this rational patter of history. \r\n\r\nAs a result of the cultural changes, historicism rose to prominence for many reasons; including archeology, geology, biology, and nearly every academic discipline. Historicism has had countless effect on modern Christian theology, particularly biblical theology. Theologians have either embraced historicism in ways that compromised essential Christian beliefs or incorporated historicism in ways that upheld and enhanced the Christian faith.', 8, '2023-05-10 00:45:15', '2023-05-10 02:19:10', NULL),
(2935, 802, 658, 'Modern Christians have come to approach the Scriptures through biblical theology due to the effects of historicism. Theologians have either: embraced historicism in ways that compromised essential Christian beliefs; or incorporated historicism in ways that upheld and enhanced the Christian faith. There have been many Christians in many branches of the church throughout the world who have not followed the critical rejection of biblical authority. These evangelicals continue to hold that the Scriptures are true in all that they claim, including what they claim about history. Despite these unwavering commitments to biblical authority, modern historicism has had significant effects even on the ways that evangelicals approach the Scriptures. \r\n\r\nAccording to Charles Hodge, he believed in the authority of the Scripture and his commitment to biblical authority led him to teach that Christians are obligated to base systematic theology on the findings of biblical theology. Hodge insisted that systematic theology must submit to all the discoveries biblical theology made in Scripture by putting them into logical order.', 7, '2023-05-10 00:54:31', '2023-05-10 02:19:10', NULL),
(2936, 802, 659, 'We speak of biblical history as the process by which God will be ultimately glorified by extending his kingdom to the ends of the earth because at each stage of biblical history, God had specific purposes or goals that guided his act and word revelation. Biblical theologians spend much of their time delineating these diverse goals. But at the same time, in Romans 11:36, the apostle Paul pointed to the ultimate goal of history: For from God and through him and to him are all things. To him be the glory forever, Amen. As Paul put it here, all things are from God in the beginning. All things continue their existence now through the sustaining power of God. And all things are \'to him\' that is, they are for God\'s glory and praise. In a word, God so orders the history of his creation that it will ultimately bring him immeasurable glory. \r\n\r\nGod will be ultimately glorified before every creature by extending his kingdom to the ends of the earth. As Jesus taught us to pray in Mathew 6:10, \'Your kingdom come, your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven\'. The divine goal of all world history is the extension of God\'s perfectly heavenly reign to every corner of the earth. When God\'s will is done as perfectly on earth as it is in heaven, every creature will bow before God and honor him as the divine king, the supreme creator of all. At that time, the ultimate goal of history will be fulfilled. The Scriptures trace how certain historical events are crucial to reaching the goal of spreading God\'s kingdom throughout the world.', 7, '2023-05-10 01:04:35', '2023-05-10 02:19:10', NULL),
(2937, 803, 660, 'The term \'synchronic\' is defined as the occurrences that had taken place together in time or around the same time. The actual origin of the word synchronic is derived from two Greek words, \'sun\' which means \'with\' and the noun \'chronos\' that means \'time\'. When the term synchronic is referred to within biblical theology it refers to the occurrences that took place either \'together in time\' or \'around the same time\'. The author of the text refers to the example of how the Old Testament has a similar approach when referring to a synchronic study to that of how a movie may have one overall story or theme, but in essence each scene will display or portray a specific story or moment of the overall movie. \r\n\r\nWith the synchronic approach illustrated within the Old Testament and within biblical theology, specific approaches are focused on within historical moments. They may vary in length of timeframe however time passes through each of these moments and allows theologians to have a relative perspective on the moments portrayed. Theologians at times actually refer to the Old Testament as a synchronic unit, and they concentrate on the theological perspectives that are established by the end of time.', 8, '2023-05-13 00:28:40', '2023-05-24 13:05:35', NULL),
(2938, 803, 661, 'The term \'synthesis\' is defined as combining different components of something into a whole. The actual concept is rather difficult to comprehend, although we typically use it daily. A common example is going to work one day, and being in several meetings, and coming across your colleagues and listening to several different dialogues in the background. When coming home and sharing your day with your spouse, it will be nearly impossible to reiterate each conversation and theme, however in this instance you would synthesize and provide a recap of the day holistically. \r\n\r\nOne of the common denials of the Old Testament\'s logical character was that of applying logic within each discipline. For example, theologians would believe that logic was a primary feature of what they would refer to as the \'Greek\' mindset, but it was reasonably foreign to the \'Hebrew\' mindset. The argument would then focus on basis that Greeks would focus on abstraction and logical order which is much like systematic theology. However, we affirm it\'s logical coherence by looking at a few relevant points. The first being that studies have shown that the contrasts between Greek and Hebrew mindsets were discredited. Although their mindsets were different in many ways, they were also very much similar to one another. Second, while many things in the Old Testament will remain mysterious to humans as God\'s thoughts are much beyond ours, the logical thinking is only a question of how it did versus if Old Testament theology involved logic.', 8, '2023-05-13 00:38:48', '2023-05-24 13:05:35', NULL),
(2939, 803, 662, 'The two worlds of interpretation for both poetic and narrative literature includes \'that world\' and \'their world\'. That world poetry commonly is referred to poetry that gave attention to the world that they wrote about. In writing that world poetry, objective facts about God\'s acts and words were provided. Another way that Old Testament poets focused on was the actual world of their readers, which is referred to as their world. The poets would work on designing their poetry to influence the lives of their beloved readers. \r\n\r\nIn a narrative sense, that world poetry would open windows to the readers in reference to the past, present, and future. When referring to their world In a narrative sense, their world poetry can be seen in Exodus 15 when readers where encouraged to move forward with confidence to the Promised Land. The text was designed to place influence on the reader to particular ways. When reading Old Testament poetry, it should not be read the same way you read pose, instead you should recognize the unusual vocabulary and figures of speech along with its emotional impact.', 7, '2023-05-13 00:49:37', '2023-05-24 13:05:35', NULL),
(2940, 803, 663, 'When discerning historical information from the Old Testament narratives, it is important not to expect them to be like modern journalistic historical writing as it contains unusual vocabulary and syntax, it\'s figures of speech, imaginative concerns, and it\'s emotional impacts. It is important to note that within historical accounts the historical records will include every significant fact about a situation to give a balanced account. Unlike modern journalistic historical writing, all subjective evaluation that may prejudice readers will be avoided. Another important fact to point in differentiating how they will be different from modern journalistic historical writing is that they will either report details with exact precision or at least acknowledge that they haven\'t. \r\n\r\nIn modern journalistic historical writing, it is very common practice to omit the actual facts and report that all facts have been included. Or, it is also notable that the writer will be bias and when reporting out the summary they will persuade the reader to be influenced in one direction of another. This will create a completely false narrative and drive a pre-meditated approach towards the reader. This will then create propogandas and religious fantasies.', 8, '2023-05-13 00:57:24', '2023-05-24 13:05:35', NULL),
(2941, 803, 664, 'The understanding that the biblical writers were only as precise as required by the didactic purposes help us overcome challenges to the truthfulness of Scripture by understanding that the no biblical passages were written in a theological vacuum. The authors would write their texts within a context of beliefs and theological structures. We must be able to grasp more fully the coherent, synthetic structurers of the historical period knowing that God may have given other special revelations that we do not have a record of. There are a variety of levels in spectrum as to simple structures versus very elaborate structures of theological structures within the Bible. \r\n\r\nSince  there are a variety of levels: basic level structures, middle level structures, and complex level structures there will always be a range of simple to elaborate understanding in how we overcome challenges to the truthfulness of the Scripture. As a reader continues their journey within the Scripture they are able to reflect on theological structures on many different levels. It is crucial to note that every moment of the Old Testament history was governed by logical covenant structures. These covenants help us make sense of God\'s particular act and word revelations.', 8, '2023-05-13 01:08:53', '2023-05-24 13:05:35', NULL),
(2942, 804, 665, 'The word diachronic is derived from Greek origin and refers to two Greek words, Dia, meaning through or throughout and Chronos which refers to time. Diachronic is commonly pointed to the way that Old Testament theology was transformed, changed or developed through time. While synchronic synthesis commonly focuses on the theological structures which emerged within chosen periods of biblical history, diachronic refers more in line with the storyline of history moving from one scene to the next. A common analogy which is made in reference to diachronic views in the Old Testament is that of watching a movie unfold from one scene to the next. \r\n\r\nWith a diachronic approach, biblical theology is more concerned with the changes that occurred in theological structures. A diachronic approach warrants questions probing what changes occurred during a given time when God spoke or acted? Or another example would be what theological developments occurred from the time of Moses\' early childhood to his call at the burning bush. It is essential to note that while diachronic and synchronic approaches may differ, they are also highly interdependent of one another as well. Meaning, you are not able to purse one approach quite extensively without the other.', 8, '2023-05-15 00:33:29', '2023-05-24 13:06:09', NULL),
(2943, 804, 666, 'The term development is used instead of simply speaking of diachronic changes to suggest two idea. The first idea is that changes in the Old Testament theological structures tend to always move towards fulfilling the purpose of God throughout history. The second reason is because theological changes only occur through God\'s providential involvement within history. \r\n\r\nDevelopment involves divine purposes and divine providence. Within divine purposes, God\'s unfailing purposes for his creation is always accomplished, including everything in history and shifts within theology. This essentially translates into the fact that no matter what changes take place, history remains on course and reaches the goals that God had always ordained for it. When referring to divine providence, the Old Testament is filled with abrupt shifts which are ultimately caused by God\'s providence. With God\'s complex involvement within creation, divine revelations are connected to the historical circumstances. One example of this would many of the ceremonial practices that God had required of the Israelites within the Old Testament. God had commanded many practices in order to make his people into a holy place, and to our surprise some of these practices were marks of holiness.', 9, '2023-05-15 00:42:07', '2023-05-24 13:06:09', NULL),
(2944, 804, 667, 'Universal covenants are defined as covenants between God and all of humanity. First we see the universal covenants with Adam and Noah and it is referred to as universal because the covenant itself was made between God and all of humankind. So rather than thinking of it as a covenant just between God and Adam or God and Noah, it was more about a covenant to tell us much more about the relationship between God and all people. The universal covenant refers to the governance of divine-human relations established between God and man. \r\n\r\nNational covenants are defined as covenants between God and Israel. The national covenants that we can see are with Abraham, Moses, and David, or his special people. God chose Israel to be the nation that would lead the rest of humanity in service to God. That is why this covenant is referred to as the national covenant, the attention is primarily focused on one group. When we look at the covenant of Abraham, the covenant emphasized promises for the increase of Israel and the promised land, and Israel was to then spread God\'s blessings to the entire world. That is why the covenant between Abraham and God was known as the \'covenant of promise\'. Moses\' covenant was respectively known as the covenant of law and David\'s covenant is referred to the covenant of kingship. \r\n\r\nNew covenants are defined as the last major covenant that was mentioned within the Old Testament. The new covenants were predicted by Old Testament prophets and emphasized the covenant being established after the exile. The time when Israel would repent of their sin and God would bring history to it\'s final stages. The new covenant had a primary goal or design which was to govern God\'s people when God fulfilled his promises to re-establish his people after exile and to then spread his kingdom to the ends of the earth.', 9, '2023-05-15 00:54:33', '2023-05-24 13:06:09', NULL),
(2945, 804, 668, 'Biblical typology is essentially the study of diachronic developments between theological structures closely associated with significant persons, institutions and events in Scripture. In laymen terms, biblical typology is the study of types. For example, when the apostle Paul declares Adam was a \'pattern of the one to come.\' We know from text that the the one to come is Christ, and therefore from a broader view we can see that Paul is observing that Adam was a type of Christ. \r\n\r\nBiblical typology is important to study as it functions as a figure of speech. When we refer to typology serving as a figure of speech, it is important to note that specifically it can be also a figure of comparison. A figure of comparison can be an indirect way of describing something by comparing them with something else. It is crucial to identify that biblical typology should be viewed as being designed to point towards futures antitypes. For example when we look at Adam being a type of Christ, it is important to affirm that in his providence, God has arranged history so that earlier persons, institutions, and events anticipated or foreshadowed later persons institutions and events. The reason that it becomes so important is that once we begin to grasp the process of reflecting, we can that identifying typologies are an important part of studying the Old Testament. In this way, you can begin to see how the theology of the Old Testament developed over time.', 9, '2023-05-15 01:07:54', '2023-05-24 13:06:09', NULL),
(2946, 804, 669, 'We should apply the Scriptures concerning aspects of Old Testament worship (sacrifice, temple, Passover meal) in church services today under the new covenant by first realizing what is the new covenant. As mentioned earlier, the new covenant was established after the exile and at the time when Israel would repent of their sin and God would bring history to it\'s final stage. Knowing that the church is currently living in it\'s final stages today at a time where the second coming of our Lord is imminent we should indeed take heed to the Scripture and apply the aforementioned teachings of Old Testament worship. \r\n\r\nBy knowing that God has allowed for what is to come to be explained and outline within scripture, it is important to note the covenant of fulfillment regarding the coming of Christ, to spread the word, and Jesus\' return. The application of Old Testament worship to be applied also coincides the the fact that God\'s people have to remember that they can not and should not live as they were in an earlier covenant age. And they should also never forget the wisdom of what God had taught in earlier ages, hence the Old Testament teachings. In the Old Testament, worship by humans on earth always imitated the reflection of angelic worship of God in heaven. The worship of Israel was centralized at the tabernacle around the ark of the covenant, and while we as humans reside on earth, the footstool of God is here on earth. Therefore it is essential to remember that while he died for us, we have an ultimate responsibility to worship our creator.', 8, '2023-05-15 01:16:50', '2023-05-24 13:06:09', NULL),
(2947, 805, 670, 'The twofold concept of act and word revelation has characterized biblical theology of the New Testament similar to how God had revealed himself in the Old Testament, through act revelations and word revelations. Within the New Testament, there are several reports of revelatory acts of God referencing the earthly ministry of Christ. The ministry of the Holy Spirit is also referred to in the first century church relating to revelatory acts of God. The New Testament also provides foresight into the acts of God, especially things that are to take place in the future. For example, the return of Christ is depicted several times within the New Testament, and reports on word revelations with the acts of God. \r\n\r\nThe New Testament provides both the actions and words of Jesus , the apostles, and other Christians. God reveals his ultimate self through the actions and words of others in the New Testament, depicting the concept of act and word revelation. This can be seen throughout the New Testament, specifically in Acts and Revelations as well as the epistles. God clearly revealed himself through these passages between his actions actions and his words. These words would reference to God\'s actions on behalf of his people and report God\'s word to his people as well.', 8, '2023-05-16 18:25:16', '2023-05-24 13:06:42', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2948, 805, 671, 'New Testament biblical theologians differ from Old Testament biblical theologians in regards to the way that they handle diachronic developments. For example, as New Testament history moved forward, theological structures also underwent diachronic changes. The earthly ministry of Christ brought forth hope of forgiveness of sins through his crucifixion. Also, as the Old Testament hoped for an entirely new creation to come in Christ, the New Testament refers to this as the clear prediction of Christ\'s glorious return. The primary difference though is outlined in three main obstacles. \r\n\r\nThe first obstacle is that the New Testament only refers to around 100 years of history conceptually. This is much different from the Old Testament. It simply does not cover enough of time for major diachronic developments to occur. The second is that in the New Testament many of the instances are very similar to one another. In contrast to the Old Testament, the New Testament God spoke to the people appropriate to the situations and God\'s people were fairly consistent during this time as opposed to the Old Testament. Lastly, the New Testament only represents one covenant, the New Covenant in Christ, where this diminished the significance of diachronic developments within the New Testament.', 8, '2023-05-16 18:36:55', '2023-05-24 13:06:42', NULL),
(2949, 805, 672, 'Becoming familiar with the background of Old Testament eschatology helps us understand diachronic developments in New Testament eschatology. Eschatology is a theological term, which essentially means the \'the doctrine of the last days, final things, or the end times.\' The Bible is very clear about certain aspects when it comes to referencing the end times. The New Testament holds great promise and biblical theologians have approached eschatology in ways that cut across the grain of debate in a traditional sense. \r\n\r\nIn the Old Testament, the development regarding the last days developed significantly over time. It began with the covenants with Adam and goes up to the time of Noah, Abraham, Moses, and David. Within the beginning of the Old Testament and in biblical history, God revealed two crucial elements of eschatology, the first beginning with the story of mankind and how humans were created to to fill the earth and subdue it. The goal for history and creation was for the earth to be a place where glory would dwell with his people. The second element was the separation brought on as a result of sin. Two groups were formed as a result of the woman eating from the tree, and the first group is in reference to the seed of the woman who remain faithful to God. The seed of the serpent would be the second group who follow the ways of Satan. \r\n\r\nElements such as the one dating to the time of Adam, crossed over to the time of Noah, Abraham, Moses, and David. With these diachronic developments of eschatology within the Old Testament, ewe are able to see how the last days developed even further within the New Testament. First century Judaism with a twofold view on history, along with the relationship of John and Jesus which drove advancements in the development of eschatology are noted in the New Testament. Prophecies from older times were being fulfilled within the New Testament referring to the last days.', 9, '2023-05-16 18:48:26', '2023-05-24 13:06:42', NULL),
(2950, 805, 673, 'New Testament authors were preoccupied with Jesus\' three-phase eschatology because biblical theologians have demonstrated that every New Testament teaching of Jesus\' was shaped by his three-phase view of the last days. Every New Testament writer was deeply engaged with Jewish theology. New Testament authors were deeply focused on Jesus\' three phase eschatology and their deep interest in this was displayed in everything that they wrote. For example, when the authors would write, they would commonly refer to the New Testament as the \'last days.\' Christology, referred to as the doctrine of Christ displayed Jesus as the fulfillment of Israel\'s hope regarding the last days. Lastly, soteriology, the doctrine of salvation was shaped as well by eschatology. The New Testament authors had a similar theme in all of their writings and had displayed these three common themes within their writings as there was such a high importance around the end times and what was to come during the last days. The three-phase eschatology focused on components such as systemic theology, biblical theology and would concentrate on the application of salvation in recurrent themes, ordo salutis. \r\n\r\nThis three-phase eschatology represents a decisive break between first-century Judaism and Christianity because Jesus\' teaching about the last days represented one of the most decisive ways in which Christians would break away from first century Judaism. An important factor in this was that Jewish leaders and the general population would be extremely upset and raged against Christians in regards to their views on the last days. They were furious that Christians believed that their Messiah had already come.', 9, '2023-05-16 19:05:28', '2023-05-24 13:06:42', NULL),
(2951, 805, 674, 'My understanding of the kingdom of God shapes the way that I do my ministry in several different ways. To begin, having a strong foundational knowledge of the Scripture is vital to ministry. Unless you have a clear understanding about a subject, you are not capable to speak to it, defend it, or be able to advance it. That can be taken into consideration in any field of work, but most importantly with the Word of God, as that is something you can not merely substitute. The kingdom of God is displayed throughout the Old Testament and New Testament and the Scriptures teach us that without salvation, there is no other path to heaven. There were many themes we read in the Old and New Testament about how the perception of human kind and nature perceived the teachings of Jesus and his prophets and disciples. Many of times, it was not received. In ministry, there is no guarantee that they ears that hear or the eyes that see have to believe as we can clearly see in the Bible, however the kingdom of God is at hand and it is our dire responsibility to ensure that we clearly apply the principles that God has set forth for us to be faithful stewards even when we are not accepted. \r\n\r\nMy ministry will definitely change because I was born into this life, I did not choose this life, rather this life chose me. I need to apply the concept of God\'s kingdom to all aspects of life and ministry knowing that even when God was denied by his own disciples and his people, he did not stop doing what his mission was. I will change my mindset that because I grew up in the church, everyone in the church will accept what we speak of, which is simply not true. I have to be willing to expect and accept that even members of a church and gathering may deny Christ, his teachings and what the Scripture points out. This is a cultural and mindshift for me as I move forward and advance my studies in theology.', 9, '2023-05-16 19:12:20', '2023-05-24 13:06:42', NULL),
(2952, 806, 866, 'The Apostles\' Creed was developed over several centuries. The creed was composed of a collective work which would change and develop over time. It is a well known and commonly used summary of what a Christian believes in. Around A.D. 700, the creed was standardized in Latin. Although there is no evidence of such, it is believed that the Apostles\' Creed was written during the first century by the twelve apostles themselves. There are many speculations on who was the original author(s) however, regardless of the author, the Apostles\' Creed was widely used within the Western Churches. \r\n\r\nThe creed that we know today was at one point referred to as the latter version of the Roman Creed as there were many similarities between the two. During the initial days of referring to the creed, the wording would vary from church to church, however in the eighth century the wording was standardized into the commonly recognized form that we use today. While Creeds are well purposed and significant articles, it is important to note that they should never be given the same importance of the Bible. The Bible is the only inspired, and infallible rule of faith and practice, breathed by God. The Creed was created and formed to assist Christians to learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible.', 8, '2023-06-14 12:11:14', '2023-07-03 19:32:29', NULL),
(2953, 806, 867, 'The church believed it was important to create and use a creed because the Apostles\' Creed was created to help Christians learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible. During the early days, many Christians were suspicious of creeds, and questioned whether a creed held the same authority as Scripture. No creed should ever be elevated to this level whether in theory or practice. The importance of creating a creed was to have a tool that summarized the understanding of the Scripture and help Christians learn and remain true to the teachings of the Bible. \r\n\r\nThe use of a creed was to originally look at the Scripture in it\'s original form, holding true to doctrine. Next the churches\' traditional teachings as affirmations of Scripture were viewed and then the Apostles\' Creed was used to summarize the churches understanding of Scripture. Therefore the main reason why the creed was important and created was to provide an understanding of the core beliefs of a Christian and provide a summary. The early church would rely heavily on Scripture for the basis of all its doctrine, but they would still depend on and require traditional teachings of the church to be able to summarize and understand the teachings found in the Scripture.', 7, '2023-06-14 12:17:36', '2023-07-03 19:32:29', NULL),
(2954, 806, 868, 'The Trinity in terms of persons and nature are described in the Creed on the belief that there is only one God and this God exists in three persons, yet one essence. The Trinity is one of the most important yet complex beliefs of Christianity, the belief of one essence being only one God, yet three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The first person, Father is referred to in nature as Almighty and the maker of Heaven and Earth. The Son is resembled in nature as one who went through incarnation, suffering, death, burial, resurrection, and ultimately ascension. Lastly, the Holy Spirit which allowed for the virgin Mary to become pregnant with Jesus and is associated with the experience of salvation. \r\n\r\nThe different work attributed to each by the Apostles\' Creed is clearly outlined as the creed has a lot to say about the Son, Jesus Christ. For example, several mentions of details of his earthly life and ministry are revealed in the creed. His incarnation, coming to earth as a human baby boy, living a genuinely human lift, and suffering, death, burial, resurrection, and ascension into heaven. The creed also mentions that Jesus will judge all of humanity on the last day, condemning the wicked while granting the believers eternal life. The creed may not distinctly call out each statement extensively, however the creed says enough about God and his persons to affirm the belief of Trinity and the historic Christian faith.', 9, '2023-06-14 12:26:45', '2023-07-03 19:32:29', NULL),
(2955, 806, 869, 'It is important to participate in the church and to preserve its central doctrines as the Bible clearly teaches Christians to form an actual physical community and the Creed also emphasizes the importance of participating in the church. The church is identified in prominence as Christ has appointed the church to protect and proclaim the gospel of Jesus Christ. The main and primary job of the church is to protect the truth. The Apostles\' Creed refers to the church in two different phrases, one being the holy catholic church and the other being the communion of Saints. It is important to note that the holy catholic church does not refer to the Roman Catholic Church, but as a church in all its parts throughout the world. The phrase communion of saints refers to the fellowship of believers, re-affirming the need and importance of the church. \r\n\r\nEven in the first century, believers wanted to avoid the gathering of saints and communion of fellowship, but the Scripture teaches that the church is not only important, but necessary for Christians. The Bible teaches Christians to form an actual physical community. The community is to be like a family where people are able to interact with one another face to face. The Apostles\' Creed does not teach or encourage a believer or Christian to believe everything the local church teaches, rather the Creed affirms the fact that Christ appointed the church to protect and proclaim the gospel and its truth. \r\n\r\nThere are many false teachings around us, whether in the church or outside the church. There are many sinful practices as well. During all of this, God has never drawn back the assignment of the church, which was and is to protect the doctrine of the truth. The church has a job to do, and sometimes it is done better than other, however we have to keep trying to preserve doctrine in the church. If deny or abandon this responsibility, we are denying a major article of Christian faith.', 9, '2023-06-14 12:36:24', '2023-07-03 19:32:29', NULL),
(2956, 806, 870, 'The Apostle\'s Creed is foundational to Christian theology as it introduces the teachings of the apostles recorded in Scripture. Just a building requires a strong foundation, the church is symbolized as being founded on the apostles and prophets. Without a strong foundation that is true the church could not be built in a way that would honor God. Therefore, in a similar manner, Christian theology required a strong foundation on true doctrines and principles. This allows us to honor God and be useful to his people. \r\n\r\nThe Apostles\'  Creed is foundation to theology since it introduces the teachings of the apostles in Scripture. The Apostles\' Creed provides a summary of the central beliefs within Scripture, and the Bible is so clear on these points that we can not compromise them. If a false teacher or prophet presents us with bad or incorrect theology, the creed is a standard that helps us remain faithful to Scripture when standing against these false teachers and prophets. The Apostles\' Creed serves as an anchor even when you are up against some of the most convincing false teachers that come against you. An example of this took place during the early centuries and the teaching of Gnosticism. The Apostles Creed would confidently reject this idea on the basis of the Scripture and be able to stand against such false teachings. \r\n\r\nLogical Basis is the other method in how the Creed is foundational to Christian theology. We need to initially learn the big doctrines first in order to get the shape of the tree in place, and be firmly rooted in Scripture. When we are able to picture it this way, we can see the value of the beginning with the Apostles\' Creed. We are able to see the relationship between different beliefs within our theological systems.', 9, '2023-06-14 12:47:17', '2023-07-03 19:32:29', NULL),
(2957, 807, 871, 'Christians say that the God of the Bible is the one and only true God because of two concepts that they strongly believe in: the singularity of God along with the simplicity of God. To begin with the singularity of God, many people believed in false gods of the Greeks and Romans in the early days. This belief was so common that polytheism was at times required by law. Polytheism is the belief in the existence of multiple gods. However as the Scripture teaches us, people of all kinds know that deep within their hearts that the universe could not have been formed without the hands and work of a divine creator. People accept and understand the existence of God in the Scripture, however through humans sin and wrong doings we refuse to glorify him and give God the thanks He deserves. Within the belief of polytheism there is a belief in multiple gods, however no one god is able to claim being the only supernatural being, which is the role of the one true God.  \r\n\r\nThe second point refers to the singularity of God. Although God, being a single being exists in three persons. Christians believe in the Trinity and worship all three persons of the Trinity. By neglecting any one person of the Trinity is to reject God himself. Father Almighty known at the creator of all things refers to the entire Trinity hence the singularity of God. \r\n\r\nThe creed denies polytheism by using the singular form of \'god\' and by dismissing any plural forms of \'god\'. The Scripture also refers to the presence of God in different names, El Shaddai (almighty God), El Elyon (God most high), Adonai (Lord, Master, Ruler), and Yahweh (Jehovah, Lord). The creed proclaims that their is only one God that deserves to be called \'God\' and that is the God of Christianity.', 9, '2023-06-16 12:13:53', '2023-07-03 19:33:01', NULL),
(2958, 807, 872, 'Simplicity in terms of describing God\'s essence refers to the fundamental nature/substance that makes up his being. God is anything but simple in nature, He is not easy to understand. However when using the term simplicity, His essence is not a combination of different substances. He is a unified whole that purely consists of a single substance. Central to the core belief of the Trinity, God has three persons but only one essence, which again illuminates His simplicity. The Scripture commonly refers to God in three persons, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. However, the Scripture also states that there is only one God. So when teaching about God, we don\'t separate three separate Gods, but rather join them together in the Trinity allowing for three to become one. \r\n\r\nThese three persons had always existed together as only one God. The church made it absolutely clear that three persons in one God is one divine being. The Apostle\'s Creed also refers to the belief of one God who exists in three persons. Even in worship we fully acknowledge and offer worship to all three persons of the Trinity. To neglect any one person of the Trinity would be to reject God himself. The essence of God is fundamental in nature, and is not a composite of different substances, rather substance that makes up his being. He is unified and simple consisting of only one substance.', 8, '2023-06-16 12:22:31', '2023-07-03 19:33:01', NULL),
(2959, 807, 873, 'Both ontological Trinity and economic Trinity speak of the same Trinity, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. However the two emphasize different aspects of the relationships between the three persons. Ontological Trinity is a perspective of the Trinity and relates to being, and essence. If you are referring to or focusing on God\'s being, the ontological Trinity perspective focuses on the three persons of the Trinity and how the persons are integrated into one another and share a single essence. From this perspective, all three of God\'s persons are eternal, infinite, and unchangeable. With each of these three persons, they also have the same essential attributes as well. They include divine attributes such as wisdom, power, holiness, justice, truth and goodness. \r\n\r\nEconomic Trinity is also a perspective of the Trinity and relates to household management, and how the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit relate to one another as distinct personalities. When we refer to the economic Trinity, we are taking into consideration how God\'s persons interact with one another. As well as studying how they are able to relate to one another as individual persons. Although the concept is three in one, each of the three persons hold a different set of responsibilities. They each have a different level of authority, and a different assigned role and task to perform. The three persons engage in communication with one another and act upon each other. \r\n\r\nFrom both ontological and economic Trinity point of view, the Father is said to be the first person. The reason being the Son was created by the Father and the Holy Spirit was proceeded from the Father. We can see of the Father\'s authority in the Scripture as the Son refers to doing the Father\'s will. Therefore with both economic and ontological Trinity, they both share the similarity that they accept the belief of three persons in one essence, however they differ from one another as economic Trinity is focused on the relation of Father to son and Holy Spirit and the level of authority and household versus the ontological Trinity which is the essence of one God but three persons.', 9, '2023-06-16 12:35:17', '2023-07-03 19:33:01', NULL),
(2960, 807, 874, 'Fatherhood as it relates to his role as Creator refers to God being the creator of all things. He is the father of everything he has created. Paul affirmed this idea in the Scripture by stating that to create something is to become its Father. While the creator has formed and brought all things to being, He continues to show great patience time and time again while the world that He created continues to fall into sin and grow further and further away from Him. \r\n\r\nFatherhood as it relates to his role as King is revealed in the Scripture similar to how a king  at times is referred to as the father of the people he serves and the people he serves are referred to as his children. David was referred to as a King and father to his people and when the people referred to David as their father, they were referring to him as their king. Similar to this concept, God was called Father because he was the great king. The great king ruled over all of the world and because he ruled over his chosen nation, Israel, they were his children and he was their father. God is referred to as Father since he sits on his heavenly throne and rules over all of creation and leads armies into battle. This shows an authoritative father and divine king caring for us, giving us comfort and confidence. Similar to the Lord\'s Prayer, we refer to God as our Father in Heaven, having dominion over all the Earth.  \r\n\r\nFatherhood as it relates to his role as family head over his people is seen in the Old Testament in many different areas. For example, as Adam was the family head over his wife and sons, God was the head of Adam. God is also portrayed as the head of the nation of Israel, he takes care of his people and portrays his love for Israel since it\'s childhood. Jesus also taught in the Scripture that our Father answers our prayers in the same way that a human father provides for their children. And he also disciplines us for our own good in the same manner that a human father would discipline his children. The church is commonly referred to as God\'s household or family and the love he has for his children is displayed throughout the Scripture.', 8, '2023-06-16 12:46:37', '2023-07-03 19:33:01', NULL),
(2961, 807, 875, 'God\'s authority over creation as absolute, exclusive and exhaustive is described by his unlimited power. God has unlimited, omnipotence, and matchless power when it comes to doing anything he will do at anytime. He can create the universe while choosing to destroy everything that he has created. He can control the weather, he is able to rule and control human governments, defeat his enemies. He has and continues to save his people while performing mighty miracles. He is the Father and has the ultimate and unlimited authority and absolute power to do as he wishes. When we refer to God\'s authority being absolute, we can see within Scripture how God chose to create the heavens and the earth, how he chose to create man and woman, and how he also chose to wipe out the creation that he had made. This reveals his absolute reign as being the King and freedom to whatever he wishes to do with his creation. \r\n\r\nWhen we refer to the exclusive authority belonging to God, we acknowledge that the final word and authority  belongs only to the one that created everything, God. If God chose to undo or wipe out the world, he is the only one who can do so, because he is the creator of the  universe and without his authority nothing can come to be. While man and mankind do possess and hold some level of rule, no one holds the authority that belongs only to the creator. Humans have a limited measure on authority and the Father\'s authority is always superior to the authority of any creature or mankind. \r\n\r\nLastly, God\'s authority as being exhaustive is described as God having authority over everything and everything is under God\'s authority. That means everything over the universe is subject to the authority of God. His authority extends over everything that he has created and it clearly outlines that nothing is free from the obligation to obey God. Everything that he has created was created for a concise purpose, and no matter what the subject is, there is no moral neutrality.', 9, '2023-06-16 12:56:41', '2023-07-03 19:33:01', NULL),
(2962, 808, 876, 'When the Scripture refers to the \"Son of God\" when applied to Jesus, it is  referring to Jesus being God\'s son in a unique way. The Trinity depicts this example of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, and Jesus is the divine and eternal Son of God. When we refer to the deity of Christ, we must realize that Jesus is fully God and also fully man. We can not have one without the other. Jesus came in the form of man to this world and he is also fully God. This is one of the most fundamental pieces of fact that we must understand to comprehend the divinity of Jesus. \r\n\r\nThe Scripture refers the \"Son of God\" commonly to talk about things in the Scripture that are not divine, for example angels, the nation of Israel, the Kings of Israel, Adam and believers were all referred to as the Son of God throughout the Scripture, and  none of these beings were divine. Jesus, being the son and part of the Trinity comes to us with authority of power, and glory to the Father and the Holy Spirit. There is nothing that can be changed, added, revised about the Trinity and Son of God, He holds the same divine attributes of wisdom, power, holiness, justice, goodness, and truth. Although there are three different persons within one essence, each person holds a different level of responsibility. A father can not replace a son, a son can not replace a father, and a son can not replace the Holy Spirit. The Son of God is subordinate to the Father\'s authority and the Son has authority over the Holy Spirit. Therefore, with each role, comes a level of authority and responsibility. \r\n\r\nThe divine essence of the Trinity and Jesus being the Son of God is what provides the clear distinction of who Jesus is. He has always been the Son of God and is the eternal Son of God. We are told in the Scripture that Jesus was God\'s divine Son and is the exact representation of the Father\'s being. While God has three persons, there is only one essence. While the highest rank always belongs to the Father, there is no conflict between the Father and Son since the Son and Father always agree.', 9, '2023-06-19 12:13:54', '2023-07-03 19:33:43', NULL),
(2963, 808, 877, 'The title \"Lord\" points to the divinity of Jesus by meaning ruler or master also known as Kurios. Kurios was the term that was used in translation of the sacred name of God by Jewish scholars when they translated the Old Testament. The use of the word Kurios in the New Testament refers to the divinity of Jesus. Kurious was a common word that was used for a ruler or master and was considered a polite form of address. Not everyone who would call Jesus recognized his divinity, at times they were simply calling Him Lord out of respect. However as the Apostles Creed teaches us and as we see when the church confesses, God is indeed a full member of the Trinity. \r\n\r\nWhen the churches formally confesses that Jesus is Lord, we are affirming the biblical teachings that Jesus is indeed our God and is a full member of the Trinity. He holds the same divine attributes as the Father and the Holy Spirit. When we refer to the divinity of Jesus, it means that we should acknowledge him and worship him both in our prayers and in our songs. It also means that we should pray to him just as we pray to the Father and Holy Spirit. We can take comfort knowing that God has redeemed us from sin and we can see the divinity of Jesus.', 7, '2023-06-19 12:21:10', '2023-07-03 19:33:43', NULL),
(2964, 808, 878, 'Jesus\' experiences prove that he was truly human by a few different factors. To begin, Jesus was conceived through human birth. Although Mary was a virgin and she and Joseph did not have physical relations leading to the birth of Jesus, He was conceived by the Holy Spirit and born to a female. He began as a baby in his mother\'s worm and was formed in the same form and fashion as every other baby is molded in a mother\'s womb. He did not descend from heaven as a supernatural being, rather was brought into this world through his mother\'s womb as a human being. The next point to note is that Jesus was a real person, a real man with a physical human body. He offered his physical being to be punished, humiliated and tortured for divine judgement to atone for the sins of humankind. \r\n\r\nIt can be a common belief for people to accept that a god could disguise himself as a human being, but that is not what Jesus did here. He actually became a human being and went through the physical and emotional parts of a human being. Which then brings us to the point of his physical form, Jesus was arrested and crucified after being flogged, spit on, forced to wear a crown of thorns. His body was one that was beaten, bruised and bled. A body that at one point from exhaustion, collapsed, and was buried in a tomb and allowed for his soul to depart. When Jesus was on the cross, he committed his spirit into the hands of the Father. We can see that when Jesus died, his physical body was buried by men, but his spirit was committed into the hands of God, the Father. When our bodies die as humans, our souls are separated from our bodies and continue in a conscious state.', 8, '2023-06-19 12:31:03', '2023-07-03 19:33:43', NULL),
(2965, 808, 879, 'The Old Testament title \"Christ\" means the actual title of Jesus\' office, such as \"king\" or \"judge\". The word Christ is a translation of the Greek word christos which is actually another translation of the Hebrew word mashiach, or messiah which means the anointed one. In the Old Testament, the anointed one referred to anyone that was appointed by God to serve in a particular capacity. There have been many prophecies in the Old Testament that encourage people to long for the one who would deliver them, the Messiah. The Messiah would save them from their suffering and bring them into God\'s glorious blessings. This was referring to the coming of Christ, and was revealed as He took form of a human and was able to reveal himself as the Son of God. \r\n\r\nThe Messiah had a few prerequisites, he had to be the Son of David, be able to atone sacrifice, and be the second Adam. The reason that he had to be the second Adam, refers to the fact that someone had to come in to succeed where the first Adam or man had failed. Adam was initially setup as the head of the entire race, but since he fell and failed, this had to established. God never changed his requirements, human beings still had to build the kingdom of God. In order to build it, we needed a human itself, so eventually the Father sent his own Son to fix the problem. And that is how Jesus is able to fulfill his office. He is able to succeed where Adam and the rest of humankind has failed throughout.', 8, '2023-06-19 12:39:25', '2023-07-03 19:33:43', NULL),
(2966, 808, 880, 'One of the areas that are disputed time and time again, is whether or not Jesus had a real flesh and human nature, or was it just a fake appearance. The human nature of Jesus and it\'s relationship to his divine nature has been revealed in the Scripture, and Christian theology has maintained that Jesus is fully human in every aspect. He has a body, and a soul and was subject to the same types of physical and emotional strains that every human has including sickness, injury and death. \r\n\r\nThe areas of differentiation comes into play when we consider the birth of Jesus. We were all born into sin, however the birth of Jesus was different, therefore he was born without sin. He lived a perfect, sinless life. Before Adam sinned, he was human, and the belief is that humans are able to live a life without sinning which is was Jesus has shown us. It is possible to have a life that does not sin. When we do die, we will still be entirely human, however we will lose the ability to sin.  Jesus is the only person that possesses two natures, a human nature and a divine nature. While every other human only has one nature, Jesus is both God and man. He is fully divine at the same time as being fully human. \r\n\r\nHe is one person with two natures, and we can not dismiss the fact that Jesus never put aside his glory or his divine nature. That is not possible, God\'s nature is immutable. He is not able to put aside his  nature and become separate. Jesus was able to take on an additional nature that did not replace, but merely veiled his divine nature and glory while taking on the form of man. This is how Jesus\'s human nature and relationship to divine nature were able to coexist while he was in human form.', 8, '2023-06-19 12:47:34', '2023-07-03 19:33:43', NULL),
(2967, 809, 881, 'Although the Apostles\' Creed does not state that the Holy Spirit is divine, the Apostles\' Creed does affirm that the Holy Spirit is divine in two way. First, as the Trinity hold true to the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit being equals and as well as the creed\'s description of the conception of Jesus as being divine. The Trinitarian formula emphasizes the belief of having only one God which exists in three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. During the early church, Hippolytus explained the baptismal creed used in his day made the structure of the Trinity very explicit. For example, a person was immersed three times during water baptism. First confessing in the articles of faith relating to the Father, then the Son, and lastly the Holy Spirit. \r\n\r\nThe second way the creed affirms the divinity of the Holy Spirit is through the conception of Jesus. When the angel of the Lord came and spoke to Mary, the angel state the Holy Spirit will come upon you, in reference to the Most High. Only God the Father can have the power of the Most High, all pointing to the work of the Holy Spirit. The Apostles\' Creed affirmed the full divinity of the Holy Spirit through this conception affirming that creating the body of Jesus was specifically the work of God. With the belief and confirmation of the Trinitarian belief, we can see the expressed form of divinity as the angel states to Mary that the Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you. This references the magnificence of the Father as the authoritarian that will be divine and fulfill the will of the Father.', 8, '2023-06-21 12:09:56', '2023-07-03 19:34:16', NULL),
(2968, 809, 882, 'The biblical basis for the creed\'s teaching about the Holy Spirit\'s divinity can be revealed in several different ways. The creed has always been recognized as a summary of the Scripture and never to be taken in as a replacement for the word of God. The Bible however points to the teachings of the divinity of the Holy Spirit in several different ways. The areas of focus are around the names that he is called, the attributes that he possesses, the work he performs, and the Trinitarian formulas that refer to him.  In the Bible, there are several names that are connected to the Holy Spirit and and some of these names suggest the divinity of the Holy Spirit in a very clear and concise manner. Though the word holy does not in itself indicate the Holy Spirit is divine, God is commonly referred to as the Holy One which is part of the Trinity. Names that were implicit on the continuum were: Spirit of the Lord, Spirit of God, Spirit of the living God, Spirit of Jesus, Spirit of Christ, Spirit of your Father, Spirit of his Son, Spirit of him who raised Jesus from the dead. \r\n\r\nAttributes that were possessed by the Holy Spirit demonstrates the continued divinity through communication, and forms of non-communication that can be expressed. God possesses the attribute of communication with human beings. He demonstrates that we were created by a rational God that we are able to communicate with him and he to us with a form of communication. This is also relevant in a non-communication form through the omnipotence of God. While God is perfect, his power is all throughout which exemplifies the divinity of the Holy Spirit. This can also be revealed through the works that he has done, which are appropriate for God alone and none other. He is creates new life, and regenerates our spirit and is the access to the Father. There is also power behind his works and his miracles which are purely divine. \r\n\r\nThe Trinitarian formula affirms the Holy Spirit being a member of the Trinity while being of one essence. The qualities possessed are unique to persons and includes the will of the father, intelligence, emotions and agency. There may not always be a clear distinction between the Spirit and the other persons as we have learned from previous chapters since the Father controls the Son and the Son the Holy Spirit but they are always going to be three persons with one essence. The father will always hold the authoritative power over the son and the holy spirit while being unchangeable but with divine attributes.', 9, '2023-06-21 12:22:41', '2023-07-03 19:34:16', NULL),
(2969, 809, 883, 'The Bible ascribes personal attributes to the Holy Spirit as being too many to list, however we will list a few for demonstration. To being the holy spirit does indeed have a will. This is the vehicle used to plan, to desire and to choose. If this were possible, it can not be of a simple force of power, rather of a divine nature that is able to have the ability to have a will and act upon it. If the holy spirit has a will and wishes to impart on specific people, specific gifts than the spirit must indeed be a person. Forces that are impersonal can not have plans or desires and fulfill  such actions. The holy spirit also has the personal attribute of intelligence. This intelligence is revealed in more than one ways, for example the searching and knowing the mind of God is known through this attribute. He also gives wisdom and knowledge to others. Forces that are impersonal are not able to do this and can not think, know or teach. \r\n\r\nThese attributes demonstrate his personhood by acknowledging first and foremost that the Holy Spirit is the third person of the trinity. Not the third god or third spirit, but the third person of the Trinity. The Trinity is composed of three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The responsibility that each person holds is different from one another and each has their own authority and toles. The spirit is subject to the higher authority of the Father and the Son. Which in return means that the Father has dominion and power over the other two, although they are one essence. The mission of the spirit is to carry out the will of the father, and as we can see in the Scripture, to carry out the instruction of the Father and to glorify the Father and the Son.', 9, '2023-06-21 12:31:42', '2023-07-03 19:34:16', NULL),
(2970, 809, 884, 'The Holy Spirit is distinct from the Father and the Son as a separate person within the Trinity as there are three persons part of the Trinity. While the Trinity is composed of one essence, there is indeed three persons part of the Trinity with clear responsibilities, authority and roles for each person. The holy spirit is a subject to a higher authority to the Father and to the Son. The primary role of the spirit is to carry out instructions and glorify the Father and the Son. This is why the holy spirit is distinct from the Father and the Son as a separate person within the Trinity. The holy spirit is called the \'third person\' of the Trinity. \r\n\r\nThe scripture clearly highlights the distinction of the Spirit and the other persons of the Trinity that shows their interaction with one another in ways that emphasize their difference. The Scripture points to how the Father would send the Son first and the Holy Spirit, with each person they had an appointed role. The spirit would not begin the work appointed until the son had completed his work on earth and ascended into heaven. This makes it clear that the spirit is  separate from the son and a member of the trinity. The spirit is also our defense attorney, pleading our cases before God when we intercede with the Father. When Paul taught us about the holy spirit, he also mentioned the trinity and how  there is one person that is interceding while the others are hearing the intercession.', 8, '2023-06-21 12:37:26', '2023-07-03 19:34:16', NULL),
(2971, 809, 885, 'The Holy Spirit\'s relationship to the other two persons of the Trinity have been described from two different perspectives. The ontological and the economic trinity perspective have both mentioned the perspectives on the same trinity - the father, the son, and the holy spirit. When referring to the ontological trinity, the reader is focused on the existence of God, the Father. The holy spirit is equal in power and glory to the father and the son in this perspective. All of the three persons in this belief are infinite, eternal and not changeable. The have the same and divine attributes as wisdom, power and holiness. When referring to the economic trinity, you consider how the three persons interact with one another. The trinity here is described as having three different roles and assignments. The highest authority is granted to the Father and then the Son and then the holy spirit is subject to the higher two. The role of the spirit is to carry out the will of the Father and the instructions that are set forth in order to bring glory. \r\n\r\nThe reason the holy spirit is the third person of the trinity is because he is breathed out by the father, who is the first person, and by the son who is the second person. There have been claims in other churches during the early days that it was only the father that breathed out the holy spirit, however since the creed was created before this controversy, it did not address these claims. The holy spirit does hold the lowest rank from the perspective of the economic trinity, however it is crucial to note that the he is still fully God and has complete authority over every aspect of creation. There is also a mutual deference, what one person does, they all do. They are alike and agree on everything in nature, the lower rank of the holy spirit or subordination to the Father and Son does not mean he is inferior to them, he is not, the spirit is equal to them both.', 8, '2023-06-21 12:45:02', '2023-07-03 19:34:16', NULL),
(2972, 810, 886, 'The church in the New Testament finds its roots in the Old Testament several different times. In the New Testament, the church is spoken of by using the Greek word ekklesia. However, this term was derived from the Greek translation of the Old Testament. It was commonly used to describe the gathering of the nation of Israel. Even while the the term ekklesia may be used to refer to the church, the word is originally found to refer to the gathering and assembly of the people of Israel. While there may be many differences in translation, the church does not represent a physical building or structure, rather a congregation of people here on earth. \r\n\r\nThe church is established for a purpose and has been granted authority to establish and maintain our relationship with God. The church is equivalent to God\'s kingdom on Earth through which he dispenses his grace. We can see that within the New Testament, Peter refers to the church by names that were applied to Israel in the Old Testament hence finding and connecting its roots back to the Old Testament. The church was identified as a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, and a people belonging to God, 1 Peter 2:9. One of the key differences to note between the Old Testament church and gathering against the New Testament church was that the Old Testament church was commonly depicted as the root of the tree. The strong foundation and level of the church was the Old Testament, and the New Testament was considered to the be branches of the same tree. This illustration was was identified in several portions of the Scripture and the Old Testament was the bulk of the tree consisting of primarily Jews, while the Gentile Christians were the branches of the tree.', 8, '2023-06-26 13:10:25', '2023-07-03 19:34:49', NULL),
(2973, 810, 887, 'Jesus built the church in a way that relied on but also advanced the Old Testament church. Within the New Testament there are three occasions where Jesus mentioned that he will build the church referring to the word oikodomeo, meaning build. We can also learn from the teachings of Paul that Jesus was rebuilding and restoring the church of the Old Testament. Jesus goes into details within his teachings and speaks of different attributes that the church should adhere to. For example, on one occasion, Jesus refers to the people of the church and their responsiveness. In that text, Jesus is talking about church discipline. In Mathew18:17, \"If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the church; and if he refuses to listen even to the church, treat him as you would a pagan or a tax collector. \r\n\r\nThe verse mentioned in Mathew was referring to the guidance that a sinner or person  who has not repented should come before the church. The utmost fundamentals between the Old Testament church and the New Testament church remain the same. The purpose, which is to unite and turn the world into God\'s earthly kingdom. That is the first and foremost purpose of the church, to unite mankind and rather than proclaim a structure to be a church, gather amongst one another and assemble. This should include both believers and unbelievers. That is the verse mentioned above from Mathew, to be able to guide a sinner or unrepented person the church. \r\n\r\nThe church is advanced throughout the Old Testament listed by Jesus and the apostles to fulfill the fundamental obligations of loving God, spreading and advancing the kingdom, and bringing him glory. Jesus is the found of the new Testament church and he is the one bringing restoration and renewal transforming the church of the Old Testament that was failing. The passages in Ephesians 5:22-23 describes Jesus as being the husband of the church.', 8, '2023-06-26 13:20:16', '2023-07-03 19:34:49', NULL),
(2974, 810, 888, 'Some of the implications of the relationship between the Old Testament and the New Testament church is that Jesus ordained the New Testament church as the restoration and development of the Old Testament church. We can see the continuum of the gathering of the people of Israel and the church described in the New Testament. There are several fundamental connections between the Old Testament and New Testament churches and they all are driven with the same purpose, group of  people and obligations.\r\n\r\n To begin with the purpose, the purpose has never changed between the church of the Old Testament and the New Testament. The history of the world can be summed up in three stages, between the creation, the fall and redemption. Beginning with the creation of the world, man and animals by God. He formed man and the beautiful garden of Eden, and following the creation, there was a mandate or ordinance to follow, and through the failure to abide by this, God has has now created the opportunity for man to redeem itself through redemption and restoration. \r\n\r\n The goal of the gathering of Israel and the New Testament church remains the same and continues to be that their purpose is to turn the world into God\'s earthly kingdom. Simply put, the church has a moral purpose to convert the world and the people of this world to be a replica of the kingdom of God. The group of people gathering can also be identified within the implications of the Old Testament and New Testament church, the gathering of the assemblies should included both believers and unbelievers. \r\n\r\nWe can clearly see this portrayed within the New Testament throughout several parables and teachings of Jesus as to how no one should be separated or go astray, and if one were to, then the it is our responsibility to bring them to Christ. Lastly, the obligations of the Old Testament and New Testament church are similar in nature. To love God, to spread and advance his kingdom, and to bring him glory.', 8, '2023-06-26 13:29:38', '2023-07-03 19:34:49', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(2975, 810, 889, 'The word catholic means, universal or including all Christians in all congregations. The reference is not to the Roman Catholic church, rather it is a description of unity that exists between all of the churches that follow Christ faithfully. When we speak of the Apostles\' Creed and how it was understood within the creed, it is important to note that there is no organization of all Christian gatherings mentioned. Rather, the creed is referring to the unity of the Holy Spirit and how it should exist between all Christian churches, despite any organizational difference. The term catholic is meant to be inclusive and include  all Christians in all congregations. \r\n\r\nThe term catholic is derived from the Latin word, catholicus which means whole or complete. Meanwhile the latter churches were fragmented by divisions and strife, reversing the original intention and causing them to be exclusive. However the creed has the original intention of the unity of the Holy spirit among the churches and continue the covenant with God and under the headship of Christ. In 1 Corinthians 1:2, Paul wrote in his teachings, \"To the church of God in Corinth, to those sanctified in Christ Jesus and called to be holy, together with all those everywhere who call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ - their Lord and ours.\" As it is mentioned, called to be holy and together in the name of our Lord and theirs can be a clear portrayal of how the church is to be viewed as universal. The creed refers to this as the catholicity of the church, where it does not have in mind a organization of ALL Christian gatherings, rather the unification of the Holy Spirit acknowledging the purpose to be holy and just advancing the kingdom of God among all churches.', 8, '2023-06-26 13:41:01', '2023-07-03 19:34:49', NULL),
(2976, 810, 890, 'The visible catholic church is defined as one worldwide fellowship of all people in covenant with God under the headship of Christ. The catholic church has distinct markers including the word, sacraments, and discipline. The means of grace considered as the tools or mechanisms that God would ordinarily use to apply grace to his people are measured through these three areas, word, sacraments and prayers. The visible catholic church also believes in spiritual gifts for the purpose of building up the church through the Holy Spirit. These spiritual gifts are shared by the entirety of the church through public worship services, sign for the unbelievers, building up the entire church and believing that unbelievers are condemned. The visible catholic church also believes in the sharing of material goods with one another who were in need during the early church and within the Bible. \r\n\r\nTo decide which churches should be embraced as part of the catholic church or the universal church should be grounded within the word of God. There is no church, claim, interpretation or proclamation that has the exclusive right to interpret and teach the Scripture. God has given his word to the whole visible church and he has given the Holy Spirit to help us understand the Bible. \r\n\r\nThis is a common problem faced today, to know which church should a believer embrace, the catholic church or the universal church of Christ. Either one churches opens their arms too wide and include any church that claims to be a Christian or the other is too exclusive and only accept narrowly defined congregations of denominations. The sacraments to administer and conduct baptism is given to the entire church, not just one denomination or another. Lastly, discipline is granted to all churches and not exclusive to a single denomination or congregation.', 8, '2023-06-26 13:53:02', '2023-07-03 19:34:49', NULL),
(2977, 811, 891, 'The problem of sin is that it takes us away from the blessings of God and places us under his curse. We are not able to overcome this problem on our own, and that is why Jesus had to die. He had to die in order to solve the problem created by our sin. Sin condemns us, we are unable to save ourselves through our own strength or might. Eventually we must suffer the consequences of the sin unless we believe in Jesus and are redeemed. Sin originated with the creation of the human race, and their eating of the of the forbidden fruit of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. When God had created humankind, we were very good, however man had the ability and the opportunity to sin. \r\n\r\nAt any point in time that a human being chooses to think, speak or act in anyway that differs from God\'s will and violates his law, we sin. We are choosing evil over good and there are dire consequences with sin. With the action of sin committed by both Adam and Eve, God judged and cursed the entire human race. That curse remains until this very day as a result of the corruption in our beings both body and soul. Ultimately the curse continues until death, the curse began as a result of the sin that entered the world through one man and through that sin, all of humankind were born as sinners. \r\n\r\nThe main consequence associated with sin is the physical death we all know along with the spiritual death. That is the eternal separation from God and the Scripture teaches us that sin can cause God to hide his face from you so that he will not hear you. Creating that separation is no small fate, it is indeed  the factor that can cause someone to suffer in hell as a result of their separation from God.', 8, '2023-06-28 12:12:21', '2023-07-03 19:35:23', NULL),
(2978, 811, 892, 'Divine grace involves all three persons of the Trinity by first pointing out that God did not want all of the human race to remain under the curse of sin. He felt that humankind was sill worthy of this presence and planned to send a Redeemer to solve the awful problem of sin. The Redeemer is none other than Jesus Christ, the son of God. Forgiveness starts with the one who has the highest authority, our father, our heavenly father. The only way forgiveness came about was because the Father allowed it and planned for it come. The father is also the one who gave authority and empowered Jesus to come as his son to redeem his people. \r\n\r\nThe Son, our redeemer, sent by the Father was to fulfill the Father\'s will and promise, which was to come into this world as a man and die for the sin of humankind. The curse that mankind brought upon themselves, death was taken up by the Son and credited to our own account. He was obedient to the Father and as the Son was punished for our sins. The father had to architect the entire plan and delegate the son to perform the will of the father. \r\n\r\nThe Holy Spirit is the third person of the Trinity and is the one applying forgiveness in our lives. The Father was the one who orchestrated and laid out the plans, the Son was able to die for our sins and put the plan into motion, and the Holy Spirit is able to do his work and applies forgiveness in our lives.', 7, '2023-06-28 12:19:41', '2023-07-03 19:35:23', NULL),
(2979, 811, 893, 'Individual responsibility plays a role in the forgiveness of sins by two factors. The first is having the fear of the Lord which can receive his forgiveness. The Bible teaches us that God does not punish us according to our wrong doings and mistakes,  however we must acknowledge that the Lord is slow to anger, compassionate and gracious. He has the ability and authority to not treat us according to our sins. However, with that concept comes the fear of the Lord. If you set your hearts towards seeking him and seeing the true meaning of repentance and forgiveness, God is able to forgive. In a spirit of true brokenness, and repentance, we must be able to express the depth of our sorrow and regret to the king who is the only one able to forgive. \r\n\r\nWe must strive to have faith in God, knowing that he is divine and acknowledging his grace. He is able to show mercy and forgiveness to those who fear him. When we do ask for forgiveness of sins, individual responsibility also means to show our brokenness. Our genuine remorse and guilt over the sin we have committed breaking God\'s law. Forgiveness can be granted through prayer, asking God for forgiveness. Meditate, seek God\'s face, pray or petition on behalf of one another in your individual responsibility. These are the methods to seek forgiveness of one\'s sins, rather than mistaking grace as a lifeline, we must understand that the basis is always the merit of Christ. We are able to  inherit this grace through his obedient life and sacrificial death on the cross for us. We are able to earn this forgiveness through this manner.', 7, '2023-06-28 12:29:06', '2023-07-03 19:35:23', NULL),
(2980, 811, 894, 'Man\'s fall into sin corrupted not only our souls but also our physical bodies as well. Human beings consist of two main parts, one being the human body, and the second being the soul. In Genesis 3:19, God placed a curse on mankind as a result of the sin committed by Adam and Eve, \"By the sweat of your brow you will eat your food until you return to the ground, since from it you were taken; for dust you are and to the dust you will return.\" As a result of the sin of Adam and Eve, God cursed them to be mortal. Until then, they were not supposed to die. However, now the Bible shows us that God declared that they would eventually die and return to dust. This is a clear result of how man\'s fall into sin corrupted not only their souls, but their physical bodies too. \r\n\r\nGod did not create us to die, that was never his intention. Our salvation will never be complete, until Christ returns and redeems our bodies. Death is inevitable, it is a universal human experience that everyone must face at one point in time or another. However, the way to view death is that we are taken directly into the presence of Christ.\r\n\r\nAs all human beings descend from Adam and Eve, all human kind were born with sin and corruption, and continue to carry that curse. The corruption of sin results in physical hardship, sickness and eventually death. In Psalm 90:10, the Bible refers to how even the greatest of days are filled with trouble and sorrow, the years quickly pass and we are gone. The wonderful promise that we get to hold onto though is that God is our salvation and Jesus died for ours sins. This allows for both our souls and bodies to be redeemed.', 8, '2023-06-28 12:37:26', '2023-07-03 19:35:23', NULL),
(2981, 811, 895, 'The three stages of bodily redemption are composed of the things believers experience during our current life on earth, the second is the intermediate state of our bodies that begins with physical death, and the last is the new life of resurrection which begins when Christ returns. \r\n\r\nAccording to the Scripture we will experience these three stages quite differently. To begin with the the current life on earth, we refer to redemption on earth with the belief that once we believe in Christ, we are sanctified, or set apart for God and purified. He is able to continue to sanctify us and use our bodies in ways that honor him, resulting in us giving our bodies to God as a living sacrifice. This is experienced as a result of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit\'s indwelling presence throughout our lives allows our bodies to continue to benefit and live a life pleasing to the Lord. \r\n\r\nThe second one we experience is the redemption of our bodies in the present life. The process of redemption continues even after we die, when are bodies are temporarily separated from our souls. This is the stage between life on earth and the lives we will have in resurrection, during this period is when our souls dwell with Christ in heaven. This is revealed in the Scripture in Matthew 17:3 and 2 Corinthians 5:6-8. Death frees the body from sin, while the body may be returning to dust and decaying, our bodies are in a state where they are incapable of any thought, action, or feeling whether it may be good or bad. Whether our bodies are buried or cremated, our bodies continue to  be a part of us. \r\n\r\nThe last experience is the new life of resurrection that begins when Christ returns. With the resurrection comes the relief that the consequences of sin will finally and forever be completely put away from us. Our bodies will receive a new and perfect life which allows for a glorious life that allows for perfected human beings. Just as Jesus body had gone through changes in resurrection, our bodies will also be changed and we will finally become the people that God had always intended for us to be.', 9, '2023-06-28 12:49:51', '2023-07-03 19:35:23', NULL),
(2982, 812, 761, 'During the week of creation, one of the things that is taught by Scripture is that when God, the Father was creating the world, God the Son was beside the Father. The Scripture also reveals that the Father created the world through his Son. The role of God the Father was that once the world was created, all of dominium and ownership would be given to Son to inherit and rule over it\'s entire creation. \r\n\r\nIn the book of Colossians, 1:6 we read, \"For by him all things were created: things in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, all things were created by him and for him.\" Therefore the passage implies that everything that God the Father had created was indeed for God the Son and created with the Son. The Scripture is very clear on the fact that God the Father was the overall designer of the world. He was able to create the world through his ways of what would please him. For example, after each creation, the Scripture states that \"God saw that it was good\" and he was pleased with what he had created. So for six days, God filled a void, shapeless, world without light to become full of creation, shape, light, sea, land, animals and vegetation. \r\n\r\nDuring the first three days of creation, God the father began to create and fill the world in ways that would be pleasing to him. He formed the world, and allowed there to be light. He separated the sky from the sea and the dry land from water. During the next three days, God the created the sun, moon and stars. He assigned the sun to rule the day and the moon to rule the night. He created the fish, the birds, and all of the animals that live on Earth. \r\n\r\nAt the end of the creation week, God the Father had completed his kingdom. He then appointed humankind to govern over the Earth so that they could bring him glory. God did this with the authority of keeping the God the Son as the heir of all things that was the king of all of the earth who would inherit, and rule over the entire creation.', 9, '2023-07-05 13:16:59', '2023-08-07 16:04:29', NULL),
(2983, 812, 762, 'God\'s eternal counsel regarding Christ is that humankind has a purpose and redemption is available only through Jesus Christ. The Bible teaches us clearly the purpose that man was created in the Old Testament in the beginning of time. When God designed and Adam and then Eve to have authority over the Earth and gave them free will, they chose to dishonor and disobey God which then allowed for the infamous curse to fall upon them and all of mankind. The ultimate consequence around their sin has been passed down since then leading to death. However, God the Father allowed for his Son to die for our sins, to allow for redemption to take place. \r\n\r\nThis redemption has created a hope for humanity. God the Father promised he would send a Redeemer, who we know as his Son. The Redeemer would bring salvation to the sinner and be able to restore the world as well as bring judgement. Jesus will return only at a time that is known to his Father and will return in the same manner that he ascended into Heaven and it will be public and visible for all to see. He will return as a powerful conqueror and all those who have ever died will be resurrected and the last judgement will take place. God the Father gives us this counsel that Jesus will destroy all his enemies and allow the blessing of all of his faithful people. No human being will be exempt from judgement, and sinner will be purged and creation will be renewed. This will then allow for all of creation to be restored to the original plan of God.', 9, '2023-07-05 13:27:26', '2023-08-07 16:04:29', NULL),
(2984, 812, 763, 'The divine promises that made redemption certain can be traced back to the very beginning. If God has declared a promise, that promise remains as he does not ever change. The world may come and go, but the word of God will remain the same forever and ever. We are taught that God\'s promises are immutable and he will never break them. Regarding redemption, the divine promises that are made fall in line with the original plan of God. That man is to live on earth as it is the kingdom of heaven and the promises that are rooted between the Father and Son will come to pass. The covenant of redemption which is the promise to redeem fallen humanity will take place and secure redemption after the fall into sin. \r\n\r\nThere are several examples of promises or covenants that were made which ensure redemption is certain. The initial covenant of redemption is where the Father, Son and Holy Spirit promised to redeem fallen humanity, the covenant of Grace is the arrangement between the Father and Son and redeemed humanity. There were other examples of promises that ensured redemption such as the Adamic Covenant where redemption was offered to humanity when it was not deserved. The Noahic Covenant which promised to preserve humanity until the Son\'s redeeming work was fulfilled. The Abrahamic Covenant which promised that one of Abraham\'s descendants would be the Redeemer. \r\n\r\nThese examples provide us with the assurance that the promises made by God is eternal and unchangeable. There is no refuting any of the other examples mentioned in the Bible whether it be the Mosaic, Davidic, or New Covenant. These are all examples of how promises surrounding redemption and the covenant between God and mankind were fulfilled and continues to this day.', 8, '2023-07-05 13:36:51', '2023-08-07 16:04:29', NULL),
(2985, 812, 764, 'Jesus was able to accomplish redemption for sinners through his redeeming work by a few different examples. The first example that we can see is through his inauguration of his kingdom. The people of God yearned for the day and time when God would bring his kingdom to earth. Jesus was able to bring the kingdom of God to earth in his day. Which ultimately reveals that we as children of God are still waiting for the kingdom of God. \r\n\r\nAnother major example of how Jesus was able to accomplish redemption for sinners was through the obedience and submission to the will of the Father to step in where Adam had failed. Jesus was put through an immense period of humiliation, suffering and ultimately crucifixion and took the place of man to suffer and sacrifice on the cross for one purpose. This freed man from God\'s condemnation allowing the reward of salvation to be given to us. Through the resurrection of Christ, Jesus conquered death and secured everlasting life for his people. This is the work that Jesus had done for the redemption of sinner, he came into this world, took the place of man, died for their sins, ascended into heaven after his resurrection and promised us salvation. That salvation was paid for on the cross so that we can have eternal life.', 8, '2023-07-05 13:43:17', '2023-08-07 16:04:29', NULL),
(2986, 812, 765, 'The crucial events that Jesus will initiate at the consummation revolve around the end times. The consummation consists of the destruction of all of God\'s enemies and the ultimate blessing of all his people. This will be the complete renewal of all of creation where the children of God will dwell forever. This will be fulfillment of Gods kingdom finally coming to pass. \r\n\r\nThe first crucial event that will initiate the consummation will be the return of Jesus. During his return he will come down from heaven as he went and his return will be public and visible. All will see him and there will be a loud noise from a trumpet and the voice of an archangel. He will come back as a powerful conqueror, and it will be sudden. The timing of his return is only known to God the father.\r\n\r\nDuring Christ\'s return, all who have died will be resurrected and everyone will be given new bodies that will be endured forever. Our final bodies will be glorious, however the bodies of those who are not redeemed and non-believers will last forever and their bodies will continue to be plagued by the curse of God. \r\n\r\nThe last judgement will be exercised by Jesus overall of mankind. He will destroy all his enemies and bless all the faithful people at the last judgement. No one will be able to escape the final judgement. After the last judgement, the final major event that will take place is the renewal of creation. Jesus will judge all of humanity and purge the sinners and those who did not believe and those who do believe will inherit everlasting life in the kingdom of God.', 8, '2023-07-05 13:52:05', '2023-08-07 16:04:29', NULL),
(2987, 813, 766, 'Peter\'s apostolic confession along with the transfiguration affirms that Jesus was the Christ. Jesus would come to Earth in a unique way that could only be manifested by God the Father alone. Jesus was born to a virgin woman who remained a virgin until she had given birth to Jesus. Through the miraculous intervention of the Holy Spirit, Jesus was born as a true human. \r\n\r\nWhile Jesus was on Earth, his identity was confirmed by the teachings of the apostles through the miraculous works that he had performed that were specially anointed by God. The miracles demonstrated a strong approval of God\'s anointing and approval of everything that Jesus had said. Jesus was able to prove that the gospel message was true through miracles and that he wanted to bring the last stage of God\'s kingdom to earth. Apostolic teachings confirm in Luke 11:20, \"If I drive out demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom of God has come to you.\" The works and miracles performed by Jesus proved that he was Christ and he had come to put an end to Satan\'s rule over the people of God and his creation. \r\n\r\nAt the time of the transfiguration, Jesus was revealed in his glory to his disciples. At that time, Jesus had taken Peter, James and John up to a mountain to intercede and pray. Two men appeared to be with Jesus at that time, both Moses and Elijah. The voice of God the father was heard from heaven at that time and confirmed that Jesus was his son. This was a direct correlation to the affirmation of Jesus being Christ during the time of the transfiguration.', 8, '2023-07-07 13:10:45', '2023-08-07 16:05:25', NULL),
(2988, 813, 767, 'The purpose of Jesus\' temptation in the wilderness was to prove that although Jesus took the form of man, he was able to overcome temptation and bring salvation to his people. During this time of temptation, Jesus was fasting for forty days, which means for forty days he disconnected with the world and the required daily consumption of the world so that he connect with God. This time was a period where Jesus would be weak in his physical body, but he continued to remain powerful in both his spirit and mind. \r\n\r\nDuring this time, Satan took it upon himself to tempt Jesus and wanted to test the limits to see where Jesus would fail. However Jesus failed to give into the tricks of the devil and refused to prove himself. One of the greatest lessons to be taken from the time of temptation of Jesus was that this similar temptation occurred during the days of Adam and Eve. While God had created man and woman, the serpent came and tricked Eve into eating from the tree of knowledge and evil. Eve was not able to sustain her focus on what is pure and moral, but rather fell into the strong temptation of sin. Jesus on the other hand, was able to come into this world, born of a woman and was able to fight through the temptations known to man, which the devil brings upon us, and was able to withstand these temptations whereas Adam and Eve were not able to do. \r\n\r\nJesus had a greater plan and vision which drove the path forward to salvation for all of humanity. That was to take the sinful path of man, the failures of each test and temptation that humankind failed at, and to redeem all people, the entire human race and bring salvation to his people. Jesus was sinless and did not fall into sin, rather, he  bore the sin and became our substitute so that we did not have to perish. That is why it was necessary for him to be tempted and to show us that he was able to overcome those temptations and save us.', 8, '2023-07-07 13:18:48', '2023-08-07 16:05:25', NULL),
(2989, 813, 768, 'The crucifixion of Jesus was very unique to say, at the very least. During the days of the ancient Roman Empire, crucifixion was when victims were condemned to die, tied to a cross, or at times, nailed to the cross and required to hang on the cross until they died. Around the cross, you would find skulls, and extreme temperatures and birds of the sky looking to peck and eat these wounded flesh. \r\n\r\nThe reason that Jesus\' crucifixion was unique was because it served as an atoning offering for sin. For Jesus, it was his responsibility to die on behalf of his people. God had appointed Jesus to be the substitute on our behalf for his people and was assigned to take on the guilt of sinners. If we take a look back at the life of Adam and Eve, as a result of their sin and fall into temptation and sin, they were condemned to die as a result, and have a time frame on their on Earth. Death is considered as a judgement against sin, death was not to be there, until sin crept in and caused that sin to be passed on from generation to generation. The death of Jesus was a divine judgement against sin, where Jesus paid our ransom and sacrificed himself per the will of the Father to stand for us. \r\n\r\nHis death was unique in the way that he was blamed for our sins even though he never committed any wrong doing. His human physical form was never corrupted by sin , yet God counted Jesus as if he had personally committed each crime and sin committed by man. That is what makes his death and crucifixion so unique. A blameless and perfect man, was punished so harshly for something he did not do, and was assigned a guilty verdict to stand in place of us.', 8, '2023-07-07 13:28:11', '2023-08-07 16:05:25', NULL),
(2990, 813, 769, 'The resurrection of Jesus accomplished a few different things. The first thing that the resurrection accomplished was that Jesus Christ conquered death for everyone. When he was able to rise up from the dead, and out of the grave through the power of the Holy Spirit, he was able to prove to all of humanity that he is who he said he was and that he was the true Son of God. This also ensured another thing, which was taught by Jesus several times, the final resurrection to believers and his faithful followers. \r\n\r\nThe Scripture teaches us in many different passages about redemption and the promises of God through salvation. When Jesus was resurrected, we can see that he was able to redeem all of humanity and that nothing else would be able to save them. He created the manifestation of his promise of eternal life after death if you believe in him. Another thing we learn from the resurrection of Jesus is that he was able to show us that we are forgiven as he bore the shame and torture for us. He was able to take on human form, live on this earth and be persecuted and tortured for something he didn\'t do. This resulted in his death and eventually his resurrection, which shows us that he took on that guilt and bore that punishment for our salvation so that we can be forgiven for our wrongdoings. \r\n\r\nThe plan of resurrection was to fulfill the promise of God, which was to establish his kingdom on Earth and redeem his people. As a part of this blessing of salvation, and through his resurrection, we received an eternal inheritance, justification, regeneration, and salvation for believers.', 8, '2023-07-07 13:34:53', '2023-08-07 16:05:25', NULL),
(2991, 813, 770, 'I am able to affirm without a doubt and confess that Jesus is the Christ in my present circumstances and areas of influence clearly by admitting that I have no power, authority or dominion over anything in my life. Looking back to specific instances in my life, I was in places of high distinction and blessings of many sorts, but through the highs and lows, and through many examples, I was able to lose practically everything in my life. Only after complete surrender and proclaiming that Jesus has complete control over my life, did I see the hand of God piecing everything back together again. \r\n\r\nThere are many real life examples within my life, where I have sinned, and I have fallen short and I have missed the mark. There are also examples of where I have tried my level best as a human being to achieve certain things, but I have learned through personal failures and struggles that without Jesus being at the center of my life, I am not able to  take a single step in the right direction. Jesus has complete authority over my life, whether it be my family, marriage, career, ministry, without the hand of God over my life and these areas, I fail. I have tasted it and witnessed it with a front row seat. There are no good works and words that can save me and give me any satisfaction, but knowing that I walk with God and he has me in the palm of his hand, gives me the relief that there is nothing that can\'t be done without him. \r\n\r\nHe has literally pieced my life back together again, stitched it so beautifully and allowed for my blessings to multiply and shut the mouth of the devil that tried to destroy me. There was  time when I was not able to stand against the blows that the devil brought against me, and through each blow, I realized at the end that Jesus bore my sins on the cross and fought the battle for me. I don\'t need to fight this battle, I need to surrender and give it to God. When I did that is when I realized who God was and is in my life. And in all areas of my life and areas of influence, I was able to see the work and hand of God in my life. He was able to do things that not I or any human could do, and as a result of what he did, my testimony continues to be a beacon of hope for many, that God lives and he is still in the business of redemption.', 8, '2023-07-07 13:42:45', '2023-08-07 16:05:25', NULL),
(2992, 828, 761, 'In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God and the Word was God.  The Word was God and that means since the beginning, before the universe was created. The Son Jesus Christ is the radiance of God\'s glory and the exact representation of his being,sustaining all things by his powerful word. In Colossians 1:16, the writer mentions that for by him all things were created, everything visible and invisible, all things created by him and for him.  God formed and gave shape to the world through Logos which is the power of his word.  God created man in his own image male and female to be fruiful and subdue the earth.  God the Son was active in creation in that he was the heir of all things.  The purpose of creation is to serve as Gods special kingdom.  Trinity thus has a significant role in all creation, because everything was void until God spoke into it. One of the known facts of our Christian faith is Jesus not only redeems us, but he is also more the Creator and the Redeemer.  The whole universe we can consider governed by the Logos of God.  Every created thing is obligated to submit obediently  to God as Son as its King.  The heart of Jesus goes to all of Creation.', 8, '2023-08-30 21:44:26', '2023-09-12 14:12:24', NULL),
(2993, 828, 762, 'Jesus Christ is a distinct person of the Trinity until the New Testament.  Scripture teaches that Jesus has been God from all of eternity.  Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today and forever.  Jesus\' divine nature is true in various ways.  God knew everything was going to happen before it did. When Jesus comes back he wil reign as king over the kingdom of God and bring it reverance.  This refers to God\'s eternal plan for the universe which happened before creation started.  There are various theological beliefs about the nature and extent of God\'s plan.  Some think that God\'s eternal plan are meticulous with every historical fact and others say God fixed certain things and not others.  We all believe one thing that what Christ accomplished is central to God\'s plan, we will all have salvation through Jesus Christ.  Ephesian 1:4 and 11 says God chose us in Christ before the creation of the world to be holy and blameless in his sight, in him we were also chosen, having been predestined according to the plan of him who works out everything in conformity with the purpose of his will. God created the universe in order to display his increasing glory through us his creation. Some theologians have found it helpful to describe Gods eternal decree regarding his glorious kingdom as a covenant of redemption.', 8, '2023-08-30 21:59:40', '2023-09-12 14:12:24', NULL),
(2994, 828, 763, 'God\'s promises are immutable which will never change.  All of fallen humanity would be redeemed through each individual of the Trinity.  The covenant of grace was an agreement that God the Father on one hand, and the Son and the redemption of humanity on the other hand.  In this covenantial agreement, God the Father vowed to ensure his kingdom plans for creation through Jesus his Son.  In Genesis chapter 2 we read how God had vowed to kill humanity if they ate the forbidden fruit, but God showed his mercy. God did something even greater by promising a Redeemer which many call the first gospel and it was found in the curse God gave the snake after Adam and Eve had sinned.  God made a promise that one day the generation of the woman would be able to rescue all of humanity. The Redeemer was sent to crush the Serpent\'s head was to come to the rescue of all of humankind from sin that was committed.  The Redeemer came to restore a good and right relationship with God the Father.', 7, '2023-08-30 22:13:18', '2023-09-12 14:12:24', NULL),
(2995, 828, 764, 'John 3:16-18 states this that God so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him shall not perish but have eternal life.  For God did not send his Son into the world to condemn the world, but to save the world through him.  Whoever believes in him is not condemned, but whoever does not believe stands condememned already because he has not believed in the name of God\'s one and only Son.  Jesus died on the cross of Calvary for my sins, and he conquered death and the enemy so that I wouldnt have to face that.  Jesus motive was for creation to fulfill Gods purpose.  Through what Jesus did on the cross and his resurrection, he was establishing the kingdom of God, showing his love to the Father in obedience.  The Bible says that we were hopeless, without direction, unable to solve the problem of sin and so God the Son becomes a man, lives out a life of complete submission to God.  Jesus represents the resurrected redeemed life.  Jesus represents the new man, the new Adam which has been redeemed from its sinful ways.  The ascension of Jesus allowed him to be our mediator in heaven.  We have salvation in no one else other than Christ Jesus!', 9, '2023-08-30 22:24:59', '2023-09-12 14:12:24', NULL),
(2996, 828, 765, 'The consuming of the heavens and the earth is about Jesus second coming and the final stage of salvation.  In this time all of God\'s enemies will be destroyed, it will be where there is a renewal of creation.   This is when the world will finally be God\'s glorious kingdom.  Jesus\'s first appearance back showed great humility.  1 Thesalonians 4:16 states that the Lord himself will come down from heaven, with a loud command with the voice of the archangel and with the trumpet call of God.  This scripture indicates how Jesus will return, it will be clearly evident and he will return in the same manner that he left.  The second coming will be victorious.  The scripture also says that his return will be imminent, it will never happen when we think it will.  At Jesus\' return, everyone that died will be resurrected, he is going to tranform our lowly bodies into his glorious body which Philippians 3:20-21 states.  Judgement will also happen immediately after the resurrection, every person alive will be included in the last judgement. When Jesus comes back he will reward our devotion to him, all those who have been faithful will receive the new heavens and earth and we will be able to enjoy God\'s presence in a even better way than what Adam and Eve had in Eden.', 9, '2023-08-30 22:38:27', '2023-09-12 14:12:24', NULL),
(2997, 829, 766, 'When all the disciples were asked who Jesus was, some of them said Elijah, some said Moses, but Peter was the only one that confirmed he was the messiah, the Son of God.  This was clearly given to Apostle Peter through the Holy Spirit.  The transfiguration was the revealing of the glory of God through Jesus Christ, where Christ was so bright.  God mentioned at the transfiguration that Jesus was completing the guidelines set by people of leadership in the past of Israel.  The voice of God on this mountain of transfiguration confirmed that Jesus was the second part of the Trinity.', 4, '2023-08-31 21:41:21', '2023-09-12 14:13:53', NULL),
(2998, 829, 767, 'The purpose of Jesus\' temptation was to overcome the enemy.  We see that Jesus was fasting for 40 days, he was famished and this is when the Holy Spirit takes him to the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.  Jesus was frail at this time, very vulnerable, he could have gave in to the enemy but he overcame through the word of God orating to the devil.  This proves that Jesus was truly human as well as divine, he had the same tendencies that we as humans have today.  He proved that we can overcome temptation unlike Adam and Eve in the Garden of Eden. Jesus gave us the example on how to overcome temptation by never giving in to the devil.', 5, '2023-08-31 21:46:06', '2023-09-12 14:13:53', NULL),
(2999, 829, 768, 'Crucifixion was the ultimate penalty given in Roman times.  The wooden cross was used as well as nails to put criminals to a gruesome death.  The uniqueness of the crucifixon of Jesus is that he took the place of my sins, he was sinless but paid the ultimate price for me.  Jesus paid it all on the cross, to him I owe, sin left a crimson stain on him, in essence he paid the ultimate sacrifice so never again will any animals have to be slaughtered for my shortcomings as they used to do in the Old Testament times.   His death shows us that he took our judgement on his shoulders!', 2, '2023-08-31 21:52:58', '2023-09-12 14:13:53', NULL),
(3000, 829, 769, 'Jesus resurrection from the dead crushed the enemy\'s head.  The devil thought the grave could hold Jesus, but it couldnt, this was God the Father\'s plan all along.  God desired for all of creation to redeemed, his promises wouldnt come true unless Jesus was raised from the dead.  Jesus\'s resurrection also shows us as Christians what we have to look forward to at the second coming which is the glorious body promises us.  We as Christians no longer have to fear death because Christ conquered it and we have a hope that when the roll is called up, I\'ll be there in eternity with my Lord!', 3, '2023-08-31 22:00:17', '2023-09-12 14:13:53', NULL),
(3001, 829, 770, 'I affirm that Jesus Christ is in my life by always maintaining a good relationship with him daily.  I am able to walk differently since I know that Christ is in control of my life in every aspect of my life.  In my career, I am able to display the love of God through my words, my deeds and just displaying Gods glory in everything that I do.  I can express Christ via social media platforms just as facebook and instagram which I am currently doing to encourage those around me.  Even in bad times or good times, I am able to have a smile on my face and be positive that God is omniscient, omnipotent, and is sovereign in every circumstance of my life.  In ministry as a minister to students, I am able to be a role model with my wife by being an example in behaviors and actions as well as being present for all those that need me around me.  I affirm that Jesus is my Lord and Savior every morning that I get up, by devoting everything I have for his kingdom cause. My wife and I are able to have small groups weekly with young couples where we encourage each other, we build each other up, have fellowship and in the end pray for each others needs and this is how we are able to influence others.', 8, '2023-08-31 22:08:38', '2023-09-12 14:13:53', NULL),
(3002, 830, 776, 'Many believe that Paul was not the author of Hebrews because he never introduces himself in the book.  Many theologians today reject that Paul was the author because again Paul disliked plagiarism and would make sure that in each of the epistles to introduce himself.  In the Epistles that Apostle Paul wrote he does not refer to Old Testament very much but the writer of Hebrews is different and mentions the tabernacle as well as the High Priest.  The writer has a different way of words that relate to a Greek upbringing which is quite different from the letters that Paul wrote.', 5, '2023-09-05 22:08:54', '2023-09-12 14:18:24', NULL),
(3003, 830, 777, 'There are five characteristics of the original audience of Hebrews.  The author of Hebrews is probably talking to a Jewish crowd due to mentioning generations past and how God spoke through various men of God.  In the first verse of Hebrews it refers to forefathers and a revelation. The 2nd is that it may have been written to a group outside of Palestine which were sound in doctrine. In chapter 2 of Hebrews the writer mentions many who heard from Christ directly and passed it on to future generations.  Third the audience needed to be taught and was probably ignorant as well as childish based on the way it was written.  Fourth we can see that the audience may have been under constant attacks by political leadership at the time.  The writer mentions sufferings and not to throw away their faith in Chapter 10.  The fifth and main thing the author is pointing out is that the problems the church faced was enabling them to turn away from Jesus.  The writer supports the 5th characteristic by pointing out that they were intentionally sinning even after knowing what is right, judgement would be coming.', 7, '2023-09-05 22:22:20', '2023-09-12 14:18:24', NULL),
(3004, 830, 778, 'The Book of Hebrews is a huge sermon to the church and its approach was to persuade dramatically.  The writer is presenting his side like a debate and doing his best to convice the readers to sway to what he is preaching. He mentions the supremacy of Jesus as it relates to the Old Testament priest in Melchizidek. There are many analogies between Christ and the High Priest in the Old Testament and his defense is that Jesus is the ultimate High Priest. The author shows many pictures by mentioning the hall of faith in chapter of 11 of great men and women of God that deserved to stand out for their faith.  The writer is mentionining Old Testament law and how people were punished than and comparing that to of rejecting Christ and making the reader understand how essential it is to acknowledge Jesus as the High Priest.', 7, '2023-09-05 22:32:42', '2023-09-12 14:18:24', NULL),
(3005, 830, 779, 'Some areas in my spritual life where I need to work on is keeping a check on spritual arrogance.  I always have to ensure that I stay humble in every aspect of my spritual walk.  Another area that I need to work on maturing my spiritual walk is respecting my spiritual leadership and being receptive to be shaped under their tutorship.  Just like with Hebrews, I need to be willing to be taught, being open to correction by my spiritual shepherd is something that I am working on and have learned a lot over the last decade.  Another area in my life that need maturity on is being able to forgive completely like Jesus did and not holding any grudges.  These are areas I am constantly in prayer about as well as asking God to give me grace to overcome to be very effective in ministry.', 8, '2023-09-05 22:38:29', '2023-09-12 14:18:24', NULL),
(3006, 830, 780, 'The false teachings that I may face is that once I accept Christ as my Savior there will be no more challenges.  This is a false teaching that prosperity comes after making a decision to follow Christ.  Another false teaching that I face is believing that once saved is always saved and taking scripture out of context when it comes to not having any condemnation for being in Christ.  The way that I constantly battle these false doctrines is by continuing to read the word of God daily, reading books from theological scholars, as well as having a personal relationship with Jesus on a daily basis.  My strategy has always been to be a living testimony, despite many hardships physically, financially and emotionally to keep on pressing on towards the ultimate goal of spending an eternity with Christ Jesus.  The Holy Spirit gives us discernment, I pray daily that it helps me discern false teaching from right teachings.  One other false teaching that I faced growing up especially with my parents was seeking out prophets, but I know that God doesnt have to speak through a prophet, he can speak directly to me through my one on one relationship.', 8, '2023-09-05 22:47:33', '2023-09-12 14:18:24', NULL),
(3007, 831, 781, 'The author of Hebrews refers to Old Testament scripture over a hundred times.  He mentions how Moses led the Israelites tried to bring them closer to God but each time kept falling away from the grace of God.  The Old Testament reference to Mount Sinai was mentioned on how Israel was afraid of God and how this is in relation to what we as New Testament believers have to look forward to in eternity. Hebrews refers to the covenant God made with Abraham, it also refers to God as a burning fire that can devour you. The writer compares the High Priest Melchizedek to Jesus Christ in that he died and paid the ultimate price for each of our sins.  The writer of Hebrews mentions so many in the Old Testament in chapter 11 as those who stood for the faith and persevered.', 7, '2023-09-06 22:10:27', '2023-09-12 14:19:13', NULL),
(3008, 831, 782, 'The author of Hebrews states that Jesus is above the angels because he is the only begotten son of God.  The author states that in the Old Testament angels appeared to various people and if they were not obedient to the angels that they would receive punishment.  So in essence we are unable to avoid the final verdict from God if we continue to ignore who Christ is and that through him we have redemption of our sins.  In chapter 2 the writer mentions that Jesus is over all of the angels.  The author specifically mentions that we as the human race has been set just below angelic beings.  The author is very clear about the genealogy of Jesus is through Abraham and David.  Jesus\'s human tendencies allowed him to become the High Priest that paid for my sins on the cross of Calvary.', 7, '2023-09-06 22:20:01', '2023-09-12 14:19:13', NULL),
(3009, 831, 783, 'The new covenant is superior to the old because it will never be destroyed.  The covenant that Moses had in the Old Testament was established by the tribe of Levi based on the law given by God.  The priests of the Old Testament failed because Israel kept on sinning.  The new covenant is based on the remission of sins by the ultimate sacrifice of Jesus Christ on the cross.  The Old Testament priest had to continue to offer up animals as sacrifices where as in the new covenant Jesus completed the work of priesthood by laying down his life as a ransom for all.  Jesus became sin so that we would be wiped of it clean, he ultimately became the High Priest for us thus establishing the New Covenant.', 7, '2023-09-06 22:27:33', '2023-09-12 14:19:13', NULL),
(3010, 831, 784, 'I love the Old Testament scripture and do not take it lightly.  There are many promises in the Old Testament and many patriarchs of the faith that I look up to on a daily basis to always encourage me.  Hebrews 11 mentions many of these great men and women of God that persevered in the midst of suffering and trials.  I can see how hard it was for Abraham to be obedient to God, also see and crave the intimacy that Abraham and Moses had with God the Father and I pray daily that God would speak to me like he did with them in the Old Testament. I believe hearing from God on a daily basis is critical just as Moses and Abraham stood in the gap and God couldn\'t keep his judgement hidden from them.  Without faith it is impossible to please God, I have to believe that God is a rewarder of anyone that seeks him diligently.  My faith in Jesus is the only way that I am able to overcome any obstacle that comes in my way and I can learn from David, despite his constant sins, he repented and he was ultimately a man after Gods own heart.', 8, '2023-09-06 22:34:03', '2023-09-12 14:19:13', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3011, 831, 785, 'The way I am able to communicate the sufficiency of Christ is by being a role model in every aspect of my life.  I have to be joyful in all circumstances, I have to display the fruit of the Spirit in my life which is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, longsuffering, these have to be evident in my life.  I have to be able to be vulnerable in that I was once lost in my sins, but when Christ came into my life a drastic change occurred.  I have to be willing to share my personal experiences of where God led me from and where I am today because of being content in Christ Jesus.  I have to be mindful of my social media, I have to be mindful of who I represent in every area of my life, in my career, in my ministry, this is how others can see Christ evident in my life. I am able to show the sufficiency of Christ by being there for those who are in need, sharing a meal, providing clothing, ultimately for me to be the hands and feet of Jesus Christ!  I can always let others know of how my old nature was and how I am a new creation in Jesus Christ and the impact it has made in my life.', 8, '2023-09-06 22:43:24', '2023-09-12 14:19:13', NULL),
(3012, 836, 866, 'Apostles Creed is a statement of Christian belief that is used by churches both Catholic and Protestant. It reflects traditions that were affirmed officially by the entire church. Although its roots are much earlier, in its present form it dates to about eighth century.    The earliest  written from this creed is found in a letter that Ancyra wrote in Greek to Julius, the bishop of Rome. About 50 years later, Tyrannius Rufinus wrote a commentary on this creed in Latin. In it, he recounted the view point that apostles wrote the creed together after Pentecost, before leaving to Jerusalem to preach. The title \"Apostles\" Creed is also mentioned about 390 by Ambrose, where he refers to the \"the creed of the Apostles which the Church of Rome keeps an guard in its entirety.  Whatever we now know as the Apostles creed is an enlargement of the Old Roman Creed. Over the next few centuries, the Apostles Creed in its final form gained acceptance through out France and Germany. The Creed as it exists today consists of three main articles, like the Old Roman Creed divided according to a Trinitarian arrangement. The apostles Creed seems to represent some form of what the early church called the rule of faith.', 8, '2023-10-18 02:15:32', '2023-11-08 09:56:30', NULL),
(3013, 836, 867, 'The church needs to clearly define Christianity in biblical terms, which the Apostles, Creed succinctly provides to us. The Apostles creed is the most historic and universal summary of the Christian faith in the entire history of the church. It instructs, guides, defends and enshrines the most important question of all: \"What must I do to be saved?\"  Church and Christians must return to historic Christianity, which emerged from the rich doctrinal commitments and evangelical fervor of the apostles. This summary of timeless, biblical truths provides us with the essence of the Christian belief throughout history as followers attempted to construct a worldview and theology based upon the teachings of the Bible. The Apostle\'s Creed not only enlightens Christians, but it impacts and shapes their lives so that they might live daily in the freedom that the Lord Jesus Christ can provide. Seeking God through popular fads and other half-measures provide  us with nothing but short-term gratification. We can only find the true path to salvation in the historical Word of God.', 7, '2023-10-18 02:28:19', '2023-11-08 09:56:30', NULL),
(3014, 836, 868, 'There is one and only one true and living God. This one God eternally exists in three persons - God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit.  These there persons are completely equal in attributes, each with the same divine nature. While each person is fully and completely  God, the persons are not identical.  First the Father: I believe in God the Father Almighty, creator of heaven and earth. He cares about us, tends to our needs, listens to our concerns, is strong, compassionate, and loving. The Son: I believe in Jesus Christ, His only Son, our Lord. Jesus parents were Mary, a teenager and virgin, and God. He was born into Jewish tribe of Judha and adopted by Joseph, a carpenter. Having both human and divine origin, Jesus is both fully human and fully God. The Holy spirit: The aspect of God that lives inside believers. He guides people into God\'s wisdom and truth and consistently points the focus onto Jesus and to the God.', 7, '2023-10-18 02:37:39', '2023-11-08 09:56:30', NULL),
(3015, 836, 869, 'Worshipping God is a commandment: As one of His Ten Commandments, God commanded that we keep the Sabbath day holy. One of the ways we keep this commandment is to meet together to worship God and give His thanks.  At church, we partake of the communion. Jesus commanded, Take, eat : this is my body, which is broken for you; this doin remembrance of the me. Keeping this commandment weekly at church helps us remember what Jesus has done for us and commit to follow him. In the Bible, the Apostle Paul compared the church to a body, with Jesus as the head. He said for a body to work, every member is important and must do  its part. Participating in church and offering your time and talents will improve and strengthen us. What we learnt at church gives us guidance on how to follow God, improves our spirituality , and we live better lives.', 7, '2023-10-18 02:48:06', '2023-11-08 09:56:30', NULL),
(3016, 836, 870, 'Apostle\'s Creed teaches us on how to live a life in His light and pursue a life of faith. It also teaches how to worship. The apostles Creed teaches us how to worship and follow the glory and truths of the faith. It helps us praise God and His teachings. The Apostles Creed is therefore a summary of what church teaches, and of what Christians together believe, rather than a detailed statement of individual and personal belief. Creed binds together as a believing community, across different traditions and practices.  Creeds define the truth : As Jesus told his disciples, and you will know the truth and the truth will set you free. We must study the creeds of the faith. Creeds correct error. Creeds provide rules and standards for God\'s people.  Creeds teach the church how to worship and confess the faith. Creeds connect us to the faith of our fathers. Creed summarize the faith. Creeds define true Christian unity.', 7, '2023-10-18 03:01:09', '2023-11-08 09:56:30', NULL),
(3017, 837, 871, 'The bible is clear that although there be other so-called gods, there is only one eternal God who exists. As  in Isaiah 43:10 - you are my witness, says the Lord, and my servants whom I have chosen, that you may know and believe me, and understand that I am he, before me there was no God formed, nor shall there be after me.\r\nThough the Bible makes reference to false gods it does not state that there are actual god who exist. The Apostle Paul wrote: But, then indeed, when you did not know God, you served those which by nature are not gods.\r\nThese so-called gods were inventions in the mind of people who rejected the truth of the one true God. Only the God of the Bible has real substance. Since the God of Bible has given us reason to believe in His existence whatever He might say on the matter of other gods is final. If another God did exist, then God would not be the self-existent, all-powerful God. Two all-powerful, all-knowing, everywhere present beings could not occupy the same space.', 8, '2023-10-21 01:11:36', '2023-11-08 09:57:02', NULL),
(3018, 837, 872, 'Simplicity means God is not made up of parts; he is not composite of compounded being. He is simple; all that is in God is God. God is not made up of parts, nor is he compounded or composite in nature. That means he does not possess attributes, as if his attributes are on thing and his essence another. God is his attributes, That means, all that is in God simply is God. Simplicity is key for it distinguishes between the infinite, eternal, and immutable creator and the finite, temporal, and mutable creature. God\'s attributes are not external to his essence, as if they added a quality to him that he would not otherwise possess. God has no properties but is pure essence. They neither differ from his essence nor do they differ materially from each other. God is in no way composite. Rather, he is entirely simple.', 7, '2023-10-21 01:25:38', '2023-11-08 09:57:02', NULL),
(3019, 837, 873, 'The term \"economic Trinity\' focuses on what God does; \"ontological Trinity\" focuses on who God is. Taken together, there two terms present the paradox of the Trinity; The Father, Son, and Sprit share one nature, but they are different Persons and have different roles. The Trinity is both united and distinct. \r\nJesus said in John 5:19-23. He declared: \"I don\'t do anything on my own. I do what the Father tell Me to do. I do what the Father sent Me todo. I watch the Father, and I do what the Father does. The Father is preeminent. The Father is the One to whom I am obedient and subordinate. He even affirmed that He could not do anything of Himself, only what He saw the Father do. Then Jesus stated that the Father would show Him even greater things, so they should expect His work to become greater. In this context, Jesus specifically mentioned the raising of the dead.', 7, '2023-10-21 01:38:34', '2023-11-08 09:57:02', NULL),
(3020, 837, 874, 'The attributes of fatherhood described in this article are all present in Christ. He establishes, feeds and maintains a household. He is the Head and the second Adam of a new creation. Christ is seen in the Gospel as established His people by instituting the Church. The greatest glory of God, therefore, is not that he is separate and far beyond us; the greatest glory of God is that the one who is separate and far beyond us, who is high and lifted up, who created all things and needs nothing. The glorious God also chose to become our Father, lovingly adopting us as his own children forever. Pastor Tim Keller helps us understand how wonderful it is to have God as our father. The only person who dares wake up a king early is his son. We have that kind of access. This astonishing right of entry into the presence of God is not a minor emphasis in the gospel.', 7, '2023-10-21 01:46:44', '2023-11-08 09:57:02', NULL),
(3021, 837, 875, 'As the Creator, God has authority over man and all of creation. As in Isaiah 45:18 - For thus says the Lord, who created the heavens, who is God, who formed the earth and made it, Who has established it, Who did not create in vain, Who formed it to be inhabited. I am the Lord, and there is no other.\r\n\r\nGod created man in His image, and He also gave man authority over the fish of the sea, over the birds of the air, and over the cattle , over all the earth and over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.\r\n\r\nGod is the ultimate authority simply because He is \"the Lord, and there is no other\" It is He who created everything, including mankind. God is the Father of mankind, and as such, He has authority over us.  Because God is our authority in every area, we need to make sure that we know what His word says, and strive to make it our authority in every area of life.', 7, '2023-10-21 02:00:36', '2023-11-08 09:57:02', NULL),
(3022, 840, 876, 'Son of God is arguably the most important title of Christ in the New Testament, appearing more than forty times. In the simples of terms, Son of God is how the New Testament describes Jesus relationship to God the Father, and it also shows us His divine identity, that He is fully God.  As in acts13:33 - He has fulfilled for us, their children, by raising Jesus; as also it written in the second psalm, your my son; today I have begotten you. As in Hebrew 1:5 - For to which of the angels did he ever say. \"You are my son. today I have begotten you?\" And again, I will be Father to him and he shall be son to me?\" . Therefore, it is the united testimony of the New Testament that Jesus was the Son of God.', 7, '2023-10-23 01:11:47', '2023-11-08 09:57:41', NULL),
(3023, 840, 877, 'The final title \"Our Lord\" also reflects Jesus\'s divinity as well as the role he should play in our lives today. It reflects Jesus\'s divinity as the people of Israel called God \"the Lord\"; this was the translation of the name Yahweh. When we survey the Old and New Testaments, we find at least four different ways in which \"Son of God\" is used. \r\n1. Creation Usage: One of the God\'s creatures can be termed a \"Son of God\" because he owes his existence to God\'s creative activity. Malachi observes; Have we not all one Father? Did not one God create us - Malachi 2:10 a \r\n2. Family Usage: \"Son of God\" can describe the relationship of men to God as the objectives of his loving care, spiritual sonship. Israel as God\'s elect people is sometimes referred to in this way. For example, God declares, Israel is my firstborn son - Exodus4:22 \r\n3. Messianic Usage: \"Son of God\" is used in a messianic way, where the king whom God has set in place is referred to as the \"Son of God\" especially in the promise made to David.\r\n4. Divine Son Usage. Finally , and most important, Son of God is used in a theologies way. Both in the New Testament and later in Christian Theology, Jesus is said to  be the Son of God because he is God and share the divine nature.', 8, '2023-10-23 01:26:17', '2023-11-08 09:57:41', NULL),
(3024, 840, 878, 'In the four Gospels, there is ample evidence that Jesus was fully human since He was born to a human mother ( Matthew 1:25), He experienced hunger (Matthew 21:18) and thirst ( John 19:28) . Jesus also experience temptation (Matthew 4:1) pain and suffering (Matthew 16:21), and He died (Matthew 27:50)\r\n\r\nThe apostle Paul wrote, who (Jesus) being in the form of God did not consider it robbery to be equal with God, but made himself of no reputation, taking the form of a bondservant , and coming in the likeness of men. And, being found in appearance as a man, He humbled Himself and became obedient to the point of death, even the death of the cross (Philippinas 2:6-8)\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\n1. Jesus became tired, like we do.\r\n2. He knew physical hunger.\r\n3. He got thirsty.\r\n4. He grew weak physically.\r\n5. Jesus know anger.\r\n6. Jesus felt Sadness.\r\n7. Jesus was tempted.', 8, '2023-10-23 01:38:46', '2023-11-08 09:57:41', NULL),
(3025, 840, 879, 'Christ means \"Anointed One\", and in the Old Testament, Prophets were anointed with oil. Priests were anointed with oil. And kings were anointed with oil too. So the Christ, the Anointed One, was the One anointed for this unique threefold office of Prophet, Priest and King. \r\n\r\n1. Jesus as Prophet: The people of Jesus\'s day referred to Him as a prophet many times. Both Peter and Stephen spoke of Jesus as being the ultimate fulfillment of Moses\'s prophecy in Deuteronomy 18:15\r\n\r\n2. Jesus as Priest: Old testament priests served as mediators between humans and God. It was the priest who offered sacrifices on behalf of the people. Jesus is our mediator and our High priest. \"For there is one God and one mediator between God and mankind, the man Christ Jesus\" 1 Timothy 2:5 \r\n\r\n3. Jesus as King: The angel Gabriel told Mary that Jesus will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High. The Lord God will  give him the throne of his father David, and he will reign over Jacob\'s descendants forever; his kingdom will never end.', 7, '2023-10-23 01:55:04', '2023-11-08 09:57:41', NULL),
(3026, 840, 880, 'Jesus is Impeccable because he is the divine Son who assumed a human nature, and as such, his human nature never existed apart from it\'s union in the Son. Jesus is not merely another Adam; he is the head of the new creation, there eternal Son incarnate. Jesus is fully human and fully divine - complete existence, utter essence. And through him we realize what God\'s final purpose always was; to brings us into essence - into eternal truth. Jesus is God stretching out and saying  \'Come into the essence of all things to be with him. \r\nJesus became man so that he could die for our sins. He had to be human in order to pay the penalty for humans. Because Jesus is human like us, he is able to be a merciful and faithful High priest . His humanity enables him to more fully sympathize with us and identify with us.', 7, '2023-10-23 02:07:36', '2023-11-08 09:57:41', NULL),
(3027, 839, 781, 'While the author of Hebrew remains unknown, they used the Old Testament to support their theological views. For example within the book of Hebrews, there is a combination of either quotes or references to the Old Testament nearly 100 times. The historical facts of the Old Testament have been intertwined into the presentation of Christian faith into the book of Hebrews. \r\n\r\nSpecific examples that have been referenced in the book of Hebrews with context dating to the Old Testament include: referencing God\'s son (2 Samuel 7:14), angels as serving spirits (Psalm 104:4), lower than angels (Psalm 8:4-6), blessings of God\'s vindication (Isaiah 8:17), God as a consuming fire (Deut 4:24). \r\n\r\nThe author of Hebrews called attention to the fact that history had reached its last days in the times of Jesus. He did this through referencing the Old Testament as support and then a number of exhortations to continue and prosper in the Christian faith. This was done primarily due to the fact that the author had to challenge the teachings of the local community which was mainly Jewish. The one thing that remained sacred and in common was the Old Testament, and that is why we can see nearly 100 references or quotes from the Old Testament in the book of Hebrews.  In these scenarios, rather than noting just historical facts, the author spent more time on theological beliefs about God himself and the matters in close relation to God.', 8, '2023-10-25 00:41:01', '2023-11-08 09:58:34', NULL),
(3028, 839, 782, 'In the first century, Jewish communities often exalted angels as being powerful, glorious creatures who brought divine revelations to inferior human beings. While the outlooks of the community were rooted in biblical beliefs and passages, they gave excessive honor to angels. This raised a serious challenge to those who followed Christ. \r\n\r\nThe author of Hebrews taught his audience that Jesus is superior to angels. This was accomplished in five steps, the first of which the author wrote to the audience that they should follow Jesus because Jesus is God\'s supreme source of divine revelation. The author declared that God spoke through angels. The second step was through the explanation that Jesus is greater than angels because he alone is God\'s messianic Son. Thirdly, the author told his audience to heed and pay careful attention to the great message of salvation first announced by Jesus. Fourth, the book of Hebrews supports the belief in the supremacy of Christ by explaining that Jesus is now ruler over all the angels. And in the future all of the believers would reign over them with him. Lastly, the author demonstrated that Jesus is Abraham\'s descendant, and Jesus will share his glory with Abraham\'s descendants, not with the angels. \r\n\r\nThrough these five steps laid out in the book of Hebrews, the author was able to provide the honor that only Christ brings and how Jesus brought revelation that was far superior to any revelation given through any angel.', 8, '2023-10-25 00:50:02', '2023-11-08 09:58:34', NULL),
(3029, 839, 783, 'In order to discuss how the new covenant is superior to the old, we must first understand the reference for the \'new covenant\'. The new covenant comes from Jeremiah 31:31, where the prophet predicted that God would grant a covenant of final renewal to Israel and Judah after Israel\'s exile in the final days. This was also referred to as the \'covenant of peace\'. \r\n\r\nThe New Covenant is based on the ultimate sacrifice of Jesus Christ and providers eternal forgiveness of sins. The Old Covenant would require ongoing animal sacrifices and offerings that could not fully take away the sins of mankind. The New Covenant is focused more on a personal and direct relationship with God rather than a system of laws and rituals. The New Covenant is also not prohibited to just the Jewish people, but rather to people of all nations. \r\n\r\nThe New Covenant cannot fail per Hebrews 8:6 as it is founded on better promises. This offers the complete transformation of God\'s people and the the final eternal forgiveness of their sins. Jesus did away with sin once and for all because of his sacrifice and the shedding of bless. It was not merely sprinkle in a man made sanctuary, but He entered heaven by the sacrifice of himself, just as God had promised. \r\n\r\nJesus died as a ransom in order to set his people free from judgement, and the author clearly states that Jesus will return but not to bear sin again, rather to bring the fullness of salvation to those who wait for him. \r\n\r\nThe New Covenant is considered superior to the Old Covenant because it provides a more complete and personal relationship that is enduring with God through faith in Jesus Christ, which is opposed to the conditional, ritual based system of the old.', 9, '2023-10-25 01:00:59', '2023-11-08 09:58:34', NULL),
(3030, 839, 784, 'The different methods that I esteem and uphold the importance of the Old Testament in my life and ministry begin with a few key areas. The first would be to understand that in life hardships do arise, and as they do come, it may be a father discipling their son. This may be one of the hardest things to accept in life, and I have personally gone through periods of discerning the discipline of God versus something that was not, and knowing which is discipline results in a harvest of peace. I have been able to witness and taste the joy that comes with surrender and obedience to God through those times. \r\n\r\nAnother focus areas in my ministry is to encourage others through my own suffering. There have been countless times that I have had my personal failures and struggles but through it all, God was faithful and sustained me. I was able to still arise when I fell and knew that God allowed me to be a vessel and encourage others through similar paths and walks of life that they were on. \r\n\r\nBeing thankful not only in my personal life, but in ministry as well. God has blessed me abundantly, whether it be through a secular, financial means or through family ties. I have been a person who has witnessed and tasted the loss of a large magnitude and the wealth of the richest. Through it all, gratitude that God has allowed me to survive among all that I lost. Perseverance in God\'s sight has allowed me to stand when many others have fallen and that allowed for immeasurable blessings to follow.', 8, '2023-10-25 01:09:08', '2023-11-08 09:58:34', NULL),
(3031, 839, 785, 'One of the greatest quotes that I love to share is, \"do all the good that you can, in all the ways that you can, to all those you can, for as long as you can.\" That is what Jesus Christ means for me each and every day. He died for me, when I was the least deserving, and that is the anchor for my life, my influence, and my ministry. When sharing about my life story with countless people around the world, the one anchor in my message is that, without surrendering and giving it to God, I would not have been able to recover and stand again. \r\n\r\nThis influences me in every decision that I make. When I let my own decisions take over my life, it was clear that the devil, and sin was ready to consume me into destruction. The fact that there is redemption with Christ and that he has overcome the shame, the guilt and the sin for us is the greatest testimony we can ever be a part of. He took that to the cross, so that I wouldn\'t have to. That is the core anchor for my ministry. When I was ready to call it a day and be done with my marriage, my family, my career, and myself, I finally came to the point of hopelessness. I gave up and realized that there was no where else to turn. And it\'s usually in those failed moments, you realized who can turn the greatest stories out of these monumental failures of life. \r\n\r\nBut what good would it serve me to keep this blessing inside? I know that there are countless people hurting all throughout the world, the community, and in our church. They all come with different pain, and different baggage. If Christ was not the one to cast a stone and condemn, then who am I to do so? That is the method to ministry and effective impact towards guiding them to the cross. Christ loved the sinners and that love is what eventually turned them to the cross and salvation.', 8, '2023-10-25 01:15:36', '2023-11-08 09:58:34', NULL),
(3032, 841, 881, 'It  implicitly affirms the Spirit\'s divinity in at least two ways. First, it\'s Trinitarian structure equates the Holy Spirit with the Father and the Son in important ways. And Second, the creed\'s description of Jesus conception indicates the the Holy Spirit is divine.  The first thing that the Apostle\'s Creed affirms is the the Holy Spirit is the third person of the Trinity. The structure of the Creed shows that the church is confessing the divinity of the Holy Spirits.  The divinity of the Holy Spirit is evident from the divine attributes the he shares with the Father and the Son.  First, the Holy Spirit is eternal. Hebrews 9:14 says that Christ \"through the eternal Spirit offered himself without blemish to God. Second, the Holy Spirit is everywhere present. Psalm 139:7 says, Where shall I go from your Spirit? Or where shall I  flee from your presence. Third,  the Holy Spirit knows all things. This is stated in 1 Corinthians 2:10 \" For the Spirit searches everything, even the depths of God\"', 7, '2023-11-05 01:39:31', '2023-11-08 09:59:05', NULL),
(3033, 841, 882, 'The Apostle\'s Creed says that Jesus Christ, the Son of God was conceived by the Holy Spirit. When speaking of Jesus\'s conception, the creed alludes to Luke 1:35, where the angel spoke these words to Mary: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the Power of the Most High will overshadow  you. So the holy one to be born will called the Son of God. In this God\'s word, the Holy Spirit equated the power of the Most High. So, by alluding to this verse as an example of the Holy Spirit\'s work, the Apostles Creed affirms the Spirits full divinity. The conclusion is confirmed by Hebrews 10:5-7, which says, \" Therefore , when Christ came into the world , he said : Sacrifice and offering you did not desire, but a body you prepare for me; with burnt offerings and sin offerings  you were not pleased. Then I said, Here I am - its written about me in the scroll - I have come to do your will, O God\"', 7, '2023-11-05 01:48:34', '2023-11-08 09:59:05', NULL),
(3034, 841, 883, 'The Holy Spirit produces behavior traits that are representative of Jesus. The apostle Paul referred to there characteristics as the fruit of the Spirit. \"The fruit of the Spirit if Love, Joy, Peace, Patience, Kindness, Goodness, Faithfulness, Gentleness, and Self-control\"  - Galatians 5:22-23. As we examine the Biblical text, several Key characteristics emerge as we study the nature of the Holy Spirit. The first and foremost feature is the characteristic of \"Personhood\". The Bible repeatedly describe the Holy Spirit as  person rather than a force emanating from God.  The clearest indication of the \"Personhood\" of the Holy Spirit is simply  the fact personal pronouns are used when the Holy Spirit is being referenced.   While Peter was reflecting on the vision, the Spirit said to him, Behold there men are looking for you. But, get up, go downstairs and accompany them without misgivings, for I have sent them myself.  John 15:26 -  When the Helper comes, whom I will send to you from the Father, that is the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father, He will testify about Me.', 7, '2023-11-05 02:02:27', '2023-11-08 09:59:05', NULL),
(3035, 841, 884, 'The Father, Son, and Holy Spirit are all God, but they have different roles. Here are the some distinctions between them: \r\n1. The Father is the origin of creation, redemption, and consummation\r\n2. The Son is the mediator.\r\n3. The Holy Spirit is the one who brings every work to completion. \r\n4. The Father is not the Son, the Son is not the Spirit, the Spirit is not the Father, but each is God individually, and yet they are together the one true God of Bible.\r\n\r\nThe doctrine of the Trinity means that there is one God who eternally exists as there distinct Persons - the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. \r\nAs per the God\'s word 2 Corinthians 13:!4 - May the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the Love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with  you all.\r\nIn Conclusion, the Holy Spirit is a distinct personage from God the Father, God the Son, and is the third person of the Trinity.', 7, '2023-11-05 02:15:07', '2023-11-08 09:59:05', NULL),
(3036, 841, 885, 'For  the Holy Spirit the relation is: Giver - Gift, the Father and the Son being together the Giver, and \"Gift\" being a name of the Spirit, often designated thus in Scripture. \r\n\r\nIn Matthew 28:19 : As part of the Great Commission, Jesus said \" Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the son, and of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit if often called the third Person of the Trinity because, in this \"Trinitarian formula\", He is listed third.\r\n\r\nAs God exists, all three Persons are co-equal. However, as God has revealed Himself to us and as He interacts with us, each person of the Trinity has taken on certain roles. The Spirit attention to the Son (John 16:14) and the Son directs attention to the Father (John 14:13) in this sense, also, the Holy Spirit is third.', 7, '2023-11-05 02:23:14', '2023-11-08 09:59:05', NULL),
(3037, 842, 886, 'The church has its roots in the promise made to God\'s people in the Old Testament, particularly that God would bless the world through Abraham\'s offspring. While there is continuity between the Old Testament people of God and the church. the church is the community of Jesus. new Pentecost.  The Old Testament was Jesus\'s only scripture and makes up three fourths of our Bible. If space says anything, the OT matters to God, who gave us his Word in a book. In fact, it was his first special revelation, which set a foundation for the fulfillment we find in Jesus in the New Testament. The ministry of apostles and prophets are foundational ministries. They were in the Old Testament and they were in the early Church. Scriptures rank them first and second respectively in order of importance of all ministries in the Church. The old Testament provides the historical setting out of which Christianity and the New Testament emerged.', 8, '2023-11-07 03:12:33', '2023-11-08 09:59:39', NULL),
(3038, 842, 887, 'Jesus builds his church by Spiraling : he gathers, constitutes, and sends. Jesus draws in people and gathers them to himself. He then constitutes them as his community. And then he sends out his people to the world: \" As you send me into the world, so I have sent them into the world (John 17:18) . Jesus Christ established a church in his name for a very special reason: to bring to pass the immortality and eternal life of man. Immortality refers to endless life, a gift granted to us through the Resurrection of Jesus Christ.  The Church has its roots in the promises made to God\'s people in the Old Testament, particularly the God would bless the world through Abraham\'s offspring. While there is continuity between the Old Testament people of God and the church, the church is the community of Jesus, new at a Pentecost. Jesus the Rock is building His church upon the foundation of Himself.', 7, '2023-11-07 03:21:30', '2023-11-08 09:59:39', NULL),
(3039, 842, 888, 'Although God spoke in many and various ways through the Prophets, the common goal was always the Son of God ( Hebrew 1:1) . And thus, both testament points to Jesus Christ. More inductively, the New Testament fulfills the promises of the Old Testament and completes all God began in the Law and the Prophets (John 1:45) . The OId Testament Reveals God\'s Faithfulness. God sits sovereign over the world as it spins out of control, but He is also present with us, personally. Old Testament scriptures help u s understand why Jesus\'s sacrificial death on the cross was both necessary and final. Humanity falls short of the glory of God. Briefly describe the relationship between Old and New Testament. The new Testament does not replace the Old Testament, but fulfills it. Without the New Testament the Old Testament would just be a collection of tragic stories and unfulfilled promises.', 7, '2023-11-07 03:32:33', '2023-11-08 09:59:39', NULL),
(3040, 842, 889, 'The term comes from two Greek words that together mean throughout the whole. The single word Catholic means throughout all time and places and also points to the essential unity or wholeness of the church in Christ. The term Catholic should not be confused with the Roman Catholic church, for the term Catholic. This is from the Greek Katholikos , derived from Kath holos, meaning through the whole. Catholic means universal. The church is universal in two ways. First, the Church is catholic because all baptized people are part of the Church and Church possessed the means of salvation. Second, the mission of the Church is universal because the church has been sent to proclaim Christ to entire human race.  Catholics share Jesus\'s vision and model their lives after Jesus example of love. Catholics are called to care for one another and to use their unique gifts and talents for the good of the community and the world.', 8, '2023-11-07 03:45:23', '2023-11-08 09:59:39', NULL),
(3041, 842, 890, 'The visible church, which is also catholic or universal under the gospel  consists of all those throught our the world that profess the true religion; and of their children; and in the kingdom of the Lord Jesus Christ, the house and family of God. Church visible is a term of Christian theology and ecclesiology referring to the visible community of Christian believers on the Earth, as opposed to the Church invisible or church triumphant, constituted by the fellowship of saints and the company of the elect. The church invisible , invisible church, mystical church or church mystical, is a Christian theological concept of an invisible Christian church of the elect who are known only to God, in contrst to the visible church - that is the institutional body on earth which preaches the gospel and administers the sacraments. Every member of the invisible church is saved. according to the bible passages such as Matthew 7:21-27', 8, '2023-11-07 03:54:19', '2023-11-08 09:59:39', NULL),
(3042, 843, 891, 'Traditionally, the origin has been ascribed to the sin of the first man, Adam, who disobeyed God in eating the forbidden fruit and, in consequence, transmitted his sin and guilt by heredity to his descendants. \r\nThe scriptures tell us over and over that sin leads to death and destruction because there are consequences with going against the grain regarding how God designed us to live and obey him. Physically , sin an cause an internal conflict with God and us, leading to health issues and taking a physical toll on our body. \r\n\r\nAs a result of original sin, human nature is weakened in its powers, subject to ignorance, suffering and domination of death, and inclined to sin. According to 1 John 3:4 - any violation of God\'s law: Everyone who does sin also does lawlessness, and sin is lawlessness. Any lack of conformity to the perfect moral standard of God is sin.', 7, '2023-11-09 02:40:12', '2024-03-03 05:49:26', NULL),
(3043, 843, 892, 'The entire process of creation and grace is viewed as a single shared action of the three divine persons, in which each person manifests the attributes unique to them in the Trinity, thereby proving that everything comes from the Father, through the Son, and in the Holy Spirit.  It\'s rooted in the fact that God came to meet Christians in a threefold figure : 1. As a Creator. Lord of the history of salvation, Father, and Judge, as revealed in the Old Testament 2. as the Lord who, in the incarnated figure of Jesus Christ, lived among human beings and was present in their midst. Creation, Salvation, and Sanctification are known as the divine missions of the Blessed Trinity, We must remember that all  three Persons of the Trinity act as one and are fully present in all the missions. A Trinity doctrine is commonly expressed as the statement that the one God exists as or in three equally divine Persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.', 6, '2023-11-09 02:52:44', '2024-03-03 05:49:26', NULL),
(3044, 843, 893, 'Forgiveness builds trust and accountability in your relationship, breaks down resentment, and stops the blame game. Taking responsibility for your behaviors in your relationship requires honest and open communication and a willingness to address unhealthy excuses with your partner. It is important that you admit what you did wrong and truly feel sorry that you did it. You must come to God, pray using scripture, and ask Him to forgive you. Then you must believe that He has. After you are forgiven, work on leaving sin behind and living a new life.  Taking personal responsibility means we rightly blame where it belongs in our own lives. Bible says that the only people we should blame when sin is ourselves. Whenever we sin, there is an urge to look to alternative explanations for what caused the sin. Be open and honest with God through prayers and admit that you have made mistakes. Once you have confessed your sins, ask for help.', 5, '2023-11-09 03:02:45', '2024-03-03 05:49:26', NULL),
(3045, 843, 894, 'Traditionally, the origin has been ascribed to the sin of the first man, Adam, who disobey God in eating the forbidden fruit. The moral ramification of the Fall of the man are the we, humans, are dead in our tress passes and sins, and we are prone to speak, do, and pursue many sorts of evil. We enter the world and sin. They physical ramification of the Fall of the man are pain of child birth, bodily deterioration, the strain of work, and sickness. Therefore, we see in Romans 6:6 the the Holy Spirit  calls our body \" the body of sin\" because before we experience the dealing of the cross, before we yield our members as weapons of righteousness to God, our body is simply the body of sin. Before we reckon ourselves as dead to son and yield our bod to God.', 5, '2023-11-09 03:09:19', '2024-03-03 05:49:26', NULL),
(3046, 843, 895, 'Redemption refers supremely to the work of Christ on our behalf, whereby he purchases us, he ransoms us, at the price of his own life, securing our deliverance from the bondage and condemnation of son.  Redemption: God initiated a plan to rescue humanity and the material universe from the now inevitable divine judgement for evil. Genesis 3:15 through Revelation 20. Renovation: God created a new universe out of the old one, a new heavens and new earth. God was revealing His plans to defeat Satan and offer salvation to the world through Jesus, Himself. Therefore , we have in Genesis 3:15 the first promise of a Redeemer. It is the beginning of a long line of Prophecies concerning the coming of Messiah. The one who would make things right between God and man. Jesus Christ redeemed us by His Passion, Death, Resurrection, and Ascension. To Redeem means to buy back something that has been lost, sold or given away.', 5, '2023-11-09 03:20:36', '2024-03-03 05:49:26', NULL),
(3047, 848, 776, 'The  majority of the interpreters believe that Paul was not the author of the book of Hebrews due to the fact that the author never revealed or identified himself. The other cause for this is that the book of Hebrews was never included in the Marcionite Canon and Muratorian Canon. Another fact to note from the writing style of the author of Hebrews is that it is different than Paul\'s letters, the style of Greek that is used in Hebrews is more sophisticated than the Greek that is used in Paul\'s other letters. There is also a different level of theological emphasis in Hebrews that is distinct from the other writings of Paul. \r\n\r\nHowever, there are three distinct reasons for the stance that is believed to identify Paul as not being the author of the book of Hebrews. The first is that is was Paul\'s practice to name himself in his epistles. He was concerned deeply around individuals forging his writing and spreading lies under his name. The second reason is subjects don\'t receive much emphasis if any in Paul\'s letters. The fact that Melchizedek was mentioned three times drew attention to the difference in styles compared to Paul\'s letters. The third reason is the way the writer of Hebrews distanced himself from the first generation of  Jesus\' followers.', 7, '2023-11-22 13:56:10', '2024-03-03 05:48:58', NULL),
(3048, 848, 777, 'The five characteristics of Hebrew\'s original audience include  Jewish, Hellenistic, Immature, Persecuted, and Near Apostasy. \r\n\r\nFor the Jewish audience, Hebrews 1:1 make this clear, \"In the past God spoke to our forefathers through the prophets at many time and in various ways.\" The Old Testament Israelites are referred to as \"our forefathers\" which are considered to be the ancestors of the author and his audience.\r\n\r\nThe Hellenistic audience, which interpreters believe have lived outside of Palestine. The content in Hebrews reveals that the audience was familiar with theological teachings that were more common among Jews who lived outside of Palestine than the traditional Jews who lived within Palestine. \r\n\r\nThe Immature audience which is known as the original audience of Hebrews was described in Hebrews 5:12, \"Though by this time you ought to be teachers, you need someone to teach you the elementary truths of God\'s word all over again.\" This interprets to say that the audience has been listening to and exposed to the word of God over and over again for a long time, but they have yet not matured in the word. That is the emphasis the author gives when saying that this audience should have been teachers by now. \r\n\r\nThe Persecuted audience is addressed in Hebrews 10:32, \"Remember those earlier days after you had received the light, when you stood your ground in a great contest in the face of suffering ... So do not throw away your confidence, it will be richly rewarded.\" In this passage, the audience mentioned is being praised for their strength and were told to not throw away their confidence. The author of Hebrews mentions this group was facing the threat of more persecution even in the future and this was addressed in Hebrews 12:3-4. \r\n\r\nLastly the Near Apostasy, this audience was near danger of turning away from Christ entirely. In Hebrews 10:26-27, it reads, \"If we deliberately keep on sinning after we have have received knowledge of the truth, no sacrifice for sins is left, but only a fearful expectation of judgement and of raging fire that will consume the enemies of God.\" The author of Hebrews was not concerned about peccadillos or small sins, rather he was concerned more about those in danger of turning away from God.', 8, '2023-11-22 14:08:21', '2024-03-03 05:48:58', NULL),
(3049, 848, 778, 'The author of Hebrews used different literary devices and methods to reveal and express his exhortations to his audience. Frequency, for example allows us to understand the urgency of his message. The author exhorted to the audience using intensity and the hortatory subjunctive, a Greek verbal form used to urge or implore. At least 30 times these exhortations appear explicitly which would urge the audience often to \"let us\" do this or that. \r\n\r\nThe author also exhorted using imperatives, which is often translated as direct orders. Hebrews 12:12-16  reveals the importance of adopting new attitudes or actions. He did not want to simply inform the audience of theological doctrines, he wanted them to adopt different viewpoints and attitudes towards it all. \r\n\r\nThe author of Hebrews is described as highly rhetorical, meaning there was a persuasive or urgent debate mechanism in the first century. This is found more often in Hebrews than in any other book in the New Testament. This helped the author accomplish their purpose, containing a strong literary argument, using lots of different structural features to accomplish that, drawing readers in and convincing them of the argument that he is making. Some of these rhetorical devices included synkrisis, exempla, and qol wahomer.', 8, '2023-11-22 14:16:03', '2024-03-03 05:48:58', NULL),
(3050, 848, 779, 'Hebrews 5:12 mentions the need to be taught elementary truths of God\'s word all over again. There are areas within my own spiritual life that needs doctrinal progress. This is something that is dealt with on a regular basis to get better rooted in the word of God. While I was born into a pentecostal faith home throughout my life, I have heard countless sermons, messages and teachings about the Bible, Jesus Christ and Salvation. However, I can say with confidence that the firm foundational support of elementary teachings were not provided in a digestable format for me as I was being raised in the church. I believe that is what I see as a need today in the youth of our church and in my ministry. The areas of life that need doctrinal progress rely heavily upon character formation and being able to witness what it means to be a better example and follower of Christ. \r\n\r\nWhile Scripture reveals the need for salvation and living a life that is pleasing and holy before God, there are some areas of doctrine that is in need of maturity. The real need of repentance and living a life that is separated for God in all senses must be a core belief and practice in a Christian\'s walk with Christ. That becomes very much blurred in the year 2023, because while temptations have remained the same for thousands of years, the way that we address it has become softer. The word of God is sharper than any two edged sword, but because of culture and what others will think, I personally struggle with how to connect these conversations with younger folks. In my personal walk with God, I have been able to separate and have self control over my decisions and choices in life. However that did not come without mistakes and slip ups, the doctrines on living a faith based life with the end in mind is something we do not emphasize enough in today\'s day and age.', 9, '2023-11-22 14:24:21', '2024-03-03 05:48:58', NULL),
(3051, 848, 780, 'False teachings that we are facing today are evident throughout social medial and television at a rapid pace. This includes the teachings around prosperity and how God is a God of blessing and will not punish you for living a double standard life. Or another teaching of how good works alone can help you enter into the kingdom of God. The other main false doctrine that is penetrating our churches is that times have changed and there are certain sins that are permissive and able to be tolerated. \r\n\r\nThere are billionaire preachers that talk about prosperity and there are average income preachers that talk about prosperity. The false teachings attached to this is to make the audience believe that financial blessings are a direct result of blessing from God. However that can also be perceived as being cursed by God if you are living in poverty or if you are not wealthy. The second piece is teaching is about how good works alone can help justify that you are a good person or a good Christian and that if you live a good life you can enter God\'s kingdom. That alone will not save you or allow you to enter heaven, and the time will come when these false doctrines will prove themselves as being lies. The last one about how because it is 2023 and the norm is to have sins that are modern and with the times, is a huge lie that the enemy has planted into the church. It is okay to do certain things because it was not there in the time of Jesus. \r\n\r\nJesus being born a man came into this world and had the same temptation and urges that a human would have, but would have the self control and set an example of what it mean to fight those struggles. Having a strong biblical doctrine and being able to ask those questions and feeling empowered to do so is the first step. I personally know that all answers can be found through the word of God and spending time in meditation and from spiritual counselors and guidance from my spiritual leaders. There is no other way to attack this form of evil that the devil places in front of us.', 9, '2023-11-22 14:30:57', '2024-03-03 05:48:58', NULL),
(3052, 844, 977, 'At least two factors often cause us to feel distant from the Old Testament. On the other hand, God gave these scriptures to humanity through a process know as organic inspiration. And on the other hand, God designed the Old Testament to fulfill it\'s purpose through a process known as divine accommodation.  The concise answer is the the Old Testament occurs first and shows us how the world began and how the people of Israel came to be. Whereas, the New Testament comes after and documents how the world is saved through what Jesus did for his people. As for the New Testament, it is the compliment of the Old. Without the New Testament, the Old is merely a collection of historical or religious writings.', 5, '2023-11-23 04:06:02', '2024-03-03 05:48:27', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3053, 844, 978, 'At least two factors often cause us to feel distance from the Old Testament, On the one hand, God gave there scriptures to humanity through a process know as organic inspiration.  And on the other hand, God designed the Old Testament to fulfill it\'s purpose through a process known as divine accommodation. The concise answer is that the Old Testament occurs first and shows us how the world began and how the people of Israel came to be. Whereas, the New Testament comes after and documents how the world is saved through Jesus did for his people. As per the new Testament, it is the compliment of the Old. Without the New Testament, the Old is merely a collection of historical or religious writings. The old could not complete the work, for it was incomplete, being alone.', 5, '2023-11-23 04:12:39', '2024-03-03 05:48:27', NULL),
(3054, 844, 979, 'The old Testament reminds us of our need for a savior , in Christ Jesus. The prayers of our Old Testament heroes and the miracles God performed in their lives, the stories of redemption and the compassion of our great God on display, all point to the coming of Christ Jesus. The key teachings of Jesus are  to love God and love your neighbors. There is nothing more important than this. People also need to follow the teachings and laws of their church and state. Even though society have changed, the meanings of Jesus teachings remains the same. The old Testament contains doctrine and principles of timeless importance. It contains prophecies that are yet to be fulfilled. It teaches us about where we came from. And it teaches us about the Abrahamic covenant, which is still in effect today.', 5, '2023-11-23 04:19:32', '2024-03-03 05:48:27', NULL),
(3055, 844, 980, 'It is known by some members of the Christian community as the 400 Silent years. Because it was a span where no new prophets were raised and God revealed nothing new to the Jewish people. All though God spoke in many and various ways through the Prophets, the common goal was always the Son of God (Hebrew 1:1). And thus, both testaments point to Jesus Christ. More Inductively, the New Testament fulfills the promises of the Old Testament and completes all God began in the Law and the Prophets ( John 1:45) . The Old Testament books were written well before Jesus, and all of the New Testament books are written by roughly the end of the first century A.D. But the Bible as a whole was not officially compiled until the late fourth century.', 5, '2023-11-23 04:30:19', '2024-03-03 05:48:27', NULL),
(3056, 844, 981, 'In the section, Jesus fulfills and reinterprets the Old Covenant and in particular it\'s Ten Commandments, contrasting with what you have heard from others. For example, he advised turning the other cheek, and to love one\'s enemies, in contrast to taking an eye for eye. The Sermon on the Mountain includes many of Jesus\'s most famous teachings and sayings like the Beatitudes, salt of the earth. Light of the world., teaching on understanding God\'s law, murder, adultery, money, worry, the Lord\'s prayer, and more.  In the Sermon on the Mountain, Jesus explains to his followers what kinds of human lives are  blessed by God. The statements he made are known as the Beatitudes. According to Jesus, God gives his blessing to the meek - meaning humble people.', 5, '2023-11-23 04:38:38', '2024-03-03 05:48:27', NULL),
(3057, 845, 982, 'The Lord was the supreme authority, the maker and enforcer of every law. Mercy, justice, wrath, and fury were his to display as he found fit. Thus, in Genesis 2:16, as the Lord God places a command upon man, the authority with which he speaks immediately witness to his sovereignty. The sovereignty of God is the fact that he is the Lord over creation; as sovereign, he exercises his rule. This rule is exercised through God\'s authority as king, his control over all things, and his presence with his covenantal people and throughout his creation. The Old Testament presents great truths about God and humanity. In the first five books of the Bible (The Torah), God reaches out to humanity and introduce Himself. God introduce Himself as the Creator ( Gen 1-2) , the Savior of His people ( Exodus).', 5, '2023-11-24 04:06:56', '2024-03-03 05:47:57', NULL),
(3058, 845, 983, 'Biblical primeval history, Genesis 1-11, recounts the periods beginning with God\'s creation of the heavens and the earth and the forming of the first human beings through a series of largely dismal expressions of human sinfulness, and concluding with the dispersion of human beings over the face of the earth. The sin stories in the Book of Genesis address theological , cosmic, social , and ethical questions. These sin stories, the Fall of Man, Cain and Abel, and Noah and the Flood, and the Tower of Babel show the functions of myths and demonstrate man\'s likeliness to sin. The creation narrative is made up of two stories, roughly equivalent to the two first chapters of the Book of Genesis. Primeval history is the period before the invention of writing.', 5, '2023-11-24 04:13:08', '2024-03-03 05:47:57', NULL),
(3059, 845, 984, 'Biblical primeval history, Genesis 1-11, recounts the period beginning with God\'s creation of the heavens and the earth and the forming of the first human beings through a series of largely dismal expressions of human sinfulness, and concluding with the dispersion of the human beings over the face of the earth. With the coming of Christ, the kingdom begins not in the coronation of mighty king but in the birth of a crying baby. Yet as Jesus ministry begins in Mark, he announces, The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand. As per Mark 1:15 - Repent and believe in the Gospel.  The early chapters of Genesis, Genesis 1 through 11 are known as the Primary History.', 5, '2023-11-24 04:18:47', '2024-03-03 05:47:57', NULL),
(3060, 845, 985, 'Israel developed into a united kingdom under the leadership of King David. Who consolidated the various tribes under his single rule. The kingdom of Israel existed as an independent state until 722 BCE when it was conquered by the Neo-Assyrian Empire.  The kingdom of Judah existed as an independent state until 586 BCE when it was conquered by the Neo-Babylonian Empire.  From Abram a great, blessed kingdom people would be born - the nation know as Israel. The nation of Israel was God\'s chosen people. He ruled over them as king on earth, in a way that mirrored his rule in heaven. God ruled Israel and they represented the rule on earth.  Ancient Israel was a monarchy that was run as both a political and religious entity. The king ruled on behalf of Yahweh and was supposed to uphold the religious covenants.', 5, '2023-11-24 04:26:05', '2024-03-03 05:47:57', NULL),
(3061, 845, 986, 'Establishing the kingdom of God on earth has been the purpose of every gospel dispensation. The kingdom exists wherever there are members of the Church. The only way the kingdom of God is going to be manifest in this world before Christ comes is if we manifest it by the way we live as citizens of heaven and subjects of the King.  The kingdom of God is also present spiritual reality, such that those God calls in this age are figuratively translated into that kingdom ( Ephesians 2:6, Colossians 1:13) even as they live out their lives in, but not of, the world. As per the Gospel of Matthew, the Kingdome of heaven described as a process, a course of events, whereby God begins to govern or act as king or Lord, an action, therefore, by which God manifests his being-God is the world of men.', 5, '2023-11-24 04:33:12', '2024-03-03 05:47:57', NULL),
(3062, 846, 987, 'The archaeological finds of the temple in Tell Qasile help us understand that the events related in the Bible actually took place in space and time - that is , these events occurred in history. Archaeology can enlighten us regarding many aspects of daily life and how people lived in ancient times. One of the best preserved and most intriguing artifacts from biblical archaeology is not merely one scroll or one fragment. In the mid-20th-century, archaeologists found troves of scrolls in the Dead Sea caves and the Jordan Valley. Scrolls like these are exceedingly  rare find, and they are fragile. Archaeologists , though have been able to corroborate elements of the New Testament story of Jesus. While some disputed the existence of ancient Nazareth, his biblical childhood home town, archaeologists have unearthed a rock-hewn courtyard house along with tombs and cistern.', 7, '2023-11-25 04:07:36', '2024-03-03 05:47:32', NULL),
(3063, 846, 988, 'Covenants are one of the most important themes in the Bible. They are the key o God\'s redemptive plant to restore humanity to its divine calling. Starting in Genesis, God enters into one formal partnership after another with various human in order to rescue his world.  Each covenant established the basis of a relationship, conditions of that relationship, promises and conditions of the relationship and consequences if those conditions were unmet. One of the most familiar examples of the a covenant for us is marriage. A covenant is a scared agreement between God and His children. God sets specific conditions, and He promises to bless us we obey those conditions. Making and keeping covenants qualifies us to receive the blessings God has promised.', 6, '2023-11-25 04:14:45', '2024-03-03 05:47:32', NULL),
(3064, 846, 989, 'Covenants may be expressed in the affirmative or negative, just as a person can promise to do, or not to do, something. The word has come to have special meanings w hen used in connection with land. A covenant is a relationship between two partners who make binding promises to each other and work together to reach a common goal. They are often accompanied by oaths, signs, and ceremonies. Covenants define obligations and commitments, but they are different from a contract because they relational and personal. A covenant in the ancient world was similar to what we in the modern worlds would call a contract, treaty, or will. Each covenant established the basis of a relationship, conditions for that relationship, promises and conditions of the relationship and consequences if the conditions were unmet.', 5, '2023-11-25 04:20:02', '2024-03-03 05:47:32', NULL),
(3065, 846, 990, 'In Isaiah 54:9 - That covenant affirms God\'s commitment to creation after the flood. However, while the concept of a covenant may not appear until after the flood, the major divine-human covenants - Noahic, Abrahamic, Mosaic, Davidic, and the new covenant all support and advance God\'s crative and redemptive goal. Generally, there are two types of primary covenants included in agreements: affirmative covenants and negative covenants. In addition, a third type of covenant- financial covenants - is sometimes separated into its own category. Covenants are one of the most important themes in the Bible. They are the key to God\'s redemptive plan to restore humanity to it\'s divine calling. Starting in Genesis, God enters into one formal partnership after another with various humans in order to rescue his world.', 5, '2023-11-25 04:29:14', '2024-03-03 05:47:32', NULL),
(3066, 846, 991, 'Making and keeping covenants qualifies us to receive the blessings God has promised. When we choose not to keep covenants, we cannot receive the blessings. Our covenants guide the choices we make and help us resist temptation. The consequences of a breach of covenant generally include:\r\n1. Penalty or fee charged to the debtor by the creditor.\r\n2. An increase in the interest rate of the bond or loan.\r\n3. An increase in the collateral.\r\n4. Termination of the debt agreement.\r\n5. Waving the violation without importance consequences. \r\n\r\nHowever, there are five foundational covenants that God makes with Noah, Abraham, Moses, and David, before establishing the new covenant through Jesus.', 5, '2023-11-25 04:34:46', '2024-03-03 05:47:32', NULL),
(3067, 847, 992, 'A biblical canon is the collection of books that compromise the sacred scriptures or Bibles of Jews and Christians. The study of canon formation, that is, the study of the origin, transmission, and recognition of the books that compromise the Bibles of Judaism and Christianity, has expanded considerably. One who hears the Word of God but does not act accordingly is like the one who observes his bodily face in mirror but turns away and forgets what he looks like. In James\'s parable, we learn that scriptures is fundamentally practical. Canonical criticism, sometimes called canon criticism or the canonical approach is a way of interpreting the Bible that focuses on the text of the biblical canon itself as finished product.', 5, '2023-11-26 04:07:46', '2024-03-03 05:47:00', NULL),
(3068, 847, 993, 'Synchronic linguistics aims at describing a language at a specific point of time, often the present. In contrast, a diachronic approach, as in historical linguistics, considers the development and evolution of a language through history. The difference between synchronic and diachronic linguistics depends on the their focus of study. This is because the former looks at language at a given period of time while the latter looks at language through various periods in history. However, both branches are important in order to study a language properly. For an example, analyzing the word order in a sentence in Old English only would be a study in Synchronistic linguistics. If we looked at how world order changed in a sentence from Old English to Middle English, that would be a diachronic study.', 5, '2023-11-26 04:14:40', '2024-03-03 05:47:00', NULL),
(3069, 847, 994, 'The Three-fold purpose of Criticism: To help us solve a problem in the reading. To help us soft between and resolve conflicting readings. To enable us craft interpretative, yet scholarly judgements about literature. There are three basic components to literary analysis. They include comprehension, interpretation, and analysis. Comprehension is when the aspects of the analysis are identified. Interpretation and analysis are when the reader looks deeper into the aspects of the passage. perspective in literature is the narrator\'s outlook or view on the story\'s events, characters and world. The types of perspective are first-person, second-person, and third-person. First-person perspective has a narrator who is also the protagonist or the main character.', 5, '2023-11-26 04:21:28', '2024-03-03 05:47:00', NULL),
(3070, 847, 995, 'An understanding of the history of the New Testament, how it has been passed down to us, and who wrote it can increase our appreciation of this remarkable book of scripture and in turn give us great spiritual strength as we, like the early followers of Jesus Christ, face our own trails. It is important to place scriptures in proper context. The primary purpose of considering context, then, is to derive correct meaning and intent of the author. By relying on isolated passages without giving due consideration to their context, misunderstanding and misinterpretations may result. So the meaning of an event or word is affected by its place within the context of a whole culture and way of life.  In terms of biblical hermeneutics, this means that a text needs to be understood not only in its immediate context, but also in its wider context, which is the whole Bible.', 5, '2023-11-26 04:28:17', '2024-03-03 05:47:00', NULL),
(3071, 847, 996, 'The historical-grammatical method is a modern Christian hermeneutical method that strives to discover the biblical original intended meaning in the text. The write\'s intentions are :\r\n1. Begin your bible study with prayer.\r\n2. You don\'t have to start at the beginning. \r\n3. Choose a topic relevant to you.\r\n4.Get to know a character.\r\n5. Write down what you learn.\r\n6. Listen to the Bible online.\r\n7. Read or share with someone else.\r\n8. Look up what you don\'t understand.\r\n\r\nRead a passage in context. To really understand a passage, you have to read the surrounding chapter and verses to understand the context. Take it slow and ask questions. Remember that you\'re not going to understand it all in one day.', 5, '2023-11-26 04:35:34', '2024-03-03 05:47:00', NULL),
(3072, 849, 977, 'Two causes that create a sense of distance between us and the Old Testament is organic inspiration and divine accommodation. When we look at the Old Testament, God meticulously controlled the content of Scripture so that it is without error and may be rightly called the Word of God. The word of God, written is Scriptures was employed by God through individual personalities so that we may also rightly speak of the Scriptures. For example, in Scripture, we can see how Peter confirmed Paul\'s letters were written with wisdom that was given to Paul by God Himself. The spirit of God inspired Paul to write his letters so that they were not just mere human writing, but instead writings from God. If you look back into the Old Testament, this can also be portrayed when referring to the law of God also being referenced as the law of Moses. The reason behind that is because it came from God and Moses. \r\n\r\nThe second being divine accommodation, a term that describes that every time God reveals himself to humanity, he speaks to us in finite human terms. The reason behind that is God is transcendent and not easy to comprehend, incomprehensible even. The only times that finite humans can understand is when he reveals himself and he condescends in a way that we humans can then understand. For example, we know that the thoughts of God are so much more infinitely greater than our thoughts and exceed our limitations. He designed the Old Testament to be relevant in the day and age for the people at that time, the ancient Israelites, and this showed how God accommodated himself to the specific situations in the Old Testament.', 8, '2023-11-27 03:09:37', '2024-03-03 05:46:32', NULL),
(3073, 849, 978, 'Three kinds of distance we commonly find between us and the Old Testament are: theological distance, cultural distance, and personal distance. When referring to theological distance, we are thinking about the differences between the revelation that the Old Testament authors received and the fuller revelation that we have received in Christ. For example, when looking at the obedience of Abraham and when he was asked to sacrifice his son, God rewarded Abraham and blessed his willingness to do so. However, if we were to hear of that in today\'s day and age, we would not even begin to take such a theological claim seriously. This is relevant to the example of Moses and the great army from Egypt to the Promised land, how strange would it be to see a group of people or Christians marching through a wilderness to reach the promised land? \r\n\r\nCultural distance exists even today, and especially in the context of the Old Testament because human cultures are constantly changing. When looking back at traditions, or older customs, they seem outdated and odd. Technology was very different in the days of the Old Testament and looking at today\'s day and age, we can not survive without technology as it is deemed necessary for today\'s communication, work and functions of daily living. In the days of the Old Testament, we also see many examples of arranged marriage, and biblical figures of high importance practiced polygany, and various forms of slavery existed. \r\n\r\nThe personal difference is very real when referring to another form of distance with us and the Old Testament. For example, in the Old Testament, we can see how individuals had remarkable spiritual encounters and experiences unlike any that we have today. They had visions, dreams and even heard the audible voice of God and would be able to interact with heavenly beings. This is something that is very different on a personal distances and level with us and the Old Testament. Looking at today, many of us have never faced these situations and that causes a distance with identifying with people who have described such encounters in the Old Testament.', 9, '2023-11-27 03:18:59', '2024-03-03 05:46:32', NULL),
(3074, 849, 979, 'There are many things to learn from the teachings of Jesus and Paul when it comes to the relevance of the Old Testament in the modern world. The first thing to note is the negative comments that have been described. Some of the teachings of Jesus appear at a first glance to present itself with a negative view of the Old Testament, listed in Matthew 5:21-44. However, it is essential to note that Jesus did not contradict the teachings of the Old Testament, rather he confirmed and affirmed the authority set forth by refuting common misunderstandings of the teachings listed. \r\n\r\nThere were positive affirmations of how Jesus would stand in complete harmony with the teachings of the Old Testament and would fulfill them all. Jesus would consistently insist that his disciples affirm and follow each and every details that was listed within the Old Testament and not just adhere to only specific parts that were listed out. When comparing to Jesus, Paul would opposed the ways in how individuals would mishandle Scriptures in his day as well. \r\n\r\nWhen referring to negative comments, Paul would have negative comments about the Old Testament when he would speak of law and faith. Paul would also oppose the misuse of the Old Testament as a guide for meritorious legalistic religion. Paul also appealed to prove the New Testament principle that justification is by faith alone. For positive affirmations, Paul strongly affirmed and committed the Old Testament authority and relevance for the followers of Christ.', 9, '2023-11-27 03:26:25', '2024-03-03 05:46:32', NULL),
(3075, 849, 980, 'There are three significant developments that took place between the period of the Old Testament and the period of the New Testament. The first of these three is Epochal. Epochal is described as when God disclosed himself to his people progressively in major periods of time that correlate to God\'s covenants. Within each of these covenants, God would reveal more of himself each time, for example if you look at Adam and Noah, God revealed more to Noah than he did to Adam. This also applies as you continue to move on, and God revealed more to Abraham than he did to Noah. God chose to disclose himself to his people as time went on, more progressively. \r\n\r\nThe second is cultural, and we must take into account the similarities along with the differences between patterns in the Old Testament and our current world. We must define how present day has changed vastly from previous generations and times, and how different customs and traditions are today as well. The last is personal, we must ask ourselves what role do we play in society? By taking into account ancient Old Testament people and modern people, we can have a better understanding of how to apply the Old Testament teachings into our present day.', 8, '2023-11-27 03:35:21', '2024-03-03 05:46:32', NULL),
(3076, 849, 981, 'Jesus contrasted teachings with had been said or heard of, and would rather always speak of what was written of read. For example, some of these areas included topics of murder, adultery, divorce, oaths, revenge, and love for our enemies. Jesus contrasted with what was being taught by the religious leaders of the time and would offer a more in depth, profound interpretation. Jesus would highlight the heart of the law with an internal attitude and aimed to reveal the truth with intent and spirit of the law. \r\n\r\nJesus was always about correcting misinterpretations that would develop over time and his goal was to provide a more accurate description that focused on righteousness from the inside out. In today\'s world modern Christians would be tempted to make errors like the ones that Jesus refuted as today there are many false teachings and many of these teachings come from people with a large follower base. Whether this be on social media, or mega churches there is most time an underlying agenda that causes people to have a lack of biblical knowledge and depth. There is very little foundational knowledge that is being spread and it causing Christians and believers to have a lack of trust and foundation in their walk with God.', 8, '2023-11-27 03:44:01', '2024-03-03 05:46:32', NULL),
(3077, 850, 982, 'While there are many ways to describe God\'s complete and unchanging sovereignty, there are two specific directions to view this. The first is to acknowledge that God\'s rule is complete and unchanging, the second is God\'s rule is also incomplete and developing throughout history. For this specific topic, we are going to take a look at His complete and unchanging rule. \r\n\r\nIn Psalms 93, we find the psalmist is praising the Creator, declaring \"the Lord reigns.\" Over and over again, the Old Testament reiterates how God is sovereign over everything and is universal and unchanging. In the olden days, neighboring religions would teach of other gods and how they would compete for sovereignty and in most cases these gods would either rise or fall. However, these concepts were not a part of biblical faith, Yahweh, the God of Israel was the sole Creator and the only one who is unwavering over all of dominion and creation. \r\n\r\nThe kingship of God is understood as being universal and unchanging, and in this context all of creation will always be his kingdom. This belief extends as far back as to the ancient Israelites and the faith that they had of God\'s reign and kingdom being universal, unwavering and unchanging.', 8, '2023-11-29 01:38:50', '2024-03-03 05:46:01', NULL),
(3078, 850, 983, 'The primeval history first established the place of God\'s kingdom, and God acted as king during this time and exerted his royal authority to create, arrange and populate the world. This is where he would eventually extend his sovereign rule from heaven. The Bible describes the earth as being the place of God\'s kingdom and identifies the people for God\'s kingdom and it\'s purposes. The Bible also describes the early progress of God\'s kingdom. \r\n\r\nGod created the Sabbath day as a day of rest for himself on the throne to receive honor for having successfully proclaimed his authority and power over all of creation. God created two realms, one being heaven and the other being earth. Heaven being the place above and earth being the visible world or earth below. In the book of Genesis we can see that the earth was void, dark and without any form. This was a sharp contrast of the magnificence of heaven which is filled with the blinding glory of God. \r\n\r\nThe Old Testament refers to how God created light from darkness and filled the void through his royal wisdom and power. Once his work was done, God sits back on the throne in heaven and is delighted in the accomplishments of what was created. He was so pleased in this that he commanded the people of Israel to commemorate his accomplishment every Sabbath day.', 9, '2023-11-29 01:53:51', '2024-03-03 05:46:01', NULL),
(3079, 850, 984, 'The progress of God\'s kingdom during the primeval period can be described in different segments of time periods. The initial phase of progress for God was to define a place below the heavens, which is the visible earth. Once this was defined, the initial preparations took place. God began to orchestrate his glorious reign to extend from heaven to earth, described in Genesis 1:2-2:3. \r\n\r\nAfter six days of ordering and forming the world, God sat back on the throne to rest and enjoy the delight of his work, recognized as the Sabbath. Once God saw that all that he created was good, he adorned Eden as the centerpiece of Earth and placed within it a sacred garden for a specific purpose. To honor the special presence of the King of Heaven. Once the ongoing expansion was underway, God determined to fill the earth with people and humanity for a purpose that was too great for one man to accomplish on his very own (Gen 2:18). \r\n\r\nGod then appointed man and woman to be of his service, as priests, and as vice-regents or royal representatives. This is true as they were created in the like image or likeness of God. During this time in primeval history, God assured the human race that his grand vision and purpose for them would not fail. This was known as the progress phase.', 9, '2023-11-29 02:03:35', '2024-03-03 05:46:01', NULL),
(3080, 850, 985, 'Nearly every generation of Israel has rebelled against God and suffered under the judgement of God in one way or another. However no matter how many times they rebelled, God brought Israel to repentance and would renew them. However, during the end of the Old Testament, the sins of Israel became so great that God brought down a huge curse of exile towards Israel from the promised land. During this period, hundreds of years, Israel had no homeland, no temple, no priesthood, no one to help lead them in spreading God\'s kingdom. \r\n\r\nThe three stages of progress in God\'s kingdom during this period are covered over three main factors, the promise, exodus and conquest, and empire. When referring to promise, God made many promises about the future of the nation. Abraham is revealed as a symbolic figure, the father of many nations and his descendants would possess the land of Canaan. The second stage being Exodus and Conquest, we can see of how the Israelites praised God because he was guiding them towards his holy dwelling place. God redeemed Israel from Egypt and highlights the fact of God furthering Adam and Eve\'s original mission by expanding his kingdom on earth. Lastly the Empire stage, mistakenly by many interpreters, they believed God never intended for Israel to ever have a human king. However Israel was to have a human king in order to further the kingdom of God. As Christians, we know that Jesus is the perfect king who is righteous and comes from the line of David and will bring the kingdom of God to earth as it is in heaven.', 9, '2023-11-29 02:16:42', '2024-03-03 05:46:01', NULL),
(3081, 850, 986, 'The kind of kingdom that God is establishing is similar to what we read in Isaiah 66, where God\'s throne was in heaven, but the footstool of his throne where feet would touch earth, was the ark of the covenant in the temple. Psalm 72 reveals the ultimate goal for all the developments that took place in Israel and these stages of the kingdom were intended to fill the entire earth with God\'s glory. God\'s glorious presence would fill the whole world just as it fills heaven. \r\n\r\nWhen God\'s kingdom is manifested on earth as it is now manifested in heaven, life on earth will be like how Jesus and his first century apostles and prophets challenged widespread expectation. They would announce that Jesus, being the Messiah would bring the kingdom of God to earth over a long period of time. In Matthew 13:31-32, Jesus goes on to explain how the kingdom of God was going to come to earth. It would come small, like a mustard seed and over time it would grow and spread and become like a large mustard plant. The kingdom of God would extend over the entire earth and and unfold over three stages, inauguration, continuation, and consummation. This is where Christ will return and fulfill the plan of God to turn the whole world into his kingdom. He will defeat every enemy of God and the world will become the kingdom of God and of his Christ and from that time forth, he will reign forever and ever, everywhere.', 7, '2023-11-29 02:32:52', '2024-03-03 05:46:01', NULL),
(3082, 851, 987, 'Archeological discoveries can give us insight into biblical covenants by helping us grasp how the faithful Israelites were able to understand that there were fundamental connections between God\'s covenant and his kingdom. There are three types of royal documents from the ancient Near East which often appear within the context of biblical covenants: first, royal and parity treaties. These are considered as the covenants which kings of somewhat equal status were able to acknowledge their relationships of each other. \r\n\r\nRoyal parity treaties in regards to divine covenants portray that although men would consider themselves of equal status to mutually negotiate the terms of covenants between, that was never the case when it came to humans and God. God never negotiated with a human being purely because they were never seen as his equal. The royal grants were writings that revealed how ancient kings would bestow land or special status towards their faithful servants. \r\n\r\nMuch of the ancient royal documents that have allowed for continued understanding of biblical divine covenants are suzerain-vassal treaties. These ancient writings offer insight into how the authors of the bible were able to understand and grasp the relationship between God\'s kingdom and his covenants. These were also international agreements that greater established kings would have with lesser established kings and their nations. This would offer additional context and insight into the relationship between God\'s kingdom and his covenants. We are able to see within the Old Testament of how the Pharaohs of Egypt and the kings of Hittites, Babylonians, Assyrians, and even of Israel would conquer and establish their territories by controlling the weaker kingdoms.', 9, '2023-12-01 01:41:34', '2024-03-03 05:45:29', NULL),
(3083, 851, 988, 'The national covenants moved God\'s kingdom purposes forward starting with the covenant made with Abraham. Knowing that Abraham was the patriarch of every Israelite, God made his first covenant through Abraham. There is direct reference of this within Genesis 15, 17. Within the book of Genesis 15:18, the Lord made a covenant with Abram. God later confirmed this covenant that He made with Abraham. Through this covenant, God chose Israel to take the role of spreading His kingdom throughout the world. It was through this covenant that Abraham\'s descendants would spread to the ends of the earth. \r\n\r\nAfter the covenant with Abraham was made, God made his covenant with Moses. The national covenant with Moses was built on the covenant of election that God had made with Abraham. The renewal of this covenant can be seen with the second generation of the exodus in the book of Deuteronomy. This covenant stressed the requirements for what the nation needed at this time and the law of God. \r\n\r\nOnce Israel became established and a fully supported kingdom, God made His next covenant with David. It was built on and further continued the covenant He had with Moses. His promises to David were contingent on the faithfulness to Moses\' law. David and his house was required to walk within God\'s law and and God\'s covenant focused on the royal house of David. \r\n\r\nThese national covenants would then reveal how God would advance His kingdom by establishing the house of David as the line that would eventually rule over Israel forever. This would eventually lead to the house of David remaining in control over Israel and spreading God\'s kingdom to the ends of the earth.', 9, '2023-12-01 01:51:10', '2024-03-03 05:45:29', NULL),
(3084, 851, 989, 'Covenant dynamics expressed in the universal covenants can be seen in a few different examples. To begin we can see that with Adam\'s covenant, there was an emphasis stressed around specific foundational patterns at the commencement of the world. Moving onto Noah, we are able to note the stability of nature being emphasized. The Moses covenants was structured around God\'s law. Lastly the covenant with David was highlighting the specific chosen royal family which was a fulfillment being stressed. \r\n\r\nThese are all highlights of the covenants that were made, but the way that they are expressed in the universal covenants is the dynamics of living with God that is always involved. God exercised His right as king towards the implementation of covenant dynamics to ways that would fulfill His purposes. God would administer his universal covenants through the dynamics of human loyalty, divine benevolence, and the consequences for obedience and disobedience. \r\n\r\nThe consequences for obedience can be seen through the examples of how God told Adam and Eve that they would suffer the consequences of His curse if they were to disobey Him and eat from the forbidden fruit. Noah was blessed by God for his faithfulness but God did warn him against rebellion.', 8, '2023-12-01 02:00:19', '2024-03-03 05:45:29', NULL),
(3085, 851, 990, 'The divisions of humanity within all the types of divine covenants can be described as God\'s covenant engaging different groups of people in different ways. When looking at within covenants, the believers and non-believers are included within God\'s covenants. The universal covenants that were made with Adam and Noah included all the people to God, which included both the believers and unbelievers. The national covenants that were in Abraham, Moses and David also included all people. Specifically the people of Israel, whether they had received salvation or not. \r\n\r\nThe difference is that the Old Testament prophecies indicated that with the new covenant, only the true believers would be included. This is emphasized within 1 John 2:19 where it states that until the return of Christ, believers and unbelievers are counted among God\'s people. Now while all of humanity was included in the universal covenants, Gentile nations were excluded from Israel\'s national covenants. The reason being that during that time there were three different kinds of people in the world. The first consisted of true believers who were in Israel\'s covenants, the second were the unbelievers who were in Israel\'s covenant and lastly there were the Gentiles who were excluded from Israel\'s covenants. \r\n\r\nThe divine benevolence was shown to all people through God\'s divine grace and outside the covenants, unbelievers have a basic understanding of their obligations to God. While unbelievers can experience temporary blessings and curses, their eternal judgment will become clear to them at the final judgement.', 9, '2023-12-01 02:09:28', '2024-03-03 05:45:29', NULL),
(3086, 851, 991, 'All biblical covenants contained consequences of blessing and cursing. There are several ways that I can see this dynamic at work within the modern world, our churches and within my life. To begin let\'s take a look at the modern world for example. We live within a country that was formed on the foundation of Christian beliefs and principles. Even on our monetary notes, the depiction that we \'trust in God\' was emphasized with such reverence at one point in time. We also would have prayers in schools, and the Ten Commandments would sit in public buildings and federal buildings. However as time as progressed, the country has become very liberal to be \'inclusive\' to all genders that they believe that exist, races, ethnicities, religions and beliefs. Although all of these people which are believers and unbelievers are children of God, there will be a judgement day where the believers will be separated from the unbelievers and judgement will fall upon them. \r\n\r\nWithin our churches, the same methodology applies, to where we have succumbed to the Woke Culture and trying to be more inclusive to modern day practices and beliefs to gain more attendance within the church and popularity. However the church needs to call sin, as sin and teach the true meaning of repentance and forgiveness of sins. The dynamics of church have to draw more people to faith. \r\n\r\nFinally, towards my own conviction, I try my best to practice leading a life of separation knowing the calling upon my life. God\'s judgement matters more than anything and He has held me to a high standard of obedience. Knowing what I know today, I can not go back to any of lifestyle practices during my younger years, and that is the ultimate goal, to bring as many people to heaven with me as I possibly can.', 9, '2023-12-01 02:15:59', '2024-03-03 05:45:29', NULL),
(3087, 852, 992, 'The use of Canon as a mirror is justified by the character of Scripture and biblical examples through several different areas. To begin with the character of Scripture, the Old Testament is composed of smaller elements that came together to form larger sections which then eventually formed entire books. Within each of these levels and section, the contribution towards what the Old Testament book offers us was formed. Thematic analysis recognizes often draws attention to the minor topics of secondary areas. Genesis 1:1 talks about creation itself, however if there are so many topics that appear in verses like Genesis 1:1, imagine how many more topics are present in larger passages. \r\n\r\nWhen looking at biblical examples, authoritative characters and biblical writes would frequently draw attention to minor aspects of the Old Testament passages. This is demonstrated within the book of Hebrews 11 where the referenced examples of Jephthah and Samson appear. Both of these men were able accomplish victory over the enemies of God, when they had turned their faith towards God. Therefore the writer of Hebrews went ahead and drew out the positive accomplishments of these two men and looked for examples of faith within the Old Testament. The writer of Hebrews stressed minor themes that were important, but remained faithful to the book of Judges.', 9, '2023-12-01 14:09:28', '2024-03-03 05:44:58', NULL),
(3088, 852, 993, 'Synchronic snapshots function by emphasizing a specific events that occurred within a set period of time. When looking at scripture, biblical theologians would create snapshots by focusing in on a period of biblical history and then putting together a summary of the complex work of God\'s acts and words which took place during that period of time. There are several ways and methods to create these synchronic snapshots within historical periods, such as the primeval history, the history of the nation of Israel, and the future history of the New Testament. During the covenantal periods of time, synchronic snapshots are viewed through the period of universal covenants, national covenants, and the new covenant. This is specifically useful because it allows us as the reader to obtain specific snapshots of this time in biblical history and help identify formative events which took place. \r\n\r\nDiachronic traces are referenced as events or ways that something has developed over time. It connects one thought with one another over a period of time from one time to the next. We know that there are different affinities or periods of time that span over thousands of years. However with divine revelations, these ages did not contradict one another. An example of this is the covenant made with Abram, although at this specific point in time, humanity had not sinned, God expected humans to continue to strive for dominion over the earth even after the fall into sin. God continued to maintain his command to spread his kingdom to the ends of the earth. This was established as God had appointed humanity as his royal priests and instructed them to take dominion over the entire earth (Gen 1:28).', 9, '2023-12-01 14:19:35', '2024-03-03 05:44:58', NULL),
(3089, 852, 994, 'The value of a threefold focus on the writer, audience, and document in literary analysis must be studied when considering the importance of the writers of the Old Testament Canon. Beginning with the writer, it is crucial to note that we may not know everything that we would like to know about them, but we can still grasp many things that will still enable us to understand their writings. For example the authors of 1 and 2 Kings and 1 and 2 Chronicles are not known to us. We also do not know much about their psychological tendencies, so we are forced to rely on the interpretation of the book and a bit of speculation. At the same time, we are able to obtain and derive valuable information about both of these authors from the Old Testament and see that the author of Kings wrote the book during the exile of Israel in Babylon. By knowing some of the facts about the authors help us understand the differences in the their theological views and motives. We also know the authors were among the educated elite of Israel.\r\n\r\nNext is the audience, we know of their general location from the Old Testament and the major events that they had experienced. We are also able to determine that most of the groups were faithful and some were unfaithful to the covenant responsibilities before God. When looking at the book of Kings, we know that the audience was still in exile. When looking at the book of Chronicles, they audience that had just returned to the Promised Land is addressed. Simply understanding the context of the audience helps us better understand that repentance was required before Israel could return to the promised land and align us with the literary portraits of these two books. \r\n\r\nDocument in literary analysis are the full authoritative word of God, therefore special attention is required to the dangers and benefits of the document itself. Between the vocabulary grammar, figures of speech and structure, outline, context, genre and so on, these all contribute a great amount to understanding the original meaning and application set forth.', 9, '2023-12-01 14:31:15', '2024-03-03 05:44:58', NULL),
(3090, 852, 995, 'Understanding the diachronic trace of biblical events helps enrich my understanding of Scripture by allowing me to look at the series of events through a lens over time. Rather than looking at the scripture as an isolated series of books and themes, it is important to understand how the promises of God and covenants carried on from one period of time to the next. This can be seen beginning with Adam and how God had ordained for Adam and Eve to rule over Eden and be kings of the Earth. However due to their fall and disobedience we can see over time how God dealt with sin and trying to get His people to return to him. Knowing that Abram had a covenant with God and God has promised him to the father of many nations and for his descendants to inhabit all of the earth, that lineage continues down to the life of David and eventually Jesus. This is where the larger picture of scripture comes together. If the reader were to just look at the Israelites entering the promised land, we would not understand the promises made before that and the covenants in place. There is a certain significance to that promise and that goes back to how God had seen his people, all of humanity as his royal priests and wanted them to have dominion over earth. He told Adam and Eve to be fruitful and that they would have dominion over every living thing.', 9, '2023-12-01 14:35:54', '2024-03-03 05:44:58', NULL),
(3091, 852, 996, 'By knowing the writer\'s intentions, I can help determine two things to better understand Scripture. The intent and the context of the writing. The intent of the writer was to get a point or a message across to the audience. They were taking it upon themselves to document and gather the context of the information that they had witnessed, seen or were told to be able to share that with an intended audience. Whether the writer was writing an interpretation of the blessing of God or the curse of God, there is a reason that the writer took the responsibility to ensure this was document. It was to benefit the audience and provide a certain level of influence. Something that can be looked back to and referenced to see how Israel for example was referred to in Chronicles and the steps that were required to take in order to return to the Promised Land. The writer focused on the blessings that would be received for obedience and the curses that would be received for disobedience as they rebuilt their nation. Rather than question why one was blessed versus cursed, the write had an intention to allow the audience to be able to share this throughout generations to come. \r\n\r\nNext the context, knowing that there is some set of beliefs, and hopes that the author has, they are looking to influence their original readers and have focus more on the context versus themselves. For example the writer does not reference themselves, their bias views or opinions, rather the emphasis falls upon the need for Israel to repent, and the author of Kings emphasizes God\'s justice in sending his people to exile. This allows us to know that the author has the context to what is happening and what will continue to happen unless the children of God turn from their ways and turn back to God.', 9, '2023-12-01 14:42:18', '2024-03-03 05:44:58', NULL),
(3092, 853, 927, 'Romans 11:33-36 is a masterful presentation of the doctrine of God that affirms such things as His wisdom, knowledge, inscrutable judgements, and more. Because the doctrine of God is foundational to all of our theology. One is essence , three in person is the most concise definition of the doctrine of the Trinity. The Christian faith is not unitarian, confessing that one divine nature is possessed only by a single person or a single acting subject. Instead, the Christian faith says that three distinct persons are the one divine nature in its entirety. The Father possesses all that makes God who He is; the Son possesses all that makes God who He is: and the Spirit possesses all that makes God who He is. Many biblical passages teach the Trinitarian existence of God. Among the most famous of these is John 1:1-18, which says that the Word-the Son- is both identical to the Father in some way and distinct from Him in another.', 6, '2023-12-29 03:16:00', '2024-03-03 05:44:31', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3093, 853, 928, 'According to Latin Trinitarians, God, the Trinity , is an individual rather than a community of individuals sharing the same divine nature and each Person of the Trinity is that individual. God the Father: revealed by the Old Testament to be Creator, Lord, Father and Judge. God the son: who had lived on earth amongst human beings. God the Holy Spirit; who filled them with new life and power. As per the Bible, a person is both body and soul, or more accurately, body-soul. The Old Testament supports this holistic view of persons who are not segmented into parts known as body, soul, and spirit. Genesis 1:20 uses the Hebrew expression, living soul for moving creature that has life. The revelation of the Trinity is accomplished by the coming of God himself into human history; God so loved the world that he gave his only Son (John 3:16); God\'s love has been poured into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who has been given to us (Romans 5:5)', 7, '2023-12-29 03:27:02', '2024-03-03 05:44:31', NULL),
(3094, 853, 929, 'In monotheistic theology, the doctrine of Divine Simplicity says that God is simple (without parts). This is the idea that God does not exist in parts but in one unified entity, with no distinct attributes: that is God\'s existence is identical to God\'s essence. One such affirmation appears in James 1:5 where God is described as one who gives motives. Simplicity, divine, and human, is a central theme of the book of James. The doctrine of divine attributes, then, is set out as a reflection on Holy scripture; the One God as omnipresent, omnipotent, and omniscient, and all these as expressions of the Love who is God. Simplicity creates margins and spaces and openness in our lives. It honors the resources of our small planet.', 5, '2023-12-29 03:38:55', '2024-03-03 05:44:31', NULL),
(3095, 853, 930, 'In ways similar to the way of orthodox Christianity views the preexistence of Christ, the belief is that the Christ that was born on this Earth is same Son of God or Word who existed before this world. However, He is seen as having been created by God the Father. In the four Gospels, there is ample evidence that Jesus was fully human since He was born to a human mother ( Matthew 1:25), He experienced hunger (Matthew 21:18) and Thirst ( John 19:28), Jesus also experienced temptation (Matthew 4:1) pain and suffering (Matthew 16:21), and He died (Matthew 27:50) . Jesus name in Hebrew was Yeshua . Since the world was created out of nothing, nothing prevailed. Therefore God was idling, just existing, perhaps contemplating creation.', 5, '2023-12-29 03:46:45', '2024-03-03 05:44:31', NULL),
(3096, 853, 931, 'The Trinitarian formulas of the New Testament helps us to understand that the Holy Spirit is fully divine and fully personal. Baptism according to the Trinitarian fomula is seen as being a basis for Christian ecumenism, the concept of working towards the eventual unity of Christians. The Trinity has a very significant application to prayer. The General pattern of prayer in the Bible is to pray to the Father through the Son and in the Holy Spirit ( Ephesians 2:18). Our fellowship with God should be enhanced by consciously knowing that we are relating to a tri-personal God. The Trinity can be a model for our relationships on earth. Just as Father, Son, and Spirit are joined, so we are joined together in our families.', 5, '2023-12-29 03:54:39', '2024-03-03 05:44:31', NULL),
(3097, 854, 932, 'Although the word Trinity does not appear in Scripture, the essence of the doctrine is revealed in both the Old and New Testaments. In the Old Testament, God\'s triunity is alluded to rather than explicitly revealed. This is due, in part, to the preparatory nature of Old Testament revelation. Furthermore, Jesus asserts the equality of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit in the baptismal formula of Matthew 28:19. Early Christians thought Jesus first referred to the Trinity in Matthew 28:19, by stating, \" in the name of the Father, of the Son, and the Holy Spirit. In John 10:30, Jesus said, I and my Father are one. The belief in the Holy Trinity emerged during the life and shortly after the death and resurrection of Jesus.', 7, '2023-12-30 03:08:03', '2024-03-03 05:43:35', NULL),
(3098, 854, 933, 'Rev 5:1 Then I saw in the right hand of him who sat on the throne a scroll with writing on both sides and sealed with seven seals. We can\'t go past this verse too quickly because it holds the key to what happens in the rest of the chapter. We see that the one who sits on the throne is holding something special in His right hand. it is a scroll. A slightly odd scroll for it has writing on both the back and front and is sealed with seven different seals.\r\nThe one on the throne: This pictures God the Father who is upon His throne. He holds this scroll in HIs right hand but, as we shall see, is looking for One with the right to open it. \r\nThe scroll: Being in the Father\'s right hand, this scroll is obviously very important. The Old Testament, in Jeremiah 32:6-15', 5, '2023-12-30 03:21:31', '2024-03-03 05:43:35', NULL),
(3099, 854, 934, 'In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. The Word, a translation of the Greek, is widely interpreted as referring to Jesus, as indicated in other verses later in the same chapter. For example, the Word became flesh, and dwelt among us ( John 1:14) . John 1:1-8 is a poetic introduction of Jesus Christ. Jesus is referred to using the Greek work logos, meaning \"The word\" This passage clearly described Jesus as identical to God, and co-creator with God. John 1:1, establishes that the universe had a beginning, contrary to the idea that it is eternal, it also states that God has existed from the beginning. Later verses will show that God, in fact,  created all that exists.', 5, '2023-12-30 03:29:18', '2024-03-03 05:43:35', NULL),
(3100, 854, 935, 'Romans 8:27 - \" And He who searches our hearts knows the mind of the Spirit because the Spirit interceded for the saints in accordance with God\'s will ( John 14:26) But the counselor, the Holy Spirit, whom My Father will send in My name, will teach you all things and will remind you of everything I have said to you. The Holy Spirit is divine person equal in divinity and power to God the Father and God the son. He is not a power to get hold of and use as we will but He is a loving, sensitive and Divine Person who wants to get hold of us and use us according to God\'s will. He is certainly not merely an impersonal force for good in the world.', 5, '2023-12-30 03:37:24', '2024-03-03 05:43:35', NULL),
(3101, 854, 936, 'The reason Jesus needed to be both God and man is that God designed humans for relationship with Himself, but ever since Adam\'s fall, our sin has corrupted us and broken our relationship with God. In His infinite love, God still wants a relationship with us; He wants us to be reconciled to Him. Jesus is important to us because His atonement, teaching , hope, peace, and example , He helps us change over lives, face our trails, and move forward with faith as we journey back to Him and His Father. Through Jesus incarnation, God began the process of salvation from sin, making it possible for humans to have a full relationship with him and go to Heaven. As Jesus was the son of God, most Christians believe he could not have been conceived and born like a normal human.', 5, '2023-12-30 03:45:01', '2024-03-03 05:43:35', NULL),
(3102, 856, 897, 'There were several significant events which preceded the arrest of Paul. While Paul was near the end of his third missionary journey, he went to Jerusalem. The Holy Spirit warned Paul on several occasions that he would be imprisoned once he arrived in Jerusalem. Not only did the Holy Spirit warn Paul, the Holy Spirit worked among believers who would prophecy to Paul regarding his imprisonment. \r\n\r\nAgabus, a prophet went to the extent of binding his own hands and feet as a sign of the prophecy and warning that Paul would be arrested and bound if he were to continue to Jerusalem. As the Bible indicates, there were several warnings and prophecies provided to Paul preceding his arrest, but through it all Paul remained strong as he knew this was within the plan of God.', 7, '2024-02-01 01:46:35', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3103, 856, 898, 'Paul entered Jerusalem with the great hope that the funds he had brought along with him would be received well by the Jewish Christians. Unfortunately, rumors began to float that Paul was preaching to the Jewish Christians to disregard their traditional Jewish practices (example: circumcision). While these rumors were false, and contrary to the rumors, Paul actually followed Jewish tradition while in the Jewish community. At the same time, Paul did not hesitate to behave like a Gentile while with the Gentiles. \r\n\r\nPaul knew that they actions he performed would affect the way that Jewish Christians perceived him along with Gentile Christians. As Paul was near the end of his purification week, he was recognized by some Jews. They were outraged and wrongly assumed Trophimus had also entered the court and were extremely upset. The angry crowd ended up chaining Paul and arresting him into custody.', 7, '2024-02-01 01:54:25', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3104, 856, 899, 'During Paul\'s imprisonment under Roman rule, he was transported by ship heading for Asia minor. As the ship first landed in Sidon, Paul was allowed to visit some friends of his. From Sidon, the ship said to a port in Myra. From Myra, the ship sailed for Italy and during this voyage they experienced rough sailing. Eventually after many shipwrecks and troublesome voyages Paul arrived in Rome in A.D. 60 and was placed under house arrest for two years. \r\n\r\nDuring this time, he was allowed to have guests and allowed to teach them freely. Through Paul\'s preaching, some of the Roman Jews were converted to Christianity while others rejected his claims about Jesus. For over two years, Paul was able to boldly welcome people to his house and preach the word of Jesus Christ. Although he faced many hardships along the way, he was able to make an impact without any obstacle or hinderance regardless of his imprisonment, in the capital city of the most empire in the world during his day.', 8, '2024-02-01 02:01:36', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3105, 856, 900, 'Paul\'s imprisonment revealed important insights about God and even about his own ministry through the awareness of suffering. For example, Paul knew he would be imprisoned and even be put to death, and he was preparing himself for it and willing to be martyred. He also was able to have the insight of the awareness of purpose. He knew his purpose in suffering ahead of time, and that if God were to allow him to go through suffering, he would also plan to use this suffering for a greater purpose. \r\n\r\nHe was convinced that his ministry would including be imprisoned and testifying to the gospel and this would be an important talk to convert individuals. The last awareness was about blessings. Paul was very much aware of God\'s blessings over his ministry at that time. He was able to receive and interpret visions to protect the lives of the people that were on the ship with him that crashed on the reef. he also was aware of the blessing that came with healing the sick on Malta and all those who came to see him.', 8, '2024-02-01 02:05:34', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3106, 856, 901, 'Three things that we can learn from the book of Acts about Paul\'s ministry during imprisonment was that he knew his responsibility and obligation to preach the gospel regardless of his circumstance. He knew that he would go through suffering and situations that would not be favorable, and he asked the Ephesians to pray for him so that he would receive the strength needed to fulfill his responsibility. \r\n\r\nThe second thing we can learn about was that Paul was in constant prayer for the churches. He was probably able spend more time in prayer due to being imprisoned, since he had no other distractions, jobs to do and keep him busy from praying. Paul would continually pray for the Ephesians and believed that prayer was indeed powerful and hoped that God would honor his prayers. \r\n\r\nLastly, suffering was the key piece we can learn from Paul\'s ministry during imprisonment. He was suffering on behalf of others and while suffering is typically considered a hardship and not ministry, in this sense it was minstry and as the goal and product of the suffering is a direct advancement of God\'s kingdom through the promotion of the gospel. His suffering did not go in vain, rather it blessed the church and increased the glory to God\'s people.', 9, '2024-02-01 02:10:26', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3107, 856, 902, 'Three ways which Paul developed the doctrinal theme of Christ\'s kingship over creation include sovereignty, honor, and determination. When referring to sovereignty, we are talking about his power and authority and according to Paul when Jesus went up to heaven, God the Father vested him with this type of sovereignty over creation. The Father had granted that to Christ in this manner and with absolute power over everything, including heaven and earth. \r\n\r\nChrist is honored as he holds the highest position of authority, and he is perfect holy, and worthy. Not only is Jesus the rule and creator of everything, but he also established the lower offices that other rulers hold, both in the spiritual and earthly realm. Paul speaks of this in relation to the return of Christ to earth to consummate his kingdom. \r\n\r\nWhen referring to determination, Paul insisted that the future inheritance is guaranteed and that God has promised himself and will not change his mind. While the age to come will have some periods of overlap, Paul knew Christ\'s plan for all of creation and was determined Christ would consummate his kingdom.', 8, '2024-02-01 02:16:58', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3108, 856, 903, 'Some of the implications of the believer\'s union with Christ is that the Christian life can be hard and it can include great suffering. However, through our union with Christ, we are united to Christ\'s death, united to Christ in his resurrection and life, united to Christ to his ascension and kingship, and we are united in being seated with Christ in heavenly places. \r\n\r\nThe main indication regarding this doctrine was to ensure confidence within believers that while they may suffer for some time, they are not suffering alone and their suffering is not in vain. Paul was confident and determined that while we may suffer for some time, Christ is suffering and empathizing with the one suffering for the sake of the gospel.', 7, '2024-02-01 02:21:19', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3109, 856, 904, 'The main theological truth that underlies the theological unity of Paul\'s prison epistles is the multifactored doctrine of the kingship of Christ over all of creation and believers to live ethically. As relating to the themes of our union with Christ and ethical living, we can use the life of Paul as the perfect example. He was on his way to Jerusalem, where he was warned by the Holy Spirit and people that he will suffer, but knowing his calling and the ultimate goal regarding his union with Christ, he pressed onward willing to be persecuted, and even put to death. When we look at the life of Jesus and how we gave his life for everyone, Paul followed the example Christ set and lived an ethical life while others created false rumors about him and imprisoned. He was willing to suffer for the gospel and the main truth is that through pain and suffering the kingdom was able to be advanced.', 7, '2024-02-01 02:25:34', '2024-03-03 05:42:51', NULL),
(3110, 857, 905, 'Paul had a very unique relationship with the Colossian church. The reason being, he never actually visited the Colosse church, however he had a deep extent of care for the them. It is possible that he may have visited Colosse prior to the establishment of the church and it is also possible that he may have never visited Colosse. However, the fact is that he did not know more of the believers personally.\r\n\r\nPaul had an indirect relationship with the Colossians though his friendship with Epaphras, Philemon, Onesiumus, and Tychicus. And while Paul did not visit the church, letters were exchanged back and forth to one another. Paul would pray for the church and specifically asked for blessings that he knew would benefit them the most. On various individual levels, Paul had friends who were from Colosse, these were individuals who labored alongside Paul in the ministry. \r\n\r\nPhilemon was a close friend of Pauls, and Epaphras was also another close fellow worker and fellow prisoner that was with Paul in prison as Paul was writing his letters to the Colossian church. Onesimus who was once a slave, ended up feeling away from Philemon and later ministering to Paul in prison. So while Paul had a minimal direct relationship with the Colossian church, he had strong feelings for the church and his acquaintances.', 8, '2024-02-02 14:08:01', '2024-03-03 05:42:11', NULL),
(3111, 857, 906, 'There were problems in Colosse, for example when it came to Greek philosophy, many of the beliefs and practiced based in Greek religion were advocated by false teachers in Colosse and included elements of asceticism. Their beliefs were based on the principles that aligned to the world. The Church had false teachers who would attempt to mix the church\'s teachings with Greek traditions. This resulted in false teachers incorporating many practices based on Jewish law.\r\n\r\nWith the Jewish law, it is true that Paul upheld Mosaic law, and for the sake of the gospel would accept and participate in Jewish practices as well. However, the false teachers were using the Jewish teachings in corrupt ways. The false teachers corrupted the laws, using idolatrous worship and pagan asceticism. They also advocated for a form of Christian circumcision and Paul would teach that once you were baptized, the need for circumcision was no longer required. \r\n\r\nDuring the time of the Colossian church, Paul wrote about the worship of angels, second he addressed the matters of rulers and authorities, and lastly he addressed the problems that dealt with the basic principles of the world. These false teachers would claim to have received false visions from angels and would go about encouraging Christians to complete the required rituals in order to receive similar visions. It is possible that these false teachers did receive visions, however instead of receiving the visions from God or God\'s holy angels, it may have been from demons.', 8, '2024-02-02 14:17:38', '2024-03-03 05:42:11', NULL),
(3112, 857, 907, 'Only after knowing the background of the Colossians can we truly understand the reason for Paul writing this letter. The supremacy of Christianity among all other religions of false teachings is clearly evident as Paul instructs the church of Colosse to share and instruct the Laodiceans as the intention of the can be applicable to different audiences. The Colossian church was swayed quite a bit through the teachings of false teachers and knowing this, Paul was able to provide the important aspects of the supremacy of Christ. He was able able to refute the teachings of the false prophets about how to worship, and the image of of God and the ultimate revelation of God. \r\n\r\nPaul\'s authority was far superior than that of the false teachers, in Paul\'s younger days, he was a zealous persecutor of the church and as time went on, the Lord Jesus appeared before him and he was converted. Though Paul was converted, he went through suffering and his suffering benefited the church by providing a powerful witness to the gospel. Through his walk with God, he would have powerful and significant revelations from God, and they were not mere human inventions like the teachings of the false teachers.', 8, '2024-02-02 14:26:47', '2024-03-03 05:42:11', NULL),
(3113, 857, 908, 'Paul demonstrated to the Colossians that Christ was supreme in several different ways, the supremacy of Christ, the supremacy of Christ\'s Ministers, the supremacy of salvation in Christ, and supremacy of Christian living.\r\n\r\nPaul demonstrated and preached the supremacy of Christ as the lord and king and the superiority of Christ\'s ministers. Through Christian living, there is an incomparable value of salvation in Christ that allows for victory over sin. During each point of explaining that Christ was supreme, Paul was able to make it clear that what the false teachers would promise, only Christ could deliver. \r\n\r\nThrough demonstration of how one lived, Paul lived a life by example. The false teachers were not living an ethical life, and they were not inwardly renewed by God as Paul had been. The false teachers were not even true believers to begin with and they did not follow the standards of God.', 7, '2024-02-02 14:31:58', '2024-03-03 05:42:11', NULL),
(3114, 857, 909, 'Christ\'s ministers were supreme over false teachers in Colosse as Christ was superior to the false gods. Paul\'s argument consisted of five main ideas, the reconciliation accomplished through the Christian gospel, Paul own altruism, Paul\'s divine commission, the revelation provided by the gospel, and the empowerment of Christ\'s ministers. \r\n\r\nThe ministers of Christ are superior over false teachers as they preach a gospel that reconciles believers to God. The false teachers would encourage individuals to placate demons, and in reality no reconciliation ever took place as their gospel had no power to save. The reconciliation that we believe in, provided people with the encouragement that they were already forgiven and stood before God in righteousness.\r\n\r\nWhile the false teachers relied on traditions and wisdom passed down from human hands, Paul was receiving revelations for three years in the desert of Arabia and in Damascus from God. The false teachers relied on ideas that idolatrous human beings invited, where as Paul had been granted authority to speak for God and teach the church.', 8, '2024-02-02 14:37:43', '2024-03-03 05:42:11', NULL),
(3115, 858, 910, 'Having looked at the style of Paul\'s teachings and the way he displays language and doctrine show a close correlation with Colossians. This would suggest that there is a possibility that Paul wrote both at the same time. According to Acts 19-21, we can see that Paul had lived in Ephesus for about two years and maintained close ties with the elders. \r\n\r\nThe primary audience of indicates that the church in Ephesus is the target audience as it was the capital city of the Roman province of Asia. Several of the early fathers of the church had declared that Paul had sent this letter to the Ephesians. The secondary audience is the churches of Lycus valley. A significant portion of these individuals were those he had not seen firsthand and he did not contain any personal reference. Which inclines us to see that the churches of the Lycus Valley and those of the Ephesians and Colossians all faced similar problems.', 7, '2024-02-06 01:46:06', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3116, 858, 911, 'Paul wrote the letter to the Ephesians because there were several problems that were being faced by the church at this time. Rather than deal with or face these problems individually, his strategy was to deal with all of the problems at once. Paul would commonly speak of Christ\'s work in terms of how God would pour out his blessings and curse. As Paul also believed in the manifestation of the kingdom of God, we can see that even while Paul was in prison within Rom, he wrote to the Ephesians preaching the kingdom of God. He was prepared to share the gospel or good news with topics around forgiveness of sins. He drew attention in Ephesians to citizenship and how God\'s people were part of God\'s kingdom, and the chief goal of Israel was to expand it\'s dominion over Earth. With that would come problems, and Paul\'s effort was to ensure that the church was ready to turn to the challenges that Paul addressed.', 8, '2024-02-06 01:50:59', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3117, 858, 912, 'Knowing the background of Paul\'s letter to the Ephesians helps me better understand the letter he wrote as he was looking at the broader view of the church declaring the gospel and addressing the aspects of spiritual warfare that is to be expected. Had he focused on a single issue, than other churches would likely not adhere to his writings or teachings in this book, but rather because he wanted to address common issues among churches with the context that Christians who engage in spiritual warfare are implied citizenship in the kingdom of God. \r\n\r\nBased on the context of citizenship, Paul displayed that if we choose to obey and be faithful to the king, we may inherit the blessings of the covenants including forgiveness of sins and obtain eternal life. Whereas if we reject the king, and the salvation that is offered, the person no longer inherits the kingdom of God. Knowing that these topics were all written without Paul having a direct connection to the people firsthand but rather in other means, allows us to see the revelation that Paul had throughout his journey before getting to this specific place.', 8, '2024-02-06 01:55:09', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3118, 858, 913, 'Paul\'s teaching about praise and prayer within the overall structure of the letter is worthy of attention. To begin, Paul honored God for his sovereign rule and praised him for predestining certain individuals to be his special people. Paul continues to praise God for his sovereign rule over all of creation. Paul then continues to praise God\'s benevolence towards his people and praised God for our inheritance in Christ. This is really important as inheritance rights only belong to the citizens of the kingdom. \r\n\r\nRegarding prayer, Paul\'s prayers repeat all the same elements. It includes strong Trinitarian theology honoring the work of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. In reference to Christ\'s glorious inheritance, there is hope called for the believers. Paul is able to speak of this hope as he continues to teach within the body of his letter. Christ shared his inheritance with us, so that inheritance is for us too.', 7, '2024-02-06 02:01:30', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3119, 858, 914, 'Paul\'s teaching regarding citizenship, administration and code for living within the kingdom of God were emphasized within Ephesians. His teachings on citizenship were divided into three sections, first the focus on the fact that fallen human beings were born into the kingdom of darkness and are enemies of God by nature. The second section speaks of how God grants us citizenship in his kingdom by transferring us from darkness into light. The third section speaks of our citizenship within the kingdom of light. \r\n\r\nReferring to administration, Paul was aware that false teachers would arise and create problems within the church, so he assigned the elders the right to condemn false teachers. The apostles possessed special grace from God that allowed them special revelation in their ministry that taught them infallible truth. These elders are accountable to God and to one another. God had appointed Paul to be his official representative on Earth, and this apostleship made Paul\'s word authoritative. \r\n\r\nLastly the code for living contained many different instructions on Christian behavior. Paul emphasized how the roles of positions within leadership, influence and authority all work together for the good of all. Christ divided his gifts in what that allow the citizens of the kingdom to serve one another. The kingdom of God is to be morally as pure as possible, and reflect the character of it\'s king. Therefore God blesses and honors moral purity, and teaches us that by refraining from sin and doing good works, the citizens increase their blessedness and inheritance.', 9, '2024-02-06 02:08:48', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3120, 858, 915, 'Christians should work to build the kingdom of God because as Christ has given us the opportunity of this inheritance, we must not take it for granted. A great king has provided the opportunity to be a part of his heavenly kingdom and to be citizens of this kingdom. However living an immoral sinful life would not qualify us to receive this inheritance. In terms of why we should work to build this kingdom, there are many reasons, but one of the major themes would be around love. The love of the father and the love to show others. If Christ loved us enough to sacrifice his own son for the atonement of our sins to live in heavenly abode with him, wouldn\'t we want to live that eternal life with others as well? \r\n\r\nBy regeneration and transformation, we are made to be citizens of this kingdom. If we had an opportunity to share the greatest blessing or gift with our loved ones, should we not extend that same opportunity to others as well? Christ did not come for the holy individuals, rather the sinners and the lost people who needed to be saved. By offering obedience to the will of the father, we are claiming the inheritance that he has provided us.', 9, '2024-02-06 02:13:10', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3121, 858, 916, 'My understanding of the gospel aligns with Paul\'s teachings of the kingdom of God in several ways. First as we learned about the citizenship of the kingdom of God, it is interesting to note the correlation between the citizens of the kingdom and the king. Unless we honor the king and keeps his commands, why would he allow us to inherit the blessings that come along with it? We have been given the solid teachings of how being called to be a part of the kingdom has expected circumstances and spiritual warfare. However the scripture also provides guidance on how to withstand such circumstances, from relying on God\'s word, identifying false prophets, relying on elders, to praise and worship. Paul took a practical approach to how to convey his message to the churches he had not physically gone to visit, but based on the teachings of how God would demonstrate wisdom and authority, that level of authority to a certain extent (handed to an apostle) is seen in Paul\'s teachings. He dealt with these issues face on and used the gifts that God gave him to send that message to advance the kingdom of God.', 9, '2024-02-06 02:16:35', '2024-03-03 05:41:35', NULL),
(3122, 859, 917, 'The three main people involved in the content of Paul\'s letter to Philemon were Philemon, Onesimus, and Apphia and Archippus. \r\n\r\n\r\nPhilemon\'s relation to Onesimus was that Onesimus was Philemon\'s slave and a resident of Colosse. He lived with his master, Philemon. Philemon\'s relation to Paul was that Paul had brought Philemon to faith and possibly may have saved his life in some other way. Therefore, Philemon owed Paul a great deal of debt and gratitude. While we don\'t know for sure how they came to know of one another, we can say with confidence that they knew each other well. \r\n\r\nOnesimus, the slave of Philemon was a member of Philemon\'s household. Onesimus was not a believer and he had left Philemon\'s house to seek the help of Paul, who led him to faith. The last two people mentioned that we are going to focus on is Apphia, who is mentioned as Paul\'s \"sister\" indicating that she may have been a believer and Archippus, who may have been the host of the local church. We are not sure who Apphia is but she may have likely been a member of Philemon\'s household.', 7, '2024-02-09 16:16:40', '2024-03-03 05:40:48', NULL),
(3123, 859, 918, 'The problem that Paul was seeking to resolve by this letter was that some servants are not good servants. Some people would not accept their responsibilities and be willing to fulfill their obligations. For example, Onesimus was one of these individuals who upset his master, Philemon and it came to the point where Onesimus had feared punishment from his master so much, that he ran away. \r\n\r\nPaul is looking to explain that when a slave is scared of punishment and not looking to gain freedom, the Roman law would permit the slave to run away. And in this case, Paul explains to Philemon that Onesimus may have proven to be a useless or profitless slave. Paul is looking to ensure that it may be better that Onesimus is no longer a slave within the household of Philemon and good that he has run away. Through this separation, Philemon would rather benefit with Onesimus running away.', 7, '2024-02-09 16:20:46', '2024-03-03 05:40:48', NULL),
(3124, 859, 919, 'Onesimus went to the city where Paul was kept as a prisoner and knowing that Paul was under house arrest and not able to move around freely, he pursued a meeting with Paul. In Paul\'s letter to Philemon, he indicated that he had converted Onesimus to Christianity and that he had proven himself to Paul. He indicates that Onesimus was seeking Paul\'s help in the situation that he was in. \r\n\r\nThe agreement came in the form that Onesimus was repented of all his sins that had angered Philemon and dedicated himself to ministering to the apostle in prison. Paul, in return cared deeply for this child of God and grew to love him as a son. Once favor was gained with Paul, wrote to Philemon mentioning that he is sending Onesimus to Philemon despite wanting to keep him. He asked that Philemon and Onesimus reconcile and this was fulfilled by Philemon accepting Onesimus and forgiving him.', 7, '2024-02-09 16:25:57', '2024-03-03 05:40:48', NULL),
(3125, 859, 920, 'The six aspects of Paul\'s petition were  salutation, where it is broken down with an explanation of Paul\'s role as an advocate, an explanation of Onesimus\' role as a petitioner, an explanation of Philemon\'s role as a master, an explanation of God\'s role as a providential rule of the universe, and Paul\'s statement of confidence that the petition would be granted. \r\n\r\nPaul spoke as a weak man who was elderly and in need of help, he had wanted Philemon to respond to his situation with genuine love. Onesimus was returning to Colosse to petition Philemon for mercy, hoping for reconciliation and release. As a master, Paul wanted for Philemon to do the right thing, and as a ruler Paul suggested that God himself orchestrated the events in the life of Onesimus and Philemon in order for Onesimus to be reconciled. In regards to petition, Paul stood interceding for Onesimus to Philemon and with confidence Paul believe that Philemon would adhere to Paul\'s request.', 7, '2024-02-09 16:34:21', '2024-03-03 05:40:48', NULL),
(3126, 859, 921, 'When you appeal for mercy, you are exhibiting Christ like behavior towards others. Paul could have easily portrayed authority as an apostle, or voice of God and dictated to Philemon to forgive and accept Onesimus. However Paul took Onesimus in, mentored him, guided him to faith, talked about repentance so that not only will he change his behavior, but Philemon should see that change too. \r\n\r\nIf Christ can change our hearts and the hearts of those who despised him as the son of God, then when a slave is able to turn from his ways and repent back to the master, should the master not heed his slaves appeal for mercy? This strategy works often when you are made to be more empathetic about a situation and if you make demands, the true motive behind the request can be manipulative. There may be other intentions driving that request, where as when you show someone mercy, it is because they have a change of heart to reconcile with one another.', 8, '2024-02-09 16:37:34', '2024-03-03 05:40:48', NULL),
(3127, 860, 922, 'Paul had a special relationship with the Philippian church as Philippi was an important city within the Roman province of Macedonia. Paul, during his second missionary journey planted the church in Philippi. The reason being that Paul had a vision of a man who was begging for him to bring to the gospel to Macedonia. As a result of the vision that Paul had, he began his journey to Macedonia moving quickly towards Philippi. During his time within Philippi, Paul had a very prosperous ministry, it was so successful that even when he left the city, Paul kept receiving monetary gifts from the Christians of Philippi. \r\n\r\nThe church of Philippi loved Paul, and even though there were some among them who were wealthy, the general population of the believers were exceptionally poor. They were not able to help Paul as frequently as they would have liked, but they rose to the opportunity when they could and would give generously. It was not a one-sided relationship, Paul also had a deep affection to the church of Philippi as well, he enjoyed there fellowship and would commonly describe them as his closest co-workers. They were supportive of one another in nature.', 7, '2024-02-20 01:53:01', '2024-03-03 05:40:19', NULL),
(3128, 860, 923, 'The reason behind understanding the background of Philippians and knowing why Paul wrote what he did is essential because it allows the reader to know the context of the mutual respect and relationship of one another. For example, in the context of how Paul would miss the fellowship of the people he would refer to his as his closest co-workers, sends a great message about the affection he shared for this group of people. In the context of how Paul build a close bond with Lydia his hostess continues that affirmation. Paul while in prison, had a close bond with the jailor all because he had once saved his life, had we not know the relationship prior we would have thought this would be odd. Paul and the slave girl whom he rescued from demon possession is also mentioned as well, and this is all a result of how Paul had grown to love the believers of Philippi. That is why, even during their own financial challenges, they wholeheartedly gave to Paul when Paul was in need because of that mutual love they shared for one another.', 7, '2024-02-20 01:58:08', '2024-03-03 05:40:19', NULL),
(3129, 860, 924, 'While Paul was imprisoned, he suffered quite a bit, to the point that he felt that death would be his only escape. However by overcoming all of these challenges, he continued to persevere and later exhorted to the Philippians to persevere. He instructed them to continue to remain faithful to Christ no matter what and to live their lives exemplary even during difficult times in their lives. \r\n\r\nThe types of exhortations Paul provided can be categorized into four main topics, the first being the importance of perseverance, the help that ministers provide, Paul\'s personal example, and the challenges to persevere. No matter how difficult the challenges were that the Philippians faced, nothing was beyond God\'s control. God had actually planned their suffering as a means of blessing for them. That is why it was so important for them to understand that. Paul did not want them to simply undergo this suffering, he wanted them to rejoice in the midst of it. and he continue to persevere by sending ministers to help care for them. \r\n\r\nDespite the hardships that Paul faced, he used himself as a positive example of perseverance in faith, behavior and mindset. Paul mentioned that purely professing faith alone is not enough to guarantee salvation, you must also persevere in faith in order to obtain eternal life. Paul stressed the importance of living holy lives, maintaining ones faith. If one is not able to persevere, then we prove that our faith was false to begin with and not genuine.', 9, '2024-02-20 02:06:04', '2024-03-03 05:40:19', NULL),
(3130, 860, 925, 'The overall structure and content of Philippians begins with highlighting the problems within the church. The church faced persecution from outside, they were threatened by the possibility of false teachings, and they struggled with conflicts among one another. Once the church in Philippi was established, we can see that Paul, being the primary author provides salutation to the Philippians which is a testament to the relationship that they had one for another. \r\n\r\nThe next content goes onto thanksgiving, where Paul gives thanks, emphasizing his love for them which was deep and personal. Following that is prayer, which is brief but filled with expression of Christian love that honors God. Moving onto the body, where Paul talks about his suffering and his deep love for the Philippians. Paul then moves onto to talk about his perseverance, and his exhortation to the Philippians to persevere. Paul went on to advise the Philippians not to let false teachers coming among them or within the church. He went on to describe how the false teachings could invade the church and perseverance of the church. Once that is complete, is the affirmation of perseverance and final greetings.', 9, '2024-02-20 02:13:56', '2024-03-03 05:40:19', NULL),
(3131, 860, 926, 'There have been many moments in my life, where my desire was to have the individuals whom I shared fellowship with and broke bread with to impact my life by ministering to me in a time of need. Unfortunately, my response will be the opposite, which is that I did not have that experience yet in my life where I needed someone to pick me up when I needed that the most in my life. I can however share an example of how I was able to do so for a few individuals. \r\n\r\nThere was once a fellow friend who shared some of the darkest, most personal intimate struggles with me. They confided me in more than anyone else at that time, and I was not in a position where I felt that I did anything to deserve such openness from this individual. However, the Holy Spirit guided me during that time, to spend more time with than individual, share from the word, pray with them, meditate, talk to them and offer them approaches to seek additional help. This allowed for the other individual to see a response different than what they had ever expected and would constantly ask, \"why do you care so much about me?\" I would never even think twice to \"care\" about a fellow individual who I knew was hurting and struggling, it felt natural and the \"christ like\" thing to do in my opinion. God has called us to partake in the good and the bad when our brothers and sisters are going through happiness and sorrow. I only pray that when the time comes, I too can experience that same love and perseverance from christ like minded believers.', 9, '2024-02-20 02:18:30', '2024-03-03 05:40:19', NULL),
(3132, 861, 826, 'When defining Christian ethics and focusing on God and his blessings, it is imperative to note that our focus is more reflective on the fact that those things which receive God\'s blessings are good and right. Whereas the things that do not receive his blessings are wrong and evil. This demonstrates the nature of God as being moral and the actions of God being moral as well. \r\n\r\nSince God is the ultimate standard of what is right and wrong, we are inclined to see God\'s nature as the standard model of goodness and righteousness. When it comes to his blessings, he shows his approval for what is considered right and wrong be showering his blessings upon them.', 7, '2024-02-21 01:44:25', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3133, 861, 827, 'Proper motive is significant when it comes to actions because unless the work that is performed is done with the right intention or motive, it will not be a work that that God will reward with his blessings. The action must originate and flow from a heart that is purified through faith and it should come from Christian love. The type of faith that a believer holds is faith that is pure, faith that saves and faith that motivates good works. \r\n\r\nIf proper motive is not driven by love, then the works that are bestowed are useless. The scripture states to love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your mind. To reject God\'s law is to simply reject him', 7, '2024-02-21 01:47:58', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3134, 861, 828, 'The threefold criteria for good works can be outlined by proper motive, proper standard, and proper goal. With proper motive, you are driven by a heart that is purified by faith. Without seeking God\'s blessing founded on basis of faith, we are not able to please God. Love is the next step of proper motive, everything that we do should be stemmed around the basis of love. \r\n\r\nWith proper standard, every work that is done must adhere to the standard of God\'s work and his commandments. When referring to commands, there are no commands that can be bypassed or omitted, each command of God still holds true to be upheld. All scripture is basis for commands and law, and there is good reason for scripture to be used for ethical training. \r\n\r\nLastly, proper goal is when the ultimate goal should always be considered. God\'s glory is to be a central, and foundational goal in our lives and God only rewards and approves the work of those that have his glory as their ultimate goal.', 7, '2024-02-21 01:54:40', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3135, 861, 829, 'When looking at the different perspective that must be employed when approaching ethics, the first is situational. This approach can be referred to as \'teleological\' because of the focus on the end result or action. In scripture we can see how Paul would encourage his readers to live a holy life and abstain from sin that they had once committed. This situational perspective creates awareness for the sake of righteousness and leads towards the reward that is mentioned for demonstrating situational perspective. \r\n\r\nNormative perspective is the fact that God\'s word is the norm or the basis of ethical standards. When considering normative perspective, you are looking to the Bible in order to be told what is the correct thing to do. This is the basis for behavior and the commandment for how people should behave in a certain way. \r\n\r\nLastly, is existential which is viewing ethics through the lens of the individual\'s personal experience. The Scripture affirms the leading of the Holy Spirit as a valid means to determining what is right and wrong, and God moves among his people to bring them towards conviction on a personal level. When we acknowledge this, we are using existential perspective.', 9, '2024-02-21 02:01:10', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3136, 861, 830, 'God serves as the absolute judge of morality as he himself is the absolute ethical norm. Goodness is a personal attribute of God and God\'s goodness is the ultimate standard for all goodness. When referring to personal attribute, God is the standard measure of how all morality is measured. Therefore he is the absolute judge of morality, he is not just good and right, rather he is actual goodness and righteous itself. \r\n\r\nGod being referred to the metaphor of light enables a moral evaluation as light is commonly referred to as good and darkness is referred to evil, sin and lies. Scripture also states that no one is good except for God and within God there dwells no evil. God is also the ultimate standard for for all goodness. Scripture consistently reminds us of how the absolute standard by which all ethical issues are to be evaluated is against God\'s own personal goodness.', 7, '2024-02-21 02:06:10', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3137, 861, 831, 'Three categories of revelation that are part of God\'s word include general revelation, special revelation and existential revelation. When speaking of general revelation we are concerned with the way that creation and history unravels the true things about God and his moral requirements of us. The complexity and importance of general revelation is important. Complexity speaks of how general revelation is common to all people, whereas the importance factors the character of God and what he has made, and his continual interaction. \r\n\r\nSpecial revelation is a normal that is binding on our lives, and in the complexity form special revelation is reliant on the words that are spoken, written, or communication involving God with people. In some cases, God appears to be audible and seen visibily, where in other cases he is not seen, but rather heard. In special revelations, there are also the considerations for dreams, and natural phenomenom to communicate the truth to individuals. The importance of this is that all individuals should adhere to this standard of special revelation as a norm. \r\n\r\nExistential revelation reflects the concept of God speaking to or revealing himself through persons. None of these presentations are considered simple, rather they contain God\'s revelation through human beings and are the inward leading and illumination of the Holy Spirit. This can be categorized into both the external aspects and internal aspects.', 7, '2024-02-21 02:16:22', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3138, 861, 832, 'Existential revelation teaches us about God\'s standard by using different people and private spirits to reveal his will to his people.  While all reveal the same God and the same standard, each of these revelations are binding and authoritative. God\'s standard is the basis for all moral teachings and revelations. When considering existential revelations, God give to his believers the illumination of the Holy Spirit the the Holy Spirit reveals the truth about God. When referring to the illumination, we are referring to the divine gift of understanding that God grants to believers and non-believers as well. The person that has been illuminated now has the ability or knowledge that they had once lacked. God did this when he revealed knowledge to Peter at the moment that Peter did not figure out that Jesus was the Christ. While believers and nonbelievers may not grasp the truth and revelation the same way, God is able to provider the supernatural understanding of the gospel and God\'s truth to everyone. \r\n\r\nWith existential revelation, human existence is also taken into consideration and humans were created in the image of God. and that is to say that in some form we are a reflection of God. The second point is that while we are created in God\'s image, we are able to exercise authority, revealing God\'s character. This authority was delegated to us by god and whatever Adam did when the animals and exercising judgment to govern and rule over them, is the delegation that we have been granted over as a reflection of the character of God and his standards.', 9, '2024-02-21 02:25:12', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3139, 861, 833, 'As Christ created us in his image, and hold us to the standard that he abides by, walking in the light with Christ is also a call for separation from sin and darkness. While man was initially created by God and his image, Adam was brought into this world without sin or fault. However with the fall of temptation and man sinning, we caused all of humankind to sin and suffer through each generation. When God says to walk in his light and be a part of his fellowship, we are given the chance to be freed from this bondage of sin that was placed upon us if we choose to adhere to the teachings of Christ and be like Christ in his image and standards. That will eventually lead to the forgiveness of our sins in Christ and allow for us to exercise the divine privilege that has been granted to us to accept the grace of Jesus dying on the cross for our sins to accept and receive eternal salvation.', 7, '2024-02-21 02:28:39', '2024-03-03 05:39:39', NULL),
(3140, 862, 834, 'As the Bible derives from divine inspiration, it also carries God\'s authority. Since God carries all authority, whenever and however he speaks is an obligation for all to obey him. There are a few claims of this authority and it can be explained through historical examples and the way authority is claimed. In the earliest record of the Bible, we can see of how God spoke directly to humanity and how his dialogue carried authority. When God had instructed man with specific guidelines and man disobeyed God, we can see of how God\'s authority was demonstrated by punishing Adam and Eve. When looking at the days of Moses, we can also wee that God\'s spoken word was the basis for his written word and the people were obligated to obey it. \r\n\r\nRegarding the claims of authority, we can see how the apostles used this authority in their speaking and writings within the New Testament. Paul even carried his delegated authority from Jesus to instruct the people and churches in his writings. As the Bible is God\'s word, it still carries authority even to this day.', 8, '2024-02-23 01:47:42', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3141, 862, 835, 'When we read and understand Scripture, we are better able to understand God and his will for all mankind. WE can see how God\'s will is revealed with enough clarity to guide us ethically. If one is able to study the word of God diligently, then by all means they are able to apply it to themselves and practice a lifestyle of ethical means. \r\n\r\nHowever, not everyone has the equal ability to comprehend the Bible and not everyone makes an equal effort to try and understand the Scripture. While in fact, there are some portions of Scripture that are a bit less clear than others, God\'s will is perfectly made clear and well known enough for us to have a standard of morality.', 5, '2024-02-23 01:50:59', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3142, 862, 836, 'Scripture was written to give people a clean understanding of God\'s will and his character. This would allow humankind to be able to follow the ways of God and conform themselves to living a life according to God\'s will and character. While Scripture is clear on some matters and less clear on other matters, it is very clear on how salvation is achieved through repentance and faith in Jesus Christ. \r\n\r\nAll of Scripture is useful and has clear implications on how it should be used. If we apply ourselves with diligence to learn the word of God, we are able to understand God\'s will to conform ourselves to his standards of morality. The well understanding of Scripture enables us to live a moral life with ethical decision making as God had intended. It teaches us what is right in the sight and eyes of God and allows to conform to the will of God and his character.', 5, '2024-02-23 01:56:01', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3143, 862, 837, 'While the gospel was considered foolish in the eyes of some, that did not stop Paul from visiting Rome and preaching the gospel. We can see in Romans 1, how Paul reported his faith in Jesus has allowed him to have the boldness to share the gospel to all who were in Rome. He went on to explain that righteousness is revealed and achieved by faith, a faith that is blind but has great rewards, as the righteous abide by this faith. \r\n\r\nThe implications that this for our lives today is that many people continue to reject the gospel and word of God and need to see sights and wonders to believe. But even during the time of Paul, we can see that he shared the gospel to Greeks, and non Greeks, both to the wise and foolish. He was fulfilling the commission to take the gospel to the ends of the earth and preach. In our lives today, we may not be accepted and received by all, but with that blind faith, the righteous are able to fulfill the will of God and there is power in that gospel through God\'s salvation.', 8, '2024-02-23 02:00:23', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3144, 862, 838, 'The variety of language found in the Bible and the different uses of language notated varies in many different ways. The Scripture is full of language ranging about different forms of human communication and if we are able to understand how each of these types of languages are used, there is a strong possibility that we may misunderstand the Bible. \r\n\r\nBecause the Bible is God inspired, many times the interpretation that is represented may be so extraordinary that it is not comprehensive for the human mind. Whereas, many Christians have testified that the extraordinary form in which scripture was written makes its language easier to interpret. While God does speak in mysterious ways, the Bible is not hidden with meanings that are not meant to be interpreted through special means, or spiritual gifts or special offices, rather it is meant to be studies and revealed with the gift of the Holy Spirit. It is simply not enough to know what the Bible says, but rather how the Bible is trying to communicate with us, so that as a reader, we are able to understand it better.', 8, '2024-02-23 02:06:24', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3145, 862, 839, 'The primacy of the Ten Commandments are both historical and theological. The historical primacy is dependent on the fact that these were the first set of written legal codes that were handed to the nation of Israel. Prior to this, there was no documented legal codes provided and Paul is calling special attention to this matter in Galatians 3:17. He referred to the Ten Commandments as the introduction of the law. While it is true that the people of God had many commandments and rules to follow prior to the time of Moses, such as the time of Noah where God had a standard for people to follow and they rejected God, and the consequences that followed. Hence, the written law was passed onto the nation of Israel to serve along with the Book of the Covenant which was later formed. The theological primacy around the Ten Commandments is how the law was received by Israel. The law handed to Moses was done so with thunder, lighting, smoke, clouds and heavenly trumpets. Jesus also reference the ten commandments in his teachings in a remarkable way.', 8, '2024-02-23 02:11:52', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3146, 862, 840, 'Three traditional types of law that we can help apply law in the modern world include: moral law, ceremonial law, and civil law. Moral laws are revolved around God\'s ethical standards and are typically associated with the Ten Commandments. Ceremonial laws reflect instruction around worshipping God. Civil law is in regards to the governing of society. \r\n\r\nWhen looking to apply these to the modern world, moral law helps us as the world is rapidly changing for the worst. There are immoral things happening which directly impact the laws and ethical standards God had put in place, through Scripture and also the Ten Commandments. Ceremonial laws help us abide by the way the modern world is changing in regards to how we worship God and the forums around the challenges that we see in regards to either restrictions in the government realm and leniency in the modern churches which go against a lot of what Scripture has taught us. Civil law in regards to how society is governed was once adhered by the US constitution and forefathers, however we live in a day and age where this no longer applies and as a county civil law established by God has been diminished by mankind. Whether it has been diminished or not, the law of God around ethical standards still stand.', 8, '2024-02-23 02:17:10', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3147, 862, 841, 'The word of God never changes regardless of time and society. We now live in a day and age where everything around us defies what we know is morally correct and biblically sound, however the one constant declared within Scripture is, that heaven and earth may pass away, but the word of God will never. As society changes and we see things we could never dream of or imagine, the civil and ceremonial laws still apply, but humankind continues to reject the laws of God and apply their own understanding to these things. While many of the specific behaviors that were once part of the Mosiac time period do not apply today, we must understand the basis for that is because Jesus paid the ransom for the ultimate sacrifice. With his death, burial, and resurrection, God\'s people are no longer required to perform the specific acts that were once required in the Old Testament, however the basic moral principles still apply.', 7, '2024-02-23 02:21:03', '2024-03-03 05:38:52', NULL),
(3148, 863, 842, 'Three types of situational content of God\'s revelation that we must know in order to make ethical decisions are facts, goals, and means. To begin, God is the ultimate fact and ultimate ethical environment. Every ethical question or dilemma that we face should be faced with the facts that God has displayed for us in Scripture. WE are obligated to live by these ethical standards and as God\'s authority is absolute, his character is the perfect expression of his will. \r\nWhen referring to goals, God\'s revelation requires us to set goals such as doing good to other and living in unity with one another. In every situation in life, our ultimate goal should be to glorify God. When considering means as a situational content of God\'s revelation, we must consider the teachings of God in our decision making process. We must recognize the fact that there are poor people in the world who are in need of food and clothing. They must also be kept warm, and fed. This type of context around God\'s revelation allows us to feed these individuals physically as well as spiritually.', 7, '2024-02-23 14:04:09', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3149, 863, 843, 'Facts are relational to God and he is our ultimate fact and our ultimate ethical environment. The rule of God\'s existence is over every ethical question and requires us to live by the standard of his character. Through revelation, God is able to tell us facts about himself and what he requires of us in our lives. Without revelation, we can still obey God but would know how to truly obey God and make ethical decision based on God\'s will and revelation. As similar to the laws we are made aware of, we know how to live a decent life, but without the laws being made known to us, we would not have known what is considered permissible versus what is not. With God\'s revelation being made known to us, we are able to use discernment to set proper goals for us in regards to behavior and decision making.', 7, '2024-02-23 14:07:06', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3150, 863, 844, 'Goals provide the direction to our actions, and that is something we practice on a daily basis in our lives. We wake up each morning with the things that we want to accomplish and work towards fulfilling those tasks in an orderly fashion. When we take the time to understand biblical goals, we are able to understand the ultimate goal which is to glorify God and live a godly life. By adopting this understanding, we live and abide by Christian ethics and strive for unity in the church, being good to our neighbors, and raising children in Christ. If we understand what our biblical goals our, we make ethical and biblical decisions in every single thing that we do and teaches us to to set God\'s glory as our highest goal. We are also filled with the discernment to know what is a good goal versus what is an evil goal.', 5, '2024-02-23 14:11:44', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3151, 863, 845, 'An overly rigorous approach to revelation is characterized by an overly restrictive behavior rather than on the side of permitting it. When a Christian endorses a rigorous strategy mistakenly with their understanding of the Bible being vague, the proper response is to apply Scripture in restrictive ways. However, with an overly rigorous approach we would tend to build a fence very close to a hose to forbit most or all of what is unclear to us to avoid falling into immorality. When this happens, we face the negative consequences of rigor. It destroys the freedom a Christian has by prohibiting behaviors that are considered wrong under certain conditions and right or good under others. The second is by turning God\'s  word into a heavy burden. When this happens, extreme behavior takes over and we become too far from following God and more about overly rigorous practices. The way to avoid falling into this strategy is by learning and listening to the word of God and applying it to our lives as God had intended for it.', 7, '2024-02-23 14:18:02', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3152, 863, 846, 'Jesus emphasized the importance of the Kingdom of God in three different sections of the Sermon on the Mount by explaining the Beatitudes which is also referred to as christian ethics. This is in relation to the blessings of God and viewed as a means of determining which human persons and which human acts receive those blessings. The second important of the Kingdom of God was summarized in the Lord\'s Prayer to acknowledge that Jesus set forth his kingdom and told everyone to pray for the fulfillment of God\'s kingdom on earth. This taught that the kingdom of God was extended to earth and is a high priority for Christian ethics. The last emphasis is reflective of earthly needs. Of every need that humans have, the first concern should be to glorify God through the his triumphant return. Jesus made it very clear on the mount that the greatest goal of a Christian\'s life is to strive to glorify God throughout everything.', 7, '2024-02-23 14:23:36', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3153, 863, 847, 'Three main components of the Kingdom of God are categorized by the role of the king, the people of the kingdom, and the covenants that govern the relationship between the king and his people. In Scripture, God is commonly referred to as the supreme king and ruler over all creation. Everything on earth is put on this earth to serve and glorify him. This is a major theme that runs throughout the Bible. While God is the emperor over all of creation, his reign continue be over all people. He starts Genesis with appointing Adam and Even as rulers over his creation, but they were accountable to God. Humanity\'s role was to fulfill the will of the King and master ruler over all people and creation. Lastly, covenants were imposed in the ancient world to administer great empires. When it comes to the Bible, God administered his kingdom through divine covenants, with the obligations of the people to God. The obedience or disobedience for these obligations would be dealt with as a result of the importance of these declared covenants. To connect the relationship of all three, God  is the supreme ruler over all people and provided them with the covenant. This covenant allows for blessings or curses over the people subject to God\'s authority over them.', 7, '2024-02-23 14:29:39', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3154, 863, 848, 'The historical development of the kingdom of God can be summarized into three major periods. The three phases can be related back to creation where the period of initial peace takes place. The second period is relative to the fall which was motivated by rebellion, and the last period is about redemption reflecting on final peace. The first period of initial peace began with Adam and Eve who were obedient servants at first, there was peace at that time between God and all of humanity. However moving to the second period, we can see humanity broke their commitment to God and rebelled and as a result of their rebellion, a curse was placed upon humanity and leading to their fall. The last period of final peace relates to how God began to restore peace to his kingdom after the fall of humanity and God sent his son to rescue humanity from their pitfall of sin.', 5, '2024-02-23 14:33:30', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3155, 863, 849, 'My view of God\'s law relates to joy and delight as God had clear expectations for humanity when he created mankind. However when humanity rebelled and caused the fall of mankind into a curse, God still allowed for redemption. This shows the natural saving grace of our King and how he continues to provide humanity with an opportunity to inherit the kingdom of God. Had he continued to punish mankind for our sinful nature, we would not be able to have this opportunity for redemption and salvation. That is why I view his guidelines and principles as life lessons to salvation versus restrictions on earth. \r\n\r\nGod provided mankind with the rule to glorify God and provide discernment in their lifestyle to live an ethical life pleasing God. While we live on this earth, our primary objective is to glorify God and enjoy the blessings that God has provided to us. We do this by loving God and following the laws he has provided us with delight in fellowship with one another. If we view it in this manner, we are able to seek God\'s law as a great blessing and gift that he has given to us.', 8, '2024-02-23 14:39:29', '2024-03-03 05:38:08', NULL),
(3156, 865, 858, 'When you talk about loving God, loyalty is the cornerstone of that concept. In the Old Testament there are references of how people were required to be loyal to the suzerain and they were to obey and honor him as king and be loyal to him. In the concept of allegiance to God, obeying God\'s commands, and remaining loyal to God motivates us to honor and care for those who live under his rule. In regards to orientation, there is no part of any of our lives that takes place outside of his sovereign rule. For us, our highest priority must always be oriented around God, whether it be the core focus of our desires, he must be the core focus of our worldview, and priorities. The Bible exemplifies this when Jesus oriented his whole life around God and around the people that he came to save. This motivated him to obey God perfectly in all things. This should motivate us to obey God perfectly and be willing to sacrifice as Jesus made sacrifices for us. When you love and orient your priorities correct, naturally you are responsible for keeping and upholding that relationship through accountability. When God provided the 10 commandments, certain behaviors and actions were forbidden, but they were meant for God\'s people and if we don\'t seek responsibilites before God, it is very easy to stray from the temptation of sin.', 8, '2024-03-01 01:47:39', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3157, 865, 859, 'God\'s actions of atoning grace is described in Matthew 18:23-35 when Jesus teaches that we are to imitate God\'s forgiveness and just like God\'s forgiveness of us, we are to forgive others and be genuine, heartfelt and motivated by true compassion for them. As sinners, we hated God and were offensive to God. We were set against him and we did not deserve any mercy. Rather we deserved to be punished, but the grace of God obligates us to to give up possessions, even our lives to protect and care for our brothers and sisters in Christ. We are taught to love unconditionally and with this in mind, we are able to understanding atoning grace. When it comes to common grace, we refer to it as God\'s kindness to those who will never be saved. This is when God\'s non-redemptive kindness beyond to those who will never receive salvation. That is the expression of God\'s love for humanity, that knowing some folks will never accept him, he still continues to to love and all people.', 8, '2024-03-01 01:51:56', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3158, 865, 860, 'Reflecting upon my own heart, I have always believed to always treat others as I would want to be treated. I know that the Bible teaches us to love our neighbors. But there are many ways to love our neighbors, and some actions can be louder than others. However, looking at the example that Jesus puts before us, we can either choose to be outward expressions of that love or keep that on the inside. My heart is truly committed to God and his word in the sense that I just want to honor God in anything and everything that I do. I honestly have never cared much for the spotlight or recognition, I have always just wanted to love and to be loved. My passion is to always see others grow and be successful and along the way, God has honored me through his grace and favor which I do not deserve. When I continue to experience his favor over my life, I know that God is teaching me to be more humbly and act on that grace to pay it forward. To be able to be kind above and beyond to the point where you would think it is unnatural to behave that way. That is the way God loved and cared, and I want to model that example.', 9, '2024-03-01 01:55:19', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3159, 865, 861, 'When I first accepted the Lord as my personal savior, I was at a young age and I knew at that moment that I did not want to be like all my friends and people I was in contact with. This of course came with it\'s own challenges and struggles, but my love for God was greater than the fear of what others thought about me. I was not concerned about my relationship with others, I was more focused on my motivation and behavior to know God on a more personal level. This has only grown from the young age of 13 years old. Of course, I have gone through more in my 40 years on this earth than what some 80 year olds will face, but it has only grown my faith through the trials I have faced and gone through. My personal experiences have helped mold my behavior into being someone who listens more, talks less. Someone who is more patient than reactive, someone who is modeled by Christ like behavior and strives to be better each day, through each challenge and struggle rather than someone who strives to be perfect.', 7, '2024-03-01 01:59:07', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3160, 865, 862, 'Imagination is the ability to form a mental image or picture of something that is beyond our experience. Imagination can be broken down into three categories: creativity, ways that imagination enables us to think about subjects during different periods of time, and lastly the way that imagination allows us to think about things that are separated by physical distance. Our imaginative abilities are vital to learning and thinking about God, the world and ourselves in the sense that we use creativity basically every day. Imagination helps us to form moral patterns and analogies and we can use it to help us think about things which may be separated from us by time. When we try to learn and think about God, we can imagine how Jesus came to this world and was persecuted and rejected by man and was crucified for our sins. We can see this in movies, pictures and even in churches. This helps us to relate to how we see things in this world and different types of things that are occurring causing us to view things in time, distance, and within reason. We can at the very least try to imagine what Jesus went through on this earth and take us back to a time many years ago and see the world in different views.', 8, '2024-03-01 02:12:56', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3161, 865, 863, 'Assessing knowledge is not only important to the process of making ethical decisions, it is crucial. There are so many instances of how people can go from one extreme to another when they think of ethics. Our intellect comes from God, and with the guidance of the Holy Spirit we are able to use it rightly. When we assess the importance of knowledge in making ethical decision, we must use sound reason to compare different facts to extensively determine logical relations. When we use reason with the presence of the Holy Spirit, we are able to have a successful way assessing the knowledge we have acquired.  In terms of conscience, this refers to the God-given ability to discern good and evil. While our conscience has been corrupted by our nature of sin, we are bound to definitely make mistakes from time to time, and that is why we need to have proper discernment to rely on the power of the Holy Spirit. Lastly, emotions are very useful and helps us to determine how God\'s word applies in our modern lives. We must therefore submit our emotions to the Holy Spirit.', 7, '2024-03-01 02:21:00', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3162, 865, 864, 'Will is the capacity to make decisions or choices. In each and everything that we think, say or do, we have either active will or passive will. Each time we make a choice or decision, are using some form of will. More often that not, making the right decision will be hard because as humans, we are naturally affected by our fallen nature. Therefore, while the Holy Spirit enables us, we are still privy to fall into sin and make sinful decisions. When considering the role it plays in the decision making process we must follow the word of God and what the word commands. If we allow the Holy Spirit to work within us, then it will influence us in positive manners and help is in making Godly choices.', 8, '2024-03-01 02:31:21', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3163, 865, 865, 'When your heart is aligned to God and you have a relationship with God, then naturally your works and speech will be a reflection of that walk with God. So if your heart bares good thoughts and the will of God, then naturally your words, actions, and behavior will be a reflection of that. If you have an evil heart with evil desires, then the fruit of what you do will proceed with evil. For whatever you sow, you will reap, and if you have a positive christ like behavior you are enabled to make decisions that please God. However if you are not right with God and living a live of sin, then you may be persuaded to make sinful decisions.', 5, '2024-03-01 02:38:24', '2024-03-03 05:37:14', NULL),
(3164, 869, 681, 'Hermeneutics is an effort to interpret the meaning of communication, particularly communication that is being interpreted in a different cultural context. Biblical hermeneutics is the method of interpreting Scriptures so we can bridge the gaps between modern-day readers of the Bible, it\'s original audiences, and God as its ultimate author. \r\nHermeneutics is foundation to the theological study, whether or not they know it, every biblical reader interprets the Bible through their own hermeneutics. Developing a clearly communicated biblical hermeneutics helps Bible students to understand the biases they bring into their interpretive work. without effective methods of interpretation, we can\'t understand the meaning of the text and therefore build cohesive biblical theologies, and therefore, a Christ-honoring, mission-driven Christian life. \r\n\r\nThe church today faces many important issues as technological innovation transforms everything from communications to family structures to social relationships. We need the wisdom form God himself to properly engage and  understand the world we live in.  Yet the primary way we learn what God has to say is the Bible , a book written between 2,000 to 3,500 years ago.  \r\n\r\nTheological interpretation of scriptures is an approach to biblical interpretation that approaches the text with explicitly theological presuppositions, questions, and concerns. seeking to hear in scriptures not only thoughts and voices human authors but a word from God that functions as the primary.', 8, '2024-03-11 02:16:31', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3165, 869, 682, 'It was not unusual for us to read a book through three or four times. Each time finding out new bits of intriguing information about interesting characters and plots. After experiencing a deeper conversion to Christ, when we start reading the Bible with great enthusiasm , understanding that it was actually God\'s word that made reading the Bible different that any other book. Our zeal for the Bible was bolstered by this understanding that the entire book was inspired; God was the author. \r\n\r\nWithout the assurance of it\'s inspiration, our new love for the Bible may have easily slipped into skepticism, resulting in our Bible collecting dust on the shelf. The Bible is divinely inspired. What do the words \"Inspired\" and \"Inspiration\" mean? the tern \"Inspired\" comes from the Greek word , theopneustos, which mean \"God breathed\" . When the Church speaks of the Bible as inspired, she means that the principal author of Scriptures is God. \r\n\r\nThe Apostle Paul describes how the principal author of Scripture, the Holy scripture, made known the wisdom of God by communicating spiritual thoughts with spritual words. The spirit was \"describing spritual realities in spritual terms\" . We must never stop marvelling at how fasr God has gone in adapting his language with thoughtful concern for our weak human nature.', 8, '2024-03-11 02:31:04', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3166, 869, 683, 'The apostles were very clear that what was written was what God wanted written. For example Exodus 33:11 says that God spoke to Moses \"face to face\" as a man speaks to his friend.  Moses worte down  many things that God told him personally (Exodus 24:4, Deuteronomy 31:9). All of the prophets were moved by the Holy Spirit to write down their messages. 2 Peter 1:20-21 says, above all, you must understand that no prophecy of scripture came about by the prophet\'s own interpretation of things. \r\nWe have evidence from comparing newer and older copies of the Old Testament in Hebrew and Aramaic, that the copying was extremely accurate.  The most important reason for confidence in the translations is that we know the contents of the original Hebrew manuscripts. \r\nThe old testament is trustworthy and the translations we have today are an accurate reflection of the original . we can read our Bibles and be confident that we have message God intended for us.', 8, '2024-03-11 02:46:24', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3167, 869, 684, 'The three most prominent theories of knowledge are:\r\n1. Foundationalism: Foundationalism is the view that all knowledge is based on a foundation of self-evident or indubitable beliefs. These foundational beliefs are then used to justify all other beliefs.\r\n\r\n2. Coherentism: Coherentism is the view  that all knowledge is justified by its coherence with other beliefs. In other words, a belief is justified if it is a part of coherent system of beliefs. \r\n\r\n3. Reliabilism: Reliabilism is the view that all knowledge is justified if it is produced by a reliable process. In other words, a belief is justified if it is produced by a process that is likely to lead to true beliefs. \r\n\r\nThese three theories of knowledge has its own strength and weakness. Foundationalism is appealing because it seems to provide a solid foundation for our knowledge. However, it is difficult to identify any self-evident or indubitable beliefs. Coherentism is appealing because it allows us to justify beliefs that are not directly based on experience.  However, it\'s difficult to say how coherent a system of beliefs must be in order to be justified. Reliabilism is appealing because it seems to focus on what is important in practice. \r\n\r\nUltimately, none of these theories of knowledge is perfect. However they provide us with different ways of thinking about what justifies our beliefs.', 8, '2024-03-11 02:59:15', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3168, 869, 685, 'Whether we are aware of it or not, we always bring certain beliefs and philosophical assumptions to our research. Sometimes these are deeply ingrained views about the types of problems that we need to study. what research questions to ask, or how we go about gathering data. These beliefs are instilled in us during our educational training through reading journal articles and books.\r\nThe difficulty lies first in becoming aware of these assumptions and believes and second in deciding whether we will actively incorporate them in our qualitative studies. Often, at a less abstract level, these philosophical assumptions inform our choice of theories that guide our research.\r\nTheories are more apparent in our qualitative studies than philosophical assumptions and theories are made explicit for us in our scholarly training and in report research studies. \r\n\r\nQualitative researchers have underscored the importance of not only understanding the beliefs and theories that inform our research but also actively writing about them in our reports and studies. \r\n\r\nA close tie does exist between the philosophy that one brings to the research act and hour one proceeds to use a framework to shroud his or he inquiry. Further, various interpretive frameworks are suggested that link back to philosophical assumptions.', 8, '2024-03-11 03:14:26', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3169, 869, 686, 'There are many different types of subjectivism. But in general, we can say that subjectivist recognize that human beings and the world, and especially matters of faith, are often too complex to be discerned by scientific rationalism. So their search for typically relief strongly on personal faculties like intuition and emotions. For example in John 13:34-35, Jesus gave this familiar instruction:\r\n\r\nA new command I give you: Love one another . As I have loved , so you must love one another. By this all men will know that you are my disciples. if you love one another.\r\n\r\nSubjective approaches to meaning shifted again in the late twentieth century in a movement known as post-structuralism. In our days, subjectivism has become widespread , especially in the interpretation of art and literature. Subjective interpreters argue that since we can\'t discover the objective understanding of the world around us.\r\n\r\nIdeally, followers of Christ do not allow the currents of culture surrounding them to influence the way they interpret the Bible. But, in reality, none of us can entirely escape the effects of culture on our approach to biblical hermenutics. In recent recades, hermeneutical subjectivism  has moved beyond the confines of academic discusssions.', 8, '2024-03-11 03:31:44', '2024-03-20 15:08:58', NULL),
(3170, 871, 692, 'Immutability means God does not change in anyway; he is unchanging and for that reason perfect in every way. Impassibility, a corollary to immutability, means God does not experience emotional change in any way, not does God suffer. To clarify, God does not merely choose to be in impassible; he is impassible by nature.\r\n\r\nGod, in His great love, provides this encouragement through two unchangeable things; the word of His promise and the oath which confirms that promise. Unlike us, God is always truthful. He never lies or breaks His oaths.\r\n\r\nThus God, determining to show more abundantly to the heirs of promise the immutability of His counsel, confirmed it by an oath, that by two immutable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we might have strong consolation, who have fled for refuge to lay hold of the hope set before us.\r\n\r\nGod has give both his promise and his oath. These two things are unchangeable because it is impossible for God to lie. Therefore, we who have fled to him for refuge can have great confidence as we hold to the hope.\r\n\r\nAs God is a most pure spirit, invisible, without body, parts, or passions. Impassibility is the notion that God does not suffer and can\'t be acted upon or moved by any other source.', 8, '2024-03-12 02:16:11', '2024-03-20 15:09:28', NULL),
(3171, 871, 693, 'There is a common feature that runs through all of them except the books to Timothy and Titus. There three books are to individuals while all the others are to churches. Philemon fits both categories as it was written to both individuals and a church. We see similar terms like beloved, saints, and faithful. \r\n\r\nThe three worlds of the Text provide an uncomplicated interpretive structure in a way that is consistent with teaching on biblical interpretation. The worlds of the text have three dimensions. The world that gave rise to it - the world behind the text. Our world as readers today.\r\n\r\nWhat are the differences between that biblical audience and us? we today are separated from the biblical audience by differences in culture, language, situation, time, and covenant. These difference form a river that hinders us from moving straight from meaning in their context to meaning ours.\r\n\r\nBecause there were twelve tribes in ancient israel. many concluse that the original audience was composed primarily christians.', 8, '2024-03-12 02:35:44', '2024-03-20 15:09:28', NULL),
(3172, 871, 694, 'It is roughly contiguous with the Second Temple period and encompasses the age of Hellenistic Judaism. It is known by some members of the church community. God revealed nothing new to the Jewish people. \r\n\r\nFirst, we can capture it is terms of four major plot movements; creation, fall, redemption, and new creation. Second, we can describe the unfolding of God\'s eternal plan from creation to the new creation through the progression of the biblical covenants. \r\n\r\nThe intertestamental Period:\r\n1. The Last Recorded Prophet.\r\n2. The Rise of the Scribes\r\n3. The Hellenization of Judea\r\n4. Judea under the Hellenistic Kingdoms\r\n5. The Hasmoneans and the Maccabean Revolt\r\n6. Pharisees and Sadducees\r\n7. The Essenses\r\n8. Judea under the Romans.\r\n\r\nThe biblical metanarrative is divided into four parts: Creation, Fall, Redemption, and Consummation. As you study Scripture, read the text through this gospel lens and watch for this arc, knowing that the smaller stories are all a part of one larger Story.', 8, '2024-03-12 02:47:46', '2024-03-20 15:09:28', NULL),
(3173, 871, 695, 'The first part of the Bible is the Old Testament which consists of 39 books. These books are divided into 4 general categories: Books of Law called the Pentateuch or Torah, the Historical Books, the Poetic Books, and the Prophetic Books. \r\n\r\nThe Hebrew Bible was also divided into some larger sections. In Israel, the Torah were divided into 154 sections so that they could be read through aloud in weekly worship over the course.  The Hebrew bible also called TaNak, or Old testament refers to the collection of Hebrew books that were recognized as Scriptures in ancient Israel. The traditional order of these books is called the TaNak.\r\n\r\nThe Bible is one book, composed of two testaments. Comprised of Thirty-nine books. the Old Testament is God\'s unfolding promise to his covenant people Israel. With Twenty-seven books, the New Testament fulfills all the promise of the Old Testament in Christ, for Jews and Gentile alike. The new Testament explains the person and work of Christ by showing how he fulfills the Old testanment. Together these two testaments are a literary unity, progressively revealed.', 7, '2024-03-12 03:04:09', '2024-03-20 15:09:28', NULL),
(3174, 871, 696, 'The New Testament comes after and documents how the world is saved through what Jesus did for his people.  The old Testament focuses primarily on the history of Israel and God\'s law, whereas Jesus is the main focus of the New Testament. \r\nThe inclusion of Gentiles into early Christianity posed a problem for the Jewish identity of some of the early Christians: the new Gentile converts were not required to be circumcised not to be observed the Mosaic Law. \r\n\r\nWhile Christianity recognizes the Hebrew Bible as part of its scriptural canon, Judaism does not recognize the Christian New Testament. The relative importance of belief and practice constitute an important area of difference.\r\n\r\nAn understanding of the history of the New Testament, how it has been passed down to us, and who wrote it can increase our appreciation of this remarkable book of scripture and in turn give us greater spiritual strength as we, like the early followers of Jesus Christ, face our own trail.', 7, '2024-03-12 03:23:17', '2024-03-20 15:09:28', NULL),
(3175, 870, 687, 'It must be said that scared scripture is divinely  ordered to this; that through it, the truth necessary for salvation may be made known to us. So Thomas situates scripture squarely within the divine economy. Man was made to share in the divine Trinitarian life of God; This is his end.\r\n\r\nThe emergence of the allegorical method of interpretation in the church saw much of the Old Testament as \'unhelpful\' or \'sub-christian\'. The only way to save the Old Testament for Christian use was to distinguish a hidden spritual sense. \r\n\r\nOne serious effect of the allegorical method was that it tender to hinder people from taking the historical or natural sense of the Old testament seriously. Nor did this problem exist only for the Old Testament. Not only  was the unhelpful natural sense of the Old Testment given its spritual sense from the natural sense of the New Testament; even the natural sense of the New Testament was seen  to require its own spritual interpretation.', 3, '2024-03-14 02:19:02', '2024-03-20 15:10:01', NULL),
(3176, 870, 688, 'The early Christian theologians frequently interpreted scripture in multiple difference sense.  For example, they often draw the distinction between the plain meaning of passage and its spiritual sense.  What\'s important to realize is that any passage of Scripture can be interpreted in both sense. There is not a mutual exclusivity between the plain sense of a passage and it\'s spritual sense. Indeed, the different senses always to together; any passage of Scripture is susceptible to both levels of interpretation. \r\n\r\nOften the early theologians employ these different levels of interpretation without particularly constructing a doctrine of Scripture around them. However, some of the early theologians did construst such systems. \r\n\r\nPerhaps the most well-known of the early system of interpretation was origen\'s three levels of interpretation of Scriptures.', 3, '2024-03-14 02:29:00', '2024-03-20 15:10:01', NULL),
(3177, 870, 689, 'The word interpret can be used to mean to understand to translate or to explain . These three functions of the interpretive process are also appropriate for preaching. First, we seek to understand what the text is saying. Then we translate that information into the intended theological message. Finally, we explain that message to the congregation.\r\n\r\nThe interpreter needs to have a working knowledge of basic principles of interpretation. These hermeneutial principles are like the tricks of the trade for an interpreter.\r\n\r\nThey guide us in our examination of the text so that our work is kept within the bounds of legitimate hermeneutics. The assumption behind these principles is that  properly handled , the text will disclose its meaning to the interpreter. \r\n\r\nInterpreting the Bible - hermeneutics- is the science and art of understanding , translating, and explaining the meaning of the scripture text. \r\n\r\nTo guide this process the preacher can follow basic principles that help the interpreter discern the intended meaning of the text writer rather than imposing his own ideas on the text.', 3, '2024-03-14 02:38:51', '2024-03-20 15:10:01', NULL),
(3178, 870, 690, 'The common fallacies are usefully divided into three categories; Fallacies of Relevance, Fallacies of Unacceptable premises, and formal Fallacies. Many of these fallacies have latin names, perhaps because medieval philosophers were particularly interested in informal logic.\r\n\r\nFallacies are another way of saying false logic. These rhetorical tricks deceive your audience with their style, drama, or pattern, but add little to y our speech in terms of substance and can actually detract from your effectiveness.\r\n\r\nThere are a considerable number of different types of fallacies, many of which overlap. Five of the most common fallacies are the Appeal to ignorance, the False Dilemma, the False Cause, Ambiguity, and the Red Herring. \r\n\r\nFallacies are errors in logic and/or reasoning. We should avoid the use of fallacies in our arguments because they detract from our credibility and the legitimacy of  our message.', 3, '2024-03-14 02:52:45', '2024-03-20 15:10:01', NULL),
(3179, 870, 691, 'Proper, contextualized interpretation of the Bible is of the utmost importance to prevent the misuse and misapplication of its truths. Interpretation in context seeks to allow the Bible to speak for itself within its original setting before drawing conclusion about how it applies to our modern setting. \r\n\r\nBeing familiar with the various genres helps us read the text more accurately . For example, one of the dominant literary types in the Bible is historical narrative, such as Genesis and Acts. The Bible also contains wisdom literature such as song of Solomon, Proverbs, and Ecclesiastes. \r\n\r\nKnowing the context is key to being able to interpret anything. including the stories from the Bible. Understanding how Jesus words would have been interpreted by his original audience is an important step to being able to properly apply the truth in our own lives.\r\n\r\nIn seeking to understand the scriptures, it is important to recognize figures of speech. Scripture writers often used figures of speech to add beauty, power, and life to their messages. Some  used these devices to veil the meaning from the reader; still other saw the figures as a means to exaggeration or comparison.', 3, '2024-03-14 03:10:05', '2024-03-20 15:10:01', NULL),
(3180, 872, 619, 'There are linguistic and theological similarities between the Gospel of Luke and the Book of Acts. As on scholar writes, the extensive linguistic and theological agreements and cross-references between the Gospel of Luke and Acts indicate the both works derive from the same author. \r\n\r\nThe extrabiblical evidence that the these two books are by the same hand dates as early  as the last third of the second century, when both the Muratorian Canon and the early church irenaeus, bishop of Lyons, identify Luke as the author of both.\r\n\r\nThe Gospel of Luke and the Acts of the Apostles make up a two-part work, Luke-Acts, by the same anonymous author. It is usually  dated to around 80--90 AD. The first part, the Gospel of Luke, tells how God fulfilled his plans for the world\'s salvation through the life, death, and reserruction of Jesus of Nazareth, the promised messiah. Acts continues the story of Christianity in the 1st century.\r\n\r\nBoth of books are addressed to Theophilus, the author\'s patron- and perhaps a label for a Christian community as a whole as the name means \"Beloved of God\", and the preface of Acts explicity references \"my former book\" about the life of Jesus - almost certainly the work we know as the Gospel of Luke', 7, '2024-06-08 03:12:47', '2024-06-18 23:44:24', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3181, 872, 620, 'The early Christian church almost unanimously believed that Luke, a physician and companion of Paul the Apostle, wrote both the Gospel of Luke and the book of Acts:\r\n\r\nPapyrus Bodemr XIV\r\nThis manuscript, which dates back to around 200 AD, contains the end of Gospel of Luke and uses the subscription \"The Gospel According to Luke\"\r\n\r\nAncient Sources\r\nIrenaeus, Tertullian, Clement of Alexandria, Origen, and the Muratorian Canon all believed Luke was the author.\r\n\r\nTheophilus\r\nBoth books are written to a man named Theophilus, and the book of Acts begins with \"the former treatise have I made\" which may refer to the Gospel of Luke.\r\n\r\nThe common authorship of the two books suggests that Acts was written after Luke probably between 75 and 95 CE. during a period of increasing pressure for followers of Christ to define themselves in relation to their Jewish roots. Roman historians have generally taken the basic historicity of Acts as granted.', 7, '2024-06-08 03:23:47', '2024-06-18 23:44:24', NULL),
(3182, 872, 621, 'Third of four New Testament Gospel nd , with The Gospels According to Mark and Matthew, one of the three Synoptic Gospels. It is traditionally credited to St. Luke the beloved Physican, a close associate of the Paul the Apostle. Luke\'s Gospel is clearly written for Gentile converts: it traces Christ\'s  genealogy, for example , back to Adam, the father of the human race rather than to Abraham, the father of the Jewish people. The date and place of composition are uncertain, but many date the Gospel to 63-70 CE.\r\n\r\nLike St. Matthew, Luke derives much of his Gospel from that of St. Mark. generally following Mark\'s sequence and incorporation about 50 percentage of Mark\'s material into his work. \r\n\r\nDespite its similarities to the other Synoptic Gospels, however , Luke\'s narrative contains much  that is unique. It gives details of Jesus\'s infancy found in no other Gospel. Luke\'s Gospel is also unique in its perspective.', 7, '2024-06-08 03:38:28', '2024-06-18 23:44:24', NULL),
(3183, 872, 622, 'The essential difference between Jews and Christians is that Christians accept Jesus as messiah and personal savior. Jesus is not part of Jewish theology. Amongst Jews, Jesus is not considered a divine being . Therefor all holidays that have a connection to the life of Jesus and part of Jewish life and/or practice. \r\n\r\nJudaism originates as a result of the conventional relationship between God and Abraham. The Bible is our sacred literature. The relationship between the Jewish people and God is documented in the text. In the Bible, the history, culture, language, theology, and practices of the Jewish people are presented. \r\n\r\nThere are notable similarities in notions of sacrifice, good works, hospitality, peace, justice, pilgrimage, and afterlife and loving God with all one\'s heart and soul. All three religions honor the concept of purification by fasting.', 5, '2024-06-08 03:49:41', '2024-06-18 23:44:24', NULL),
(3184, 872, 623, 'Jesus often said things \"The kingdom of God is among you, is near, has come\". In Luke 13:10-21, Jesus has the perfect opportunity to talk further about the Kingdom. After ticking off the local synagogue ruler for doing a healing on the Sabbath, Jesus tells two super short parable about the kingdom.\r\n\r\nFirst is the Mustard seed; it\'s tiny , but grows huge. I\'ve never seen this is real life. The second is about Yeast; again it is tiny, but infects a huge lump of dough. \r\n\r\nIn the above two parables Jesus teaches that the kingdom starts small, but grows and expands with surprising hugeness. Let the Kingdom of God grow in us. By the mercies of God, to present your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable to God, which is our spiritual worship. Do not be confirmed to this world. but transoformed by the renewal of mind.', 4, '2024-06-08 04:05:19', '2024-06-18 23:44:24', NULL),
(3185, 875, 633, 'Eschatology is defined as the study of the last things. Especially when in consideration of theology, eschatology is referred to as the final days or end times of humanity and the study thereof. The term itself is derived from two words originating from Greek origin, meaning last or final and the second being study. This period is broadly understood to cover the timeframe which began with the life and ministry of Jesus and will be completed upon His return. \r\n\r\nTwo historical periods are covered within this time period, the first beginning with the Old Testament and the expectations for end times. The second with the New Testament and the realizations  associated with it. Beginning with the Old Testament, Creation to redemption leading into Eschaton shows how God had plans to lay out at the very beginning of Creation which revealed an extension of God\'s heavenly kingdom to the point of redemption where the great son of David would rule forever in Jerusalem. \r\n\r\nThe New Testament reveals that Jesus indeed brought the kingdom of God down to earth and He rules over it as king, and during the time from the Old Testament and the coming of Christ, Jewish theologians developed a view of eschatology that gained large acceptance. The viewpoints were divided into two ages, the first being the \"this age\" and the second being \"the age to come\" and during these contemporaries, Jesus made it clear that blessings would come to those that followed him.', 8, '2024-06-10 14:42:38', '2024-06-18 23:45:15', NULL),
(3186, 875, 634, 'The creation account of Genesis 1-2 tells us that we have specified God-ordained roles and responsibilities. In the book of Genesis, we can see distinct roles that God had ordained for humankind, the first being that we were designed to be servant kings to rule over all of creation on behalf of our King, God Himself. God being the great emperor had commanded humankind to reproduce and create humans throughout the world to work and take care of the Garden as we can see in Genesis 2:15. \r\n\r\nHumankind was designed to be God\'s royal priesthood to serve and honor him by ruling over the land that was given to them by God. Since man was created by God in the \'image of God\' we were given the responsibility to rule over the land and continue to populate it and turn it into His earthly kingdom. The plan was never to just populate and be done, or just rule and be done, rather the plan was to rule, populate, and to ensure that the kingdom of heaven was brought to this earth. This was referred to as the mandate by theologians, as the purpose was not to just create and populate enough people to live in this world, but rather to create a culture throughout the world like families, government, farming, animal husbandry, and even arts and sciences.', 8, '2024-06-10 14:52:59', '2024-06-18 23:45:15', NULL),
(3187, 875, 635, 'The proto-euangelion is the \'first gospel\' and is first introduced after the Fall (Genesis 3:15). This is where the plan of redemption was first introduced after the fall of mankind into sin. Due to this sin, God had cursed all of humanity and it appears that the great hope of God\'s eschatological kingdom appears to be lost. However that is where God appears in His mercy to introduce a plan of redemption and hope. He presents a plan of mercy and redemption that would rescue individuals from eternal damnation and through personal salvation, conviction and restoration allow for the hope of his great messianic kingdom on earth to be fulfilled. \r\n\r\nThe reason that theologians offer this as the first gospel or the proto-euangelion is because this is where the offer of redemption takes place and the offer of salvation after the fall into sin occurs with humankind. It is worth noting that in Genesis 3:15 soon after the fall occurs, God demonstrates his mercy and promise that he will send a deliverer to deal with our sin and problems.', 8, '2024-06-10 15:02:43', '2024-06-18 23:45:15', NULL),
(3188, 875, 636, 'Five major redemptive covenants in the Old Testaments are as follows: \r\n\r\nAdam: after he had sinned, God extended mercy and salvation through proto-euangelion commonly referred to as the first gospel. This is where redemption was offered to humankind after the fall. Despite the redemption offered, it was made clear that there would be clear consequences for the sin that was committed. \r\n\r\nNoah: while the story is familiar of how God destroyed the world that Noah and his family lived in, there was a promise and covenant that was made, that until the end of the present order of creation God would spare such a harsh rebuke and not flood the earth again. \r\n\r\nAbraham: The covenant that was made with God and Abraham was that He would extend his redemption to the rest of humanity and establish His kingdom presence in the Promised Land and towards the ends of the Earth. This would included members from all nations on earth and the kingdom of God would not just consist of a single nation or people. \r\n\r\nMoses: While people would be exiled from the Promised Land and be scattered among the people and many nations, the mercy of God would not allow Him to abandon either Moses or his people forever, and there would come a time where God will fulfill his promises of blessings for his people and judgement against his enemies. \r\n\r\nDavid: with his kingdom rule and dynasty, the covenant with David was that his dynasty would rule over God\'s earthly kingdom forever. While the kingdom of God is a real place that is populated by real people, the expectation is that this dynasty would also be real and permanent just like each of God\'s words and promises.', 8, '2024-06-10 15:13:32', '2024-06-18 23:45:15', NULL),
(3189, 875, 637, 'The two great ages expected by first century Jewish theologians were \"this age\" and \"the age to come\". When referring to \"this age\", the characterization of sin, suffering, exile, and death were mentioned at the beginning of the fall and corresponds all the way to the age of redemption. The second being the \"the age to come\" when God\'s kingdom would fill the earth followed by forgiveness, righteousness, peace and eternal life. \r\n\r\nJesus and His apostles for the most part did agree on the way that Jewish theologians characterized these ages. They were teaching that Messiah would indeed end this age and time period of sin and death while ushering in a age to come with all it\'s blessings. In 1 Corinthians 2:6-8, 2 and 2 Corinthians 4:4 and 1 Timothy 6:17-10 we can see how Paul would distinguish between this present age of evil and the age to come with the return of Christ, the Messiah. \r\n\r\nWhile Jesus and the prophets did agree to many of the teachings that were set forth, it is important to note that not all expectations were met, for example he did not lead armies to victory over all their enemies, and he did not restore the reign of David in Jerusalem. And during his time after his resurrection, Jesus spent time explaining why he did not do everything as his followers had expected him to do.', 8, '2024-06-10 15:25:19', '2024-06-18 23:45:15', NULL),
(3190, 876, 675, 'Biblical hermeneutics is a common word that is used frequently within theology and biblical studies derived from the Greek terminology of \'hermes\' the mythological messenger of the gods. The term itself is defined as to \'interpret\' or to \'explain\'. Therefore when defining what is biblical hermeneutics, the meaning is simply the study of interpreting the meaning and significance of Scripture. Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768-1834) is the father of modern hermeneutics. Biblical hermeneutics refers to the interpretation and or explanation of some type of message or communication.', 5, '2024-06-10 15:31:15', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3191, 876, 676, 'The biblical roots of scientific hermeneutics can not be discounted or avoided simply because people living in biblical times were not modern scientists. Quite the contrary, they were sophisticated and had many accomplishments, and there are examples of biblical roots of scientific hermeneutics within the Bible. One notable example is the most influential figure in biblical interpretation of Origen of Alexandria. He lived from A.D. 185-253 and went far beyond scientific interpretations, and devoted himself to careful factual and rational analyses of the Bible. He crafted the Hexapla, a 6,000 page work consisting of more than 50 volumes which made a word-by-word comparison of various Hebrew and Greek version of the Old Testament. \r\nAnother example would be Augustine of Hippo who would focus on factual and rational analysis of the Bible which at time was often painstaking. Then came Thomas Aquinas, followed by the Renaissance period which analyzed the grammar and historical contexts of the biblical manuscripts. The Protestant Reformation dealt with a thorough analysis of the Bible\'s grammar and historical context.', 7, '2024-06-10 15:38:07', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3192, 876, 677, 'The biblical roots of devotional hermeneutics are emphasized significantly as theologians and biblical scholars have encouraged followers of Christ to read the Scripture time and time again, in the presence of God in order to bring upon enlightenment and even supernatural experiences from God. This can be displayed in Scripture, in the passage of Hebrews 4:12, where the word of God is noted to be living and sharper than any two edged sword and can penetrate the soul. \r\nExamples of biblical roots of devotional hermeneutics include the Letter of Origen to Gregory where Origen told Gregory that he should devote himself to the divine reading or Lectio Divinia. Throughout the medieval period, Lectio Divinia was practiced in four steps: the reading of the scriptures, the silent ponderings of the content which was read, praying to God to grant enlightenment on what was read, and then waiting for the Spirit of God to grant intuitive and deeply emotional convictions of a passage\'s significance. \r\nCalvin had insisted that when you read the Scriptures, you should be so affected as though God himself were face to face with us. Therefore you should take the initiative to read from Scripture as an all consuming, intense emotional and humbling experience in God\'s presence.', 7, '2024-06-10 15:47:45', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3193, 876, 678, 'Two important aspects of organic inspiration are that you need to prepare yourself for when you enter the presence of God whether that is reading or meditating before God. When you get ready for God\'s presences you are getting your heart ready to believe in God\'s words as being true. For example, if you believe in false concepts and since before the word of God, without an open heart and mind, you will not be ready to accept the words that you read and you must be willing to draw near to God to read through that behavioral preparation. Another is to seek God\'s nearness through emotional readiness and preparation. This includes all of our attitudes, preconceived notions, and the our feelings about God, human beings, and the rest of creation. Our emotions can have either a positive or negative affect on how we approach the presence of God and whether we are ready to hear from God. \r\n\r\nThe other aspect that you need to get ready for is the investigative piece. You have to look at the context of what the biblical authors intended with their writings and how God intends to bring that message to us. Paying attention to the detail and concepts that God and his authors intended for these writings to communicate with us must be closely tied to the organic acts of inspiration for us to be willing to accept the words we read. Lastly application, don\'t treat the word of God as a lifeless object, rather be willing to accept the word humbly and on an emotional level being able to apply that Scripture to our attitude and feelings as well.', 8, '2024-06-10 15:55:20', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3194, 876, 679, 'The doctrine of illumination is simply answering the question of how do we receive knowledge from God/of God and how does that knowledge impact us? We have to be able to answer the question of how does God make himself known to us? By reading the word of God and allowing the Holy Spirit to help us understand the truth of God\'s word, we are able to understand the concepts through biblical inspiration and interpretation. This involves devout study and truth in spending time before God\'s word. While the Holy Spirit indwells within our lives, the Holy Spirit also allows for the guidance of all truth to be revealed as described in John 16:13-14. The Spirit will guide you on all the truth, and will reveal all that is to come. That is the illumination that the Bible teaches us about. \r\nBy following the teachings of Paul in the writings of Ephesians, Christians are encouraged live out their faith by reading the gospel and he prays for illumination to happen in Ephesians 5:15-19. He asks that the revelation of God be revealed with his spirit of wisdom and knowledge, so that we will know what is the hope of his calling and the riches of his glory and the boundless greatness of his power towards those who believe.', 8, '2024-06-10 16:02:21', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3195, 876, 680, 'The benefits that I have gained from an interpreter of Scripture has unfolded in a few different ways. For example, in my personal devotion, I always try to take the context of the writer and the audience. I then try to imagine the times in which this was written and understand the context and relate it to myself if it were me. I then try to take it in a forward thinking approach to present day and see how I can make it apply to my current day situation. The next thing I have gained is by being able to take conceptual teaching and translate them into modern day examples and be able to teach that back to my family, children, and youth group. For example, one common example is the story of Joseph and how he was abandoned by his family and taken as a prisoner, and then brought in a ruler and had the opportunity to fall into temptation. We can use that one story to describe many things we face on a day to day basis and I believe that once you are able to sit before God, in his presence and mediate, God will reveal many things to you. Another example is how I was able to learn from the life of Jonah, and many times we overlook the tiny worm that came and ate the shade that he was sitting under, but sometimes in our own lives, we overlook the creature, the worm that comes and ends up taking away the one thing that was protecting us and giving us cover. Being able to exegete the scripture, in a meaningful way is to look before the black and white text and allowing God to speak to you.', 8, '2024-06-10 16:07:26', '2024-06-18 23:46:36', NULL),
(3196, 879, 692, 'The immutable character of God helps us to see that there will always be a vital connection that occurs between the original meaning and the modern application of Scripture. We as Christians believe in the same God as the audience of Scripture as God is never changing and immutable in his promises and covenants, He continues to fulfill everything that has been sworn. This is displayed in Scripture through various aspects, beginning with Covenant Promises. When a promise is made by the Creator of Heaven and Earth, there is a certain level of weight that comes with that promise, and it is immutable. If it were simply a man that were to make a person, we are flawed and can be free to change our minds, however the Scripture uses several examples, one for example in the context of Abraham where God made a covenant that Abraham\'s offspring would be as numerous as the stars in the sky.\r\n\r\nThere are certain instances where God did not make a promise, and rather his words are best understood to be either threats, or even curses, and also offers of blessings. While all of this has occurred in Scripture, we as mankind continue to live in this same world that continues to evolve and change just as the days in Scripture. The first audiences lived in times where the world was not constant and change was inevitable. The people also are relatable from the first audience to those of us in today\'s day and age. While we are all created in the image of God, we have all fallen into sin and sinful nature. We continue to also have division amongst us, and religious quarrels. There continues to be several classes of people existing both then and now. Therefore while we may feel that we live in modern times, we should note that many of the similarities exist from the days when Scripture was written to today\'s day and age, however one thing remains constant, and that is God and His promises.', 9, '2024-06-15 03:10:47', '2024-06-18 23:47:14', NULL),
(3197, 879, 693, 'There are several ways that people today are similar to the original audiences from Scripture. The first is that while God had created man in his own image and intended for them to live a life of dominion over the Earth, mankind sinned and fell into sinful nature. That continues to occur day after day even in today\'s day and age. Every person born on this Earth, except for the Son of God share this same sinful nature. The second is religious divisions between the original and modern audience. There are three groups that are identified in Scripture, the unbelievers, the false believers, and the believers. The Unbelievers are categorized as the people who refuse to submit to God, and there were several mentions of these type of people in the Bible and we experience that even now. Then there are the false believers who make superficial committments to God and continue to do so in today\'s age. Lastly, the believers who make faithful committments to God, which is what we are all called to do. Lastly, there are the different classes of people that existed both in the original times of Scripture and also modern times. Many of the context around Scriptural portions are directed towards particular classes, and the connections shared between these classes can help guide mankind even in today\'s modern times with the use of Scripture.', 8, '2024-06-15 03:15:57', '2024-06-18 23:47:14', NULL),
(3198, 879, 694, 'There are many epochal or significant developments that have occurred over the course of redemptive history that have made our world different from the world of the Bible. For example the perspective laid out in the Bible is separated by three stages. The first being Creation, when God first made the world. The second being the Fall, when mankind fell into the temptation of sin and when sin was first committed and mankind was cursed. The last being redemption, when God showed His mercy and redeemed us from our sin. One example of how epochal developments occurred within history can be traced back to the laws regarding atoning sacrifices and how they required different things at different points in history. For example, during the time of Moses, they required sacrifices at the tabernacle, and then during the days of Solomon, they required sacrifices at the temple. In the early days of the New Testament, the required Jesus to be hung to death on a cross, and then later on in the New Testament, they ceased to be offered altogether. There have been many cultural developments between the original and modern audiences as well that must be taken into consideration, along with personal developments that distinguish people today from the audiences of the Bible.', 8, '2024-06-15 03:22:53', '2024-06-18 23:47:14', NULL),
(3199, 879, 695, 'Theologians have divided Old Testament history in a variety of ways and there are explanations for this diversity. One popular way was by associating each epoch with one of God\'s covenants. There are six major covenants that are notated in the Old Testament, Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, and the new covenant that Old Testament prophets predicted would come at the end of Israel\'s exile from the Promised Land. Another way that theologians have divided the Old Testament history is through implications. Due to the divisions in the Old Testament, we can clearly see that God wanted his people to apply different theological themes at different times in different ways. For example, if there were a time that God had commanded a new way for sacrifice in worship, he expected that the people of God would abide by the new commandment and not ever return to their old ways.', 5, '2024-06-15 03:29:48', '2024-06-18 23:47:14', NULL),
(3200, 879, 696, 'New Testament believers should never apply the Scriptures as if they live in earlier periods of history because once God commands a new way for his people, He expects his people to never to return to the old ways. This can be seen in the example of how God had commanded his people to offer sacrifices only at the temple. In historical context, by offering a sacrifice at Moses\' tabernacle would violate God\'s will. This would be true as well if you lived under the covenant of Moses and ended up building altars in new locations, just as Abraham and the forefathers had originally done. Once Jesus was slain for our sins, there no need for these sacrifices any longer. Christ gave us the perfect sacrifice once and for all, and  that is why when we look at the Old Testament, it is very important to study and get familiar with, however we live in a very different, yet significant time period. We should never try to disregard and ignore the old ways, however we should also not try to live and serve God as if we are still living in the original days.', 5, '2024-06-15 03:35:43', '2024-06-18 23:47:14', NULL),
(3201, 880, 638, 'Individual eschatology is defined as the study of how individual human beings experience the events of the last days. This is specifically relevant when considering the context around life, death, the intermediate state, and the final state or conclusion of our bodies an soul. \r\n\r\nRegenerate means being \'recreated\' or \'reborn\' in the terminology of theology. In terms of individuals who are spiritually alive or \'living\'. While humans are considered \'born\' when they receive physical life, this term is in relation to a spiritual birth which is obtained by the Holy Spirit. Our spiritual life is everlasting and eternal and that life never ends, when our earthly bodies die, our souls will continue to last forever.\r\n\r\nUnregenerate refers to human beings who are considered spiritually dead. They are defined by three characteristics, the first being their spiritual death, the second their moral inability and the last being their enmity with God. The only way to give them life is through the Holy Spirit which would allow them to \'regenerate\' allowing for a rebirth spiritually. While Adam and Eve were created with spiritual life and given dominion to rule over earth, their sin caused them to be cursed and resulted in spiritual death.', 8, '2024-06-27 01:37:15', '2024-11-20 20:32:53', NULL),
(3202, 880, 639, 'The present state of the unregenerate which is when humanity is currently living during the last days is separated by three characteristics. The first being their spiritual death, the second being moral inability, and the last being their enmity with God. When considering spiritual death, it is far different from a physical death. Physical death will result in physical decay of our flesh and cause us to return to dust. With spiritual death, your soul is still living within our bodies. Spiritual death was first brought on by Go through the disobedience of Adam and Eve when they fell into sin. God had cursed humanity with spiritual death. \r\n\r\nThe second characteristic of moral inability is known as the fact that humans do not have the capacity to please God or obtain his blessing through merit. When considering Augustine\'s states of moral ability before and after the fall, he referred to Adam and Even living in a state where they were to be \'able to not sin\' and they were empowered to completely avoid sin. But they also had the ability to sin, and when the did indeed sin, they along with all of humanity lost the moral ability to avoid sin.\r\n\r\nThe last characteristic is enmity with God which according to scripture refer to man loving their sin and hating God. This is called out in Ephesians 2:1-3 where Paul refers to the unregenerate and states the dead in their sins are following the ways of this world and gratify the cravings of their own desires', 9, '2024-06-27 01:45:34', '2024-11-20 20:32:53', NULL),
(3203, 880, 640, 'The regenerate come to experience spiritual life in the present state by experience a passing from death into life. This is a type of spiritual resurrection where rebirth occurs. In order for us to receive God\'s blessings, we have to admit that we can not do so by good works, or merits. Rather we have to undergo a second birth and that is a spiritual rebirth. John 3:3-6 refers to how no one is able to inherit the kingdom of God unless you are born of water and spirit. The Holy Spirit has to give us rebirth and render regeneration. \r\n\r\nIt clearly affects our moral ability as we know that nothing we ever do will allow us to obtain the blessings of God through our own merit. Rather than trying to obtain your salvation, forgiveness, righteousness, and eternal life are free gifts from God which is based only on God\'s merit. The Holy Spirit regenerates our soul and gives us spiritual life. The Holy Spirit also allows us the ability to avoid sin and be able to pleas God to do good works. \r\n\r\nBy being able to have this moral ability and prepare good works proves the fact that we have regained our moral ability and as a result of grace we have the Spirit dwelling in us. This then in returns allows for our good works to be rewarded with heavenly treasures and crowns, and even those who endure persecution will be rewarded. God calls us to imitate him and love each other while we keep his law. If we are to do so, our rewards prepared in heaven are great.', 9, '2024-06-27 01:53:21', '2024-11-20 20:32:53', NULL),
(3204, 880, 641, 'Unregenerate souls experience the intermediate state even after a body fails to exist and when we die. In Ecclesiastes 12:7 we read of how when the dust returns to the ground it came from, the spirit returns to God who gave it. For the unregenerate, you should be afraid, because even when your physical death occurs, your soul continues to exist and will return to God. And when that soul returns to God, it won\'t be to enjoy the pleasures and rewards that awaits you in heaven, rather it will be a time of judgement and punishment in hell. \r\n\r\nIn Luke 12:4-5, we read of how you should not fear the one who kills your body, because that is the extent of what they can do to you, however the one you should fear is the one who has the power to cast you into hell even after your body has been killed. The names that are typically used for \'hell\' in scripture teach us about the nature of the situation in a variety of ways. For example, in the Old Testament, the term Sheol is referred to as the home of unregenerate souls. Along with Abaddon known as a place of destruction. Bowr or pit refers to a place of punishment. In the New Testament Hades is used as a place of punishement, and Abussos refers to a place for prison or demons.', 8, '2024-06-27 01:59:20', '2024-11-20 20:32:53', NULL),
(3205, 880, 642, 'The Bible presents the experiences of regenerate souls during the intermediate state as those who are able to enjoy a sample or taste of their final blessings. In Luke 23:43, we see the thief on the cross who was promised by God to be in paradise that very day. In Revelation 6:9 we read of the souls of the martyrs being close to the altar in God\'s heavenly tabernacle. In Philippians 1:23 we read of how \'I desire to depart and be with Christ, which is better by far.\' When the regenerate die, their bodies are put in the grave, and their souls are immediately taken into the presence of God in Heaven, where they will remain until the final resurrection. Regenerate souls have fellowship with other regenerate souls in heaven. \r\n\r\nIn Revelation 6:9 we read of how martyrs in heaven know and have fellowship wit hone another and they continue to speak with one voice as they appeal to God to hasten the final judgement. Hebrews 12:22-23 speaks of a gathering of heavenly assembly where the spirits of righteous men made perfect assemble together.', 8, '2024-06-27 02:08:11', '2024-11-20 20:32:53', NULL),
(3206, 881, 648, 'Two predominant first-century schools of thought concerning the resurrection of the dead were between the Pharisees and the Sadducees. The Pharisees believed that there would be a general resurrection of both the righteous and the wicked. While the Sadducees denied that a physical resurrection would take place for the dead. \r\n\r\nThis is relevant to note as the Pharisees rooted their belief on the entire Old Testament, whereas the Sadducees only accepted the five books written by Moses (Genesis through Deuteronomy). When looking at Scripture and the positions that Jesus, Paul, and the rest of the early church, the view that was taken was clearly that of the Pharisees. It is clearly taught in Scripture, in Isaiah 26:19, and Daniel 12:2 that there will be a general resurrection, and this will be for both the righteous and the wicked. \r\n\r\nEven while the Sadducees would argue and challenge this issue, Jesus responded in Mark 12:26-27 that the Sadducees had indeed misunderstood Moses and that Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob were to be alive as spirits and that they would eventually be resurrected.', 8, '2024-06-28 01:37:12', '2024-11-20 20:33:27', NULL),
(3207, 881, 649, 'Divine authority is God\'s legal and moral right to carry out his will. If God decides to do something, it is well within His rights to do so and he never makes a mistake. Everything that He chooses to do is right. When it comes to the authority of God in regards to the general resurrection over both hell and heaven it is crucial to note that God has complete authority over hell. This is commonly misunderstood that Satan rules hell and God has no authority over hell. The people that have fallen and are suffering in hell is because God is allowing for that to happen and they are facing judgement. God rules over hell, rules over Satan, and the demons including the unregenerate souls that he has imprisoned there. \r\n\r\nWhen it comes to God\'s authority in heaven, heaven is the throne room of God and that is where his authority and glory are manifested openly. God has declared this in Isaiah 66:1, where He declares \"Heaven is my throne.\" Even in the Lord\'s Prayer, we see that God\'s will is done in heaven perfectly and that is why it is the model for the new heavens and earth that will be created at the end of the eschaton.', 8, '2024-06-28 01:43:03', '2024-11-20 20:33:27', NULL),
(3208, 881, 650, 'The general resurrection will have an impact and effect on creation three different ways. It will impact the natural world, hell, and heaven. In regards to how the general resurrection will impact the natural world, God will uses events in the end time to alter how the natural world operates in order to prepare for his presence. When mankind fell into the trap of sin, the curse of God was placed upon us and it became a place of danger and death. However, in Romans 8, Paul looked forward to the general resurrection as a solution to this curse/problem. With the general resurrection, the Earth would be rescued through the resurrection of redeemed humanity. With this redemption, creation itself will be liberated. \r\n\r\nThe impact on hell will be that when the unregenerate are resurrected, their souls will be removed from hell and then they will have their bodies returned to earth so that they can face God\'s judgement. However this won\'t just apply to the unregenerate, Satan and the demons in hell will be emptied from hell. According to some theologians, Revelation 20 teaches that Satan and demos will be released from their imprisonment in hell to participate in a final rebellion against God. \r\n\r\nThe impact on heaven is that heaven will no longer be the residence of regenerate souls as a result of the general resurrection. While heaven is a wonder place, the intention of God was never for us to live there forever. God has something even better for us in mind in the new heavens and earth. The resurrected regenerate will return to earth in order to appear before God\'s judgement throne. Heaven will be emptied of it\'s angels and when Jesus returns, he will bring all of his angels with him and they will collect the resurrected regenerate  according to Matthew 24:31.', 8, '2024-06-28 01:52:29', '2024-11-20 20:33:27', NULL),
(3209, 881, 651, 'The three parties or groups that will face the final judgement are the fallen angels which are also known as demons, the unregenerate and the regenerate. When it comes to the fallen angels, 2 Peter 2:4 and Jude 6 mention that the demos which at one point were angels will be awaiting their final judgement. These are the angels that rebelled against God, and abandoned their heavenly homes and his authority. The angels that did not fall, and never sinned against God will not be included in this judgement. \r\nThe second group being the unregenerate are the wicked people that rejected God and they are separated from the righteous people that accepted God. John received these visions and it is mentioned in Revelation 11:18, and 20:11-15, Christ will indeed condemn the unregenerate. Christ will sit on his throne and separate the people one from another and say to those on the left, \'depart from me\' and they will be sent to eternal punishment. \r\nThe last group is the regenerate which is mentioned in Matthew 25:33-34. Christians are mistakenly believing that the regenerate won\'t be judged at all. However, unless the regenerate are part of the judgement, they would not receive their everlasting rewards.', 8, '2024-06-28 02:00:14', '2024-11-20 20:33:27', NULL),
(3210, 881, 652, 'Ecclesiastes 12:14 mentions that God will bring every deed into judgement, including every hidden thing, whether it is good or evil. God will take into consideration every imaginable piece of evidence and listen to witnesses, and weigh extenuating circumstances so that nothing will be left out and nothing will be inadmissible. This is done for perfect justice and every reward and every punishment will perfectly fit for everyone who is judged. For example, in Matthew 12:36, it is revealed that man will have to give account for every word, every careless word that has been spoken. \r\nHowever, not everyone will be held to the same level of accountability. We will be judged according to our situations, for those who have sinned blatantly, with more knowledge of God\'s requirements, will be judged more harshly. God will hold us accountable to our covenant obligations in the judgement, and we are bound to God and his judgement and we are obligated to obey him. We need to be very clear with this judgment as it is extremely strict.', 8, '2024-06-28 02:04:17', '2024-11-20 20:33:27', NULL),
(3211, 877, 681, 'The theological basis for giving attention to the biblical writers in our exploration of the meaning of Scripture is done so in three aspects. First the basis is giving attention to the writer, second to the original audience, and lastly to the document itself. Jesus describes in Matthew 22:41-45 that David\'s authorship of Psalm 110 was being referred to. This led the audience and reader to be specifically in line with the fact that a human author was acknowledged as significant. There is incredible value in understanding the original context that a writer has to understand a specific part of the Scripture. \r\n\r\nSecond basis being the original audience, because looking back God would also allow for His Word to be delivered to His people in ways that suited their historical circumstance. God\'s words and ideas were revealed to his people in a way that would be understood by it\'s first audience. The words and ideas of Scripture were adapted to meet the needs of the current culture, technology, social structures and even the religious experiences of the time so that everyone who heard it would understand what was being said. Along every spectrum, the Scripture was written to suit the universal condition of humans. \r\n\r\nLastly, the detail given to the document requires a reader to go back and look at Scripture itself. While modern translations are great and represent one of the most important teaching ministries of the church, we must be careful and wise to not be dependent on a single word of phrase which may differ from one translation to another. There is organic inspiration and the doctrine of divine accomodation which are two important doctrines that emphasis the biblical doctrines in their respective ways. With organic inspiration, the Holy Spirit inspired humans to write scripture. Whereas with Divine Accomodation, everything in Scripture came from cultural conventions of it\'s day.', 9, '2024-06-28 12:45:12', '2024-11-20 20:34:06', NULL),
(3212, 877, 682, 'Organic inspiration and divine accommodation helps us understand the original meaning of biblical document. Organic inspiration being Holy Spirit driven inspiration that led human authors to write the Scripture. As humans are mere mortals and make mistakes in their natural form, the only true way to understand the words of God are to go back to the original words ands translations that were set forth, because over time translations caused changes that have taken place over time and this can alter the meaning that was originally intended. One important factor to note is that the original documents of the Old Testament did not exist in Jesus day, only slight variations of the text in Hebrew versions existed at that time. \r\nWith divine accomodation, the words, grammar, and literary style rose from the conventions of culture as it related to that day and age. If we are to pay careful attention to the ways that Scripture reflects these conventions, we are more likely to interpret it correctly. \r\nThese two aspects are very important as the original context and scripture of God has the full authority of God, and the better that we understand the original meaning, the better that we as mankind will be able to apply it to our lives each day.', 9, '2024-06-28 12:54:12', '2024-11-20 20:34:06', NULL),
(3213, 877, 683, 'I personally find a high level of confidence in knowing that scholars today have access to many manuscripts that contain the original words of Scripture. Biblical passages can be complicated and the original intentions of the authors may at times be multifaceted. While considering the genre considerations as well, things tend to be more complicated as well. This can create several intracacies of the original meaning of the Scripture, and can then require Christians to have a complex approach when considering the meaning of Scripture. \r\nWhile there is a high level of confidence in knowing that scholars have the access to many manuscripts which contain the original words of Scripture, the greatest defeat that must be considered is that while God had spoken his inspired Word to come to life, the iterations and translations and interpretations are still being done by humans. That is where there is still room for error to be made, motives to be different and where one person can decide how the word should be interpreted. This can then create vague limitations and boundaries of the biblical passage and meanings, and the translation can vary from one person to another.', 9, '2024-06-28 13:03:50', '2024-11-20 20:34:06', NULL),
(3214, 877, 684, 'Three major approaches towards the objects and subjects of knowledge are objective, subjective, and dialogical. When considering objective approaches, scholars believe that they can impartially interpret the Scripture. With background and influence, this is achieved by scholars in obtaining an exhaustive understanding of everything that they have studied. There are both philisophical and cultural backgrounds to consider with this approach of knowledge. \r\nThe second approach is that of subjective which is where subjectivists acknowledge human beings, the world, and matters of faith are often too complex to be discerned by scientific rationalization. Therefore many of their search for answers are relied upon heavily with emotions and intuition. For example, when Jesus said that we must love one another, there was an authoritative command with that. However as human beings, this is taken into consideration according to the weakness of mankind with emotion and intuition. \r\nLastly, the dialogical approach is where dialog and discussion is required between the reader and the text. The Psalmist refers to this when they talk about spending time in the word and meditating on it day and night, this requires that a reader be able to spend time with the text and have an open dialogue to understand the context and the meaning with that Scripture.', 9, '2024-06-28 13:16:13', '2024-11-20 20:34:06', NULL),
(3215, 877, 685, 'The philosophical and cultural background of subjective approaches to interpretation were tied back to David Hume who believed that reason and scientific study can not lead us to objective knowledge, and rather our emotions and intuition will guide us to influence our thinking. Immanuel Kant believed that we only perceive the world as it appears to us and that we already have a conceptualized realization in place. When considering cultural backgrounds, romanticisim would consider that poetry, drama and music would provide an understanding of reality that is far superior to rationale scientific discourse. This is where subjective interpretors would argue that the meaning of art and literature would be included within us. Despite several backgrounds and errors, this background does allow for a significant impact in how we view and understand the Bible. God allows for a different outlook in our lives and how we understand the Bible to be able to apply it in our lives.', 9, '2024-06-28 13:22:30', '2024-11-20 20:34:06', NULL),
(3216, 877, 686, 'The authority dialog and objective models and the authority dialog and subject models can be compared and contrasted as follows. The authority dialogue model acknowledges that the objective truth can be found within the context of the Scripture. It helps us by avoiding the thought process that we can be utterly objective while approaching the Scripture. The subjective model acknowledges that our perspectives and beliefs tend to influence the way that we are interpretting Scripture. Which essentially means that if we do not submit ourselves to the authority of the Scripture, then our view and interpretation of the Scripture will be severely hindered. This is a lifelong process in the life of a Christian and will allow for us to grow and mature in our Christian faith.', 0, '2024-06-28 13:26:04', '2024-11-20 20:34:15', NULL),
(3217, 878, 687, 'Thomas Aquinas and other interpreters argued for a singular meaning of Scripture as they insisted that every legitimate spiritual interpretation was an extension of the literal sense of a passage. He taught that nothing necessary to faith was communicated without being taught somewhere else in Scripture in literal sense. During the time period of the Renaissance, Protestants would execute on the ideas that were championed by Thomas Aquinas. They would argue that all the spiritual aspects of a text were actually intended by the author and it would be for the original audience and are actually aspects of it\'s literal sense. \r\nWhile the Scripture is multifaceted, the passages within Scripture have meanings with many small parts and aspects that can contribute to singular, unified meanings called by literal sense.', 8, '2024-06-29 12:37:36', '2024-08-14 04:06:05', NULL),
(3218, 878, 688, 'The three types of biblical elaborations that are often used in Scripture are broken down into three parts, repetition of a facet of the original meaning, a clarification of things which were not entirely understood, and lastly an expansion of the meaning of a particular passage. The repetition of a facet is confirmed in Deuteronomy 5 where Moses is reminding the nation of the content which was within the Ten Commandments to Israel. This continued repetition enabled the people of God to remember the terms of his covenant. With clarification, we can see this example portrayed with the clarification of the commandment against murder described in Numbers 35. Moses clarifies between murder and other accidental manslaughter and the differences between the two. Lastly with the expansion of the meaning of a particular passage, an example is laid out for us in Matthew chapter 5, where Jesus criticized the rabbis of his day for incorrectly limiting the commandment\'s scope around murder. Jesus went on to explain the commandment that he had set out and went beyond just simple clarification. It did not just simply explain what was laid out, rather it brought additional clarification to the meaning of the commandment itself.', 8, '2024-06-29 12:46:48', '2024-08-14 04:06:05', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3219, 878, 689, 'The more that we are able to study Scripture, the more we are able to see and decipher that the Scripture elaborates and provides clarity over and over agin. While interpreting the Scripture, we are able to make these words relevant of those in our sphere of influence without misapplying them. WE are to explore the meaning of Scripture, and acknowledge and submit ourselves to each place that the Scripture elaborates on. \r\nWe as Christians who live in a modern world that deals with sins such as abortion, euthanasia, suicide, war, poverty and other threats to human life and dignity have responsibilities that have been placed upon us to make sure that we interpret the Scriptures appropriately. It is definitely normal to be bias and sway our opinions and thoughts one way versus another, however the Bible is clear and distinct on the prohibition of murder for example and many other topics. It is our responsibility to interpret Scripture and figure out what these responsibilties mean. While we are in the process of doing so, we are able to reveal more fully what the meaning of the commandment truly is and we are able to influence those that are around us without have a misapplication of the commandment.', 9, '2024-06-29 12:51:57', '2024-08-14 04:06:05', NULL),
(3220, 878, 690, 'The more that we are able to study Scripture, the more we are able to see and decipher that the Scripture elaborates and provides clarity over and over agin. While interpreting the Scripture, we are able to make these words relevant of those in our sphere of influence without misapplying them. WE are to explore the meaning of Scripture, and acknowledge and submit ourselves to each place that the Scripture elaborates on. \r\nWe as Christians who live in a modern world that deals with sins such as abortion, euthanasia, suicide, war, poverty and other threats to human life and dignity have responsibilities that have been placed upon us to make sure that we interpret the Scriptures appropriately. It is definitely normal to be bias and sway our opinions and thoughts one way versus another, however the Bible is clear and distinct on the prohibition of murder for example and many other topics. It is our responsibility to interpret Scripture and figure out what these responsibilties mean. While we are in the process of doing so, we are able to reveal more fully what the meaning of the commandment truly is and we are able to influence those that are around us without have a misapplication of the commandment. \r\nThe', 8, '2024-06-29 12:56:32', '2024-08-14 04:06:05', NULL),
(3221, 878, 691, 'The uniqueness of the biblical interpreter can lead us to multiple summaries of biblical texts and these multiple summaries are important to interpretation as they bring us as the reader closer to what the real intent and meaning of that passage was meant to be. All interpreters are different, they come with a different set of backgrounds, concerns, assumptions, and viewpoints. Their personal strength and weakness have a definite impact on how the passage is defined and interpreted. \r\nBy having more than one summary, you are able to have more than one viewpoint on a passage and different viewpoints allow for the reader to have less of a bridge between the original meaning of the passage and the audience and what it is actually intended for. As readers, it is crucial to find summaries to the passage so that you can gain a better viewpoint and understanding of what is written. This allows for a multifaceted view and approach in understanding what is actually set forth for us a reader of the Word of God.', 8, '2024-06-29 13:00:32', '2024-08-14 04:06:05', NULL),
(3222, 882, 927, 'God is three persons in one essence, meaning that there is one substance in three persons. Our belief is that there is only one God, not three Gods. There are three persons within this one God, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The Father is the one who sends the Son and the Holy Spirit. Think of God as being the Father figure, the Father sends the Son to the Earth, and once the Son dies and is resurrected, He goes to the Father. At that time, they send the Holy Spirit. \r\n\r\nBeing able to understand this also requires the understanding that God is one divine essence. He was never created, He was the Creator. He is the one who created the universe and all of the orders within. Many times we try to think of God as three different individuals, however they are the same essence, but with three different roles and responsibilities. The Father can not replace the Son, and the Son cannot replace the Father. Likewise the Holy Spirit can not replace the Father and vice versa. The three in one has a distinct meaning in that God has provided a clear distinction for each role of the Triune concept to understand the real meaning of Trinity.', 9, '2024-08-05 01:15:34', '2024-08-14 04:04:55', NULL),
(3223, 882, 928, 'In respect to the Trinity, a person is the distinction given to the roles of Father, Son and Holy Spirit. One thing to be clear on is that we do not mean the modern concept of an individual focused on the concept of personality. Looking at the New Testament, we can see passages of how the Father, Son and Holy Spirit speak to one another about each other. They love one another in some way that suggests the notion of communication amongst each other. While there remains one substance, you have three persons in that one substance. The persons do not have their own individual essence, rather they abide within one divine essence. \r\n\r\nA great example of this is displayed during the baptism of Jesus Christ by John the Disciple. When Jesus had emerged from the water, there was a sound from heaven, declaring the favor of God upon His Son in whom He was well pleased. That is the distinct person of the Father speaking directly to His Son. Then we see the example of the Holy Spirit in the form of a dove coming into the scene. This is the person of the Holy Spirit within the realm of the Triuness of God. There is one essential essence, but three persons.', 7, '2024-08-05 01:24:04', '2024-08-14 04:04:55', NULL),
(3224, 882, 929, 'The reason that God\'s divine threeness does not violate the doctrine of God\'s essence is because you can not divide God into three different people. It is tricky, because people tend to confuse Trinity with tritheism, and what it makes it more difficult is that man cannot analyze him and figure this out for themselves. Theology shows us the \'simplicity\' of God, and an analogy that has been used many times to describe the essence of God is related to water. With water, you can have different forms of one essence, for example when water is at normal temperature, it take the form of liquid. Under cold temperature, the water turns hard and into ice, but still remains under the essence of water. Lastly when water is boiled and heated to a certain degree, it can cause steam and evaporation which is still an essence of water. The Father will always remain the Father of His Son. His Son will always remain the Son of His Father, and that goes back to forever in time. The Holy Spirit has always been there, and the three of them are inseparable. \r\n\r\nIn the book of Matthew, we see of Jesus talking about how if anyone regardless of who, receives \'one of my disciples in my name, they receive the One who sent me.\' When you receive the disciples that were sent by the God, and has the Spirit of God in them, then you are receiving God, the Father, and the Son and the Holy Spirit. God is one but three persons that are of one divine essence.', 8, '2024-08-05 01:33:28', '2024-08-14 04:04:55', NULL),
(3225, 882, 930, 'Christ always existed, simply put he was there before time. Creation happened through Christ, existed through Christ, and continues through Christ. In the opening verses of the Bible, \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the Earth\" Without God, there would be no creation. If we look at the book of Hebrews, we can also see the first thing that is called out is the creation and how God appointed him the \'heir of all things.\' If you are a Son then you are considered as the heir, and so as the Son, he is the revelation of the God and identifies with God and the Creation. \r\n\r\nChrist was the beginning of everything and the Scripture teaches us about the void before the Creation. Only after there was creation, was there the Earth, moon, and stars. Only then was there a distinction between the Earth and the waters, and only then was there any distinction of any form of being. While the word of God teaches us that \'in the beginning was the word, and the word was with God, and the word was God.\' There was no other alternative to God being created, he was the beginning of all beginnings. The other factor to consider here is also faith, without the belief of faith in your Christian life, you will begin to doubt and question many things. The way to get past that is to ask, \'does this hinder my walk with God, and does it affect my eternity?\' and if the answer is no, simply move and accept the word and truth of God.', 8, '2024-08-05 01:40:42', '2024-08-14 04:04:55', NULL),
(3226, 882, 931, 'The Trinitarian formulas in the New Testament help us see the equality of the Holy Spirit with the other members of the Godhead by clear distinction within the Scripture. The Scriptures mentions God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit as being equal members in the Trinity. One of these examples is portrayed in water baptism. When an individual is baptized, Scripture teaches us to baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. One name, three persons in one, and one God. When the book of Matthew ends, Scripture teaches us that Jesus ordered his disciples to go and make disciples of all nations and baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. He did not command them to baptize them in just the name of the Father, or just the name of the Son, or just the Holy Spirit, but giving equal rights and power among all three to do so. \r\n\r\nThe Great Commission and the commands given in 2 Corinthians are clear examples of how the Bible portrays the Trinitarian formulas describing the equality of the Holy Spirit among the other persons of the Triune God. With baptism, we have listed how the command is to baptize in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. And in 2 Corinithians, the command given is to depart in fellowship as \'the grace of our Lord Jesus Chris, the love of the God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with us all\" which describes the three in one approach.', 8, '2024-08-05 01:48:27', '2024-08-14 04:04:55', NULL),
(3227, 883, 932, 'The Trinity is more clearly expressed in the New Testament than in the Old Testament. This does not necessarily mean that the Old Testament is wrong or less important, rather is an incomplete revelation where God did not reveal himself all at once. This occurs through progressive revelation where through the canon of Scripture and moving throughout the Scripture and New Testament we can see that in passages such as Matthew 28, where the Great Commission is outlined. Or you can take a look within Ephesians 4 and 2 Corinthians 13 where you can see the full package or depiction of the Trinity. \r\n\r\nWhen you look at the Scripture, you can see that throughout history, God is moving closer to us progressively. Before the creation, God was above us, and as time goes on, He takes steps to be closer to us, and through his Son dwelt among us. That then continues to the New Testament era where the Holy Spirit has taken a role to be closer to us and live within us. This canonical lens to understand the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit is portrayed in a progressive fashion in the New Testament and not clearly identified in the Old Testament. This again helps set the context for the unfolding of our Savior known as the eternal Son of God, which is also true of the Holy Spirit as we can see.', 9, '2024-08-06 01:08:43', '2024-08-14 04:04:01', NULL),
(3228, 883, 933, 'The images of a lion and a lamb described in Revelation 5 demonstrates that the Son is a divine person that is equal to the Father. When John holds the scroll which is the secret to God\'s future, there is an announcement from an angel that states there is one who is worthy. The one who is worthy is the Lion, the Lion of the tribe of Judah. The symbolic gesture of a lion is royalty and with the tribe of Judah described as a lion\'s cub, the picture of a reigning king from the line of David is seen here. John is expecting to see a powerful lion to be depicted in this instance, however instead of a powerful lion, John sees a lamb. A symbol of sacrifice and suffering is portrayed. \r\n\r\nWhile Jesus is the conquering king and the Messiah, he accomplishes salvation by suffering and dying as the sacrificial lamb. In order to attain that salvation, the lamb must undergo suffering and sacrifice himself to pay for our sins. This is a powerful depiction of a Son being a divine person that is equal to the Father. Only the Father can redeem us of our sins, and understanding the godhood, the reader can grasp the divine essence of God. Only in this context can you see how a lamb would be worshipped within heavenly courts and while one may think this may anger God and be offensive to Him. He encourages the entire universe to worship the Lamb in front of him and that is where the full divinity of Jesus is revealed, he is not one of God\'s creatures, rather he is equal with God.', 8, '2024-08-06 01:15:27', '2024-08-14 04:04:01', NULL),
(3229, 883, 934, 'John 1:1 teaches us that Jesus is God because the deity of Christ is portrayed here. In the beginning was the word, the word was with God and the word was God. This is a passage that was taught during elementary sunday school days, and in the most concise way possible was described as the word being equal to God. In translation \'what God was, the word was God\'. Jesus is fully God himself, he is described by John as the Son of God, the divine agent, and the one who brings life. \r\nAs you continue to read the passage of John 1, you will see that the word became flesh and dwelt among us. John continues to go on about how Jesus is God and in John 10, you have Christ saying, \"I and the Father are one.\" Where Christ himself is placing himself alongside the Father as one and emphasizing His equality with His Father. When the text of John 1:1 is outline, the term \'word\' is not simply an abstract concept, rather it is the distinct relation to God in existence. It is a direct statement of the divinity of God and His word, which affirms that the Word is the same essence of God himself. While the word became flesh, the reference is made to Jesus and both distinct from God while being described as One.', 7, '2024-08-06 01:22:33', '2024-08-14 04:04:01', NULL),
(3230, 883, 935, 'In order to demonstrate the personality of the Holy Spirit from Scripture, we can see in John 14-16 about the Spirit doing things that only a person would be able to do. When Jesus goes on to describe that he will send forth another advocate such as himself, the Holy Spirit is the divine essence being described here. The Holy Spirit then has a role to go into the world and prosecute the world concerning sin and righteousness and judgement. Just as Jesus was a witness, the Holy Spirit serves in that capactiy as well and is identified as a distinct person in a number of passages. \r\n\r\nThe intellect of the Holy Spirit is demonstrated in 1 Corinthians chapter 2 where the Holy Spirit is listed as searching all things and even the deep things of God. No one knows the thoughts of God except for the Holy Spirit. Another personal quality or attribute is that there are emotions involved, the Spirit is teaching, guiding, comforting, leading, all of which a person does. In Ephesians 4, we can see that we should never grieve the Holy Spirit, and knowing that grief is an emotion, this is another personality of the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit is able to speak to us as well just like a person, this was portrayed in Act 13 where the Holy Spirit gave specific instructions with personal communication.', 8, '2024-08-06 01:31:31', '2024-08-14 04:04:01', NULL),
(3231, 883, 936, 'It was necessary for the man Jesus to also be God because while Jesus was fully God, he was also fully human. And with his life and death through humanity, through death and resurrection we are saved. Being a human yet also being God, he is equal in power and glory to the Father and we can trust him that he is able to redeem us from our sins. Only God is able to forgive sins, yet he has to be man in order to represent us and be able to to be put through the necessity of sacrifice and death. While only God can forgive sin, Jesus had to substitute himself for us by taking the full form of human yet being without sin in order to complete this substitution. \r\nBeing the second person of the Trinity, it\'s an amazing truth that he became man and lived among us. He died, rose again, and has great love for us. And because Jesus is also God, we can now have freedom to worship him and adore him for all of his power, and goodness. While he remained as the perfect sacrifice, his substitutionary atonement required that Jesus would take the place for humanity and bear the infinite wrath that God had against humankind\'s sinful nature. Only Jesus being fully God could have stood in the gap between God and humanity, and as a mediator reconcile us back to God. As God, Jesus had dominion and power over life and death and demonstrates his victory to believers.', 9, '2024-08-06 01:38:11', '2024-08-14 04:04:01', NULL),
(3232, 884, 937, 'If the Father has authority over the Son and Spirit, and if they disagree then what happens is the question, but one of the key fundamentals to understand is the relationship between the Father and the Son and the Spirit. We know that whatever it is that makes the Father the Father, the same is that of which the Son and Spirit is made of. They exist in unity as one God, three persons. We can see the relationship clearly identified in the New Testament where the Son gladly submits to the will of His Father and how the Spirit gladly rejoices (during the example of the baptism). \r\nThe Bible does not depict any instance or indication that the Father and Son and Spirit disagreement or have miscommunication with one another. They always of one mind and work in absolute perfect unity in every area. \r\n\r\nThe Father\'s authority is one that loves the Son and desires for His Son to be glorified, where the Son also desires for the Father to be glorified, and the Spirit is glorified as well. In the concept of God, there is unanimity of will and love, because of the unique fellowship of the Trinity. There is no area or reason within Scripture for us to think that the Father and Son or Spirit would disagree or against the will of each other. The Son obeys the will of His Father and the Spirit rejoices, and the three in one manifestation of the Trinity continues to salvation.', 9, '2024-08-07 01:06:16', '2024-08-14 04:03:03', NULL),
(3233, 884, 938, 'When we say that the Son is eternally begotten of the Father, we are describing a very unique relationship which at times can be confusing to the reader. In Scripture we assume that the Son was born like every other person that was born, however in the book of Revelation we see that the Son is eternal. He always existed, and goes back to eternity. If there was always a Father, then there was alwys a Son, and this relationship goes back to eternity. There was never a beginning or start to this relationship, it always was. He was not a creature that was created out of nothing, however the son was begotten of the Father. \r\n\r\nThe doctrine of eternal generation speak of God who is eternal and His Son. While Jesus took on the form of a full human, he was able to empty himself of his eternal manifestation of eternal glory that he had with His Father. He was still omnipotent, and omniscient, having perfect knowledge as the Son of God, however having all of this he surrendered none of them while taking on the form of mankind coming to redeem the world from sin and lead them to everlasting salvation. Since the Father was always there, so was the Son. That is what the meaning of eternally begotten is related to in the terms of this context that we read.', 8, '2024-08-07 01:11:06', '2024-08-14 04:03:03', NULL),
(3234, 884, 939, 'The early church councils affirm the eternal procession of the Holy Spirit from the Father. We are not relating this to a  procession of how a being is created or conceived through birth. Rather the Spirit is eternal just like the Father and Son and there is no beginning or end to either. So when we say that the Spirit eternally proceeds from the Father we affirm the divinity of the Holy Spirit. The Council of Nicea and the Council of Constantinople defended this unique attribute of the Holy Spirit  by stating that the Holy Spirit is Lord and to be worshipped as the Father and the son. \r\n\r\nIn 381, the Council of Constantinople met and wrote two-thirds of the Nicene Creed and regarding the section of the Holy Spirit stated, \"We believe in the Holy Spirit, and the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son, and with the Father and the Son is worshiped and glorified.\" It reaffirms the divine status of the Holy Spirit and the divine attributes of the Spirit. The Spirit was not created, because if it was created, then it would not be of God, because God was not created, he was God eternal and so was His Son and His Spirit. While this may come with controversy, and theologians argue that the Spirit came or proceeded from the Father and the Son, the Spirit indeed proceeds from Christ and the Son and is taught in John 16, 20, and Galatians 4.', 9, '2024-08-07 01:17:13', '2024-08-14 04:03:03', NULL),
(3235, 884, 940, 'Implications of the Council of Toledo for the doctrine of the Holy Spirit was that the Western Church added on very early to the ecumenical councils to describe the relationship of the Son to the Father, and more so the Son and Spirit relationship. The implications revolved mostly around the filioque where the argument revolved around the Father sending the Son, yet the Father and Son sending the Spirit. In Act 2, we can see the Day of Pentecost described where the the people exalted the Lord, and Jesus Christ ascends to the right hand of the Father and from the throne of authority, send the Spirit. With this depiction, the church was trying to isolate the roles and relationships between the Father, the Son and Holy Spirit. \r\n\r\nWhile some of the reasons for the division were complicated, the key fundamental is to understand that the Spirit\'s work is to bear witness to the Son and glorify the Father. With the council at Toledo in 589, the argument made was around the addition of \"and the son\" which was around the double procession of the Holy Spirit. This was done without consulting or seeking any theological input and caused a series of disputes that lasted for generations and centuries to come. And with this issues the division occurred between the East and West on the doctrine of the Trinity, speicifically around the issues of the filioque which concerned the double procession of the Spirit.', 9, '2024-08-07 01:23:48', '2024-08-14 04:03:03', NULL),
(3236, 884, 941, 'Ontological Trinity is referred to as \"immanent Trinity\", which has to do with the existence and personal interaction between the Father, the Son and Holy Spirit, apart from their acts of creation and salvation. When referring to ontological Trinity, we are referring to nature, that the there are three persons in one, and they are equal in substance, power and glory. While none of them are superior in this regard, the the Father is God, the Son is God, and the Holy Spirit is God. They are all 100% God equally. This term also refers to the coexisting nature of all three in one, where they love one another. The Father who loves the Son, who is the beloved, and the Holy Spirit who communicate the love between them and within that being of god is given love and communicates love. \r\n\r\nThe same God at work in creation is the Father, Son and Spirit which relates to one another eternally. God speaks to us, interacts with us, and is the same God as he always so so Trinity give us this view of ontological Trinity. The Father, which is the origin of the godhead, and the Son which was eternally begotten of the Father, sharing the same essence, and the Holy Spirit which is of the same essence as well. They all in agreement submit to the will of the Father.', 8, '2024-08-07 01:29:39', '2024-08-14 04:03:03', NULL),
(3237, 885, 771, 'Three reasons that critical scholars challenge the authorship of James is because the writer opens up chapter 1 by saying that he is a \"servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ.\" While the writer assumed that this would be sufficient and carry enough authority, this also created reason for confusion to challenge the authorship of the book. Critical scholars have challenged the authorship by stating that there was another James but not the one who indeed wrote the book, the brother of Jesus or son of Zebedee. The second reason is because the author is aware of Hellenistic or Greek culture and James was a Jew from Palestine. However in the time of Jesus, many of the Jews from Palestine had a decent awareness and understanding of Hellenistic and Greek culture. Pseudonyimity, where the written works is assigned to someone else other than the actual author was brought up as there was no mention of the author to Jesus in the writing, and the inaccurate assumption of inaccuracies that the epistles don\'t match James\' theological outlooks in the New Testament. \r\nEven with these arguments and reasons set forth by critical scholars, the argument is weak at best. The arguments of James being the author and brother of Jesus are actually much stronger than it not being evident. And due to this compelling fact, evangelical scholars affirm that James was indeed the author of the book, and the brother of Jesus.', 8, '2024-08-09 01:17:50', '2024-08-14 04:01:58', NULL),
(3238, 885, 772, 'The clues that we can gather from James\' epistle about his original audience start from chapter 1 verse 2, \'the twelve tribes scattered among the nations.\' This appears to be the Jews who reside outside of Israel. James also continues to go on by addressing his audience as \'believers in our glorious Lord Jesus\' chapter 2:1. With these two verses alone, the reader is able to identify the audience is Jewish Christians who were living outside of Palestine. In chapter 2:2, James addresses the readers with the word \'synagoge\' which is in reference to Jewish gatherings, and \"kurious saboth\" which is a common Old Testament name for the God of Israel listed in James 5:4. While many of the audience members were of traditional Jewish heritage, followers of Moses and the Torah, it is important to understand the audience by incorporating the fact that they are derived from a rich heritage with tradition. And James continues to talk to them about faith and living a wise life. He also goes on to explain how they should apply this into their own lives in light of the resurrection of Jesus Christ. \r\n\r\nNow while the audience is likely Jewish Christians, that does not avoid the fact of Gentile believers in church. We know based on the book of Acts, there were many Gentile God fearing converts who began to practice Judaism and attended synagogues. While the Bible states that the Gentile believers were regarded as \"Abraham\'s offsprings\" in Romans 8:9, they were considered as much of the twelve tribes of Israel than any of the Jews by bloodline.', 9, '2024-08-09 01:30:51', '2024-08-14 04:01:58', NULL),
(3239, 885, 773, 'James wrote his epistle likely in AD 44 to AD 62, the main consideration for these dates are anchored by the fact that Peter was released from prison in AD 44, and James was martyred in AD 62. We also know that James was a leader of the early church in Jerusalem which continues to validate the dates of origin for his writing. Acts 17 describes that James became a leader of the early church soon after Peter\'s release from prison. James dies at the hand of the priest Ananus in AD 62, which allows for the window of time where the book was written to be narrowed to the time of AD 44 to AD 62. James wrote his book in Jerusalem where he resided and ministered until the time of his death in AD 62. The reason behind why he wrote this epistle was to share to the church and audience of the trials and problems that he had to face and endure. James also knew that the audience that he was addressing was facing many trials as well. James was calling them to have joy and peace admist the trial, as he went on to explain that whoever is able to persevere will become mature, and complete. James goes on to say in James 1:5, \'if any of you lacks wisdom, you should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to you.\" The trials that some were facing caused them to leave their walk with God and abandon God and pursue friendship with the world. James was teaching the audience about continuing on in joy, perserverence, and choosing humility as a path to wisdom.', 9, '2024-08-09 01:39:20', '2024-08-14 04:01:58', NULL),
(3240, 885, 774, 'By the time that James had written his book, there was a long lasting line of books and teachings about wisdom. This was clearly visible in Job, Proverbs, Ecclesiasties, and the book of Psalms. Even in the book of James chapter 5, James addresses the life and perservernce of Job, and how one should live their life similar to that during the moments of trials and temptations. In his writings, James goes on to exemplify the way that one should speak, the treatment of widows, and orphans, poverty, and favoritism. James goes on to say to the reader and audience, \'that if any of you lacks wisdom, then let him ask God who gives generously to all without reproach, and it will be given to him.\' There are many ties to how similar the writings of Jesus are to the writings of James. Matthew 5:10 describes how the persecuted are blessed, and James writes in his book of the man who is under trial and perserveres is blessed. The structure of the letter similar to other wisdom literature of James\' day was unlike any other literature of wisdom. The book of James was targeted and specific to churches and featured some of the organizational features of other New Testament epistles.', 9, '2024-08-09 01:46:49', '2024-08-14 04:01:58', NULL),
(3241, 885, 775, 'James did encourage his audience to have \'pure joy\' in the midst of trials in chapter 1:2. The pursuit of wisdom in order to make something like this possible is only done through our faith and belief in Jesus Christ. The belief is that the suffering that we face, if we are to use wisdom and allow God to strengthen our confidence, then God has ordained for us the blessing of eternal salvation. Even in the book of Matthew 5, we can see how Jesus describes those who undergo trials and persecution are to be considered as blessed. While it can be rather difficult to see how suffering can lead to blessing or even see the light at the end of the tunnel, James continues to tell his audience to pursue wisdom, as that is the only means to see how suffering and wisdom go hand in hand. James teaches the audience that if you perservere among trials, and that too with joy you will be able to overcome it. You will end up being mature and complete with the wisdom that God provides you. James states that if anyone lacks wisdom, then they should ask of God who will freely give. When we truly understand how Jesus works behind the trials we face, we will truly appreciate the wisdom that we have in God and the confidence we believe in. This is what leads to true maturity and believing in him.', 9, '2024-08-09 01:52:26', '2024-08-14 04:01:58', NULL),
(3242, 886, 619, 'Neither Luke or the book of Acts clearly mentions the name of the author for the book of Acts, however there are two types of evidence, both explicit and implicit that suggest that one person wrote the book of Acts. The explicit evidence suggests that both Luke and Acts are both dedicated to Theophilus, and while Acts does refer to a former book, evidence suggests that the third gospel is what is being mentioned. The implicit evidence is that both of these books (both Luke and the book of Acts) have similarities between them which is inclusive of the orderly account, compositional structure, chronological length, the themes that are mentioned, the similar story lines and the commonalities of a redemptive historical vision, which incorporates the purpose and beliefs along with the author. The fact that there is also a connection between the two books and they are both addressed to Theophilus highly suggests the evidence that it is the same writer.', 8, '2024-08-19 01:24:04', '2024-11-20 20:32:14', NULL),
(3243, 886, 620, 'While there are explicit and implicit evidence that the writer of Luke and Acts are the same, there is also historical evidence that stems from the early church affirming that the same person wrote these two books. The Muratorian fragment (AD 170-180) confirms that Luke is the author of the third gospel, also known as the book of Luke and the book of Acts. The Anti-Macrionite Prologue which was an introduction to the third gospel, also describes the author of the book of Acts as being Luke. \r\nThe early church leaders, Irenaeus (AD 130-202) stated, \"Luke also, the companion of Paul, recorded in a book the gospel preached by him.\" This is later affirmed by Clement of Alexandria, Tertullian and Eusebius. While all these confirmations are there, there no single indication in the literature from the early church that anyone other than Luke was the author of the third gospel and the book of Acts. Eusbius, the great church historian wrote around AD 323 that Luke was the author of the book of Acts, and while he was never designated as an apostle, the early church passed on what it had received as the truth, that Luke wrote both of these books.', 9, '2024-08-19 01:29:58', '2024-11-20 20:32:14', NULL),
(3244, 886, 621, 'Luke was immensely influenced by the Old Testament\'s view of history in general, and secondly was impacted by the history of Israel. Pascal\'s threefold outlook in history was reflected in Luke\'s writings of the early church in Creation, the Fall, and Redemption. In the first-fold, the Creation writings were in general and in the book of Acts. Followed by the Fall, in general, and in the book of Acts. Lastly Redemption which is listed in general and the book of Acts. When accounting for Luke\'s view of Israel\'s history, we can see how Luke\'s record in the context of Acts was depending on the history of Israel starting with Abraham. God had called out Abraham to be the father of many nations and through him all people on earth would be blessed. Luke reports on this as the blessing of Christ came to the Jews by fulfilling the promises of God to Abraham. Jewish Christians received the gospel and brought it to the Gentiles, which continued to fulfill the promises of God to Abraham. This then was  depicted through the life of Moses and Exodus, where Moses had prophesied that another prophet would be sent by God to redeem his people from sin. Luke points out that this prophet is Jesus and to reject Jesus was to reject the law and Moses.', 9, '2024-08-19 01:37:16', '2024-11-20 20:32:14', NULL),
(3245, 886, 622, 'Unbelieving Jews and Christians today share some similarities but also have distinct differences based on religious beliefs, cultural practices, and historical backgrounds. The similarities between the two are the ethical framework, where both often emphasize moral values such as justice, compassion, and charity which have roots in the shared scripture of the Old Testament. Cultural practices and ethical practices such as respect for Sabbath and dietary laws can overlap, although these are typically more pronounced among religious believers. Scriptural heritage, where both groups share the Old Testament stores, laws, and teachings that form the foundation for many ethical and religious concepts in both traditions. The differences lie in the belief of Jesus Christ, the most distinct difference is that Christians believe in Jesus to be the Messiah, the Son of God, where the Jews do not accept Jesus as the Messiah or divine figure. Jews also follow the Torah, and observe Jewish holiday, and traditions whereas Christians celebrate sacraments such as baptism, holidays and teachings from both Old and New Testament. By knowing the Jewish teachings, and how they view Jesus, we are able to be more impactful in our teaching and evangelism to them.', 9, '2024-08-19 01:43:43', '2024-11-20 20:32:14', NULL),
(3246, 886, 623, 'We need to focus on evangelism and discipleship, when evangelism becomes a central focus, our priorities change and we are able to emphasize the importance of sharing the message of Jesus with others. This could mean investing more time, energy, and resources into missionary work, outreach programs, and personal evangelism. Differential thinking, by viewing every interaction as an opportunity to share the love and message of Jesus, recognize the eternal significance of leading others to faith. Living as a witness, in way that reflects the values of God\'s kingdom, such as love, humility, forgiveness, and service. Living in a way that is aligned to God\'s teachings and the powerful testimony of Jesus Christ. Kingdom oriented values, shifting focus from worldly success, to kingdom values, and reframing success in terms of faithfulness to God\'s will rather than society\'s standards. All while having eternal perspective, living with the mindset that earthly life is temporary, and eternal life with God is the ultimate goal, make decisions that reflect eternal significance.', 9, '2024-08-19 01:48:51', '2024-11-20 20:32:14', NULL),
(3247, 887, 624, 'Luke had a twofold purpose for writing the book of Acts, starting with the stated purpose which was to capture a historical view of the early church. Secondly, his intention was to convey the reality and power of the gospel message in the book of Acts. When we take a look at the historical account, we can see on more than one occasion of how Luke mentions that he had \'carefully\' examined and investigated the details that he recorded. He also ensured to write an orderly account of the truth was reported so that it would be communicated clearly and accurately. He was very much concerned about recording true history because he understood the importance of a basic principle and how it repeated throughout the Bible. God revealed himself in real history, space and time and worked throughout all of history to ensure that his salvation and judgement were brought about. Luke ensured that he was not trying to write fiction, rather report real history. When Luke goes on to write the book of Acts, he wanted to confirm what Theophilus and others had been taught. This means that the book of Acts may be characterized as a didactic history. Luke was viewing the world and all of history as Christ\'s lens of lordship and kingdom. When reading the book of Acts, this purpose should impact the reader as we are not just reading true fact, rather we should draw out attention to teachings that were foundational to the early church and it was from reliable witnesses to the Holy Spirit.', 9, '2024-08-20 01:26:13', '2024-11-20 20:30:45', NULL),
(3248, 887, 625, 'The structural elements that reveal the structural pattern of the book of Acts can begin with the geographical spread of the gospel. In Acts. 1:8, there is a thematic outline which states, \"you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem,  and in all Judea, and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.\" In chapters 1-7, Jerusalem is referenced where the early churches activities and growth took place. Going on into chapters 8-12, the expansion of the gospel to Judea and Samaria is mentioned, and in chapters 13-28 the missionary journeys of Paul, symbolizes the gospel reaching the heart of the unknown world. Key figures are revealed such as Peter and Paul, church growth is also mentioned. This is all done through summary statements, and Luke would provide this in the book of Acts to highlight the successful stages of the gospel and the growth of the church. This pattern helps us understand the message of the book as it draws attention to specific features of history through summary statements, and the pattern of church growth emerges from this.', 8, '2024-08-20 01:32:23', '2024-11-20 20:30:45', NULL),
(3249, 887, 626, 'We should take into account the kind of literature that Acts is when we make modern application of Luke\'s message. The literary character of how Luke portrayed the book of Acts can be revealed by how he highlighted selective content in what he reported. He also designed the book of Acts to be episodic, and he also communicated many of his teachings in implicit ways. With the selective nature of how Luke displayed the book of Acts, he had to consider that there were too many facts, people and events in the world that any human being can efficiently provide a report on, so he had to take the significant events which took place in the church and take a fraction of that which was led by the Holy Spirit to bring forth to his readers. In an episodic account, distinct details describing how the church is to continue building God\'s kingdom in Christ through the gospel is portrayed, and we need to pay attention to both the small picture and the big picture. Lastly, being implicit allows the author to be direct with the reader and Like would comment explicitly on the significance of elements of his stories.', 8, '2024-08-20 01:38:34', '2024-11-20 20:30:45', NULL),
(3250, 887, 627, 'God\'s words and actions are perfect and infallible, we must always take note of what God approves of and blesses, and what God disapproves of and curses. One thing to remember is we must emulate the beliefs, attitudes and behaviors that are pleasing to God and avoid those that oppose him. For example, if we take a look at the life of Abraham, and how God had required faith and trust to be in Him and Him alone, we can see that God blessed Abraham for his faith and obedience in Genesis 12:1-3, and 15:6. By obeying His commands, Israel\'s covenant continued with the promise of God and His blessing. Jesus goes on to say in Luke 11:28, that whoever hears the word of God and obeys it will be blessed. God also blesses those who are repentant and turn to Him Luke 15:7. At the same time, God disapproves of idolatry ad false worship, injustice and oppression, pride and arrogance, and sexual immorality.', 8, '2024-08-20 01:49:14', '2024-11-20 20:30:45', NULL),
(3251, 887, 628, 'Our circumstances resemble the circumstances of Acts as we have the same Triune God and exists in the persons of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. The goal remains the same which is to build the Kingdom of Heaven in Christ. In order to do so, the same gospel remains, the same message remains, and we required to respond in faith to receive the message of salvation and redemption. The God remains the same, the goal remains the same, and the gospel remains the same. As we serve the same God, we must serve and testify to Jesus Christ. We continue to be empowered by the Spirit, and do so for the glory of the Father, Jesus Christ. The goal of the church is the same goal as the early church, which is to conform to God\'s mission of building his church. Lastly, the same gospel, we all need the same salvation, the one that takes us to heaven. This salvation is only available through Christ, and this gospel remains the same for all people, in all times, and in all places. The modern church needs to consider these similarities and differences in our lives, because while the goal, gospel, and God remain the same, times have changes and so has culture. Culture has come into the church today, and attempts to change the goal, and it\'s not about repentance, or salvation, but just being okay, or doing good works. The gospel is manipulated and twisted to conform to the likes of the population with changing times, but that is not what is accurate. And with the God, a God who shows mercy and compassion is taken for granted as a God who will look away from sinners continuing to sin, that is not true and that is why we must adhere to the teachings of the early church.', 9, '2024-08-20 01:59:55', '2024-11-20 20:30:45', NULL),
(3252, 888, 630, 'Jesus\' teaching about the Holy Spirit before Pentecost in terms of the purpose and timing of the coming of the Holy Spirit is referenced in the book of Acts 1:3-11 where Luke mentions that Jesus had spent forty days between the period of his resurrection and ascension, teaching his apostles about the kingdom of God. Jesus went on to mention that the Holy Spirit would soon come to them in a particular way, and this was provided in the instruction to the apostles were Jesus stated, not to leave Jerusalem, rather wait for the gift of the Father that was promised. The purpose was to wait upon the promised time, yet the apostles were concerned about whether the restoration of the kingdom in response to Jesus\' statements about the baptism of the Holy Spirit, yet the Old Testament had revealed through prophets that God would indeed pour out his Spirit as never before on the land. The purpose was that Jesus stated his disciples would receive power through the baptism of the Spirit and the power was also manifested in dramatic ways in people\'s lives.', 8, '2024-08-21 01:28:38', '2024-11-20 20:31:36', NULL),
(3253, 888, 802, 'The work of the Holy Spirit continued even after Pentecost and continued to empower gospel ministry throughout the book of Acts. We can see in Samaria, that believers received the Spirit after their conversion, and this marked the first time that a Samaritan convert came to Christianity in large numbers. When the time came where the gospel reached Samaria, a new stage within the fulfillment of Jesus\' commission to his disciples was reached. \r\nContinuing onto Caesarea, the Gentiles were for the first time converting to Christ in large numbers. People who believed in the gospel had a manifestation of the Holy Spirit by speaking in tongues. This also was manifested within Ephesus, where converted individuals began to speak in tongues and even prophesied. \r\nJews who were receiving the word of God, and repented, began to witness that the Spirit had reached a new level and allowed to bring closure between the witnesses who experienced John the baptist and Jesus.', 8, '2024-08-21 01:35:39', '2024-11-20 20:31:36', NULL),
(3254, 888, 803, 'The apostles were given a certain level of authority in terms of their function, God\'s blessing on their ministries, and the miracles they performed and the revelation that they received and provided. Prior to the ascension of Jesus into heaven, he called forth his disciples and appointed them to continue the work that He had started and to continue to extend His kingdom from Jerusalem to the very ends of the earth. The Spirit was given to the apostles so that they could become His witnesses throughout the world. The primary function of the apostles were to be messengers, agents and ambassadors of Christ. Since the word apostolos itself means, \'the one who is sent\' it is fitting to be commissioned by Jesus himself and be delegated with the authority as an ambassador who is equipped and authorized to speak on all matters related to the church and the establishment. As they performed miracles, it proved that they were the messengers that were sent by God and His delegated authority, and the miracles proved their testimony of Christ was true.', 8, '2024-08-21 01:40:33', '2024-11-20 20:31:36', NULL),
(3255, 888, 804, 'The Holy Spirit plays a central role in empowering Christians to live transformed lives and to evangelize the world. Through sanctification the Holy Spirit works within believers to sanctify them, to make them more Christ like. This process involves changing their hearts, minds and behaviors to align with God\'s will. Through the fruit of the Spirit, the Holy Spirit produces the fruit of the spirit in lives of believers as mentioned in Galatians 5:22-23, through love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control. By providing guidance and wisdom, the Holy Spirit provides direction in life. This allows believers to make decisions that in line with God\'s purpose, this might be through a sense of conviction, inner peace, or through the wisdom found in Scripture, and by understanding Scripture, the Holy Spirit illuminates the Bible and helps believers to understand and apply God\'s word to their lives. The empowerment of the Holy Spirit has been experienced in my life by providing me clarity in guidance and wisdom in my life and giving me the clarity of character formation, of who I am and need to be in Christ.', 8, '2024-08-21 01:45:08', '2024-11-20 20:31:36', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3256, 888, 805, 'My church is carrying out the mission of expanding the kingdom of God by several different measures. While we continue to enforce that we are a church built to reach everyone by spreading God\'s word, we put that into action aside from the pulpit. Our church is one that welcomes all new comers and evangelizes to them in different forms. We have teams that will reach out to the new comers, help them to find housing, jobs, and provide them the footing that they need. This is a perfect example of being the hands and feet of Jesus, where we are not only sharing the word with them, but are living the word with them. We also do community outreach to reach the unsaved, unbelievers and do evangelism. This is done through either street ministry, easter plays, christmas programs, or vacation bible school. While we do all these measures, I believe every single church and believer has room to grow, we can do many things to improve our efforts, beginning with our own four walls. We have to focus on eternity more, and the longer term vision versus our short term plan to be on this earth. By enforcing that mindset, we will do so much for the lost on a continual basis.', 8, '2024-08-21 01:48:44', '2024-11-20 20:31:36', NULL),
(3257, 889, 826, 'Our Christian ethics is rooted in God.  God is our moral standard, he is the basis for right and wrong.  Ethics is a belief system which allows us to make decisions according to what God states.  God is our standard when it comes to decisions, the word of God is very clear on how we should live on Earth and to follow every principle and example that Christ has set forth for us. If we clearly study the Bible, we will get an understanding on how to make the best decision on any ethical issue that comes our way. When people of this world create laws that go against biblical principles, it is our responsiblity to go against the status quo.  When we prioritize God and his blessings, we are declaring two things, one to reiterate, God\'s nature is our compass of morality, and second God\'s doings demonstrate the baseline for morality. We will declare that God is who we we immitate when it comes to a morality compass.  We know that God is light and there can be no darkness found in him and Jesus\' blood that he shed on Calvary is the what cleanses us from all sin. God demonstrates his acknowledgement for ethics by blessing us.  God is able to show dislike for wrong by taking away blessings many times we see this in Scripture. In the book of Leviticus chapter 26 it is written that the children of Israel do not listen to me and obey my commandments, God will appoint terror over them. God can threaten our blessings if we fail to be obedient and take his binding promises for granted. Ethics is a pivotal aspect of all Christian teaching and doing.  All scripture is breathed by God and has the ability for us to learn from it, and can convict us when we are wrong.', 0, '2024-11-26 02:20:45', '2024-11-26 02:20:45', NULL),
(3258, 889, 827, 'Our rational when it comes to our behaviors should always have the right intentions.  If we do something with the wrong intentions, God will never reward us.  We have to ensure that our core is sanctified by our belief in Jesus Christ.  Our works has to generate from the love of Jesus Christ.  The bible says in Hebrews 11:6, Without faith it is impossible to please God, for the one who comes to God must believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder of those who seek him.  Our rewards from God should be rooted in our faith so in essence without faith in Jesus Christ, we are unable to do deeds that are favorable.  Apostle Paul states in Romans chapter 14, everything that is not from faith is sin. Remember that our deeds are in vain if we dont do it out of a deep fondness of God.   Apostle Paul says in the love chapter of 1 Corinthians 13, I can speak in heavenly languages, have prophecy, have mysteries revealed, have all faith but have no adoration towards God it means absolutely nothing. The Bible clearly says to us that the greatest commandment is to, \" Love the Lord your God with all of your heart, and with all your soul, with all your mind, this is the greatest and most important commandment.  He also says that the second greatest commandment is to love your neighbor as yourself. God\'s standard is a high standard which is all based in love this is why an unbeliever is unable to please God with their works.  We as Christians should not do anything for recognition but we should always aim to do it from the kindness of our heart and knowing that Christ is the ultimate standard of love, and we should always look to him and how he treated others!', 0, '2024-11-26 02:39:44', '2024-11-26 02:39:44', NULL),
(3259, 889, 828, 'In this lesson, there are three matters that are looked at in reference to good works and they are three norms of groups of Christians, three understandings on making wise choices, and how these can be related all together.  Believers tend to make decisions based on various different buckets. Many Christians put a significance on our inner voice and a direction of the Holy Spirit. Others in the faith use the Bible as their moral compass. There are others that say that our behaviors have repercussions can be lead to good or bad outcomes.  There are many questions that believer has to ask him or herself, what is my point of view, am I grown up enough to make the right call, and am I able to apply the word of God to the problem at hand?', 0, '2024-11-26 02:55:34', '2024-11-26 02:55:34', NULL),
(3260, 889, 829, 'There are three ways that we should view how we approach ethics.  The first point of view is derived directly from the word of God.  The second point of view is from the actual problem and the third point of view is from the individual. The three outlooks is one based on situation this focuses on the end.  This point of view allows us to explore what is the best outcome on accomplishing what God desires. This point of view will also look individuals with good morals from the Bible such as Jesus. The Bible tells us in Romans chapter 6 that we have died to sin and have been buried together with Christ, Christ was raised form the dead, so that we may walk in the newness of life.  Paul also writes in that same chapter that we were once in bondage to sin and when freed from the clutch of sin, it leads to holiness and the outcome of that is eternal life. Apostle Paul mentions that there are rewards for living according to the principles of Christ.', 0, '2024-11-26 03:10:35', '2024-11-26 03:10:35', NULL),
(3261, 889, 830, 'God is the absolute judge of morality.  God has complete right to act on his determinations.  God will release some authority to us as humans to make right and wrong decisions.  For example, some governments are given the ability to reward good and evil actions.  The Word of God instructs us that our human intentions are only right if they imitate Gods decisions.  God is going to judge all of our deeds and has the ability to rebuke or approve all decisions that are made by us.  God has the ability at that time of judgement to curse anyone whose deeds are corrupt and he also has the ability to bless those whose actions are great.  In the gospel of John chapter 5, we read that God is a just judge and has given authority to his son Jesus Christ to make that call about our lives here on earth.  God ultimately is going to be the deciding factor on whether we lived our lives according to God\'s principles.  We are never unable to excape Gods claim and strength over our lives. We have two options before us, one is to yield to God in complete surrender seeking solace in his mercy through Jesus or the latter which is to despise God and his commands.', 0, '2024-11-26 03:26:23', '2024-11-26 03:26:23', NULL),
(3262, 889, 831, 'There are three categories of revelation that are a part of God\'s word and they are: general revelation, special revelation and God\'s revelation. Special revelation has to do with direct speakings with God through various forms such as: the Word of God, prophets, dreams and visions.   General revelation has things like history, the world, botany, geography, and all of creation.  There is a revelation which is called existential which is revealed through people.  General revelation is unable to teach us everthing such as how we are saved. General revelation educates us on the baseline for God\'s personality. The lesson provides an example on how the sun shines on everyone regardless of your deeds.  There is a complex aspect to general revelation in that it prompts all people to repent.  The Bible is the main form of revelation in our day and he shows his personality through his creation.  Anyone who acts against what God has revealed is guilty of his sin.  Special revelation is the norm that is binding on our lives. Sometimes God will break the normalcy of events in our live to be able to talke to us directly. The Bible is bound all people at all times.  God instills in each of us a basic understanding of his law.', 0, '2024-11-26 03:44:06', '2024-11-26 03:44:06', NULL),
(3263, 889, 832, 'We can learn things about God by looking at people.  God created man in his own image but unfortunately his creation fell far from his standard.  Sin entered the world through one man and this called the fall of all of creation. Fallen humanity\'s nature has been defiled to where we despise God and love sin.  The Holy Spirit gives us access to Gods revelation in our life. We have to be obedient to Gods revelation. Revelation prepares believers to walk with Christ through tribulation. We have to be willing to listen to Gods work to get his revelation at times. Our existence is to glorify God and we have to realize that there is a purpose that God has in store for our lives.', 0, '2024-11-26 03:59:49', '2024-11-26 03:59:49', NULL),
(3264, 889, 833, 'John said that our obligation to obey God\'s standard to obey Gods standard is related to our forgiveness in Christ is because of the price Jesus Christ paid for our sins.  We were in depravity, our condition was eternal damnation but God had a plan and that was to redeem all of mankind.   John says very clearly who God is, his character embodies light, that there is no darkness in him. Jesus paid the price for our sins on the cross of Calvary which involved every drop of blood, he paid the ultimate ransom for us so that we should feel pressed to live a life worthy daily giving him glory and honor.  If we truly walk in the light, we will be able to live in peace with one another and have fellowship. Fellowship and purity of sins only comes through the son Jesus Christ and he is the only and ultimate standard by which we should follow.', 0, '2024-11-26 04:06:13', '2024-11-26 04:06:13', NULL),
(3265, 890, 834, 'The Scripture is all breathed by God and the authorship is holy.  The word of God has immense power, and has sovereignty.  Many Christians understand the Word of God is applicable to every generation.  The Word of God is living and breathing and does not dicatate our actions but emboldens us to honor God which leads us to his blessings.  The Bible can take multiple forms such as its dominion over God\'s creation.  In Genesis chapter one we read that God spoke and brought everything into being and so through his spoken words the whole universe was brought into light. In the book of Psalms there is a scripture that says that the heavens and their starry host were made by God\'s command.  God\'s words is the basis behind every accomplishment that God desires. Gods power can also be demonstrated through prophesy just like in the time of Isaiah which allowed God\'s divine will to be fulfilled.  Another way we see God\'s word in authority is by preaching it, because by preaching, it is the power of God for the salvation of all who believes.  God can use our experiences, our testimonies while preaching to bring people to the faith.  Meditating on Gods word allows us to increase our faith to live out Gods purposes in our life.  Hebrews 4:12 and 13 state like this, the Word of God is living and active, it judges our thoughts and attitudes of the heart. Nothing in creation is hidden from Gods sight.  The Word of God saves us from eternal damnation and empowers us to live a righteous life free from sin.  The scripture is not just God\'s mere voice, but it also has the power to empower us live out our faith by choosing good over bad even when others maybe against it.  God spoke directly to Adam and Eve in the garden of Eden, but the chose to listen to the serpent we have a choice.', 10, '2024-11-27 02:20:52', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3266, 890, 835, 'The Bible allows us to be wise for our salvation through the faith we have in Christ Jesus.  All Scripture can be used for instructing us, convicting us, improving us to lead towards a life that is upright.  The Scripture not only has power to lead us to saving grace but also to give us the appropriate tools to be able to shape our values to be able to make good choices.  We often as Christians get so upset and discouraged to go against the status quo.  God will always fulfill his purposes for our life through Scripture.  Since the Bible is God breathed, it has a proven dominance that it is indeed the voice of God. The Bible gives examples from history about its authority. There is a direct relationship between the spoken word of God and the written word of God. Adam and Eve made a rash decision by listening to the voice of the snake instead of the actual word of God which ended up in punishment and for sin to enter the whole world.', 7, '2024-11-27 02:32:40', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3267, 890, 836, 'When God led the authors of the Bible to write, he had a end game already planned out. God always wanted to provide his children with a clear guidance concerning his purpose and his personal qualities so that we are able to not conform to this world but to him.  One thing to understand about the Scripture is that its very clear, not hard to understand and not just for laity.  The Bible talks very plainly about how we receive salvation through our repentant hearts and belief in Jesus Christ.  In Deuteronomy chapter 29, Moses was told to tell the Israelites that the secret things belong to the Lord our God, but the things revealed belong to us and to our children forever that we may follow all the words of this law.  Focusing on the Word of God allows us to not deviate from the plans of God.  Everyone will not be able to understand the teachings of the Bible alike.  Everyone isnt able to make an essential effort to meditate on the Word of God, but if we are able to use its applications, we are all able to conform ourselves to God\'s standard of morality.', 9, '2024-11-27 02:44:11', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3268, 890, 837, 'Paul was compelled to preach the gospel because he knew it was the only way for people to receive salvation. Paul clearly mentons in Romans that anyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved, how, then can they call on the one they have not believed in? And how can they believe in the one of whom they have not heard?  And how can they hear without someone preaching to them? Consequently, faith comes from hearing the message, and the message is heard through the word of Christ.  Paul makes it very simple that without preaching there is no way for faith to be establised in the people.  We all can come to the faith by the reading of the word as well as the preaching of the word. Paul knew that the preaching of the gospel has a lasting impact because it brings people to know Christ better. Paul made a statement in 1 Corinthians chapter one that the message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God.  We have to come a realization that the Scripture has dominion over creation and we will be able to understand the written word of God.', 8, '2024-11-27 02:56:29', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3269, 890, 838, 'The Word of God has various languages that is found in all communication that people have.  The Word of God has statements, concerns, assurances, curses, threats, condemnation, instructions, tears of pain, adoration, and many more attributes.  Biblical language can be shy or very direct when it comes to feelings. The Bible has derision as well as genuine language.  Many times its hard to understand because of the many languages it poses.  Many early bible scholars believed that all of the word of God is inspired and so it beyond human understanding.  Based on this understanding of bible scholars it would mean that every reference has some metaphor behind the author\'s motives.  Some argue to take the word of God literally as possible. The Bible can speak in an ordinary language as well for example how in the Gospel of Matthew it states in the Lord\'s prayer to give us this day our daily bread.  In the book of Amos, Amos uses sarcasm to tell the people what not to do in going to Bethel  to sin, we have to understand that we have to be have good stewards of interpreting the words correctly.', 9, '2024-11-27 03:14:24', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3270, 890, 839, 'The preeminance of the Ten Commandments is both factual and is scriptural. As a matter of fact that we have learned from our Sunday School days, that the ten commandments was a written legal law of conduct given to the nation of Israel. The ten commandments were given directly to Moses on Mount Sinai and by this Israel was the first country to have a detailed code of conduct for God\'s mandate. The biblical preeminance of the ten commandments is demonstrated by how the nation of Israel received via signs and wonders like thunder, lightning.  God didnt show himself to the nation of Israel only to Moses but also Joshua, Aaron and seventy elders of Israel.  In Deuteronomy 4, we read that the ten commandments served as a binding agreement in which the nation of Israel was to follow precisely.  Also the ten commandments we know were put in the ark of the covenant which was very much intertwined with Gods presence with Israel.  Even in the New Testament Jesus mentions the ten commandments arent the only commandments to observe to receive eternal life.', 8, '2024-11-27 03:28:40', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3271, 890, 840, 'The three groups of law were considered the moral law, ceremonial law and civil law.  Moral law describes God the father\'s baseline of right and wrong usually closely tied to the ten commandments.  Civil laws give a set of rules for society.  Ceremonial laws are teachings on how to adore God.  No where in the Bible is there a clear distinction or portrait of these three laws. In Exodus chapter 20, we read that one of the ten commandments is to observe the Sabbath and keep it holy, this represents moral law.  The commandment about murder can be considered as both moral or civil. Many of the Old Testament laws can have multple areas of the law where it could be placed.  Every law had a moral code or baseline of how people should live their lives.  Moral law usually refers to the office of prophets, ceremonial law goes well with the priestly duties, and civil law is usually has to do with the king and its governing. We have to remember that the traits of God is our baseline for what is right and wrong. The Old Testament law reveals God\'s personality and we know that his character will never change. Jesus mentioned in Matthew chapter 5 that every law will show us God\'s baseline until his will is fulfilled.  Everything will not be fulfilled until the second coming of Christ. So we have to realize that the ceremonial and civil laws arent just for Old Testament believers, it continues to teach us what God desires from our lives. We have to remind ourselves that all Scripture not just some parts, is supposed to teach us, correct us and lead us towards eternity.', 9, '2024-11-27 03:44:48', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3272, 890, 841, 'I would tell my friend that these laws still hold relevance and the expiration refers to their direct application under the New Covenant.  The principles behind these laws continue to give us moral and ethical direction. The civil laws were directly related to the nation of Israel but we can adhere to our local and national governance today. Jesus\' teachings place an emphasis on love and justice.  Moral laws unlike civil laws are seen as universal and have no expiration date.  The Old Testament highlights God\'s holiness and standards which serves as the base for our understanding of biblical principles.  The moral teachings reflect God\'s unchanging nature. Ceremonial traditions are a foretelling of the suffering of Christ on the cross of Calvary.  We as Christians should study these laws to gain a better understanding of who God is in our life.', 8, '2024-11-27 03:56:36', '2025-02-18 20:21:02', NULL),
(3273, 891, 842, 'Situational material focuses on what revelation teaches us about right and wrong decision making.  The second thing is making a note that God\'s mysteries can be learned within certain problems.  God has a way of revealing his purpose in our lives.  There are certain goals that can be achieved from results of our thoughts, our speech and actions on how we should be doing life.  We can look at in two ways, one being reality, second what goals require us to reach.  We have to remember that God is the most basic fact that learn through revelation.  God is our true reality, and he requires to live according to his personality. Revelation from God is what allows us to obey God and have an understanding on the how.  The government that we are a part of is an example of how we abide by its laws and we have a duty to the government. In the same manner God is our ultimate authority, his personality is perfect.  God', 8, '2024-11-28 02:16:02', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3274, 891, 843, 'God is our ultimate fact, which creates the baseline for ethics as a whole.  God\'s mere presence reigns over every right and wrong decision and mandates us to live according to his standard.  In order for us to realize our responsibilities towards God we have to first receive the revelation of God from above. We have to understand who God is, how he is in order for us to be able to live according to his standard.  God is perfect, blameless and without sin and he wants us all to live like him a righteous and upright life.  There is a saying that says what would Jesus do, this is not just a saying but when we go through the ups and downs of life we have to really examine how we come across making decisions in our life.  Are we making decisions that are wise according to the counsel of God or are we making rash decisions without seeking God\'s answer to whatever question we are going through.  God is the ultimate moral standard and this is why God is critical in us making right decisions to be able to accomplish what goals that he has intended for our life.', 8, '2024-11-28 02:26:03', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3275, 891, 844, 'Goals when it comes to ethics have to do with the results behind our actions.  We all have goals that we set in life such as an alarm clock to wake up at a certain time daily, or to buy a thoughtful gift for my loved one on their birthday.  Goals have to do with where are we headed in life and the actions we take along that journey.  Goals can have two levels one is the immediate effect and the longterm such as building a tree house, we would need to measure the wood and cut it and eventually build the treehouse.  Our ultimate goal in life is to give glory to God such as Apostle Paul mentions it in 1 Corinthians 10:31. Even in the minute things of our life like what we should wear, or eat, this should never dishonor God.', 8, '2024-11-28 02:34:44', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3276, 891, 845, 'A rigorous approach to revelation is when we are overly thinking on how to prevent sin.When we respond to the the word of God\'s instructions in a restrictive manner, we often neglect things that God allows and many other commands that God gives.  There are many bad results to this approach of rigor one of these is that it breaks freedom by limiting actions that are wrong under certain situations and good under other situations.  Secondly rigor instills chaos in that it prevents us from understanding our responsibility and to take happiness in God for our salvation.  The rigorous strategy denies Christian freedom and drives us into confusion.', 5, '2024-11-28 02:48:57', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3277, 891, 846, 'The teachings on the mount about being blessed all have to do with the teachings of Jesus.  Our behavior, actions and overall mentality can determine what blessings we receive from God and indeed if we will receive them.  Jesus began the sermon by mandating the people to live morally. He described blessings in terms of the kingdom of God.  In Matthew 5, the kingdom of heaven is used multiple times and many theologians believe this is referring to the kingdom of God.  In verse 5 Jesus refers to receiving the earth, this was a kingdom blessing because it actually refers to the new earth that God will create when it comes in all of its glory.  In verse 9 it mentions about being sons of God which refers to kingship.  We often call God our father because in a sense he is our royal father. Jesus always presented the kingdom of God as a primary view point for our walk as Christians.', 5, '2024-11-28 03:00:19', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3278, 891, 847, 'The first part of the kingdom of God is when we speak of the status of the King.  Second is about turning to the people of the kingdom and third we will look at all of the binding agreements between the king and his people.  Many have a hard time recognizing God as the king but in the Scripture God is our supreme emperor who is over all that he created.  Psalms 47:9 we read that the kings of the earth belong to God, he is greatly exalted.  God is king over all countries, he is special in that when the throne of David was established, it showed the power of God himself.  Since God is the ruler over all people, his people are referred to as those that he has called to himself in sharp contrast to those of this world.  God intended for Adam and Eve to have rule over everything. Through the good news, salvation spread so rapidly beyond the walls of Israel that the church now includes all races.', 6, '2024-11-28 03:11:00', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3279, 891, 848, 'The history of God\'s kingdom goes beyond the period of inital peach to a time of aggresion against God.  This is a time when all people broke their binding agreements to their king.  We know in the garden of Eden, Eve was tempted by the snake, they ate the forbidden fruit and it violated the covenant.  In the new testament when God sent his only son to die on the cross this established a heavenly kingdom that we should all strive to be a part of.', 5, '2024-11-28 03:26:34', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3280, 891, 849, 'I always think of Gods law as a joy and delight.  The more and more I study the word of God, the more i feel inclined to share the love of Christ with others.  Retribution is not taught in the Bible and so we have to able to align our actions with those of Christ Jesus  He is the author and finisher of our faith and we need to ensure that we live according to his purposes.  We need to always be kingdom minded, how can i be a blessing to someone, not just with words but with actions.  If I know that someone is struggling with any issue, finances, and or family life, step in and be the hands and feet of Jesus.', 5, '2024-11-28 03:34:46', '2025-02-18 20:22:01', NULL),
(3281, 892, 850, 'From every book in the scripture we know that God has complete dominion over everything. This is a privilege that comes from God the Father has created the whole universe.  There are three traits og God that we can remember in Christian ethics, first that his dominion is complete and its only for those who believe in him, it can also be very tiring.  God has given us a complete mental capacity to think on our own and make good and bad decisions.  In Jeremiah, we read about the potter, we have no right to question the potter because the clay is in his hand and he determines how the shape is to be made.  God is able to do as he wishes over us as he sees is best fit. No one can question God\'s moral baseline because in him was what right and wrong is based from, there can be found no wrong in him.  We need to understand that Gods character is divine and he knows everything when it comes to our lives.  We have to realize that God is in control over every circumstance of our life and we always tend to question that.  Phillipians 2:12 and 13 says to work out our salvation with fear and trembling, for it is God who works in you to be able to accomplish Gods will in our lives. God will never force us into any situation where we are unable to get out of.  God gives us the ability to avoid sin and overcome templations.  1 Corinthians 10:13 says that when you are tempted that God will provide a way out of it.  We always have to think clearly when we make decisions, does my decision glorify God, does it do God justice.  God has always been present with humanity and he will never leave us. God has an eternal covenant with his people that lasts from one generation to another.  Our ethical decisions must always give credence to Gods physical presence with us as a judge now and always.', 9, '2024-11-28 15:18:43', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3282, 892, 851, 'The prenatural realm is a reality that is beyond what nature presents.  It is never above God but provides a distinct perspective about creation.  This is the world where angelic beings, evil spirits and the unseen spirits dwell.  In today\'s day and age we pay little attention to this prenatural realm because it is hard to picture the concept of angelic and evil spirits that are always around us and have personal dealings with us.  We have to realize that this is a huge reality where angels and demons have a huge role in what happens in our personal lives.  We are all living in our own little world not realizing that God dwells in our universe and both demons and angels have an intellect on their own and have the ability to make rational decisions.  We know that when God created angelic beings they were all perfect and righteous, but some turned to rebel against God and this is where blessing turned into damnation.  We have to understand that demons and angels have a huge impact on the daily moral dilemmas that we face and the decisions they bring to make.  Angels are God\'s personal message givers to us just as the Holy Spirit can speak to us in every situation of our life.  In 2 Kings there is an instance where the angel of the Lord destroyed 185,000 Assyrian troops in in one night in order to stop the invasion of Judah.  Other times angels are preventing us as Christians from falling.  The writer of Hebrews mentions that aren\'t angels there to minister to us serve those who will inherit salvation. The angels are constantly making sure that we are on our best behavior and do their best to prevent us from making decisions that are rash or abrupt.  Satan can also harm us, in Job he asked God to touch Job because to test him and to prove that Job would betray God but he stood faithful.  We have to be victorious over spirtual warfare because our struggle is not against flesh and blood but against the rulers, against authorities and against the powers of this dark world.', 9, '2024-11-28 15:32:51', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3283, 892, 852, 'In Genesis 1, the creation of everything was perfect and we are made from the dirt of the ground. God blessed the human race by saying be fruitful and multiply, increase in number, fill the earth and subdue it.  God intended us to rule over all of the earth, he called us to govern the whole world.  God always intended us to have life and growth which would turn the whole world into a place which would have Gods kingdom.  When Adam and Even in the garden listened to what the snake said, this caused an rebellion against God\'s sovereignty and hard work and toil came as a result of their disobedience to God.  This caused sin to enter the world which caused us to be mean to each other and nature as it was designed to be changed as a whole.  We have to know that the Earth still belongs to God and the earth still shows God\'s goodness despite of the sin.  We see in Psalms 19 that the heavens declare the glory of God, the skies proclaim the works of his hands.  Paul mentions that everything in creation is good. God is in the business of redemption and is creating a new heaven and and a new earth where he can reside with his people.', 6, '2024-11-28 15:42:44', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3284, 892, 853, 'The preternatural realm consist of angels and demons their relationship and impact on us in the world.  God has infiltrated thew world with a huge number of spiritual people known as angels and demons in which they both are smart and have characteristics and a will as well.  Angels were created to be pure just like God but when some of them decided to become rebellious towards God this caused them to go towards damnation.  Angels have been given a task to keep Gods children in line and interact with us on behalf of God the father.  Angels have a responsibility to ensure that we behave morally and prevent making decisions that are harmful to us.  God uses our salvation to instruct our angels in heaven. God\'s original purpose was to make known the wisdom of God to all the rulers and authorities in the heavenly realms.  Demons have the ability to harm us in the natural realm just like what was done to Job.  The demons are in a constant battle with angels, good and evil is always fighting against each other. God has given us an armor of God in ephesians which is there to equip us against every vile of the enemy.  Spiritual warfare is the real battle between forces of good and forces of evil.  Remember that Jesus has torn the evil spirits capability to overcome us, through his death and resurrection all enemies are destroyed.  The demons will continue to attack us until Gods last judgement and we have to be faithful until the end and constantly sharpen our weapons which is be dived in the word of God.', 8, '2024-11-28 15:59:20', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3285, 892, 854, 'God sees us as all one group of people.  There are similarities in what the people of God go through and we have to look at how society will stand before God.  God had a purpose for Adam and Eve to be the first of many generations that would fulfill the cultural mandate of God.  The cultural mandate was for humanity as a whole to regenerate and build cultures.  We all are a part of a corporate task which involves unity and being considerate of others feelings when we make decisions.  Our decisions work together to achieve the goal of expanding God\'s kingdom to the ends of the Earth.  One problem of universal sin is that the human race doesnt fulfill the mandate in the way it should.  Sin prevents us from glorifying God and honoring him.  Our responsibility is to build God\'s kingdom and maintain a vigilatn watch for sin.  God reminds us that we are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a people that belongs to God. Our primary goal is to live out Gods purpose on this earth by our life, our actions, our behaviors should allow people of this world to see that we are a different breed.  We have a responsibility to make sure everyone of our decisions do bring glory to God.', 7, '2024-11-28 16:10:15', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3286, 892, 855, 'The fall allowed sin to enter everyone which provided a curse for all of humanity. Sin allows many to walk according to the flesh, set their minds on that which is hostile toward God, which doesnt give room to the law ofGod. and those in the flesh can never please God.  Prior to the fall, humans were perfect and created to reflect God and his character.  With the fall, God gave us the ability to make choices that may not please God.  As God cursed the human race, he messed up our personal wills. Sin is what controls the fallen human race making it literally impossible to satisfy God.  The fall has prevented the human race to have access to revelation of the Holy Spirit because God cursed humanity. Unbelievers have an instinctive knowledge og god\'s law and many recognize justice and know difference of right and wrong.  The Holy Spirit doesnt provide the same measure of an inward leading that he provides to believers as to unbelievers.  God chooses to reveal himself in ways that bless those who love him and that curses those who hate him. Humanity\'s fall into sin has provided unbelievers the incapacity to understand the mysteries of God.  Many non cognitive factors shape our ethical decision such as our feelings, inner voice, leadings, affinities, needs, shortcomings, scares and ultimate rejection of God.  The unbelievers heart has become calloused that they hardly hear with their ears and they have closed tehir eye.  The unbelievers heart is so hardened against God and his truth which prevents us from properly understanding his revelation. God desires in loyalty and not sacrifice.', 8, '2024-11-28 16:25:42', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3287, 892, 856, 'Christians find comfort in the immutablilty of God because it means that God is always the same, he is always faithful, and dependable, he never changes his characteristics or his promises.  God provides a steady rock in which we can focus on in challenging and uncertain times.  God is not like the ocean\'s waves when it comes to his faithfulness, he will never abandon his promises upon our live and will alway remain loyal to them.  God\'s personality will never based on our actions but will always be the same. Being knowledgeable in the Scripture allows his love and mercy to remain as the anchor that brings us strength.  No matter what happens in our life, we will have assurance that God is soverign and he has our best interests at heart.  Psalms 102:27, states like this, they will perish but you will remain, they will all wear out like a garment. You will change them like a robe and they will pass away, but you are the same, your years have no end.  This verse describes God\'s unchanging character.', 8, '2024-11-28 16:37:13', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3288, 892, 857, 'When I battle sin as a Christian, my prayer life is critical to me being victorious over sin. Diving into scripture, meditating on it day and night, have a core group of people in my life that keep me accountable to prevent me from wandering into sin.  I have to constantly remind myself of the end goal which is my eternity and remember that sin will take me away from that goal. I have to constantly train my mind to think positively because the devil always wants me to think that God is out there to get me. I have to understand where my weakness lies and where the devil takes a hold in my life and be actively conscious about avoiding those temptations and avoiding those people or environments that may lead us to that temptation.  What doesnt help is trying to rely on my own will power to overcome sin, it isnt possible because without God\'s grace it is never attainable.  I always tend to beat myself up when I make a mistake and this is something that will not help me become an overcomer and this is something that the enemy desires for me to dwell in that completely.  The last thing I would say that is harmful that my parents growing up did was comparing myself to others and wanting to become like so and so, this is truly harmful to my spiritual growth and this is not something that God truly desires.  The Holy Spirit that dwells inside of me is what gives me the strength to avoid harmful situations that will ultimately effect me reaching eternity.  My focus and aim should always daily start with prayer and end with prayer, we have to have an attitude of gratitude and know that there our outside forces that will try to influence me in the wrong way and I have to make a conscious effort not to engage in those deeds or behaviors that is unlawful to God.', 8, '2024-11-28 16:47:51', '2025-02-18 20:23:08', NULL),
(3289, 893, 858, 'Our intentions serve as a reason and our deeds are the results of them.  Our intentions come from within and shape our characteristics.  One\'s love for their family will give them a mandate to go to work to provide for their loved ones.  There will be an internal conflict that happens for the man of the home to stay at home be lazy but the intentions are all there to avoid tension and live with unity with one another.  Our desire to share Jesus Christ with those who dont know the gospel is showing our loyalty to God and shows that we take ownership in others coming to the faith as well.  In 1 Chronicles chapter 28 David is associating intentions are related to one\'s personal heart. God is a searcher of our hearts and understands every intention and we have to be willing to serve God with our whole heart.  David taught Solomon that obedience is better than sacrifice and this must come from the deepest parts of our soul.  God doesnt want our partial love, he expects us to love him with all of our heart, soul and every ounce of our strength. The Bible mentions that we used to be slaves to sin but now are slaves to righteousness.  We have to remember that our every action that we take do have consequences, ultimately thinking how we glorify God should be our only intention when doing anything.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:13:13', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3290, 893, 859, 'God\'s actions of atoning grace and commong grace serve as models for our own behavior by leading us to forgive, have empathy, and being able to give to others when their merit don\'t deserve it.  God gives us his grace with no bounds, no conditions, by showing his deep affection to everyone despite their deeds and convictions.  We are always called to be immitators of God by showing others grace with every interaction with have with anyone. God sent his only son down to the earth to die for our sins so that we may have eternal life in that way expressing his intimacy towards us.  We have to be able to give grace when needed, many hurt us, many break us, but as Christians we have to show them the love that Jesus showed us.   God is about restablishing broken relationships and asking us to mend fences with those who seek rectification.  God let go of his pride by sending his son as a human into this world and to be able to serve those who were destitute, those who would never be looked at, it is our responsiblity to become the hands and feet of Jesus Christ.  God\'s grace ultimately allows us to do deeds of kindness to others when they really don\'t deserve it.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:23:33', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3291, 893, 860, 'My motivation comes from knowing who God is and what he did for me which allows me to live out my faith accordingly.  God gave bestowed upon me an agape love in that in John 3:16 we read that For God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten son to die that whoever believes in him shall not die but have eternal life. My obedience comes from my heart, my genuine passion is to help those in need with my time, my money and resources.  If anyone is in need, my first instinct is to go there and spend time with them and provide for them in the time of need as to what they are needing.  There have been many times I have been down, didnt have anything on me and there were friends of mine that came through in the clutch time show me the love of Christ.  I feel obligated to share my blessings with others. Reading Gods word daily and spending time in his presence allows me to be effective in hearing the voice of God and to be used by an annointing that the Holy Spirit provides.  My intentions have always come with a pure heart, if I do anything I always consider how I can glorify God and not desiring for any recognition.  There have been times that I can remember that my heart wanted the recognition and felt bad when not recognized but than the Holy Spirit convicts me and tells me that its not about me but what God\'s plan is, the reward is not here, its going to be in heaven.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:31:48', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3292, 893, 861, 'I remember coming to the faith at the age of 10 years old at our church New Life Church of God in plano, texas at the time.  My uncle Pastor Jose Annicattu was the pastor and he gave a challenging word that morning about Jesus Christ and how he should be our focus in life and that it convicting me to repent that day.  I asked God to forgive me of my sins and invited the Lord Jesus Christ into my life.  I remember going to Pensacola Florida when I was about 12 years old with an assemblies of God church and having an encounter with God in a personal way where all I could do was just cry in his presence and didnt truly understand what transformation was happening in my life.  When I was 13 decided to obey God\'s commandment of water baptism in my life and this is where tests in my life started to happen.  Once I took this decision, many peer pressures started to happen in my life, there were numerous challenges I faced all throughout high school that God gave me the strenght to overcome.  I know during my senior year of high school when I was 17, I was worshipping and asking God to fill me with the power of the Holy Spirit and God truly instilled in me the gift of speaking in tongues.  This gift has encouraged me and allowed me to speak with God directly in the heavenly language when my own words would fail, it would interced truly on my behalf.  I know there have been many tests regarding my health, regarding accidents, regarding my future, in everything I was able to see the merciful hand of God upon my life.  I have seen ministers in my life give me second chances, and 3rd chances to be able to achieve God\'s ultimate purpose in my life.  I am grateful to my parents, my spiritual fathers who has led me thus far and showed the example in which I should live by.  My motives and actions for sure after marriage has changed, my wife Sheba has motivated me to think from a heavenly perspective and not an earthly perspective.  Sheba has always been the light in my life to remind me that we have a heavenly call, that our reward is in heaven, we shouldnt strive for recognition from man but only desire what comes from God.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:40:05', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3293, 893, 862, 'Imagination allows us to make a mental picture of ideas that are not evident to our senses.  This imagination is what allows us see the bigger picture, to see possiblilites despite things being impossible.  This imagination allows us to find an area to expand our knowledge, and to have a personal and spiritual encounter with God.  We have to be able to see the unseen, in my personal life, I have been married for 12 years with promises that God will honor me with children.  Many years it was prophesied over us, but we gave up, but one thing is for sure God never gave up and blessed us with a son this year for God\'s glory.  We have to allow the word of God to come alive in our lives when we go through our darkest times, knowing that God was there for Abraham, Isaac,Jacob, Joseph, David, and Daniel and many others in the Bible.  Our imagination will allow us to be truly changed because we think positively.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:48:57', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3294, 893, 863, 'Having knowledge allows us to know right and wrong when issues arise, having and understanding that our actions can have various consequences for the choices that we make. We have to make educated choices that align with the principles that are outlined in the word of God.  There will be situations that arise in our life where we have to make a critical decision and without knowledge this will never be possible to make the right choice.  God asks us to put ourselves in the shoes of the other individual to understand where they are coming from and to be able to make a wise decision as a result.  We have to be able to have the ablility to analyze what my actions would do, what will be the end result.  Our responsiblity in life should be to minimize losses and prevent others from being hurt when we make these decisions.  Every one of our decisions should be based on the moral standard that God has placed.  When we make decisions based on morals it allows us to build trust with others, it shows that we respect others, and ultimately displays the example of compassion that Christ showed upon us.', 8, '2024-11-28 18:55:59', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3295, 893, 864, 'The basic definition of one\'s will is referring to the inner voice inside of us to make decisions. Our will shows our determination to not give up when situations get tough.  Our will is a motivating force, it is shaped by our values, our faith and will always give us directions on how we ultimately make a decision.  When making decisions we always have to weigh the consequences and how it may affect others around us.  When we consider others\' feelings this is when we show the love of God.  There is a saying that where there is a will there is a way, the will gives us strenght to make the wise decision when others may say its not the right decision.  God is sovereign and knows the decisions that we make have an impact and will give us the free will to make the call  God ultimately knows that we will come back to him and his purposes will come to pass in our life.', 8, '2024-11-28 19:02:57', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3296, 893, 865, 'I have always wanted to store up things here on earth, accumulate wealth, have all the best things of this world in essence to keep up with the jones family.  This is all futile is what I realized and is vanity just like Solomon said.  I realize that my treasure is not here it is in heaven and I have to strive for that.  We should not be concerned about acquiring all things of this world where moth and rust will destroy but solely be concerned about the treasure that is being prepared in heaven for me.  I have come to the realization that I shouldnt compare myself to others and what others have, but but consumed about he desires of me.  Romans 12 is one of my favorite chapters in which Paul asks us to not be conformed to this world but be transformed by the renewing of the mind according to Christ Jesus.  My actions on this world have consequences and my heart needs to be led by God in that my intentions need to be always to glorify God.   My actions should display God and should show his love in my deeds my words as well.  I have to always consider what I say can hurt others and make sure that I understand how I can be a blessing in ministry.', 8, '2024-11-28 19:08:41', '2025-02-18 20:23:57', NULL),
(3297, 894, 655, 'Historial analysis describes the miracles of God as shown in Scripture.  Historical analysis sees the Bible as an opening to history, looking at events in history which is the foundation for the Bible.  Historical analysis is a direct correlation about every act that God has done.  Confession describes that he works through an event, God achieves his will in history by acting through us. In the Scripture that we see that God inflicts sickness on Job and heals him with out any instruments.  The Confession also talks about taking the mundane and making it exceptional.  Like Sarai was 90 years old and her womb was closed when God revealed that she will bear Abraham a son.  Confession also speaks to how God works in the unconventional way as in the book of Joshua how the sun stood still until Joshua won the war. Historical analysis refers to the unordianary miracles of God that is way beyond what our minds can comprehend.', 8, '2025-03-28 00:44:00', '2026-04-16 02:48:24', NULL),
(3298, 894, 656, 'The factual approach to bible history is specifically geared towards the dates of when events occurred. The purpose of a fact based approach is very candid and to develop a record which puts together what we learn from the Bible and what we get from outside the Bible witnesses.  There are two sides to theology concerns, one being a description of other topics which can be linked to God. There is another category that goes over the foundational teachings of humanity,salvation, our moral code and the body of Christ.  The study of the Bible enables us to broaden our knowledge of all these subjects mentioned above such as our salvation, and the overall difference between right and wrong.', 7, '2025-03-28 00:53:13', '2026-04-16 02:48:24', NULL),
(3299, 894, 657, 'There was a German philosoper in the 17th century named Hegel who was famous for his presentation that every part of reality is captured in logical patterns of development known as the dialectic.  He stated the entire world was ordained by a history of logic. For example the study of rocks was used to understand the age of the earth.  Also biology became a part of history as many were introduced to Darwin and the concept of evolution. Changes in the culture caused a shift in how the study of scripture developed to bring the truth of Christ to the western world when it was dominated by various philosophies.  The study of the Bible is responding to a significant move to the challenge of the mind in the modern era.', 7, '2025-03-28 01:07:39', '2026-04-16 02:48:24', NULL),
(3300, 894, 658, 'History has had numerous impacts on the modern teaching of the Bible.  The sad thing is many students of the Bible have diluted basic Christian doctrines, while many others have included essential tid bits from history that has been sustained and broadened our knowledge of faith in Christ. Modern history led many teachers of the Bible to explore it with an open mind with new goals. Gabler\'s point of view on the Bible was to understand what the old writers and people in the Scripture understood about God and the universe that they are a part of. Gabler also mentioned that followers of Christa should also have belief in the current world by introspection on science and religion.', 7, '2025-03-28 01:21:51', '2026-04-16 02:48:24', NULL),
(3301, 894, 659, NULL, 0, '2025-03-28 01:22:20', '2025-03-28 01:22:20', NULL),
(3302, 895, 660, 'Synchronic refers to steps, for example when my son got a dehumidifier,it came with a manual which has various steps to insure that it is assembled appropriately. We are able to take apart the history of the Old Testament scriptures into bits and pieces and be able to focus on how it all comes together. Synchronic is rooted from the words with or together with.  Synchronic refers to how teachers of the Scripture look into various events in the Old Testament that occurred simultaneously. For example, there are plots to a storyline and every small event tells a portion of the story. Synchronic can refer to limited periods of time or other time focus on the lengthier periods of time.  For example, I might say I spoke a long time with my wife just a moment ago, making this event of talking a single event.  Many other instances we might recap the things we did in a week or a year in a small statement.', 8, '2025-03-28 02:45:01', '2026-04-16 02:48:58', NULL),
(3303, 895, 661, 'Synthesis is not hard to understand.  We allow synthesis daily in our practical lives which means to bring together various items of something into one. Synthesis is all about summarizing and not going into every specific detail of an even that has happened. In the 20th century many used common sense in disciplines presented by the study of Scripture. We aknowledte the common sense of the personality of the Old Testament on two basises. Old Testament Scriptures will remain shocking to many since the mind of God is not ours. God reveals to us his vision about certain events but also directs them to common sense, ways of understanding and and understanding about God.', 7, '2025-03-28 02:56:41', '2026-04-16 02:48:58', NULL),
(3304, 895, 662, 'Poets in the time period of the Old Testament were absorbed in two different aspects that spekd of history. On one side, they gave heed to the world they wrote which allowed poetry to go into the past. One example is when Moses and Miriam on what happened to the Red Sea. Being in deep thought about the word of God it allows us to know the value of the law of God.  Writers also took us to the future which showed a time when captives will finally come back to their land of promise. Miriam sang a song which motivated the children of Israel to keep going despite all of the challenges.', 7, '2025-03-28 03:08:30', '2026-04-16 02:48:58', NULL),
(3305, 895, 663, 'Since the 17th century Enlightenment in Europe, many that studied students of history tried to apply science to the writings. Old Testament stories were only as detail oriented as they needed to be. For example the writer of Chronicles doesnt write everything about Solomon and leaves out various things like him marrying the Pharaoh\'s daughter and building the temple for God. The writers of the Old Testament felt no need to mention every truth.The stories of the Old Testament are not objective by today\'s standards. God prompted the Old Testament writers to influence the perspective of those that read it.', 7, '2025-03-28 03:20:02', '2026-04-16 02:48:58', NULL),
(3306, 895, 664, 'The writer of Chronicles put together the history of King Solomon\'s life.  The writer left out the negative aspects of his kingship, left out the marriage to the daughter of Pharaoh,and the other foreign women. Solomon\'s failures led to the division of the nation but despite that the writer of Chronicles wanted to focus on the positives, despite that, the stories are factual about the past. In our day to day lives, we often talk embellishing what actually happened. Let us remember the fact the narratives of the Old Testament are not objective to the standards of modern day.', 7, '2025-03-28 03:33:25', '2026-04-16 02:48:58', NULL),
(3307, 896, 665, 'Diachronic comes from two words in the Greek language which means through or to go throughout. Diachrony refers to a timeframe being past which involved change that may evolve over months or years.  Looking at the Old Testament diachronically is similar to focusing on a book from chapter to chapter. This perspective to the Bible refers to how events unfolded over time. The diachronic approach is focused on how frameworks have changed. Diachronic analysis goes hand in hand with a sequence of events.', 3, '2025-03-29 00:40:43', '2026-04-16 02:53:48', NULL),
(3308, 896, 666, 'The reason we use development is because of two main concepts. Changes in the Old Testament theological structures lead us towards accomplishing God\'s divine will for history. The second thing is theological changes happen only through God\'s divine touch. The Bible is all about accomplishing God\'s divine chapter in all of our lives.  Isaiah 46:10 states that my purpose will stand and God will do all that he pleases. God has short term and long term goals over our lives such as David being king over Israel. God\'s goal was to establish David and his generations to come to fullfill God\'s short term goals. David\'s kingdom had a future goal of extending God\'s kingdom to every part of the Earth. God is always going to be involved in our lives and he provides for us in mysterious ways. God asked Moses to do certain things to make his people holy such as him. God always shows himself through his deeds and his actions.', 3, '2025-03-29 00:53:22', '2026-04-16 02:53:48', NULL),
(3309, 896, 667, 'The covenant with Adam and Noah were considered universal because it was between God and all of mankind. The bond that Adam and God had put significance on all of mankind having the basic responsiblity which shows our dedication to God. There was a covenant that was made with Noah that God will never curse the ground.  God knows that we are evil from infancy and that sin is running rampant in our lives.  God intended to redeem mankind with a new grace to be able to fulfill God\'s purposes. God established a national covenant with the people of Israel, God shortened his covenantal attention to just one group of people such as Israel which would lead all of mankind to service to God. The covenant to Abraham is considered the covenant of assurance.  Israel in the future became a huge kingdom under the rule of David and God made a covenant with him as well. God\'s choice of David\'s family would never be cast away. The new covenant was made to rule the people of God, when God accomplished his promises, was to reinstate his people after slavery to spread the kingdom to the ends of the earth. The new covenant was fullfilled once and for all through the cross of Calvary, when Jesus died and bled and was risen after three days. Christ promised the Holy Spirit upon the apostles which allowed them to be super effective in ministry. Christ is coming back for the church who will be completely loyal to him.', 3, '2025-03-29 01:08:24', '2026-04-16 02:53:48', NULL),
(3310, 896, 668, 'Typology refers to an order of being controlled such as studying such as science and studies of Scripture. Typology can be referred to as the foundations of God or ways of worship which is evolved through the Scripture. Biblical typology involves certain people, groups of people and a sequence of events that happened in Scripture.  Typology is the analysis of various types. Romans 5:14 Apostle Paul mentioned that Adam was a pattern of the one that has yet to come, which refers to him as a type of Christ. Baptism in the New Testament is derived from the flood in Noah\'s time. Paul mentions a shadow of things to come which refers to being with Christ. Jesus made a comparison about Moses lifting up the snake in the desert, so the Son of Man must be lifted up, that everyone who believes in him may have eternal life. Jesus showed the bronze serpent in correlation to his crucifixion. Typology can be seen as a figure of speech. Typologies is a venn diagram of important people and events in history. Christian baptism is related to similar beliefs because it shows our death from a world ending in judgement to become a new creation in Christ.', 3, '2025-03-29 01:26:07', '2026-04-16 02:53:48', NULL),
(3311, 896, 669, 'We should allow the Scriptures to come alive in our lives.  The moral law in the Old Testament is a guideline for us to follow which shows God\'s own character, his integrity, love and his faithfulness. The Old Testament teaches us to worship the Lord with gladness, come before him with joyful songs, knowing that he is the Lord our God. God is the one who made us, and we are his people, the sheep of his pasture, we are to enter his gates with thanksgiving and his courts with praise. As Paul states in Romans 12, our bodies should be given as a living sacrifice, not being conformed to the ways of the world and be transformed by the renewing of our mind and this would be your reasonable service. We have the Lord\'s supper in church which reminds of the ultimate sacrifice that Jesus paid on the cross of Calvary and reminding us to live according to how Christ did while on the Earth. Jesus has given us the Holy Spirit which is alive and well in our hearts daily which allows us to speak to God in a heavenly language as well as comforts us when we are most distraught. The new covenant is an everlasting covenant that will never fade because we serve a risen Savior.', 3, '2025-03-29 01:37:04', '2026-04-16 02:53:48', NULL),
(3312, 897, 670, 'God revealed himself during the Old Testament in two forms; one through revelations and word revelations. The New Testament reveals many works of God such as Jesus\'s ministry while on Earth, and how the Holy Spirit is evident in the first church. It also reveals to us that Christ is coming back. The Gospels show what God talked through Jesus\'s words which than revealed his actions. Old and New Testament biblical theology both emphasize on the acts of God and also his word revelations. The parables of Jesus give account to what he does as well as is a shadow of his works that will happen in the future.', 4, '2025-03-29 02:39:31', '2026-04-16 03:02:33', NULL),
(3313, 897, 671, 'There are major changes between the ministry on earth of Jesus, the past of the church, and the second coming of Jesus Christ. The New Testament usually treats these as one which displays a portrait of Jesus and his acts. For example, the Gospels only tell us about the life of Jesus and refers to the continuance of the ministy after Jesus leaves. Acts of the Apostles express what happens after Jesus leaves and Revelation only talks about the glorious return of Christ. New Testament scholars focus on the return of Jesus Christ and the church being reunited with our Savior.', 3, '2025-03-29 02:54:42', '2026-04-16 03:02:33', NULL),
(3314, 897, 672, 'God revealed himself through act and word revelations which caused developments in theology.Escatology teaches about the end times and what is yet to happen. The Old Testament set the standard for what New Testament scholars have found out about what is to come. In the beginning when God created us, he created us in his image. We have a call to be his preisthood to be fruitful and multiply. There are two forces fighting in the time of Adam, one is the seed of the serpent and the other being the seed of the woman. These two groups of people will always be at battle for victory.', 3, '2025-03-29 03:01:00', '2026-04-16 03:02:33', NULL),
(3315, 897, 673, 'The writers of the New Testament tested both personal and group eschatology that focused on the people of God being redeemed into the presence of God in the new Jerusalem, which refers to Eden. The majority of Jews in Palestine when the first church was established had views reflecting that of the Old Testament. Israel suffered so much but throughout these centuries, many Jews were waiting in the fullfillment of Old Testament prophesy.', 2, '2025-03-29 03:09:31', '2026-04-16 03:02:33', NULL),
(3316, 897, 674, 'My knowledge of the kingdom of God always affects how i do ministry.  I always have to think what would Jesus do, how will my actions affect others.  The kingdom of God mindset will not avoid life and it will allow me to be true to my responsibilities which will involve Christ in every aspect. Kingdom theology is a separation from the world which is ruled by Satan and we live out the kingdom while on Earth.  The kingdom mentality will allow me to trust God blindly knowing that he will provide and being content regardless of what we have or not. My ministry will change because I allow the Holy Spirit to direct our paths.', 2, '2025-03-29 03:18:21', '2026-04-16 03:02:33', NULL),
(3317, 898, 633, 'Eschatology refers to the end times.  Eschatology is the studying of the final things or the teaching of them.  It goes over a period of time of the final days of Jesus\'s life and his ministry and what will happen when he does come back to receive his church. In the Old Testament, there are three steps which are: the inception of the world and all of its beings, period of salvation after the fall due to sin, and finally when the heavenly kingdom is fulfilled.', 2, '2025-04-09 00:06:24', '2026-04-16 03:02:02', NULL),
(3318, 898, 634, 'Genesis chapter one instructs us that God established the heavens and the earth. God created every aspect of the universe including the oceans, the Earth, the heavens, every living creature such as all of the animals.  He also created the human race to be able to have dominion over all of creation which included the Earth and every living thing on it. God ultimately created us in his own likeness, which was both man and woman, and gave the command to bear fruit and establish generations to come. God had a purpose for all of the human race to be his instruments or essentially rulers of the world. The mandate placed on all people was to build a way of life throughout the universe but also creating the family unit and every part of society which includes governments and sciences. God\'s ultimate was for us to fill the world with a mirror that show we are community minded in giving glory to God.', 5, '2025-04-09 00:18:46', '2026-04-16 03:02:02', NULL),
(3319, 898, 635, 'Proto-euangelion refers to the first gospel, because it serves as the first opportunity of being saved after being subject to failing to meet the mark. The intent first for our salvation shows in Genesis chapter 3 verse 15 because immediately following that the lapse of judgement, there is a promise of one who rescues us from our difficulty with sinning. In the same chapter in Genesis we read that there will be fighting between the two seeds in which the snake will make a mark on the woman\'s child, but that child will eventually trample the head of the snake. The remainder of the Bible displays for us the salvation story that the destroying of the head of the snake will finally happen on the cross of Calvary.', 4, '2025-04-09 00:25:58', '2026-04-16 03:02:02', NULL),
(3320, 898, 636, 'God\'s first major redemptive covenant was with Adam who missed the mark in the Garden of Eden. From the inception of humanity, God had intentions for all of humanity and he knew that there will be two groups of people, one in which follow after God and the other group that rebel God.  The second redemptive covenant was made with Noah which he guaranteed that he would never ever destroy the world with a flood anymore. Through this promise, he did this to allow all of humanity to serve God\'s purposes.  God made his third redemptive covenant to Abraham to serve God in a special way.  Abraham and his generations to come played a role in the end times coming to pass. In Genesis chapter 22, God establishes that through his seed all the nations on the earth will be blessed. God\'s covenant with Abraham created the expectation that his earthly kingdom would not just include the nation of Israel but all peoples of the earth. God made his 4th redemptive covenant with Moses, that if the people of Israel follow God and all of his commands he will bless them. He mentioned to Moses that he will never abandon his people and will restore them to favor. The final covenant was with David that he would establish his house as a permanent dynasty ruling over God\'s earthly kingdom. God promised never to violate the covenant in Psalms 89 and it will be established like the moon and the faithful witness in the sky.', 5, '2025-04-09 00:45:32', '2026-04-16 03:02:02', NULL),
(3321, 898, 637, 'The two great ages were the present age which is marked by sin and the resiliance of Gentile nations which is seen as the the slavery of Israel. There is also an age to come which is when God will establish his heavenly kingdom on Earth.  In the Gospel of Mark 1:15 it tells us that the kingdom is to come, so we should repent and hear the good news. Luke 17:10 states that we have the kingdom of God within us which shows our future assurance of Jesus coming back. In Matthew chapter 25 we read that the Son of Man will come in glory to establish his kingdom. In Daniel chapter 12 we hear there is a belief that there will be a resurrection of the dead.  Jesus and his apostles disagreed with those students of the word about the age that is to come. Jesus and the apostles taught that the ages to come would not happen quickly. In revelation 1:5-6 Jesus has freed us from our sins by his blood and has made us to be a kingdom and priests to serve God. God is preparing a new heaven new earth for all of his church to go to.', 5, '2025-04-09 01:07:30', '2026-04-16 03:02:02', NULL),
(3322, 899, 638, 'The term regenerate is a symbol of the human race\'s current state or it refers to those in faith. The uregenerate refers to those individuals that do not believe and experience a lot of difficulties of today\'s day and time.  Regenerate also refers to being born again or we can refer to the people that are living in the spiritual realm and the unregenerate would be the opposite of that which refers to them being without life. The Bible talks about the fall through Adam and being dead until being renewed by the Holy Spirit.  The consequences of sin is referred to in Romans 6:23 which states the wages of sin is death but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ.', 4, '2025-04-10 00:14:10', '2026-04-16 03:01:31', NULL),
(3323, 899, 639, 'The unregenerate still have the ability to have thoughts, to have emotions, to have aspirations, to make decisions, and have conversations with the world just like the regenerate does.  God instructed not to eat from one particular tree which is the tree of knoledge of good and evil, in the garden of Eden and you will surely die, what he meant was not physically but spiritually.  We have to realize that when we sin, it takes us away from God and his glory. God has only two perspectives and that is either we live in Adam\'s sin or we are free from sin by the redemption in Christ Jesus. According to Augustine, Adam and Eve had the ability to prevent sin completely, but also had the ability to choose to sin. Through Adam all of his descendants including us lost the ability to prevent sin in our lives.  God essentially cursed the human race and humans became spiritually corrupt which leads to death spiritually.', 5, '2025-04-10 00:27:30', '2026-04-16 03:01:31', NULL),
(3324, 899, 640, 'In order for us to be reborn or to have life spiritually, our inner souls have to go from a dead state into being alive. We are born through our mothers womb initially but there is a second birth that comes from us accepting Jesus Christ as our personal savior and accepting him into our hearts.  Nicodemus who was wise beyond years, had a hard time understanding what it meant to be born again but Jesus was able to explain it to him. Jesus emphasizes us to be born of the Spirit which gives death to our flesh. Titus 3:5 states that God saved us through the renewing of the Holy Spirit and the blood of Jesus. God restores our moral ability to be obedient to the purposes of God and to go after the blessings of his heavenly kingdom. Ephesians 2:4-5 states that God who is rich in mercy, made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our sins and it is by grace we are saved.', 5, '2025-04-10 00:38:02', '2026-04-16 03:01:31', NULL),
(3325, 899, 641, 'The intermediate state refers to the period that happens between when we die and the resurrection. This intermediate state are for the ones in faith and also to those not in the faith alike. When we all pass away, we go to the intermediat state which is waiting for the resurrection. The  unregenerate, their souls still are alive which causes fear.  When the spirit returns to God who gave it, their punishment is in hell. Luke 12:4-5, Do not be afraid of those who kill the body and after that can do no more, fear him who, after the killing of the body, has power to throw you into hell. Hell is a reality because Jesus Christ mentioned it many times. In hell, the the rich man was in torment who saw Abraham far away with Lazarus by his side. This is shown in Luke 16 how the rich man asks for mercy from Abraham and he states to him to remember your lifetime and now you are in despair. Being apart from Christ leads to eternal damnation or Hades, the lake of fire.', 5, '2025-04-10 00:56:00', '2026-04-16 03:01:31', NULL),
(3326, 899, 642, 'In Luke 23:43 Jesus told the thief on the cross that this very day they would be together in paradise. Revelation 6:9 mentions the souls of those who died for the sake of the Gospel being very near to the altar in God\'s heavenly tabernacle.  Paul mentions in Philipians 1:23 that his decision would be to away from his physical body and to be in eternity with his heavenly father. Jesus says that he leaves us to prepare a place for us and that he will come back and take us to be with him. Jesus says that I am the way, the trutght and the light in essence he takes us right by his side to the ultimate spot which is heaven. Jesus through the cross of Calvary and dying on the cross, allows our sins to be washed, and we find our place in heaven with him.', 5, '2025-04-10 01:02:51', '2026-04-16 03:01:31', NULL),
(3327, 900, 648, 'In the first century there are two dominant schools of thought concerning the resurrection of the dead, the Pharisee view and the Saducean view.  The Pharisee view was that at the end of time, the righteous and wicked will be judged and the righteous will be raised. Their belief is grounded in the re-establisment of Israel. Daniel 12:2 states that many of those who are who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt. The Sadducees view rejected the idea that the dead would be resurrected, as well as belief in angels and anything of heaven. They held firm to the teachings of the Torah, and they were an elite priestly class.since they were only focused on the worship in the Temple.  Acts 23:8 states that the Saduccees say that there is no resurrection, nor angel, nor spirit, but the Pharisees acknowledge them all. In Matthew 22:23 the Sadducees approach Jesus mocking the resurrection. Jesus view on the resurrection is stated in John 11:25 I am the resurrection and the life and anyone who believes in me, though he die, will live. Paul says is 1 Corinthians 15 that if Christ hasnt been raised that our faith is in vain. In Acts 24, we read that Paul is saying there will be a resurrection for the just and the wicked.', 8, '2025-04-11 00:21:00', '2026-04-16 03:01:00', NULL),
(3328, 900, 649, 'Divine authority means the complete, and sovereign power that God has to govern, and give life according to his divine plan.  The divine authority is built in God\'s character which he has created all things and is our sustainer. God\'s authority can never be questioned. John 5:28-29 states that do not marvel at this, for the hour is coming, in which all who are in the graves will hear his voice and come forth. Jesus has divine power to call people from the dead. God has an authority to be the ultimate judge of our lives.  Revelation 20:12 states that everyone both small and great will stand before the throne and the books were opened. Heaven is where God lives, and hell he has rule over as well. God has power to give us eternal life and he determines where our eternity will be whether in heaven or the lake of fire. Ultimately Jesus is our advocate for God, and all authority has been given to him as mentioned in Matthew 28:8. Jesus victory over death which allows God to raise us from the dead and judge all of humanity.', 8, '2025-04-11 00:36:33', '2026-04-16 03:01:00', NULL),
(3329, 900, 650, 'The general resurrection is seen as a restoration of the universe. Romans 8:19-23 speaks of creation groaning in expectation of given freedom from sin. God will renew the physical world in removing the curse of sin and death shown in Genesis 3. There is a new heaven and a new earth that is established where there will be no more tears, no more pain, and we will be free from suffering. The general resurrection also refers to the wicked, and has impacts on a final judgement. Revelation 20 states that there will be a separation for all who reject Christ. Revelation 21 shows the heaven is established on Earth and the new Jerusalem is established which is the final image of God dwelling with humanity again like before the fall in Eden. Philippians 3 states that we will have glorified ressurected bodies like the one Jesus had. Heaven is a place where we will have a perfect fellowship with God once and for all.', 8, '2025-04-11 00:48:58', '2026-04-16 03:01:00', NULL),
(3330, 900, 651, 'There are three groups of people that will be judged: the righteous, the wicked, and the fallen angels. In Matthew 25, Jesus states to come, you who are blessed by my Father, take your inheritance, there is a kingdom that is being prepared from the creation of the world. The righteous who have obeyed all of God\'s commands, demonstrated their affection for Jesus Christ and showed it through their actions will receive the crown of righteousness. The second group is the wicked who are those who live in rebellion and reject God and deny he exists, they will also be judged. The wicked are ultimately separated from God forever and has an eternal punishment. The third group that will be judged will be the fallen angels which is the devil himself. In Revelation 20 it mentions that God will through them into the lake of fire and they will be tormented day and night forever.', 8, '2025-04-11 00:57:08', '2026-04-16 03:01:00', NULL),
(3331, 900, 652, 'God will consider every piece of evidence in our final judgement in that God will be fair and just. God does not show any partiality like humans and he sees our inner most being which is our heart. God will look at the circumstances in which we grew up in, as well will take into consideration if we have had opportunities to accept Jesus as our personal savior.  For example someone who grew up in a place where the Gospel was not preached as much will not be judged the same as the person who has access to the Gospel very freely and has a knowledge of the word of God. Luke 12:47-48 shows us about servants who knew the master\'s will but chose not to do it.  God will keep us in check by our understanding, our access to the gospel.  God\'s judgement will be full of mercy and is not cookie cutter based but it is based on the individual.  God will take into consideration our situations and our accountability will be different based on our backgrounds.', 7, '2025-04-11 01:05:24', '2026-04-16 03:01:00', NULL),
(3332, 901, 927, 'The word trinity means essentially tri-unity which means three in one. The teaching of the Trinity can be described as three holy individuals in one divine essence. There is only one God, but God shows his identity through the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. We understand that God the Father is the one who sent the Son who was crucified and risen, who than goes to be with his Father in heaven and promises us the comforter which is the Holy Spirit. We have to understand that there is only one God but displayed in three different persons.', 5, '2025-04-16 00:09:34', '2026-04-16 03:00:29', NULL),
(3333, 901, 928, 'With respect to the Trinity, the person is a specific individual as being related to God the Father. A person usually refers to an individual who can make their own choices, and has their own perspectives. The three individuals of the Trinity do not symbolize three different gods, but it represents one with God the Father with one divine essence.  In the teaching of Trinity, a person refers to a unique relationship with God the Father, but still being equal persons with the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit.', 3, '2025-04-16 00:21:25', '2026-04-16 03:00:29', NULL),
(3334, 901, 929, 'Since God is one, we understand that there is one holy essence. God\'s oneness is refers to God being simple.  John 4:24 says that God is spirit, and as spirit, he is indivisble. God is not one that is hard to understand. We have various ways to describe God\'s character in that he is omniscient, omnipresent, and omnipotent and also represents love, he is one that sheds mercy and also just in his judgements. God is not made up of man made ingredients which means there is no unrighteousness in our God. God reveals himself to Moses in Exodus as I am who I am, which means he is pure spirit. God is wholly together with all that he accomplishes. God is never in fight between his anger and his mercy. All of God\'s characteristics work simultaneously, also working individually which makes him without any error.', 4, '2025-04-16 00:30:56', '2026-04-16 03:00:29', NULL),
(3335, 901, 930, 'Christ has always existed based on the teaching of the Trinity.  John 1:1 says that in the beginning was the word, the word was with God and the word was God.  In the same chapter in verse fourteen, we read that the word became flesh and made his dwelling in us. There are those who are against the Trinity that like to quote scripture from Colossians 1 that God has not always existed as Trinity. They believe that he was a created being. In the Old Testament when we refer to the firstborn its not referring to chronologically but it is referred to as priority or supremacy. Jesus symbolizes our access to the God the Father and the Holy Spirit as providing us our salvation. Paul urges the church of Corinth in 1 Corinthians to worship God the Father and God the Son, this is how we be assured that Jesus is God and he establishes that Jesus is a part of creation.', 5, '2025-04-16 00:43:09', '2026-04-16 03:00:29', NULL),
(3336, 901, 931, 'The Great Commission in the last chapter of the gospel of Matthew teaches us to go and baptize people in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit and so we have one God. In the Old Testament the priests were to bless the children of Israel, as stated in Numbers 6, the Lord bless you, and keep you, make his face shine upon you and make his face turn towards you and give you peace. The Spirit is of heaven which shares equality with the Father and the Son. Whenever we say the benediction we mention Trinity which is only provided through the death and resurrection of Jesus Christ. When God decides to create something, he consults every part of Trinity including the Holy Spirit. In Genesis we read that the Spirit hovered over the waters which means it was present from the beginning of creation. The Holy Spirit is always alive in every part of creation, inlcuding our very salvation because it is the Spirit at work to give us new life. Ultimately there is only one God who co-exists as three individuals which is the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Each individual of thte Trinity are one in their characteristics from heaven. Each member of the Trinity are equal in power and glory.', 5, '2025-04-16 00:56:20', '2026-04-16 03:00:29', NULL),
(3337, 902, 932, 'The teaching of the Trinity instructs us that there is one God, one divine essence, and every individual of the Trinity are equal and fully God. Much of the truths about Trinity come from the New Testament. God is showing us in gradual steps more and more about who he is and how he works. Throughout time, God has been on the move gradually to become more intimate with us. God spoke in the Old Testament through creation like the clouds, the burning bush but there was always a space between God and us.  In the New Testament God became closer to us through his son Jesus Christ and the Scripture says through the Holy Spirit, he is now dwelling in us. The same resurrection power that Jesus Christ had we also have through the Holy Spirit. This Holy Spirit allows us to do greater things on the Earth than Jesus did and accomplish the will of the Father. We have to realize that the Holy Spirit helped breath the scriptures into life.', 5, '2025-04-18 00:10:31', '2026-04-16 02:59:31', NULL),
(3338, 902, 933, 'Revelation chapters one and four states that the one who sits on the throne is God the Father. John 1 speaks to the word of God becoming flesh.  Apostle John sees a roll of paper and is told that no one can open the paper. John is crying understanding that this roll of paper needs to be opened in order to understand the inner most revelation of God. The Lion shows kingship, also represents the tribe of Judah. John also sees a lamb which shows us sacrifice which depicts pain. Jesus is the king who has conquered everything and provided us our redemption. The Lion shows majesty while the lamb represents humbleness. The divine nature of Jesus is displayed who died as the ultimate price for our salvation. Revelation 5 puts Jesus in the bucket of being divine. He is not a creature of God but he is one with God who will utterly destroy all of his enemies when he comes back.', 5, '2025-04-18 00:25:18', '2026-04-16 02:59:31', NULL),
(3339, 902, 934, 'The first chapter of John has been well known through out time and gives us evidence that Jesus is the Son which is the second individual of the Trinity. The scripture shows us that Jesus he is God and has been one with God since the beginning. Jesus has also been a part of making all things. John 1:1 teaches us the deity of Jesus Christ. John depicts Jesus as God himself and to refer to Jesus as the Word of God is pointing to his divine nature. John says in Jesus we become a new being and he is the one that brings us life. The revised version of this verse states what God was,the Word was.  John is basically saying that Jesus is equal in every aspect including dominion, and adoration.', 5, '2025-04-18 00:42:50', '2026-04-16 02:59:31', NULL),
(3340, 902, 935, '1 Corinthians 3:16 says that we are the temple of God this basically means that God abides in us.  Romans 8 mentions that the same spirt that rose Jesus from the dead is the same spirit that is inside of us.  The Spirit examines everything, especially the depth of God. No one is able to understand the mind of God except through the Spirit.  In the upper room the Holy Spirit came down upon the apostles which gave them an authority and a special boldness. The Holy Spirit has emotions, Paul says in Ephesians that we can hurt the Holy Spirit and not to put out its fire.  The Holy Spirit sanctifies us and helps us live righteously on Earth.  In order for us to demonstrate the Holy Spirit, we should be witnesses everywhere we go about who Jesus is in our lives.', 5, '2025-04-18 00:57:47', '2026-04-16 02:59:31', NULL),
(3341, 902, 936, 'The Bible instructs that Jesus is the one and true living God. The thing about Jesus is that he was fully human as well as fully God and it is through his life that we have redemption of our sins. Since Jesus the human is also the eternal God, which has the same authority and glory to the Father, we are able to believe that he is able to give us our salvation. Jesus is awesome because he came to the earth as a human with no blemish, he died and rose again so that we can be identified with God the Father. The reason why Jesus became man is to truly be a presentation of us, he had to be enticed just like we are,', 5, '2025-04-18 01:07:06', '2026-04-16 02:59:31', NULL),
(3342, 903, 937, 'All three members of the Trinity are from heaven and not created. Throughout creation all members of the Trinity existed.  The Father has a special relationship with the Trinity. The Father was never conceived and does not come from any other members of the Trinity. The Bible never mentions every the Son and the Spirit disagreeing with the Father because they will all have the same mindset. The three members of the Trinity will always be in together on every circumstance.  Despite the Father who is the one brought the idea of salvation, Jesus Christ accomplished it for us and it is the Holy Spirit who brings it to application in our lives.  So there will never be any disagreement because it is never shown in God\'s word.', 3, '2025-04-19 00:12:20', '2026-04-16 02:58:59', NULL),
(3343, 903, 938, 'Every member of the Trinity has a special relationship with the Trinity and so does the Son. The Bible states that the Father begat the Son which can be confusing when we think of it in the human sense.  Revelation chapter 5 states that the Son is from heaven and that he is always been around. God from heaven enabled Jesus Christ come into the world through the Holy Spirit to defy odds. Anything that has to do with our human character, Christ placed it on himself.  Jesus was fully human and fully divine and we would not be saved if the Son did not take on human form. Jesus knew he left the heavenly glory to take upon our sins with his punishment.  The Son was still omnipotent, omnipresent, omniscient, which means he fully knew how salvation would be achieved. The son never gave up any of his divine qualities when he came in the form of a human.', 3, '2025-04-19 00:25:43', '2026-04-16 02:58:59', NULL),
(3344, 903, 939, 'The Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit all are eternal which have no beginning or end, Many early church councils such as Nicea and Constantinople came to the defense of the autonomous character of the Holy Spirit. In 381 the council of Constantinople was summoned to talk about the Macedonian controversy. The bishop of Constantinople said many conflicting things about the Holy Spirit that it was a establised individual like an angel. The Nicene Creed stated that the Holy Spirit is the Lord, gives life and is to be adored just like the Father and the Son. The teaching of the Holy Spirit eternal procession always had controversy. Some scholars had conflicts that the Holy Spirit came from the Father and others said that the Spirit came from both the Father and the Son. Galatians 4:6 mentions that the Holy Spirit is what enables us to be called his children.', 3, '2025-04-19 00:41:44', '2026-04-16 02:58:59', NULL),
(3345, 903, 940, 'In 589 A. D. a church council met in Toledo Spain and met to make a case for the teaching of where the Holy Spirit comes from. They added a latin term which was filoque which meant and the Son to the Nicene Creed.The Byzantine Empire was was the eastern portion of Rome rejected filoque from the West. They were trying to correlate the work of the Holy Spirit, is to glorify the Son,and the Spirit will bear witness to the Son.  Their argument was that all three members of the Trinity will allow God\'s glory to be fulfilled. What separated the East and the West on the doctine of Trinity was the concept of the filoque, or the double procession of the Holy Spirit.', 3, '2025-04-19 00:51:30', '2026-04-16 02:58:59', NULL),
(3346, 903, 941, 'The word ontological refers to being or existence. All three persons of the Trinity have the same value, authority, and adoration. We know that God is omnipresent and it refers to God is not consumed by time or controlled by it. Augustine gave us a simple explanation to understand in that the Father who loves the Son who is beloved and the Holy Spirit who communicates the love between them and within the very being of God, love is provided and also told of. The ontological Trinity is shown from Scripture as the relationship within God himsely beyond all creation.', 3, '2025-04-19 00:59:29', '2026-04-16 02:58:59', NULL),
(3347, 904, 866, 'There is really no specific person that has wrote the Apostle\'s Creed. In 200 AD there was a person named Tertullius which relates to a Roman Creed which has similarities with the Apostle\'s Creed. This creed is developed for candidates that want to be baptized, do you have belief in God the Father, the Son Jesus Christ and the Holy Spirit. The Apostle\'s Creed was meant to be a measure of faith and to keep the doctrines of the church in tact. The doctrines were meant to train up a set of leaders. The Apostle\'s Creed was generated to give aid to help people in faith remain faithful to the instructions of Jesus. The Apostle\'s creed rose to importance in order to fulfill the need, this was meant to be acceptable to every congregation that calls itself Christian. The Apostle\'s Creed is a story of a Christian\'s faith. The Apostles Creed was developed to help one share their faith easily.', 5, '2025-04-29 00:22:33', '2026-04-16 02:58:29', NULL),
(3348, 904, 867, 'There are many reasons why its important for the church to use a creed, one being that it is the building blocks of our faith. This creed serves as an anchor that holds a place together in place which gives energy and stability for the entire building. Christ Jesus is our chief cornerstone, in Christ the whole structure is put together and creates a holy temple of God. The church is not able to function without ground rules such as a firm foundation. The teachings of Jesus sets the foundation of our faith, as the disciples introduced Jesus to the world equals the Apostle\'s Creed. The Apostle\'s Creed is a summary of of the basic teachings of Jesus of who God is and how the Holy Spirit works out for our salvation. The Creed serves as a baseline which presents the biggest fundamentals of Christianity. The Apostle\'s Creed come from a logical sense which refers to God being our creator and believing in his divine authority.', 5, '2025-04-29 00:39:51', '2026-04-16 02:58:29', NULL),
(3349, 904, 868, 'The Gospels present the truths behind the birth, the life, ministry, death, the resurrection and the ascension. The Apostle\'s Creed argues for the divinity of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The Creed tells us about the Triune character of God.  God is the Father, God is the Son, and God is the Holy Spirit. The statement of Trinity is a declaration about who our God is in his nature. God is the Father who has ultimate authority who is the creator and sustainer of all things often shown as the loving parent. God the Son Jesus Christ became mortal man to deliver us from sin and its punishment.  God the Holy Spirit gives us a supernatural strength know that God is with us and it also convicts us when we sin. The Trinity is a complex teaching but is an essential part of the teaching of Christ and how we worship.', 5, '2025-04-29 00:59:47', '2026-04-16 02:58:29', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3350, 904, 869, 'It is always important to be an active part of church because we are essentially the body of Christ.  We represent the temple of God and so we have to live out our faith daily.  Being involved in various ministries of the church will allow us to strengthen the gifts and talents God has blessed us with to bear fruit for his kingdom. Being an active part of church ministry allows us to fulfill Jesus own description of his church. Being unified with the church allows us to chase after unity with one another despite differences may be there. When we are an active part of church ministry it allows us to stand together with fellow believers of Christ which will make our sharing the gospel more effective. There are many Christians that become martyrs for Christ for standing up for their beliefs in the Word of God. Ephesians 4:11-13 states that Jesus has appointed some to become apostles, some to be prophets, some to be evangelists, and some to be pastors, and teachers, so that the church which is the body of Christ can be built up until we all reach unity in the faith and in the knowledge of the Son of God and become mature, attaining to the whole measure of the fullness of Christ. Being involved in the day to day ministries of the church allow us to keep to the main themes of the teachings of Jesus Christ.', 5, '2025-04-29 01:10:00', '2026-04-16 02:58:29', NULL),
(3351, 904, 870, 'The Apostles Creed is used to affirm that new believers of Christ has the basic teachings of Jesus.  The Creed helped the church worship and understand the basic principles of God. The Creed reaffirmed that God is the Father, God is the Son and God is the Holy Spirit. The Creed reminds us that Jesus came to fulfill the plan of God the Father in redeeming us from our sins by serving as the punishment for our sins on the cross of Calvary. The Apostle\'s Creed also helps us remember to praise God and remember what he did for the church. The Apostle\'s Creed served as a declaration of faith and our believe in the Trinity and that each individual of Trinity had an equal part in our salvation.', 5, '2025-04-29 01:18:24', '2026-04-16 02:58:29', NULL),
(3352, 905, 871, 'Scripture defines God as only one true God and that is what we as Christians believe. This equates to God having the ability to create, to punish and to save. Our faith in God is really essential to all that we believe in. We have to realize that God is in the middle of our lives and everything revolves around God. We have to remove our self pride or conceit and be rooted on the mind of Christ Jesus. In the beginning when God created the universe we are able to see God\'s characteristics that may not be visible but have a divine nature. Our sin is what prevents us from truly knowing the one true God.', 4, '2025-04-29 02:05:58', '2026-04-16 02:57:17', NULL),
(3353, 905, 872, 'When we talk about the simplicity of God, it is his essence that we have in our mind, his character and what he is made of. The teaching of Trinity teaches us about three persons, but only one essence. When we mention that God has a simple, unified essence, we are denying that the Father, the Son and Holy Spirit were three separate Gods that by chance came together in the Trinity. The simplicity refers to the Trinity as to always have been around. As Christians we believe in one God who is three persons that have significance in a Christian\'s life. Our worship as Christians has always focused on the Trinity, so we are unable to disregard any one person of the Trinity because it will be as if we are neglecting God himself.', 5, '2025-04-29 02:13:25', '2026-04-16 02:57:17', NULL),
(3354, 905, 873, 'Ontological Trinity has to do with its being or existence. The idea of ontology, all three persons of the Trinity are infinite, eternal and unchangeable. All three persons of the Trinity have the same characteristics such as wisdom, power, holiness, being just, his truth. When we look at the economic Trinity, we see how they all have different roles, as well as things to do. All three persons of the Trinity talk to each other, they make mutual agreements and they act in unison with one another. In both the economical and ontological views, The Father is the first person.', 5, '2025-04-29 02:23:14', '2026-04-16 02:57:17', NULL),
(3355, 905, 874, 'The fatherhood of God is described in three main ways such as: the creator, fatherhood in terms of his position as king over everyone and finally God as the family head over his people.  Psalm 145:8-9 says that the Lord is gracious and compassionate,slow to anger and rich in love. The Lord is good to all and has compassion on all he has made. Kings in the oldern days were called as fathers because they were always being caring and proctecting of all needs as a whole. God was called the Father because he was the great king that had dominion over all the rulers of the world. Its good that we know that the king in heaven truly cares for us because when we are unable to fight against the principalities of the enemy, he is always there to help us. Genesis 5 shows the relationship that Adam had with his children is the same relationship that God desires. Hosea 11:1 shows God as a parent who loves Israel. Jesus taught that God answers our petitions in the same manner as if a earthly father would do for his children.', 5, '2025-04-29 02:37:30', '2026-04-16 02:57:17', NULL),
(3356, 905, 875, 'When God is angry, the earth tends to shake and many nations will be unable to sustain the wraths of God. It is with tremendous power that God made the earth, he is the founder of the world by his wisdom was able to stretch out the heavens by his knowledge. God is the one who sends all types os weather as well. We have to realize that God is sovereign over everything and knows my circumstances and is the one that keeps walking alongside me. God is omnipotent, omniscient, and omnipresent, we serve an amazing and powerful God. God is the same yesterday, today and forever, he will never change.', 4, '2025-04-29 02:47:38', '2026-04-16 02:57:17', NULL),
(3357, 906, 876, 'Jesus Christ is fully God and also fully human. The Scripture states that he is the Son of the one true living God. This is in essence the basic truth of our Christian faith. Paul the apostle mentions that in him all things are created and that he has all the dominion.  Jesus is divine and this is why we refer to him as the Son of God. The New Testament is adamant about who God is and that Jesus is the unique son of God. We are adopted into the family of Christ by the realtionship we have. Jesus has always been the Son of God because in John chapter eight we read that he has come from above and was never cfreated in the world.  Jesus in the New Testament spoke of himself as God\'s son which means that he is equal with God the Father.', 3, '2025-04-29 23:53:45', '2026-04-16 02:56:37', NULL),
(3358, 906, 877, 'Romans 10, we read that if you confess with your mouth, Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved. For everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved. Paul said that Jesus is Lord and he meant that he is the Lord and God of the Old Testament. When we acknowledge Jesus as our Lord, we worship Jesus as our God in our prayers and songs. It tells us that we should also pray to him and that we can take peace in the stability of our redemption, knowing that God took away our sins. Joel 2:32  proves that everyone who calls on his name will be saved as well as Isaiah 40 says that those who wait upon the Lord will be renewed and they run and not be weary.', 3, '2025-04-30 00:03:38', '2026-04-16 02:56:37', NULL),
(3359, 906, 878, 'There are three ways that Jesus proves that he was truly human and they are: his human experiences, his human office and his human nature as relates to his divine nature. Jesus was born by the Holy Spirit, born to Mary the virgin and he endured a lot under Pontus Pilate. Jesus was crucified on the Cross, died, and was buried. God formed Jesus in the womb of Mary in the same way that every human is conceived. Jesus development in the womb was a natural thing.  Jesus had to wear a crown of thorns, he had an execution on the Cross of Calvary and he bled out every drop of blood which made him fully human. In order for us to receive redemption of our sins, Jesus had to come down in the form of a human being and suffer on our behalf. When Jesus died, his soul was separated from his body in the same manner as our bodies do as well.', 5, '2025-04-30 00:17:54', '2026-04-16 02:56:37', NULL),
(3360, 906, 879, 'The English word Christ is a greek translation of christos which means messiah or the annointed one. In the Old Testament especially in the Chronicles it refers to prophets as annointed priests. David was promised by God that his kingdom on earth will be established forever. Many of the Old Testament prophets mentioned the Messiah as a descendant of David who would bring the captives back to the Promised Land and restore blessings to a nation. Jeremiah 23:5-6 says that the Days are coming when i will raise up to David a righteous branch, a king who will reign wisely and do what is just and right in the land. Jesus fulfilled his office by dying and three days later being risen and having defeated the grave.', 5, '2025-04-30 00:29:23', '2026-04-16 02:56:37', NULL),
(3361, 906, 880, 'Jesus has the same characteristics that any human has such as a physical and a human body and a human soul. Jesus was not immune from sickness, death or bodily issues and he had normal physical restraints.  Jesus was born without sin and also lived a life that was void of sin. Jesus had the ability to sin but he never did. Philippians 2 Paul wrote that Jesus Christ being in the very nature of God, made himself nothing, taking the nature of a servant, being made in human likeness. Jesus contained every personality trait tied to divinity and also tied to humanity. Jesus ultimately became the mediator between us and God and through his death we have been made right with God the Father.', 5, '2025-04-30 00:40:32', '2026-04-16 02:56:37', NULL),
(3362, 907, 881, 'The council of Chalcedon confirmed that the Holy Spirit should be worshipped as fully divine like Jesus Christ.  The Apostle\'s Creed says that Jesus Christ is the Son of God and that he was born by the Holy Spirt. The Holy Spirit is equal to the power of the Most High. Hebrews 10 says that when Christ came to the world, he didnt desire sacrifice or offering, but a body prepared for me, with burnt and sin offerings you were not pleased.  The Holy Spirit is called by a variety of names which demonstrates its divine nature. The Holy Spirit is only able to do what God is able to do.', 3, '2025-04-30 01:56:54', '2026-04-16 02:54:50', NULL),
(3363, 907, 882, 'The Creed serves as a summary of the Scripture.The Holy Spirit can only perform what God is able to do. The Bible basis is described in various names and attributes such as his divine nature,. The term righteous often refers to being one with God. In the Old Testament God is referred to as the Holy Spirit. God is united with the Holy Spirit in the same manner that a human is united to his own spirit. The Holy Spirit has various character traits such as love, omiscience, and omniprescence. The Bible says that the Spirit knows the mind of God. The Holy Sprit brings life to those who are destitute. The Holy Spirit also inspired the Bible being written.', 3, '2025-04-30 02:09:52', '2026-04-16 02:54:50', NULL),
(3364, 907, 883, 'In John chapter 14 we read that the Holy Spirit serves as our Counselor who is one to bring things into the light. According to Jesus the church of today is better with the Holy Spirit as opposed to having Jesus in the flesh. Some of the attributes of the Holy Spirit include intelligence in which he has the smarts to teach many. John 14:26 talks.  about the Holy Spirit being able to teach you all things and to remember all the command i gave to you. The Holy Spirit has feelings which goes into effect when certain events happen. The Holy Spirit will intercede on our behalf to ensure that we stay away from that fire that is burning.', 3, '2025-04-30 02:20:02', '2026-04-16 02:54:50', NULL),
(3365, 907, 884, 'The Holy Spirit is different from the Father and the Son.  The Holy Spirit came into the world once Jesus died and rose again, and he serves as our Counselor when we are in need. God is the one who plans our salvation, The Son accomplishes salvation and the Holy Spirit brings application to our lifes. The Holy Spirit\'s individualty is a valuable part of Christian teachings. The character of the Holy Spirit clearly identify him as a specific, self aware personality. The Holy Spirit prays with us and dwells in our hearts.', 3, '2025-04-30 02:35:50', '2026-04-16 02:54:50', NULL),
(3366, 907, 885, 'John 15:26 says that shen the helper comes, he will come from the Father. God the Father sends the Son Jesus Christ and when Jesus leaves he promises the gift of the Holy Spirit. Being the third person of Trinity has nothing to do with inferiority but its an order in eternal procession. The Holy Spirit provides gifts to his people for the benefit of the church. These spiritual gifts are the power of God which is given to help the saints mature in Christ Jesus.', 3, '2025-04-30 02:50:12', '2026-04-16 02:54:50', NULL),
(3367, 908, 886, 'The idea of the body of Christ in the New Testament finds its basis in the Old Testament. The church did not just form at the day of Pentectost or when Jesus ascended into heaven. The church today is always progressing from the many individuals in the Old Testament such as Abraham. In chapter 7 of the book of Acts we see that Moses was in the assembly in the desert with an angel who spoke to him at Mount Sinai which living words were given to him. 1 Peter 2:9 mentions that we are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people that belongs to God. Apostle Peter is quoting from various parts of Old Testament scripture and speaking of the nation Israel. Apostle Peter is teaching many churches that are mostly consisted of Gentiles to look at them as the fulfillment of the promises of God to Israel and to understand where there personality comes from.  The Old Testament church was primarily Jews and us as Gentile Christians were the wild frutit branches that were added into the tree.', 8, '2025-04-30 23:42:47', '2026-04-16 02:52:59', NULL),
(3368, 908, 887, 'Jesus never built a new church, the church was based from the Old Testament. Jesus mentioned ekklesia on three different times which are shown in the gospels. Matthew 16:18 Jesus says that he will build his church and the gates of Hades will not overcome it. The church in the New Testament was always a progression of the gatherings in the Old Testament. Jesus never came to make changes to Israel with the church, but he came to redeem Israel in the image of the New Testament church. Jesus represents the fruition of God\'s promises to Israel. Jesus is the one perosn that brought renewal to the Old Testament church into the New Testament church.  The relationship that an Old Testament believer has with God is the same as the relationship with God that we have in the New Testament, the only thing is that we have direct access through the sacrifice that Jesus paid on the cross of Calvary.', 8, '2025-04-30 23:52:36', '2026-04-16 02:52:59', NULL),
(3369, 908, 888, 'One important sign that there is a progression between Israel in the Old Testament and the Christian church in the New Testament. The people of God in the New Testament display a foundation that can be found in the Old Testament. There are three stages that we can put the world into and they are creation, fall, and redemption. When Adam and Eve sinned, they corrupted the whole world to sin. God was always trying to restore mankind to the purpose he had intended which was to be holy and righteous in essence perfect. Our hope and prayer is that Jesus is coming back and that we should display the love of Christ in every one of our actions.  The second thing that we know why the church is deriving from the Old Testament is that we see two groups of people, one being believers and the other group the ones that do not believe. The church was never perfect in the Old Testament, some were loyal to God and others were against God and received curses of God. The children of God regardless of being in the Old Testament or the New Testament had a calling of loving God, and fulfilling the great commission.', 7, '2025-05-01 00:03:00', '2026-04-16 02:52:59', NULL),
(3370, 908, 889, 'Catholic means universal or inclusive of all followers of Christ.  Catholic refers to a sense of unity that belongs to all of the denominations that are loyal to Jesus Christ. The Apostle\'s Creed when talking about the catholicty of the church is not referring to catholicism but in essence referring to the bringing together of the Holy Spirit that is living in all churches despite differences within those entities. Paul mentioned it in his letters to the church in Corinth, to anyone that is sanctified in Christ Jesus and called to be holy, together will all the saints who call on the name of Jesus Christ. Every Christ follower has a covering under Christ\'s covenant headship and all those who believe can be blessed with the gifts of the Holy Spirit. The church that can be seen in essence can be referred to as catholic.', 7, '2025-05-01 00:10:34', '2026-04-16 02:52:59', NULL),
(3371, 908, 890, 'We can imply that the visible catholic church is the fellowship of everyone that is in covenant with the same God and under the headship of Christ. Protestants agree that the visible church is catholic because you are able to find it everywhere that people are in covenant with God in the unity of the Holy Spirit. Churches had markers which enabled anyone to find out where the church is located, whether or not the word of God is being preached there, and if the sacraments of the church are done properly. The second mark of the church is the sacraments such as baptism and the lord\'s supper which belong to the universal church. The third marker is discipline where individuals sin is so deep that it puts the church in harm, this is where formal discipline can be administered. This is what helps us understand who the true church is and understand when there are duplicates or fakes.', 7, '2025-05-01 00:20:29', '2026-04-16 02:52:59', NULL),
(3372, 909, 891, 'Romans 6:23, Apostle Paul says that the wages of sin is death but the gift of God is eternal life through Christ Jesus.  Sin is a separation from God and it puts us very far from Christ. The farther we go from Christ the more deeper we have got into sin.  Sin can be described in various ways such as missing the mark, being rebellious towards your spiritual authority and just being disobedient to the Scriptures. 1 John 3:4  mentions that everyone who sins breaks the law and in fact sin is lawlessness. Sin is refusing to obey the commands of God as well as utter neglect for what is right. In order to be free of sin, we need to love God and if we do we will also love his precepts. Sin started in the garden of Eden with Adam and Eve refused to trust God and ate of the forbidden fruit of the tree of knowledge of good and evil. This is described as the fall for all of humanity. Sin entered through one man and death through sin came to all men through Adam\'s disobedience. The consequences of sin are hardship, pain, being spiritually dead and a physical death. Sin is a complete fight against God, it is breaking the law. The problem with sin is that we are lost and doomed.  There is no way for us to make ammends for our sin apart from God\'s forgiveness.', 7, '2025-05-01 01:48:23', '2026-04-16 02:52:32', NULL),
(3373, 909, 892, 'God will not allow all of the human race to be under the curse of sin. He still wanted humanity to fulfill his plan and reveal his glory. His plan was to send his only begotten son to die on the cross of Calvary, suffer for us and than be resurrected after three days. Jesus as our Savior is the only one that can save us from our sin and he provides us with an unmerited favor. Forgiveness is inclusive of all three individuals of the Trinity, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. The Father had a plan to send Jesus to accomplishes it and the Spirit who helps us apply it. All three persons help us achieve salvation and they also act in love and on the other hand also upholds righteousness and judgement. The Holy Spirit is what allows the forgiveness to become real in our lives. When we first accept Jesus Christ as our personal savior, we are through the Holy Spirit made right with God by forgiving us of all the sins we have committed up until that point. 2 Thesallonians 2:13 says that God chose us to be saved through the sanctifying work of the Spirit and through belief in the truth.', 5, '2025-05-01 01:56:14', '2026-04-16 02:52:32', NULL),
(3374, 909, 893, 'The Bible states that God will not forgive the sins over every individual. In order for an individual to be forgiven we have to have complete assurance in God. We as an individual have a responsibility to  recognize God is in control, being submissive to him and trust that his mercy will endure forever. In Psalm 103 we read that God is slow to anger and abundant in love, he does not treat us as our sin deserve, for as high as the heavens are above the earth, so great is his love for those who fear him, the lord has compassion on all who fear him. We also have to be remorseful about our sin and feel a sens of brokenness for God to forgive us.', 4, '2025-05-01 02:03:38', '2026-04-16 02:52:32', NULL),
(3375, 909, 894, 'When Adam and Eve fell for sin, sin ruined not only their souls but also their physical bodies. This corruption is what lead them to their physical death. When Adam and Eve sinned they went from being immortal like God to becoming mortal which meant they will eventually return to the dust. The corruption of our bodies result in pains, becoming sick and will lead to death. There is a promise of God that gives us salvation through Christ Jesus which will save both our bodies and our souls. Death happens in the physical sense as well as the spiritual as mentioned in Romans 6:23. Death can be seen as a blessing to the saints because we are reunited with Christ Jesus.', 4, '2025-05-01 02:11:59', '2026-04-16 02:52:32', NULL),
(3376, 909, 895, 'The three stages are justification, sanctification, and glorification.  Justification is the process of redeeming us from our past sins. Justification begins the moment that we accept Jesus Christ as our personal savior.Sanctification is the liberty from the power of sin in our lives. We are encouraged in Romans chapter 12 to present  our bodies as a living sacrifice unto God for this is a reasonable sacrifice. Glorification will be the final stage which provides us with an immortal body when Christ comes back.', 4, '2025-05-01 02:26:47', '2026-04-16 02:52:32', NULL),
(3377, 911, 766, 'There were two important events affirmed that Jesus was Christ. In Matthew 16:15-17 as well as Mark 8:27-30 in addition to Luke 9:18-20. Jesus asked His disciples, \"who do you say that I am?\" Peter boldly replied that \"You are the Christ, the Son of the Living God.\" Jesus blessed him and explained that this truth was not revealed by human understanding but by God the Father. Peter\'s words showed that he recognized Jesus as more than a prophet or a miracle worker. He was the promised Messiah foretold in the Old Testament. This confession was an important affirmation of the true identity for Jesus being the Christ. The second important even was the Transfiguration which was recorded in Matthew 17:1-8, Mark 9:2-8, as well as Luke 9:28-36. Here we see Jesus taking Peter, James, and John up a mountain where His face shone brightly and His clothes became white. Moses and Elijah appeared with Him, representing the Law and the Prophets. God\'s voice from heaven declared that \"This is my Son.\" This was significant because Moses and Elijah were two of Israel\'s greatest leaders, yet God showed that Jesus was greater than both of them. He fulfilled their expectations as the final Davidic heir who came to bring God\'s kingdom to earth. Both of these events, Peter\'s confession and the Transfiguration gave the disciples confirmation of the identity of Jesus.', 0, '2025-08-31 02:10:53', '2025-08-31 02:10:53', NULL),
(3378, 911, 767, 'The main purpose of the temptation that Jesus endured was to prepare him for his mission and to prove that he truly was the Son of God. You can see this in both Matthew 4:1-11 as well as Luke 4:1-13. Here we see that after fasting for forty days in the wilderness, Jesus was tempted by Satan. While Adam failed when he faced temptation in the Garden of Eden, Jesus resisted every attack that was thrown at him. This shows perfect obedience even in his weakened physical state. This goes on to prove that he was qualified to be the Savior who could represent humanity without sin. Jesus showed his strength in both spirit and mind. He refused to use his divine power for selfish purposes such as turning stone to bread and he rejected satan\'s attempts to make him test God or bow down in exchange for power. Rather, Jesus responded to each of these temptations with Scripture which shows us his complete trust in God\'s Word. His victory showed that he is not only sinless but is also committed to God\'s plan of salvation. In addition to all of this, Jesus understands the struggles we face and can sympathize with our weaknesses because he has walked in our same shoes. His victory in the wilderness gave assurance that he is both the perfect example of obedience and the merciful savior who can help us overcome temptation.', 0, '2025-08-31 02:29:34', '2025-08-31 02:29:34', NULL),
(3379, 911, 768, 'The crucifixion was already a unique form of the death penalty that was used by the Roman Empire. Even though some might view this as a Roman execution, it was more than that. It showed God\'s plan to save humanity. The crucifixion was already a cruel and shameful way to die during the Roman Empire. Victims were tied or nailed to a cross as was in the case of Jesus. They were usually left to hang until death. In most cases this was reserved as punishment for criminals but Jesus was innocent and sinless. Instead of dying for his own crimes, Jesus willingly gave his life for the sins of others. This idea is called imputation. This means that God placed the guilt of humanity\'s sin onto Jesus even though he has never sinned. From a legal perspective, God counted Jesus as if he personally committed every sin imputed to him. Jesus took the punishment that sinners deserved, making his death an atoning sacrifice. This was along the act of divine judgment as Jesus bore the full weight of God\'s wrath against sin. In doing so, not only was our sins imputed on him but his righteousness was imputed on us sinners. This showed God\'s justice in punishing sin and God\'s mercy in providing forgiveness.', 0, '2025-08-31 02:49:01', '2025-08-31 02:49:01', NULL),
(3380, 911, 769, 'The resurrection of Jesus accomplished so much for God\'s people as well as God\'s plan. According to the text, Death is the greatest tragedy humans face and the most painful result of sin in this world. The good news here is that Jesus conquered death through resurrection. By rising from he grave through the power of the Spirit, he proved to all creation that he is truly God\'s Son in addition to being the heir to his kingdom. This also guaranteed the future resurrection and blessing of all how believe in him. This also furthered God\'s plan of redemption. God\'s covenant promise was to establish his kingdom on earth under the kingship of a descendant of David. This obviously could not happen if Jesus remained dead. His resurrection was a crucial step that allowed God to fulfill these promises. Another point to make is that the resurrection brings countless blessings of salvation to believers. You can see in Romans 4:25, it results in justification and the forgiveness of sins. Because Jesus rose of the sea, those who trust in him are now justified and made right with God and given a new spiritual life. It gives hope for the future. Just as Jesus was raised with a real physical body, believers will also be raised to eternal life. The resurrection shows that God\'s plan is not to abandon the world but to redeem and restore it fully.', 0, '2025-08-31 03:11:42', '2025-08-31 03:11:42', NULL),
(3381, 911, 770, 'To be honest, this is a daily challenge in my life. Trying to balance the roles of being a father, husband, brother, son, uncle, leader, musician, etc. can be very overwhelming. The responsibilities of work, family, and the calling that God as on me is always a struggle and becomes a juggling act. Working extra hours to make sure bills are paid, trying to be a loving husband, bring present for my kids, helping my parents, taking care of my health, doing ministry at church just to name a few. It is in these redundant and exhausting moments that I come to realize it\'s not just confessing that Jesus is Christ on a Sunday but rather how I live everyday. I have come to the understanding that life will always be busy but I have to make the intentionally effort with the opportunities that are given to men to show others the love of Jesus. This comes through treating coworkers with respect even when I get angry at them, praying for scared patients at the hospital where I work, showing grace when my kids get on my last nerve, make time to listen to my wife after a long day, being intentional about family prayer. Doing this helps me see that Jesus is at the center of my life. It is of course all about Him. Don\'t get me wrong, there are moments I do fail. I sometimes get too tired to pray and spend time with God everyday, I get impatient with people, etc. but despite all that I remember that Jesus hasn\'t given up on me and that he give me the strength to do better tomorrow. Confessing Jesus in my life isn\'t about trying to be perfect but it\'s about always pointing myself and those around me to him. I want Jesus in every part of my life. I want my kids to grow up not just hearing about Jesus but rather seeing it through the way I live my life. I want others to see despite what all might happen or what I may be going through in my circumstances, Jesus shines through it all.', 0, '2025-08-31 03:30:29', '2025-08-31 03:30:29', NULL),
(3382, 912, 866, 'The Apostle\'s Creed didn\'t appear all at once but rather is slowly developed over time during the first few centuries of the Christian Church. During the early years of Christianity, short summaries were used called the rules of faith. What these statements did was help protect the truth of the gospel, train leaders and also gave ordinary believers a clear way to understand their faith. You have to consider back then most people could not read and the Bible was not widely available so these summaries were very helpful to them. Over time these turned into creeds. These were short easy to remember statements of beliefs. Creeds were often recited during worship services and baptisms so new believers could clearly express their faith. The Old Roman Creed is one of the earliest examples of this. It closely resembled what is known as the Apostle\'s Creed. At first different churches had slightly different versions of the creed but around A.D. 700, it became standardized into the form we recognize today. This shows us how the early church wanted a unifying statement that could be used everywhere. It also provides a simple way to teach new Christians, help the church stay focused on biblical truth as well as offer protection against false teachings that were common at the time and are still relevant to this day.', 7, '2025-09-11 01:45:25', '2026-04-16 02:52:00', NULL),
(3383, 912, 867, 'The church believed it was important to create and use a creed for many reasons. One of the first reasons was it gave Christians a clear summery of what they believed. During the early centuries, most people were not educated to read and write and very few people had access to the Bible. These short simple statements like the Apostles\' Creed made it possible for everyone to learn the essentials of the Christian faith particularly for new believers as well as those with little to no education. Secondly the creed was also important because of the commonality of false teachings during that time which in fact is still relevant to this day. Groups such as the Gnostics taught that the physical world was evil and denied that Jesus had truly come in the flesh. There were also others like the Docetists that claimed that Jesus only appeared to suffer and die but was not really human. By teaching the creed, churches during that time were able to give Christians a way to guard themselves against these false teachings. Thirdly the creed also played a major role in baptism and discipleship. Whenever new believers were baptized they were asked to confess their faith. The creed played a role here and gave them the words to express their faith in the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit as well their belief in forgiveness, resurrection, and eternal life. This was a confession and proof that they understood at least the basics of the Gospel. Lastly the creed promoted unity among the churches. Christians from different places as well as from different times may have worshiped in different ways. However by sharing the same confession they show that they belonged to the same family of faith in Jesus worldwide.', 9, '2025-09-11 01:59:27', '2026-04-16 02:52:00', NULL),
(3384, 912, 868, 'The Apostles\' Creed teaches that God is the Trinity. This means that there is one God in three persons who are the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. To make more sense of this you need to understand the persons and nature. A person is a distinct self aware individual. The Father, Son, and Spirit are not the same person but rather they are related to each other and do different things. Nature means God\'s essence or being. All three persons share the same divine nature. What this means is that Christians believe in one God not three separate gods. Going into more detail, the creed shows the different works of each person. The Father for example is almighty and the maker of heaven and earth. His role is creation and ruling over everything. The Son, who is Jesus, was conceived by the Holy Spirit born of the Virgin Mary. Jesus suffered under Pontius Pilate, then died and buried and then rose again on the third day. He is ascended to heave and rules at the right hand of the Father. Jesus will return to judge the world. His name work is salvation which was achieved through His death and resurrection. Lastly the Holy Spirit gives life and works in the church. The creed mentions Him in connection with the Holy Catholic Church, the forgiveness of sins, and life everlasting. The main role here includes creating faith, uniting believers, and applying Christ\'s salvation to us. In short the Trinity means one God, three persons who is working together in creation, salvation, and in the church.', 9, '2025-09-11 02:13:27', '2026-04-16 02:52:00', NULL),
(3385, 912, 869, 'It is important to participate in the church because Christianity was not ment to be practiced alone. You can see this from the start that believers came together to worship, learn, and care for one another. The Bible also warns us not to give up meeting together. This is because being with other Christians helps us grow in faith and stay strong. An example of this could be were a person might hear a sermon that helps them understand God\'s Word more clearly or they may be encouraged when others pray for them during a hard time. Just like a basketball team or a football team needs every player, the church needs every member to use their gifts to serve and build up the whole body. The Apostles\'s Creed says that we believe in the Holy Catholic Church and the communion of saints. This should tell us that the church is much bigger than one congregation. Rather, it is all believers everywhere and throughout the history of the world. Whenever we gather with the church, we are going something much larger than ourselves. It is also equally important to preserve the church\'s central doctrines. From the earliest centuries there have been many false teachers who have tried to distort the Gospel by denying truth such as the humanity of Christ, His resurrection, or the forgiveness of sins. This happens even today. Surprisingly even at Calvary Assembly were members of the Shincheonju Church of Jesus came. Reciting and teaching the Apostles\' Creed helped the church protect these essential beliefs. Without guarding these truths the church in general could easily drift into false teachings and loose the truth of the Christian message. Participation and preservation ensures that the church remains a faithful community of believers. Gathering in worship and guarding core doctrines is the only way the church can continue its mission to proclaim the Gospel and pass it on for future generations.', 9, '2025-09-11 02:30:39', '2026-04-16 02:52:00', NULL),
(3386, 912, 870, 'The Apostles\' Creed is foundational to Christian theology because it summarizes the most basic and essential truths of the Christian faith. For example, just as you need a solid foundation for a building to stand, Christian beliefs also need a solid starting point. The Creed gives the bases that is needed by setting out who is God, what Christ as done for us, the work of the Holy Spirit, the importance of the church, and the hope of forgiveness, resurrection, and eternal life. One of these ways that the Creed is foundational is that it acts as a standard for testing all other beliefs. If someone claims that Jesus was only a good teacher but not truly God for example, the Creed reminds us of the truth which is Jesus Christ is God\'s only Son, our Lord. In the same way if a teaching denies the resurrection, the Creeds protects us by affirming the resurrection of the body. This will help keep the true followers of Christ from being misled by convincing false ideas. The Creed is also foundational because it provides the logical basis for other Christian teachings. For example, if God is the maker of heaven and earth then he also has authority over creation and we can trust His power in our lives. If Christ rose from the dead then we can be confident that He too will raise us as well. These are some of the the basic truths which support everything Christians believe. The Apostles\' Creed is foundational because it sets boundaries of authentic Christianity which anchors us to Scripture and also provides the core truths on which all other theology is built.', 8, '2025-09-11 02:42:42', '2026-04-16 02:52:00', NULL),
(3387, 913, 871, 'Christians says that the God of the Bible is the one and true God because evidence of this is found all throughout the scriptures. The Bible repeatedly teaches that there are no other gods. Many other religions call their deities gods but Christians believe that these are imaginary beings or idols that were made up. In some cases they could also be evil beings or spiritual forces. The Bible explains that from the beginning of creation you can see where God has made evidence showing his existence clear for all the world to see. You too can see this evidence of His power and divine nature in creation itself. The sad fact of the matter is that humans have fallen into sin and because of this they refuse to honor God and choose to instead worship false gods or other created beings. The belief that Christians have is the belief in one God which is called monotheism. Similar to and extent to Judaism and Islam which also believe in monotheism. However, there is something different with Christianity that both Judaism and Islam rejects. Christians believe that this one God exists in three persons which are the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. These three persons are not separate gods but share the same divine essence. This God is what Christians believe to have the power to create everything from nothing. Also having the power to ultimately rule over all and save people from their sin. This is clear in the Apostles\' Creed when it states that \"I believe in God the Father Almighty, Maker of heaven and earth.\"', 8, '2025-09-18 01:20:06', '2026-04-16 02:51:29', NULL),
(3388, 913, 872, 'When you use the term simplicity it is easy to fall into the narrative of sounding like it means easy to understand. The truth of the matter is that that is not the case at all. Instead it describes God\'s essence which is completely unified and not made up of different parts. God is not a mixture of pieces that you put together like love, power, and justice but rather he is one perfect and complete being. All of His qualities are fully present in everything that He does. An example of this that was explained to us to help us understand is the example of a glass of pure water. Pure water is completely water and nothing else. You can by all means add dirt and then it becomes mud. This is then a mixture of two different substances. However, God\'s essence is not like this at all, His essence is like the pure water which is entirely one and undivided. This is very important to understand especially when Christians believe in one God even though God the Father, Son and Holy Spirit exists. The three persons are not separate gods joined together but rather they share the same exact divine essence. This is what it means to Christians believing in one God while also affirming the Trinity. God\'s simplicity means that He is unchanging and perfectly consistent. All his attributes work together in perfect harmony. For us believers this means that God can always be trusted. He will always act according to His good and perfect nature.', 8, '2025-09-18 01:33:58', '2026-04-16 02:51:29', NULL),
(3389, 913, 873, 'Both of these terms, \"ontological Trinity\" and \"economic Trinity\" are used in a way for Christians to describe the relationship between God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit. The ontological Trinity primarily focuses on who God is in His being. The word ontological means related to being. This term focuses on God\'s essence showing that all three persons of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, share the same divine nature. Each person is fully God as well as equal in all power and glory as well as all divine attributes. This helps us remember that there has never been a time when one person has existed without the other. The economic Trinity on the other hand focuses on what God does. In other words, it helps us understand how the three persons relate to each other. This view describes the roles each person has. An example of this is seen when the Father sends the Son into the world. The Son obeys the Father and accomplishes salvation. When then the Holy Spirit applies that salvation to believers. The Father has authority over the Son and the Spirit but is not because He is more powerful or more God like than they are. It is because of their roles to one another. These two views are similar because they both describe the same God and affirm that the Father, Son and Spirit are one. However both are different in respects to the ontological Trinity explains God\'s being while the economic Trinity explains his actions and relationships.', 8, '2025-09-18 01:46:31', '2026-04-16 02:51:29', NULL),
(3390, 913, 874, 'The scriptures show that God is our Father in three different but important ways which are as a creator, as a king, and as the head of His family. First let\'s look at how God is a father because He is the creator. According to the Word, God is the creator of everything. Just as a human father gives life to his children, God gave life to the world and to everyone in it. The Word says that from one man God made al nations and gave them places to live. This means that everyone is God\'s child because we come from Him. Like a good father God cares about what he has created and shows patience and mercy towards people even when they turn away from him. Secondly let\'s look at God the father as the King. In the ancient times, the kings were called the fathers of their people because they were supposed to protect and provide for them. God is the perfect royal Father because He rules from heaven defending His people and defeating their enemies. Jesus also taught His disciples to pray to \"Our Father in heaven\" which also serves as a reminder to the fatherhood of God as the King. Last but not least, God as the head of His family. The Bible calls us believers His children and describes the church as His household. Similar to loving parents, God guides us, provides for us, and disciplines us so that we may continue to grow in faith and become who He wants us to be for His glory and purpose.', 8, '2025-09-18 01:58:40', '2026-04-16 02:51:29', NULL),
(3391, 913, 875, 'God\'s authority over creation as absolute, exclusive, and exhaustive help explain just how complete His rule is. First let\'s discuss how God\'s authority is absolute. This means that there is no limits to His power or control. He has made everything by His words including everything in heaven and on earth. Because of this, everything belongs to Him. Nothing can stop God from doing what He has planned. This was very clear in the scriptures. You can see many examples of where He clam the storms, raise the dead, and even changed the hearts of kings. God authority is not shared with anyone and no one can overrule Him. Second we see that God\'s authority is exclusive. This means that He has the right to rule creation. There is no one that can claim to be the Lord of the universe. There are many other false gods, spirits, and idols but they have no authority. Most if not all of these so called gods are still under God\'s power. Last but not least you see that God\'s authority is exhaustive. This means that it covers every part of creation. There is no place, no situation, no moment in time that is outside of His control. From the largest galaxy to the smallest detail of our life we can have assurance that God is in charge of it all. Even if things happen to us that may feel as if they are random or accidental, they are all under God\'s sovereign will. With all of these truths in minds, we can be at peace knowing that everything is in God\'s hands. This should also give us the confidence that his plans will always come to pass.', 8, '2025-09-18 02:12:08', '2026-04-16 02:51:29', NULL),
(3392, 914, 876, 'Whenever the Bible uses the term or call Jesus the \"Son of God\" it means that Jesus has a very special and very unique relationship with God. You can see in different parts of the Bible when the words \"Son of God\" is used, it also refers to angels, kings, or even believers. For these examples, it just means that they belong to God or that they were chosen for something special or important. However, when the title is used for Jesus it\'s different. It means so much more. Jesus is not just close to God but rather He is actually God\'s eternal Son. He has always existed with God the Father and the Holy Spirit. That makes Jesus equal with God. He has the same power, wisdom, love, and holiness as God the Father. The scriptures goes further and shows us this in two distinct ways. The first is that Jesus is fully God, the same as the Father and the Spirit. Secondly, it explains that Jesus took on a role where He chose to obey the father to save the world. Don\'t misunderstand this by thinking that it makes Jesus less than God, on the contrary it shows the Father, Son, and Spirit work together but have different roles.', 8, '2025-09-27 01:44:01', '2026-04-16 02:51:00', NULL),
(3393, 914, 877, 'Whenever the Bible calls Jesus \"Lord\" it is showing that He is truly God. In the Greek language, the word \"Kurios\" is used in reference to \"Lord.\" This means master, ruler, or someone with authority. However, when you look at the Old Testament whenever the Jewish scholars translated God\'s name \"Yahweh\" into Greek, they used the same word \"Kurios.\" So what this tells us is that when the New Testament calls Jesus \"Lord\" it is not just for the sake of saying that he is a respected teacher or even a leader but rather that He is equal to God. Calling Jesus \"Lord\" shows that He has authority over all people and all creation. It means that He rules as King and is worthy of worship like God the Father. We pray to Jesus and worship Him because we believe that He is divine. If Jesus was only a man then it would be wrong to worship Him, but because He is God, this makes it right. The title of \"Lord\" also shows that Jesus is the one who saves His people. You can see in the Old Testament that God is the one who redeems His people from sin as well as from slavery. In the New Testament you can see that Jesus takes on this role. By calling Him \"Lord\" the writers of the Bible makes it clear that Jesus is the same God who has the power to forgive sins as well as bring salvation.', 8, '2025-09-27 01:57:08', '2026-04-16 02:51:00', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3394, 914, 878, 'This life of Jesus shows many clear signs of how He was truly human. This was not the case of God just appearing in disguise. Let\'s first look at His birth. Yes He was conceived by the Holy Spirit but He was born as a baby by the Virgin Mary.  Even though His birth was a miracle, He still grew inside Mary\'s womb just like every other baby. He did not just magically appear but rather he went through the normal process of being born. Secondly, Jesus had a real human body. He grew up from that baby to an adult. He ate food, got tired, and even felt pain. He could bleed, suffer, and eventually die. All of which are the same things that normal humans go through. The Apostles Creed even mentions that He was crucified, died and was buried. All of these experiences are only possible if He has a real physical body. Next you can see that Jesus had a human soul. Just like everyone else, Jesus had emotions, thoughts, and a spirit. While Jesus was on the cross He prayed to God the father and gave up His spirit. this shows that His inner life was truly human as well. Last looking at his resurrection, this proves His humanity. When He rose from the dead, it just wasn\'t a spirit that was floating away but rather it was His real human body that was raised and reunited with His soul. You can even see where he showed his disciples his wounds and even at food with them after rising from the dead. This proves that He was a real man. All of these experiences, the birth, body, soul, suffering, death, and resurrection, shows that Jesus was fully human.', 8, '2025-09-27 02:09:29', '2026-04-16 02:51:00', NULL),
(3395, 914, 879, 'Often times there are many who misunderstand the word \"Christ\" and think that it is the last name of Jesus. This is not the case. The word \"Christ\" is a title. In the Old Testament, the Hebrew word Messiah and the Greek word Christos both mean anointed one. This title was given to people who were chosen by God for a special job. You can see throughout scriptures that kings, priests and prophets were sometimes anointed with oil to show that God had set them apart to lead, serve, and guide His people. The Old Testament also promised a future great king who is a descendant of King David. This king would be the \"Anointed One\" or the Messiah. This messiah would rule with justice, save God\'s people and bring peace to the world. Israel waited and hoped for this Messiah for hundreds of years. Jesus fulfills this office in every way. He is the true Christ and the Messiah that the Old Testament points to. As a descendant of David, Jesus is the rightful King. As a priest, Jesus offered Himself as the perfect sacrifice to pay for our sins and still represents us before God. As a prophet, Jesus revealed God\'s truth very clearly by teaching people about God\'s Kingdom and how to live in it. Jesus fulfilled all of these roles, prophet, priest, and king. This shows us that Jesus is the Christ. He is not just another leader or another teacher but rather He is the one that God has promised to send, the Messiah.', 8, '2025-09-27 02:22:41', '2026-04-16 02:51:00', NULL),
(3396, 914, 880, 'Jesus is unique because he has two natures. You see that he is fully human and he is fully divine at the same time. This idea is called hypostatic union. This means that in one person Jesus has both a human nature and a divine nature. The human nature of Jesus was just like us in many ways. He had a real body that had the same types of experiences that we do as well. For example he could get hungry, tired, and feel pain. He had a mind that could learn and grow and he also experienced emotions like job, sadness, and compassion. He suffered and died as well. All of which humans can do. This shows us that Jesus was truly one of us and that He shared in our struggles and because of this He is able to relate and understand what we are going through. Now at the same time He is also divine in nature. This means that He is God. He has all the attributes of God. He is eternal, all powerful, holy, and perfect. Because He had all of these attributes, he could do things that no other person was able to do. Examples of this are found throughout scripture where He preformed miracles, forgave sins, and rose from the dead. What is important is that both of these natures are not mixed together. Jesus is not half of a God or half of a man but rather he is fully God and fully man at the same time. His human nature and His divine nature remain distinct and are united perfectly in one person. This means that Jesus can bridge the gap between God and humanity. As God, Jesus has the power to save us at the same time, as man He can represent us before God.', 8, '2025-09-27 02:39:32', '2026-04-16 02:51:00', NULL),
(3397, 915, 881, 'The Apostles\' Creed affirms the Holy Spirit\'s divinity in an indirect manner. You can see in the Apostles\' Creed where it says, \"I believe in the Holy Spirit.\" Even though it may appear brief it has a very powerful meaning. The Apostles Creed is written in three main sections. It covers God the Father, Jesus the Son, and the Holy Spirit. This Trinitatian structure shows that the Holy Spirit is placed on the same level as the Father and the Son. By doing so, the Apostles\' Creed treats the Holy Spirit as fully divine and not as a lesser being or anything of that nature. The Creed also mentions that Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit. This can be found in the Gospel specifically in Luke where the angel tells Mary the power of the Most High will come upon her. That power mentioned is God\'s alone. When the Holy Spirit performs this miracle, it shows that He shares God\'s divine power and nature. The Bible itself also calls the Spirit by divine names like \"Spirit of God\" and \"Spirit of Christ.\" You can see that this credits the Holy Spirit with divine works such as creating the world, inspiring Scripture and renewing believers\' hearts. The Spirit\'s divinity is not only shown in two main ways by first its Trinitarian structure which places Him equally with the Father and Son and by describing His role in conception of Jesus, an act only God could perform. Together these truths affirm that the Holy Spirit is not just a helper or some kind of influence but rather is truly God Himself.', 8, '2025-10-05 02:15:47', '2026-04-16 02:50:23', NULL),
(3398, 915, 882, 'The Apostles\' Creed teaches us that the Holy Spirit is fully God. This belief comes directly from the Bible. You see the Apostolic Creed by itself is not meant to replace the Word of God but rather what it does is summarizes what the Bible already says about the Holy Spirit\'s divine nature. The Word shows the Spirit\'s divinity through His names, characteristics, and actions. You can see throughout Scripture the different names of the Holy Spirit. The Bible reveals who He is. He is called the Spirit of God, the Spirit of the Lord, the Spirit of Christ, and the Spirit of the Living God. These titles show that the spirit is not separate from God but one with Him. You can also see an example in the Book of Acts where Peter was telling Ananias that lying the Holy Spirit is the same as lying to God. This makes it very clear that the Holy Spirit is God. Next the Bible gives the Holy Spirit divine qualities that only God can have. You see throughout Scripture where it mentions that He is eternal, meaning He has no beginning or end. He is all knowing meaning he understands even the deepest thoughts of God. He is also all powerful and present everywhere. The Holy Spirit\'s work also reveal His divinity. He took part in the creation of the world, giving life to all living things, inspiring the writers of the Bible, and changing people\'s hearts by bringing them to faith in Jesus. These are examples of thing that God can only do.', 8, '2025-10-05 02:30:51', '2026-04-16 02:50:23', NULL),
(3399, 915, 883, 'The Bible shows us clearly that the Holy Spirit is not just a spiritual force or some kind of an energy but rather a real person with a mind, a personal will, and real emotions. He speaks, as well as teaches and guides and shows love. This shows that He has personal qualities that only a living being can have. These traits reveal that the Holy Spirit is a divine person and not just a power or symbol of God\'s presence. An example of this can be found in 1 Corinthians 2:10-11 where the Word says the Spirit \"searches all things, even the deep things of God.\" This helps us see that the Holy Spirit has intelligence and understanding. This means that He knows and understands the thoughts of God perfectly which no created being could ever do. He also teaches believers spiritual truth reminding them of what Jesus said. This shows actions of a thinking person where the Spirit has knowledge and communicates it to others. You can also see where the Spirit also has a will. In 1 Corinthians 12:11 it goes on to say that the Spirit gives different spiritual gifts to believers \"just as He wills.\" This means that the Holy Spirit makes decisions based on the purpose and wisdom He has which goes on to prove that He acts intentionally. Another example you can find is in Ephesians 4:30 where the Holy Spirit is showing emotions. Here we can see where Christians are warned not to \"grieve the Holy Spirit.\" This shows that the Holy Spirit can feel sorrow when the people sin or reject God\'s ways. On the other hand you can see in Romans 15:30 where Paul speaks of the \"love of the Spirit.\" This shows that the Spirit feels and expresses love towards believers. Lastly were can see where the Spirit takes personal actions as He guides people, intercedes for them in prayer, speaks to believers, and calls people to serve. Again these actions are that only a person can accomplish and not that of some being of energy or power.', 9, '2025-10-05 02:47:49', '2026-04-16 02:50:23', NULL),
(3400, 915, 884, 'The Bible teaches that God is one in essence but exists in three persons, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. These three persons are equal in power, glory, and divinity. Even though they are equal they are distinct from one another in their roles and in their relationships. The Holy Spirit is fully God just like the Father and the Son but He is also a unique person within the Trinity. You can see specifically that the Holy Spirit is distinct in His identity. As stated clearly in John 14:16-17, the Father is not the Son, the Son is not the Spirit, and the Spirit is not the Father. Jesus said that the Father would send another Helper. He was referring to the Holy Spirit as the another person. The Spirit is the one who will continue the work of Jesus among His believers. Next you can see that the Spirit is distinct in His roles and in His functions. You can see in the Scripture that each person of the Trinity carries out specific works. The Father plans and sends while the Son accomplishes redemption through His life, death and resurrection. The Holy Spirit now has a role that applies that salvation to the believers by giving them faith, transforming their hearts, and guiding them in truth. In addition to this the Spirit intercedes for believers by speaking to God the Father on their behalf. This clearly shows the interaction between distinct persons. Lastly we can see that the Spirit is distinct in relationship. In the \"Economic Trinity\" where it describes how the persons of God work tother, the Spirit carries out the will of the Father and of the Son however in the \"Ontological Trinity\" all three are equal in being and in glory.', 9, '2025-10-05 03:02:17', '2026-04-16 02:50:23', NULL),
(3401, 915, 885, 'The relationship of the Holy Spirit to that of the Father and the Son is shown in unity and cooperation within the Trinity. The Trinity means that God exists as one divine being in three equal and eternal persons. These persons are as the Father the Son, and the Holy Spirit. Each of these persons is fully God while still sharing the same divine nature but yet they are still distinct in their roles and relationships. Looking into their relationships, you can see that the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit are completely equal in power and in glory but they relate in different ways. Starting with the Father, He is often seen as the source or the origin. He sends the Son into the world and later sends the Spirit. The Son in turn willingly obeys the Father and accomplishes the work of salvation through His life, death, and His resurrection, The Holy Spirit\'s role here is to carry out and apply the will of the Father and the work of the Son. The Spirit brings God\'s presence to believers and gives them a new spiritual life and even guides them in the truth. You can see in John 15:26 where Jesus describes this relationship where \"When the Helper comes, whom I will send to you from the Father, the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father, He will testify about Me.\" This shows us that the Spirit comes from both the Father as well as the Son. Not only that but it also shows that the Spirit works to glorify them as well. You can also see in Romans 8:9-11 where it explains that the Spirit of God and the Spirit of Christ dwells in believers. This shows the unity and the harmony that is between all three persons. Even though the Holy Spirit is called the third person of the Trinity, it does not mean that He is any less important. Rather on the other hand it shows the traditional order in how God\'s work is revealed. The Father is usually mentioned first as the sender, next is the Son as the Savior, and then comes the Holy Spirit as the one who applies God\'s power and presence to believers. The relationship here to the Father and the Son is that of quarry divinity and shared purpose with a distinct role.', 10, '2025-10-05 03:19:20', '2026-04-16 02:50:23', NULL),
(3402, 916, 886, 'The church in the New Testament grew out of God\'s people in the Old Testament. It was not something new that was created. God had chosen Israel to be His special people. They worshiped Him, as well as followed His commands, and lived as an example to other nations. They were called an assembly which is similar to the word church. This shows us that God has always had a group of people set apart for His purpose. When Jesus came into the world, He didn\'t start a completely different group of people but rather He continued and completed what God had already begun with Israel. Jesus spoke about building His church. What he ment by this is that He would restore and strengthen God\'s people. You can see that the apostles also showed this connection as well. An example of this is where they used Old Testament titles like holy nation and royal priesthood to describe Christians. The purpose also were the same. You can see in the Old Testament that Israel was called to love God as well as obey him and to be a light to the world. Throughout the New Testament the church can be seen receiving the same calling by preaching the gospel, living holy lives, and the mission to spread God\'s Kingdom. Paul goes on to explain that the believers from all the nations are like branches added to Israel\'s tree. This is showing that the church shares the same roots as well as the same promises. In this manner, the New Testament church is not a. separate story but rather the next chapter.', 10, '2025-10-19 21:19:59', '2026-04-16 02:49:29', NULL),
(3403, 916, 887, 'Jesus built His church by continuing what God started in the Old Testament. He didn\'t stop there but rather He went on to bring it to its full purpose. In the Old Testament God formed a covenant people who were Israel. They were chosen and set apart to worship Him and represent His kingdom on the earth. They had leaders, laws, worship, and a mission. However, over time, you can see throughout scripture that they failed to live faithfully. Jesus didn\'t come to destroy this foundation but rather He came to complete it and restore it. First you can see where Jesus relied on the Old Testament church by honoring its teachings, traditions, and scriptures. He worshiped in the synagogues and taught from the law and from the prophets. He went on to affirm God\'s covenant with Israel. He also choose 12 disciples which symbolizing the 12 tribes of Israel. He not only did this but he also advanced the church beyond its Old Testament form. How was this possible? He fulfilled the prophecies about the Messiah and offered salvation not just to the Jews but also to all nations. Even though Israel was just one nation, Jesus opened the covenant to everyone who believed in Him. He gave new authority to the church saying that I will build my church and the powers of death could not defeat it. He went even further by sending the Holy Spirit. Unlike the Old Testament where the Spirit only came to certain leaders, here in the New Testament the Spirit was for all believers which empowered them to spread the gospel across the world.', 10, '2025-10-19 21:34:52', '2026-04-16 02:49:29', NULL),
(3404, 916, 888, 'The relationship between the Old Testament and the New Testament church carries several important implications. First you can see that it shows that God has one continuous plan for His people. The church as not some last minute ideas that began in the New Testament but rather a continuation of God\'s covenant that started with Israel in the Old Testament. What this basically shows us is that Christians are a part of the story that began with Abraham, Moses, and the prophets. Secondly we can see where this continuity reminds us that the church has a clear purpose, which is to spread God\'s kingdom. In the Old Testament, Israel was called to be a light to the nations, in addition to that also showing God\'s truth through obedience and worship. You can see similarly in the New Testament where the church carries the same mission by preaching the gospel and to living out the love of Christ. Thirdly you can see that both the Old and New Testament communities have a mix of believers and unbelievers. Just as Israel has faithful people as well as people who decided to turn away from God, you see the in today\'s church as well. There are plenty of people who may appear religious but lack true faith. This is important because it shows us that we must continue to preach the gospel within the church and to examine our hearts. Lastly you can see Israel\'s ongoing obligation and commitment to love God, obey His laws, and glorify Him. The New Testament church has these same responsibilities.', 10, '2025-10-19 21:50:38', '2026-04-16 02:49:29', NULL),
(3405, 916, 889, 'You can see in the Apostles Creed that is uses the word catholic. You may think it refers to the Roman Catholic Church but it does not. It comes from the Greek word which means \"universal.\" When Christians refer to using this word by saying that they believe in the \"Holy Catholic Church,\" what they mean is that they believe in the universal church made up of all true believers in Jesus across every place and time. In the earliest centuries of Christianity, churches were spread across different regions, cultures, as well as many different languages. They did not all look the same or even share the same leadership but they were united by one faith. The word Catholic was used to remind believers that the church is bigger than any single local congregation or denomination. It includes all who choose to follow Jesus sincerely. No matter where you are whether it be a large city, small village or even in secret. This idea is inclusive not exclusive. This means that any church holding to the core truths of the Christian faith is part of the one true church. Churches may have different traditions or different customs but they are still united together with Christ as the Head.', 8, '2025-10-19 22:02:53', '2026-04-16 02:49:29', NULL),
(3406, 916, 890, 'The visible Catholic Church refers to all the congregations around the world that outwardly professes faith in Jesus and gather together in His name. It is called visible because it can be seen through the gatherings, worship, teaching, sacraments, as well as the church life. This will of course include all the different denominations and traditions as long as they acknowledge Christ as Lord and follow the core teachings of Christianity. There are three key signs that Christians have traditionally looked for to decide whether or not the church should be embraced as part of the universal church. This is called the marks of the true church. The first mark is faithful preaching of God\'s word. A true church teaches the Bible accurately especially the message of salvation through Jesus Christ. It does not deny essential truths like the Trinity, the divinity of Jesus, or the resurrection. The second mark is the right administration of the sacraments. A true church practices baptism as well as the Lord\'s Supper in a biblical way. The third mark is the practice of church discipline. This is where the true church takes holiness seriously. It corrects serious sin and encourages repentance. It also seeks to protect the community from false teaching and harmful behavior. If the church in question holds to these core practices and beliefs. it should be recognized as part of the wider body of Church even if it differs in worship style, culture, or secondary doctrines. There are two extremes that are present that believers must recognize to avoid. The first is accepting every group that simply calls themselves as Christians and secondly rejecting all church except our own.', 8, '2025-10-19 22:17:22', '2026-04-16 02:49:29', NULL),
(3407, 917, 891, 'The problem with sin is that it separates us from God and it brings His judgement on all of humanity. Sin isn\'t just doing something bad but rather it is disobeying God and breaking His law. You can find this in the scriptures as \"lawlessness\" meaning that it goes against what God says is right. Whenever we sin, we choose our own way instead of God\'s way. What this does is break our relationship with God because He is holy and perfect. Because of this, He is not able to just ignore sin. Sin is the reason that people feel guilt, shame, and spiritual emptiness. Essentially it is the case of all the brokenness in the world. Sin originated from the Fall. This is were Adam and Eve disobeyed God int he Garden of Eden. God told them that they could eat from any tree expect one but they instead decided to listen to the serpent\'s temptation and ate the fruit anyway. By doing this they chose to rebel against God. Because of this act of disobedience, it brought sin into the whole human race. From that point on, every person has been born with a sinful nature. The consequences that is associated with sin is essentially death. Spiritual death is being separated from God. In addition to this, it also brings corruption which affects both our hearts and our bodies. This in return leads to pain, sickness, and selfishness. Because of sin, physical death became a part of life and people also face the danger of eternal separation from God after death. Sin just ruined the good relationship people once had with God and it affects every part of life. Because of this is why we all need God\'s grace and forgiveness through Jesus.', 10, '2025-10-25 01:16:07', '2026-04-16 02:47:45', NULL),
(3408, 917, 892, 'To begin, divine grade is the free gift of forgiveness and salvation which involves all three persons of the Trinity. The Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit though all have different roles, they all work together in unity to show God\'s love as well as His mercy and desire to save humanity from sin. Starting with God the Father, you can see that He is the source of divine grace. The Father is the one who planned salvation from the beginning. Out of His deep love for us, He chose to send His Son, Jesus to redeem the world. The Father didn\'t act out of anger but rather out of compassion. He wanted to restore the broken relationship between Himself and His creation. His plan shows that grace starts with God\'s initiative and not any of our efforts. We cannot ever earn forgiveness. This is the reason why God the Father provided a way through His Son Jesus. God the Son, Jesus Christ carried out that plan. Jesus stepped into this world as both fully God and fully man to accomplish what was never done. He lived a perfect life without sin and died as a sacrifice for the sins of humanity. He took it upon himself to endure the punishment that we deserved and made forgiveness possible for everyone who believes in Him. Through His resurrection, Jesus conquered death and opened the door to eternal life. He was not done quite yet. God sends the Holy Spirit next. The Holy Spirit helps people understand the truth about God, feel genuine sorrow for sin, and believe in Jesus. After one accepts Jesus, the Holy Spirit continues to live in them and helps them by guiding, teaching, and strengthening their faith.', 10, '2025-10-25 01:32:39', '2026-04-16 02:47:45', NULL),
(3409, 917, 893, 'Individual responsibilities plays a very important role in the forgiveness of sins. Even though forgiveness is a free gift from God, it requires a personal response from each person. God will offer His grace to everyone but it is up to us to choose to respond with faith, repentance, and a humble heart. The scripture teaches us two main conditions that are needed for forgiveness which are faith and brokenness. Faith means believing and trusting in God as well as his power, trusting in His mercy, as well as submitting to His will. It is about truly depending on Him and believing that Jesus died to save us. Brokenness means that being truly sorry for our sins. Don\'t get it mistaken, it\'s not about just feeling bad because we got caught but rather because we know we have disobeyed God. It\'s about realizing that sin hurts our relationship with God and us wanting to change. Whenever we admit our wrongs and come to God with a sincere heart, He is ready to forgive us. God has also provided ways for us to receive forgiveness. Some of the main ways is through prayer, faith, and the sacraments. Prayer is how we confess our sins and ask for forgiveness. Whenever we pray honestly and with faith, God cleanses our hearts and restores our relationship with Him. There are also times where prayers that are made on behalf of others, also called intercessory prayers, can also bring forgiveness and healing. Lastly the sacraments such as baptism and the Lord\'s Supper reminds us of what Jesus did for us and it also helps us experience His grace again. Forgiveness depends on God\'s mercy but each one of us have the responsibility to respond. Through faith, repentance, and prayer, we open our hearts to receive the forgiveness that God has freely given through the price that was paid by Jesus.', 10, '2025-10-25 01:46:19', '2026-04-16 02:47:45', NULL),
(3410, 917, 894, 'When humanity fell into sin it affected every part of our lives not just our souls but also our bodies. Before sin came into the picture, God created humans perfect and good. You can see in scriptures where Adam and Eve lived in harmony with God, with each other and with the rest of creation. They were essentially free from death, pain, and suffering. However that all changed when they disobeyed God int he Garden of Eden. Their sin not only broke their spiritual connections with God but also brought physical corruption and death into the world. The fall into sin first corrupted our souls. We are all spiritually dead, which means that we are separated from God. Our thoughts, desires, and emotions are no longer pure. People began to struggle with selfishness, pride, fear, and guilt. The image of God that was in us was damaged. Instead of naturally desiring to obey God, we because drawn to disobedience and rebellion. This spiritual corruption is what the scriptures call the sinful nature. It didn\'t stop there, sin when on to affect our bodies as well. When Adam and Eve sinned, God\'s curse included pain, sickness, and eventually death. The body which was once perfect and free from decay, began to age, weaken, and eventually die. Disease, suffering, and physical imperfections all came into the procure because of sin\'s entrance into the world. Even nature became broken and out of balance.', 9, '2025-10-25 01:58:20', '2026-04-16 02:47:45', NULL),
(3411, 917, 895, 'Bodily redemption is part of God\'s plan to heal and restore humanity. It does not just apply to the spiritual side of things but to the physical as well. When sin entered the world, both our souls as well as our bodies because corrupted. This brought in pain, sickness, and death. However through Jesus, God promised full redemption. This will occur in three main stages, renewal in this life, peace after death, and complete restoration at the resurrection. The first stage of the bodily redemption because in this life when a person puts their faith in Jesus. When this occurs, the Holy Spirit comes to live within them and brings new spiritual life. Even though our physical bodies still experience weakness, aging, and sickness here we see God slowly starting to change our hearts. The Spirit helps us resist sin and gives us strength to follow God\'s will. At this point, our physical suffering may not go but it helps bring hope that our pain and struggles are only temporary. It helps us start a lifelong transformation that prepares us for eternity. The second stage happens after death. When a believer dies their body remains in the grave but their soul goes to be with God. In this state, the soul is freed from sin, pain, and fear. Believers experience comfort and joy in God\'s presence while waiting for the day when their bodies will be raised again.The last stage will happen when Jesus returns. This is when God will raise the dead and give believers new glorified bodies. These bodies will never grow old, get sick, or die. They will be perfect and eternal in addition to completely being free from the effects of sin.', 9, '2025-10-25 02:15:08', '2026-04-16 02:47:45', NULL),
(3412, 918, 826, 'Christian ethics focuses on mainly and solely on God and His blessings by focusing on all moral understanding in who God is and what He has done for humanity. We tend to base things on right and wrong on human reasonings and cultural standards but rather Christian ethics begins with the belief that God is the ultimate source of truth, goodness, and authority. This means that ethical behavior is not just about following a set of rules but rather its about living in a way that would reflect God\'s character. Since God is holy, loving, and just we are called to act in ways that would mirror those same qualities in our lives. The blessings of God also play a central role as well. The Christian life is not motivated by the fear of being punished but rather it is full of gratitude for God\'s grace and the blessings that He provides. These blessings include salvation through Jesus, the guidance of the Holy Spirit and the promise of eternal life. Because of all of this, we as Christians are inspired to live ethically. We are not here to earn God favor but rather live in a way that shows thankfulness for His generosity. When you look at it this way, through your obedience it becomes an act of worship and moral living. We start to understand that God\'s moral laws is designed for human flourishing. God\'s commands are not burdensome restrictions but rather it is a blessing that is meant to bring peace, justice, and harmony. When we decide to follow His will, we start to experience the fullness of life that comes from living according to divine wisdom.', 9, '2025-11-02 01:16:25', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3413, 918, 827, 'A proper motive is significant in Christian ethics because God not only cares about what people do but also why they choose to do it. You see, actions alone do not define righteousness but rather the heart and the intent behind those actions determine their true moral value. A person might do a good deed such as helping the poor, donating, attending church, etc. but if it is done for selfish reasons like praise, recognition, or guilt it looses its spiritual worth. While we may look at these actions, God looks beyond the outward behavior and judges the inner motives. The reason for this is because genuine righteousness comes from love and faith not from obligation or personal gain. Christian ethics emphasizes integrity of heart as much as obedience in action. Proper motives are those that align with God\'s character and His purpose. It comes down to the love you have for God and the love you have for others. Jesus taught that all the commandments come from these two principles. When we act out of love for God, we should desire to please Him, reflect His goodness and express gratitude for His blessings. At the same time, when we act to show love for others, we show compassion, kindness, and humility, seeking the well being of others before ourselves. Proper motives comes from a heart that is transformed by God\'s grace. We are not called to act out of fear, pride, or duty alone but rather out of sincere devotion to God. Whenever motives are pure, actions become an expression of worship and ethical living becomes a joyful response to God\'s love rather than a burdensome task.', 9, '2025-11-02 01:34:27', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3414, 918, 828, 'The threefold criteria for good works in Christian ethics teaches us that a truly good action must meet three key standards. These are as follows; the right standard, the right motive, and the right goal. Each of these works together to make sure that an action is pleasing to God and not just outwardly good by human standards. The first criteria is the right standard. This means that good works must be based on God\'s revealed will. Christians do not need to determine what is right and wrong by personal opinion, cultural trends or even by emotions but rather by Scripture. The Bible provides the moral foundation for all ethical behavior because it shows us God\'s commands and it also reflects on His perfect character. Actions that ignore or are contradicting to God\'s Word can\'t be considered good even if it may appear to be noble to others. The second criteria is the right motive. Even if someone appears to obey God\'s commands outwardly, their actions are not truly good unless they come from a sincere heart of love and faith. You see, God looks at the inner reasons behind what we do. That is why proper motives come from gratitude from God\'s grace, love that we have for Him, and a deep desire to worship Him in everything that we do. Acts done for selfish reasons such as pride, fear of punishment, or the desire for approval all fall short of this. The last criteria is the right goal. This focuses on glorifying God. The ultimate purpose of every good work is not personal benefit or social recognition but rather it is honoring God and advancing His kingdom. When Christians have this goal in mind, the works that are done reflect His character and as a result it will point others to Christ.', 9, '2025-11-02 01:49:59', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3415, 918, 829, 'The three perspectives that you must keep in mind when considering ethics are the normative, situational, and the existential perspectives. Each one of these are designed to help believers see moral decisions from a different but complementary angle. This helps give a fuller and more balanced understanding of what it means to live righteously before God. Together they show that Christian ethics involves not only knowing God\'s standard but also applying them wisely and sincerely in real life. Starting with the normative perspective, it focuses on God\'s law and his commands as the standard for right and wrong. Here we can see the questions being asked, \"What does God say?\" or \"What rules or principles apply here?\" This perspective helps remind us that moral truth comes from God\'s revealed Word and not from our reasonings or emotions. The Bible sets the standard for ethical living because it expresses the character of God and His will. The second perspective is the situational perspective. This perspective considers the circumstances and the results of an action. The questions one might ask here is \"What are the outcomes or effects of this choice?\" or \"How can I best show love in this situation?\" Here we see this perspective emphasize wisdom and discernment. This helps us understand how to apply God\'s commands appropriately in different context. The Bible gives us unchanging principles even though we may encounter situations in life that are diverse and complex. We as believers must thoughtfully evaluate how to act in ways that show God\'s intended good. The last perspective deals with the heart and the person that is making the decision. The questions that come here are \"What kind of person am I becoming?\" or \"Am I acting out of faith and love?\" This perspective focuses on motives, integrity and spiritual maturity. Ethics is not only about external actions but rather it is also about inner transformation.', 9, '2025-11-02 02:06:54', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3416, 918, 830, 'God serves as the absolute judge of morality because He is the Creator, the Lawgiver, and the ultimate standard of what is right and wrong. Since God made the world and everything in it, all moral truth comes from Him. His character defines what is goodness, justice, and love. Unlike human standards which change over time and also differ from culture to culture, God\'s standards are perfect and unchanging because they reflect who He is. What this means is that morality is not something that people can invent or decide for themselves but rather it is something God has revealed and continues to uphold through His Word. God has the final and absolute authority to declare what is right and wrong. His commandments that are given in the Scriptures are not suggestions but rather what is expected of His righteous will. They are based on His wisdom and holiness showing humanity how to live in harmony with Him and with one another. Because God knows all things perfectly, His judgements are always fair. He not only sees people\'s actions but also their motives and thoughts. What this means is that His evaluation of morality goes deeper than any human judgment. God also serves as the final judge who holds everyone accountable for their actions. At the end times He will judge all people according to His perfect standards. This should remind us all that ethical living is not just about pleasing others but rather its about honoring God. In other words, God is the absolute judge of morality because He is the source, standard, and the enforcer of all moral truth. His perfect character guarantees that His judgment are just and His authority is final.', 9, '2025-11-02 02:21:37', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3417, 918, 831, 'The three categories of revelation that are part of God\'s Word are decrees, commands, and works. Each of these forms of revelation communicates God\'s truth in a very unique way. It helps believers understand His will and character. First looking at God\'s decree, this refers to His eternal plans and purpose. This is showing us what He has determined will happen throughout history. This decrees reveal God\'s sovereignty and control over all things. From creation to redemption, everything happens according to His divine plan. An example of this is where God decreed the salvation of His people through Jesus Christ before the world was made. By studying God\'s decrees, we can learn that He is all powerful, wise and faithful. This helps remind us that nothing happens by chance and everything serves His divine purpose. Secondly we can see God\'s commands. These are God\'s moral instructions that He gives His people. These include the laws, principles, as well as teachings. God\'s commands show us that His holiness and how to live in a way that would honor Him. They tell us what is right and wrong as well as what is good and evil. Examples of this can be found with the Ten Commandments as well as the teachings of Jesus. Lastly we see God\'s works. Here we find God\'s actions in creation, providence, and redemption. Everything God has done from the beauty of the world that He created, the order of the universe, and not to mention the work of Jesus providing us salvation, all shows us God\'s glory and His goodness.', 8, '2025-11-02 02:37:08', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3418, 918, 832, 'Existential revelation teaches us about God\'s standard by showing that His truth is in our hearts and we experience it personally in our everyday lives. This means that God does not only reveal His standards through the Bible or the world around us but rather through our inner sense of right and wrong. Because we are all created in the image of God, each one of us has a conscience that helps us recognize good and evil. When we feel joy for doing what is right or guilt for what we do wrong, it shows us that God\'s moral law is active within us. This inner voice helps remind us that God\'s standard is not just a set of rules but rather it is part of who we are in Him. Even though sin can cloud our understanding and lead us to ignore God\'s truth, our conscience will continue to point us back on the right track. For the ones who follow Christ, this becomes even stronger because the Holy Spirit works within our hearts. It helps us understand God\'s Word by convicting us when we disobey and guides us toward a life that is pleasing to God. Instead of just obeying to follow rules, we learn to do what is right out of love and faith.', 7, '2025-11-02 02:47:22', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3419, 918, 833, 'In 1 John 1:7 it says, \"But if we walk in the light as He is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus His Son cleanses us from all sin.\" What this shows us is that our obligation to obey God\'s standard is closely tied to our forgiveness in Christ. The reason for this is because both come from the same source. Walking \"in the light\" means living according to God\'s truth. We do this by showing that we have truly been forgiven and transformed by Christ. John also points out that obedience is not what earns forgiveness but rather it is the natural result from being forgiven. Once you have received salvation, your heart as well as your desires begin to change. You no longer live for yourself but rather you start to live for God. Obedience becomes an expression of gratitude and love, not a way to gain acceptance. It shows that a person\'s faith is real and that they are living in fellowship with God. Forgiveness in Christ removes the barrier of sin that once separated us from God. When our relationship is restored we are called to live in the light. You can see here where John links obedience and forgiveness as two parts of the same. For those of us who are forgiven will seek to obey and those who obey have been forgiven and made new through Christ.', 7, '2025-11-02 02:59:12', '2026-04-16 02:46:41', NULL),
(3420, 919, 834, 'The Bible teaches us that it has divine authority because of the fact that it is not just the work of human authors but rather it is the Word of God Himself. You can see this in 2 Timothy 3:16 where Paul writes that \"All Scripture is God breathed...\" This means that God inspired the human writers so that every word reflects God\'s truth and His purpose. Because of this, it makes the Scriptures completely trustworthy. God cannot lie or make any mistakes. The Bible holds a unique place among all writings. It stands as the ultimate source of truth and moral guidance. Since it comes from God himself, it carries His authority and not just human opinion or cultural wisdom. The implications of this are very important for how we live and think. You see if the scriptures truly speaks with God\'s authority, then we must approach it with humility and with obedience. It\'s not something that we can debate or try to adjust to fit our modern preferences. Rather it is the standard by which everything should be measured. Secondly, the authority of the scriptures gives us confidence and stability in a changing world. We all know that human ideas, governments, and even cultures rise and fall but the Word of God remains firm and unchanging. It tells us what is right and wrong, what is true and false, despite the fact that everything of the world\'s values may change. If we understand this, we have to study the Word thoughtfully and seek to understand its true meaning and apply it faithfully in our lives.', 9, '2025-11-09 01:44:42', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3421, 919, 835, 'When it comes to making ethical decisions, we benefit from understanding the characteristics of Scripture because it gives us a solid and a dependable foundation to stand on. The Bible isn\'t just another book filled with human thoughts or moral lessons but rather it is the inspired Word of God. This means that every word in Scripture comes from God Himself, who used human writers to communicate His truth. When we read the Bible, we are hearing God\'s wisdom and not just human opinions. The Scriptures are also authoritative. This means that it carries ultimate power over our lives. It tells us what is right and what is wrong. It also teaches us the truth that outweighs our personal feelings, our traditions, or even cultural trends. Whenever we recognize this authority, we start to learn to base our moral choices on what God says rather than what we think society accepts. This will help us keep grounded when the world\'s stands keep changing all the time. Thirdly the Scripture is sufficient. The Bible gives us all the knowledge we need to live in a way that honors God. It will not cover every single issue directly but rather it provides principles that help us make better, wiser, and faithful decisions in any situation. Last but not least, the Scripture is clear and unified. You can see from the beginning to the end, its message points us to love God and to love others. It does not contradict itself or allow us to be in a confused state. When we start to understand the qualities of God\'s Word, we can approach every moral decision with humility, confidence, and peace. We start to understand that we are not left along to just figure things out but rather God has already given us everything we would need.', 10, '2025-11-09 01:59:28', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3422, 919, 836, 'The Scripture helps us become more like God by showing us who He is and what He cares about. The Bible isn\'t just a list of rules but rather it is a message from God that reveals His heart and His desires for His people. We can see this through the stories, instructions, and the promises all throughout the Word. We learn about God\'s love, His mercy, justice, and holiness. As we dive deep and study the Scriptures, we begin to understand what is pleasing to God and what isn\'t. The Word gives us a clear picture of God\'s character and it helps us see how we can reflect that character in our own lives. One of the biggest ways that Scripture helps us grow is by changing how we think. We see in Romans 12:2, \"Be transformed by the renewing of your mind.\" This only happens when we read and start to apply God\'s Word in our lives. The more time we spend in Scripture, the more our thoughts start to align with God\'s truth instead of the world\'s opinions. It teaches us to see life through the lens of God. It helps us value what He values and to reject what goes against His will. The Bible acts like a mirror by showing us both our weakness and the areas where we need to grow. Whenever we face decisions, it reminds us of God\'s wisdom and it helps us choose what is right. In addition to this, Scripture also gives us the strength to live according to God\'s will. It continues to encourage us when we\'re tired and weak and corrects us when we are wrong. Not only that, it comforts us when we might be struggling as well. As we follow the examples of faithful men and women found in the Word and continue to trust in God, we being to change from the inside out. We start to love what God loves and hate what He hates.', 10, '2025-11-09 02:12:50', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3423, 919, 837, 'In Romans 1:15-16, Paul says that he is eager to preach the Gospel because he is \"not ashamed of the Gospel, for it is the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes.\" Even though there were many people in his time that thought the message of Jesus\'s death and resurrection was foolish or weak, Paul knew that it was the only message that could really change lives. The Romans valued wisdom, strength, and status. To even think about the idea of a crucified Savior sounds ridiculous. However, Paul understood that what seems foolish to the world was actually the exact news they needed so desperately. The Gospel revealed God\'s plan to save humanity from sin and give eternal life through faith in Jesus. This is what Paul was compelled to preach because he himself had a personal encounter with God. Once a persecutor of Christians and being transformed by Jesus Himself, how can he keep quiet? That transformation gave him an unshakable confidence that the Gospel does work. It really does have the power to rescue people and change hearts. Paul wasn\'t motivated by fame or comfort but rather he preached because he loved God and wanted others to know the true God. Paul\'s example for us today reminds us not to be ashamed of our faith. Even though it may seem unpopular or mocked by the world, we must be bold and remain strong spreading the good news. Life Paul we are called to share it boldly trusting that God\'s power will work through His Word touching the hearts of the unbelievers.', 9, '2025-11-09 02:26:30', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3424, 919, 838, 'The Bible is filled with many different kinds of languages because it was written by many different people, in different times, and for different purposes. Even though all of these factors are at play it is written all under the inspiration of God. You can see throughout Scripture there is poetry, stories, songs, letters, proverbs, laws, as well as prophecies. Each of this type of writing helps us understand God in a unique way. If you look at Psalms for example, it uses poetry and vivid imagery to express emotions like joy, fear, and sorrow. The historical books tells real stories of God\'s people to show His faithfulness. The Gospels share the life and teachings of Jesus through storytelling and dialogue. You can also see that Paul\'s letters use thoughtful reasonings to guide believers in their faith and in their moral living. This wide variety of language shows that God speaks to people in ways that touch both the mind and the heart. Some of these passages use extraordinary language such as visions, miracles, and prophecies. These are used to help reveal God\'s power. When Isaiah describes seeing the Lord on His throne or when John writes about heaven in Revelations, the language that is used helps us understand that it is beyond ordinary. It gives us a sense of God\'s greatness as well as mystery. At the same time, much of the Bible uses ordinary or everyday language. You can see that Jesus often spoke in parables about simple things like farmers planting seeds, and shepherds caring for sheep, or even the example of a father welcoming a lost son. \r\nAll of these stories make a deep spiritual truth that anyone would be able to understand. By combining both the extraordinary and the ordinary language it allows us to be reminded that God\'s Word is both majestic and approachable. It reveals divine mysteries but at the same time also meets us in our daily needs.', 9, '2025-11-09 02:41:38', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3425, 919, 839, 'The Ten Commandments are important both historically and theologically because they show how God has guided His people through time and revealed His moral standards for everyone. You can see historically that the Ten Commandments were given by God to Moses on Mount Sinai after the Israelites were freed from slavery in Egypt. This event was turning point for them because it marked the beginning of their life as a nation chosen and led by God. The commandments were more than just a set of rules but rather they were the foundation for how Israel were to live. They were written by God Himself on stone tablets showing that they were permanent and carried divine authority. These laws were placed in the Ark of the Covenant and kept in the center of Israel\'s worship. This helped remind them that obedience to God was at the heart of their identity. Theologically, the Ten Commandments reveal who God is and what He values. The first four commandments teaches us how to love and honor God by worshiping only Him and avoiding idols, respecting His name and keeping the Sabbath day holy. The last six teaches us how to treat others like honoring our parents, valuing life, being faithful in marriage, respecting others property, telling the truth, and being content with what we have. All of this together reflect God\'s character. If we look at it even today, the Ten Commandments are a guide for how to live a moral and faithful life. They remind us that doing what is right isn\'t about following just an empty set of rules but rather it\'s about loving God and as a result loving people.', 0, '2025-11-09 02:56:02', '2025-11-09 02:56:02', NULL),
(3426, 919, 840, 'You can see in the Old Testament that God\'s law is often understood in three traditional categories which are the moral law, civil law, and ceremonial law. Each of these types of laws have a special purpose. The moral law includes God\'s unchanging standard of right and wrong. Similar to those found in the Ten Commandments. These laws reflect God\'s character and apply to all people for all time. They teach us how to love God and how to love other. This is done by honoring our parents, speaking truth, avoiding theft, murder, and adultery and most of all worshiping only God. Even though we live under grace, the moral law still guides our behavior and shows us what pleases God. Next is the civil law. This was given specifically for ancient Israel as a nation. These laws governed daily life, justice, and social order. It handled issues with things like property rights, punishment for crimes, and rules about how to handle debts. While these specific laws don\'t necessarily apply to our present day, they still show God\'s concern for fairness, justice, and compassion. If you dive deeper, you can find principles that would help shape our views and how we treat others. The last is the ceremonial law. This specifically deals with worship sacrifices, rituals, festivals, and cleanliness. When Jesus came, He fulfilled these laws through His life and His sacrifice. We no longer have to follow these laws literally but they still teach us important truths about holiness, forgiveness, as well as God\'s desire for relationship with His people. If we can understand these three categories, we can wisely start to apply God\'s law today.', 8, '2025-11-09 03:09:07', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3427, 919, 841, 'Even though it may seem a little confusing at first, the key idea to keep in mind is that the Old Testament civil and ceremonial laws have expired in their original form but their principles still apply today because they reveal God\'s character and His values. The civil laws were given specifically for the nation of Israel when it was both a religious and political community. These laws covered things like property rights, punishments, and community justice. Even though modern societies are not bound to follow these exact laws, the principles behind them such as fairness, honesty, justice and care for the vulnerable all reflect God\'s heart. We can still learn how God wants His people to act justly and how God wants us to treat others rightly. The ceremonial laws were about worship and sacrifice. These would include things like temple rituals, animal sacrifices, and purification practices that helped Israel stay in right standing with God. All of these laws were fulfilled by Jesus who because the ultimate sacrifice for sin. Because of Jesus, we do not need to offer sacrifices or follow those rituals anymore. However, the meaning behind them such as God\'s holiness as well as the seriousness of sin and the need for forgiveness all still applies to this day. So even though all of these laws may have expired because Jesus fulfilled their purpose, they still apply spiritually because they teach eternal truths about who God is and how we should live.', 8, '2025-11-09 03:21:09', '2026-04-16 02:45:28', NULL),
(3428, 920, 842, 'To make ethical decisions it requires understanding the situational content of God\'s revelation. What this means is that we must pay attention to what is happening around us and how God\'s truth speaks into that situation. There are three types of this situational content; facts, goals, and means. The first, facts, describe what is actually true in the situation that we face. This includes the people involved, the events that are taking place, as well as the responsibilities or the relationships that shape. At the same time, we must keep in mind the spiritual realities as well such as God is always present, always ruling, and always faithful to His people. When you are able to understand all of this, it helps see the situation more clearly and avoid making decisions based on fear or confusion. The second type of situational content is goals. This is the purpose or outcome that we are hoping for. God uses His revelation to teach us what goals we should be pursing. There will be many situations that seem complicated but God gives clarity through His Word by showing us what truly matters. The most important face we must remember is that our goals must be shaped by what God values and not by what feels good or convenient in the moment. The last is by means. This referee to the methods or the actions that we use to reach our goals. God doesn\'t just tell us what to aim for but rather he shows us the proper way to pursue those goals. We should not choose sinful or selfish methods but rather do it the right way. God cares not only about our goals but how we achieve them as well.', 10, '2025-11-13 00:50:49', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3429, 920, 843, 'Facts are things that are very true in any situation. They can include what has happened in the past, who is involved, and what responsibilities we have. Facts are not just our feelings or our guesses. They are actually details that describe what is going on. Facts also include the spiritual truths that are found in God\'s Word. These are things that we could never figure out on our own such as God\'s character, His promises, and the truth that He is always present and in control. Knowing this is important because good decisions must be based on truth and not on any type of concussion or assumptions. If you and I were to misunderstand what is actually happening, we could make a wrong choice that might hurt someone or lead us away from what God would want us to do. Facts help us assess our situation more clearly. It keeps us from acting too quickly, over reacting, or even reacting with fear or anger. When we start to understand the real situation, we can make wiser and more responsible choices. Knowing important facts about God such as how God is loving, wise, powerful, and always with us helps us change the way we face our decisions. Knowing who God is gives us the courage to do what is right even when it might be very difficult. His promises remind us that obedience brings blessings. He controls everything and makes everything work together for our good. Knowing this helps bring us comfort and peace in any decision we make. Putting Him first in all thing will make our decisions just that much easier.', 9, '2025-11-13 01:02:04', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3430, 920, 844, 'Goals are the purposes and the outcomes that we try to aim for whenever we make decisions. It is what we would like to accomplish or what we hope will happen because of our actions. Some goals are small and immediate such as fixing a problem, keeping peace with someone or finishing a task. Others are much bigger and long term such as building a strong family or growing in faith. Goals help give us direction to our choices. Without goals, we would just be reacting to life instead of making thoughtful and intentional decisions. Trying to understand biblical goals is very important because not every goal we want is good, wise, or even pleasing to God. Most of the time our desires are often shaped by comfort, fear, pride, or even the pressures around us. If we only consider what feels right in the moment then we will choose goals that will be either selfish or short sighted. However, God reveals goals that help us see what truly matters. God\'s Word teaches us that our highest purpose is to honor God, love our neighbor, and live in a way that reflects His character. When we start understanding these biblical goals, our decisions that we make become more clearer. Our questions will change from \"What do I want right now?\" to \"What would honor God in this situation?\" Biblical goals helps us see beyond our immediate emotions and start to think about the bigger picture of God\'s work in our lives. They also help us avoid choices that might seem helpful in the moment but later would lead to harm.', 9, '2025-11-13 01:12:59', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3431, 920, 845, 'An overly rigorous approach to revelation happens whenever someone treats God\'s commands in a harsh, strict, or an overly literal way that actually goes beyond what God actually requires. This will more than often add extra rules, create heavy burdens, or even demand one correct answer in situations where the Bible allows freedom or requires wisdom. Instead of using God\'s revelation to guide and help share our decision, a rigorous approach turns it into a rigid system that leaves little room for prayer, judgment, or even grace. When you fall into this type of mindset you may believe that you are protecting obedience but more often than not you end up making God\'s law seem stricter than He made it. This in fact is very dangerous because it can distort God\'s character. When you add strict rules, we may start seeing God as harsh or demanding instead of wise, patient and loving. A rigorous mindset can make people feel full of guilt and fear because they will feel like they can never meet the impossible standards placed on them. It can create pride as well because people who keep the rules will feel superior or better than others. Instead of growing in wisdom, humility and faith you will be focused on strict rule keeping. This by all means can be avoided by remembering that God\'s revelation always points us to wisdom and love and not rigid legalism. Even though the Bible tells us to obey it also teaches us to use good judgment.', 8, '2025-11-13 01:25:02', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3432, 920, 846, 'Jesus teaches us many different things throughout the Sermon on the Mount. There was one important theme that He spoke about as well which was the Kingdom of God. He describes what the kingdom is like, who belongs to it, and how people in the kingdom are called to live. In the first section you can see Jesus highlights the kingdom in the Beatitudes. He starts by describing the kind of people who receive God\'s blessing. You can see that He mentions twice \"theirs is the kingdom of heave.\" This shows that the kingdom is not just something in the future but rather it has already belongs to those who follow Him. The Beatitudes shows us a picture of the kind of character that fits in God\'s kingdom. This helps remind us that God values the heart more than outward success. Secondly you can see Jesus emphasizing the kingdom when he teaches about righteousness and the law. He says that those who belong to the kingdom must have a righteousness that is deeper than that of the religious leaders. Instead of just obeying the rules outwardly, kingdoms people obey from the heart. They refuse anger, lust, dishonesty, and revenge. True righteousness comes from a transformed life. Living under God\'s rule means letting His character shape every part of our lives. Lastly you can see that Jesus spoke about the kingdom when He teaches about worry and priorities. He tells His listeners not to be anxious about food, clothing, or daily needs. Instead what we should do is not be controlled by fear. Jesus says to \"seek first the kingdom of God and His righteousness.\" When the kingdom becomes our first priority, God promises to take care of our needs.', 8, '2025-11-13 01:37:51', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3433, 920, 847, 'The Kingdom of God has three main components that work together to help us see what life under God\'s rule is meant to be. These are God\'s rule, God\'s people, and God\'s place. The first component of God\'s rule is that the kingdom exists whenever God\'s authority is recognized and obeyed. God has always ruled over the wold but His kingdom becomes visible when people accept His rule in their hearts and live it out in their lives. This plays out practically when we trust Him and obey His commands, living the way He has called us to live. God\'s rule is the foundation of the kingdom. The second component is God\'s people. A kingdom must have citizens and God\'s kingdom is made up of those who belong to Him through faith. These are the people who hear God\'s voice and have a desire to live according to His will. They live out their lives to reflect His character. It is through these people that His kingdom become visible on earth. The third component is God\'s place. What this is in reference to is the environment where God\'s rule is displayed. You can see in the Old Testament that this place was the land God gave Israel. Likewise in the New Testament, the place of the kingdom expands to the whole world because God\'s presence is with His people wherever they go.', 8, '2025-11-13 01:54:36', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3434, 920, 848, 'The outline of the historical developments of the kingdom of God in three major periods show how God has worked through history to establish His rule among His people. These periods move from creation to the fall and God\'s work to the restoration of all things in Christ. Each of these periods build on the one before it. The first is the original kingdom at creation. God created the world as His kingdom and everything He made was good. Adam and Eve lived under God\'s rule enjoying His presence and carrying out His commands. They were called to fill the earth and care for it. Even though it was the early stage, God\'s rule, His people, and God\'s place were all perfectly aligned. Humanity lived in harmony with God as well as each other and the creation. The second period is the fallen kingdom and God\'s work of restoration. After sin entered the world through Adam and Eve, the perfect order was damaged. Humans no longer obeyed God\'s rule. As a result, relationships were broken and suffering entered creation. Despite all this, God did not abandon His kingdom but rather He became restoring it through covenants. First it was with Noah, then Abraham, Israel, and later with David. Through all of these covenants, God formed His people, gave them His law, and placed them in a land where His rule could be seen. This period shows God patiently rebuilding His kingdom despite the fallen world. The third period is the fulfilled kingdom in Christ. This has both a present as well as a future reality. Jesus announced that the kingdom of God has arrived because He is the true King. Through His life, death and resurrection, He defeated sing and began renewing God\'s people. The kingdom continues to grow as people turn to Christ and one day it will be completed when He returns.', 8, '2025-11-13 02:10:22', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3435, 920, 849, 'When I think about God\'s law, I don\'t see it as a burden or something irrelevant but rather a way to help shape us into the people God created us to be. I understand what the lessons and the text say such as God\'s law shows His character, protects us from harm and teaches us how to love Him and others. Still I know at times it is a struggle because it can feel demanding or difficult to follow all the time. These are areas where we need to strive to improve. I used to see it as rules that take away freedom and even avoiding it altogether. Influence from culture shapes our thoughts and without strong biblical teachings and understanding is makes us confused or allows us to believe in ideas that are skewed. But after growing in faith a more biblical way to think about God\'s law is to see it as something that brings joy and life. It is meant to guide us and not crush us. The problem is not the law but rather our heart. When we rely on our own strength and our own ways it feels too heavy to bear. However when we trust in God, the law becomes a path that leads us closer to Him. Instead of feeling trapped we start to experience the freedom that comes from living the way God intended for us to. Don\'t get me wrong, I still have alot of growing to do myself. I need to understand more that God\'s commands are not random but rather they show what matters to Him. I need to examine my heart everyday with the help of the Holy Spirit to make sure I am living the way God intended for me to live. I am not blinded in any way and be able to live in His freedom ultimately drawing closer and deeper relationship with Him.', 10, '2025-11-13 02:23:27', '2026-04-16 02:44:36', NULL),
(3436, 922, 858, 'Loving allegiance to God means being loyal to Him. This would look like to shaping our lives around Him and accepting the responsibilities that along with that. First of all, Loyalty means that you stay faithful to God no matter what. It is choosing Him as our King and not letting other things take His place in our hearts. When we choose to be loyal, we trust God, depend on Him, and keep following Him even when life is difficult. This kind of loyalty isn\'t just about keeping rules but rather it comes from loving God because He has already loved us. Secondly, loving allegiance shapes our orientation. In other words it shapes the overall direction of our lives. When we love God, our thoughts, choices, and goals are tuned in toward what pleases Him. Instead of living for ourselves, we begin to ask what God wants. His Word becomes our guide and keeps us pointed in the right direction. Even when we mess up or get distracted, this helps us turn our hearts back to God. Lastly loving allegiance includes responsibility. When we choose to love someone, it affects how we act. In the same way, loving God means that we take responsibility to obey Him, love others, and live in a way that reflects His goodness. This isn\'t meant to weigh us down but on the other hand it\'s to show our love in action.', 7, '2025-11-29 01:42:20', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3437, 922, 859, 'God\'s atoning grace and God\'s common grace gives us powerful examples of how we should treat people in our day to day lives. Starting with atoning grace, this is the grace God shows when He saves us through Jesus. This is something that we did not earn and we did not deserve but God freely gave it to us. This teaches us that our love for others should also be generous and willing to sacrifice. When God forgives us, He does not hold things over our heads but rather He lets them go completely. We should also try to forgive others the same say. Atoning grace reminds us to be patient, kind, and willing to show mercy even when someone has hurt us. Now common grace is the everyday goodness God gives to all people. He sends sunshine, rain, beauty and blessings to everyone not just His believers. This shows us that we should treat all people with respect and kindness not matter who they are. If God is good to everyone then we should also look for ways to be good to everyone. This can be done by being fair, helpful, and generous. Common grace helps us see the value in every person because they are all made in God\'s image.', 8, '2025-11-29 01:55:07', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3438, 922, 860, 'When I honestly reflect on my on heart and what motivates me, I see a mix of good and not so good reasons. There are times that I do what is right because I want things to go smoothly but there are also other times I act out of habit rather than real convictions. Even though both of these still lead to outward obedience they don\'t always come from a heart that is fully focused on God. I try to make it my goal. I am still a work in progress and I hope that one day it is second nature. A heart truly committed to God is one that acts because it trust Him. I would say I have learned through many experiences that now majority of the time I do trust God. However there are moments when my obedience is shallow and moments when my heart is not fully aligned with God\'s purpose. This is where the Holy Spirit nudges me and it helps remind me that spiritual growth is a process. God doesn\'t expect perfection but He does invite me to keep returning to Him. When I slow down, pray, and focus on His grace, my motives begin to change and I start wanting what He wants and not just acting out of pressure or convenience. At the end of the day, I do want my obedience to grow from a heart that is more committed to God and His Word. I know that He can help me get there as I stay close to Him.', 8, '2025-11-29 02:10:51', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3439, 922, 861, 'I do remember what it was life when I first came to the faith. In the beginning I was full of excitement and passion. Everything felt new and I was \"on fire\" for God. my motives were simple and strong. I wanted to please Him, learn as much as I could, and share my faith. Obedience felt joyful and doing the right thing came more naturally because my heart was overflowing with gratitude. However as time went on and life brought challenges, there were seasons when my motivation faded and times when I struggled with doubt, fear, and disappointment. Some situations pulled me away from God and other times I just felt tired spiritually. My motives were always clear and my behavior didn\'t always match what I knew was right. These ups and downs didn\'t mean that my faith was gone but rather they just showed that the Christian journey is not always smooth. Over the years though, God\'s faithfulness has been steady. He has shown love, protection, and grace to me and my family again and again. Seeing His care in real life situations has changed me. It has deepened my motives and made my faith more mature. Now my desire to follow God is stronger than before. I want to be right with Him not out of fear but rather out of love and respect. Today, I feel more in tune with God than I ever have and I want to grow closer to Him each day. My journey isn\'t perfect but it has brought me to a place of deeper trust, gratitude, and genuine devotion.', 9, '2025-11-29 02:24:24', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3440, 922, 862, 'Imagination is the ability that God gave us to picture things in our minds even when we can\'t see them with our eyes. It helps us think about ideas, possibilities and situations that are not always right in front of us. Imagination is not just for art or for stories but it is an important part of how we learn, understand, and make sense of life. Our imagination is very important when we think about God. Since we can\'t see God physically, we use our imagination to understand what He is like. When we read the Bible, our imagination helps us picture the stories, teachings, and the way God works. It helps us see His love, power, and care in a way that feels real and meaningful. Imagination also helps us apply God\'s Word in our lives by picturing how we should act, speak, and respond to different situations. Imagination also helps us understand the world. It helps us to think ahead and plan and consider the results of our choices. When we imagine different outcomes, we become wiser and make better decisions. Imaginations helps us learn new things because we can connect ideas and think beyond what we already know. Imagination also helps us understand ourselves. It lets us picture who we are becoming and how God is shaping our character. We can imagine the kind of person we want to be and how we can grow in faith, love and in obedience.', 9, '2025-11-29 02:39:27', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3441, 922, 863, 'Assessing knowledge is important when we make ethical decisions because it helps us make choices based on truth and understanding and not just on our feelings or guesses. When we take time to think about what we know, we can make wiser and more thoughtful decisions. Assessing knowledge helps us figure out what is true. We get information from many places such as friends, family, social media and even from our own experiences and our emotions. Not everything we hear or think is correct. By checking what we know and asking if it is reliable, we avoid making decisions based on wrong information. This in return helps us from acting in ways that might hurt ourselves or others. It also helps us slow down. Ethical decisions are often complicated and if we act too quickly, we might choose something that might be unwise. When we take the time to stop and examine what we understand about the situation, we become more careful and less impulsive. This gives us time to think about the consequences of our actions. Assessing knowledge also helps us see the bigger picture. It encourages us to think about how our choices affect other people and our relationship with God. Instead of focusing on what we want right now, we start to think about what is fair, loving, and responsible. Lastly it also reminds us that we need God\'s wisdom. We don\'t know everything so we look to the Bible and in prayer we seek wise advice to help us make good decisions.', 8, '2025-11-29 02:50:21', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3442, 922, 864, 'The will is the part of us that makes choices. It is where our thoughts, feelings, and our desires come together so we can decide what to do. Our minds helps us understand information and our emotions help us feel and react but the will is what actually chooses the actions we take. Throughout the decision making process the will plays a major role. It first looks at what we know. This would include what we\'ve learned, what we believe is right or wrong and what God teaches in His Word. It then considers our motives such as why we want to make a certain choice and what we hope will happen because of it. After thinking through these things, the will makes the final decision. The will also shows what we truly value. It is possible to know the right thing and even feel strongly about it but the choice doesn\'t become real until the will says yes. This is where if our hearts are focused on God and we want to please Him, our will becomes stronger and more able to choose what is right, even when it\'s hard. However, if our hearts are distracted or set on selfish desires, our will follows those instead.', 9, '2025-11-29 03:02:15', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3443, 922, 865, 'Reflecting on Luke 6:45 \"A good man brings good things out of the good stored up in his heart, and an evil man brings evil things out of the evil stored up in his heart. For the mouth speaks what the heart is full of.\" \r\n\r\nWhen I think about what Jesus says here, it reminds me that my actions and words show what is really in my heart. So when I look honestly at my own life, I can see signs of what I\'m truly committed to on the inside. There are times when my works and speech show good things. When I\'m patient, kinds, or willing to help someone. Or when I choose to forgive or when I speak gently instead of reacting in anger. These moments point to a heart that care about God\'s will. They show that I am trying to live a way that honors Him. These moments my heart feels focused on God . On the other hand, there are also times when my actions and words show weakness. There are times I complain, get frustrated too quickly, or say things without thinking. This becomes clear that my heart still struggles. These moments show that I don\'t always trust God as much as I should. They show areas where I need to grow and let God change my heart. My works and speech show a mix of these good and bad moments. I still need God\'s help everyday. I truly do want to honor Him but I am not perfect. Seeing both the good and the bad reminds me to keep seeking God everyday so that my He can shape my heart so that my actions and words are more clearly aligned with Him.', 10, '2025-11-29 03:15:41', '2026-04-16 02:43:33', NULL),
(3444, 921, 850, 'God\'s authority, control and his presence helps shape our ethical responsibilities in meaningful ways. First, looking at God\'s authority means that He has the right to tell us what is right and what is wrong. Because He is our creator, moral rules are not based on personal opinion or even cultural trends. Rather, they come from God\'s character and His commands. What this means for us is that our ethical responsibility is obedience. We are called to listen to God\'s word, respect His commands, and submit our choices to Him even if it seem difficult or even unpopular. Acting ethically isn\'t about doing what feels the best to you but rather its about honoring God and doing what is pleasing to Him. Second, God\'s control reminds us that nothing happens outside of His power and His plan. God rules over everything, including history, circumstances, and outcomes. This does not take away our responsibility but rather it strengthens it. Because God is in control, our decisions matter and are part of His purposes. It is our responsibility to act wisely and faithfully, trusting God that He can use our choices for good. Even when the circumstances that we face may seem confusing or challenging, we have to continue to do what is right knowing that God is able to work through our obedience. Lastly, God\'s presence means that He is always with us. We never make decisions alone. God sees our thoughts and motives and actions even when no else does. Our ethical responsibility is to live the same way in our private lives as we do in public. God\'s presence is always there to bring us comfort in knowing that He helps us make wise and Godly choices.', 8, '2025-12-06 11:15:59', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3445, 921, 851, 'The preternatural realm is the created spiritual world that exists beyond what us humans can normally see or touch. It is not the same as God\'s divine nature because God is uncreated and He is eternal but at the same time it is also not part of the physical universe. This realm includes spiritual beings that God created and it works under His authority and control. The preternatural world reminds us that reality is bigger than just the material world that we live in every day. The primary beings that inhabits the preternatural realm are the angels and the demons. Angels are good spiritual beings created by God to serve Him. Their purpose is to carry out God\'s commands, worship Him, and even at times help or protect people according to His will. Angels are powerful, intelligent, and moral beings. However, they are not all knowing or all powerful. They only exist to glorify God and support His purpose int he world. Demons on the other hand are fallen angels who rebelled against God. They rejected His authority and chose sin. This in return separates them from God. Demons can still exist within God\'s control but they go against His work and choose to deceive and harm humans spiritually. Their main goal here is to lead people away from God by promoting lies, temptation, and creating confusion. They do not have unlimited power and they can only act within the limits God has allowed. The interactions that occur within the preternatural realm are full of real conflict. Here Angels and demons are apposed to each other because they both serve completely different purposes. This struggle is not equal though since God\'s authority is absolute and the outcome of the battle is already determined. Even though this is the case, it still affects the human world. Spiritual beings can influence thoughts, events, or circumstances. Despite that, humans still remain responsible for their own choices. It is still our responsibility to live wisely and faithfully to God\'s plan.', 8, '2025-12-06 11:30:27', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3446, 921, 852, 'The natural realm is the physical world that God has created. This is the world that we live in and that we can experience with our sight, touch, hearing, smelling, etc. This realm includes the earth, plants, animals, and human beings. It works in accordance with the laws of nature that God has established. The natural realm was created good and orderly reflecting God\'s wisdom and His purpose. Humans hold a special place in this realm because they are made in His image. What this means for us is that people have a moral responsibility, the ability to reason, and the calling to rule over all of creation in a way that honors God. The inhabitants of the natural realm include all living creatures but humans are unique. Animals act according to instinct while us humans are capable of moral decision making and meaningful relationships with God. People were created to live in harmony with God and with one another. The work we do, rest, creativity, and community were all originally part of God\'s good design for life in the natural realm. However, over time the situation of the natural realm has changed because of our sin. When humanity rebelled against God, sin entered the world and disrupted the original order. The natural realm became affected by suffering, decay, and death. The relationships between people were damaged, work became more difficult and creation itself began to experience brokenness. We started to see more natural disasters, disease, and hardship. All of these were signs that the world is no longer as it was originally intended to be. Despite all of this, the natural realm is not abandoned by God. He still continues to sustain us and work within this realm to accomplish His plan and His purpose. You can see all throughout history God has been guiding events toward restoration. The Bible says that one day the effects of all this sin will be removed and creation will be renewed. Until that day comes, we are called to live responsibly in the natural realm by making ethical choices that reflect God\'s plan and purpose for us.', 8, '2025-12-06 11:46:18', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3447, 921, 853, 'The preternatural realm and the natural realm are both part of God\'s creation but they are very different in nature and purpose. The natural realm is the physical world that we live in every day. This includes humans, animals, plants, etc. It operates according the physical laws like gravity, space, and time. People experience this realm directly through their senses. Human are given a moral responsibility within it because they were created by God in His image. The preternatural realm on the other hand is the spiritual realm created by God. It includes angels and demons who do not have physical bodies like humans do. This realm is normally unseen by humans but it is just as real and it also exists under God\'s authority. While humans live mainly in the natural realm, spiritual beings operate in the preternatural realm and serve either God\'s purposes or oppose them. One of the major similarities between the two realms is that both are fully under God\'s control. Neither one operates independently from Him. However a  key difference is how they function. The natural realm follows physical laws while the preternatural realm functions spiritually. Humans belong to the natural realm but are affect by the preternatural realm while the spiritual beings do not belong to the natural realm. There are several ways that these realms influence each other. First you can see that angels may protect or guide people int he natural realm as part of God\'s plan. At the same time, demonic influences can lead to temptation, deception, or moral confusion which impacts human decisions and behavior. Lastly, prayer connects the natural realm to the preternatural realm. Our prayers are used by God in spiritual activity beyond what we can physically see. Bringing this all together we can see that these interactions show that ethical choices in the natural realm often have spiritual significance beyond the physical world.', 9, '2025-12-06 12:00:40', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3448, 921, 854, 'Humanity is described as good because people are created in the image of God. What this means is that human beings reflect God in a special way that no other part of creation does. People can think, make choices, create, and form relationships. Because of this, every human life has value and has dignity. No matter who you are or what you do or even what you have done int he past, they matter to God. Humans being made in God\'s image also means that humans are responsible for living in a way that shows God\'s character. Some of these are love, truth, justice, and the compassion to care for others. God also gave His blessing to humanity. This blessing was more than just kind words but rather it gave people the ability to grow, thrive, and enjoy the world God created. God\'s blessing showed that He wanted humans to succeed in their role. Because of this blessing, people are meant to depend on God, be thankful, and trust in Him every day. This blessing reminds us that life comes from God and is meant to be lived for Him. Along with being created in God\'s image and receiving His blessings, we were also given a cultural mandate. This is God\'s command for people to rule over the earth in a caring and in a responsible way. Humans were called to work, build families as well as communities in addition to taking care of the world. We must not use what God have us in a selfish way but rather be good stewards who protect and develop what God has made.', 8, '2025-12-06 12:15:42', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3449, 921, 855, 'The fall has deeply affected humanity\'s ability to clearly understand and follow God\'s commands. When sin came into the picture through human disobedience, it did more than damage behavior, it also damaged understanding. Unredeemed human beings are no longer able to see God\'s truth clearly on their own. Sin darkened human thinking. Because of this people naturally misunderstand, ignore, or even reject what God has said. Instead of trusting God\'s wisdom, humans tend to rely on their own judgement. This often times leads us away from God\'s standards. Because of the fall, our hearts are also affected. People are not neutral when it comes to God\'s commands. Sin has caused us to resist God\'s authority and prefer independence. Even when God\'s rules are known, unredeemed people often twist them to fit their own desires or excuse their sinful behavior. This moral rebellion makes it difficult for people to accept that God\'s commands are good and are ment for their benefit. We start to see that pride and self-centeredness replaced humility and obedience. The fall also affects how people interpret the world around them. Without redemption, we may recognize right and wrong in a general way but we fail to understand God\'s commands as part of a loving relationship with Him. God\'s law is often seen as restrictive. Because of this, people follow cultural rules and personal feelings instead of God\'s truths. Lastly, the fall separates people from God spiritually. Without God\'s guidance, human reasoning is limited and its flawed. Proper knowledge of God\'s commands require a heart that is renewed and that is willing to listen. Until a person is redeemed, sin will continue to block clear understanding making it impossible to fully know or obey God\'s commands as He as intended.', 9, '2025-12-06 12:30:34', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3450, 921, 856, 'Christians can find great comfort in knowing that God\'s attributes are immutable. This means that they never change. In a world were everything is uncertain and constantly changing, God remains the same. His love does not fade and His promises do not fail. In addition to all this, His character is always trustworthy. This gives believers the stability and the peace we need. When circumstances are confusing or painful, Christians can be confident that God\'s goodness, wisdom, and His faithfulness are exactly the same as they have always been. God will never just suddenly stop being loving or just or even act in a way that goes against His nature. God\'s immutability also brings comfort because it means that His moral standards do not change. What God calls right and wrong does not change with culture or with time. Christians can trust that God\'s commands are consistent and reliable. Even when we live in a society that may pressure believers to compromise, we can rely on God\'s unchanging truth as a steady guide for life. This reassurance helps Christians make ethical decisions without fear or confusion. There is also comfort in knowing that God\'s attributes never conflict with one another. God is never loving at the expense of justice or just without being loving. His wisdom, power, holiness, mercy, and love all work together in perfect harmony. What this means to us is that everything God does is completely good and completely right even when there are times that we don\'t fully understand it. When we face suffering or unanswered questions, we can trust that God is not divided or confused. He never makes a mistake or acts unfairly. Knowing this we can be at peace and have confidence in Him, trusting His plans are loving, wise, and purposeful in every situation we go through.', 7, '2025-12-06 12:43:47', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3451, 921, 857, 'In Christ I know that I\'ve been given the ability to live in true goodness again but I still struggle with sin every day. One of the most helpful strategies that I\'ve found in resisting sin is prayer. When I pray honestly and often, it helps remind me that I can\'t fight sin on my own and that I need God\'s strength. Reading the Word has also been important for me. When God\'s Word fills my mind, it helps me recognize temptation more clearly and respond with truth rather than emotions. Another strategy that has helped me is accountability. Having trusted Christians in my life who encourage me, pray with me, and speak truth when I\'m struggling makes a big difference. Sin is much harder to fight when it stays hidden but by bringing struggles into the light helps weaken its power. I\'ve also learned that avoiding situations that lead to temptation is wise, not weak. Setting boundaries protects my heart and keeps small temptations from turning into bigger failures. Relying on the Holy Spirit instead of my own strength has been especially important. When I depend only on willpower, I usually fail and feel frustrated. I started to realize that growth happens when I ask God to help change my desires not just my actions. Some strategies have not worked well for me such as ignoring sin or minimizing it only makes it stronger. Guilt without turning back to God leaves me discouraged and comparing myself to others often leads to pride or shame. What helps me the most is staying honest with God, trusting in Him, and continuing to walk forward even after I have failed.', 8, '2025-12-06 12:56:47', '2026-04-16 02:42:28', NULL),
(3452, 926, 776, 'There are three main reasons that the majority of interpreters believe that Paul was not the author of the Book of Hebrews. The first is that Hebrews is completely anonymous. You can see in Paul\'s other letters that the book never names its author. Paul on the other hand always introduced himself at the beginning of his writings. This is seen in the book of Romans and in Corinthians. He was also careful about false letters being written in his name. Because of this, it would be very unusual for him to leave out his name. The second reason is that the themes in Hebrews are very different from Paul\'s usual focus. You see Hebrews spends a lot of time explaining Jesus as the High Priest, the role of the tabernacle, and the significance of Melchizedek. All of these topics are either missing or only briefly mentioned in Paul\'s letters. While Paul wrote deeply about salvation and grace you can see in Hebrews that it uses a different approach and emphasizes Old Testament priesthood and sacrifices in a unique way different from Paul\'s. The third and the most important reason is that the author of Hebrews places himself in a later generation of believers. You can see in Hebrews 2:3 \"So what makes us think we can escape if we ignore this great salvation that was first announced by the Lord Jesus himself and then delivered to us by those who heard him speak?\" The writer says the message of salvation was confirmed by those who heard Jesus. This means that he received the gospel from eyewitnesses and not directly from Christ. Paul on the other hand strongly taught that he received the gospel directly from Jesus himself. This can been seen in Galatians 1:11-12, \"Dear brothers and sisters, I want you to understand that the gospel message I preach is not based on mere human reasoning. I received my message from no human source and no one taught me. Instead, I received it by direct revelation from Jesus Christ.\"', 8, '2026-04-12 23:16:26', '2026-04-16 02:41:04', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3453, 926, 777, 'The book of Hebrews was written to a specific group of believers and it gives us a clear clue about who there were for. First, the audience was mostly Jewish. In Hebrews 1:1 the writer talks about the Old Testament people as \"our forefathers,\" which shows us that both the author and his readers shared a Jewish background and understood the Scriptures well. Secondly, we can conclude that they were Hellenistic. This means that they were influenced by Greek culture and likely lived outside of Israel. The ideas and teaching style that are found in Hebrews fit with  Greek speaking Jewish communities. This shows that they were part of that environment. Thirdly you can see that they were spiritually immature. In Hebrews 5:12, the author tells them that they should be teachers by now but instead they still need to learn the basic truths again. This shows that they had not grown in their faith like they should have. Fourthly they were facing persecution. In Hebrews 10:32-34 it explains how they suffered publicly, lost property, and stood with others who were imprisoned. This shows us that their faith came with very real hardship and pressure. Last but not least they were close to turning away from Christ. In Hebrews 10:26-27, the author warns his readers about the dangers of continuing in sin after knowing the truth. This shows that some were at risk of abandoning their faith. With all of these characteristics, it helps us understand why the writer speaks so strongly. His main goal was to encourage them to grow, stay faithful, and not give up on Jesus.', 9, '2026-04-12 23:32:37', '2026-04-16 02:41:04', NULL),
(3454, 926, 778, 'The author of Hebrews used many different methods and literary devices to express and reveal his exhortations to his audience. They were very clear and powerful ways to speak to them and encourage them to stay faithful. His goal was not just to teach information but rather was to move people to take action and stay strong in their faith. First he used strong encouragements. He often said things like \"let us hold on\" or \"let us draw near.\" These phrases are ment to include the readers and push them to respond. It shows that the message is personal and urgent and not just some general teaching. Secondly he gives clear commands. He told the readers to persevere, obey their leaders, and stay faithful to Jesus. These commands made it clear that their situation was serious and that they needed to act right away. Thirdly, the author used comparisons to help explain his message. He compared Jesus to angels, Moses, and the Old Testament priest. Each time he showed that Jesus is greater and this helped the readers understand why they should not go back to their old ways. Fourthly he used examples of faithful people. In Hebrews 11, you can see that he lists many people from the Old Testament who trusted in God even during hard times. These examples were meant to encourage the readers to do the same. Lastly, he used a \"lesser to greater\" way of thinking. This means if something was important in the past, it is even more important now because of Jesus. By using all of these various methods, the author was able to make his message very clear to his readers.', 9, '2026-04-12 23:45:35', '2026-04-16 02:41:04', NULL),
(3455, 926, 779, 'Hebrews 5:12, \"You have been believers so long now that you ought to be teaching others. Instead you need someone to teach you again the basic things about God\'s word. You are like babies who need milk and cannot eat solid food.\" This verse reminds us that the original audience had not grown in their faith like they should have. It also challenges me to think of ares in my own life where I still need to grow. At the top of my head I see that there are two important areas for me, one is gaining a deeper understanding of Jesus and second becoming a better father by reflecting how God cares for His children. First I want to grow in my understanding of who Jesus truly is. I know that Jesus is my Savior, but Hebrews teaches that He is also our High Priest who stands before God on our behalf. He understands our struggles and helps us in our weakness. I want to go beyond just knowing facts about Jesus and really understand how His role affects my daily life. This means trusting Him more, depending on Him in hard times, and seeing Him as someone who is always present and working for my good. Secondly I want to grow in being a better father by showing my kids the same kind of love and patience that God has shown me. God is patient, kind, and full of grace even when I make mistakes. I want my children to experience that same kind of love through me. This means being more patient, listening better, guiding them with wisdom, and leading them toward God in both my words and actions. These are some of the areas of practical grown that reminds me that spiritual maturity is not just about knowledge but rather it\'s about living out my faith every day.', 9, '2026-04-12 23:59:44', '2026-04-16 02:41:04', NULL),
(3456, 926, 780, 'The author of Hebrews wrote to encourage believers to reject false thinking and to stay faithful to Jesus especially when life is difficult. In my own life, one false mindset I face is becoming complacent because life feels busy and overwhelming. Even though I am currently in Bible School, I know that I delayed starting and even when I was enrolled I moved slower than I should have. I allowed the responsibilities of life to slow me down and made excuses instead of stepping forward when God was calling me. It is easy to stay comfortable and move at a slower pace when things feel chaotic but that can keep me from fully walking in what God has for me. Another struggle I face is getting discouraged or mentally checking out when life gets hard. When challenges come, it is easy to feel frustrated or distracted and lose focus. Instead of pushing through, I can sometimes feel like giving up or even pulling back. This kind of thinking can weaken my faith if I let it continue. To remain faithful to Christ I am learning to stay consistent even when life is not perfect. I remind myself that God\'s timing is still good even if I started later than I wanted. I focused on being faithful where I am now instead of looking back with regret. I also try to stay grounded in prayer and staying consistent in God\'s Word. This helps me to keep the right perspective. Most importantly, I am learning to take steady steps forward trusting that God is still leading me and growing me daily.', 9, '2026-04-13 00:14:04', '2026-04-16 02:41:04', NULL),
(3457, 927, 781, 'The author of Hebrews used the Old Testament to support his theological views by making it as the main focus for his teaching. He didn\'t create new ideas but rather he showed that everything about Jesus was already pointed out in the Old Testament. Since the author and his readers already believed that the Old Testament was God\'s Word, all he had to do was to prove that Jesus is the true fulfillment of God\'s plan. A few examples of how he accomplished this was first he used historical facts to explain deeper truths. An example of this can be found in Hebrews 7:2 where he explains the meaning of Melchizedek\'s name which means \"king of righteousness\" and \"king of peace.\" This shows how Jesus is the greater king and the greater priest. Another example that can be found is how he used theological truths that were already known. If you read Hebrews 1:7 it says that the angels are servants while Jesus is the son. This shows that Jesus is greater than angels. Another area is how he reminded them of moral lessons. This can be seen in Hebrews 3:7-15. In this passage the author warns his readers not to harden their hearts like Israel did in the wilderness. The author also points out prophecies about the future as seen in Jeremiah 31 which speaks of a new covenant. He shows that this promise is fulfilled in Jesus. Last but not least, he showed that Jesus fulfills the ideal king from David\'s line by perfectly living out what earlier kings could not. All of these different examples show that the Old Testament and Jesus are fully connected and furthermore shows that Jesus is the center of God\'s plan.', 9, '2026-04-18 10:44:47', '2026-05-13 00:19:52', NULL),
(3458, 927, 782, 'The author of Hebrews teaches that Jesus is superior to angels by showing his readers both who Jesus is and what He as done. You see in the Old Testament angels where highly respected because they delivered messages from God. As a result of this there were some people that even began to elevate angels too highly. This is where the author makes it clear that Jesus is far greater than angels in every way. He does this by first showing that Jesus is the Son of God and not a servant. Angels are described as servants who carry out God\'s work but Jesus on the other hand is the one who rules. Hebrews points to Old Testament passages that call the Song \"God\" and place Him at God\'s right hand. This position that is given is the position of power and authority. This shows that Jesus has a much higher status than angels. Secondly the author explains that Jesus brings God\'s final and greatest message. You see in the past God spoke through prophets and sometimes through angels but now God has spoken through His Son. What this means is that Jesus is not just another messenger but rather He is the complete and final revelation of God. Next the author shows that the angels worship Jesus and not the other way around. This is very important because it proves that Jesus is above the angels. You see angels are created beings but Jesus on the hand is the one that they serve and honor. Last but not least the author explains that Jesus became human and died to save people from their sins. Angels never did anything like that. Jesus took on flesh, suffered, and paid the price for sin so that people could be saved. By using all these  evidences by showing that Jesus is the Son, the final message, the one worshiped by angels, and the Savior, the author makes it very clear to his readers that Jesus is far greater than angels in every way.', 8, '2026-04-18 11:00:49', '2026-05-13 00:19:52', NULL),
(3459, 927, 783, 'The new covenant is superior to the old because it is built on the perfect work of Jesus. You see the old covenant given through Moses. While this was good because it came from God, it could not fully solve the problem of sin. It showed people God\'s law and it also included sacrifices and priests. However, it could not completely cleanse the heart or bring final forgiveness. But what it did do was point forward to something better that was still coming. The new covenant is better because Jesus is its mediator. Under the old covenant, human priests served in an earthly tabernacle and offered animal sacrifices again and again. These sacrifices were ment to be temporary and had to be repeated because they could not take away sin. Jesus on the other hand is our perfect High Priest. He serves in the true heavenly sanctuary and not an earthly one. Another example of how the new covenant is better is because Jesus offered Himself once for all. Instead of bringing the blood of animals again and again, He gave His own life as the final sacrifice for sin. Because His sacrifice was perfect, it never has to be repeated. This is one of the greatest differences between he old and the new covenants. The old system reminded people of sin again and again but Jesus brings real forgiveness. Another reason the new covenant is greater is that it fulfills the promise of Jeremiah 31. You can see here that God said that He would make a new covenant, write His law on His people\'s hearts, forgive their wickedness, and remember their sins no more. The author shows us that this promise is fulfilled in Christ. Because of the new covenant, believers can now draw near to God with confidence, have real hope, and rest in the finished work of Jesus. The new covenant is better because Jesus is better, His sacrifice is better, and His covenant brings what the old covenant could only point towards.', 8, '2026-04-18 11:14:59', '2026-05-13 00:19:52', NULL),
(3460, 927, 784, 'The book of Hebrews makes it clear that the Old Testament is extremely important and honestly I have to admit that I am not as familiar with it as I should be. For a long time it has been easier to focus on the Gospels and the New Testament because they feel more direct and easier to understand. However through this study I am starting to see that the Old Testament is not just background it is the foundation of everything we believe. Because of this, I am trying to be more intentional about reading through the Old Testament and spending time studying it. I realize now that if I skip it, I am missing a huge part of God\'s story. The Old Testament teaches about who God is, His holiness, His faithfulness, and His patience with His people. It also helps me understand why Jesus had to come and what His work really means. We all know of the inspiring accounts, examples and lessons in the Old Testament stories like David, Jospeh, and Moses but there are more detailed lessons and examples we can really learn if we spend the time to dive deeper. There are many real life examples of trusting God in difficult situations and these are not just stories but incredible lessons that can be applied in my life today. In my life and ministry I want to grow in valuing the whole Bible and not just leaning towards one part of it. This means that that I continue to study the Old Testament and learn how it all points to Jesus. An additional bonus is using this to teaching my family as well as when I get opportunities at Children\'s Church. Overall, I am not where I want to be yet, I am still a work in progress but I am taking the steps to now and give the Old Testament the importance it truly deserves.', 8, '2026-04-18 11:28:27', '2026-05-13 00:19:52', NULL),
(3461, 927, 785, 'One of the main messages of Hebrews is that Jesus is completely enough. It teaches that He has fully paid for sin and nothing else needs to be added. In my own life and ministry, I want to clearly communicate that truth in simple and practical ways. One of these ways is that I can communicate the sufficiency of Christ by how I live my daily life. You see people are always watching, especially my kids. If I am constantly stressed, trying to control everything, or acting like everything depends on me then I am not showing trust in Jesus. However, when I trust God and stay steady during hard times and rely on Him instead of myself, it shows that Christ is truly enough for me. Secondly, I can communicate this truth by teaching and speaking about it clearly. It doesn\'t matter if it\'s with my family or others around me, I can still express that the sacrifice that Jesus made for us was complete. He does not need our good works to finish what He already did. The book of Hebrews teaches that His sacrifice was once and for all. That in itself should give us the confidence and peace we need. Another example is that I can point people back to Jesus instead of to performance. What I mean is that it is easy for people to feel like they have to earn God\'s love to fix themselves first. It is a good reminder that Jesus already did the work. Our role is simple, just trust Him and follow Him. Lastly in my role as a father, I can model this by showing grace, patience, and consistency. When my kids see that love is not based on performance, it reflects how God treated us. Overall my life, my words, and the leadership positions that I am in should point people to the one truth, Jesus is enough.', 9, '2026-04-18 11:43:59', '2026-05-13 00:19:52', NULL),
(3462, 930, 897, 'Before Paul was arrested there were several important things that happened to help paint the picture for us to better understand the situation. Near the end of his third missionary journey, Paul was traveling back to Jerusalem. His main goal was to bring money to help Christians there who were suffering from a famine but you see this gift was not just about helping people financially. Paul also hoped that it would bring unity between the Jewish and the Gentile believers and also help them see each other as one family in Christ. As Paul was making his way to Jerusalem, he kept getting a lot of warnings. The Holy Spirit made it clear to him that he would face trouble, suffering, and even imprisonment when he arrived. In a place called Miletus, Paul told the church leaders that he did not know exactly what would happen but he knew it would not be easy. Even with that knowledge, he still felt strongly that God wanted him to continue. In other cities like Tyre and Caesarea, there were people who also warned Paul not to go. One powerful moment happened when a prophet named Agabus took Paul\'s belt and tied up his own hands and feet. This was a visual way of saying that Paul would be arrested and bound if he went to Jerusalem. The people who loved Paul were very concerned and begged him not to go because they did not want him to suffer. Despite all of this, Paul did not change his mind. He understood that these warnings were not meant to stop him but rather it was to prepare him. He trusted that God had a purpose for everything, even if it were to involve pain. You see Paul was committed to his mission that he said he was ready not only to be arrested but even to die for the name of Jesus. With this in mind, Paul chose to follow God no matter what the cost may be. As a result, that decision led to his arrest.', 9, '2026-05-03 21:19:31', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3463, 930, 898, 'Paul\'s arrest in Jerusalem happened because of a lot of unfortunate misunderstandings, accusations, as well as growing tension between different groups of people. You see when Paul arrived in Jerusalem, he met with James as well as the church leaders. They were very glad to see him but they also had their concerns. The reason for this is because there were rumors going around that Paul was teaching Jewish Christians to stop following their traditions like the laws of Moses. Even though this was not true, there were many people believed it. Paul actually respected those traditions and was willing to follow them when it helped share the gospel. To calm the situation down, the leaders asked Paul to take part in a temple ritual with four men who had made special vows. They wanted Paul to show publicly that he still honored Jewish customs. Paul agreed to do this because he wanted peace and unity especially between the Jewish and the Gentile believers. However, that did not go according to plan. You see while Paul was in the temple, there were some Jews from Asia that saw him. They had previously seen him with a Gentile man named Trophimus. They wrongly assumed that Paul had brought this Gentile into a part of the temple where only Jews were allowed. This was actually a very serious accusation and it made them very angry. Now because of this false assumption and accusation, a crowd quickly formed. The people started becoming violent, grabbed Paul and dragged him out of the temple. They were so upset that they were ready to kill him right there on the spot. It was at that moment a Roman soldier stepped in and stopped the mob. The commander arrested Paul. It wasn\'t because he was guilty but rather to bring order and stop the chaos. So all in all Paul\'s arrest wasn\'t because he broke the law but rather because of the false accusations and misunderstandings about his teachings and the anger of the crowd.', 9, '2026-05-03 21:36:25', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3464, 930, 899, 'Paul\'s imprisonment in Rome came after a very long and difficult journey. This turned out to be one of the most important seasons of his ministry. After being held in Caesarea for about two years, Paul used his rights as a Roman citizen to appeal his case to Caesar. What this meant was, he had to be sent to Rome for his trail. The trip to Rome was not easy at all. Paul traveled by ship under Roman guard. Along his journey the entire crew as well as Paul faced dangerous weather. Even though Paul warned the crew not to continue sailing, they went ahead and were caught in a violent storm that lasted for many days. Eventually the ship was wrecked near the island of Malta. Everyone survived just as Paul had said that they would. While they were on Malta, Paul continued to serve others. He healed the sick people and showed God\'s power even though he was still a prisoner. After a few months passed, Paul finally arrived in Rome. Instead of being placed in a dark prison, he was kept under house arrest for about two years. This meant that he lived in his own rented place but he was always guarded by a Roman soldier. Despite the fact that he was not free to leave, he was allowed to have visitors to come and visit him. Even though he was restricted physically, he was not spiritually. He continued to preach the gospel and teach about Jesus, as well as encourage people who came to see him. There were many people who heard the message of Jesus because of this situation. Paul also used this time to write letters to the various churches as well as to individuals. These letters later became part of the New Testament and have impacted Christians even to this day. Despite the face that Paul was in chains, his ministry didn\'t stop but rather reached even further. Paul\'s imprisonment in Rome shows that God can use difficult situations for a greater purpose. What looked like a limitation actually became a very powerful opportunity to spread the gospel.', 9, '2026-05-03 21:53:45', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3465, 930, 900, 'There are a lot of things here to uncover. Paul\'s imprisonment teaches a very powerful truth about God and Paul\'s ministry. From the outside, Paul\'s situation looks like a complete failure. He was falsely accused, attacked by a mob, and stuck in prison for years without justice. However, when you take a closer look you can see that God was still fully in control and working through every part of it. One important insight about God is that He is sovereign. This means that God is in control even when life seems unfair or confusing. Paul\'s arrest was not an accident. God used every part of it to move Paul exactly where He wanted him to be. Throughout all the trails, court hearings, and even on the dangerous journey, Paul was eventually brought to Rome. This allowed the message of Jesus to reach people it might not have reached otherwise. It shows that God can use even the suffering and the injustice to accomplish His plans. Another point to consider is that God\'s plans are not stopped by hardship. Even though Paul was in chains, the gospel was not. Paul\'s situation was used to help spread it even further. This teaches that God\'s work does not depend on perfect circumstances. He can use any situation for good. Paul\'s imprisonment also reveals a lot about his own ministry. First of all, Paul did not see prison as the end of his calling. He continued to preach, teach, and encourage others. He also continued to speak boldly to leaders, guards, as well as visitors. He also spent a great deal of time praying for others as well as writing letters that would later become part of the Bible. Secondly, Paul understood that suffering was part of following Jesus. He was not at all surprised by the hardship that he faced but rather he accepted it and trusted that God had a plan and purpose for it. The attitude towards it showed courage, faith, and a deep commitment. All this to say that Paul\'s imprisonment showed us that God is always working even in difficult times and that true ministry continues no matter the circumstances.', 9, '2026-05-03 22:12:49', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3466, 930, 901, 'There are three things the book of Acts teaches us about Paul\'s ministry during his imprisonment. These lessons helps us understand how Paul continued to serve God even though he faced very difficult situations. First of all, Acts shows that Paul was fully aware of the suffering that was coming. He was not surprised when he ws arrested or when he was put in prison. Before going to Jerusalem, Paul had already been warned many times that he would face some hardships. Instead of running away from it, he accepted it. He understood that following Jesus would sometimes lead to pain and sacrifice. This shows that Paul\'s ministry was build on courage and obedience. He chose to follow God not matter the cost even if that meant that he had to suffer. Secondly, Acts shows use that Paul had a strong sense of purpose. He believed that God would use his imprisonment for something greater. Even though Paul was a prisoner he didn\'t stop sharing the message of Jesus. He spoke to crowds, religious leaders, governors, and even kings. Every time he was given a chance to speak, he would use it to tell others about Christ. Prison did not stop his ministry but rather it gave him new opportunities to reach people he might never have met otherwise. Thirdly the book of Acts shows us that Paul experienced God\'s blessings even during his imprisonment. God continued to guide him, protect him, and work through him. An example of this can be seen when Paul was able to encourage others during a dangerous storm at sea. He even healed people when he was on the island of Malta. These are the moments that show us that God was still with Paul and God was still using him in powerful ways. Even though these are just a few examples, the Book of Acts teaches that Paul\'s ministry was full of suffering, but it was driven by purpose and supported by God\'s ongoing presence and blessings.', 9, '2026-05-03 22:30:12', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3467, 930, 902, 'Paul developed the idea that Christ is King over all creation in three main ways. This helps us understand who Jesus is and why it matters especially during Paul\'s time in prison. First of all Paul taught that Jesus has complete authority over everything. This means that Christ is not just a spiritual leader but rather a true ruler of all creation. After the resurrection and return to heaven, Jesus was given power and authority over the entire world. Even though earthly leaders like the Roman Empire seemed powerful, Paul made it clear that Jesus is above them all. This truth was especially important because Paul himself was in prison under Roman authority. Secondly, Paul emphasized that Jesus deserves the highest honor. Since Christ is not only King but also God, He is worthy of worship, respect, and obedience. Paul wanted believers to understand that Jesus is not just important but also supreme over everything. this means that people should respond to Him with their whole lives. They should honor Him not only with words but also through their actions, choices, and attitudes. Thirdly, Paul taught that Christ is determined to complete His rule. Right now not everything in the world fully obeys Jesus but there is coming a day that will change. Paul goes on to explain that Jesus will return and fully establish His kingdom. This gives believers hope for the future. No matter how broken the world may seem, there is coming a day that Christ will rule and will make it complete and perfect.', 9, '2026-05-03 22:39:23', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3468, 930, 903, 'Pauls idea of being united with Christ is a pretty simple idea when you break it down. He\'s saying that when someone follows Jesus, they\'re not just believing in Him from a distance but rather they are closely connected to Him, almost like their lives are joined together. A way to think of it is when Jesus died, Paul teaches that it\'s like our old life died with Him. The person we used to be especially the part of us controlled by sin, doesn\'t have the same power anymore. Then when Jesus rose from the dead, we were given a new king of life. So being a Christian isn\'t just about trying harder but rather it\'s about becoming a new person on the inside. Paul also explains that because we are connected to Jesus, we share in what he has. Jesus has authority and a place with God and in a spiritual sense, we are connected to that. That means our identity changes. We are not defined by our past mistakes anymore. We are defined by being connected to Christ. This has some really important effects on how we live. First it gives us confidence. If we are connected to Jesus then e are secure in Him. We don\'t have to constantly worry about losing that relationship. Secondly, it changes how we act. If we are connected to Jesus, our lives should start to look more like His. What that means in showing love, being humble, and making better choices. Not because we have to earn anything, but because we\'ve been changed. Finally it gives us hope. Just like Jesus was raised to life, we know that our future is also secure in Christ.', 9, '2026-05-03 22:48:06', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3469, 930, 904, 'The main theological truth that ties all of Paul\'s prison letters together is this simple but yet power idea that Jesus is Lord over everything and believers are connected to Him. Everything Paul writes comes back to that. Even while he was in prison, Paul kept pointing people to the fact that Jesus is in control, not Rome, not circumstances, or anything else for that matter. This truth connects directly to Christ\'s kingship. Paul teaches that Jesus is the true King over all creation. That means He has authority over the whole world even over rulers and governments. Even though Paul as sitting in prison, he know Jesus was still on the throne. That gave him confidence and peace because he understood that God was still working behind the scenes. This also connects to our union with Christ. If Jesus is King and we are united with Him, then we are closely connected to the One who is in charge of everything. That changes how we see ourselves. We are not just trying to follow rules but rather we are living in a relationship with Jesus, sharing in His life. Our identity comes from Him not from our past or the situations that we face. Because of that, it naturally leads into how we live. This is where ethical living comes in. If we are connected to Jesus and He is our King then our lives should reflect that. We should live with love, humility, patience, and obedience. This isn\'t to earn anything but in the end it\'s because we belong to Him.', 8, '2026-05-03 22:56:58', '2026-05-13 00:20:42', NULL),
(3470, 932, 905, 'Paul\'s relationship with the Colossian church was somewhat unusual because he had never met most of the believers there in person. Unlike the other churches such as Philippi or Corinth where Paul spent alot of time teaching and ministering, the church in Colosse was different. Even though Paul likely never visited them, he still cared for them deeply and was very concerned for their spiritual well being. This shows us something vvery important about Paul\'s heart. He did not only care about people he knew personally but he also cared about all believers and wanted to see them grow in their faith and remain faithful to Christ. Most of Paul\'s connection other the Colossian church came through trusted friends. The most important person was Epaphras. He was probably the one who stared the church in Colosse and shared the gospel with the people there. Whenever false teachers began influencing the church, Epaphras traveled to visit Paul in prison and told him what was happening. Because of Epaphras\' report, Paul wrote letters to the Colossians. He spoke very highly of Epaphras and described him as a faithful servant of Christ who cared deeply for the church and prayed for them often. Paul also had a close friendship with Philemon who was a respected Christian who lived in Colossee. Paul called him a beloved friend and fellow worker. Another important person was Onesimus who was a slave who had run away from Philemon but later became a Christian through Paul\'s ministry. Onesimus eventually because a valuable helper to Paul. Apphia and Archiuppus was also likely involved in the church and there is a strong change that they may have been part of Philemon\'s family. These relationships help us understand why Paul wrote with such passion and concern. He was not just writing as a distant teacher who knew nothing about the people but rather through his friendships and connections. He genuinely cared about the Colossian believers. He wanted to protect them from false teaching.', 0, '2026-06-20 18:17:49', '2026-06-20 18:17:49', NULL),
(3471, 932, 906, 'One of the biggest problems that were facing the Colossian church was the influence of false teachers who were mixing Christianity with ideas from Greek philosophy, Jewish law, and the worship of spiritual beings. These teachings may have sounded wise and spiritual but they were actually pulling believers away from the truth. The truth that Paul tried to emphasize was that Christ is completely sufficient for salvation and spiritual growth. The first problem was Greek Philosophy. During the time of Paul, philosophy was not just about learning or education. It many times included secret knowledge, mystical experiences, visions, and hidden wisdom. The false teachers claimed that they had access to deeper spiritual truths that ordinary Christians did not know of. They promoted asceticism which involved harsh self denial and sound make someone more spiritual. Paul strongly disagreed with all of these teachings. He went on to explain that these human rules could not change a person\'s heart or defeat sin. True wisdom and spiritual growth are found in Christ alone. You will not find it in some secret knowledge or self made religion. The second problem was that of the misuse of Jewish law. The false teachers emphasized food laws, religious festivals, Sabbaths, and circumcision as though these practices were necessary for spiritual maturity. Paul was not trying to attach the Old Testament law but rather he was trying to explain that these practices were only shadows pointing forward to Christ. Now that Church had come, believers should focus on Him rather than the symbols that pointed to Him. The third and most serious problem was that of spiritual beings. The false teachers encouraged people to worship angels and fear spiritual powers. They claimed these beings could provide special wisdom and protection. Paul then responded by reminding them that that Christ created every ruler, authority, and spiritual power. Not only that but Christ had already defeated the powers of darkness through His death on the cross. Because of this believers did not need to worship, fear, or seek help from spiritual beings because everything they needed was already found in Jesus. If you see, all of these messages were pretty much concluding to the same thing, \"Christ is not enough.\"', 0, '2026-06-20 18:31:52', '2026-06-20 18:31:52', NULL),
(3472, 932, 907, 'Understanding the background of Colossians is very important because it helps us understand why Paul wrote the letter int he first place. If you only read the letter without knowing what was happening in the church, some of Paul\'s teachings might seem random or even difficult to understand. Once you learn about the problems that the Colossian believers were facing, then Paul\'s message becomes much clearer. The church in Colossee was bring influenced by false teachers who were introducing the idea that were not part of the true gospel. These teachers were mixing Christianity with greek philosophy, Jewish religious practices, and the worship of spiritual beings such as angels. They claimed that believers needed more than Jesus in order to become truly spiritual. According to them, people needed secret knowledge, special spiritual experiences, strict religious rules, and help from spiritual powers. This became a very serious problem because it cased people Tod out whether Christ alone was enough. Knowing this background helps understand why Paul focuses so much on the greatness and the supremacy of Christ throughout the letter. Paul wasn\'t just teaching theology just for the sake of teach but rather he was responding directly to the false ideas that were spreading through the church. When Paul says that charts is the image of the invisible God, the creator of all things, and the head of the church, he is showing that Jesus is far greater than any angel, philosophy, or religious system. When he says that all wisdom and knowledge are found in Christ, he is answering the false teachers who claimed to have secret wisdom. The background also helps us understand Paul\'s personal concern for the church. Even though he had probably never met most of the believers in Colosse, he cared deeply about them. Paul knew what was happening and wanted to protect them from teachings that could harm their faith.', 0, '2026-06-20 18:45:43', '2026-06-20 18:45:43', NULL),
(3473, 932, 908, 'Paul demonstrated to the Colossians that Christ was supreme by showing them that Jesus is greater than everything the false teachers were promoting. The false teachers were encouraging people to trust in philosophy, religious rules, mystical experiences, angels, and spiritual powers. Paul responded by pointing the believers back to Christ and showing that everything they were looking for could already be found in Him. One of the most powerful ways that Paul demonstrated the supremacy of Christ was by describing who Jesus is. He said that Christ is the image of the invisible God. What this means is that if someone wanted to know what God is like, they should look at Jesus. Christ perfectly reveals God because He is God in human flesh. Paul also called Jesus the firstborn over all creation. This does not mean that Jesus was created but rather it means that He has the highest position, authority, and honor over everything that exists. Paul went even on to teach that Jesus is the creator of all things. Everything in heaven and on earth, whether visible or invisible, was created through Him and for Him. This also includes angels, rulers, authorities, and spiritual powers. The false teachers were encouraging people to focus on these spiritual beings, but Paul reminded them that those beings were created by Christ. They were not rivals to Jesus but rather they were under His authority. Paul also explained that Christ is the sea of the church and that all the fullness of God dwells in Him. There is no human teacher, religious tradition, or spiritual experience that can take His place. Paul also taught that Christ alone can reconcile people to God. Through His death on the cross, Jesus provided forgiveness, peace, and salvation. There was no ritual, angel, or philosophy that can do what Christ has already done.', 0, '2026-06-20 18:59:02', '2026-06-20 18:59:02', NULL),
(3474, 932, 909, 'Paul taught that Christ\'s ministers were superior to the false teachers because their ministry was build on truth, sacrifice, and God\'s power rather than human ideas and empty promises. The false teachers in Colossee claimed to have special wisdom, secret knowledge, and deeper spiritual experiences. They tried to impress people with their philosophies, visions, religious rules, and teachings about spiritual beings. Paul on the other hand showed that true ministers of Christ had something far greater than impressive sounding ideas. They had the true Gospel. First, Church\'s ministers were superior because they preached a message that actually saved people. The false teachers offered rules, rituals, and spiritual experiences. They could not reconcile people to God. Paul preached Christ and through that message people received forgiveness, salvation, and peace with God. The Colossians themselves were proof of this because their lives had already been transformed by the gospel before the false teachers arrived. Secondly, Christ\'s ministers demonstrated genuine love and sacrifice. Paul pointed to his own suffering as evidence of this. While the false teachers were seeking influence and recognition, Paul was sitting in prion because he had devoted his life to serving Christ and His church. He was willing to suffer so that others could hear the gospel and grow in their faith. His ministry was marked by humility and self sacrifice rather than personal gain. Thirdly, Paul explained that Christ\'s ministers had a divine calling and authority. Paul was not teaching ideas he invented himself. He had been personally called and commissioned by Jesus. The message he preached came from God\'s revelation not from human imagination or philosophy. The false teachers relied on traditions, speculation, and human wisdom but Paul\'s authority came from God. Lastly, Christ\'s ministers were empowered by God Himself. Paul said that he worked with the strength that God provided. His ministry was effective because God was a t work through him. The false teachers depended on human efforts and world wisdom but Paul deepened on God\'s power.', 0, '2026-06-20 19:13:24', '2026-06-20 19:13:24', NULL),
(3475, 933, 910, 'To completely understand why it is believed that Paul wrote Ephesians you have to look at the clues left behind in the letter and in early history. There are some modern scholars today that think that it was a student of Paul who wrote Ephesians after Paul died just to keep his ideas going but the evidence shows that Paul himself was the real author. Knowing that Paul wrote it is a big in itself because it means that the letters carries real apostolic authority from an official representative of Jesus. The first big rule of this is right at the start of the letter in Ephesians 1:1 where it explicitly says it is from \"Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God.\" If anyone else wrote it and put Paul\'s name on it, that would be fake. Why this matters is because Paul hated forgeries. In another letter, he warned Christians not to be tricked by fake letters that pretended to be from him. It makes no sense that a loyal student of Paul would try to honor him by doing something that Paul explicitly told people not to do. Also, the way Ephesians is written sounds like Paul\'s other letters, which all were written around the exact same time while he was stuck in a Roman prison. These letters talk about the same deep theological topics and use the exact same vocabulary to show that Jesus rules supreme over all spiritual powers and demonic forces. Finally, Paul had a massive history with the city of Ephesus. The book of Acts tells us that he planted the church there and lived with them for 2 years. He as close friends with the leaders so the people in the church would have instantly recognized if a letter was fake trying to sound like him. in addition to that, very early church leaders from history completely agreed that Paul was the true writer. When you look at internal clues and historical facts, it is clear that Paul wrote Ephesians to teach Christians how to live and follow Jesus.', 0, '2026-06-27 10:16:27', '2026-06-27 10:16:27', NULL),
(3476, 933, 911, 'To fully understand why Paul wrote the letters to the Ephesians you have to look past the fact that he was just writing to write random theological essays and consider the fact that he had a heart for these peoples as a paster. While he usually wrote letters to fix specific local problems in one exact church, Ephesians was written to be a circular letter that was passed around to multiple congregations. This includes churches like Colossee and Laodicea. Because he was addressing several churches at once, his strategy was to give all of them a single universal framework. He wrote this letter to teach these believers how to understand their new identity and how to successfully live, build, and thrive as citizens under the rule of Jesus. First of all Paul wrote to help Christians transition out of their old lifestyles. He reminds them that before salvation they were spiritually dead and trapped int he kingdom of darkness completely controlled by sin and Satan. Since many of the readers came from pagan backgrounds like Ephesus, which already was a major hub for occult practices and worship of the goddess Artemis, they struggled to change how they thought and acted. Paul wrote this to show them that because God gracefully transferred them into he kingdom of light their because had to match their new citizenship. They needed to drop their old sinful habits and put on a pure code for living that reflected the holy character of Jesus. Secondly, Paul wrote to solve intense racial and ethnic divisions between Jewish and Gentile believers. Before Christ, Gentiles were treated as outsiders and completely excluded from the national citizenship of Israel. Paul wrote to demolish any sense of cultural or spatial superiority by expelling that through the blood of Christ, the dividing wall of hostility has been torn down. He wanted them to know that Jews and Gentiles are not full equal co-citizens in one body. Finally, Paul wrote to prepare the churches for ongoing spiritual warfare. Since rival demonic forces from the \"kingdom of the air\" were constantly fighting against the church, Paul wrote to encourage believers to stand firm. He uses military language to show that they are soldiers who must put on the full armor of God to fight from the position of Christ\'s ultimate victory. Ultimately, Paul wrote Ephesians to give believers a large scale vision of the Gospel, proving that God\'s plan is to defeat His enemies and unite the entire universe under the lordship of Jesus Christ.', 0, '2026-06-27 10:32:42', '2026-06-27 10:32:42', NULL),
(3477, 933, 912, 'Knowing the background of Ephesians is very important because Paul\'s letters were not just random theology papers but rather they were real pastoral messages written to help the first century believers deal with real world problems. When you take the time to learn about who wrote the letters, who received them, and the type of culture they lived in, the text makes alot more sense. When you explore these historical details, it helps us see exactly why Paul chose his specific vocabulary and themes. First of all, knowing that the primary audience was the church in Ephesus explains why Paul talks so much about spiritual powers and glory. Ephesus was a world class Roman city famous for its heavy involvement in occult magic and its deep devotion to the pagan goddess Artemis. You can see in Acts 19 where Paul\'s ministry directly clashed with these types of dark practices. With this background in mind, you can understand why Paul warns against fruitless deeds of darkness and commands believers to put on the whole armor of God. Because the city legally claimed that Artemis made Ephesus glorious, we can see why Paul flips this cultural imagery by writing that Christ is the true Lord who nurtures the church to make her His glorious bride. Secondly, knowing that the letter was meant to circulate throughout the surrounding churches like Colosse and Laodicea, this explains the unique style of the book. Paul knew the Ephesian church intimately but this is his only letter with absolutely no personal references or greetings. This background shows us that because Paul was writing to an audience that included believers he had never met, he had to speak in a general way. He chose the theme of the Kingdom of God because it was perfect tool that could solve the problems of multiple churches all at once. Some of this was personal sin, racial tension between Jews and Gentiles, and demonic attacks. Understanding this background helps us see that Paul\'s vision of the gospel was perfectly tailored to teach early Christians how to live under the rule of Jesus.', 0, '2026-06-27 10:46:50', '2026-06-27 10:46:50', NULL),
(3478, 933, 913, 'In the overall structure of Ephesians, Paul\'s opening sections of praise and prayer serve as the important theological foundation of the entire letter. Ephesians is divided into five major parts, saluation, a section of prayer, the main body, and final greetings. Unlike this other letters where he normally includes warm personal greetings to his friends, Paul uniquely uses extended praise and prayer right after his salutation to introduce his naming theme. By doing this, he sets up the big picture of what Christ has accomplished before giving any deadly rules to the church. First of all, the praise section in Chapters 1:3-14 serves as a grand celebration of God\'s sovereign rule, benevolence, and kingdom blessings. The part acts alot like an official ancient document honoring a great king. Paul praises God for choosing and pretesting His special people before time began. This shows His absolute authority over creation. He also focuses on the King\'s generosity. He highlights spiritual blessings like adoption, redemption through Christ\'s blood and the forgiveness for sins. Finally he points out to our secure inheritance in Christ which was a legal benefit only given to citizens of a kingdom. Secondly the prayer section in chapter 1: 15-23 shifts from praising the King to asking the Holy Spirit to mentally enlighten the readers. Paul prays that the believers will truly grasp the unmatched power of God which raised Jesus from the dead and enthroned Him far above all rival spiritual or demonic governments. This functions structurally to show that Christ is ruling as King specifically for the benefit of the church, which is His prized possession and physical body. To sum up, Paul\'s teaching on praise and prayer functions structurally to open the eye of believers to their royal identity, giving them the exact understanding and motivation they need to obey the strict kingdom code for living found in the rest of the letter.', 0, '2026-06-27 11:01:25', '2026-06-27 11:01:25', NULL);
INSERT INTO `descriptive_answers` (`id`, `assignment_id`, `question_id`, `answer`, `mark`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `deleted_at`) VALUES
(3479, 933, 914, 'In the main body of Ephesians, Paul explains how the kingdom of God works by breaking it down into three main areas; citizenship, administration, and a code for living. He goes on to use these concepts to contrast God\'s righteous kingdom of light with Satan\'s sinful kingdom of darkness. First of all, Paul describes citizenship as a total rescue mission from our old life. He reminds the readers that before salvation, all human beings are spiritually dead and trapped in the kingdom of darkness serving Satan. But because of His great mercy, God uses His royal power to breathe new life into believers and transfer their citizenship into the kingdom of light. This new citizenship completely destroys old racial and ethnic walls. Gentile delivers are not longer treated as second class outsiders or foreigners but through union with Christ, they are now full, equal co-citizens with Jewish believers in God\'s household. Secondly, Paul explains that a functional kingdom needs an administrative structure which God provided through church offices. In the early church, God managed His kingdom through the foundational office of the apostle. Men like Paul were personally chosen by God and given direct, perfect revelation through the Holy Spirit to teach the church and overrule false teachers. Today, while that specific office has ceased, the church still submits to this apostolic administration by following the teachings of the Scripture. Thirdly, Paul gives a strict code for living which acts as a practical handbook for kingdom citizens. This code covers church life where Christ gives spiritual gifts and leaders to equip ordinary citizens to serve one another. It commands moral purity, telling believers to strip off their old sinful habits and put on a new self that reflects the King\'s holy character. It also sets up domestic order showing that family and work relationships must be shaped by loving leadership and respectful submission. Finally, it drafts every citizen into a spiritual army suiting them up in the armor of God to stand firm against demonic forces. In the end, Paul teaches that because believers have a new royal identity, they just completely change how they live to honor their true King.', 0, '2026-06-27 11:14:49', '2026-06-27 11:14:49', NULL),
(3480, 933, 915, 'The reason Christians should work to build the kingdom of God is because helping it grow is an essential part of our calling and our response to God\'s grace. While humans do not create the kingdom by their own power, God explicitly calls believers to participate in His great building plan. Working to expand the church on earth allows us to honor our King, love our neighbors, and push back spiritual darkness. First of all, we need to build the kingdom because God created us new in Christ for the exact purpose of doing good works. Good works are not how we earn our salvation but rather they are the primary tools God uses to expand, purify, and care for His kingdom. When Christians actively preform these tasks, they accept their appointments and callings as servants of the King, adopting His goals and bringing Him real glory. Secondly we build the kingdom by preserving deep unity and breaking down social barriers. Paul uses the picture of a holy temple where Christ is the cornerstone and every Christian is an individual building stone. Building this temple means destroying pride, racial divisions, and wealth gaps, as well as treating all believers as equals. Thirdly, we build the kingdom by using our unique spiritual gifts to serve the church. Paul also compares the kingdom to a body where Christ is the head and the believers make up the physical parts. Church leaders are there to equip ordinary citizens for ministry so that every single member can do their part. This mutual service builds up the church in truth and love, protecting it from false teachings and helping it mature. In the end, working to build the kingdom allows the church to grow into a perfect dwelling place for God\'s spirit which progressively brings the entire world under the rule of Jesus.', 0, '2026-06-27 11:28:24', '2026-06-27 11:28:24', NULL),
(3481, 933, 916, 'Before this lesson, it was easy to think of the Gospel as a small personal message. Most people think it just means that Jesus forgives their individual sins so they can go to heaven when they die. While those personal beliefs are partially true, Paul shows that the real biblical gospel is actually alot bigger than that. His teachings connects our personal faith to a massive, universe sized plan centered entirely on the kingdom of God. Paul explains that the gospel is a royal announcement from a powerful King. It is the good news that King Jesus has arrived to defeat sin, conquer His enemies, and rescue His people from the control of Satan. Instead of just saving individuals one by one, Jesus is building a brand new kingdom community. This big picture connects our personal faith to big actions like stopping arguments between different races, staying morally pure and using our special spiritual gifts to help our local church family grow together. Paul also talks about living in a time where two ears overlap. We already belong to Jesus but we still have to live in a broken world where Satan\'s kingdom of darkness is active and trying to make us fail. That is why good deeds, testing our families with love and fighting spiritual battles are so important. They are not just extra rules that we have to follow to get to heaven but rather they are practical ways we show our true loyalty and devotion to our King. Paul shows us that the gospel is not just a private contract but rather a solid promise that Jesus will fix the entire universe and rule over us in peace.', 0, '2026-06-27 11:45:03', '2026-06-27 11:45:03', NULL);
